\par
and clarifying leadership roles and accountability between Cluster actors.
\par
Given its prominence as the global leader in foreign assistance and
\par
emergency relief, we also consider the role of US perceptions of and
\par
attitudes toward the Logistics Cluster and the overall Cluster Approach. We
\par
find that in spite of lingering challenges (eg civil--military coordination on
\par
logistics and global funding arrangements for the Clusters), the US
\par
government is positively disposed toward the Logistics Cluster. We
\par
conclude that the Logistics Cluster has met if not exceeded expectations as
\par
a key component of the Cluster Approach, and in a very short period of time
\par
has elevated the effectiveness of it as a global humanitarian response
