strategy.
\par
Introduction
\par
Whether it be in response to natural disasters like the 2005 Asian
\par
tsunami, man-made disasters like the ongoing conflict in the
\par
Democratic Republic of Congo, or the now common `embedded' or `hybrid'
\par
disasters involving both natural and man-made causes, such as the 2010
\par
Haiti earthquake, adopting a supply chain management approach to
\par
humanitarian logistics (HL) holds great potential to enhance the future of
\par
humanitarian response and relief. HL stands as a key practical issue
\par
surrounding the systematic improvement of humanitarian action.
\par
Analysing HL effectively involves a number of challenges for researchers.
\par
Altay (2008) has detailed the myriad issues affecting the study of HL, most
\par
of which are soft, ill-defined or mathematically difficult to code problems.
\par
Even though recent reviews of emergency management and the disaster
\par
relief literature provide a growing list of Operations Research/Management
\par
Science research, oddly enough many of the OR/MS models or applications
\par
developed so far fail to recognize this point (Altay and Green, 2006;
\par
Natarajarathinam et al, 2009; Simpson and Hancock, 2009). Moreover, the
\par
range and roles of actors involved in humanitarian action are both sizeable
\par
and complex, making it difficult to draw correlations, much less establish
\par
causality among factors and outcomes.
\par
However, the importance of HL as a central component of humanitarian
\par
response has become increasingly recognized in recent years (Chaikin,
\par
2003; Gustavsson, 2003). Logistics links nearly all stakeholders in
\par
humanitarian relief operations, including donor governments,
\par
intergovernmental organizations (IGOs), international nongovernmental
\par
organizations (INGOs), local NGOs, the military, the private sector, and
\par
local communities (Oloruntoba and Gray, 2006). Operationally, the
