\par
constraints facing many of these actors are multiple and overlapping.
\par
They include donor demand/pressure for accountability and transparency,
\par
competition for scarce funding resources, marketization of the INGO
\par
sector, high levels of staff turnover and low levels of institutional memory,
\par
and a lack of effective evaluation mechanisms. Enhancing humanitarian
\par
logistics capacity and operational effectiveness has thus formed a critical
\par
element of global humanitarian reform undertaken by the international
\par
community and the structural manifestation of that process, the United
\par
Nations (UN) Cluster Approach (CA).
\par
In this chapter we utilize this humanitarian supply chain management
\par
framework and its associated elements to examine more closely the
\par
implications carried by the establishment of the CA, broadly, and the
\par
Logistics Cluster (LC), specifically. Many of the norms undergirding this
\par
relatively new approach to humanitarian response reflect key elements
\par
constituting effective humanitarian logistics and supply chain frameworks.
\par
They include preparedness, coordination, leadership, accountability, and
\par
information and knowledge management. Given its prominence as the
\par
global leader in foreign assistance and emergency relief,1 we also explore
\par
shifts in US perspectives on the CA and the LC, and offer conclusions about
\par
the future of humanitarian response under the CA and the role the LC is
\par
likely to play in this area. It is important to note, however, that much of the
\par
analysis in this chapter derives from examining the `donor' side of
\par
humanitarian logistics. The impetuses for global humanitarian reform and
\par
evolving perspectives regarding the role of logistics that are constitutive of
\par
that effort have typically been driven by developed countries. While we feel
\par
that this inherent constraint necessarily limits the kinds of conclusions we
\par
can reasonably draw, it is, for the present time at least, a reality of the
\par
politics of humanitarianism with which we must grapple.
\par
Background
\par
Prior to the roll-out of the CA, very little natural or planned `alignment'
\par
occurred among humanitarian actors to meet the challenges of enhancing
\par
HL. This had been the case both at the international level and even at the
\par
national level in some countries (eg the US response to Hurricane Katrina).
\par
Effective alignment requires, among other strategies, a `standardization of
\par
tasks and products and a well designed infrastructure which consequently
\par
will promote coordination through assessment, management and
\par
dissemination of information' (Altay, 2008: 163). In part, this has been due
\par
to the nature of the actors operating within and across the humanitarian
\par
sector, donor funding priorities, a lack of common operational standards,
\par
and a scepticism among many humanitarian actors regarding centralization
\par
and coordination from above.
\par
HUMANITARIAN LOGISTICS
\par
01
\par
CHRISTOPHER
\par
