\par
Some scholars argue that HL currently resembles the corporate world's
\par
logistics environment of 20 years ago, characterized as it was by
\par
underinvestment, lack of recognition, and no real training opportunities/
\par
standards across the industry (Thomas and Kopczak, 2005; Beamon and
\par
Kotleba, 2006). This was largely the norm both at the international and
\par
donor country levels. It has also been claimed that because the humanitarian
\par
sector operates without the market forces of demand and supply regulated
\par
through price, there are few incentives to coordinate efforts or to use lessons
\par
learned to improve over time in any area, including humanitarian logistics
\par
(Van Wassenhove, 2006). Furthermore, it has been pointed out that the
\par
supply network of HL differs from corporate logistics in that there are no
\par
clear or stated linkages among the actors in HL (Kov\'acs and Spens, 2007).
\par
HL has also historically taken a back seat in emergency response
\par
planning, budgetary processes, and decision-making in part because of the
\par
lack of consensus about its function and importance. According to some,
\par
the `basic mentality' of logisticians in such situations has tended to be `led
\par
by project managers' (Rickard, 2003: 9). The sub-optimal potential of this
\par
mindset has been compounded when humanitarian actors working in the
\par
same crisis zone failed to collaborate and instead maintained stove-pipe
\par
arrangements that perpetuated ad hoc arrangements, inefficient procurement
\par
strategies, and duplicate efforts across organizations working in the same or
\par
related sectors (or parts of a disaster-affected country).
\par
Recent evidence suggests however, that there is growing recognition at
\par
the global and national levels among key humanitarian actors, including the
\par
UN system, IFRC, INGOs and the private sector, of both the importance of
\par
and need for preparedness (hence also indirectly logistics) in response to
\par
disasters (Van Wassenhove, 2006). An example of regional cooperation on
\par
HL involving developing countries has been the creation, by UN Office for
\par
the Coordination of Humanitarian Affairs (OCHA), of the Emergency
\par
Preparedness and Response Working Group (EPRWG). The EPRWG is
\par
constituted by 17 countries from the Great Lakes region and East Africa. It
\par
aims to improve practical collaboration and develop regionally informed
\par
solutions to common humanitarian response problems, develops initiatives
\par
to share procurement services and organizes resource sharing for logistics
\par
components of relief operations such as charter flights and contingency
\par
stocks. The EPRWG also trains staff for joint emergency preparedness;
\par
attempts to discover and apply to humanitarian operations appropriate
\par
corporate-derived logistics systems and encourages common standards for
\par
relief resources and equipment so more agencies can use the same kinds of
\par
materials but still keep their individual logos and markings on them for use
\par
on the ground (Fenton, 2003).
\par
Another example is the UN Joint Logistics Center (UNJLC). From a
\par
coordination standpoint, management of uncertainty during a humanitarian
\par
response, particularly through information sharing and cooperation
\par
between agencies, is critical to effective response (Altay, 2008). The need for
\par
an inter-agency coordination mechanism to deal specifically with logistics
