management among various actors; and serves as an information clearing
\par
house and disseminator to humanitarian and other actors on the ground.
\par
Despite these advances, more effective mechanisms for coordination and
\par
collaboration in HL as well as the field of humanitarian assistance and
\par
response were needed (OCHA, 2005). The inadequacy of the initial
\par
international response to the Asian tsunami in late 2004/early 2005, for
\par
example, was a wake-up call for many humanitarian actors. The 2005
\par
Humanitarian Response Review (HRR) process and the subsequent
\par
establishment of the CA are the results of this recognition. The HRR
\par
recommended a new humanitarian response strategy premised on a range of
\par
factors, including predictability, timeliness and effectiveness via: 1) ensuring
\par
predictable funding; 2) strengthening the Humanitarian Coordinator
\par
system; and 3) strengthening the overall humanitarian response capacity
\par
(OCHA, 2006a). The Central Emergency Response Fund (CERF) fulfills 1
\par
above; 2 is being addressed by developing mechanisms to improve training
\par
and qualification systems for HCs; and 3 is being fulfilled via the CA.
\par
Collectively, the HRR and the CA represent one of the most significant
\par
innovation attempts in the coordination and capacity-building of
\par
humanitarian response and disaster management undertaken by the
\par
international community. From the perspective of HL, the CA reflects the
\par
potential value-added of investing in and integrating how to get the `right
\par
goods to the right people in the right time frame' into a more holistic and
\par
centralized effort to make humanitarian response more effective.
\par
Change afoot -- the cluster approach and
\par
implications for humanitarian logistics
\par
The cluster approach stands as the prevailing organizational meta-strategy
\par
for humanitarian emergencies response. It emanated from the 2005 HRR,
\par
which identified gaps in and offered far-reaching recommendations for
\par
humanitarian coordination, preparedness and response to both natural
\par
disasters and complex emergencies. The review was thorough and
\par
comprehensive. We do not attempt to cover all areas of it in this chapter,
\par
but rather focus on areas of the review that tackled issues most closely
