\par
related to HL. This overview also helps inform our analysis in the remaining
\par
sections of the chapter.
\par
In the broadest terms, the HRR found that efforts designed to ensure
\par
logistical capabilities have been inconsistent and were closely linked to
\par
deficiencies in organizational coordination and capacity (OCHA, 2005).
\par
These factors, in turn, affected preparedness and response, both constituting
\par
vital dimensions of the disaster management cycle (Tomasini and Van
\par
Wassenhove, 2009). For example, although radio and telecommunications
\par
capabilities varied widely across organizations, most humanitarian agencies
\par
either did not know how to use their telecom technology or had difficulties
\par
accessing them when they were most needed. This was especially true in
\par
fluid response situations and/or where military presence on the ground
\par
complicated obtaining technology-use clearance. Moreover, efforts designed
\par
to ensure adequate stockpiling of emergency relief supplies were inconsistent
\par
and performance was almost always linked to organizational capacity.
\par
Smaller humanitarian organizations, for example, retained limited stockpiles
\par
designed for short-term use. Larger organizations, by contrast, stockpiled a
\par
wider range of goods, using them across different emergency phases.
\par
The HRR also revealed that organizational reliance on individual
\par
solutions rather than coordinated, collective action constituted a major
\par
constraint to improved logistics and the supply chain across the humanitarian
\par
spectrum. For example, very few INGOs indicated that they utilized
\par
common sources of humanitarian supplies (eg through the UNJLC), whereas
\par
nearly all UN agencies made some use of pooled resources (OCHA, 2005).
\par
In addition, the review noted that procurement processes among agencies
\par
were carried out independent of one another; 80 per cent of the organizations
\par
surveyed indicated that they did not utilize any coordinated procurement
\par
policy (OCHA, 2005). Some 75 per cent of humanitarian spending is
\par
handled by fewer than 15 large transnational organizations (Feinstein
\par
International Famine Center, 2004). From a logistics standpoint, the
\par
purchasing power of this cadre should not be overlooked. Coordination
\par
among these mega-INGOs could dramatically lower costs of procurement
\par
and warehousing, and result in more efficient outlay of resources that does
\par
not compromise flexibility and speed in response. It could also render
\par
investment in technology much more cost-efficient and financially feasible,
\par
reduce information processing costs and increase the value of shared
\par
information and data among humanitarian actors at all levels.
\par
The CA was thus born out of a widely-recognized need for building
\par
systemic capacity for coordinated humanitarian response. The HRR
\par
recommended developing clusters constituted by organizations and other
\par
stakeholders with a designated lead agency, in gap areas such as service
\par
provision (emergency telecommunications, logistics); traditional relief and
\par
assistance sectors (water/sanitation, nutrition, health, emergency shelter);
\par
and cross-cutting issues (camp coordination/management, early recovery,
\par
protection).
\par
Key HRR recommendations concerning humanitarian logistics included:
