Enthusiasm for the development of the CA was not shared by all major
\par
humanitarian donors. The US, although an interested party, was not a
\par
driver of the process. The UK and several northern European countries were
\par
among the most active and supportive actors in the process. According to at
\par
least one professional closely involved in the CA's establishment,2 the US
\par
role was in keeping with its prevailing scepticism regarding whether
\par
centralized UN coordination constitutes the `silver bullet' to achieve
\par
meaningful reform or that coordination is the single most important factor
\par
to improve humanitarian response effectiveness. As the world's largest
\par
provider of foreign assistance and emergency relief, the US may also have
\par
been sanguine about committing to new arrangements that might, in turn,
\par
necessitate the commitment of new resources. After the IASC agreed on the
\par
CA, the US was an active participant in the Geneva-based donor group that
\par
worked with the IASC agencies and clusters in funding the CA's initial
\par
implementation.
\par
Among the humanitarian donors polled by HRR, however, more than
\par
two-thirds cited coordination to be among the top three most important
\par
elements for improving global humanitarian performance. Sound needs
\par
assessment; effective management; greater accountability; and reducing the
\par
number of actors operating in humanitarian settings were also ranked as
\par
high-priority reform issues. Interestingly, donors ranked higher levels of
\par
preparedness and faster reaction as being less important for improving
\par
contemporary humanitarian response (OCHA, 2005).
\par
Most scholars and practitioners agree that the CA represents a major
\par
change in the way humanitarian assistance is implemented at both the
\par
global, regional and national levels and creates complementarities among
\par
the actors involved. The CA is innovative because it generates clear lines of
\par
accountability and reporting; addresses capacity-building through
\par
unconventional approaches; and should lead to partnerships for common
\par
planning and implementation of humanitarian relief (OCHA, 2006a; Jury
\par
and DeMaio, 2007).
\par
Global clusters were developed in 11 sectors -- in the `service provision'
\par
area, Logistics is chaired by the WFP, and Emergency Telecommunications
