\par
The field-level rollout of the CA was not without challenges. First
\par
implemented in 2006 in the Democratic Republic of Congo (DRC), an
\par
interim review revealed high levels of confusion among humanitarian
\par
actors regarding the roles each agency should play. Many of the concepts
\par
embedded within the CA were not well understood by implementing
\par
agencies or INGOs (eg organizational and operational concepts such as
\par
provider of last resort, accountability, and cluster versus sector leads). The
\par
review also noted that, in spite of OCHA efforts there was a general lack
\par
of understanding among field staff regarding what cluster leads were
\par
expected to do and how they would carry out their mandates (OCHA,
\par
2006b). In Chad, the CA received initial praise and appeared to have
\par
enhanced stakeholder buy-in as participating organizations were required
\par
to affiliate with a cluster, take part in overall strategy design for that
\par
cluster, and then implement the agreed-upon cluster strategy. According
\par
to some INGOs on the ground (eg CARE), this approach also enhanced
\par
cooperation between technical coordination within and across clusters by
\par
identifying which humanitarian organizations were best-equipped to
\par
provide particular services and reaching consensus on adapting
\par
international standards for implementation (IRIN, 2008).
\par
The two most recent evaluations of the CA yield further insights into its
\par
impact of HL and supply network management on the effectiveness of
\par
humanitarian response.3 The first review, finalized in 2007, focused on CA
\par
processes at the global level. The second evaluation concentrates on the
\par
results and effects of the CA at the country and field level. The combined
\par
findings of both undertakings reveal the CA as a maturing institutional
\par
arrangement that enjoys continued strong commitment from important
\par
stakeholders. Its roll-out has not been without difficulties, but with
\par
continued and careful monitoring and analysis, the CA is well-positioned to
\par
facilitate higher levels of humanitarian response effectiveness in the medium-
\par
and long-term.
\par
It should be noted that all clusters are being held to achieving a single
\par
overarching standard, with other operational standards flowing from it.
\par
The IASC Working Group baseline capacity parameters and preparedness
\par
standards are that all clusters should be able to respond to three major new
\par
emergencies per year, affecting 500,000 beneficiaries each, with two of the
\par
annual emergencies occurring simultaneously. At the time of writing, only
\par
one of the 11 clusters has met this baseline as of 2007 -- the Logistics
\par
Cluster.
