\par
In terms of operational standards, the 2007 review showed that early
\par
challenges to the CA, which included confusion among cluster leads and
\par
implementing partners, some `lingering ill-will', and uneven performance
\par
across case settings, did not overshadow what was an overall `systematic
\par
improvement in coordinated humanitarian response'. Clusters in the field
\par
appeared to produce many improvements on their own, as the global
\par
clusters worked to complete capacity projects in the initial phase of the
\par
rollout (Stoddard et al, 2007: 1). From the standpoint of disaster cycle
\par
management, the LC (along with the Emergency Telecommunications
\par
Cluster) has excelled in the area of coordination, including sharing
\par
material stockpile lists, personnel rosters and deployable staff lists. Where
\par
individual clusters underperformed, the attributable cause was nearly
\par
always a lack of leadership -- either by the cluster lead organization or by
\par
coordination actors. `It goes without saying that agencies with the best
\par
funding, the best-quality and largest number of staff, the best in-depth
\par
field presence and the best NGO network are also the best cluster leads'
\par
(Stoddard et al, 2007: 9).
\par
One important finding of the 2010 review is that the institutional learning
\par
has continued to improve within all clusters, including the LC. In addition,
\par
the report found that leadership has become more `predictable' and
\par
leadership roles are now better clarified. Overall coordination levels are also
\par
higher among cluster organizations, and this provides focal points for other
\par
actors on the ground (local and governmental actors, including local
\par
NGOs). The 2010 review also noted that clusters are engaging in peer
\par
accountability, sharing information, developing lessons-learned exercises
\par
among clusters and dialoguing with one another on strategic, operational
\par
and technical issues (Streets et al, 2010). A lingering challenge however,
\par
involves inter-cluster coordination, which remains weak and ineffective. As
\par
was the case in 2007, leadership by cluster coordinators still has a direct
\par
impact on cluster effectiveness.
\par
All of the reviews and critiques of the CA convey the message that it is
\par
an evolving and innovative concept and it is not business as usual for the
\par
humanitarian sector any more. For example, WFP continues to strive to
\par
improve the operations of the LC. WFP enlists field experts specializing in
\par
the appropriate use of MCDAs and to manage their implementation in
\par
humanitarian response. It maintains established procedures for managing
\par
and mitigating airfield congestion by entering into agreements with local
\par
authorities and experts to identify strategic air hubs and negotiate with
\par
local authorities. And, WFP continues to sustain supply chain management
\par
capacity via the LC web interface (http://www.logcluster.org/), which
\par
consolidates resources on every crisis setting where the LC has been
\par
activated. The site provides up-to-the minute information on stockpiles,
\par
road conditions, cargo tracking, customs information, offers logistics
\par
capacity assessments, information management kits, accurate maps and
\par
logistics operational guides. It is, literally, a one-stop shop for
\par
humanitarian logistics.
