\par
US perspectives on humanitarian logistics,
\par
the CA and the LC
\par
Humanitarian assistance and relief have long constituted key foreign
\par
assistance undertakings of the US government, USAID, its Office of Foreign
\par
Disaster Assistance (OFDA) and the Federal Emergency Management
\par
Agency (FEMA). This does not mean that the US and international-level
\par
strategies have naturally aligned with one another, but in the area of logistics
\par
there are several trends worth noting, particularly where they align with the
\par
CA and the LC.
\par
First, and in spite of the CA being a `centralized' institutional arrangement,
\par
both it and US disaster-response reforms are guided by the principle of
\par
subsidiarity in relation to strategic thinking and operational response
\par
parameters. Subsidiarity focuses operational authority and implementation
\par
at the most effective level -- in the field of emergency management and
\par
response, this means favouring smaller, decentralized approaches to mitigate
\par
crises. In the US governance framework, local governments are considered
\par
to be first responders, with state government intervening when local
\par
capacities are overwhelmed or when more `complex' solutions need to be
\par
carried out by higher level authorities. Much like the CA, US federal
\par
authorities assist in emergency response by providing critical and emergency
\par
funding, access to resources, and orchestrating coordination during the
\par
response phase. Moreover, all CA rollouts are carefully tailored to meet the
\par
needs of specific crises. Not all clusters are activated where national capacity
\par
in a particular sector is deemed to be sufficient, and even within sectors (eg
\par
logistics) there is a preference for utilizing national-level resources wherever
\par
possible.4 And, in the CA, cluster leads provide last resort, filling gaps where
\par
other organizations within a particular cluster are unable to do so.
\par
Second, organizational alignment exists between the CA and the US
\par
Disaster Assistance framework (see Table 5.1). Like the LC, USAID/OFDA's
\par
Logistics Sector focuses on preparedness, coordination and capacity-
\par
enhancement. It pre-positions and stocks relief items at regional hubs
\par
around the world, including Florida, Italy and the United Arab Emirates.
\par
Like WFP's Augmented Logistics Intervention Team for Emergencies, the
\par
Sector also fields a full-time logistics team that can be augmented with
\par
additional US-based logisticians from other agencies who specialize in areas
\par
such as search and rescue. Coordination is also key to the Logistics Sector's
\par
work. Logistics experts at USAID/OFDA headquarters coordinate relief
\par
supply transportation and delivery (USAID, 2010), much like the LC lead
\par
agency, WFP, coordinates relief supply delivery in tandem with the UN
\par
Humanitarian Air Service, its own ocean transportation and surface
\par
transport services, and the UNJLC.
\par
Third and perhaps most importantly, at both the international and
\par
national levels, logistics appears to be enjoying a renewed importance as a
\par
key element in effective disaster response. In 2007, for example, FEMA
