\par
budgetary funding for the LC (the US typically is among the first donors
\par
approached for these needs). It favours a more sustainable arrangement
\par
involving the funding mainstreaming within the core operating budgets of
\par
the cluster lead, or through some share of country operations that the US
\par
presently supports.6
\par
Conclusion
\par
The intention in the adoption of the CA as part of the global humanitarian
\par
reform was to strengthen predictability, response capacity, coordination
\par
and accountability in emergency and disaster relief by strengthening
\par
partnerships in key sectors of humanitarian response, and by formalizing
\par
the lead role of particular agencies/organizations in each of these sectors
\par
(Stoddard et al, 2007). As of late 2009, the CA had been rolled out in 36
\par
countries -- and OCHA's goal is to extend the roll-out to every country that
\par
has a Humanitarian Coordinator.7 At the international level, the CA can
\par
help ensure predictable leadership and accountability, as well as
\par
strengthening system-wide preparedness and coordination of the technical
\par
capacity to respond to humanitarian emergencies. At the country level, it
\par
creates high standards of predictability, accountability and partnership in
\par
all areas of humanitarian response (Jury and DeMaio, 2007). There are, on
\par
the other hand, at least two issues that remain unaddressed.
\par
It should be noted that not all humanitarian actors found the establishing
\par
of the CA, including the LC, to be praiseworthy. Among the most common
\par
complaints registered was that the CA process has been top-down and
\par
UN-driven (IRIN, 2008). Others expressed the view that the CA appeared
\par
to be more about money and politics rather than creating positive outcomes
\par
for aid beneficiaries. From a bureaucratic standpoint, some emphasized that
\par
one of the CA's most serious drawbacks was that it increased humanitarian
\par
agency workload by creating new layers of administration and consultation
\par
without providing additional resources to perform these added
\par
responsibilities.
\par
Many humanitarian actors outside the UN system feel that the current
\par
structures related to humanitarian response are very much `UN driven' and
\par
do not always meet INGO expectations (OCHA, 2005: 46). Here, the lead
\par
organization concept was encouraged to be implemented system-wide -- to
\par
increase efficiency by encouraging effective use of expertise and technical
\par
know-how of a variety of organizations and especially a designated lead
\par
organization (OCHA, 2005).
\par
The US has been a bit less enamoured of the CA stemming from its
\par
general scepticism of centralized UN coordination as a solution to global
\par
humanitarian problems. The US supports the coordination agenda of the
\par
UN -- its position on a variety of coordination mechanisms (not all) has been
\par
positive. It remains less convinced that centralized coordination is the silver
