@article{barkerCanCompanyfundManager2012,
  title = {Can Company-Fund Manager Meetings Convey Informational Benefits? {{Exploring}} the Rationalisation of Equity Investment Decision Making by {{UK}} Fund Managers},
  shorttitle = {Can Company-Fund Manager Meetings Convey Informational Benefits?},
  author = {Barker, Richard and Hendry, John and Roberts, John and Sanderson, Paul},
  year = 2012,
  month = may,
  journal = {Accounting, Organizations and Society},
  volume = {37},
  number = {4},
  pages = {207--222},
  issn = {03613682},
  doi = {10.1016/j.aos.2012.02.004},
  urldate = {2023-07-24},
  abstract = {Conventional economic theory, applied to information released by listed companies, equates `useful' with `price-sensitive'. Stock exchange rules accordingly prohibit the selective, private communication of price-sensitive information. Yet, even in the absence of such communication, UK equity fund managers routinely meet privately with the senior executives of the companies in which they invest. Moreover, they consider these brief, formal and formulaic meetings to be their most important sources of investment information. In this paper we ask how that can be. Drawing on interview and observation data with fund managers and CFOs, we find evidence for three, non-mutually exclusive explanations: that the characterisation of information in conventional economic theory is too restricted, that fund managers fail to act with the rationality that conventional economic theory assumes, and/or that the primary value of the meetings for fund managers is not related to their investment decision making but to the claims of superior knowledge made to clients in marketing their active fund management expertise. Our findings suggest a disconnect between economic theory and economic policy based on that theory, as well as a corresponding limitation in research studies that test information-usefulness by assuming it to be synonymous with price-sensitivity. We draw implications for further research into the role of tacit knowledge in equity investment decision-making, and also into the effects of the principal--agent relationship between fund managers and their clients.},
  langid = {english},
  note = {Accounting, Organizations and Society 37 (2012) 207--222
\par
Contents lists available at SciVerse ScienceDirect
\par
Accounting, Organizations and Society
\par
j o u r n a l h o m e p a g e : w w w . e l s e v i e r . c o m / l o c a t e / a o s
\par
Can company-fund manager meetings convey informational benefits?
\par
Exploring the rationalisation of equity investment decision making
\par
by UK fund managers
\par
Richard Barker a,{$\Uparrow$}, John Hendry b, John Robertsc, Paul Sanderson a
\par
a University of Cambridge, United Kingdom
\par
b University of Reading, United Kingdom
\par
c University of Sydney, Australia
\par
a r t i c l e i n f o a b s t r a c t
\par
Conventional economic theory, applied to information released by listed companies,
\par
equates `useful' with `price-sensitive'. Stock exchange rules accordingly prohibit the selec-
\par
tive, private communication of price-sensitive information. Yet, even in the absence of such
\par
communication, UK equity fund managers routinely meet privately with the senior execu-
\par
tives of the companies in which they invest. Moreover, they consider these brief, formal
\par
and formulaic meetings to be their most important sources of investment information.
\par
In this paper we ask how that can be. Drawing on interview and observation data with fund
\par
managers and CFOs, we find evidence for three, non-mutually exclusive explanations: that
\par
the characterisation of information in conventional economic theory is too restricted, that
\par
fund managers fail to act with the rationality that conventional economic theory assumes,
\par
and/or that the primary value of the meetings for fund managers is not related to their
\par
investment decision making but to the claims of superior knowledge made to clients in
\par
marketing their active fund management expertise. Our findings suggest a disconnect
\par
between economic theory and economic policy based on that theory, as well as a corre-
\par
sponding limitation in research studies that test information-usefulness by assuming it
\par
to be synonymous with price-sensitivity. We draw implications for further research into
\par
the role of tacit knowledge in equity investment decision-making, and also into the effects
\par
of the principal--agent relationship between fund managers and their clients.
\par
\'O 2012 Elsevier Ltd. All rights reserved.
\par
Introduction
\par
Fund managers are the primary investment decision
\par
makers in the stock market and so play a central role in
\par
the allocation of economic resources. In making their invest-
\par
ment decisions they rely heavily on published information,
\par
aspects of which may be clarified by a company's investor
\par
relations team. In the UK (and in somewhat different forms
\par
in other markets) they also meet regularly, in private, with
\par
senior executives of the companies in which they invest
\par
(Reuters, 2011). Market regulation prohibits the disclosure
\par
of price-sensitive information in these private meetings
\par
(FSA, 1996). Yet conventional economic theory suggests that
\par
information is not useful to investors if it is not price-sensi-
\par
tive (Fama, 1970; Kothari, 2001). Accordingly, at least when
\par
viewed through the lens of conventional economic theory, it
\par
is difficult to understand what information such meetings
\par
provide which might help fund managers in their invest-
\par
ment decisions. Yet, in the prior research that we review be-
\par
low (and that our own research supports), these meetings
\par
are perceived by fund managers not only as important but
\par
as the primary source of information to inform their
\par
{$\Uparrow$} Corresponding author. Address: Judge Business School, University of
\par
Cambridge, Cambridge CB2 1AG, United Kingdom.
\par
E-mail address: r.barker@jbs.cam.ac.uk (R. Barker).
\par
0361-3682/\$ - see front matter \'O 2012 Elsevier Ltd. All rights reserved.
\par
doi:10.1016/j.aos.2012.02.004208 R. Barker et al. / Accounting, Organizations and Society 37 (2012) 207--222
\par
investment decisions (Barker, 1998; Holland, 1998; Lok,
\par
2010; Reuters, 2011). What is happening here?
\par
This apparent paradox is economically important.
\par
Conventional economic theory appears to suggest that
\par
the considerable investment of time by senior company
\par
managers and fund managers in meeting one another does
\par
not yield any return. More fundamentally, the importance
\par
ascribed to such meetings invites us to refine our under-
\par
standing of the rationality and effectiveness of capital
\par
resource allocation decisions on stock markets.
\par
In what follows, drawing upon interviews with fund
\par
managers and CFOs, supported by direct observations of
\par
the meetings between them, we explore three possible,
\par
and non-mutually-exclusive, (theoretical) explanations
\par
for the apparent paradox.
\par
The first possibility is that, by constraining `useful' to
\par
mean `price-sensitive', conventional (neoclassical) eco-
\par
nomic theory is too narrow with respect to useful informa-
\par
tion. Our research points to two reasons to conceptualise
\par
useful information more broadly. The first concerns the ta-
\par
cit nature of much knowledge, and the associated need for
\par
rich face to face interaction through which information can
\par
be effectively communicated and interpreted. The second
\par
concerns the narrow practical scope of the concept of
\par
price-sensitive information, which fails to take account of
\par
uncertainty and the related value of subjective evaluations
\par
of management capability. The implication here is that the
\par
actual investment decision making process of fund manag-
\par
ers differs from that assumed by neoclassical theory. Our
\par
qualitative research suggests that both fund managers
\par
and CFOs distinguish between short-term corporate per-
\par
formance horizons of about 2 years, where quantitative
\par
forecasts are relatively firm, information is tightly con-
\par
trolled, and the scope for anticipating market movements
\par
is very limited, and longer term horizons, where perfor-
\par
mance will depend on management decisions that have
\par
yet to be taken, in response to situations as yet unknown,
\par
in respect of which information is not formally price-sensi-
\par
tive. For fund managers, privileged access to corporate
\par
management allows them to judge whether company per-
\par
formance over this longer period is likely to differ from
\par
current market expectations.
\par
The second possibility is that the apparent paradox
\par
arises because fund managers place an excessive confi-
\par
dence and importance in the information gleaned from
\par
company meetings; the paradox arises here from a failure
\par
of fund managers to act with the rationality that economic
\par
theory assumes. Notwithstanding the arguments above for
\par
the usefulness of non-price-sensitive information, and
\par
thereby the possible credibility of fund managers' claims
\par
to derive informational value, an obvious question is
\par
whether the short, infrequent, formal and (typically) for-
\par
mulaic meetings we observed can in fact provide a rational
\par
basis for such (necessarily subjective) judgements of man-
\par
agement capability. It was clear from our interviews that
\par
fund managers think, or at least hope, they can. Yet we
\par
suggest several reasons to question whether the fund man-
\par
agers actually are able to adopt the trading strategies they
\par
describe, and whether the access to senior managers pro-
\par
vided by the meetings can in fact give them the competi-
\par
tive advantage they claim.
\par
Finally, the third possible explanation is that the pri-
\par
mary value of the meetings for fund managers is not re-
\par
lated directly to their investment decision making but
\par
rather to the claims of superior knowledge that they can
\par
make to clients in marketing their active fund manage-
\par
ment expertise. We argue that while it is difficult in prac-
\par
tice to distinguish between subconscious, irrational bias,
\par
and conscious, rational misrepresentation, prima facie
\par
there is an agency problem in the fund manager--client
\par
relationship. Active fund managers' claims to be able to
\par
beat the index on the basis of their superior knowledge al-
\par
low them to charge clients, such as the trustees of pension
\par
and endowment funds, a premium over passive index-
\par
based fund management.
\par
The paper contributes to the literature in the following
\par
ways. First, we challenge the assumption, held in conven-
\par
tional economic theory and common in market-based
\par
accounting research, that `useful' and `price-sensitive' are
\par
synonymous. Our findings suggest that `useful' is a broader
\par
concept, the understanding of which requires a greater fo-
\par
cus on the role of tacit knowledge in investment decision
\par
making, especially with respect to relatively uncertain
\par
information relating to periods beyond the short term. Sec-
\par
ond, our evidence concerns not just the actual and per-
\par
ceived usefulness of information from company meetings,
\par
but also the claims made by fund managers to their clients
\par
with respect to this usefulness. We therefore also identify a
\par
potentially important principal--agent problem. Overall,
\par
these contributions to the literature can be summarised
\par
as follows. Conventional economic theory does not support
\par
the existence of the meetings that are the focus of this pa-
\par
per. The importance of the meetings therefore suggests, in
\par
a classical hypothetico-deductive sense, that the theory
\par
must be rejected in this context. In its place, we offer three
\par
alternative theories. We find evidence in support of each,
\par
and none is rejected. We therefore propose further re-
\par
search to test the validity and implications of these
\par
theories.
\par
Our paper also has a potentially important policy impli-
\par
cation, relating to the selective disclosure of information
\par
that is useful but not price-sensitive. This concerns a pos-
\par
sible trade-off that would result from a greater restriction
\par
on the selective disclosure of information, for example in
\par
the form of a prohibition of private meetings between
\par
companies and fund managers. This trade-off would be be-
\par
tween, on the one hand, greater equity from the restriction
\par
of privileged access to information and, on the other hand,
\par
lower efficiency from constraining the flow of information
\par
and, as a result, compromising the stock market's alloca-
\par
tion of economic resource. Further insight into this policy
\par
implication would result from more evidence relating to
\par
each of the three theoretical possibilities identified and ex-
\par
plored in our paper: specifically, the questions of whether
\par
the informational benefits of company meetings are genu-
\par
ine, falsely perceived, or misleadingly claimed. The market
\par
efficiency argument rests upon evidence in support of the
\par
first of these three possibilities, and it would lose force in
\par
the face of evidence supporting the second or third.
\par
The paper is structured as follows. The next section
\par
comprises an exposition of the theoretical foundations of
\par
the paper. This is followed by a review of prior researchR. Barker et al. / Accounting, Organizations and Society 37 (2012) 207--222 209
\par
and a description of our own research design. We then
\par
provide evidence supporting the existence of the apparent
\par
paradox that we seek to explain and then, in each subse-
\par
quent section, findings and analysis relating to each of
\par
our three possible explanations for the apparent paradox.
\par
The final section of the paper presents conclusions and
\par
draws out implications for future research.
\par
Theoretical foundations
\par
Price-sensitive information is defined in the London
\par
Stock Exchange's Listing Rules as information that may,
\par
or would be likely to, lead to a substantial movement in
\par
the price of a company's listed securities (Financial Ser-
\par
vices Authority (FSA), 1996). These rules place continuing
\par
obligations on listed companies to announce unpublished
\par
price-sensitive information without delay via a mechanism
\par
that disseminates to the largest number of market partici-
\par
pants. The policy concern here is for equity in the form of
\par
disallowing unequal gains resulting from private access
\par
to readily monetisable information (Argouleas, 2005; King
\par
\& Roell, 1988). There is an implicitly accepted trade-off
\par
with the pricing efficiency that would result from trading
\par
on privately-held information (Grossman, 1976; Manne,
\par
1966; Meulbroek, 1992).
\par
Underlying the FSA's definition of price-sensitive is the
\par
implicit assumption that `price-sensitive' and `useful' are
\par
synonymous. This assumption also underpins the event
\par
study methodology that tests the `value-relevance' of
\par
incremental financial disclosures (Jagolinzer, Larcker, \&
\par
Taylor, 2011; Kothari, 2001; Landsman \& Maydew, 2002;
\par
Landsman, Maydew, \& Thornock, 2011). The (assumed)
\par
synonymity of price-sensitive and useful arises from the
\par
marginalist foundations of neoclassical economic theory
\par
(Aspromourgos, 2008), whereby only information that is
\par
incremental to that already impounded in share prices
\par
can be used in rewarding the incremental costs of informa-
\par
tion acquisition and analysis (Goshen, 2003). According to
\par
this theory, equity investors act on strictly rational pecuni-
\par
ary preferences, respond strictly rationally to all new infor-
\par
mation (which is assumed to be shared) and base their
\par
decisions upon calculation of the expected value of total
\par
returns, as predicted by fully determined probabilistic dis-
\par
tributions (Muth, 1961; Fischer, 1980; Lucas, 1987). A con-
\par
sequence is that because efficient capital markets can be
\par
expected, by definition, to impound price-sensitive infor-
\par
mation, private economic gain results from a timing
\par
advantage in gaining such information before it enters
\par
the public domain (Fama, 1970).
\par
According to neoclassical economic theory, therefore,
\par
private company meetings cannot be useful if they do
\par
not release price-sensitive information. Our research ques-
\par
tion is therefore stimulated by an apparent paradox: if
\par
these meetings do not convey price-sensitive information,
\par
why are they considered by fund managers to be their
\par
most important source of investment information (Barker,
\par
1998; Holland, 1998; Lok, 2010; Reuters, 2011)?
\par
In this section of the paper, we identify and explore
\par
three possible explanations for this apparent paradox.
\par
These possible explanations are not mutually exclusive.
\par
We consider first the possibility that the apparent par-
\par
adox arises because neoclassical economic theory is too re-
\par
stricted in its description of useful information. Doubts
\par
have long been raised in the literature with respect to
\par
two aspects of the assumed nature of information in this
\par
theory: whether it can plausibly be assumed, first, that
\par
data are `given' and shared, and, second, that the expected
\par
value of total returns is a fully determined probabilistic
\par
distribution. On the first of these issues, Hayek (1945) crit-
\par
icised the assumption that all investors share the same
\par
knowledge and forecasts, noting that heterogeneity is not
\par
only intrinsic to the operation of markets (since investors
\par
must differ in their valuations if they are to trade) but also,
\par
in the case of equities markets, essential to its effectiveness
\par
as an institution of resource allocation. Similarly, Gross-
\par
man and Stiglitz (1980) noted the paradox that the absence
\par
of abnormal returns in an informationally efficient stock
\par
market denies the economic rationale for the existence of
\par
informational intermediaries, such as analysts and fund
\par
managers. Our attention is thereby drawn to the mecha-
\par
nisms of market efficiency. At a minimum, a theory with
\par
conclusions based upon equilibrium conditions is surely
\par
incomplete in the absence of an explanation for how those
\par
conditions can be assumed to come about (Hayek, 1937). It
\par
is possible that our paradox holds under equilibrium, but
\par
not under the mechanisms of information transfer, pro-
\par
cessing and usage by which equilibrium is reached, and it
\par
is these mechanisms that are the focus for our fieldwork.
\par
In particular, the possibility may exist that some informa-
\par
tion is useful yet not formally price-sensitive, meaning that
\par
it can be communicated privately.
\par
On the issue of whether the expected value of total re-
\par
turns can be assumed to be a fully determined probabilistic
\par
distribution, there is a long and rich literature. Nearly a
\par
century ago Knight (1921) drew attention to the distinc-
\par
tion between the `risk' associated with a probabilistically
\par
calculable range of outcomes, and the `uncertainty', nowa-
\par
days referred to as `radical uncertainty', associated with
\par
the incalculable (since essentially unknowable), and sug-
\par
gested that in the context of business investments, deci-
\par
sion-makers were generally faced with the latter. Keynes
\par
(1936) concurred, noting that one consequence was that
\par
the aim of investors becomes to predict what the markets
\par
will predict that the markets will predict, rather than to
\par
determine intrinsic value. What is perceived to be `known'
\par
is actually based upon inter-subjective determinations of
\par
probability, or `conventions' (Gillies, 2006). These are not
\par
(indeed they cannot be) objective truths; they can instead
\par
be interpreted as (inter-subjectively shared) constructions
\par
of reality (Berger \& Luckmann, 1966). Consistent with this
\par
interpretation, Shackle (1955), Shackle (1961) challenged
\par
the application of the repeated-game logic of probability
\par
theory to investment decisions, noting that each decision
\par
is a one-off, with the conditions prevailing at the time of
\par
the decision gone forever. Shackle's approach has not been
\par
formally developed, but something of it is captured in re-
\par
cent developments in behavioural finance (Barberis \&
\par
Huang, 2001; Shleifer, 2000) and imperfect knowledge
\par
economics (Frydman \& Goldberg, 2007; Frydman \& Gold-
\par
berg, 2008). Both of these draw heavily on prospect theory
\par
(Kahneman \& Tversky, 1979; Tversky \& Kahneman, 1992),210 R. Barker et al. / Accounting, Organizations and Society 37 (2012) 207--222
\par
replacing total expected returns with asymmetric utility
\par
measures based on trading gains and losses. Imperfect
\par
knowledge economics also breaks with the conventional
\par
assumption that agents share the same fully prescribed
\par
probabilistic forecasts, imposing a much weaker constraint
\par
of theories-consistent expectations. The implication here is
\par
that information might be useful for reasons not captured
\par
by conventional theory, because its use arises in the con-
\par
text of the uncertainty that conventional theory assumes
\par
away.
\par
Our second explanation for the apparent paradox is that
\par
fund managers place irrational confidence in company
\par
meetings as a source of information (such that the appro-
\par
priate theoretical explanation does not lie in the capacity
\par
of neoclassical economics to explain rational economic
\par
behaviour). One way of supporting this line of reasoning,
\par
and the way favoured by contemporary economic theories,
\par
is to argue that fund managers act on the basis of irrational
\par
bias. For example, in the behavioural finance literature,
\par
excessive confidence in private information (Alpert \& Raif-
\par
fa, 1982: Langer, 1975) is used to explain irrational stock
\par
trading in response to new information (Barberis \& Thaler,
\par
2002), and this could be argued to underlie fund managers'
\par
perceptions of the informational usefulness of company
\par
meetings.
\par
Our third, and final, explanation is that the apparent
\par
paradox arises because the meetings do not primarily
\par
serve an investment purpose. An implicit assumption in
\par
neoclassical theory is that of rational economic usage,
\par
whereby information that is useful (i.e. price-sensitive) is
\par
monetised by means of investment decision-making. The
\par
possibility here, however, is that fund managers may have
\par
a different, yet still rational, economic incentive in their
\par
meetings with companies. This possibility arises because
\par
of the principal agent relationship between fund managers
\par
and their clients (Ross, 1973). While the economic incen-
\par
tives of fund managers' clients are captured by investment
\par
gains (and so by neoclassical economic theory), these differ
\par
from the economic incentives of fund managers them-
\par
selves, which derive from the value of funds under man-
\par
agement and not from investment performance per se.
\par
This difference in incentives can be expected to create
\par
moral hazard to the extent that there is an information
\par
asymmetry between fund managers and their clients, and
\par
this in turn raises the possibility that company meetings
\par
form the basis for this information asymmetry, either in
\par
reality or in perception. The fund managers' different, yet
\par
still rational, economic incentive therefore lies in the way
\par
in which the meetings allow them to make claims to their
\par
clients in relation to their informational value.
\par
Taken together, our three possible explanations can be
\par
viewed as an exhaustive set. Given that conventional the-
\par
ory concerns the nature of information and of economic
\par
agents' rational use of that information, then either: first,
\par
the characterisation of information in neoclassical theory
\par
is too restricted, or; second, the conventional assumption
\par
of rational economic use of information is inappropriate,
\par
or; third, our theoretical explanation of why information
\par
is valuable is confounded by there being a different rela-
\par
tional purpose for the meetings, implying a need for addi-
\par
tional theorising beyond that concerning the relationship
\par
between useful information and investment decision-
\par
making.
\par
This paper provides evidence relating to each of our
\par
three theoretical possibilities. In the next section, we first
\par
review prior research and set out our research design and
\par
method.
\par
Prior research and research design
\par
Research into information flows between companies
\par
and investors has focused largely on the quantitative ef-
\par
fects of public disclosures (e.g. Bushee, Jung, \& Miller,
\par
2011; Han \& Tan, 2010), with relatively few studies into
\par
corporate/fund manager meetings which, while in the pre-
\par
cise form described here are unique to the UK, have vari-
\par
ants in many jurisdictions. These few studies have been
\par
consistent, however, in finding that the meetings were
\par
seen by investors as their most important source of infor-
\par
mation, especially on long-term strategy and prospects,
\par
and executive capabilities and orientation (Barker, 1998;
\par
Bence, Hapeshi, \& Hussey, 1995; Gaved, 1997; Holland
\par
1998; Holland \& Doran, 1998; Lok, 2010; Marston, 2008),
\par
and in this way serve as a vital source of competitive
\par
knowledge advantage (Holland \& Doran, 1998). They were
\par
also valued by companies seeking investor confidence and
\par
a reputation for openness (Armitage \& Marston, 2008; Hol-
\par
land, 2005). These studies also find consistently, however,
\par
that no private or price-sensitive information is perceived
\par
to change hands, which raises the question of how the
\par
added value and competitive advantage reported by fund
\par
managers might arise. Roberts, Sanderson, Barker, and
\par
Hendry (2006) have suggested, and Lok (2010) has rein-
\par
forced, that the meetings serve fund managers' general
\par
interests by ritually subjecting corporate executives to
\par
the demand for `shareholder value', which becomes inter-
\par
nalised by executives and then imposed on the company
\par
at large. But this does not in itself explain why fund man-
\par
agers find them so important, especially as related research
\par
has found that the fund managers behave consistently (and
\par
are seen by corporate managers as behaving consistently)
\par
as traders, acting in competition with each other, and not
\par
as owners of the company (Hendry, Sanderson, Barker, \&
\par
Roberts, 2006). While there is occasional, ritual reference
\par
by fund managers to due diligence monitoring, it is evident
\par
that their primary interest in the company meetings is to
\par
inform their investment choices rather than to influence
\par
directly the performance of their investee companies.
\par
The present study explores further the significance of
\par
these private meetings for investment decision making
\par
by drawing on two data sources: a series of semi-struc-
\par
tured interviews with representative samples of both par-
\par
ties to the meetings and direct observations of meetings.
\par
While direct observation has the merit of internal validity,
\par
interviews have the distinctive benefit that interviewees
\par
can articulate their views on the aims of the meetings,
\par
including whether and when these are achieved. There is
\par
a degree of triangulation achieved by interviewing both
\par
fund managers and CFOs, and also by observing both `in ac-
\par
tion'. Finally, an unstructured approach, which avoids
\par
overt steering of interviewees or direct influence on meet-
\par
ings, is suitable to an under-researched area, because inR. Barker et al. / Accounting, Organizations and Society 37 (2012) 207--222 211
\par
contrast to a narrower approach of formulating and testing
\par
hypotheses, it enables the emergence of hypotheses that
\par
might not have been apparent in advance.
\par
A first series of interviews was carried out in 2002 with
\par
18 CFOs and investor relations managers from 14 FTSE 100
\par
companies. A second series of interviews was carried out in
\par
2003 with 19 senior managers from 11 asset management
\par
companies.1 Job titles varied from firm to firm but the gen-
\par
eric roles were those of senior fund manager (referenced be-
\par
low as FM), head of UK or European equities research (HoR)
\par
and head of equities, managing director or chief investment
\par
officer (CIO).2 The interviews, which were carried out at the
\par
company's offices, averaged 80 min in length and all but
\par
three were recorded; each was attended by at least two
\par
members of the research team, and where we were not per-
\par
mitted to record, detailed notes were taken by a project re-
\par
searcher. The interviews that we recorded were transcribed
\par
and coded using a two stage process, with questions of
\par
emphasis or meaning checked back against the audio
\par
recordings and handwritten notes. In addition we directly
\par
observed, but did not record, eight meetings hosted by fund
\par
managers with the CEOs and/or CFOs of large investee com-
\par
panies; in five of these we were the guest of an asset man-
\par
agement company and in three of an investee company.
\par
These observations served primarily to contextualise the
\par
interviews and provide a further check on our interpretation
\par
of these.
\par
Given the `private' nature of these meetings, only such
\par
qualitative research methods offered any possibility of
\par
understanding how and why investors attached such
\par
importance to them. Like all interpretative research our
\par
interviews and observations provided the opportunity to
\par
engage with the experiences and understandings of skilled
\par
practitioners -- in this case senior executives, analysts and
\par
fund managers -- as these informed and were themselves
\par
shaped by their interactions with each other (Gid-
\par
dens,1984; Kaplan, 1986). However, in developing our
\par
analysis of this field research in what follows, we want to
\par
explicitly relate these back to the three alternative and the-
\par
oretically derived possibilities for the paradoxical nature of
\par
the meetings outlined in the previous section, as part of a
\par
process of `iteratively seeking to generate a plausible fit be-
\par
tween problem, theory and data' (Ahrens \& Chapman,
\par
2006). The traditional economic, behavioural and agency
\par
theories that ground these alternative explanations were
\par
certainly broadly familiar to us as researchers and were
\par
probably also part of the training of those we interviewed.
\par
As such they possibly served as largely tacit and taken for
\par
granted frames for our sense making. However, used
\par
explicitly each theoretical framework then served as a
\par
point of focus through which to explore emergent themes
\par
from our interview data, whilst the contrasting assump-
\par
tions of each encouraged critique and reflection. At the
\par
same time, the richness of the context specific interview
\par
data itself provided a further lever through which to ex-
\par
plore, elaborate and reflect upon each alternative theoret-
\par
ical possibility and its limitations. Such a `process of
\par
abduction' (Lukka \& Modell, 2010) -- the iterative and re-
\par
peated movement between data and theories -- has not re-
\par
sulted in a single and unambiguous reading of the private
\par
meetings between companies and institutional investors.
\par
However, we believe that the process has allowed us to
\par
tease apart and analyse key aspects of the significance of
\par
these meetings for investment decision making, whilst at
\par
the same time pointing to an open horizon of questions
\par
to be pursued in future research.
\par
In what follows we first present evidence that is consis-
\par
tent with prior literature in terms of the perceived impor-
\par
tance of company meetings, and that therefore supports
\par
their apparently paradoxical nature. In each of the following
\par
sections, we then present findings for each of our three pos-
\par
sible explanations for the apparent paradox. We then syn-
\par
thesise our findings in the concluding section of the paper,
\par
in which we also draw implications for future research.
\par
Evidence of the apparent paradox
\par
Our fund managers were broadly consistent, amongst
\par
themselves and with our CFOs, in their descriptions of
\par
the meetings. They described that these meetings typically
\par
involve the company's CEO and CFO on one side, with an
\par
investor relations manager in attendance, and anything
\par
from two to a dozen fund managers and analysts on the
\par
other side. They are relatively formal affairs, almost always
\par
of one hour duration (a corporate team will typically meet
\par
with six investors in a day, on a tight schedule), comprising
\par
a brief introduction by the company followed by questions
\par
and answers, with the fund management team setting the
\par
agenda.
\par
Our fund managers' accounts of the meetings and their
\par
significance were also consistent with the findings of prior
\par
studies (Barker, 1998; Holland 1998; Marston, 2008). First
\par
and foremost, these meetings were clearly perceived by
\par
the fund managers to be very important. Of the 11 invest-
\par
ment houses researched, eight participated regularly in the
\par
meetings, the others being quantitative or predominantly
\par
index investors. Representatives of the latter group were
\par
sceptical as to what the meetings might contribute, on
\par
which more will be said later, but interviewees from all
\par
eight of the former group explicitly noted their central
\par
importance. As one put it, the meetings were ``the bedrock
\par
of what we do'' [CIO]. There was also agreement that the
\par
primary reason for this importance lay in some kind of
\par
informational advantage. Although there were occasional
\par
references to monitoring and corporate governance, the
\par
dominant theme throughout our interviews concerned
\par
the informational value of the meetings and the competi-
\par
tive edge this conferred on the fund management
\par
participants:
\par
1 There has been no substantial change in market practice since our
\par
fieldwork was conducted, as evidenced for example by comparing the most
\par
recent Thomson Reuters Extel Survey (Reuters, 2011) with the 2003 edition
\par
(Primark, 2003). To the best of our knowledge, the Extel Survey has long
\par
been the most comprehensive source of UK survey data concerning stock
\par
market analysts, fund managers and corporate investor relations'
\par
departments.
\par
2 A potential source of confusion is that the term ``fund managers'' is
\par
conventionally used to refer both to the individuals who make investment
\par
decisions and to the asset management firms that employ them. This usage
\par
is so well established that we have generally followed it, but we have used
\par
more specific terms, such as ``individual fund manager'' or ``asset manage-
\par
ment house'', more briefly ``house'', where the distinction is salient and
\par
undetermined by the context.212 R. Barker et al. / Accounting, Organizations and Society 37 (2012) 207--222
\par
We place a lot of store on meeting the management, a lot,
\par
because they tell you a lot that you don't see written down.
\par
[CIO]
\par
In describing the framework and context for this infor-
\par
mational usefulness, the fund managers were broadly con-
\par
sistent with what would be expected from conventional
\par
theory. In particular, they all processed information
\par
through analytical models, developed by their research
\par
teams, which they used to seek outperformance in a highly
\par
competitive stock market. As one HoR put it, ``we have a re-
\par
search process and framework that every analyst operates un-
\par
der.'' These analytical models might include elements of
\par
the analysts' traditional DCF valuation model, using pub-
\par
lished quantitative data and proprietary forecasts, coupled
\par
with classifications or characterisations (quantitative and
\par
qualitative) of the company and its strategic positioning.
\par
They might give different weights to different valuation
\par
criteria, investment styles or other measures thought to
\par
provide incremental information to the standard DCF valu-
\par
ations they believed were implicit in the current market
\par
price. To take a typical example:
\par
The way our investment process works, is you first figure
\par
out the value drivers. `Is this a good or bad company?'
\par
We are actually fairly explicit in saying good or bad com-
\par
pany is measured along 3 criteria, the ability to return a
\par
profit over your cost of capital, the ability to grow the busi-
\par
ness, and risk surrounding this forecast. [HoR]
\par
The discourse around the use of these models was
\par
explicitly competitive. They were vehicles for marshalling
\par
information in order to achieve superior investment per-
\par
formance. As agents for other people's money, the fund
\par
managers applied their models to the aim of outperform-
\par
ing competitor fund managers, measured in terms of beat-
\par
ing a benchmark index as opposed to maximising overall
\par
returns. Their brief was typically to run the UK equities
\par
component of a client portfolio invested across a range of
\par
asset classes. If, quarter on quarter, their investment strat-
\par
egies resulted in a better-than-index performance, they
\par
had performed well and would be rewarded, as individuals
\par
and teams, through their firms' incentive schemes, even if
\par
the UK stock market as a whole had declined and their cli-
\par
ents had lost money.
\par
All of the above is broadly consistent with conventional,
\par
neoclassical economic theory, in that an informational
\par
advantage is the perceived incremental reward for invest-
\par
ing in the process of meetings with companies. However,
\par
there was also universal agreement that no price-sensitive
\par
information was normally imparted through the meetings.
\par
One interviewee did suggest that coming early in the cycle
\par
of meetings might yield a time advantage, on the basis that
\par
``with this particular message and strategy the shares were
\par
only going one way'' [CIO]. But even if true (and there was
\par
a distinct flavour of braggadocio in this particular account)
\par
this kind of situation was clearly exceptional. The consen-
\par
sus view was that companies handled price-sensitive
\par
information with enormous care and fund managers were
\par
very unlikely to come out of a meeting with information on
\par
which to trade. As one of them put it:
\par
I do learn a lot from meeting with companies but it's not
\par
normally something that triggers a buy or sell decision
\par
because, unless they're making me an insider they
\par
shouldn't be telling me something that isn't already close
\par
to public knowledge. [FM]
\par
Taken together, the evidence presented here supports
\par
the presence of the apparent paradox. The meetings are
\par
perceived to be of central informational importance to
\par
fund managers. This is because they are perceived to in-
\par
form analytical models, the purpose of which is to outper-
\par
form in a highly competitive market. This is consistent
\par
with neoclassical economic theory. Yet the fund managers
\par
do not perceive the meetings to convey price-sensitive
\par
information, which is clearly inconsistent with this theory.
\par
In the next section of the paper, we therefore ask what kind
\par
of informational advantage the meetings can be argued to
\par
confer. We take as our benchmark the synonymity in neo-
\par
classical theory between information which is `price-sensi-
\par
tive' and that which is `useful'. We explore whether this
\par
synonymity can be said to approximate reality or, alterna-
\par
tively, whether the characterisation of information in neo-
\par
classical theory is too restrictive.
\par
Can non-price-sensitive information be useful to fund
\par
managers?
\par
In this section we explore how information that is not
\par
price-sensitive can nevertheless be useful to fund manag-
\par
ers, in contrast with the predictions of neoclassical theory.
\par
We identify several reasons. These concern: the relative
\par
efficiency of information channels; the confirmatory value
\par
of information; the level of confidence with which fund
\par
managers feel able to interpret information: the tacit nat-
\par
ure of knowledge and the process by which it is acquired;
\par
the effect of improved communication through relation-
\par
ship-building; the narrow practical scope, in the face of
\par
uncertainty, of the concept of price-sensitive; and the nat-
\par
ure of fund managers' investment horizons, given the
\par
inherent uncertainty under which their investment deci-
\par
sions are made.
\par
We start with a simple observation from our fieldwork,
\par
which is that the meetings represent an efficient channel
\par
through which fund managers can accumulate and assim-
\par
ilate information. A common theme from the interviews
\par
with fund managers was that they felt overwhelmed by
\par
information:
\par
There is more than enough information out there, far too
\par
much. [CIO]
\par
Everyone is struggling. . . to assimilate the information.
\par
[CIO]
\par
For every company and every sector there was a pleth-
\par
ora of published data and broker analyses, yet the typical
\par
fund manager had a wide remit and so was constrained
\par
in the time available to analyse any given company. More-
\par
over, the information in question is by nature wide-rang-
\par
ing, complex and imprecisely defined. In this context, and
\par
because senior managers can be viewed as experts with re-
\par
spect to their own businesses, the meetings were useful
\par
simply as economically efficient mechanisms for fundR. Barker et al. / Accounting, Organizations and Society 37 (2012) 207--222 213
\par
managers to build their understanding. Of course, the fund
\par
managers had other means of doing this: they got regular
\par
information from companies' investor relations teams, for
\par
example, and for technology-based investments they
\par
might rely on technical experts to help them interpret
\par
specialist, otherwise opaque public domain information.
\par
But, especially at the more strategic level, direct communi-
\par
cations from the CEO and CFO were helpful in making
\par
sense of the primary determinants of business perfor-
\par
mance, of the structure and management of the business,
\par
its strategic direction, competitive environment, and so on:
\par
market conditions. Again, the twin drivers of confirmation
\par
and confidence are evident. One head of research described
\par
the motivation for ad hoc meetings in the following way.
\par
We have if you like a sort of base position in every com-
\par
pany so we know where we are positioned, what our
\par
[research] recommendation is and what the key drivers
\par
are behind that. The ad hoc meetings tend to come about
\par
because there is something in the key drivers that has
\par
changed or we think there is something in the industry
\par
or whatever, and therefore we need to verify that position
\par
and just check that our base position is as we perceived it
\par
to be. [HoR]
\par
Sometimes when you read the papers you can misinterpret
\par
strategic themes and you need to understand how every-
\par
thing fits together, to avoid misinterpretations. [HoR]
\par
This acquisition of information should be interpreted
\par
against the backdrop of the analytical models that are
\par
maintained with respect to each company and used to pro-
\par
ject future earnings, cash flow etc. One of the commonly ci-
\par
ted functions of the meetings was to confirm, or
\par
alternatively throw doubt upon, the information and
\par
assumptions used in these models: ``You meet the people
\par
to check the numbers'' [HoR]. The information gathered in
\par
this way was perceived by fund managers to be useful even
\par
if it provided only reassurance, as opposed to (price-sensi-
\par
tive) news. The meetings would often serve simply to con-
\par
firm the existing assumptions in the fund managers'
\par
models -- ``generally these are always update, reassurance
\par
meetings, in the main'' -- but that in itself was seen as a po-
\par
sitive outcome. A meeting that strengthened confidence
\par
was seen as particularly valuable, as was one that caused
\par
them to change their assumptions.
\par
A successful meeting is when you can look at your invest-
\par
ment thesis and say the meeting helped me reinforce my
\par
view or is making me rethink my view. [HoR]
\par
The fund managers were concerned with temporal con-
\par
sistency, checking that a company was still pursuing the
\par
same strategy and seeing things the same way as at the
\par
previous meeting, for example, and that nothing had hap-
\par
pened meanwhile to render the model inaccurate. Corre-
\par
spondingly, an unsuccessful meeting was one that simply
\par
added noise, that cast doubt on the existing model without
\par
pointing clearly to a new one.
\par
A good meeting is something that helps you understand
\par
whether you are on the right track or not. A bad meeting
\par
is where you go in thinking that you have got this picture
\par
of the company and come out and the picture is fuzzier.
\par
[CIO]
\par
The need to provide reassurance in the investment posi-
\par
tion is typically satisfied by regular meetings, held once or
\par
twice per year after the announcement of the company's re-
\par
sults. However, there can be occasions when additional
\par
meetings are sought. These arise when there is a shock to
\par
the system -- something that threatens the fund managers'
\par
confidence in the investment position, something that
\par
makes them feel that the company is no longer understood.
\par
This could be a significant event, such as a takeover, the
\par
departure of a senior executive, or unexpected changes in
\par
Overall, two things are going on here, neither of which
\par
is understandable if `useful' is interpreted to mean only
\par
`price-sensitive'. The first is that information can be useful
\par
even if it simply confirms that nothing has changed. A deci-
\par
sion to hold is a decision to invest, and the fund manager
\par
needs current information to provide reassurance that
\par
the decision to hold is understood and remains support-
\par
able. The second is that a company's communication of
\par
information can either be successful or not in inspiring
\par
confidence. If the `picture is fuzzier' to the point where
\par
fund managers no longer feel that they understand the
\par
company, then they are likely to withdraw their invest-
\par
ment. In contrast with neoclassical theory, which accom-
\par
modates the concept of confidence within a fully
\par
determined probability distribution, there is a binary con-
\par
cept at work here, whereby fund managers are either suf-
\par
ficiently confident in their understanding, or they are not.
\par
This `willingness to invest' is captured by Shackle (1955),
\par
who writes that if news (i.e. information) `seems to provide
\par
internally inconsistent or conflicting evidence. . . or is for
\par
any reason difficult to interpret, the consequence would
\par
be to inhibit some kinds of business activity; not because
\par
the news was regarded as bad. . . but because it is
\par
unintelligible.'
\par
A further important attribute of the information com-
\par
municated during the meetings was its inherent intracta-
\par
bility. Again in contrast with neoclassical theory, this is
\par
not information in a form ready to be a straightforward in-
\par
put into a valuation model. Communication involved not
\par
just the transfer of information but also the associated cog-
\par
nitive processes whereby the fund manager assimilates
\par
and interprets the information. A reported benefit of the
\par
meetings was therefore that they helped the fund manag-
\par
ers to make sense of all the information by providing `soft'
\par
insights in terms of which they could frame it. The inter-
\par
viewees talked of insights into `grey' areas, of seeing things
\par
from different angles, and of building up an `understand-
\par
ing' of the company. They suggested that the meetings
\par
gave them a `feel' for things that helped them to read bro-
\par
kers' reports, to understand and interpret strategic themes,
\par
and to `fit together' all the different information sources,
\par
which, in themselves, did not necessarily give a clear pic-
\par
ture. The meetings were thereby a forum for knowledge
\par
creation, in the sense described by Nonaka and Takeuchi
\par
(1995).
\par
With this in mind, some fund managers sought to use
\par
the meetings to build long-term relationships with com-
\par
pany managers and so change the level of the interaction.214 R. Barker et al. / Accounting, Organizations and Society 37 (2012) 207--222
\par
This could work in two ways. First, the better the company
\par
managers got to understand the investors, the better they
\par
would be able to help them by responding to their partic-
\par
ular information needs. Second, the better the fund manag-
\par
ers got to know the company managers, the more they
\par
could engage with and understand their thinking about
\par
the firm, its strategy and its context.
\par
I think once you establish a relationship with a company
\par
and you have proven to them as an investor that you
\par
understand the company and the issues, then the meetings
\par
move to a different level. This doesn't happen overnight.
\par
It's something that can take a couple of years to get into
\par
this situation. . . you find out things about the way they're
\par
thinking about the business. . . that sort of relationship can
\par
be very, very rewarding. [HoR]
\par
The suggestion here is that the relationship becomes, in
\par
effect, a source of privileged information, a rich communi-
\par
cation of the type described by Giddens (1984) as `co-pres-
\par
ence'. Yet this conclusion does not rely upon the
\par
communication of anything price-sensitive. The relation-
\par
ship is about framing and making sense of public domain
\par
information, rather than introducing new information. It
\par
concerns tacit knowledge; the largely taken for granted
\par
knowledge, beliefs and assumptions which are drawn upon
\par
as a resource for sense making.
\par
What such tacit knowledge could not provide, however,
\par
was the `right answer'. The fund managers recognised that
\par
inherent uncertainty was a further important attribute of
\par
the information communicated during the meetings.
\par
Where information was this uncertain, everyone's inter-
\par
pretation was different, depending on how they scored or
\par
weighted different factors, and as the fund managers
\par
openly admitted, investment decisions became a matter
\par
of subjective judgement: ``Investment judgement is just that:
\par
it's judgement'' [CIO]. In contrast with neoclassical theory,
\par
information was neither shared nor probablisitic.
\par
In this informational environment, the concept of
\par
`price-sensitive' becomes vague. It is not the case that
\par
information is either price-sensitive or not. Instead, these
\par
are extreme and objective positions either side of an exten-
\par
sive and subjective grey area, in which tacit knowledge can
\par
be highly relevant to equity investment decisions yet not
\par
derived from formally price-sensitive information. More-
\par
over, the value of this tacit knowledge is enhanced by
\par
means of the interaction with company managers, where-
\par
by the fund managers are seeking to clarify, to learn and to
\par
establish confidence in their understanding of the com-
\par
pany, the need for which arises precisely because the infor-
\par
mation is not price-sensitive but is instead altogether less
\par
tractable.
\par
An evident consequence of the radical uncertainty
\par
underlying equity valuations and investment decisions is
\par
that any formal definition of price-sensitive information
\par
becomes difficult. If fund managers are making investment
\par
decisions based on `soft' information, then one way or an-
\par
other that information must find its way into the share
\par
price. The guidance provided by the regulator, the FSA,
\par
which is based on neoclassical theory, treats as price-sen-
\par
sitive only that information that has a directly measurable
\par
impact on the share price, through the short term (ca.
\par
2 year) performance forecasts; otherwise, it actively
\par
encourages companies `to hold a useful dialogue with their
\par
shareholders' (FSA, 1996, para. 6). In regulatory terms, soft
\par
information relating to performance on a longer timescale
\par
is not treated as price-sensitive, but that is not because it
\par
has no impact on price: it is because no-one, including
\par
the companies themselves, can confidently claim to know
\par
what that impact will be. The uncertainty precludes
\par
straightforward measurement.
\par
In this context, the data from our fieldwork suggested
\par
an important distinction between the short-term and the
\par
long-term. There is a correspondence between the concept
\par
of price-sensitivity and information that relates to the
\par
short-term, and is relatively objective and verifiable. In
\par
contrast, consideration of the long-term requires us to en-
\par
ter the world of Knightian uncertainty, in which fund man-
\par
agers seek to acquire and assimilate information that can
\par
be used for trading but that is not formally price-sensitive.
\par
This distinction between the nature of short-term and
\par
long-term information has important consequences for
\par
our understanding of the informational value of the com-
\par
pany-fund manager meetings. These consequences arise
\par
through fund managers' attempts to outperform the index
\par
by trying to anticipate changes in share prices, recognising
\par
that these prices are a function both of fundamental value
\par
and of investor sentiment. Typically fund managers would
\par
attempt to model both the fundamental value of a com-
\par
pany as well as the value placed on it by the market,
\par
including in each case an analysis of the determinants of
\par
these values. The job of the fund manager was then to
\par
identify factors (`triggers') that would change the share
\par
price, to predict when the changes would occur, and to
\par
beat the market by trading in anticipation of that. The chief
\par
investment officer quoted below was more explicit than
\par
most of our interviewees, but the process he described
\par
seems to have been fairly typical.
\par
We have 5 questions that all major investment decisions
\par
must answer. What are the key drivers.for that asset/stock
\par
or whatever? What's changing? What's in the price, which
\par
is kind of the analysis? Why will the market change its
\par
mind, which is actually the difficult bit, and then what's
\par
the trigger, because we want to be pro-active investors.
\par
So, once we've got the first 4 questions, we identify a trig-
\par
ger. . . So some of the stuff at the company meetings might
\par
be focusing on understanding what's changing some of the
\par
key drivers, some of the stuff might be understanding what
\par
happens next. . . [CIO]
\par
Within this process the time scale is critical. A fund
\par
manager may perceive that a stock is significantly under-
\par
valued by the market, but there is no point investing on
\par
that basis if the market view is unlikely to change within
\par
a foreseeable future. If you can see ahead of the market,
\par
however, and time your trades well, there is a perceived
\par
opportunity for outperformance. In terms of time scales,
\par
the consensus among the fund managers interviewed
\par
was that there was relatively little scope for beating the
\par
market in respect of company performance over a 2 year
\par
period, since companies generally give a fairly detailed
\par
steer on expected results over this period, both directly
\par
to fund managers and indirectly via sell-side analysts'R. Barker et al. / Accounting, Organizations and Society 37 (2012) 207--222 215
\par
consensus forecasts. While important, such information
\par
rapidly becomes impounded in revisions to these forecasts
\par
and in share prices (Fried \& Givoly, 1982; Hughes \& Ricks,
\par
1987). Beyond 2 years, however, or at most three, things
\par
were much less certain, not just because the environment
\par
was less certain but because company performance would
\par
depend on management decisions that had not yet been
\par
made. To some extent, that was reflected in the current
\par
share price, although not in the well-determined way as-
\par
sumed by neoclassical theory, where there is no distinction
\par
of the type made here between the short and long-term. In
\par
this informational environment, if an investor judged that
\par
a company was stronger or its future environment more
\par
favourable than market sentiment had allowed for and,
\par
critically, that this would begin to become apparent in a
\par
reasonable time frame, as more reliable forecasts for years
\par
3 and 4 came out, there was an opportunity to invest in
\par
anticipation of the share price change that would result
\par
from those forecasts. The head of research just quoted sug-
\par
gested a typical investment horizon of 12--18 months: in-
\par
vest now on the basis of anticipated share price changes
\par
in 12--18 months, which is when the forecasts for years 3
\par
and 4 will be impacting on the market. Because the game
\par
was about beating the index, and not about absolute re-
\par
turns, these arguments applied equally well if the investor
\par
was predicting poor performance.
\par
It is in this context that, for individual fund managers
\par
making discretionary investment decisions, the emphasis
\par
in the meetings was overwhelmingly on the judgement
\par
of top management. Of the various factors that were per-
\par
ceived to influence a change in the share price between
\par
now and a year or two's time, the one they felt uniquely
\par
positioned to judge, as a result of their face to face meet-
\par
ings, was the quality of the CEO--CFO team. If a fund man-
\par
ager believed that this quality was better or worse than the
\par
market as a whole rated it to be, then they presumed that it
\par
would be reflected in company performance a few years
\par
down the line, that this would be reflected in future fore-
\par
casts, and that these would impact on the future share
\par
price. On the time horizons that counted, therefore, the
\par
evaluation of management was perceived to be critical.
\par
In this context, the fund managers described various
\par
methods for extracting information, such as observing the
\par
interpersonal dynamics between CEO and CFO, provoking
\par
the managers a bit to test their reactions, or more generally
\par
of noting how they responded to questions asked. The fund
\par
managers we observed asked many questions many that
\par
could have been answered perfectly well by the investor
\par
relations team, concerning for example changes in the man-
\par
agement team, like-for-like sales growth, or changes in
\par
financial structure. But it was often not the answers they
\par
were looking for so much as how those answers were given.
\par
Were managers talking about their strategy, or their perfor-
\par
mance objectives, in a way that was consistent with the im-
\par
age they, the fund managers, had of the management's
\par
thinking? Similarly, soft information about competitors
\par
was valuable not just in itself, but for giving some insight
\par
into how the competitors were viewed by the management
\par
team, which also told them something about the judgement
\par
of that team and the coherence of their strategic vision
\par
(which competitors were they focused on and which blind
\par
to, was their view sanguine or jaundiced, etc.). The fund
\par
managers' confidence in their judgements of management
\par
were also related to how much they had seen of them: ``usu-
\par
ally that comes with having met them more than once.'' So
\par
long-term relationships were also perceived to be a critical
\par
source of the information on which judgements were made.
\par
It is possible to argue that in a situation characterised
\par
by radical uncertainty, it was entirely rational for fund
\par
managers to make decisions in this way. The evidence is
\par
similar to that of a recent study of venture capitalists in Sil-
\par
icon Valley, in which Shapin (2008) found that while the
\par
numbers had to roughly add up for an investment to be
\par
considered, the final choice of investment depended much
\par
more on subjective judgements of people than on forecasts
\par
of returns. In Shapin's work this observation is linked to a
\par
sociology of knowledge in which people place trust in the
\par
guarantees afforded by character rather than in evidence
\par
of uncertain provenance (see Shapin, 1994). But the obser-
\par
vation also makes good common sense. In a highly compet-
\par
itive and radically uncertain world, a company's prospects
\par
depend above all on the ability of managers to respond to
\par
whatever shocks might lie in store. Of course, the uncer-
\par
tainty for a new venture, to which a venture capitalist will
\par
remain tied for some years, is likely to be greater than that
\par
for a large established company, with a liquid market for
\par
its shares, but the principle is the same. With a new ven-
\par
ture, financial projections may be no more than optimistic
\par
guesses. With established companies one can look to a his-
\par
torical track record, but so can everyone else in the market.
\par
What one is trying to predict are deviations from the base
\par
case and for that one has no better information than does
\par
the venture capitalist.
\par
Shapin's argument duly noted, however, it is difficult to
\par
determine in practice whether or not the fund managers'
\par
access to company management can actually give them a
\par
real, as opposed to perceived, competitive advantage. The
\par
latter possibility is supported by each of our two alterna-
\par
tive explanations for the perceived usefulness of non-
\par
price-sensitive information. These are, first, that fund man-
\par
agers irrationally perceive such information to be useful
\par
and, second, they have a rational basis for its usefulness
\par
that lies outside conventional theorisation of investment
\par
decision-making. We consider each of these explanations
\par
in turn in the next two sections of the paper.
\par
Do fund managers interpret `useful information'
\par
rationally?
\par
The previous section has set out possible reasons why
\par
fund managers might rationally perceive their meetings
\par
with management to generate useful information for
\par
investment decisions. Yet our fieldwork also suggested
\par
several reasons to question the fund managers' accounts,
\par
and to support the alternative explanation, discussed in
\par
the theoretical foundations section of this paper, that fund
\par
managers were placing irrational confidence in company
\par
meetings as a source of information.
\par
We start with the observation that it was difficult for us
\par
to get hold of how the fund managers actually made their
\par
judgements about management. The comments of one HoR
\par
illustrated this difficulty.216 R. Barker et al. / Accounting, Organizations and Society 37 (2012) 207--222
\par
Just thinking through the company meetings that I have
\par
attended over the years, the key thing is that there is no
\par
substitute for sitting across the table from somebody and
\par
getting an idea of their body language, their mood. . . their
\par
attitude. It's a very intangible thing because you have a
\par
variety of different. . . perceptions of. . . the quality of
\par
management. [HoR]
\par
During our interviews, we pressed fund managers on
\par
this `intangible thing' that they were seeking to assess,
\par
and that seemed to lie at the heart of their process. Two
\par
central themes emerged: judging the managers' behav-
\par
ioural attitudes (optimism or pessimism), and judging
\par
their ability to deliver. One CIO put it as follows.
\par
Obviously you can't get a raft of inside information,. . ., but
\par
normally you see companies on a regular basis. . . you get
\par
a behavioural attitude on the management, whether
\par
they're being optimistic or whatever, and an idea of their
\par
ability to implement strategy.. . . [CIO]
\par
A particular concern for fund managers was a perceived
\par
tendency on the part of company executives to optimistic
\par
forecasts. One of our interviewees noted that adding to-
\par
gether the predicted market shares of all the companies
\par
in an industry would almost always give more than
\par
100\%, and several others noted a tendency to serial opti-
\par
mism. As a general rule, fund managers do not want to in-
\par
vest in excessively optimistic management, because the
\par
corollary of such optimism is future negative surprises. In
\par
time, reality will out. Identifying the presence of over-opti-
\par
mism was therefore perceived to be a route to avoiding
\par
underperforming investments. More dangerous even than
\par
optimism, if sometimes hard to separate from it, was when
\par
management was perceived to be incompetent:
\par
I think there's still a tendency on the part of companies to
\par
think that if something is a bit bad, by the time you have to
\par
report your results it would have gone away. . . And genu-
\par
inely there are times when the management didn't realise,
\par
which is probably the most worrying aspect. The reason
\par
you had a profit warning is that they had no clue them-
\par
selves, and that is when you absolutely hit the panic but-
\par
ton. [HoR]
\par
This question of perceived management competence or
\par
ability was central. Going back to what made for a good
\par
meeting, one CIO summed it up by suggesting that it was
\par
essentially about finding out whether the management
\par
team had `got it' or not, whether they had or had not an
\par
`idea what they're doing', whether, in short, they were
\par
competent. This particular CIO concluded as follows:
\par
``That's what our judgement is about.. . . That's what we think
\par
company meetings are all about.''
\par
These findings parallel those of a recent ethnographic
\par
study of credit analysts and company executives (Ourouss-
\par
off, 2010) -- for example, one analyst in this study de-
\par
scribed the purpose of company meetings as to `get some
\par
impression as to how they (executives) articulate their
\par
strategies. . . how knowledgeable they are about their
\par
competitors. . . their credibility, their competence,' while
\par
others described the need to `check for inaccuracies' and
\par
`to reconcile discrepancies.'
\par
And so we find that fund managers' investment deci-
\par
sion-making places great emphasis on an assessment of
\par
management's behavioural attitudes and inherent abilities,
\par
evaluated primarily by direct, personal contact in private
\par
meetings. The implication is that fund managers are plac-
\par
ing great faith in their own ability to judge people, and to
\par
infer from this judgement the future performance of
\par
organisations led by those people. These are strong claims,
\par
and they find little support in our findings from the inter-
\par
views with CFOs, whose accounts of the information con-
\par
tent of the meetings differed in some important ways
\par
from those of the fund managers.
\par
The CFOs we interviewed recognised and were keen to
\par
respond to the fund managers' need to check their models
\par
and regularly confirm their investment positions. More-
\par
over, with interests in both investor confidence and a sta-
\par
ble share price, they shared the fund managers' concern to
\par
establish a clear understanding of strategy and perfor-
\par
mance. And the CFOs also valued continuity of representa-
\par
tion of fund managers at the meetings and the
\par
understanding that brought. Indeed, one of their main
\par
complaints was that the people turning up from the fund
\par
manager side changed too often, making relationship-
\par
building difficult. Their motive, however, was different: a
\par
desire to have good contacts and support within the inves-
\par
tor community, on which they could draw in a takeover
\par
situation or if things went against them. While they were
\par
very happy to help the fund managers as best they could,
\par
they were also very reluctant to be drawn into any conver-
\par
sation that might confer privileged information. Indeed,
\par
and tellingly, they saw challenge enough in ensuring that
\par
the fund managers had even a basic understanding of the
\par
business, without worrying about anything deeper. Far
\par
from raising the level of discussion, their interest lay in
\par
keeping it as simple as possible.
\par
You've got to position your company quite clearly and in
\par
very, very, simple sound bites, very consistently. . . You
\par
sit down and quite deeply think about the expectations,
\par
what are you capable of doing, and you package them up
\par
into an expectation the City or Wall Street can understand.
\par
[CFO]
\par
You should never underestimate the need to explain the
\par
simple stuff to the market. I think if we look back on what
\par
we've got wrong over the last five years. . . explaining
\par
what the market is all about and how different it is in
\par
America, from the UK, and what drives it, and who pays
\par
for the stuff, and why the growth rates are what they
\par
are, and what role technology is going to play, in a simple
\par
way, time and again, relentlessly, is what we should have
\par
done. [CFO]
\par
The key element here that is not captured in the fund
\par
mangers' accounts is the emphasis on simplicity. From
\par
the CFOs' perspective, the primary need was to distil infor-
\par
mation about their companies into simple, consistent
\par
soundbites, each relating to the key value drivers on which
\par
the fund managers sought information and reassurance.
\par
This point was made rather forcefully by some.R. Barker et al. / Accounting, Organizations and Society 37 (2012) 207--222 217
\par
To them it's just like well, you've bought your machine, you
\par
switch it on, profits go up, and as long as you guys don't
\par
screw it up it will go on up ,forever. [CFO]
\par
Unless you tell them very explicitly, they will tend to model
\par
that almost into perpetuity. [CFO]
\par
The primary focus for the CFOs was communicating
\par
with respect to the short term. On this time scale, any
\par
new information, since it was clearly price-sensitive, was
\par
very carefully managed. With such short-term information
\par
therefore effectively in the public domain, and with great
\par
care being taken to avoid surprises, there was little scope
\par
for outperformance. This is consistent with the earlier dis-
\par
cussion of the distinction between the (relatively know-
\par
able) short-term and the (uncertain) long-term. Yet, in
\par
contrast with the earlier discussion and with fund manag-
\par
ers' perceptions, the CFOs also offered little reason to think
\par
that the long-term would offer any greater scope for out-
\par
performance. Company performance beyond the short-
\par
term was not just subject to probabilistic caveats; it was
\par
fundamentally uncertain. As one of the CFOs said of the
\par
fund managers:
\par
I think that they do build fairly accurate short-term sales
\par
and profit models looking at a couple of years, but thereaf-
\par
ter, I don't know what we are doing in three years time, so
\par
nor do they. [CFO]
\par
These CFO accounts align with those reported by
\par
Ouroussoff (2010), who identified that while there is a fun-
\par
damental conflict in the understanding of risk between
\par
companies and their credit analysts, this remains hidden
\par
because the corporate need for a strong credit rating in-
\par
duces compliance with the analysts' perspective.
\par
Overall, the CFOs' accounts are of a simplified message,
\par
relating to key value drivers, and a focus on meeting short-
\par
term expectations. In the light of this simplicity, and of the
\par
brevity and formalism of the meetings we observed, there
\par
must be real doubts as to the informational claims made by
\par
fund managers. Could they really gain privileged and com-
\par
petitively valuable insights into management from annual
\par
(or at best six-monthly) meetings, that were formal and,
\par
from what we observed, formulaic, of just one hour dura-
\par
tion, with anything from 5 to 20 people in the room, meet-
\par
ings that were repeated with each of their main
\par
competitors? Could they reasonably presume a deeper
\par
understanding of a company's value drivers than that con-
\par
tained in the company's distilled communication? All of
\par
this is inherently hard to warrant.
\par
Such a conclusion would be consistent with the balance
\par
of empirical evidence, which suggests that fund managers
\par
cannot in fact outperform in this way. Most of the research
\par
on active fund performance is based on US mutual funds,
\par
and here the consensus is that on aggregate, and taking ac-
\par
count of costs, active fund managers underperform the in-
\par
dex (Carhart, 1997; French, 2008; Gruber, 1996; Malkiel,
\par
1995). It is a matter of debate whether individual fund
\par
managers can outperform. The established view is that
\par
they cannot, with past performance being no predictor of
\par
future performance and any persistence of outperformance
\par
being attributable to luck. Recent work has challenged this.
\par
Berk and Green (2004) have argued that the evidence is
\par
consistent with individual fund managers having superior
\par
stock-picking abilities but these being quickly competed
\par
away due to diseconomies of scale as investors follow per-
\par
formance. Kosowski, Timmermann, Wermers, and White
\par
(2006), using a bootstrap analysis to better separate out
\par
the effects of luck and skill, argue that observed persis-
\par
tence of fund performance is greater than would be ex-
\par
pected from luck alone (although the fact of overall
\par
underperformance remains).
\par
The situation with which we are concerned here differs
\par
from that of US mutual fund managers in two respects. On
\par
the one hand, the access to corporate management enjoyed
\par
by institutional investors in UK equities is significantly
\par
greater. Based as it is on this privileged information source,
\par
the claim to outperformance perhaps has a credibility lack-
\par
ing in the US context. On the other hand, while the London-
\par
based fund management firms typically have retail prod-
\par
ucts in the form of unit trust funds, a large part of their
\par
business, and the part which concerns us here, consists of
\par
managing money for pension funds, insurers, endowments
\par
and other institutional fundholders. For an institution to
\par
switch its mandate from one house to another is a much
\par
more complex process than to shift money from one mu-
\par
tual fund or unit trust to another. Mandates are held by
\par
the houses rather than by individual fund managers and
\par
are normally in place for several years. In this context
\par
any gain from active management would require substan-
\par
tial persistence of outperformance, and the evidence is that
\par
this does not exist. Tonks (2005) found just one period per-
\par
sistence of pension fund performance, while other UK find-
\par
ings suggest both an aggregate underperformance and
\par
performance persistence amongst underperforming but
\par
not outperforming funds (Blake \& Timmermann, 2003;
\par
Cuthbertson, Nitsche, \& O'Sullivan, 2008; Rhodes, 2000).
\par
Bootstrap analysis of UK unit trusts shows similar results
\par
to those of US mutual funds, suggesting that a relatively
\par
small degree of outperformance (but a rather larger degree
\par
of underperformance) is attributable to skill rather than
\par
luck (Cuthbertson, Nitsche, \& O'Sullivan, 2008).
\par
This evidence was endorsed by one of our interviewees,
\par
then heading a passively-managed fund, having started out
\par
as an active fund manager but become increasingly disillu-
\par
sioned with what he described as their `romantic' or
\par
`heroic' approach to investment:
\par
Over time I became more disillusioned with that as an
\par
investment approach and thought. . . what advantage
\par
have I got? Have I got an information advantage? Do I find
\par
out more stuff? Do I ask brighter questions than the next
\par
person? Do I manage to inveigle myself with the company
\par
and get information that other people haven't got? What is
\par
it that gives me an advantage? In the end I came to the
\par
nihilistic conclusion that I don't have any advantage. . .
\par
[Active fund managers] over-believe their own perceptions
\par
and under-discount the fact that there's lots of other peo-
\par
ple out there who are also asking the same questions and
\par
finding the same information. [CIO]
\par
Even if the fund managers' meetings with company
\par
executives give them an informational advantage over pri-
\par
vate investors, this argument goes, they give them no
\par
advantage over their peers. In an informationally efficient218 R. Barker et al. / Accounting, Organizations and Society 37 (2012) 207--222
\par
market, any judgement of management that could be cap-
\par
tured (even in principle) by those meetings would be re-
\par
flected in the share price and so provide no basis for
\par
trading.
\par
This discussion does not, however, deny the possibility
\par
that fund managers may still be economically rational in
\par
investing so heavily in company meetings. This is because,
\par
as will be discussed in the next section, their payoff may be
\par
in a form other than investment performance.
\par
Are fund managers exploiting a principal--agent
\par
relationship?
\par
The fund managers we interviewed took the view that,
\par
through privileged access to corporate management, to-
\par
gether with a combination of proprietary house models
\par
and individual and collective judgemental skills, they could
\par
beat the market, outperforming a benchmark index by
\par
more than the premium fee charged for their services as
\par
active traders. They also sold their services to current
\par
and potential clients on this basis. As discussed above,
\par
however, these claims beg the question as to whether the
\par
meetings really can confer such an informational advan-
\par
tage. Instead, the fund managers' claims of outperformance
\par
through privileged information may be at best wishful
\par
thinking and at worst wilful misinterpretation. This ques-
\par
tion is of particular importance because the fund managers
\par
were not acting on their own account, but as agents for
\par
others, such as pension fund trustees, with the possibility
\par
therefore arising of conflict between the objectives of the
\par
principal and the actions of the agent.
\par
At one end of the spectrum, we might see the fund man-
\par
agers as subject to established sources of cognitive bias,
\par
their confidence in their performance being explained as
\par
a product of excess confidence in private information, as
\par
noted above, and excess confidence in forecasting ability,
\par
manifest in a biased weighting of evidence of when they
\par
had beaten the market as compared with evidence of when
\par
they had not (Alpert \& Raiffa, 1982; Weinstein, 1980). On
\par
this interpretation the fund managers are effectively inno-
\par
cent but deluded. At the other end of the spectrum, we
\par
might see them as knowingly misrepresenting their abili-
\par
ties to clients and potential clients, using a story about
\par
superior performance based on privileged access, a story
\par
that they know or at least suspect to be false, to secure
\par
investment business and the revenues that derive from it.
\par
On this interpretation we have a classical principal--agent
\par
problem (Ross, 1973). Somewhere between these two ex-
\par
tremes would be an interpretation in terms of justification
\par
(Boltanski \& Thevenot, 2006) and narrative sensemaking
\par
(Weick, 1995). Trading under uncertainty, and therefore
\par
on a speculative basis, but unable to justify themselves to
\par
clients in that way, they justify and make sense of what
\par
they do in terms of rational.judgements, based on privi-
\par
leged information, and gradually come to believe in their
\par
own narratives.
\par
In practice, the difference between these interpreta-
\par
tions is not clear cut. Agency effects can follow from honest
\par
cognitive incompetence as much as from deceitful self-
\par
seeking (Hendry, 2001), and accountability to self is as
\par
fraught with uncertainties as accountability to others (But-
\par
ler, 2005). The fund managers would probably be hard put
\par
to decipher their own motives and intentions. There is
\par
however some strong prima facie evidence in the inter-
\par
views of an agency problem.
\par
First, the fund managers clearly take pleasure as well as
\par
remuneration from the activities we have been exploring.
\par
They described their relationships with company manag-
\par
ers, distant though these are, to be ``very, very rewarding''
\par
[HoR]. As Hendry et al. (2006) have noted, they take evi-
\par
dent pride in being active traders and look down with con-
\par
tempt on those who manage index funds or `closet index'
\par
funds, which depart only minimally from the index bench-
\par
mark. And they love pitting their wits against the market.
\par
``I love making investments'', said one simply, and went onto
\par
talk of the excitement he got from the ``challenge -- almost
\par
-- you know, how good are you?'' [CIO]. In other environ-
\par
ments, both sensation-seeking (Black, 1986) and the need
\par
to justify fees (Dow \& Gorton, 1997) have been postulated
\par
to lead to excessive trading. That may or may not be the
\par
case here, but there is a clear suggestion that active trading
\par
may be serving the private interests of the fund managers
\par
rather than the interests of their clients: it is, in the end,
\par
the clients who pay for the enjoyment that the fund man-
\par
agers get from the game they are playing.
\par
Second, while most of the fund managers appeared to
\par
be confident in their ability to outperform, some were less
\par
sanguine:
\par
I think I will plead the 5th on that one! I think I know the
\par
answer but it could prove the job I do adds no value what-
\par
soever in the long run! [HoR]
\par
As one interviewee observed, the key thing in pitching
\par
for business was not so much that active fund-manage-
\par
ment guaranteed outperformance as that, compared with
\par
investing in index or `quant' funds, it gave clients the pos-
\par
sibility of outperformance:
\par
They're probably only trying to get twenty or thirty (basis
\par
points) outperformance, so a net twenty let's say, whereas
\par
we're really trying to do plus two or three [\%] and we
\par
charge 30 (basis points) so that's really the difference.
\par
They're obviously much lower risk for the client. We try
\par
to play to their emotions: there is all this upside you're giv-
\par
ing away by not coming to us because we will achieve this
\par
for you. [CIO]
\par
The reference to emotions is telling, and reflects an
\par
observation by the head of a non-active house whose
\par
views on the active fund managers' claims we reported
\par
above:
\par
I think it's a human attribute to be attracted to people who
\par
are confident, express an opinion, and have got a track
\par
record to bear it out.. . . They want an emotional connec-
\par
tion. [CIO]
\par
The typical clients of these fund managers were pension
\par
fund trustees. Presented with the possibility of outperfor-
\par
mance they could, it seems, be persuaded to pay a pre-
\par
mium fee in order to take on extra risk for what was
\par
probably a lower expected return, in the uncertain hope
\par
of the possibility of an extra one or two percent that indexR. Barker et al. / Accounting, Organizations and Society 37 (2012) 207--222 219
\par
or quantitative funds could not give them. That may not
\par
have been a rational response: higher risk (a higher volatil-
\par
ity of returns, with the possibility of over but also of under-
\par
performance) should logically be accompanied by higher
\par
aggregate returns, which the evidence suggests do not ex-
\par
ist. But the fund managers' story, backed up by carefully-
\par
crafted assurances about their past performance (which
\par
could always be made to look good on some time scale,
\par
and against appropriately chosen criteria) and a story of
\par
privileged access to information, was sufficiently persua-
\par
sive emotionally for prospective clients to overlook the risk
\par
element.
\par
There may be several factors causing trustees to be be-
\par
guiled in this way by fund managers. While the fund man-
\par
agers present themselves as experts, the trustees are
\par
typically non-expert investors, and must surely have some
\par
awareness of their limitations in this respect. Faced with
\par
the fund managers' professional presence, coupled with
\par
their own need for reassurance in their invested positions,
\par
they acquiesce. In the manner described by Berger and
\par
Luckmann (1966), the fund managers may be perceived
\par
by the trustees to `know better', not based upon their ac-
\par
tual expertise, which is unobservable to the trustees, but
\par
based instead upon the roles that they fill and so the exper-
\par
tise that they represent. These perceptions may be sup-
\par
ported by the apparent credibility of knowledge being
\par
gained by fund managers through company meetings and
\par
then `brought back' for the benefit of the trustees, complet-
\par
ing the `cycle of accumulation' described by Latour (1987).
\par
Alternatively, or perhaps in addition, the trustees may fol-
\par
low Keynes (1936) in reasoning that it is better to fail con-
\par
ventionally than to succeed unconventionally. By following
\par
benchmark practice and appointing `blue-chip' fund man-
\par
agers, they avoid the personal risk of being accused of fail-
\par
ing in their statutory duties. In this decision, they are
\par
themselves distanced from their own clients, the pension-
\par
ers themselves, by a further principal--agent relationship,
\par
and one which is vulnerable (from the principals' perspec-
\par
tive) to both information asymmetry and to the difficulty
\par
of collective action. A further possibility is that, in the same
\par
way that fund managers may convince themselves of the
\par
informational benefits of meetings, when perhaps these
\par
benefits are illusory, so too the trustees may be vulnerable
\par
to self-conviction. In this context, the specialisation in the
\par
active trading of UK equities may be a contributory factor.
\par
The fund managers operate in a field on which non-expert
\par
trustees can take a view, and with which they can engage.
\par
Much as the fund managers take confidence from their re-
\par
peated meetings with corporate managers, so their clients
\par
might take confidence from repeated meetings and intelli-
\par
gible conversations with their fund managers.
\par
Conclusions and directions for further research
\par
Stock market regulation concerning price-sensitive
\par
information has a neoclassical economic foundation,
\par
whereby information that is useful is assumed to be synon-
\par
ymous with that which is price-sensitive. Informed by
\par
qualitative interview and observation based study of actual
\par
investment decision making, this paper calls into question
\par
that core assumption.
\par
Conventional theory is silent on the process by which
\par
information is communicated and understood, yet for the
\par
soft information that is most valued by fund managers,
\par
the method of communication and the associated process
\par
of understanding are inseparable from investment deci-
\par
sion-making. Through company meetings, tacit knowledge
\par
is obtained efficiently and uniquely from the communica-
\par
tional richness of the face-to-face encounter. The meetings
\par
are useful because they enable fund managers to frame or
\par
make sense of the plethora of hard data provided by the
\par
companies themselves and by analysts. They are a neces-
\par
sary precondition for fund managers to be in a position
\par
of being able to compete, and so they are useful even if
\par
they serve only a confirmatory or updating purpose, with
\par
no implication for a change in the share price.
\par
Conventional theory also fails to make a distinction be-
\par
tween the short term and the medium to longer term. In
\par
the short term, conventional assumptions concerning the
\par
objective nature of information and its interpretation can
\par
be said to hold, making `price-sensitive' a reasonable
\par
approximation for `useful'. Beyond the short-term, however,
\par
the informational context changes significantly and, for the
\par
most part, the concept of price-sensitivity ceases to apply. In
\par
contrast with conventional theory, the fund managers and
\par
CFOs in our study both made this distinction between rela-
\par
tively short-term corporate performance horizons of about
\par
2 years, where quantitative forecasts are relatively firm
\par
and objective, information is tightly controlled, and the
\par
scope for anticipating market movements is very limited,
\par
and slightly longer term horizons, where performance will
\par
depend on management decisions that have yet to be taken,
\par
in response to situations as yet unknown, and with respect
\par
to which information is subjectively communicated and
\par
understood and is not formally price-sensitive. Beyond the
\par
short term, the fund managers were effectively making deci-
\par
sions under conditions of either fundamental uncertainty or
\par
unquantifiable information, the very existence of which is
\par
denied by the probabilistic framework of neoclassical the-
\par
ory and generally assumed to be prevented by the regula-
\par
tory framework regarding price-sensitive information,
\par
which is largely based on that theory. In the view of fund
\par
managers, it is the privileged access to corporate manage-
\par
ment that allows them to judge whether, over this longer
\par
period, corporate management attitudes and abilities will
\par
result in better or worse company performance than the
\par
market currently expects, performance that will find its
\par
way into the share price, both directly through revised fore-
\par
casts and indirectly through changes in market sentiment,
\par
within roughly a 12--18 month period.
\par
These findings suggest opportunities for future re-
\par
search, with respect to both theory and policy. The chal-
\par
lenge to conventional theory is that, by taking into
\par
consideration tacit knowledge and the presence of uncer-
\par
tainty beyond the short term, the synonymity of `price-
\par
sensitive' and `useful' cannot be said to hold. This makes
\par
conventional theory limited in scope and it calls for new
\par
theorising to fill the gap. We have, in effect, a model that
\par
applies only to the short term, and a need therefore to sup-
\par
plement this with theory applicable beyond the short term,
\par
that embraces the roles of tacit knowledge and uncer-
\par
tainty. The associated challenge for policy is whether the220 R. Barker et al. / Accounting, Organizations and Society 37 (2012) 207--222
\par
characterisation of information in neoclassical theory,
\par
which forms the basis of the regulation of private informa-
\par
tion flows, is unduly narrow. This involves a welfare judge-
\par
ment, in the form of a trade-off between efficiency and
\par
equity. If, on the one hand, the definition of price-sensitive
\par
were to be broadened, for example to prohibit private
\par
meetings in recognition of their role in building tacit
\par
knowledge, then it would be reasonable to assume a corre-
\par
sponding efficiency loss in economic resource allocation,
\par
either because investors would not understand their
\par
investments as well or because, in the absence of a requi-
\par
site level of confidence, they would be unwilling to invest
\par
in the first place. A further reason to expect losses arises to
\par
the extent that fund management institutions serve a pub-
\par
lic interest role in corporate governance, by monitoring
\par
performance and calling executives to account. Moreover,
\par
it could be argued that there is little justification for impos-
\par
ing each of these losses, because the information conveyed
\par
in the meetings does not, by nature, have a simple and
\par
unambiguous value for trading purposes. Instead, it must
\par
first pass through the subjective, cognitive filter of the fund
\par
managers' analytical process, which is in itself proprietary
\par
and so a legitimate basis from which to seek private trad-
\par
ing gains. On the other hand, stock market regulations
\par
regarding price-sensitive information are designed to pro-
\par
mote equity, yet they do not `catch' the private acquisition
\par
of non-price-sensitive tacit knowledge that we describe
\par
here. Accordingly, a prohibition of private meetings would
\par
level the playing field if the meetings either communicate
\par
information that is subjective yet potentially widely useful
\par
(such as the CEO's opinion on expected growth in the mar-
\par
ket) or if they enable the acquisition of tacit knowledge
\par
that could only be achieved face-to-face (for example
\par
through debate about how to interpret expected growth
\par
for the company in question). Broadening the definition
\par
of price-sensitive could therefore be argued to lead to so-
\par
cially desirable equity gains, by denying privileged access
\par
to a privileged few. Overall, the policy trade-off here from
\par
broadening the concept of price-sensitivity is far from
\par
straightforward and is worthy of further research.
\par
In their most modest form, fund managers' claims
\par
regarding the usefulness of meetings are surely credible:
\par
an investment decision informed and confirmed by face-
\par
to-face interaction with senior management is surely pref-
\par
erable to one without. As we consider stronger claims,
\par
however, we must question whether fund managers actu-
\par
ally are able to adopt the trading strategies they describe,
\par
and whether the rich contextual information provided by
\par
the meetings can in fact give them the competitive advan-
\par
tage they claim. We acknowledge that we cannot simply
\par
draw a line between fund managers' claims that are legit-
\par
imate and those that are excessive, and there is consider-
\par
able scope here for further research, perhaps of an
\par
experimental nature. Our evidence does, however, suggest
\par
that it is asking a lot for infrequent, formal, brief and care-
\par
fully-managed meetings to impart the whole of the com-
\par
petitive informational advantage claimed, especially
\par
when the same process is replicated across competitor
\par
fund managers. Such scepticism is supported by the CFOs'
\par
reported doubts over the level of sophistication of the
\par
information conveyed, as well as by their focus on
\par
shorter-term information, which fund managers and CFOs
\par
alike regard as having little or no value for trading pur-
\par
poses. The balance of empirical evidence in the literature
\par
also supports this position, as it finds that active fund man-
\par
agers do not outperform the index. Indeed, there is no
\par
independent evidence to support the claimed distinctive
\par
strengths of fund managers, which appear to rest less on
\par
technical skills of the type associated with the professional
\par
analyst and more on fund managers' subjective abilities to
\par
interpret subjective information, not least concerning the
\par
quality of management.
\par
Such scepticism does not, however, imply that investor
\par
irrationality should be viewed as an exclusive explanation
\par
for the apparent paradox explored in this paper. It is en-
\par
tirely consistent, for example, for the meetings to be valu-
\par
able as a means of both framing the interpretation of
\par
public information and for assessing the subjective quali-
\par
ties of management, yet for these benefits to be perceived
\par
to be greater than they are. It is also consistent for the
\par
meetings to be a necessary condition for informed invest-
\par
ment decisions, yet not sufficient for the competitive
\par
advantage that enables outperformance. In short, useful ta-
\par
cit knowledge may well be acquired, just not to the extent
\par
that the fund managers' like to believe. In exploring this
\par
gap between rational and irrational claims regarding the
\par
usefulness of meetings, there is room for a variety of inter-
\par
pretations in terms of cognitive bias, post hoc rationalisa-
\par
tion and sense-making. These interpretations have in
\par
common that the fund managers (irrationally) place excess
\par
confidence in the informational value of meeting with
\par
companies. The need for further research in this area is
\par
clear: if the activities of the fund management sector,
\par
and in part those of business leaders, are indeed invested
\par
heavily in a deluded process, leading to irrational invest-
\par
ment decision-making, it is economically important to
\par
understand why this is the case.
\par
It is also possible, however, that the fund managers are
\par
not acting irrationally at all, but are instead knowingly or
\par
subconsciously making false claims, to support a non-trad-
\par
ing purpose. The fund managers are certainly aware that,
\par
as a group, they all share access to corporate management,
\par
and that the only evidence that their information on man-
\par
agement is valuable is their own stated perception that it is
\par
so. The CFOs, meanwhile, are readily compliant in the fund
\par
managers' charade. They need to maintain the fund man-
\par
agers' goodwill in order to support their share price and
\par
operating autonomy, and so are motivated to satisfy their
\par
part of the meetings, whether or not the information that
\par
they provide imparts a competitive informational advan-
\par
tage. Both parties may therefore doubt the informational
\par
purpose of the meetings, yet still they meet. Here we have
\par
argued that that there is at least strong prima facie evi-
\par
dence of a significant agency problem, as fund managers
\par
sell their services on the basis of performance claims they
\par
perhaps knowingly cannot support. Indeed, whether or not
\par
the meetings convey useful information, fund managers
\par
have a clear incentive to represent them in this way to cli-
\par
ents, because by so doing they help to justify active man-
\par
agement fees. In turn, this remunerates fund managers
\par
and enables them to enjoy the enactment of their roles.
\par
In effect, the fund managers' clients become compliant inR. Barker et al. / Accounting, Organizations and Society 37 (2012) 207--222 funding these agency costs. In turn, the pension fund trust-
\par
ees and others instructing the fund managers are them-
\par
selves agents acting on behalf of the beneficiaries of the
\par
funds and endowments being invested, so the agency
\par
problem repeats itself at this level as the interests of the
\par
principals in an optimal balance between risk and return
\par
appear to be sacrificed to the behavioural motivations of
\par
their agents. Our exploration of the company-fund man-
\par
ager relationship therefore suggests the need for further
\par
research into the related relationship between fund man-
\par
ager and trustee. Highlighting the neglect of agency rela-
\par
tionships in investment decision making is an important
\par
corrective to the assumption that the investor and share-
\par
holder are synonymous, and that agency problems are for
\par
the most part to be found in the role of executives or the
\par
interplay between majority and minority shareholders.
\par
The prima facie evidence here is that agency costs are writ
\par
large in the very significant channelling of equity invest-
\par
ments through active fund management.
\par
Our suggestions for further research therefore take the
\par
form of extending our three, non-mutually-exclusive
\par
explanations for the usefulness of non-price-sensitive
\par
information. First, the synonymity of price-sensitive and
\par
useful ceases to hold beyond the short term, with implica-
\par
tions for theory and policy. Second, fund managers appear
\par
to be excessively confident in the value of non-price-sensi-
\par
tive information, with implications for economic effi-
\par
ciency. Third, the exploitation of agency relationships
\par
explains a demand for information that is not directly re-
\par
lated to investment decision-making, implying significant,
\par
hidden agency costs. In relation to these areas we suggest
\par
the potential for each to be usefully informed by further
\par
qualitative empirical research. Our primary focus here
\par
has been on the informational value of private meetings
\par
but this could readily be extended to the related processes
\par
of portfolio selection and proprietary quantitative model-
\par
ling which in practice frame these meetings, as well as to
\par
the marketing of fund management expertise to existing
\par
and prospective clients.
