\par
Introduction
\par
The studies by Jamal and Sunder (2011), Jeacle and
\par
Carter (2011), and Downer (2011) raise two interesting
\par
questions for accounting scholars: what is an audit, and
\par
what is auditing research? As illustrated by these papers,
\par
there are widespread certification activities in modern soci-
\par
eties and the first theme of this essay is that accounting
\par
scholars can both learn from and contribute to a much
\par
broader research literature on certification and verification
\par
activities. While there are some exceptions, for the most part
\par
the accounting literature on ``auditing'' is quite narrow in
\par
scope, focusing almost exclusively on professional accoun-
\par
tants, financial statement audits by licensed auditors and
\par
the related regulatory regimes (e.g., Cooper \& Robson,
\par
2006; Francis, 2011; Nelson \& Tan, 2005).1 Thus the essay's
\par
title ``Auditing without Borders'' is an appeal to expand our
\par
thinking about auditing as a more generalizable activity, and
\par
a way of enriching our understanding of financial statement
\par
audits while at the same time contributing to a broader schol-
\par
arship on certification and verification processes.2 Even
\par
though auditing can be conceptualized as part of a more gen-
\par
eralizable activity, the second theme I take up at the end of the
\par
essay is that context also matters and is very important in
\par
understanding specific certification practices.
\par
I define ``auditing'' very broadly as any 3rd party certifi-
\par
cation by an expert, or more simply certification, and begin
\par
with a brief excursion into the wider world of certification
\par
activities. While certification can be thought of as a generic
\par
activity, what distinguishes among different types of certi-
\par
fication activities is the nature of the information being cer-
\par
tified, the 3rd party who certifies the information, and the
\par
expertise of the 3rd party and the basis for that expertise.3
\par
One way of thinking broadly about different types of certifi-
\par
cation activities is based on who does the certification. The
\par
first type is state-run certification programs in which govern-
\par
mental organizations conduct certifications. In the United
\par
States, examples of this would include the drug licensing
\par
program by the Food and Drug Administration, the food
\par
inspection program by the United States Department of Agri-
\par
culture, and the aircraft/airline certification program of the
\par
Federal Aviation Agency which is explored by Downer
\par
(2011). While financial statement audits are currently con-
\par
ducted by the private sector (with a state mandate and over-
\par
sight), the idea of state-run audits in lieu of private sector
\par
auditing has been advocated.4 In accounting, we could draw
\par
on the experiences of other state-run certification programs
\par
to better inform a serious debate over the merit of state-
\par
run financial audits, and to assess its potential effect on both
\par
audit quality and efficiency (costs).5
\par
There is a second insight from the literature on state-run
\par
certification programs and this is their vulnerability to cap-
\par
ture by private interests, including the well-known revol-
\par
1 An example is the study by Jamal, Maier, and Sunder (2002) on privacy
\par
and assurance in electronic commerce.
\par
2 Power (1994), Power (1999) explores the increase in the production of
\par
``audit-like'' activities, both by accounting firms and by other entities,
\par
especially in the public sector in the United Kingdom in the 1990s. His work
\par
focuses on the broad theme of making things auditable through calculative
\par
practices, and he poses the question of whether ``audits'' promise more
\par
than can be delivered, given what might be called the ``epistemological
\par
limitations'' of auditing. His work also examines auditing as a generalized
\par
social practice and the rituals of verification that surround and legitimatize
\par
these practices. My essay has a somewhat different focus which is that in
\par
conducting audit research, accounting scholars can both contribute to a
\par
broader literature on certification and can better inform their own research
\par
by looking at other certification literatures and settings.
