1992; Levitt \& Dwyer, 2002).
\par
The second broad type of certification is state-sponsored
\par
certification programs in which the state authorizes and reg-
\par
ulates certification activities, but the actual certifications are
\par
undertaken in the private sector with state monitoring and
\par
oversight. This is where public-company financial state-
\par
ment auditing is located (and also private company audits
\par
in those countries where they are mandated by law). The
\par
state licenses accountants as experts, requires that these ex-
\par
perts certify financial statements, and the state monitors the
\par
behavior and performance of the accounting experts. There
\par
are many examples of certification programs that are autho-
\par
rized and monitored by the state, but which are carried out
\par
in the private sector instead of state-run agencies. These
\par
activities are especially interesting because they feature a
\par
combination of public and private characteristics. A good
\par
example is the National Organic Program (NOP) in the
\par
United States, in which the United States Department of Agri-
\par
culture (USDA) registers and authorizes third-party organiza-
\par
tions to certify the authenticity of organic food production
\par
(Fetter \& Caswell, 2002; Heckman, 2006). The USDA website
\par
lists 33,082 accredited product certifiers around the world,
\par
with individual certifiers being accredited for multiple prod-
\par
ucts in many instances. The USDA does not explain exactly
\par
how it monitors the performance of these accredited certifi-
\par
ers, although the USDA does perform ``audits'' through the
\par
Agricultural Marketing Services and Accreditation Programs
\par
(a division within the USDA) and there are revocations of cer-
\par
tifications listed on the division's website. Beyond the NOP
\par
program, there is a global industry on food quality certifica-
\par
tion and related research on third-party food certification
\par
programs, e.g., Tanner (2000), Deaton (2004), Hatanaka, Bain,
\par
and Busch (2005), and Konefal and Hatanaka (2011).
\par
Another example is the Kyoto Protocol carbon credit veri-
\par
fication program. The United Nations Framework Convention
\par
on Climate Change has created methodologies for measuring
\par
these kinds of credits and identifies organizations that ap-
\par
prove, certify and register carbon offset projects. The Chicago
\par
Climate Exchange is a market for trading carbon credits and
\par
employs independent verification of greenhouse reductions
\par
to assure the integrity of their market.6 A good description of
\par
both mandatory and voluntary (discussed below) carbon credit
\par
programs can be found at the website of the Carbon Offset Re-
\par
search and Education (CORE) Initiative of the Stockholm Envi-
\par
ronment Institute (http://www.co2offsetresearch.org/).
\par
The third category is private-sector certification pro-
\par
grams and would include activities such as baseball trad-
\par
ing card certification described by Jamal and Sunder
\par
(2011). Even though many these are purely voluntary pro-
\par
grams, without state mandate, the same general informa-
\par
tion asymmetry problems exist as in all certification
\par
activities, and that is information uncertainty between
\par
buyers and sellers. However, these voluntary activities
\par
are less institutionally complex due to the lack of oversight
\par
by the state, which means that these certification pro-
\par
grams are largely driven by private interests and market
\par
forces. Examples of these private certification activities in-
\par
clude bond rating agencies such as Moody's and Standard
\par
\& Poor's, although the Securities and Exchange Commis-
\par
sion does require bond ratings for public debt issues. An-
\par
other example of private certification is the verification
\par
of security in electronic commerce by companies such as
\par
VeriSign and TRUSTe (Edelman, 2010).7
\par
In their discussion of food quality, Masters and Sanogo
\par
(2002) argue that the emergence of credible third-party
\par
certification requires the presence of `standards'' and a
\par
standard-setting authority. This would be true for all certi-
\par
fication activities. With state-run and state-sponsored cer-
\par
tification programs, the state of course has the authority to
\par
establish and enforce standards for certifications. However,
\par
it turns out that we also see the voluntary emergence of
\par
standards and standard-setting organizations in private
\par
certification activities. A good example is the emergence
\par
of ethical trading schemes and eco-labeling. For example,
\par
Fairtrade Labeling Organizations International (FLO) is an
\par
umbrella organization that establishes standards for the
\par
ethical production of food. The Fairtrade certification is
\par
undertaken by the organization, FLO-CERT. Fairtrade is
\par
best known for coffee certification, but their certification
\par
system covers many other products as well including co-
\par
coa, cotton, fruits, vegetables, nuts and spices (Ruben,
\par
2008). A second example is the Forrest Stewardship Coun-
\par
cil which is a nongovernmental organization that certifies
\par
the sustainability of forests and forest products (Auld,
\par
Gulbrandsen, \& McDermott, 2008; Eden, 2009;
\par
Gulbrandsen, 2004). Another example is voluntary carbon
\par
offset programs. These relate to voluntary carbon emission
\par
reductions that are produced or purchased by entities that
\par
are not obligated to reduce greenhouse certifications out-
\par
side of Kyoto Protocol compliance. A good example we
\par
are all familiar with is the availability of carbon offset pur-
\par
chases for airline tickets.
\par
6 The Exchange's website indicates it requires independent verification
\par
of carbon offsets by an approved ``verifier'' who must be certified by the
\par
American National Standards Institute (ANSI). The website also indicates
\par
that the Exchange and ANSI assess the competence of verifiers using ISO
\par
14065 issued by the International Organization for Standardization, which
\par
is discussed later.
\par
7 The analysis of TripAdvisor by Jeacle and Carter (2011) is interesting,
\par
and while TripAdvisor may well influence consumer behavior, customer
\par
ratings do not meet the strict definition of 3rd party certification by an
\par
expert. As Dranove and Jin (2010) put it, rather than relying on experienced
\par
certifiers, these types of ratings represent the aggregation of self-reported
\par
experiences by unknown consumers. The hotel and restaurant ratings in
\par
TripAdvisor are similar in nature to customer ratings of restaurants by
\par
Zagat, or to the customer reviews found on Google and Amazon websites.
\par
While interesting social phenomena in their own right, in my view they do
\par
not provide insight to 3rd party certification activities by experts such as
\par
auditors.320 J.R. Francis / Accounting, Organizations and Society 36 (2011) 318--323
\par
Given the importance of standards in the credible
\par
production of third-party certification (Masters \& Sanogo,
\par
2002), it is not surprising to see the emergence of non-
\par
governmental standard-setting organizations for volun-
\par
tary certification activities. The best known of these is
\par
International Organization for Standardization (known
\par
as ISO) founded in 1947 and based in Geneva. It claims
\par
to be the world's largest developer and producer of
\par
international standards, having issued 18,536 standards
\par
through December 31, 2010, with a membership of na-
\par
tional standards institutes from 163 countries.8 Each
\par
country has its own national-level organization that over-
\par
sees the development of national standards and the
\par
accreditation of certification bodies that authorize individ-
\par
ual entities to perform certifications. In the United States
\par
this is the American National Standards Institute (ANSI).
\par
In the United Kingdom it is the British Standards Institute
\par
(BSI Group), and accreditation of certification bodies
\par
undertaken by a separate organization, The United King-
\par
dom Accreditation Service. In the UK and US these organi-
\par
zations emerged from predecessor organizations created in
\par
the early 1900s for the development of engineering stan-
\par
dards. What is especially interesting is that these national
\par
standards bodies have the explicit support of the state in
\par
controlling private sector ``voluntary'' standards and certi-
\par
fication processes, and industry itself is an active partici-
\par
pant in developing ISO standards, which has led to some
\par
concerns over both the objectivity and the quality of these
\par
standards (Bialous \& Yach, 2001; Roht-Arriaza, 1995). Even
\par
though ISO is a nongovernmental entity, it is a powerful
\par
organization. And, while ISO standards appear to be volun-
\par
tary and developed in the private sector, it turns out that
\par
the state is heavily involved in the development and reg-
\par
ulation of these ``private'' certifications. ISO standards are
\par
de facto law, but in many instances they actually become
\par
de jure law when the standards are legally adopted by
\par
governments, as often is the case.9
\par
In researching this essay, I admit to surprise at the scale
\par
of the International Organization for Standardization's
\par
standard-setting activities, the related role of national
\par
standards bodies to regulate ``voluntary'' certification prac-
\par
tices. The scope of ISO standards in particular is
\par
breathtaking and even includes a standard on auditing
\par
the compliance with ISO standards. ISO 19011 Guidelines
\par
for Quality and/or Environmental Management Systems
\par
Auditing describes the methodology for conducting and
\par
documenting audits, and preparing certification reports.
\par
It also provides guidance for assessing the competence of
\par
auditors, including the personal attributes, knowledge
\par
and skills, education, training, and work experience. Per-
\par
haps it will come as no surprise that all of the Big Four
\par
accounting firms have consulting services that provide
\par
ISO certifications (including services to advise and assist
\par
clients in preparing for such certifications).
\par
Despite their importance in certification practices, the
\par
International Organization for Standardization and na-
\par
tional standards bodies are not transparent organizations.
\par
We know very little about their governance structures or
\par
operations. This opaqueness stands in stark contrast to
\par
financial statement auditing which is far more visible in
\par
terms of audit practices and regulation/oversight by the
\par
state. What we do know is that ISO's revenues in 2009
\par
were 36.349 million Swiss Francs, or approximately 41 bil-
\par
lion US dollars, making it slightly larger than the combined
\par
2009 global revenues of both KPMG and Ernst \& Whinney.
\par
The standards/certification business is clearly a big indus-
\par
try, and there would seem to be great potential for both
\par
empirical research and critical scholarship.10
\par
The preceding discussion is intended to illustrate the
\par
broad range and diversity of certification activities and re-
\par
search literatures. While these disparate certification activ-
\par
ities have important differences, I argue that there are
\par
important similarities as well. One framework for thinking
\par
about general characteristics of certification comes from
\par
the economics literature which has modeled ``quality dis-
\par
closures'' as an information asymmetry problem between
\par
sellers and buyers, and the role of 3rd parties in certifying
\par
seller disclosures (information). In reviewing this litera-
\par
ture, Dranove and Jin (2010) identify three key issues:
\par
information asymmetry and the incentives of sellers to re-
\par
port dishonestly to buyers; the inability of third-parties to
\par
accurately verify the seller's information when there is
\par
noise in data; and the self-interest of certifiers that may
\par
lead to biased reports. The information setting described
\par
by Dranove and Jin (2010) sounds exactly like the corpo-
\par
rate financial reporting environment. The seller (the
\par
reporting entity) pays the auditor (a 3rd party) to certify
\par
the seller's information. Sellers have incentives to misre-
\par
port or to stretch the truth (what we gently term earnings
\par
management). There is estimation error in accounting data,
\par
as well as possible bias, and for these reasons auditors can-
\par
not verify the financial statements with certainty. It's an
\par
inherent limitation of the audit task. Finally, the self-inter-
\par
est of auditors has been widely debated and auditing is
\par
actually cited by Dranove and Jin (2010) as an example
\par
of the bias of 3rd party certifiers. In summing up the
\par
theoretical literature, Dranove and Jin (2010, p. 948) con-
\par
clude: ``The theoretical literature cast some doubt on the
\par
ability of third party certifiers to accurately measure qual-
\par
ity [information accuracy] and, on occasion, their incen-
\par
tives to truthfully disclose it. The former problem can be
\par
alleviated if the certifier knows the sources of noise in
\par
