\par
quality data. The latter problem is more problematic be-
\par
cause all of the usual mechanisms, including competition,
\par
reputation and external monitoring, do not necessarily cor-
\par
rect the incentives of certifiers.'' While this may seem like a
\par
sweeping generalization, the point Dranove and Jin (2010)
\par
make is that given the results in the analytical literature
\par
we cannot simplistically assume that competition, reputa-
\par
tion protection or external monitoring will necessarily as-
\par
sure the objective behavior of 3rd party certifiers. This is
\par
important because it is quite common for accounting
\par
scholars to argue that auditors have reputation incentives
\par
to protect their integrity, but the theoretical literature sug-
\par
gests otherwise and also points to the limitations of effec-
\par
tive regulation in correcting for the bias and self-interest of
\par
3rd party certifiers. This conclusion has disturbing implica-
\par
tions for the quality of 3rd party certifications, including
\par
financial statement audits. As Huck Finn often laments in
\par
Mark Twain's masterpiece, ``What's a body to do?'' Perhaps
\par
the good news is that the transparency we have in financial
\par
statement audits also means that we may have better abil-
\par
ity to assess the quality of auditors relative to other
\par
certifiers.
\par
Dranove and Jin (2010) also review the limited empiri-
\par
cal evidence and it generally supports the conclusions
\par
reached in the theoretical literature. They discuss briefly
\par
two literatures close to home, the bias of financial analysts,
\par
and the auditor's independence threats. Given the paucity
\par
of empirical research on this topic, I believe that auditing is
\par
in fact an excellent arena in which to study the general
\par
properties of 3rd party certifications. As noted above,
\par
auditing is like many other state-authorized certifications,
\par
and what we learn about auditing may help to better
\par
understand other kinds of programs, as well as voluntary
\par
certification arrangements (many of which are regulated
\par
de facto by ISO and national standards bodies). Financial
\par
statement audits can be particularly is instructive for
\par
understanding other certification activities where there is
\par
interplay of public and private dimensions on incentives
\par
and the quality of certification practices. In fact, there is
\par
the same public/private interplay with ISO standards and
\par
national standards bodies.
\par
As discussed in the next section, auditing practices are
\par
also country-specific which means there is a rich compar-
\par
ative data that can be used to better understand how insti-
\par
tutions and culture in particular affect the private
\par
incentives and behavior of auditors. The insights gained
\par
from auditing research may have implications for
\par
understanding the behavior of other 3rd party certifiers
\par
as well.
\par
Context matters
\par
While advocating the expansive study of auditing
\par
``without borders'' at the same time it is important to
\par
understand the specific contexts in which certification
\par
practices take place. The impulse in modernist scholarship
\par
of all kinds is to abstractly generalize and remove context.
\par
In fact this is exactly what the information economics liter-
\par
ature does that is reviewed in Dranove and Jin (2010). It's
\par
also what we do implicitly in financial statement audit re-
\par
search. For example, when Power (1994) and Power (1999)
\par
describes the audit society, he is generalizing from finan-
\par
cial statement audits to other (new) arenas in which simi-
\par
lar calculative practices emerge and give rise to
\par
accountability. Or consider audit judgment research. Stud-
\par
ies of auditor judgment are often generalized (perhaps
\par
implicitly more so than explicitly) to all auditors, irrespec-
\par
tive of culture, organization or context. But would one nec-
\par
essarily expect an auditor in Singapore, or Norway to
\par
behave the same way as an auditor in the United States?
\par
Interestingly, the Institute of Chartered Accountants in
\par
England and Wales (ICAEW, 2011) recognizes this very
\par
explicitly and identifies five national characteristics that
\par
affect the quality of auditing practices:
\par
political, economic and business environment;
\par
legal framework;
\par
education;
\par
culture; and
\par
perceptions of the value of auditing.
\par
In fact, the very notion of an accounting firm or audit
\par
market is explicitly national in character. Consider the fol-
\par
lowing from the global website of PricewaterhouseCoopers
\par
(http://www.pwc.com/gx/en/corporate-governance/net-
\par
work-structure.jhtml):
\par
``In most parts of the world, the right to practice
\par
accountancy is granted only to national firms in which
\par
locally qualified professionals have majority or full
\par
ownership. Consequently, PwC member firms are
\par
locally owned and managed.'' 11
\par
In other words, even for the global accounting firms,
\par
each country constitutes a unique practice and audit mar-
\par
ket. The autonomy of the country-specific partnerships
\par
within the PricewaterhouseCoopers network is under-
\par
scored by the following disclaimer found on each country's
\par
website (e.g., http://www.pwc.co.uk/):
\par
``PricewaterhouseCoopers and PwC refer to the network
\par
of member firms of PricewaterhouseCoopers Interna-
\par
tional Limited (PwCIL). Each member firm is a separate
\par
legal entity and does not act as agent of PwCIL or any
\par
other member firm. Neither PwCIL nor any member
\par
firm is responsible or liable for the acts or omissions
\par
of any other member firm nor control the exercise of
\par
another member firm's professional judgment or bind
\par
another member firm or PwCIL in any way.''
\par
Given the autonomy of these country-specific partner-
\par
ships, and the uniqueness of institutions within a country
\par
that affect the education, licensing, regulation of auditors,
\par
in addition to the more subtle effects of culture, why would
\par
we expect auditing practices to be the same around the
\par
world? In other words, context not only matters, it may
\par
11 The website goes onto explain that ``The member firms are linked
\par
together through membership in PwC International Limited (PwCIL), a UK
\par
membership-based company. PwCIL does not provide services to clients. Its
\par
primary activities are to: identify broad market opportunities and develop
\par
associated strategies; strengthen PwC's internal product, skill, and knowl-
\par
edge networks; promote the PwC brand; and develop and work for the
\par
consistent application of common risk and quality standards by member
\par
firms, including compliance with independence processes.''322 J.R. Francis / Accounting, Organizations and Society 36 (2011) 318--323
\par
well be critical to assessing audits. Consider the role of pro-
\par
fessional skepticism in auditing (Nelson, 2009). While
\par
skepticism is central to the practice of auditing in Anglo-
\par
American cultures, it is less clear if skepticism can operate
\par
as freely in cultures where conflict is openly avoided and
\par
cooperation is the norm.
\par
Let me turn now to some examples closer to my own
\par
audit research. For the past 20 years I have shown that
\par
audits by large accounting firms are not homogenous
\par
across countries, and that even within a single country
\par
there is variation in audit quality across individual practice
\par
offices. Francis and Wang (2008) draw on the law and fi-
\par
nance literature (La Porta, Lopez-de-Silanes, \& Shleifer,
\par
2006) and investigate the effects of cross-country differ-
\par
ences in auditing due to differences in legal systems and
\par
other institutions that protect investor rights. They find
\par
that there is no difference in the quality of audits by Big
\par
Four and non-Big Four auditors when legal systems and
\par
institutions are weak. However, when legal regimes and
\par
institutions that protect investors become stronger, includ-
\par
ing the punishment of auditors for low-quality audits, they
\par
find that Big Four audit quality improves relative to non-
\par
Big Four auditors, where audit quality is inferred from
\par
the quality of the clients' audited earnings. Michas (in
\par
press) looks at another aspect of institutional differences
\par
across countries, and that is the level of the accounting
\par
profession's development based on World Bank ROSC Re-
\par
ports and information on the International Federation of
\par
Accountants website. For a sample of 15 developing coun-
\par
tries, he finds that the quality of auditing improves when
\par
the country's level of professional development is greater,
\par
and that this effect exists over and above the legal and
\par
institutional characteristics documented in Francis and
\par
Wang (2008).
\par
Turning to inter-office differences within a country,
\par
there are two documented sources of office-level varia-
\par
tion: the engagement office's level of expertise in the cli-
\par
ent's industry (Reichelt \& Wang, 2010), and the overall
\par
size of the engagement office, the argument being that
\par
larger offices have greater human capital to draw on in
\par
staffing engagements (Francis \& Yu, 2009). While there
\par
is strong evidence of inter-office variation in audit out-
\par
comes, it is difficult to fully understand the sources of
\par
these differences because of a lack of published data
\par
about accounting firms' internal organizational structures
\par
and operations. Barrett, Cooper, and Jamal (2005) over-
\par
come this data limitation through a field study which
\par
examines the coordination of a large audit over multiple
\par
practice offices.
\par
Let me close with the auditor--client negotiation process
\par
which may well be the most crucial aspect of the audit
\par
engagement. How auditor--client disagreements are re-
\par
solved will determine the final numbers in audited finan-
\par
cial statements. Most research on this topic is country
\par
specific. For example, Gibbins, McCracken, and Salterio
\par
(2010) examine negotiation within a Canadian setting,
\par
yet we know from a large literature that negotiation strat-
\par
egies and tactics vary across cultures (Carnevale \& Leung,
\par
2002; Triandis, 1994). Simply put, what we learn about
\par
the auditor--client negotiation in one country setting does
\par
not necessarily have relevance in other countries. The
\par
same is likely to be true for the ISO standards and other
\par
global certification activities.
\par
What I am suggesting is the need for comparative or
\par
cross-country audit research in order to learn how the par-
\par
ticular institutions and culture of a country affect auditing
\par
practices. When one investigates auditing a single country,
\par
be it an experiment, archival study, field study, or a discur-
\par
sive analysis, in essence it represents a sample of ``one'' in
\par
terms of national culture and institutions. To more deeply
\par
understand the role of country-specific characteristics that
\par
shape auditing practices one must directly compare two or
\par
more countries.
\par
There are two points of this essay. First, we can benefit
\par
from a more expansive view of auditing without borders,
\par
one that considers the commonalities of financial state-
\par
ment audits with other certification practices. We can both
\par
learn from and potentially contribute to other research lit-
\par
eratures. At the same time, context is also important. That
\par
is, we also need to examine closely the specific contexts in
\par
which auditing and other certification practices take place.
\par
In particular I conjecture that a major cause of the varia-
\par
tion in audit quality (and perhaps the quality of other
\par
3rd party certifications as well) is the specific institutional
\par
and cultural setting of a particular certification practice,
\par
and the effects that these have on the incentives and
\par
behavior of certification experts. We know very little about
\par
these contextual effects, even for financial statement
\par
audits.
