@article{garrisonjrInnovationDiagnosticImaging2011,
  title = {Innovation in Diagnostic Imaging Services: Assessing the Potential for Value-Based Reimbursement},
  shorttitle = {Innovation in Diagnostic Imaging Services},
  author = {Garrison Jr, Louis P. and Bresnahan, Brian W. and Higashi, Mitchell K. and Hollingworth, William and Jarvik, Jeffrey G.},
  year = 2011,
  journal = {Academic Radiology},
  volume = {18},
  number = {9},
  pages = {1109--1114},
  publisher = {Elsevier},
  doi = {10.1016/j.acra.2011.04.007},
  langid = {english},
  note = {Innovation in Diagnostic Imaging
\par
Services:
\par
Assessing the Potential for Value-based Reimbursement
\par
Louis P. Garrison, Jr, PhD, Brian W. Bresnahan, PhD, Mitchell K. Higashi, PhD,
\par
William Hollingworth, PhD, Jeffrey G. Jarvik, MD, MPH
\par
Innovation in the field of diagnostic imaging is based primarily on the availability of new and improved equipment that opens the door for
\par
new clinical applications. Payments for these imaging procedures are subject to complex Medicare price control schemes, affecting incen-
\par
tives for appropriate use and innovation. Achieving a ``dynamically efficient'' health care system---one that elicits a socially optimal amount
\par
of innovation---requires that innovators be rewarded in relation to the value they add and can demonstrate with evidence. The authors
\par
examine how and whether value-based reimbursement for diagnostic imaging services might better reward innovation explicitly for
\par
expected improvements in health and economic outcomes.
\par
Key Words: Innovation; diagnostic imaging; cost-effectiveness; price controls; value-based reimbursement; value-based pricing;
\par
dynamic efficiency.
\par
ªAUR, 2011
\par
In a 1984 Wall Street Journal op-ed piece, Alan Greenspan
\par
(1), then head of the National Commission on Social
\par
Security Reform, predicted the eventual collapse of
\par
Medicare's newly instituted prospective payment system,
\par
which set flat fees for hospital stays in some 470 different
\par
diagnosis-related groups: ``The major difficulty with the
\par
prospective payment system is that it has the faults of any
\par
price-control scheme and is therefore likely to go the way
\par
of similar initiatives of the past..Irresistible pressures begin
\par
to create exemptions to the control system, and eventually
\par
the exemptions become the system. Then it is only a matter
\par
of time before the control system unravels.''
\par
Well, Medicare's two major national price-control schemes
\par
for hospital and physician-related services, both of which
\par
provide reimbursement for diagnostic imaging interpretation
\par
and equipment, have lasted 28 and 19 years, respectively, and
\par
neither appears to be on the verge of imminent failure. Green-
\par
span (1) was particularly wary about the ability of such ``admin-
\par
istered pricing''systems to deal with technological innovations,
\par
claiming, ``The momentum of technology will require too
\par
many special cases.'' But Medicare has developed ongoing
\par
Acad Radiol 2011; 18:1109--1114
\par
From the Department of Pharmacy (L.P.G.), the Department of Radiology
\par
(B.W.B.), and the Comparative Effectiveness, Cost, and Outcomes Research
\par
Center (J.G.J.), University of Washington, Seattle, WA; GE Healthcare,
\par
Waukesha, Wisconsin (M.K.H.); the School of Social and Community
\par
Medicine, University of Bristol, Bristol, United Kingdom (W.H.). Received
\par
January 7, 2011; accepted April 20, 2011. Address correspondence to:
\par
B.W.B. e-mail: bres@u.washington.edu
\par
ªAUR, 2011
\par
doi:10.1016/j.acra.2011.04.007
\par
processes for revisions and updating as service provision
\par
changes. Nonetheless, Greenspan was right that such price-
\par
control schemes can create distortions and incentives that
\par
may promote inappropriate care as well as hinder innovation.
\par
Because technological innovation does not occur equally in
\par
all types of health care services, price controls may affect some
\par
areas more than others. We focus here on the rapidly growing
\par
field of diagnostic imaging procedures. They are offered in
\par
a variety of settings and are now subject to a complex set of
\par
payment schemes under Medicare. In this paper, we examine
\par
these incentives and explore the feasibility and possible impact
\par
of an alternative approach that rewards innovation by basing
\par
facility payments for imaging procedures on the expected
\par
improvements in health and economic outcomes resulting
\par
from those procedures.
\par
The motivation for this exploration derives from three
\par
notable trends. First, total Medicare spending on imaging nearly
\par
doubled from \$6.4 billion in 2000 to \$12 billion in 2006,
\par
leading Congress to make broad cuts in diagnostic imaging
\par
payment levels in the Deficit Reduction Act of 2005. These
\par
are not nuanced cuts applied to specific procedures, raising
\par
concerns about the short-term impact on access to specific
\par
imaging procedures and the long-term incentives for innova-
\par
tion. Second, there are increasing calls for greater use of
\par
evidence-based medicine, including studies of comparative
\par
effectiveness and cost-effectiveness. Third, the ongoing debate
\par
about value-based pricing for drugs in the United Kingdom
\par
raises general issues about how best to establish payment for
\par
innovative medical interventions, leading us to speculate on
\par
lessons for innovative diagnostic imaging in the United States.
\par
1109GARRISON JR ET AL ECONOMICS OF MEDICAL PRICE CONTROLS
\par
AND INNOVATION
\par
A key aim of medical price controls is to simulate or approx-
\par
imate the outcome of a market, whereby competitive forces
\par
tend to push prices to the level of ``long-run marginal cost''
\par
(ie, what it costs to make something) but including a compet-
\par
itive rate of return for any investments made or for any risks
\par
incurred. At this quantity-price point, demand and supply
\par
are in equilibrium, and value (what consumers are willing to
\par
pay) and cost are identical.
\par
Technological innovation raises special issues for such
\par
administered pricing systems. Innovation involves invest-
\par
ments---research and development---that create new knowl-
\par
edge, a ``public good'' in economic jargon, which would be
\par
undersupplied from a social point of view without interven-
\par
tions, such as the creation of intellectual property rights via
\par
patents (2). In fields with high rates of technological innova-
\par
tion, such as diagnostic imaging, inflexible fixed payments
\par
become a constraint on the rewards the inventor can expect
\par
to receive. Unless some special allowance is made for innova-
\par
tions---and systems established by the Centers for Medicare
\par
and Medicaid Services have various mechanisms that attempt
\par
this---the rate of innovation is likely to be inhibited. This is
\par
what economists call ``dynamic inefficiency,'' a socially subop-
\par
timal rate of innovation (3,4).
\par
DIAGNOSTIC IMAGING TECHNICAL SERVICES
\par
AND THE RESOURCE-BASED RELATIVE VALUE
\par
SCALE
\par
Current Medicare reimbursement for diagnostic imaging
\par
services is essentially a side product of a system designed
\par
primarily to reimburse physician services: the Resource-
\par
Based Relative Value Scale (RBRVS). Payment, assigned to
\par
roughly 9600 Current Procedural Terminology (CPT) codes,
\par
is divided into three parts---physician work, practice expenses,
\par
and professional liability---and is adjusted geographically.1
\par
About 600 of the codes apply to diagnostic imaging.
\par
For the technical component of diagnostic imaging, the
\par
weights are based on surveys of practice expenses that use
\par
reported charges for equipment and on assumptions (eg, about
\par
utilization rates and interest costs). Importantly, these weights
\par
do not in any way take into account the health benefits of new
\par
or existing imaging technologies.
\par
In 1988, the architects of the Medicare RBRVS system,
\par
Professor William Hsiao and his colleagues (6), remarked, in
\par
discussing the limitations of the system, ``Lastly, the RBRVS
\par
measures only resource inputs: the benefits of services are
\par
not considered--a rational payment system should recognize
\par
social benefits in the relative value. Perhaps this topic should
\par
1 The system is more complicated than this implies, because the Centers for
\par
Medicare and Medicaid Services include the CPT codes as level I of a broader
\par
coding system called the Healthcare Common Procedural Coding System,
\par
which is also used to identify new imaging procedures (5).
\par
1110
\par
Academic Radiology, Vol 18, No 9, September 2011
\par
have a high priority in future research.'' Hsiao et al were
\par
considering the entire system of physician-related services,
\par
not just diagnostic imaging. However, the consequences of
\par
this shortcoming may be greater in fields with high rates of
\par
innovation.
\par
For purposes of this discussion, the benefits being over-
\par
looked are included in what we call the ``economic value,''
\par
which represents what subscribers would be willing to pay
\par
and would be a sum equivalent to the value of (1) any mone-
\par
tary savings from imaging due to the reduced need for other
\par
medical services, (2) any reduction in mortality, (3) any
\par
improvement in morbidity (ie, health-related quality of life),
\par
and (4) any reduction in the uncertainty of diagnosis or
\par
outcomes (including that due to a higher quality evidence
\par
base). The first three of these are the traditional elements of
\par
most static cost-effectiveness analyses. The last element is
\par
less recognized and more difficult to estimate, but it implies
\par
that imaging may also provide some intrinsic benefits to
\par
patients that are not reflected in improvements in health (7).
\par
Jena and Philipson (8) pointed out that this concept of value
\par
is too narrow when it comes to considering long-term,
\par
dynamic efficiency. This is a static concept, and its compo-
\par
nents define what economists call the ``social surplus'' in the
\par
short term. This social surplus---the value that citizens ulti-
\par
mately place on innovations---can be split between patients
\par
(``consumer surplus'') and manufacturers (``producer
\par
surplus''). Jena and Philipson argue that dynamic efficiency
\par
will require maximizing the amount of the surplus that goes
\par
to producers and that this will move us closer to dynamic effi-
\par
ciency. In the long term, we are concerned with eliciting an
\par
optimal rate of innovation, reflecting as well, for example,
\par
spillovers of this knowledge to other areas of invention.
\par
INNOVATION IN MEDICINE: PHARMACEUTICALS
\par
VERSUS DIAGNOSTIC IMAGING
\par
In identifying the key incentives unique to diagnostic
\par
imaging, it is helpful to compare and contrast with the impor-
\par
tant economic features of innovative pharmaceuticals.
\par
Although both involve the interaction of physicians with
\par
medical technology to improve patients' health, there are
\par
some important differences economically. Newhouse (9)
\par
insightfully identified the following key features of innovative
\par
pharmaceuticals: the role of sunk costs, the grant of a tempo-
\par
rary legal monopoly, and the high commercial risk involved.
\par
All of these combine to result in a ``blockbuster'' model of
\par
financing, in which only a minority of highly profitable prod-
\par
ucts subsidize the research and development costs of the less
\par
successful innovations. And the short-term monopolies
\par
provided via patents allow innovators to capture value above
\par
the short-run marginal cost to recover the sunk costs.
\par
In comparison, for diagnostic imaging technologies, most
\par
(though not all) innovation is generally seen as incremental
\par
on an existing imaging platform or modality. Second, although
\par
imaging equipment manufacturers also patent many aspects of
\par
their innovations, the rapid pace of innovation often rendersAcademic Radiology, Vol 18, No 9, September 2011 VALUE-BASED REIMBURSEMENT FOR DIAGNOSTIC IMAGING
\par
these advances outdated within 2 to 3 years. New solutions can
\par
often be engineered without infringing on intellectual prop-
\par
erty. Manufacturers counter this rate of obsolescence by
\par
providing upgrades and other services to their customers. A
\par
large percentage of drugs fail even in final phase 3 trials,
\par
whereas most imaging innovations launched into the market-
\par
place do not typically fail on scientific or technical grounds.
\par
And because of the perceived lower risk-benefit profile for
\par
patients, they generally are not required to undergo long or
\par
expensive clinical trials for final approval. Thus, on average,
\par
they are less risky investments for their manufacturers. Finally,
\par
although manufacturers determine prices for the sale or leasing
\par
of capital equipment, the reimbursement rates for specific
\par
procedures are set by Medicare and private payers.
\par
Payments to radiologists and nonradiologists for interpreting
\par
images are called the ``professional component'' and are gener-
\par
ally the same regardless of the site of delivery. Payments for the
\par
``technical component'' cover the ``practice expenses'' of using
\par
imaging equipment, technicians' time, practice overhead, and
\par
so on, and vary by site of care. These practice expenses for
\par
inpatient hospital care are covered under the diagnosis-
\par
related group payment. For hospital outpatient care, practice
\par
expenses are covered by a preset fee for the appropriate ambu-
\par
latory payment classification, of which there are approxi-
\par
mately 800 items. The majority of payments for technical
\par
components of imaging are still made according to the
\par
RBRVS.
\par
THE IMPACT OF PRICE CONTROLS ON
\par
INNOVATION AND PRODUCT QUALITY
\par
Price controls can have the somewhat surprising consequence
\par
of almost determining the type and level of innovation. In
\par
a competitive marketplace with several suppliers, equipment
\par
manufacturers would tend to increase the quality of a delivered
\par
scan up to a level that the reimbursement level can support:
\par
price, representing the payer's willingness to pay, would then
\par
essentially dictate the quality of the product.
\par
Incentives and Standards for Appropriate Use
\par
Fee-for-service reimbursement systems are a double-edged
\par
sword. On one side, by paying on a piecework basis, they
\par
encourage greater productivity. At the same time, they create
\par
a tendency to push service provision into a zone of ``inappro-
\par
priate'' use, where the expected costs to society may exceed
\par
the expected health benefits to the patient. Hackbarth (10)
\par
cited the example of uncomplicated low back pain, for which
\par
nearly one quarter of patients received ``unnecessary imaging
\par
services, based on the ACR [American College of Radiology]
\par
standard.'' The ACR has developed a process for establishing
\par
guidelines for appropriate use, relying on expert panels and
\par
their subjective judgments to define guidelines for appropriate
\par
use. Within the profession, there is a debate on whether to
\par
replace or how to enhance this using the tools of evidence-
\par
based medicine (11,12).
\par
PROBLEMS WITH REIMBURSEMENT FOR
\par
DIAGNOSTIC IMAGING TECHNICAL SERVICES
\par
Our concern here is specifically about the reimbursement for
\par
diagnostic imaging technical services, which is the mechanism
\par
by which we indirectly reward innovative equipment manu-
\par
facturers. Besides hindering the incentive to innovate, we
\par
see the current system as overly complex and ill suited to
\par
encouraging appropriate adoption and use as well as the
\par
appropriate level of innovation, including prelaunch and post-
\par
launch data collection.
\par
In testimony before a health subcommittee of the US
\par
House of Representatives in 2006, Glenn Hackbarth (10),
\par
chairman of the Medicare Payment Advisory Committee,
\par
noted that there is wide variation in the use of imaging
\par
services across the country and that ``at least some imaging
\par
services are overused.'' He also provided a litany of other
\par
issues, including conflict of interest in the ownership of
\par
imaging facilities, the migration of services to physician
\par
offices, provider quality, equipment standards, and the lack
\par
of a correlation between the volume of use and health
\par
outcomes, among others. We cannot address all of these issues
\par
here, but we do note that ``inappropriate overutilization'' also
\par
provides rewards to innovators and may therefore counteract
\par
suboptimal reimbursement for specific procedures.
\par
Complexity
\par
Payments for imaging under Medicare are a complex mixture
\par
of schemes that vary depending on the site of service.
\par
AN ECONOMIC APPROACH TO VALUE-BASED
\par
REIMBURSEMENT
\par
Defining Value-based Reimbursement
\par
``Value-based reimbursement,'' as used here, means that
\par
payments or reimbursements are based the economic value
\par
generated rather than on the short-run marginal cost of
\par
supplying a good or service. This value would include at least
\par
the four components mentioned above but could also include
\par
other adjustments suggested by the dynamic efficiency
\par
considerations highlighted by Jena and Philipson (3,8),
\par
recognizing that the allocation of social surplus to producers
\par
is critical to stimulate innovation.
\par
UK Debate on Value-based Pricing for Drugs
\par
The appraisal of drugs and other technologies by the National
\par
Institute for Health and Clinical Excellence (NICE) in the
\par
United Kingdom is the most salient example of a payer
\par
attempting to estimate and use economic value consistently
\par
in a value-based coverage decision process. For each tech-
\par
nology appraised, NICE estimates the projected incremental
\par
value, usually measured with the ``quality-adjusted life-year,''
\par
1111GARRISON JR ET AL and compares it to the estimated incremental cost. In 2007,
\par
the Office of Fair Trading in the United Kingdom proposed
\par
that NICE should extend the concept even further to calcu-
\par
late a value-based price that would represent what their
\par
society would be willing to pay for a new drug (or other tech-
\par
nology) (13). In the ensuing 3 years, with a change in govern-
\par
ment, this approach has received new impetus (14,15).
\par
One important insight for diagnostic imaging that has come
\par
out of this debate is the importance of the ``value of informa-
\par
tion'' on how well a technology works. For example, two
\par
alternative imaging modalities need to be compared not just
\par
on technical performance characteristics but also on the
\par
amount and quality of the data they have to demonstrate
\par
economic value (and health impact).
\par
A second important insight emerging from this discussion is
\par
the role of nonlinear or differential pricing and its relation to
\par
who captures the value of innovation. Patients with different
\par
characteristics, including diseases, would benefit differentially
\par
from the same imaging procedure. Setting the value-based
\par
price requires a definition of the covered group and effectively
\par
determines how much of the value added goes to the manu-
\par
facturer as an incentive.
\par
More recently, with the change in government in the
\par
United Kingdom, there is new vision for the future of its
\par
National Health Service that includes a commitment to
\par
move toward a system of value-based pricing for medicines.
\par
This vision, as laid out in recent consultation document,
\par
includes an objective to ``stimulate innovation'' (16).
\par
Economic Models for the Evaluation of Diagnostic
\par
Imaging
\par
As in other areas of medicine, both comparative effectiveness
\par
research and cost-effectiveness analysis are being actively
\par
promoted as appropriate methods to assess value for new
\par
health technologies in the real world (17). Most economic
\par
evaluations of pharmaceuticals are based on randomized trials
\par
that do not evaluate products in a head-to-head fashion; they
\par
are not based on comparative, real-world effectiveness data.
\par
Instead, mathematical models are used to synthesize the avail-
\par
able evidence from both efficacy trials and real-world settings.
\par
The need for modeling will be even greater for economic
\par
evaluations of imaging, given the high cost of conducting
\par
long-term follow-up studies and a much faster pace of inno-
\par
vation that renders the technology outdated during the time it
\par
would take to conduct a trial. However, this is not to say that
\par
we should not do a great deal more to conduct trials before
\par
launch and collect evidence after launch on the effectiveness
\par
on imaging.
\par
In 2009, an Institute of Medicine report (18) provided
\par
recommendations on the top 100 national priorities in the
\par
United States for comparative effectiveness research. The
\par
institute's top-tier priorities included recommendations for
\par
additional comparative studies of advanced imaging modali-
\par
ties used in oncology, specifically computed tomography,
\par
magnetic resonance imaging, and positron emission tomog-
\par
1112
\par
Academic Radiology, Vol 18, No 9, September 2011
\par
raphy. A total of nine topic areas relevant to imaging
\par
approaches were included in the top 100 priorities. Most of
\par
these nine areas included recommendations to compare
\par
multiple imaging modalities used in specific indications
\par
(19). In 2010, the Patient Protection and Affordable Care
\par
Act established the Patient-Centered Outcomes Research
\par
Institute in the United States, whose mission is to ``support
\par
the production of well-validated scientific evidence to assist
\par
the nation in making informed decisions about a broad range
\par
of health care-related issues'' (20). Given the prominence of
\par
imaging in patient care, it is reasonable to expect that imaging
\par
approaches used in screening, diagnosing, and staging
\par
different health conditions will be among the comparative
\par
effectiveness research topics the Patient-Centered Outcomes
\par
Research Institute recommends for additional health tech-
\par
nology assessments and/or modeling studies.
\par
A WAY FORWARD FOR CONSIDERING VALUE
\par
Given that the current payment levels for diagnostic imaging
\par
procedures are probably only weakly correlated or aligned
\par
with incremental value delivered by diagnostic imaging, we
\par
see three major options for initiating a value-oriented reform
\par
for imaging services: (1) a comprehensive, systemwide reval-
\par
uation of all imaging procedures relying on existing CPT
\par
codes; (2) a limited revaluation of the high-volume and
\par
high-cost procedures, estimating the incremental value of
\par
the most frequently used procedures in homogenous clinical
\par
groups (ie, using CPT and diagnostic codes to define groups);
\par
and (3) establishing a procedure for evaluating and valuing
\par
new, rapidly emerging ``innovative'' imaging procedures.
\par
The initial design and implementation of Medicare's
\par
current hospital and physician schemes required significant
\par
time and resource investments in research to design and
\par
implement. Although they are adjusted over time, there has
\par
been no comprehensive effort to reestimate their fundamental
\par
parameters. The costs of rebasing may just be too high given
\par
their size and complexity.
\par
Limited Evidence Base
\par
According to the Tufts Registry of Cost-Effectiveness Anal-
\par
ysis, the mean number of imaging-related cost-utility analyses
\par
published per year grew from 1.55 in the period from 1985 to
\par
1995 to 9.4 between 1996 and 2005. Thus, the number of
\par
evaluations is small in relation to the number of imaging appli-
\par
cations and to their cost and health impact. Furthermore, the
\par
quality of many of these analyses is questionable (21). There-
\par
fore, we do not currently have a sufficient evidence base for an
\par
across-the-board shift from a resource-based to a value-based
\par
imaging payment system.
\par
