\par
CONCLUSIONS: DEVELOPING A RESEARCH
\par
AGENDA
\par
The development and implementation of the RBRVS and
\par
other Medicare fee schedules took many years of research
\par
and millions of dollars, and these schedules require continual
\par
updating and revisions. Although there are many issues to be
\par
addressed, we believe it is clear that the current payment
\par
scheme for imaging services has a significant potential to
\par
inhibit the optimal rate and composition of innovation in
\par
imaging.
\par
More than 20 years ago, Hsiao et al (6) called for a consid-
\par
eration of value in setting fees, a call for research that has gone
\par
unheeded but deserves to be rejuvenated. We need more
\par
studies of the incremental value of specific existing imaging
\par
innovations and of emerging innovations, and we need to
\par
study how or whether the current incentives affect the rate
\par
of innovation. We should also begin with some more explicit
\par
1113GARRISON JR ET AL ``experiments'' or demonstrations, such as the Centers for
\par
Medicare and Medicaid Services process of coverage with
\par
evidence development, to assess value delivered as a basis for
\par
payment levels for new and emerging imaging procedures.
\par
We could, for example, offer manufacturers unique codes
\par
for new imaging procedures and a value-based reimbursement
\par
as an incentive for providing more data at launch.
\par
Is a value-based Medicare payment system for imaging
\par
feasible or desirable? We believe that it would be technically
\par
feasible and could be an improvement over the existing
\par
system. However, we do not currently have sufficient
\par
evidence to establish the baseline clinical and economic value
\par
for the current high-volume and high-cost diagnostic imaging
\par
procedures. Alan Greenspan (1) warned us 27 years ago about
\par
the threat that price controls pose for innovation. Despite the
\par
apparently high level of innovation and spending growth in
\par
imaging, doubts remain how effectively our diagnostic
\par
imaging reimbursement system has channeled that innovation
\par
process and directed use as a consequence.
\par
Given the billions of dollars spent annually on diagnostic
\par
imaging and given the obvious benefits of making the right
\par
diagnosis in almost any clinical situation, it is hard to imagine
\par
how we could justify spending so little on the clinical and
\par
economic evaluation of these important technologies. We
\par
can ``push'' these evaluations via government funding or
\par
``pull'' them with incentives and rewards for demonstrating
\par
the value of innovation and of information. Both approaches
\par
are warranted.
