\par
A B S T R A C T
\par
We propose a flexible framework for evaluating prospect de-
\par
pendencies in oil and gas exploration and for solving decision-
\par
making problems in this context. The model uses a Bayesian
\par
network (BN) for encoding the dependencies in a geologic sys-
\par
tem at source, reservoir, and trap levels. We discuss different
\par
evaluation criteria that allow us to formulate specific decision
\par
problems and solve these within the BN framework. The BN
\par
model offers a realistic graphic model for capturing the under-
\par
lying causal geologic process and allows fast statistical compu-
\par
tations of marginal and conditional probabilities.
\par
We illustrate the use of our BN model by considering two
\par
situations. In the first situation, we wish to gain information
\par
about an area where hydrocarbons have been discovered, and
\par
use the value of perfect information to determine which lo-
\par
cations are the best to drill. In the second situation, we con-
\par
sider the problem of abandoning an area when only dry wells
\par
are drilled. For this latter, we use an abandoned revenue cri-
\par
terion to determine the drilling locations.
\par
The application is from the North Sea. Our main focus is
\par
the description, visualization, and interpretation of the results
\par
for relating the statistical modeling to the local understanding
\par
of the geology.
\par
