\par
This work has been performed using the Bayes
\par
Net Toolbox (Murphy, 2007) for Matlab\textregistered{} and the
\par
network package of R.
\par
The AAPG Editor thanks the two anonymous re-
\par
viewers for their work on this paper.Begg, 2008), that the introduction of a prospect-
\par
interdependency perspective may result in a sub-
\par
stantial difference when discussing a sequential dril-
\par
ling program or an expected portfolio evaluation.
\par
The prospect dependencies entail information
\par
about one prospect that leads to information about
\par
other prospects. This is useful in decision making, if
\par
one plans to drill the best two prospects. The best
\par
two prospects might not be the top two ranked
\par
prospects a priori because the dependence can cause
\par
two a priori quite independent prospects to carry
\par
more information in total.
\par
All dependencies between prospects come
\par
from geologic processes. A general model of de-
\par
pendency should consider all the geologic elements
\par
that are needed for oil and gas to accumulate in
\par
sufficient quantities to be worth producing. These
\par
elements include an organic-rich source rock to
\par
generate the oil or gas, a porous reservoir rock to
\par
store the hydrocarbons (HCs), and a trap to prevent
\par
the oil and gas from leaking. The source, reservoir,
\par
and trap at one prospect might share some features
\par
with those of other prospects. One discriminant
\par
factor could be the distance (prospects nearby are
\par
more likely to be interdependent), but commonly,
\par
the geologic constraints and conditions that have
\par
developed through time are more representative. A
\par
complex model of interdependencies can therefore
\par
be developed, as assessed in Bickel et al. (2008). In
\par
this work, we focus our attention on the source
\par
dependencies, which is the most interesting ele-
\par
ment for our case study. The procedure is valid for
\par
all three factors, and they could be joined at the
\par
prospect level.
\par
Within each prospect, there may be several
\par
segments, which are the potential reservoirs that
\par
can be chosen for drilling. Segments belonging to
\par
the same prospect can be distinguished by the depth
\par
(i.e., different formations), by the relative position
\par
with respect to some geologic element (i.e., on
\par
either side of a major fault), or by some other local
\par
geographic distinction.
\par
A priori geologic information can be encoded
\par
in a qualitative part and a quantitative part. Here,
\par
the qualitative part contains a list and map of pros-
\par
pects, segments, and sources (kitchens), that is, areas
\par
where the HC formation developed. At sources-
\par
1424 Bayesian Networks for Prospect Analysis in the North Sea
\par
segments-prospects, we also know the possible out-
\par
comes (oil, gas, dry). It further includes a road map
\par
of the area, indicating the migration paths of HC
\par
between sources-segments-prospects. The quan-
\par
titative part is described by probabilities, where the
\par
expert belief is translated to specific numbers. This
\par
is assigned by conditional probabilities, stating the
\par
likelihood of an outcome at one node, depending
\par
on the outcomes of its parents.
\par
Bayesian networks (BNs) (Pearl, 1986; Jensen,
\par
1996) are attractive models for encoding qualita-
\par
tive and quantitative information. Van Wees et al.
\par
(2008) presented a BN model for prospect depen-
\par
dencies, where the dependence is essentially mod-
\par
eled as a function of the distance. In our process, the
\par
involvement of local geology experts in the network-
\par
building phase allowed us to overcome this sim-
\par
plification and to generate a more reliable model.
\par
The BN model provides a flexible dependency
\par
structure between the prospects that is not only
\par
based on the spatial distance, but instead includes a
\par
complex geologic modeling. A good understanding
\par
of the geologic mechanisms that govern HC mi-
\par
gration is a prerequisite to building an effective BN
\par
model. It is well known that the integration be-
\par
tween simulation results or observed data with ex-
\par
perts' opinions can sensibly improve the quality and
\par
the predictive ability of the network. For instance,
\par
biological applications in Imoto et al. (2003) suc-
\par
cessfully integrate a priori expert biological knowl-
\par
edge with the microarray information. Similarly, an
\par
ecological case study presented in Hamilton et al.
\par
(2005) uses expert knowledge to show how the
\par
development of a bacterium and its interaction
\par
with the environment can be analyzed. The ex-
\par
pert knowledge can be used both in the definition
\par
of the possible dependencies and in the quantifi-
\par
cation of the conditional probability tables (CPTs).
\par
The Bayesian (Belief) network becomes an effec-
\par
tive framework for integrating various contributions.
\par
We present a BN model based on a set of
\par
prospects in the Norwegian part of the North Sea.
\par
The general purpose is to build a reliable and co-
\par
herent network for source, reservoir, and trap. Our
\par
main focus is on the source network, which is the
\par
most complex model for the geologic situation. The
\par
idea is to use BNs to address decision questions inthe context of complex geologic interdependen-
\par
cies. In particular, we demonstrate the applicability
\par
of a BN model for what-if scenarios and static ex-
\par
ploration strategies. By what-if scenarios, we imag-
\par
ine knowing the outcome at one or more prospects
\par
and study how this information propagates to the
\par
other prospects. For the exploration strategy, we
\par
compute which fixed number of segments are best to
\par
drill to maximize some utility criterion, see for ex-
\par
ample, Smalley et al. (2008), Cunningham and Begg
\par
(2008), Eidsvik et al. (2008), and Bhattacharjya
\par
et al. (2010). Note that we do not attempt to find
\par
the optimal exploration sequence, instead, we use
\par
various criteria to study the impact of different kinds
\par
of evidence. Finding the optimal exploration se-
\par
quence (Bickel and Smith, 2006) is a huge combi-
\par
natorial challenge in our model with 25 prospects.
\par
The first proposed criterion is the value of
\par
perfect information (VoPI), which assumes that
\par
information only has value if it can change our de-
\par
cisions. In our case, the decision is which segments
\par
to drill. The second criterion we propose is the
\par
abandoned revenue (AR), which is a modification
\par
of the VoPI. The AR is intended for areas with no
\par
proven successes, and the challenge is to minimize
\par
the possible loss by leaving the area under dry
\par
evidence.
\par
Figure 1. Bayesian network. The K nodes
\par
represent kitchens, the P nodes represent
\par
the prospects, whereas the bottom nodes of
\par
the network, marked with numbers and
\par
letters, are the segments.
\par
We explain the BN model obtained by geologic
\par
constraints in Building the Model. In Exploratory
\par
Analysis, we present what-if examples to provide
\par
insight to the model. In Exploration Strategies, we
\par
propose and discuss VoPI and AR for selection of
\par
segments in exploration drilling. A brief discussion
\par
of the benefits and limitations of our approach
\par
and of possible improvements is given in Closing
\par
Remarks.
\par
BUILDING THE MODEL
\par
In this section, we build a model with prospect
\par
dependencies. We first describe BNs, then build a
\par
network model for the source dependencies.
\par
A BN (Jensen, 1996) consists of a set of nodes
\par
and directed edges and associated conditional prob-
\par
ability distributions. A BN model is a flexible tool
\par
for establishing causal relationships between the
\par
nodes, here representing geologically defined pros-
\par
pects. The BNs are graphs G {$\frac{1}{4}$} fV ; Eg, where V is
\par
the set of nodes and E is a subset of the set V \texttimes{} V
\par
of ordered pairs of vertices, called the edges or
\par
links of G. The BN we use for the source variable
\par
in our North Sea case study is presented in Figure 1.
\par
The circles are nodes, whereas the arrows are edges.
