Bayesian Network: Qualitative Part.
\par
We divide the model presentation into two
\par
parts: qualitative and quantitative. This duality is
\par
fictitious, but it reflects the way we built the net-
\par
work itself, which led us to design the network first
\par
and then to design a method to define the CPTs.
\par
In particular, the qualitative part describes which
\par
geologic elements enter the model and how they are
\par
connected. The quantitative part states the prob-
\par
ability model for all nodes, which denote the strength
\par
of geologic expert belief attributed to the different
\par
nodes and edges of the network.
\par
Bayesian Network: Qualitative Part
\par
We focus on the source part for two reasons. First,
\par
the reservoir quality is``excellent'' in a large part of
\par
the field, as studies have shown, see for example,
\par
Ramm and Ryseth (1996). Second, the source net-
\par
work is``rich'' from a BN point of view. It contains
\par
some interesting prospect dependencies, whereas
\par
the currently used network for the trap applies to
\par
the local segment level.
\par
We use a discrete model with three possible
\par
states for each node (dry, gas, or oil), representing
\par
the actual state of the source in that node. Our
\par
network includes three possible kinds of nodes:
\par
{$\bullet$} Kitchens. We define the kitchens as areas where
\par
source rock has reached appropriate conditions
\par
of pressure and temperature to generate HCs. In
\par
our model, the kitchens are nodes that with
\par
probability 1 assume either the state``oil'' or the
\par
state ``
\par
gas.'' Kitchens are denoted by K in Figure 1.
\par
{$\bullet$} Prospects. We define prospects as structures
\par
that may contain HCs that have been fully eval-
\par
uated and are ready to drill; the prospects are the
\par
key node of the network because, through them,
\par
we can define the spatial relationships that we
\par
need. Prospects are denoted by P in Figure 1.
\par
{$\bullet$} Segments. The segments are the bottom nodes
\par
of our network, and they correspond to potential
\par
drilling sites associated with a certain prospect.
\par
For each prospect, we can have one or more
\par
segments that share part of the infrastructure
\par
needed for drilling and production. In Figure 1,
\par
1426 Bayesian Networks for Prospect Analysis in the North Sea
\par
segments inherit the number of their prospect,
\par
with alphabetical listing.
\par
The basic point of the analysis is a list of pros-
\par
pects, where great interest still exists in HC dis-
\par
coveries. For each segment, and therefore for each
\par
prospect, the following information is available
\par
given by a team of experts in local geology: mar-
\par
ginal probability of HC source; conditional prob-
\par
ability of gas given the presence of HC; list of
\par
possible paths (edges) for the gas and the oil mi-
\par
gration between different kitchens, prospects, and
\par
segments; expected volume, both for gas and for
\par
oil; expected monetary value of oil and gas; and
\par
expected operational costs.
\par
The three first points are needed for building
\par
the network, whereas the three last points concern
\par
the decision-making process. Other information is
\par
also available, such as age and formation. These were
\par
helpful when constructing edges and probabilities
\par
for the BN, but are not discussed further here.
\par
The edges represent expert geologists'
\par
as-
\par
sumptions of the prospect, segment, and kitchen
\par
connections. Within the BN formulations, the edges
\par
define a dependence structure for the nodes. For
\par
instance, in Figure 1, the prospect P4 has just one
\par
directed edge from the kitchen, K2, because a
\par
strong prior belief indicates a unique kitchen for
\par
this prospect. However, prospect P1 has directed
\par
edges from two kitchens, K1 and K3. The experts
\par
in local geology assume that the source can flow
\par
from any of these kitchens to provide HCs in
\par
prospect P1.
\par
A crucial feature of our BN is that segments
\par
never are parents, all connections are on higher
\par
levels. This allows for a local failure, where HC
\par
may have reached the prospect area, but not the
\par
actual segment we look at.
\par
We end up with a network of 42 connected
\par
nodes, where 4 are kitchens, 13 are prospects, and
\par
25 are segments (Figure 1). Each node, vi, has three
\par
possible states: dry, gas, or oil, that is,
\par
vi {$\frac{1}{4}$} s; i 2 f1;. . . ; 42g; s 2 fdry; gas; oilg
\par
We are particularly interested in the segment
\par
nodes, where we can drill an exploratory well tocollect an evidence of dry, gas, or oil. The evi-
\par
dence at one segment will affect the probability of
\par
outcomes at other segments, via the BN model.
\par
We will investigate this further in the computa-
\par
tion of exploration drilling strategies.
\par
A characteristic of our model is that the vari-
\par
ables have an immediate physical and easy-to-
\par
understand meaning, directed by the geologic de-
\par
scription of the area. In general, only the observable
\par
nodes in a BN must have a physical interpretation.
\par
However, when BNs are used for modeling, it is
\par
common that all nodes have a physical interpreta-
\par
tion (Imoto et al., 2003; Hamilton et al., 2005).
\par
Edges generally only represent probabilistic rela-
\par
tions, and not causality (Howard and Matheson,
\par
2005), although they may encode a specific causal
\par
relation as in Hamilton et al. (2005). In our spe-
\par
cific case study, we are not trying to infer the di-
\par
rections of the edges from a set of data. Instead, we
\par
assign physical interpretation to the edges. An edge
\par
represents possible flow from one node (area) to
\par
another. We can do this because the network is
\par
specified by experts, and thus the possible flow
\par
directions are known. When we use the BN struc-
\par
ture to answer specific decision problems, we of
\par
course invert edges to propagate evidence.
\par
Bayesian Network: Quantitative Part
\par
The key feature as we build the BN is that we can
\par
define the conditional dependence locally. After
\par
defining the edges and nodes of the network, the
\par
next step is to assign probabilities for all the re-
\par
quired conditional distributions (from the kitchens
\par
to the prospects, within the prospects, and from
\par
the prospects to the segments). We require a con-
\par
ditional probability for the gas, oil, and dry outcomes
\par
for all nodes, given the outcome of the parent nodes.
\par
When all the conditionals are defined, a valid joint
\par
distribution is also available.
\par
We now focus on the directed edges in Figure 1.
\par
The direction of the edge has both a physical and
\par
probabilistic interpretation: physical in terms of
\par
a more likely direction for gas or oil flow from
\par
one prospect to another and probabilistic in the
\par
sense that the distribution for the child node is
\par
defined via the conditional distribution given all
\par
parent nodes.
