\par
To handle the ambiguity challenge present
\par
when two possible types of flow (oil and gas) mix
\par
in the same node, we use physical considerations.
\par
A well-known theory on HC mixture, see for ex-
\par
ample, Gluyas and Swarbrick (2003), tell us that
\par
if both oil and gas enter the same trap, the result
\par
will be gas. This is because gas is lighter than oil,
\par
and it forces itself into the pockets and squeezes
\par
the oil out of the trap. For this mechanism to be in
\par
place, we need an abundance of gas and enough
\par
time to complete the process. We consider that
\par
these requirements are met in the area under con-
\par
sideration, and we disregard the possibility of res-
\par
ervoirs with both oil and gas.
\par
Another physical assumption that we make is
\par
that gas can only appear if at least one parent is
\par
gas as well, whereas oil can be generated even if
\par
the parents are all gases. This occurs because the
\par
presence of oil can be seen as a failure in the prop-
\par
agation of gas. Obviously, the dry state only prop-
\par
agates dry.
