1432 Bayesian Networks for Prospect Analysis in the North SeaThe exploration strategy depends on the choice
\par
of the optimization criterion. We have chosen two
\par
different criteria to study this segment selection
\par
problem. These criteria are similar to the ones pro-
\par
posed in Van Wees et al. (2008) based on the
\par
maximization of the net present value, but they
\par
are slightly different and they answer to precise
\par
questions coming from the oil industry:
\par
{$\bullet$} Which is the best segment combination to use
\par
to drill a certain (fixed) number of exploration
\par
wells to maximize the total revenues?
\par
{$\bullet$} Which are the best segments to drill to max-
\par
imize our confidence that we can leave the area
\par
without losing a great potential? In other words,
\par
which are the best segments that we should drill
\par
to be (almost) sure that in the worst scenario
\par
(segments dry), the whole area is actually dry.
\par
The first question is more traditional, in the
\par
sense that it has been discussed in other case studies
\par
for the same purposes. In our analysis, this question
\par
is not trivial. We use the VoPI as a criterion. This is
\par
widely used in the petroleum industry, see for ex-
\par
ample, Bratvold et al. (2009) and Bhattacharjya
\par
et al. (2010). In some situations with uncertainty
\par
connected with the evidence, one could compare
\par
the VoPI with the value of imperfect information
\par
(Bickel, 2008).
\par
The second question relates to the decision of
\par
leaving an area after drilling dry wells. We suggest
\par
maximizing the probability that we are leaving a
\par
dry area, and this entails minimizing the AR cri-
\par
terion. We fix the maximum number of segment p
\par
that we can afford to drill.
\par
Both for the VoPI and for the AR, the compu-
\par
tational effort is increasing with the dimension p.
\par
Indeed, when p = 1, we have to consider just 25
\par
different prospects, whereas when p = 2, the number
\par
of combinations is 25
