Background for Case Study
\par
Table 2 shows the expected volumes of oil and gas
\par
for each segment. These values are specified by
\par
experts of the area. We assign no uncertainty on
\par
these values, as discussed in Cunningham and Begg
\par
(2008). Given these expected volumes and the ex-
\par
pected price for oil and gas (respectively, \$75/bbl
\par
and \$0.35/m3), we can compute the expected rev-
\par
enues for each segment.
\par
We next consider the costs related to the
\par
exploration and development. We use four kinds
\par
of costs: a fixed cost that represents the cost of an
\par
exploratory well (well fixed cost [WFC]), a second
\par
exploration cost per prospect (exploration fixed
\par
cost [EFC]), a third major cost for the infra-
\par
structure and the well development costs (drilling
\par
fixed cost [DFC]), and a fourth cost proportional
\par
to the expected volume of gas and oil representing
\par
the operation and maintenance costs. We assume
\par
that the second and the third fixed costs have to be
\par
paid just once per prospect because the segments
\par
belonging to one prospect can share infrastructure.
\par
Thus, if we are going to drill different segments
\par
belonging to the same prospect, we save consid-
\par
erably. This assumption is realistic because differ-
\par
ent segments commonly correspond to formations
\par
at different levels of depth. We note that WFC is
\par
valid at the segment level because all the wells are
\par
considered independent at the exploration stage.
\par
The expert reference cost values for the area
\par
under consideration are on the order of WFC =
\par
\$20 million, EFC = \$390 million, whereas the pro-
\par
portional part is on the order of \$20 million per
\par
billion m3 of gas and per million m3 of oil. The
\par
DFC = \$500 million in our first analysis, but we
\par
later check sensitivity to this cost. We do not in-
\par
clude any uncertainty in these values nor in the
\par
volumes. The cost parameters and the prices of oil
\par
and gas are subject to change with time, they de-
\par
pend on the location and depth, and they could
\par
therefore vary between prospects and segments.
\par
They should be viewed more as the attempt to
\par
mimic a likely situation, than as a set of invariant
\par
parameters. The quantities allow us to compute for
\par
each segment the partial revenues for oil (PRO),
\par
that is, the expected oil revenues minus the flexible
