value in the area, and one might ask if, given the
\par
possibility of drilling two segments, it is best to
\par
drill both of these. However, the two segments are
\par
close in the network, and one might provide more
\par
information by exploring segments that are fur-
\par
ther apart. In Figure 12, we compute AR for all p=
\par
2 pairs. The hinted potential of the BN is really
\par
exploited for p = 2, and the best choice is not to
\par
drill the best 2 segments of the case p = 1 (6A,
\par
10B), but to choose two segments far apart ([6A,
\par
9A] or [6A, 9B]). It is, therefore, preferable to
\par
choose those pairs that carry information from
\par
different parts of the network, instead of the two
\par
segments that appear most promising using a
\par
marginal analysis, having overlapping information.
\par
The AR for p = 2 is commonly far from the sum of
\par
two p = 1 cases. A quite strong joint interaction
\par
reduces the pair effect. Recall that this difference
\par
was not very pronounced for the VoPI, where we
\par
marginalized over the evidence. The interaction
\par
effect depends on the segment locations in the BN.
\par
The results from p = 3 confirm our results; the
\par
best choice includes sites 6A, 9A, and 13A, that is,
\par
three segments far from each other. Although the
\par
improvement brought by adding one more seg-
\par
ment is quite consistent with respect to the mini-
\par
mum for p = 2 (45\% reduction), the percentage of
\par
triple segments whose AR is lower than the mini-
\par
mum double segment is very low (4\%).
\par
The AR criterion can be useful in situations
\par
with dry evidence. Our strategy is always based on
\par
Martinelli et al. 1439Figure 11. Abandoned revenue (in million USD) with single segment (p = 1) dry evidence. The parameter setting is described in
\par
Background for Case Study.
\par
static criteria, therefore, we have to fix a priori the
\par
number of wells that we want to drill and act ac-
\par
cording to this defined plan. In this case, sites 6A
\par
and 9A are indicated as the best choice for p = 2.
\par
They return an AR so small that (on expectation)
\par
the area is no longer worthwhile.
\par
CLOSING REMARKS
\par
The modeling of prospect dependencies with a BN
\par
appears to be a good technique to model the geo-
\par
logic connections between different prospects and
\par
the impact on the evaluation of an exploration
\par
Figure 12. Abandoned revenue (in million USD) with dry evidence at two segments (p = 2). The parameter setting is described in
\par
Background for Case Study.
\par
1440 Bayesian Networks for Prospect Analysis in the North Seastrategy while maintaining statistical computational
\par
benefits. The key elements in building a BN here
\par
are to separate the geologic components (kitchen,
\par
prospect, segment), keep the connections simple
\par
in the BN, and allow for a local failure probability
\par
(oil or gas! dry) between prospect and segment.
\par
For using the model to select exploration strat-
\par
egies, we proposed two criteria: the VoPI and the
\par
AR. We indicated guidelines for the interpretation
\par
of the results. The VoPI is a monetary amount based
\par
on the expected revenues with and without evi-
\par
dence. The AR is the expected remaining revenues,
\par
given dry evidence. Both have their merits and
\par
seem very useful in exploratory decision making.
\par
One challenge with the presented approach
\par
is the construction of the BN model. We benefited
\par
from the consultancy of expert knowledge of the
\par
local geology. We believe, nonetheless, that this
\par
procedure can be automized to a certain level
\par
through the run of an ensemble of geologic models.
\par
The result of geologic modeling under different
\par
diagenetic and depositional conditions could help
\par
define the required case-specific conditional prob-
\par
abilities of the network. One could also imagine
\par
combining several BN models with different edges
\par
and probabilities.
\par
Another choice that has to be made for each
\par
specific application is the evaluation criterion. It is
\par
possible to use more complex criteria based on
\par
specific utility functions. This seems to be partic-
\par
ularly important to avoid delayed drilling of the
\par
most promising segments. The presented approach
\par
can run a large number of statistical computations in
\par
a short time. Nonetheless, for increasing dimensions
\par
of evidence p, a combinatorial problem will arise.
\par
For instance, to solve the optimal sequential ex-
\par
ploration problem for our problem, we would have
\par
to turn to some approximations. The use of deci-
\par
sion trees, combined with some kind of pruning,
\par
could provide interesting solutions.
\par
The proposed criteria share the feature of re-
\par
lying on perfect information. Instead of observing
\par
perfect evidence in a given segment, we could just
\par
have the possibility of imperfect information from
\par
that segment. In this situation, the value of infor-
\par
mation could be used to evaluate the attractive-
\par
ness of buying such data (Eidsvik et al., 2008).
\par
In the current article, we ignored the uncer-
\par
tainties in the volumes, revenues, and costs. We
\par
simply conducted a sensitivity analysis on one
\par
cost parameter. Possibly, hierarchical modeling
\par
would be a flexible way to include more sources of
\par
uncertainty.
\par
We believe that this work summarizes how an
\par
extensive use of statistical modeling tools can sup-
\par
ply instruments to improve the understanding of
\par
prospect dependencies and can help formalize a
\par
decision problem.
