@article{oteroAccuracyRiskIntrinsic2012,
  title = {Accuracy, Risk and the Intrinsic Value of Diagnostic Imaging: A Review of the Cost-Utility Literature},
  shorttitle = {Accuracy, Risk and the Intrinsic Value of Diagnostic Imaging},
  author = {Otero, Hansel J. and Fang, Chi H. and Sekar, Meera and Ward, Robert J. and Neumann, Peter J.},
  year = 2012,
  journal = {Academic Radiology},
  volume = {19},
  number = {5},
  pages = {599--606},
  publisher = {Elsevier},
  doi = {10.1016/j.acra.2012.01.011},
  urldate = {2023-10-16},
  langid = {english},
  note = {Accuracy, Risk and the Intrinsic Value
\par
of Diagnostic Imaging:
\par
A Review of the Cost-utility Literature
\par
Hansel J. Otero, MD, Chi H. Fang, MS, MPH, Meera Sekar, MD,
\par
Robert J. Ward, MD, Peter J. Neumann, ScD
\par
Rationale and Objectives: The aim of this study was to systematically review the reporting of the value of imaging unrelated to treatment
\par
consequences and test characteristics in all imaging-related published cost-utility analyses (CUAs) in the medical literature.
\par
Materials and Methods: All CUAs published between 1976 and 2008 evaluating diagnostic imaging technologies contained in the CEA
\par
Registry, a publicly available comprehensive database of health related CUAs, were screened. Publication characteristics, imaging
\par
modality, and the inclusion of test characteristics including accuracy, costs, risks, and the potential value unrelated to treatment conse-
\par
quences (eg, reassurance or anxiety) were assessed.
\par
Results: Ninety-six published CUAs evaluating 155 different imaging technologies were included in the final sample; 27 studies were
\par
published in imaging-specialized journals. Fifty-two studies (54\%) evaluated the performance of a single imaging modality, while 44 studies
\par
(46\%) compared two or more different imaging modalities. The most common areas of interest were cardiovascular (45\%) and neurora-
\par
diology (17\%). Forty-two technologies (27\%) concerned ultrasound, while 34 (22\%) concerned magnetic resonance. Seventy-nine (51\%)
\par
technologies used ionizing radiation. Test accuracy was reported or calculated for 90\% (n = 133 and n = 5, respectively) and assumed
\par
perfect (reference test or gold-standard test without alternative testing strategy to capture false-negatives and false-positives) for 8\%
\par
(n = 12) of technologies. Only 22 studies (23\%) assessing 40 imaging technologies (26\%) considered inconclusive or indeterminate results.
\par
The risk of testing was reported for 32 imaging technologies (21\%). Fifteen studies (16\%) considered the value of diagnostic imaging
\par
unrelated to treatment. Four studies incorporated it as quality-of-life adjustments, while 10 studies mentioned it only in their discussions
\par
or as a limitation.
\par
Conclusions: The intrinsic value of imaging (the value of imaging unrelated to treatment) has not been appropriately defined or incorpo-
\par
rated in the existing cost-utility literature, which could be due to a lack of evidence on the issue. Thus, more research is needed on metrics
\par
for a more comprehensive evaluation of diagnostic imaging. Similarly, the incorporation of variations in imaging tests accuracy, inconclu-
\par
sive results and associated risks has lacked uniformity in the cost-utility literature. Acknowledgment of these characteristics in future
\par
cost-utility publications will enhance their value and provide results that more closely resemble routine clinical practice.
\par
Key Words: Diagnostic imaging, risk; cost-effectiveness; sensitivity; specificity.
\par
ªAUR, 2012
\par
The use of diagnostic imaging has increased exponen-
\par
tially, growing more rapidly than any other physician-
\par
ordered services, driven by technological advances,
\par
increased clinical reliance on noninvasive imaging, wider
\par
availability, patient preference, and at least in part by compelling
\par
financial interest of physicians (1--3). This has translated
\par
to higher costs and consequently closer scrutiny by payers,
\par
including a recent report by the US Government
\par
Accountability Office examining Medicare expenses on
\par
Acad Radiol 2012; 19:599--606
\par
From the Department of Radiology (H.J.O., M.S., R.J.W.) and the Center for the
\par
Evaluation of Value and Risk in Health at the Institute for Clinical Research and
\par
Health Policy Studies (H.J.O., C.H.F., P.J.N.), Tufts Medical Center, 800
\par
Washington Street, Boston, MA 02111. Received July 23, 2011; accepted
\par
January 26, 2012. Address correspondence to: H.J.O. e-mail: hotero@
\par
tuftsmedicalcenter.org
\par
ªAUR, 2012
\par
doi:10.1016/j.acra.2012.01.011
\par
medical imaging (4), also encouraging public and professional
\par
organizations to provide evidence for the value of imaging
\par
(5). The demand to prove the value of imaging has also trans-
\par
lated into a rising number of published cost-utility analyses
\par
(CUAs) (6). CUAs are a subtype of cost-effectiveness analyses
\par
that incorporates patient preferences and summarize the impact
\par
of interventions using a single common unit of cost per quality-
\par
adjusted life-year (QALY). CUAs allow the comparison of
\par
different medical interventions and are the preferred method
\par
for conducting economic analyses of health care interventions
\par
(6,7). However, limitations remain regarding which QALY
\par
comparisons are considered appropriate and meaningful (8).
\par
Traditionally, the value of diagnostic imaging in CUAs
\par
follows a hierarchical model in which increased technical effi-
\par
cacy necessitates generating increased accuracy, and the infor-
\par
mation obtained must then be use to improve therapeutic
\par
efficacy, which is ultimately measured as a patient outcome
\par
(9). In this model, value is measured only in terms of how
\par
599OTERO ET AL imaging influences patient survival or morbidity (10,11).
\par
However, few imaging interventions can be definitively
\par
measured using standard measures of patient outcomes such
\par
as life-years saved or QALYs (12).
\par
Medical imaging potentially improves the speed and preci-
\par
sion of diagnosis (13) and can aid in the selection of treatment,
\par
thus improving patient outcomes and avoiding unnecessary
\par
procedures, thereby reducing costs. Evidence suggests that
\par
imaging technologies might fare favorably in terms of
\par
cost-effectiveness compared to medical treatment, surgical
\par
treatment, or nonintervention (14). However, in addition to
\par
the value of imaging related to its impact on treatment deci-
\par
sions (medical value), imaging has additional planning value
\par
(effect on patients' ability to make better life decisions) and
\par
psychological value (how test information affects patients'
\par
senses of themselves) (15). Clinicians might intuitively be
\par
more comfortable with increased testing because it reduces
\par
diagnostic uncertainty and liability (16). Patients also have
\par
a tendency to opt for testing as a result of psychological
\par
factors, including certainty preference and risk aversion
\par
(16). Although imaging may not always result in a change in
\par
therapy, diagnostic information may still have intrinsic value
\par
(17,18). Patients might be willing to incur additional costs
\par
for tests with no immediate treatment implications because
\par
anxiety may be mitigated after a positive diagnosis, and
\par
a negative test result may provide reassurance (19--21).
\par
However, a negative result might also provoke anxiety about
\par
the unknown cause of ongoing symptoms, or, in the case of
\par
unnecessary overtesting, negative effects might be due to
\par
incidental findings or false-positive results.
\par
A recent publication by the Agency for Healthcare Research
\par
and Quality grouped these additional attributes of testing as
\par
emotional, social, cognitive, or behavioral outcomes and
\par
presented a framework to help researchers and policy makers
\par
consider these additional effects of testing when considering
\par
to test or not to test (11). However, it remains unclear how these
\par
effects are incorporated in CUAs. In this article, we refer to
\par
the additional effects of imaging not related to treatment
\par
consequences as the potential ``intrinsic value'' of imaging.
\par
We systematically reviewed the reporting of the potential
\par
intrinsic value of imaging in all imaging-related published
\par
CUAs between 1976 and 2008 using the CEA Registry,
\par
a publicly available and regularly updated comprehensive
\par
database of health-related CUAs, first established in 1976
\par
with the goal of aggregating information from published
\par
cost-effectiveness analyses in a single database. Additionally,
\par
we review the imaging cost-utility literature for the reporting
\par
of key parameters of diagnostic imaging testing, such as accu-
\par
racy, the costs of individual tests, and safety, including radiation
\par
safety.
\par
MATERIALS AND METHODS
\par
This study was based on data from a larger database that has
\par
systematically reviewed CUAs in health care since 1976.
\par
That database incorporates 2,383 original CUAs published
\par
600
\par
Academic Radiology, Vol 19, No 5, May 2012
\par
Figure 1. Flowchart of eligible studies. CEA, cost-effectiveness
\par
analysis; CUA, cost-utility analysis.
\par
in the medical literature through 2008 that have been
\par
compiled into a publicly available registry (http://www.
\par
cearegistry.org). This registry is periodically updated; specifi-
\par
cally, in 2001, the registry was updated, expanded, and made
\par
available online, and in 2004, it was made searchable. At the
\par
time of initiation of the present study, the entire 2008 calendar
\par
year had been added to the registry. The CEA Registry has the
\par
goals of (1) providing public electronic access to a reliable,
\par
convenient, and up-to-date comprehensive database of cost-
\par
effectiveness ratios published in the literature; (2) providing
\par
a convenient source for researchers seeking ``benchmark''
\par
information with which to compare findings in cost-
\par
effectiveness; and (3) serving as source for those seeking data
\par
on cost-effectiveness, in a much simpler way using a summa-
\par
rizing form. The registry database was constructed through an
\par
extensive search for original CUAs using MEDLINE and is
\par
updated periodically. MEDLINE was searched for Medical
\par
Subject Headings and/or text keywords: ``quality,'' ``QALY,''
\par
and ``cost-utility.'' All selected studies were independently
\par
audited by two expert readers. The two readers then convened
\par
for a consensus review to resolve discrepancies and using
\par
a standardized form; roughly 40 variables on methodology,
\par
utility weights, and cost-utility ratios were included in the
\par
database for each publication. More details on the search,
\par
development, and contents of the registry are provided at
\par
the CEA Registry Web site (http://www.cearegistry.org)
\par
and in prior publications (6).
\par
All studies evaluating diagnostic imaging technologies con-
\par
tained in the registry (Fig 1) were selected. From the standard
\par
auditing process, basic publication characteristics such as year,
\par
journal, authors' affiliations, study funding, and perspective of
\par
the analysis (eg, health care payer, society) are reported here.
\par
Additionally, the target audience of the journal (ie, general
\par
medicine, medical specialty, imaging, or pharmacoeconomics
\par
or health services research) was recorded. All articles were also
\par
audited for 10 additional variables unique to diagnostic
\par
imaging testing, including clinical area of interest, imaging
\par
modalities under study, use of ionizing radiation, test accuracy
\par
(reported as sensitivity and specificity, calculated from originalAcademic Radiology, Vol 19, No 5, May 2012 ACCURACY, RISK AND INTRINSIC VALUE OF IMAGING
\par
data, or the assumption that the test is perfect [reference test or
\par
gold standard without any alternative testing strategy to
\par
capture false-negatives and false-positives]), variations on
\par
test accuracy tested in the sensitivity analysis, individual test
\par
costs, and safety. Regarding safety, record was made if the
\par
risk of additional imaging testing was taken into consider-
\par
ation. This risk was in the form of either additional morbidity
\par
and mortality (thus decreasing the utility value) or increasing
\par
costs for additional required treatment. Record was also made
\par
if inconclusive or indeterminate results were accounted for in
\par
the analyses and what additional steps were to be taken (repeat
\par
the test, conduct additional corroborative tests, considered the
\par
results all negative or all positive, etc).
\par
Finally, any account of potential intrinsic value of imaging
\par
(defined as the inclusion of additional benefits or cost of the
\par
imaging tests that do not derive from changes in therapy, eg,
\par
reassurance value of information, disutility related to a known
\par
diagnosis even if a patient forgoes therapy, or value of planning
\par
given an untreatable diagnosis) and the section in which it was
\par
acknowledged in the paper were recorded. Also recorded was
\par
whether the intrinsic value was used to calculate QALYs. If
\par
such was the case, the described methods used to set the
\par
preferences weights were reviewed. Descriptive statistics
\par
were used to summarize all these characteristics of diagnostic
\par
imaging CUAs.
\par
RESULTS
\par
The first diagnostic imaging CUA study was published in
\par
1990. Ninety-six published CUAs evaluating 155 different
\par
imaging tests were included in the final sample.
\par
The great majority of the studies (76\%) were published
\par
between 2000 and 2008. The most common journal was
\par
Radiology (12 of 96 [13\%]), followed by the Annals of Internal
\par
Medicine (six of 96 [6\%]). Approximately one third (27 of 96
\par
[28\%]) of the studies were published in imaging-specialized
\par
journals, including Radiology , the American Journal of Roentgen-
\par
ology, Academic Radiology , the Journal of Vascular Interventional
\par
Radiology , the American Journal of Neuroradiology , and the Annals
\par
of Nuclear Medicine. Sixty-six economic analyses (69\%) had the
\par
United States as the country of interest, followed by the
\par
United Kingdom (11 of 96 [11\%]) and the Netherlands (eight
\par
of 96 [8\%]). Ninety-five percent (91 of 96) of studies included
\par
authors with academic affiliations, and 53\% of the studies
\par
(51 of 96) received government funding. A majority of studies
\par
(69 of 96 [72\%]) took societal perspectives for their analyses.
\par
Classified by study, CUAs in diagnostic imaging most
\par
commonly pertained to cardiovascular imaging (43 of
\par
96 [45\%]) and neuroradiology (16 of 96 [17\%]). Gastrointestinal
\par
or genitourinary imaging technologies were examined in 13
\par
studies (14\%). Breast, obstetrics oncology, and thoracic imaging
\par
technologies were studied in six studies each (6\% each, total n =
\par
18 of 96). Fifty-two studies (54\%) evaluated the performance of
\par
a single imaging modality (including four that compared
\par
different technologies from a single modality (eg, magnetic
\par
resonance imaging with endorectal vs pelvic coils, transesopha-
\par
geal vs transthoracic echocardiography, screen-film vs digital
\par
mammography), while 44 studies (46\%) compared multiple
\par
(two or more different) imaging modalities.
\par
Accounting for the 155 different imaging technologies
\par
evaluated in these studies, 42 (27\%) were ultrasonographic
\par
technologies, and 34 (22\%), 29 (19\%), and 19 (12\%) con-
\par
cerned magnetic resonance, computed tomography, and
\par
conventional radiography, respectively. A total of 79 of these
\par
155 imaging tests (51\%) involved ionizing radiation. These
\par
results are summarized in Table 1.
\par
The sensitivity (range, 20\%--100\%) and specificity (range,
\par
43\%--100\%) of the imaging tests were reported for 133
\par
different imaging technologies (86\%); they were assumed
\par
perfect (reference test or gold standard) for 12 (8\%), calculated
\par
from primary data for five (3\%), and not reported for five (3\%)
\par
imaging technologies. The effect of accuracy variations was
\par
tested in the form of sensitivity analysis in 110 (77\%) of the
\par
technologies been evaluated.
\par
Only 23\% of studies (22 of 96), accounting for a total of 40
\par
of the 155 imaging technologies (26\%), considered inconclu-
\par
sive or indeterminate test results. Of the 40 technologies in
\par
which inconclusive or indeterminate results were considered,
\par
28 (70\%) proceeded to obtain additional noninvasive imaging
\par
for confirmation, five proceeded to invasive imaging confir-
\par
mation (eg, cardiac angiography), four proceeded to tissue
\par
sampling for confirmation, two assumed that all nondiagnostic
\par
results would go undiagnosed (adding indeterminate or
\par
inconclusive to the false-negative group), and one model
\par
knowingly proceeded to treatment in all patients with indeter-
\par
minate or inconclusive imaging results (Table 2).
\par
The additional risk of the proposed imaging intervention
\par
was accounted for in 32 of 155 imaging technologies (21\%)
\par
in 15\% of the studies (14 of 96) included in the final sample
\par
(Table 2). In seven cases, the added risk of radiation was
\par
included in the calculations of cost per QALY. In six cases,
\par
the risk for bleeding was accounted for as decreased quality
\par
of life, increased mortality, or additional cost of management.
\par
Three of 21 contrast-enhanced computed tomographic tech-
\par
nologies (14.3\%) factored contrast-related adverse reactions
\par
into their equations (Table 2).
\par
Fifteen analyses (16\%) considered the value of the diagnostic
\par
imaging unrelated to treatment. The information included in
\par
these studies is summarized in Table 3 (16,22--35); four of the
\par
studies incorporated it in the quality-of-life adjustments, while
\par
10 of the studies mentioned it only in their discussions or as
\par
a limitation of their analyses. Additionally, four studies
\par
(22,25,27,35) discussed increased diagnostic confidence and/
\par
or liability avoidance as an additional benefit of testing, even
\par
in the absence of a change in patient management.
\par
Of the studies that considered the intrinsic value of imaging
\par
into their QALY, two did so by using primary data (ie, prefer-
\par
ences obtained directly from patients from 2000 Medical
\par
Expenditure Panel Survey and Aneurysm Screening After
\par
Treatment for Ruptured Aneurysms databases) to calculate
\par
the effect of decreased quality of life for anxiety from a false-
\par
positive result on mammography (28) and the disutility of
