published work to estimate the utility of knowingly living
\par
with an untreated, unruptured intracranial aneurysm (34).
\par
DISCUSSION
\par
We reviewed all published CUAs related to diagnostic imaging
\par
for the inclusion of key parameters, including the direct
\par
acknowledgment of the additional attributes of testing, which
\par
we termed the intrinsic value of imaging. Our findings show
\par
that it is uncommon to acknowledge the non-treatment-
\par
related value of imaging during CUAs. We also found that
\par
the majority of studies acknowledging the potential intrinsic
\par
value of imaging do so by describing it as a limitation of their
\par
analyses. This could be due to a lack of evidence on the issue.
\par
Although attempts have been made to measure and model the
\par
prognostic value of imaging (17,21), none of the publications
\par
incorporated this type of metric into their analyses. We
\par
hypothesize that investigators might have tried to avoid theAcademic Radiology, Vol 19, No 5, May 2012 ACCURACY, RISK AND INTRINSIC VALUE OF IMAGING
