\par
introduction of additional uncertainty and potential bias.
\par
Concerns about both uncertainty and personal bias are often
\par
raised because of the apparent discretionary nature of model
\par
building and data selection in these types of analyses (36). These
\par
concerns have been addressed by multiple publications that set
\par
basic recommendations for appropriate analysis (37--39).
\par
The challenge of linking radiology to outcomes beyond the
\par
traditional model (6) remains an important topic for radiologists
\par
(10,18,40) because diagnostic test results are intermediate
\par
outcomes that do not directly determine patients' health
\par
outcomes (41). The validation of methods to determine the
\par
value of imaging beyond changes in treatment should be
\par
made a priority for health economic researchers. It is our
\par
opinion that both positive and negative effects on health (eg,
\par
reassurance and allowing future planning vs increases in anxiety
\par
or depression) should be incorporated accordingly. However,
\par
the new metrics must be based on empirical evidence, which
\par
is a more basic but crucial first step for achieving more accurate
\par
results from CUAs of diagnostic imaging. Additionally, the
\par
complexity of results and models in future CUAs might still
\par
represent a limitation for the incorporation of the intrinsic value
\par
of imaging in the studies. Standard quality-of-life measures are
\par
already modified on the basis of gender and age, and there has
\par
been criticism about their use despite the wide adoption of
\par
QALYs as a common metric (8). Similarly, any future metric
\par
that captures the intrinsic value of imaging will face the same
\par
complexities for development and barriers for implementation.
\par
The distribution among clinical areas was found to be fairly
\par
even among imaging subspecialties, with the exception of
\par
cardiovascular imaging. This finding has been reported
\par
previously (6,14) and might be related to an early lead in the
\par
number of publications (representing 19 of 24 publications
\par
before 2000) and a more direct relation between imaging
\par
and therapy and outcomes with some cardiac technologies
\par
(such as cardiac angiography), which makes it easier to
\par
perform economic analyses. We also found that approximately
\par
half of the studies compared different imaging technologies
\par
against each other, which shows the clear overlap of
\par
modalities for the evaluation of certain organ systems and
\par
the interest in the performance of new imaging modalities
\par
compared to well-known applications in other modalities
\par
(such as how magnetic resonance or computed tomography
\par
compares to catheter-based angiography).
\par
The accuracy of the imaging tests, presented as sensitivity
\par
and specificity, was reported in the majority of cases.
\par
However, in 23\% of the tested technologies, accuracy varia-
\par
tions were not tested during the sensitivity analyses. Given
\par
the possibility of human error and the intrareader and inter-
\par
reader variability inherent in image interpretation as well as
\par
to acknowledge the trade-off between the sensitivity and
\par
specificity (usually depicted as receiver-operating character-
\par
istic curves) of any diagnostic test, changes in accuracy should
\par
be consistently made part of CUAs in imaging.
\par
We also found that fewer than one fourth of the studies
\par
considered the effect of inconclusive or inappropriate results
\par
on patient management. We believe that inconclusive or tech-
\par
nically inappropriate results are not uncommon in routine
\par
clinical practice. For example, a 2006 study found that the
\par
number of inconclusive results due to patients' body habitus
\par
significantly increased from 1989 to 2003 (42). As such, tech-
\par
nical and patient-related factors such as this should be reported
\par
or considered when creating a cost-utility model. The fact
\par
that only a minority of studies report such evidence is thus
\par
concerning. We suggest that future cost-utility studies scruti-
\par
nizing imaging technologies incorporate a reasonable rate of
\par
inconclusive results to more closely resemble ``real-world''
\par
settings that are preferred when comparing competing
\par
strategies (41).
\par
An even smaller minority (21\%) reported the risk associated
\par
with testing, including ionizing radiation exposure, contrast-
\par
induced adverse reactions, or bleeding. This finding might be
\par
related to the widespread idea the majority of diagnostic
\par
imaging tests have minimal or no adverse effects (41). However,
\par
well-known, albeit rare, complications from certain technolo-
\par
gies such as iodinated contrast administration have the potential
\par
to tilt the utility ratios one way or the other and should be
\par
included. Additionally, the safety of medical radiation, particu-
\par
larly in relation to computed tomography, has been questioned
\par
recently and has been a major focus of attention in the medical
\par
literature and the public media, as well as an important initiative
\par
by medical imaging--related professional organizations (43--45).
\par
The incorporation of the estimated added risk of imaging
\par
would therefore enhance the value of these studies and
\par
provide a more fair comparison to other management plans
\par
including those with less imaging reliance.
\par
