\par
Academic Radiology, Vol 19, No 5, May 2012 ACCURACY, RISK AND INTRINSIC VALUE OF IMAGINGOTERO ET AL CONCLUSION
\par
At a time when there is increased demand for evidence on
\par
relevant outcomes for medical imaging technologies and
\par
mounting pressure to reduce cost and improve the safety and
\par
efficacy of medical imaging (46--48), the intrinsic value of
\par
imaging has not been appropriately defined or incorporated
\par
in the existing literature on CUAs. Thus, more research is
\par
needed on metrics for a more comprehensive evaluation of
\par
diagnostic imaging. Similarly, the incorporation of imaging
\par
test accuracy with its variations, inconclusive results, and
\par
imaging test risks has lacked uniformity in the cost-utility
\par
literature. Acknowledgment of these characteristics in future
\par
cost-utility publications evaluating diagnostic imaging will
\par
enhance their value and provide results that more closely
\par
resemble routine clinical practice.
