  urldate = {2023-06-27},
  abstract = {The use of biomarkers to ``personalize'' cancer treatment ---identifying discrete genes, proteins, or other indicators that can differentiate one type of cancer from another and enable the use of highly tailored therapies -- offers tremendous potential for improved outcomes and lower treatment costs. However, the rapid development of cancer biomarker, or genomic, tests--- combined with a paucity of evidence to support the effectiveness of the tests---presents a challenge for patients, clinicians and other stakeholders. In this article, we propose that comparative effectiveness research be used to strengthen what is now a haphazard process for developing and marketing cancer biomarker tests. We suggest novel funding approaches and a systematic process for moving from regulatory approval to the generation of evidence that meets the needs of stakeholders and, ultimately, patients.},
