\textbf{Abstract}
\par
Computer disease simulation models are increasingly being used to evaluate and inform healthcare
\par
decisions across medical disciplines. The aim of researchers who develop these models is to
\par
integrate and synthesize short-term outcomes and results from multiple sources to predict the long-
\par
term clinical outcomes and costs of different healthcare strategies. Policy makers, in turn, can use
\par
the predictions generated by disease models together with other evidence to make decisions
\par
related to healthcare practices and resource utilization. Models are particularly useful when the
\par
existing evidence does not yield obvious answers or does not provide answers to the questions of
\par
greatest interest, such as questions about the relative cost effectiveness of different practices. In
\par
this review we focus on models used to inform decisions about imaging technology, discussing the
\par
role of disease models for health policy development and providing a foundation for
\par
understanding the basic principles of disease modeling. We draw from the collective computed
\par
tomographic colonography (CTC) modeling experience, reviewing 10 published investigations of
\par
the clinical effectiveness and cost-effectiveness of CTC relative to colonoscopy. We discuss the
\par
implications of different modeling assumptions and difficulties that may be encountered when
\par
evaluating the quality of models. Finally, we underscore the importance of forging stronger
\par
collaborations between researchers who develop disease models and radiologists, in order to
\par
ensure that policy-level models accurately represent the experience of everyday clinical practices.
\par
