\textbf{2.0 Role of Disease Simulation Models in Health Policy for Radiologic}
\par
\textbf{Imaging}
\par
Researchers seeking information about the impact of imaging technologies on clinical
\par
outcomes encounter a variety of types of clinical evidence. However, each has its
\par
limitations. These limitations can often be overcome by integrating data with a computer
\par
disease simulation model. These models are particularly useful when some short-term
\par
outcomes are available about a technology, but evidence of the impact on long-term health
\par
outcomes is lacking.
\par
Most studies of imaging tests focus on test accuracy, that is, how well a test can identify
\par
disease. While accuracy studies are key to understanding one-time test performance, they
\par
provide no information about clinical outcomes (8). Randomized trials are the gold standard
\par
study design for assessing outcomes. However, they are usually not a feasible method for
\par
addressing health policy questions because of their costs and long time horizon for
\par
completion, and because of the rapidly evolving nature of healthcare technologies. This is
\par
especially true for imaging technologies. In addition, randomized trials may be ethically
\par
difficult, or even impossible, to carry out once an imaging test is widely accepted as the
\par
standard of care, as is the case with mammography, or when the benefits are widely perceive
\par
to outweigh the risks, as in the case of coronary CT angiography (9). Finally, long-term
\par
randomized trials are often not possible because of funding limitations. As a result,
\par
randomized trials generally provide information about shorter-term outcomes such as the
\par
severity of disease detected (e.g., early or late stage cancer) or 1- to 5-year survival
\par
outcomes. Trials that focus on long-term disease outcomes following imaging are rare. One
\par
example is the Prostate, Lung, Colorectal, and Ovarian (PLCO) Screening Trial, which
