\par
randomized 38,349 men and 39,115 women to receive either routine medical care or active
\par
screening. In addition to colorectal and prostate cancer screening, the active screening arm
\par
included lung cancer screening with a baseline chest x-ray followed by annual chest x-rays
\par
for three years and, among women, ovarian cancer screening with transvaginal ultrasound
\par
and CA125 (10,11). In spite of the high value of randomized trials like PLCO, the results are
\par
not available for guiding policy now: the trial began in 1993 and mortality results are
\par
anticipated in 2015.
\par
Observational studies are a common first approach for estimating the effect of imaging tests
\par
on disease outcomes because they use existing data and can be carried out at relatively low
\par
cost. Observational studies often include a broader patient population than randomized trials,
\par
providing important additional information, even when results from randomized trials are
\par
available (12). Observational studies can also provide information about longer-term
\par
outcomes following imaging and information about practices that are ``off protocol''.
\par
However, observational studies are prone to multiple biases (13--15). Although analyses can
\par
adjust for these biases, they can only account for known potential biases that were measured
\par
in the study. Accordingly, the results from observational studies alone may not provide
\par
sufficient evidence to inform health policy decision makers of the effectiveness of a
\par
technology.
\par
Computer disease models provide a method for combining results from all available studies
\par
to fill gaps in existing knowledge. Disease models can be used to extrapolate results from
\par
accuracy studies to predict longer-term clinical outcomes. For example, the Digital
\par
Mammography Imaging Screening Trial (DMIST), a large study assessing the accuracy of
\par
film and digital mammography for breast cancer screening, found that the overall diagnostic
\par
accuracy of the two modalities was similar, but that digital mammography was superior in
\par
certain patient subgroups (16). Using a model of the natural history of breast cancer that
\par
incorporated the DMIST data on test performance, Tosteson et al. (3) projected the long-
\par
term health outcomes and incremental cost-effectiveness of alternative breast cancer
\par
screening strategies, including all-film and all-digital strategies and strategies that targeted
\par
use of digital mammography based on age and breast density. They found that all-digital
\par
mammography screening was no more effective, and in some cases it was less effective,
\par
than targeted use of digital mammography in terms of quality-adjusted life years. The
\par
authors note that their modeling analysis shows ``how a shift to all-digital mammography
\par
screening may result in health gains for younger women (especially those with dense
\par
breasts), possibly at the expense of older women (especially those with nondense breasts).''
\par
Disease models can also be used to simulate comparisons that were not evaluated in a trial.
\par
For example, McMahon and colleagues (17) estimated the effectiveness of helical CT for
\par
lung cancer screening among current and former smokers in the single-arm Mayo CT
\par
screening study. Using de-identified data from the study participants, they simulated the
\par
study outcomes, as well as predicted outcomes for a hypothetical control group that did not
\par
undergo helical CT screening. They concluded that while helical CT screening could reduce
\par
lung cancer-specific mortality, it would have little impact on overall mortality because of
\par
competing causes of death in current and former smokers.
\par
For newly developed or rapidly evolving imaging technologies, studies of long-term
\par
outcomes are not possible because of insufficient observation time. In this context, disease
\par
models provide a tool for real-time evaluation of a new or evolving technology by
\par
combining information about the natural history of disease with the most recent results
\par
describing the sensitivity and specificity of the technology for disease detection.
\par
