\par
Rutter et al. Page 4
\par
Models can also be used to explore and identify aspects of a diagnostic test or intervention
\par
that have the largest impact on effectiveness and cost-effectiveness. For example, because
\par
CTC is less invasive than colonoscopy, it has the potential to draw more people into
\par
colorectal cancer screening. Models can be used to evaluate the impact of potential
\par
increased adherence attributable to this feature of CTC on the effectiveness and cost-
\par
effectiveness of CTC screening. Models can also assess the value of obtaining more
\par
information on uncertain aspects of the clinical decision, thereby helping to prioritize future
\par
research efforts (18). For example, using a model of colorectal cancer, Hassan and
\par
colleagues (19) determined that having additional information on screening uptake rates
\par
with less-invasive screening tests such as CTC compared with colonoscopy would be of
\par
greatest value in identifying an optimal colorectal cancer screening strategy.
\par
Models are typically used to predict effectiveness measures such as life-years gained per
\par
treated individual, or life-years gained per imaging exam (20). Model predictions often, but
\par
not always, incorporate costs. These include the costs of the imaging test, as well as those of
\par
subsequent interventions or treatments and associated adverse events (e.g., mammogram
\par
costs plus the costs of treating breast cancer that would not have been diagnosed without the
\par
imaging test). The net cost or savings associated with an imaging strategy are calculated by
\par
comparison to the costs of a no-intervention scenario. Cost-effectiveness analysis combines
\par
life-years gained with cost predictions, providing policy makers with a framework for
\par
identifying the interventions that yield the greatest health benefit given resource constraints
\par
(21).
\par
\textbf{3.0 Basics of Disease Models}
\par
Disease models provide a framework for integrating and synthesizing information from
\par
multiple sources, including accuracy studies, randomized trials, and observational studies,
\par
that describe various aspects of the disease of interest and relevant technologies. Models are
\par
structured with a pre-specified set of health states (e.g. living without the disease of interest,
\par
living with preclinical disease, living with diagnosed disease while undergoing treatment,
\par
etc.) that describe the relevant aspects of the disease process. Modelers integrate information
\par
from different studies by using data to choose probabilities of transitioning through these
\par
disease states. The transitions can be specified as fixed probabilities or they may be based on
\par
mathematical formulae that allow the probability of transition to depend on characteristics of
\par
simulated individuals (e.g., age, sex, disease risk factors). The goal of this process of ``model
\par
calibration'' is to build a model that precisely reproduces the existing data, so later model
\par
predictions are as accurate as possible. In selecting the data for model calibration, modelers
\par
can choose to disregard unreliable or poorly designed studies and give greater weight to
\par
more rigorous and larger studies.
\par
Computer disease models generally combine a natural history model that describes the
\par
disease process in the absence of screening with an intervention model to estimate the
\par
effectiveness of a diagnostic procedure or other medical intervention. Compared to natural
\par
history models, intervention models are relatively simple because they are based on
\par
observable outcomes (test sensitivity and specificity) and known or recommended
\par
intervention schedules. Intervention models often assume ideal or overly simplified
\par
circumstances. For example, models may assume no variability in test performance across
\par
individuals, even though such variability undoubtedly occurs because of both patient- and
\par
practitioner-specific factors (22,23). In addition, models may incorporate assumptions of
\par
perfect adherence with protocols (e.g., screening at pre-specified intervals), or equal
\par
adherence with all strategies under consideration (e.g., colonoscopy and CTC). These
\par
simplifying assumptions may be necessary because of a lack of data on adherence with
\par
repeated tests, or may be made to allow strategies to be compared in a head-to-head manner.
\par
\emph{Acad Radiol}. Author manuscript; available in PMC 2012 September 1.NIH-PA Author Manuscript NIH-PA Author Manuscript NIH-PA Author Manuscript
\par
Rutter et al. Page 5
\par
Disease models are categorized into three broad classes with increasing degrees of
\par
complexity: decision tree models, cohort models, and microsimulation models. All models
\par
make assumptions. In general, simpler disease models make different, but not fewer,
\par
assumptions about the disease processes than more complex models.
\par
\textbf{\emph{Decision tree models}} are relatively simple and are used to track outcomes associated with
\par
different courses of action (24,25). Outcomes can include disease status, 5-year survival,
\par
number of procedures, and costs. At each branching point, the tree specifies the chance of
\par
each subsequent outcome occurring, for example whether a positive result is a true or false
\par
positive finding. Alternative courses of action are compared by calculating the expected
\par
value of the outcome from each (i.e., multiplying the value assigned to each potential
\par
outcome by the probability that each occurs). A key limitation of decision trees is that they
\par
do not explicitly incorporate time and therefore are not suited to evaluation of repeated
\par
events, such as imaging implemented within a screening program.
\par
\textbf{\emph{Cohort models}} allow greater complexity than decision trees by describing the movement of
\par
groups of individuals through disease states over time. Cohort models allow movement
\par
between health states (e.g., ``well'', ``sick'', ``dead'') at fixed time intervals (e.g., one year)
\par
and are typically specified as Markov models: the probability of the next transition depends
\par
only on the current state and is independent of the prior history (i.e., how one got to the
\par
state) (26). Markov health states are, as a result, commonly described as ``memory-less.''
\par
This means that if, for example, only one health state of metastatic disease is designated in a
\par
cancer model, then once patients develop metastatic disease, patients who originally had
\par
low-grade primary cancers become indistinguishable from those who had high-grade
\par
primary cancers. Cohort models can be programmed in a spreadsheet or in a standard
\par
decision analysis software program (e.g., TreeAge Pro). However, these models may be
\par
impractical if ``memory'' of past events is important, or if the number of unique health states
\par
simulated becomes intractable (27).
\par
\textbf{\emph{Microsimulation models}} are more flexible than cohort models because they simulate the
\par
movement of individuals through disease states (28). Microsimulation models do not require
\par
the memory-less property of Markov models and allow events in continuous time rather than
\par
requiring transitions based on specified cycle lengths. Microsimulation models also allow
\par
specification of complex disease states that vary by individual and tumor characteristics, and
\par
these can be described by continuous variables (e.g., body mass index, or tumor size in mm),
\par
rather than categorically, as is often the case in cohort models. The price of greater
\par
flexibility is increased complexity and simulation times. Additionally, the complexity of
\par
microsimulation models typically requires customized programming using a flexible
\par
programming language such as C++, R, or Delphi.
\par
There is a natural tension between model simplicity and model complexity, as we
\par
demonstrate in the following section comparing models used to evaluate CTC. The
\par
appropriate degree of model complexity depends on a variety of factors, including modeling
\par
goals and available information. Complex disease models are generally developed as part of
\par
a larger research agenda focusing on specific disease screening or treatment. These models
\par
are developed specifically for repeated use including assessment of interventions that may
\par
become available after initial model development. In contrast, simpler models may be
\par
developed to address a single question.
\par
\textbf{4.0 Example: Models Used to Evaluate CTC vs. Colonoscopy}
\par
As an example of the diversity of disease models, the multiple factors they may consider,
\par
and the ways in which they can be applied, we compared models used to evaluate both CTC
\par
and colonoscopy for colorectal cancer screening. We searched the published literature and
