\par
identified 10 unique models that have evaluated these technologies. Key model
\par
characteristics, discussed below, are summarized in Table 1. When models have been used
\par
in multiple publications examining CTC, we describe the publication with the most detailed
\par
model description.
\par
Three of the models (29--31) evaluated colorectal cancer screening with CTC or with
\par
colonoscopy only, while the other seven models evaluated CTC, colonoscopy, and other
\par
screening modalities (e.g., fecal occult blood testing, flexible sigmoidoscopy, and strategies
\par
that combined the two tests) (7,32--35). One model was a decision tree (29), six were cohort
\par
models (30--35), and three were microsimulation models (MISCAN, CRC-SPIN, and
\par
SimCRC, used in a joint publication) (7). The models varied both in terms of the general
\par
level of complexity and the specific assumptions made. Most models specified a similar
\par
overall disease process, with eight (7,29,30,32,34,35) of the 10 models assuming that all
\par
cancers arose through the adenoma-carcinoma sequence (36,37). Two models also allowed
\par
for de novo cancers (31,33). Three of the models (29,31,32) allowed hyperplastic polyps,
\par
though it was unclear if they allowed co-occurrence of adenomas and hyperplastic polyps.
\par
All treated the hyperplastic polyps as ``nuisance'' polyps that induced unnecessary
\par
procedures; none allowed progression to cancer through this pathway (38).
\par
The simulation of adenoma onset by age is an essential part of a colorectal cancer natural
\par
history model. While the malignant potential of adenomas of various sizes is not fully
\par
known, an incident adenoma represents a potential starting point for disease. The modelers
\par
uniformly based adenoma incidence on adenoma prevalence data, either from autopsy
\par
studies (7,31,34,39)(MISCAN, SimCRC), screening studies (29), or a combination of
\par
screening and autopsy data (7) (CRC-SPIN). However, even when modelers used the same
\par
type of studies for adenoma prevalence (e.g., autopsy studies alone), they did not necessarily
\par
use the same specific studies. Most modelers who used adenoma prevalence data from
\par
autopsy studies selected only one or two autopsy studies from at least 14 possible data
\par
sources (40). Thus the different CTC models integrated different evidence, which may
\par
contribute to the differences in their predictions.
\par
While all of the models incorporated evidence on adenoma prevalence, the level of detail
\par
used to describe the occurrence of adenomas within individuals differed greatly.
\par
Conceptually, all models included an adenoma state, which included all individuals with at
\par
least one adenoma. The three microsimulation models (7) described this state in the most
\par
detail, simulating the size and location of multiple adenomas that may simultaneously occur.
\par
Most cohort models simulated at most one adenoma or polyp per individual (the most
\par
advanced) at any point in time and did not simulate the location of the adenoma or polyp in
\par
the colorectal tract. Models that do not allow synchronous adenomas, with some missed and
\par
some identified, need to adjust imaging characteristics that are reported at the lesion-level to
\par
sensitivities and specificities at the person-level to avoid underestimating the likelihood of
\par
detecting a person with at least one adenoma.
\par
The models also differed with respect to whether and how they accounted for adenoma
\par
location. Only the three microsimulation models (7) and one cohort model (34) accounted
\par
for adenoma location. The microsimulation models simulated six locations (i.e., cecum;
\par
ascending, transverse, descending, or sigmoid colon; or rectum) and the cohort model
\par
simulated two (i.e., proximal colon vs. distal colon and rectum). Although not the focus of
\par
this review, the implications of not explicitly simulating adenoma location are important to
\par
consider when evaluating models that assess sigmoidoscopy screening strategies because
\par
sigmoidoscopy only detects lesions in the rectum or sigmoid colon.
\par
