\par
The models varied in their description of adenoma malignancy potential. As shown in Table
\par
1, several models based malignancy potential on transitions between clinically-relevant size
\par
categories: less than 10 mm vs. 10 mm or larger (31); 1--5 mm, 6--9 mm, and 10 mm or
\par
larger (7,33,34). One model specified a functional form for adenoma growth, resulting in
\par
continuous adenoma size (7) (CRC-SPIN). One model did not describe adenoma size (39).
\par
Two models described high- and low-risk adenomas, with high-risk adenomas defined as 10
\par
mm or larger or less than 10 mm with villous features or high-grade dysplasia, and low-risk
\par
adenomas defined as all others. Adenoma size is important because accuracy is linked to
\par
lesion size for both CTC and colonoscopy. One of the models describing high- and low-risk
\par
adenomas based test sensitivity on size (32), but the other used adenoma risk category as a
\par
proxy for size (35) when specifying the accuracy of CTC and colonoscopy. Models that
\par
describe a single lesion describe the largest lesion and require person-level test accuracy,
\par
whereas models that allow multiple lesions of varying size may use lesion-level test
\par
accuracy. Two models ignored small ({$<$}6 mm) adenomas (29,34), assuming that these would
\par
not progress to cancer within the timeframe of the study (29) or would not be detected or
\par
removed at screening (34); the latter contradicts typical colonoscopy screening practices.
\par
While there was much variability in the approach to modeling adenomas, the models were
\par
quite similar in the data used to simulate cancer incidence rates. All but one model used
\par
colorectal cancer incidence rates from cancer registries (7,30--35); the decision-tree model
\par
considered a 3-year time horizon and its developers used data from observational studies to
\par
inform 3-year cancer risk. Cancer incidence data for model calibration should be selected to
\par
reflect the natural history of the disease; for colorectal cancer, that means incidence prior to
\par
the dissemination of screening. Rates during post-screening periods may be elevated due to
\par
detection of indolent cancer that would not otherwise have been clinically detected.
\par
However, colorectal cancer incidence rates have fallen over time, partially due to detection
\par
and removal of adenomas through screening (41). Most models that used registry data did
\par
not specify the specific time periods of the obtained data (30,31,33--35), although the three
\par
microsimulation models (7) and one cohort model (32) specified the use of registry data
\par
collected before the use of colorectal cancer screening tests became more common (i.e., late
\par
1970s to mid-1980s).
\par
While the models used to compare CTC and colonoscopy were broadly similar, each used a
\par
somewhat different structure: using different data to describe adenoma prevalence, different
\par
categories for adenoma malignancy potential, and potentially different data sources for all
\par
elements. In the following section, we compare the findings from these diverse studies.
\par
\textbf{4.1 Predicted Effectiveness of CTC Relative to Colonoscopy}
\par
All of the models we examined used expected number of years of life as the primary
\par
measure of the effectiveness of screening for colorectal cancer using either colonoscopy or
\par
CTC. Consistent with recommendations on the conduct of cost-effectiveness analyses
\par
(21,42), future years of life were discounted to their present value in all models, although
\par
different discount rates were used. As shown in Table 1, the models evaluated the screening
\par
strategies in somewhat different populations. Most models focused on individuals from age
\par
50 to death, though the three microsimulation models focused on the Medicare population of
\par
individuals 65 and older. The decision-tree analysis (29) estimated outcomes over a 3-year
\par
period following the initial screening exam, corresponding with the shortest interval for a
\par
follow-up exam. Many modelers reported results from sensitivity analyses, which explored
\par
the impact of their assumptions on results. Unless otherwise noted, we refer to the findings
\par
from each study's from primary, or ``base case'', assumptions. Vijan and colleagues(32)
\par
evaluated both 3D and 2D CTC. We focus exclusively on their results for 3D CTC because
\par
they found that a screening program based on 3D CTC was both less costly and more
\par
effective than screening based on 2D CTC (that is 3D CTC dominated 2D CTC).
\par
