\par
Rutter et al. Page 8
\par
All of the cohort and microsimulation models we reviewed found that colonoscopy
\par
screening every 10 years was more effective in terms of discounted years of life than CTC
\par
screening every 5 (7,35) or 10 years (30--34). The analysis by Vijan and colleagues (32)
\par
found that colonoscopy was more effective than CTC every 10 years, but \emph{less} effective than
\par
CTC every 5 years. In addition, Teleford and colleagues (35) only reported discounted
\par
quality-adjusted years of life, which accounts for the fact that a year of life in perfect health
\par
is valued more than a year of life in poor health (20). The decision-tree model also found
\par
that colonoscopy was more effective over the 3-year period that it considered. As shown in
\par
Table 2, the assumed sensitivities and specificities of CTC and colonoscopy varied across
\par
studies, reflecting differences in the accuracy studies used to inform the models. Most
\par
models incorporated data suggesting that colonoscopy was more sensitive than CTC for
\par
detection of all lesions. Lee and colleagues (34) assumed that CTC was more sensitive than
\par
colonoscopy for detection of cancers and large ({$>$}10 mm) adenomas, but still found
\par
colonoscopy to be a more effective screening strategy than CTC.
\par
\textbf{4.2 Predicted Costs and Cost-Effectiveness of CTC Relative to Colonoscopy}
\par
There was some diversity in findings related to the costs and cost-effectiveness of
\par
colonoscopy and CTC screening (Table 2). Six models found that colonoscopy screening
\par
was less costly than CTC screening (7,30,31,33,34) so that colonoscopy was dominant,
\par
being both more effective and less costly than CTC. The analysis by Vijan and
\par
colleagues(32) found that colonoscopy every 10 years dominated 3D CTC every 10 years
\par
while 3D CTC every 5 years was more effective but also more costly than colonoscopy
\par
every 10 years. Three cohort models found that colonoscopy screening was more costly than
\par
CTC (30,33,34). Compared to the other models, the relative unit cost of colonoscopy versus
\par
CTC in these three models was much higher. They were 1.5 times higher for colonoscopy
\par
without polypectomy versus CTC, compared to at most 1.2 times higher for models finding
\par
colonoscopy to be less expensive. Only two of these studies (33,34) found CTC to have a
\par
more favorable incremental cost-effectiveness ratio than colonoscopy. Compared to other
\par
models, these two studies assumed a lower accuracy for CTC detection of small adenomas
\par
that are unlikely to lead to colorectal but increase costs because of referral to colonoscopy
\par
(shown in Table 2). In addition, Lee and colleagues (34) assumed that CTC was more
\par
accurate than colonoscopy for detection of cancers and adenomas 10 mm or larger. Other
\par
modelers explored the impact of CTC sensitivity on their findings with inconsistent results.
\par
Sonnenberg and colleagues(30) found that the cost-effectiveness of CTC increased with
\par
increasing sensitivity, but their model did not explicitly describe adenoma size. Ladabaum
\par
and colleagues(31) found that decreasing CTC sensitivity made it a less effective and more
\par
costly test. Telford and colleauges(35) found that increased sensitivity for \emph{advanced}
\par
\emph{adenomas} increased effectiveness and reduced costs for CTC. These reports suggest that
\par
when determining cost-effectiveness, assumptions about CTC accuracy to detect adenomas
\par
with high malignant potential are more important than assumptions about the overall
\par
accuracy or CTC, pointing to the importance of the underlying natural history model for
\par
colorectal cancer, and, in particular, assumptions about the rate at which adenomas transition
\par
to cancer.
\par
Although 8 out of 10 models found CTC to be either dominated by colonoscopy or have a
\par
less-attractive incremental cost-effectiveness ratio than colonoscopy, several noted that CTC
\par
could be cost-effective at a lower per-exam cost (7,30--32). For example, the
\par
microsimulation models found that CTC could be cost-effective compared to colonoscopy
\par
and other screening strategies if exams cost 22\% to 41\% of the cost of a colonoscopy
\par
(without polypectomy).
\par
One limitation of these studies is that while many models (30,32--35) considered less than
\par
perfect adherence to screening recommendations, all assumed the same degree of adherence
\par
