\par
Rutter et al. Page 9
\par
to colonoscopy and CTC screening. Few explored the impact of differential adherence to
\par
colonoscopy and the less invasive CTC exam. Sonnenberg and colleagues(30) provide
\par
graphs of the effect of adherence on cost per life-years gained, but did not provide
\par
information on the impact of adherence on life-years gained alone. In sensitivity analyses,
\par
Hassan and colleagues (33) found that CTC was equally effective and less costly than
\par
colonoscopy if the colonoscopy adherence rate was 60\% vs. 65\% for CTC, which is a less
\par
than 10\% improvement in relative adherence. Microsimulation model results (7) also
\par
suggested that CTC could be cost-effective compared to colonoscopy and other screening
\par
modalities if its availability would result in otherwise unscreened individuals being
\par
screened; however, relative adherence with CTC vs. other modalities would need to be at
\par
least 25\% higher for this to be the case.
\par
\textbf{5.0 Model Evaluation}
\par
Evaluating computer disease simulation models is difficult because of their complexity, as
\par
illustrated by the models comparing CTC and colonoscopy. Therefore, it is incumbent upon
\par
modelers to enable assessment of their models by providing a clear description of the
\par
assumptions and data sources used to develop their models. That is, modelers must strive to
\par
make their models ``transparent''.
\par
Model transparency often focuses on clearly describing the model structure and inputs. The
\par
structure of the natural history model is a basic assumption, often based on a combination of
\par
published findings and expert opinion. The assumed model structure is critical, because it
\par
provides a framework for combining information used to calibrate the model. However, the
\par
implications of using a particular model structure may not always be apparent. The interplay
\par
between model structure and calibration data can further confuse model evaluation. For
\par
example, because individuals with more than one adenoma have more opportunity to be
\par
detected, assumptions about the number of adenomas within individuals affect implied
\par
person-level detection rates.
\par
Communicating all the assumptions and implications of a model is challenging. However,
\par
the models evaluating CTC vs. colonoscopy provide examples of how these features might
\par
be presented. Model predictions are both the results from a model and an important, but
\par
underused, avenue for model evaluation (43). For example, when models are calibrated,
\par
selecting probabilities and parameters to reproduce known or expected results, the selected
\par
parameters may be published but calibration results are rarely included. Thus, readers are
\par
given little or no information about how well a model was able to fit different calibration
\par
points. Lee and colleagues (34) described their model's fit to data on adenoma prevalence
\par
and provided an online appendix with information about the model's fit to data on colorectal
\par
cancer incidence, mortality, and stage at diagnosis. Such goodness-of-fit results have the
\par
potential to provide an intuitive sense of the model to end users. Another example of the use
\par
of model predictions to aid in model evaluation pertains to adenoma ``dwell time'', the time
\par
from the onset of an adenoma of detectable size to clinically detected colorectal cancer.
\par
Dwell time is an important component of all colorectal cancer models as it represents the
\par
period of potential disease prevention through polypectomy or mortality reduction through
\par
earlier cancer diagnosis and treatment initiation. However dwell time is typically an output
\par
rather than an input of a disease simulation model, thus it is not easy to compare models on
\par
this aspect. Providing model predictions of the mean dwell time may provide a more
\par
intuitive indication of a model's structure and assumptions. Assumptions about dwell time
\par
are particularly important when comparing tests with different screening intervals. For
\par
example, Vijan and colleagues(32) found that shorter dwell times improved the cost-
\par
effectiveness of CTC carried out every 5 years relative to colonoscopy every 10 years.
\par
