\par
Rutter et al. Page 10
\par
Between-model comparisons provide another method for assessing structural assumptions
\par
on model predictions. The Cancer Incidence and Surveillance Modeling Network
\par
(CISNET), a National Cancer Institute-funded program that began in 2000 that has
\par
supported the development of multiple disease models for five cancer sites, has performed
\par
several comparative modeling analyses (1,4,7,44--47). These CISNET analyses, which
\par
compare independently developed models with different structures and assumptions, shed
\par
light on the impact and importance of the different assumptions. However, CISNET is an
\par
exception. Direct comparison of models is uncommon for modelers funded through different
\par
mechanisms.
\par
Finally, model validation provides an important, but often unavailable, method for
\par
estimating the accuracy of model predictions. Models are validated by testing their ability to
\par
predict outcomes that were not used for model calibration. Since the data needed for model
\par
development are often sparse, setting aside data for later model validation is often not
\par
feasible. For this reason, validation often requires new data that arise from studies published
\par
after model development is complete. For example, one of the microsimulation models used
\par
to evaluate CTC (CRC-SPIN) was later validated using a case-control study examining the
\par
effect of colonoscopy on colorectal cancer mortality (43). While validation often requires
\par
several assumptions related to both the population and the intervention model, it remains the
\par
best ``test'' of a model's predictive ability.
\par
\textbf{6.0 Discussion}
\par
Policy makers need to gather, synthesize, and interpret information to make decisions. If
\par
presented with multiple sources of information (e.g., results from primary studies), then each
\par
decision-maker carries out his or her own internal synthesis. Disease models offer a way to
\par
integrate and synthesize information using the structure of the natural history and
\par
intervention models. Disease models range in complexity from relatively simple decision
\par
trees, to more detailed cohort models and complex microsimulation models. Simpler models
\par
are relatively easy to construct and quickly implement, and may be a natural first step
\par
towards developing more complex models. Cohort models can be readily developed within a
\par
four- or even two-year funding cycle, offering a natural adjunct to projects focusing on
\par
accuracy of imaging tests or short-term outcomes from testing. Microsimulation models
\par
require longer-term support for their development. This investment is returned in a detailed
\par
model that can be applied to a wide range of problems.
\par
A common criticism regarding the use of disease models to aid decision-making is that
\par
because models are prediction tools, their results are, by nature, more uncertain than those
\par
directly derived from patient data. However, while they are indeed predictive, disease
\par
models are developed by integrating directly observed data and they synthesize information
\par
from a broad range of studies. Improving the transparency and interdisciplinary involvement
\par
in model development will ensure that model assumptions and structure are more broadly
\par
understood. In addition, working toward methods that produce standardized and clinically
\par
relevant, relatable model predictions will allow more direct model comparisons, easing
\par
communication of model assumptions and their implications.
\par
Models are especially useful when no long-term data are available to inform decision
\par
making. In these cases, models can provide robust predictions that cannot be reasonably
\par
achieved through other means. However, model predictions should always be considered in
\par
the context of all other available evidence. For example, the Medicare Evidence
\par
Development and Coverage Advisory Committee (MedCAC) considers a range of evidence
\par
and explicitly considers the quality of evidence before it gives its coverage recommendation
\par
to the Centers for Medicare and Medicaid Services (48). When considering coverage for
\par
