\par
Rutter et al. Page 11
\par
CTC screening, MedCAC considered evidence on the operating characteristics and risks of
\par
different screening tests for colorectal cancer based on a systematic review (6) and
\par
testimony from several experts in the fields of cancer screening, gastroenterology, and
\par
radiology, in addition to microsimulation model predictions (49)
\par
The use of computer disease models to guide decision-making is a natural result of the
\par
increasing amount and diversity of information available and these models are an important
\par
tool for estimating the comparative effectiveness of different imaging studies. Models are
\par
only as good as the current state of knowledge, and like other technologies they can and
\par
should be continually improved. One avenue for improvement is increased collaboration
\par
between radiologists and modelers. Radiologist can provide critical expertise during model
\par
development, guiding selection of key studies and information for calibration. In turn,
\par
modelers could advise radiologists on how to design efficient studies that will also yield data
\par
to fill knowledge gaps in faced when modeling disease processes. At study completion,
\par
these collaborations will result in the best possible predictions of long-term outcomes from
\par
short-term trial data.
