A WORKED EXAMPLE
\par
This worked example is derived from one of the
\par
scenarios of the pharmaceutical industry included
\par
in the calibration of ART. A worker is unloading ma-
\par
terial during a shift. For this example, we assume the
\par
worker is only conducting one task; however, ART
\par
can cope with multiple tasks and provides a time
\par
weighted average of the different tasks, including
\par
any unexposed periods.
\par
During this task, the worker is transferring a fine dust
\par
(dry product; 100\% active ingredient), with a transfer
\par
rate of 1--10 kg min 1 (routine transfer; drop height
\par
0.5 m) from a blender to a keg. The work is performed
\par
indoors (room size equals 300 m3) with a high ventila-
\par
tion rate of 30 air changes per hour. There are demon-
\par
strable and effective housekeeping practices in place.
\par
The activity is performed in the near field without
\par
any secondary source in the far field. The exposure is
\par
estimated for two distinct situations: (i) without any lo-
\par
cal controls in place and (ii) with a low level of contain-
\par
ment, which is not air tight. The relevant ART input
\par
parameters are given in Table 2.
\par
Figure 3 shows the geometric mean full-shift ex-
\par
posure predictions for the two situations. The point
\par
estimates of the geometric mean are clearly reduced
\par
with the revision to low level of containment (obvi-
\par
ously, revision to other ART options like moderate or
\par
high level of containment would have resulted in
\par
a stronger shift). However, the uncertainty as ex-
\par
pressed as the inter-quartile confidence interval is
\par
in both situations identical.
\par
Alternatively, updating the situation without
\par
local controls using 10 exposure measurements (geo-
\par
metric mean 20 mg m 3; geometric standard
\par
deviation 1.6; covering three sites, repeated measure-
\par
ments of one worker per site) resulted in a shift of the
\par
point estimate as well as a reduction of the uncertainty
\par
around the estimate. Although the shift of the point954 E. Tielemans et al.
