CONCLUSIONS AND RESEARCH NEEDS
\par
The ART approach to estimating inhalation expo-
\par
sure for regulatory risk assessment has the advantage
\par
of integrating the use of a robust mechanistic model
\par
with available exposure data. The tool is able to char-
\par
acterize exposure to dust, mist, and vapours in the ab-
\par
sence of exposure data but will provide more reliable
\par
estimates with additional measurement data. ART will
\par
be useful for risk assessment under REACH and with
\par
further development in other situations, such as under
\par
the European Chemical Agents Directive. The basis
\par
for the tool was, in the course of the development,
\par
peer reviewed by independent, leading experts from
\par
industry, research institutes, and public authorities.
\par
This ensured that consideration of both scientific
\par
and practical model issues was given during the
\par
course of the development of the ART.
\par
High-priority areas for further development in
\par
ART are listed below:
\par
Currently, ART can only take into account data
\par
that are perfectly representative of the assessment
\par
scenario. Ideally, the tool should also be able to in-
\par
tegrate exposure data across measurement series
\par
with varying levels of `representativeness' or
\par
`analogy'. First attempts have been made to define
\par
similarity algorithms to take these factors into ac-
\par
count in a Bayesian analyses (McNally et al., un-
\par
published data). However, further methodological
\par
work is needed to properly integrate partially
\par
analogous measurement series using a weight of
\par
evidence approach. The latter would facilitate op-
\par
timal mining of the ART exposure database that is
\par
currently under development and will be imple-
\par
mented in version 1.5.
\par
ART integrates mechanistic model outcomes and
\par
exposure data. This works well when both are rea-
\par
sonably consistent. If both are inconsistent this is
\par
an indicator that either the model is not accurate
\par
for that situation, incorrect input parameters are
\par
used or exposure data are not analogous. The
\par
mechanistic model results and posterior estimates
\par
are displayed side by side by the tool to allow
\par
comparison. However, the ART system has no
\par
methodology to cope with these inconsistencies.
\par
As also indicated by Hewett et al. (2006), further
\par
work is required to define guidelines how to make
\par
informed decisions in situations of inconsistent
\par
priors and likelihood distributions. Currently,
\par
there is little in the Bayesian literature on how
\par
to deal with these situations.
\par
Further alignment of ART with the REACH prac-
\par
tice and available REACH guidance. This is pos-
\par
sible after initial experience with the tool in 2010
\par
and 2011 and might cover various practical
\par
issues in the user interface and output.Overview of ART version 1.0 and research needs 955
\par
