\par
ART is assessing the exposure distribution of
\par
a scenario covering multiple companies or sites.
\par
Modifications of ART so that it can also provide
\par
company and site-specific estimates are necessary
\par
to be useful in, for example, the Chemical Agents
\par
Directive. This requires a restructuring of the
\par
underlying lognormal mixed effects model.
\par
User decision strategies, linked to ART predic-
\par
tions, to help identify whether collecting addi-
\par
tional measurements (reducing uncertainty) or
\par
implementing a control strategy (reducing expo-
\par
sure) would be more appropriate. ART gives
\par
formal assessment of uncertainty and thus also
\par
provides the opportunity to estimate the value of
\par
reducing the uncertainty before proceeding to im-
\par
plementation of controls. Existing approaches in
\par
other research areas like `value of information
\par
analyses' (Morgan and Henrion, 1990) are as yet
\par
unexplored in occupational hygiene and may be
\par
very useful in refining risk management decisions
\par
and enhancing its cost-effectiveness. These deci-
\par
sion strategies may be very useful in the context
\par
of ART but are equally relevant for other exposure
\par
models available.
\par
The validity and reproducibility of ART predic-
\par
tions needs to be evaluated in order to ensure
\par
a sound use of the model in the REACH context
\par
and beyond. Several hundred exposure measure-
\par
ments from various industrial sectors have been
\par
collated after the calibration. These measurement
\par
series form the basis for a cross-validation that is
\par
currently being conducted. In addition, workshops
\par
with experts are planned to explore inter-rater
\par
agreement when applying ART. First results are
\par
anticipated to become available in the near future
\par
and will help identify areas for improvement of
\par
the ART mechanistic model. Information gath-
\par
ered in these studies will also help further devel-
\par
oping the existing ART training material.
\par
Expansion of the ART methodology to include
\par
dermal exposure is important. Dermal exposure
\par
assessment is still in its infancy. Work on the
\par
conceptual modelling of dermal exposure
\par
(Schneider et al., 1999; van Wendel de Joode
\par
et al., 2003) and the RISKOFDERM project
\par
(van Hemmen et al., 2003) may provide a starting
\par
point for a Dermal ART, although much more
\par
work on mechanistic understanding of the expo-
\par
sure process and collection of exposure data is
\par
needed. Measurements under experimental as
\par
well as under real world workplace conditions
\par
using standardized methodologies are required956 E. Tielemans et al.
\par
to built an evidence base for development of
\par
a dermal ART.
\par
Integration of ART predictions of external inhala-
\par
tion and dermal exposure with approaches for
\par
modelling internal dose (e.g. PBPK models)
\par
would potentially capture the entire source to out-
\par
come continuum, which is a prerequisite for a more
\par
informative systems approach in toxicology as
\par
proposed by NRC (2007).
\par
ART is considered to be an evolving system. The
\par
approach has a conceptual basis, which is a good start-
\par
ing point for further development. Continued research
\par
activities are needed to move exposure assessment
\par
science forward in REACH and beyond.
