Introduction
\par
Across the international research community, there is a growing and evolving research
\par
literature on Building Information Modelling (BIM). This working paper takes a snapshot of
\par
the recently published research. Rather than discussing the longer-term evolution of the
\par
research debates, or reviewing the key texts, such as the BIM Handbook by Eastman, et al
\par
(2008), the strategy is to focus on studies published in the first six months of 2012; and use
\par
this snapshot to discuss the current areas of focus in the international research; and how
\par
these relate to and inform changes in policy and practice.
\par
Practical attention is concentrated on the implementation of BIM by a desire to collate and
\par
use ope shareale asset iforatio1 through the life-cycle of buildings and
\par
infrastructure. For example, the UK government motivations for using BIM are to address
\par
issues of cost, value and carbon (BIS/Industry Working Group 2010, see also
\par
www.bimtaskgroup.org). The debate is shaped in terms of the strategic rather than
\par
technical issues, engaging public and private-sector clients and ensuring they understand
\par
the value of information to the operation of their business; as well as an output of capital
\par
expenditure. The Construction Operations Building Information Exchange (CoBIE) (East
\par
2007) has become mobilised as an initial format for translating data between packages at a
\par
series of data drops to the liet, as it is opliat ith duildigSart Idustr Foudatio
\par
Classes (IFCs).
\par
Within the UK, this debate about implementing open shareable asset information is not only
\par
about buildings, but also about infrastructure. As many leading clients are in infrastructure,
\par
there is a strong desire from the Institution of Civil Engineers (ICE) to ensure that BIM
\par
includes more than buildings (ICE 2011). On projects such as Crossrail and by clients such as
\par
Heathrow, maps are becoming used as a point of access into model and document
\par
information. There is some tension between the use of proprietary tools and the
\par
specification of open shareable asset information, especially where the rich intelligence and
\par
linkages that clients enjoy in particular tools are hard to translate.
\par
Because of both the speed at which the research literature is itself developing and shaping,
\par
and the desire to inform such policy and practice in the UK, this review uses a simple and
\par
fast method. Recently published research was collated as a starting point for discussion at a
\par
workshop on Learig fro Others, as part of a UK BIM Task Group working group in June
\par
2012. It is hoped this may also inform a discussion of the research challenges, contributions
\par
of research to BIM practice; and future research opportunities.
