\par
Method
\par
Research is a process of inquiry: in it, automated search is a useful tool, but judgement is
\par
vital both with regard to what and how to search and the interpretation of the findings. This
\par
study began on June 20 2012, when all 13 artiles i Eglish that refer to building
\par
information modellig i ere doloaded fro Google Sholar. This search was
\par
then repeated on June 24 2012, with all 428 artiles i Eglish that refer to building
\par
information modelig or uildig iforatio odellig or uildig iforatio odel i
\par
2012 downloaded from Google Scholar, with citations included. It is this latter search that is
\par
used in the analysis. From the 408 unique articles, 160 articles relevant to the topic of BIM
\par
that are included here in the bibliography.
\par
The references were put into Endnotes and, on the basis of their title and abstract, roughly
\par
coded into preliminary groups. At first the aim was to organise the groups that emerged into
\par
high-level sets using the headline areas addressed by the UK BIM strategy group: a) design
\par
and construction implementation; b) lifecycle (operations and maintenance); c) clients; d)
\par
commercial, legal and intellectual property; and e) training and education. The attempt to
\par
map the literature to these areas has however been partial (with most overlap in the first
\par
two of these areas). This is both as there are important themes that are emerging in the
\par
research that are not yet reflected in policy; and because there are important areas of policy
\par
and practice that are no longer active areas of research.
\par
Google Scholar was used, as a more inclusive search engine than traditional academic tools
\par
such as EBSCO Search, to rapidly generate an indicative snapshot of recent areas of work.
\par
Like Google searches, the boundaries and replication of the search over time are
\par
problematic; and the results needed substantial filtering and interpretation. Even in this
\par
short timeframe, the search failed to pick up on important reports and articles that were
\par
known to be published (e.g. Computer Integrated Construction Research Program 2012)
\par
included some articles that are online, but published earlier (e.g. Greenwood, Lockley et al.
\par
2010; Gu, et al. 2010; Whyte, Lindkvist et al. 2011); and included work that referred to BIM
\par
rather than have it as a focus (e.g. Andoh, Su et al. 2012; Yeheyis, Hewage et al. 2012). The
\par
benefit of this approach is that it has uncovered interesting recent work that a
\par
knowledgeable researcher in the area might not have found in established outlets.
\par
At this stage there has been no attempt to systematically read all the articles. Instead, a
\par
bibliography is provided, along with a commentary on the themes that emerge.
\par
Emergent Themes
\par
The annotated bibliography of research on BIM that was published in the last 6 months,
\par
around which this discussion paper is built, is published in Appendix A. Major themes
\par
identified in this bibliography are:
\par
WP 5, v 1.0 4Design Innovation Research Centre
\par
Working Paper, Number 5
\par
1. BIM, Lifecycle and Sustainability
\par
2. BIM in Design and Construction
\par
3. BIM Technologies
\par
4. Using BIM
\par
5. Professions and BIM
\par
The main themes above summarise a wider set of topics identified in the literature, which
\par
are discussed in the sections below, and used to categorise the bibliography. Across these
\par
papers, there is ongoing work to customize the use of BIM to a wide range of building and
\par
infrastructure types: hospital design (Osan, Hule et al. 2012); accessible homes (Jrade and
\par
Valdez); sustainable homes (Mah 2012) water transmission pipelines (Gopala Raju
\par
Doraiswamy 2011; Alin, Iorio et al. 2012); subway infrastructure (Marzouk and Aty 2012);
\par
factory planning (Chen 2012). Other uses include historical reconstruction (Boeykens, Himpe
\par
et al. 2012); and the tracking of particular materials such as precast/prestressed concrete to
\par
the construction site (Venugopal, Eastman et al. 2012).
\par
While some of this research is well developed and published in recognised research outlets,
\par
the bibliography also includes work in progress in the form or working papers, conference
\par
papers. There was undergraduate, as well as postgraduate, work included in the review; and
\par
a number of patents (e.g. Kinghorn and Willems 2012; Li, Chen et al. 2012; Mclean and
\par
Quincey 2012; Omansky and Kanner 2012; Shear, Awe et al. 2012).
\par
There is also diversity in the approach to research: some research is normative, proposing
\par
frameworks and tools to guide practice; other research is laboratory based, developing new
\par
technologies; while other studies are empirical, seeking to articulate the realities of practice.
\par
Many studies propose BIM as a solution: an example is a study in which BIM is proposed as a
\par
potential solution based on a study of 145 change orders across eight projects in Georgia and
\par
Alabama rather than without evidence of the real-world impact of BIM technologies (Olsen,
\par
Killingsworth et al. 2012). Below the shape of the literatures associated with the above topics,
\par
and the associated references are briefly discussed using examples from the annotated
\par
bibliography which follows.
\par
1. BIM, Lifecycle and Sustainability
\par
There is a substantial emphasis in the literature on research that links BIM and sustainability
\par
with 42 articles published in the first 6 months of 2012. Though smaller in scale, there is also
\par
a notable focus on Facilities Management (FM) and lifecycle uses of BIM, with particular
\par
focus on areas such as managing waste.
\par
1.1 Sustainability
\par
While carbon is a motivation for policy of BIM, the connections between digital technologies
\par
and sustainability are not well developed in policy and practice. There is however research
\par
activity that is beginning to develop new tools to use BIM in order to address a range of
\par
sustainability concerns. Russell-Smith and Lepech (2012), for example, develop an activity-WP 5, v 1.0 5Design Innovation Research Centre
\par
Working Paper, Number 5
\par
based method for lifecycle assessment, through modelling and benchmarking of building
\par
construction. The sustainability concerns addressed by such tools include: the assessment of
\par
environmental impacts (Lu, Wu et al. 2012); consideration of waste management issues
\par
(Arif, Bendi et al. 2012; O'Reilly 2012; Rajendran and Gomez); guidance to designers on
\par
environmental issues (Capper, Matthews et al. 2012; Firoz and Rao 2012; Geyer 2012;
\par
Hetherington, Laney et al. 2012; Kanters, Dubois et al. 2012; Mirani and Mahdjoubi 2012;
\par
Park, Park et al. 2012; Peters 2012; Rekola, M\"akel\"ainen et al. 2012; Zeng 2012); and a
\par
response to a government strategy for carbon reductions in both current and future building
\par
stock (McAuley, Hore et al. 2012).
\par
1.2 FM and lifecycle
\par
Recent studies are also examining the use of BIM throughout the lifecycle of construction
\par
projects, addressing issues around as-builts (Xuesong, Eybpoosh et al. 2012); facilities
\par
management and maintenance (Arayici, Onyenobi et al. 2012; Ebinger and Madritsch 2012;
\par
Shen, Hao et al. 2012); and looking at the life-cycle of particular materials such as concrete
\par
(Borrmann, Lukas et al. 2012). There is work on building services, for example work on
\par
Heating Ventilation and Air Conditioning (HVAC) proposing a 3D analyser for lifecycle, with
\par
graph based modelling for fault detection (Zimmermann, Lu et al. 2012). There are also a
\par
few studies on renovation and on reconstruction (Boeykens, Himpe et al. 2012; INC 2012;
\par
Murthy, Boardman et al. 2012) and on waste management and minimization (O'Reilly 2012;
\par
Rajendran and Gomez 2012; Yeheyis, Hewage et al. 2012).
\par
2. BIM in Design and Construction
\par
Research is split pretty evenly between a focus on the construction site, manufacturing and
\par
supply, with research on equipment and health and safety, and a focus on design and/or
\par
project or programme management. The review picked up ten papers relating to lean
\par
construction and off-site manufacturing, but these have been excluded except where they
\par
specifically reference BIM.
\par
2.1 Construction site, manufacturing and supply
\par
As the bibliography shows, there is ongoing work on issues relating to BIM and the assembly
\par
of buildings on site (Arayici, Egbu et al. 2012; Engstr\"om 2012; Hilbert, Scherer et al. 2012;
\par
Sidawi 2012). Examples of this work include studies on crane instability in high winds
\par
(Hasan, Zaman et al.); lifts (Lee, Cho et al. 2012; Zhang and Hammad 2012) and site
\par
utilization using data from BIM (Alagarsamy 2012). There is ork fro a suppliers
\par
perspective (Gillenwater 2012).
\par
2.2 Construction safety
\par
There is a notable, though small, focus on safety, with recent studies in the USA and
\par
Australia using BIM to develop new techniques for safety compliance checking as well as
\par
consideration of safety in design (Chun, Li and Skitmore 2012; Zhang, Lee et al. 2012).
\par
WP 5, v 1.0 6Design Innovation Research Centre
\par
Working Paper, Number 5
\par
2.3 Design, project and programme management
\par
The use of BIM earlier in the process, in the design stage, is considered by a number of
\par
authors (e.g. Chun, Li et al. 2012; Fleischmann and Menges 2012; Fouchal, Hassan et al.
\par
2012; Mela, Tiainen et al. 2012; Raisbeck 2012; Sharma 2012; Whyte, Lobo et al. 2012).
\par
These papers overlap with the work on architecture, but tend to take a broader view of the
\par
design of the built environment considering practices across occupational boundaries and
\par
professional groups. There are also a number of studies looking at effects on project
\par
managers and project management (e.g. Cheng and Wang, 2012; Di Marco, et al. 2012).
\par
3. BIM Technologies
\par
In the more technical literatures, there is a particular focus on developing new tools for the
\par
integration of knowledge and the interoperability of systems; on naming and code-checking;
\par
semantics, modelling; data capture and connections between geographic information
\par
systems (GIS) and BIM. There are also a number of generic papers on BIM (Isikdag,
\par
Underwood et al. 2012; Tah 2012; Vries 2012; Wang, Zheng et al. 2012); and work on
\par
developing technologies in particular focused areas such as cloud computing (Redmond,
\par
Hore et al. 2012).
\par
3.1 Integration and interoperability
\par
There is work on interoperability and the technical and social challenges of using
\par
information (e.g. Clark and Bettin 2012; Fisher 2012; Forgues, Iordanova et al. 2012; Jacob
\par
and Varghese 2012; Viljoen 2012; Wu and Hsieh 2012). This develops new tools for both the
\par
integration of knowledge in software solutions; and for interoperability between packages.
\par
3.2 Naming and code-checking
\par
There is a substantial literature on the naming conventions and code checking tools that can
\par
facilitate open shareable asset information (e.g. El-Diraby 2012; Fox 2012; Kramer, Klein et
\par
al. 2012; Laakso and Kiviniemi 2012; Lee and Jeong 2012). Within this literature there is
\par
onging work on IFCs, both in relation to their history and their future development, as well
\par
as other processes associated with open shareable asset information.
\par
3.3 Semantics, modelling
\par
There are a number of papers that refer to semantics (e.g. B\'eh\'e, et al 2012; Dom\'inguez et al
\par
2012; or investigate other aspects of modelling and simulation, including agent-based
\par
approaches. Also classified in this sub-theme are papers that use fuzzy information for tool
\par
seletio Ceika ad \"Ozta\c si ; Jiag et al, , or ontology-based approaches
\par
(Karhela, et al. 2012; Lee and Jeong 2012; Venugopal et al. 2012; Zhong et al. 2012).
\par
3.4 Data capture
\par
There is new work on laser scanning and the interfaces between data-capture and Building
\par
Information Models (dai, Prii\v cei et al. ; Wang and Cho 2012) with particular
\par
projects developing methods for creating semantic models (Dumitru 2012) and exploring
\par
WP 5, v 1.0 7Design Innovation Research Centre
\par
Working Paper, Number 5
\par
the potential to combine data from laser scans and Radio Frequency Identification Devices
\par
(RFID) (Valero, Adan et al. 2012).
\par
3.5 GIS
\par
BIM is also being mobilised with GIS in planning applications (Thompson, Horne et al. 2011;
\par
Xu, Tucker et al. 2012) and using BIM and GIS at an urban level in the Peipu township in
\par
Taipai (Lee 2012). Development research on GIS includes a study seeking to combine crowd-
\par
sourcing and GIS for multi-level interior environments (Goetz 2012); and develops new
\par
techniques for utlizing BIM and GIS (Hijazi, Ehlers et al. 2012).
\par
4. Using BIM
\par
Alongside the research that is focused on tool development, there is research that is
\par
examining the use of BIM, its economic, political and social implications, the attitudes and
\par
behaviours of users, and issues arising during its implementation.
\par
4.1 Economic impact
\par
There is now work developing tools and metrics to assess the economic impacts of BIM (e.g.
\par
Lee, Park et al. 2012; Love and Sing 2012; Schiuma, Carlucci et al. 2012; Succar, Sher et al.
\par
2012). This is an area of particular practitioner interest, as BIM implementation becomes
\par
mandated in some regions, and as firms seek to understand the commercial benefits of the
\par
technologies.
\par
4.2 Political and social implications
\par
There is also considerable research on the political and social implications of these
\par
technologies (Gajendran and Brewer 2012; Grilo, Zutshi et al. 2012; Gustavsson and Gohary
\par
2012; Lahdenper\"a 2012; Nawaz, Efstratiou et al. 2012; Neff, Jordan et al. 2012). This work
\par
looks at how the technologies change and mediate interactions across the networks of
\par
workers that become involved as multiple parties collaborate.
\par
4.3 Attitudes, behaviours and implementation
\par
Other research explores the attitudes and behaviours (Brewer and Gajendran 2012; Hatem,
\par
Kwan et al. 2012) experimentally to examine the effectiveness of different strategies for
\par
communication. There is also more contextual work, that examines a range of issues of
\par
implementation in relation to changes within different countries and contexts, such as
\par
Australia, Ireland and the UK (Kraatz and Hampson 2012; McAuley, Hore et al. 2012;
\par
Underwood and Khosrowshahi 2012).
\par
4.4 Education
\par
As BIM strategies are implemented by governments, there is interest within the higher
\par
education sector in new ways of teaching BIM. Papers report on experiments with BIM in
\par
the academic design studio (Ambrose 2012); studies of student visualization (Glick, Porter et
\par
al. 2012); teaching sustainable building (Korkmaz 2012) and interoperable learning (Leed
\par
WP 5, v 1.0 8Design Innovation Research Centre
\par
Working Paper, Number 5
\par
and del Puerto 2012); as well as the learning cycle of graduates and the industry
\par
expectations (Ahn, Annie et al. 2012; Chen and Shaurette).
\par
5. Professions and BIM
\par
Some of the research on BIM takes a particular disciplinary or professional perspective.
\par
There is a literature specifically written for particular professions, which provides practical
\par
guidance to help them to make sense of BIM; and another literature, which is about
\par
professional interactions and thus more theoretically focused on understanding the changes
\par
in roles that accompany BIM. This work can be crudely categorised by professional area:
\par
5.1 Architecture
\par
There is a growing literature about architecture and BIM (Boyd 2012; Ceccato 2012; Coates
\par
and Arayici 2012; Coenders 2012; Fouche, Smallwood et al. 2012; Leeuwis 2012; Lillicrap
\par
2012; Segonds, Nelson et al. 2012; Svoboda, Nov\'ak et al. 2012). While this overlaps with the
\par
literature described above in section 2.3 on design, it is distinguished here as the focus on
\par
the architect, and questions about how to use BIM in architectural practice, is particularly,
\par
salient in some of this writing.
\par
5.2 Quantity surveying and cost estimating
\par
There is also a literature that sets out frameworks for guidance of quantity surveyors and
\par
cost estimators (Cheung, Rihan et al. 2012; Marzouk and Hisham 2012; Quek 2012; Towey
\par
2012; Tse and Wong 2012). There are expectations that this work will be changed by the
\par
widespread use of BIM and consideration of how these activities can be achieved through
\par
the new tools.
\par
5.3 Civil engineering
\par
While there are no papers that specifically focus on BIM and the civil engineer, as shown in
\par
the bibliography, there is research on the use of BIM in transport, bridge construction and
\par
subways. Hence there are a number of reserachers using the term BIM to refer not only to
\par
buildings, but also to similar developments in information management for infrastructure
\par
and heavy civil engineering projects. This enables a cross-fertilization of ideas across
\par
infrastruction and buildings.
\par
Research Contributions and Opportunities
\par
The approach to this review reveals the sheer scale of the research literature, which makes
\par
it difficult for any individual or small team to read everything related to the BIM topic, even
\par
over such a short timescale. This may lead to both specialization within particular themes,
\par
and to authors referring to seminal papers to represent concerns within a wider theme
\par
within the literature.
\par
These studies reveal strong current research interests, for example in developing new BIM
\par
tools that address lifecycle and sustainability concerns; in helping professionals to make
\par
WP 5, v 1.0 9Design Innovation Research Centre
\par
Working Paper, Number 5
\par
sense of these tools in practice; in the political and social implications of new ways of
\par
working and new techniques for checking models. Work examining technical aspects, of the
\par
use of standards such as IFC or COBIE, or technologies such as laser scanning and data-
\par
bases, may not be fully captured in this where it does not use the more generic term
\par
duildig Iforatio Modellig though the annotated bibliography does indicate this as a
\par
strong ongoing area of research. There are also a set of studies that respond to policy
\par
making in various countries, Australia, Ireland, UK, and seek to critique and make sense of
\par
the industry developments, from the perspective of various professional stakeholders.
\par
Consideration of the themes in this literature does indicate some possible directions for
\par
future research. There is, for example, a lack of work that examines the use of BIM
\par
strategically from the client perspective. This work is needed both to provide a practical
\par
guide and to understand the client role in different models of practice. There are also
\par
promising new directions of research that explore the synthesis of data from data capture,
\par
modelling and GIS, and that examine data over the life-cycle of buildings and infrastructure
\par
assets.
