\par
The value of information is a general and broadly applicable concept that has been used for several
\par
decades to aid in making decisions in the face of uncertainty. Yet there are relatively few examples of
\par
its use in ecology and natural resources management, and almost none that are framed in terms of the
\par
future impacts of management decisions. In this paper we discuss the value of information in a context of
\par
adaptive management, in which actions are taken sequentially over a timeframe and both future resource
\par
conditions and residual uncertainties about resource responses are taken into account. Our objective is to
\par
derive the value of reducing or eliminating uncertainty in adaptive decision making. We describe several
\par
measures of the value of information, with each based on management objectives that are appropriate
\par
for adaptive management. We highlight some mathematical properties of these measures, discuss their
\par
geometries, and illustrate them with an example in natural resources management. Accounting for the
\par
value of information can help to inform decisions about whether and how much to monitor resource
\par
conditions through time.
\par
Published by Elsevier B.V.
\par
1. Introduction
\par
In recent years there has been a growing concern about uncer-
\par
tainty in natural resources management, and a recognition of the
\par
impact uncertainty can have on effective resource management.
\par
Vulnerability analysis, risk assessment, and adaptive management
\par
are three of many approaches that explicitly account for uncer-
\par
tainty and attempt to factor it into resource assessments and
\par
management policies. A particularly important source of uncer-
\par
tainty in natural resources concerns a lack of understanding
\par
about the factors and processes influencing resource dynamics
\par
(Williams, 2010b). This ``structural'' or ``epistemic'' uncertainty
\par
is often expressed through models that incorporate different
\par
hypotheses about how the resource system works. Here incomplete
\par
knowledge of natural systems is distinguished from other sources
\par
of irreducible uncertainty such as demographic and environmental
\par
variation.
\par
Structural uncertainty can limit management effectiveness
\par
because in its presence decision making relies on a less than com-
\par
plete understanding of system responses. Conversely, the reduction
\par
or elimination of that uncertainty can result in improved manage-
\par
ment that is better tailored to actual resource dynamics. It often is
\par
useful to measure the loss associated with structural uncertainty,
\par
or equivalently, to determine the potential gain in resource value
\par
that is possible with its reduction or elimination.
\par
The ``value of information'' is a generic term for potential man-
\par
agement value that is foregone under uncertainty. Among other
\par
things, the value of information can inform an assessment of the
\par
potential effectiveness of monitoring and analysis to reduce that
\par
uncertainty. For example, the value gained by reducing uncer-
\par
tainty can be compared against opportunity and other costs that are
\par
associated with collecting and analyzing information, to determine
\par
whether monitoring should be undertaken.
\par
The concept of a value of information has been around for sev-
\par
eral decades and is now well developed. Raiffa and Schlaifer (1961)
\par
provided one of the first seminal treatments, coining the name and
\par
developing many of its key expressions. Since then it has been
\par
applied in economics, finance, medicine, engineering and many
\par
other fields (e.g., Frauendorfer, 1992; Bontems and Thomas, 2000;
\par
Karnon, 2002; Koerkamp et al., 2006; Eidsvik et al., 2008). How-
\par
ever, there have been relatively few applications in ecology and
\par
natural resources management, and almost all of them involve an
\par
assessment of the utility of information with non-iterative decision
\par
making.
\par
Here we describe and discuss the value of information in a con-
\par
text of adaptive management with iterative decision making. A
\par
useful framework for adaptive management recognizes manage-
\par
ment objectives, a range of potential actions, models that forecast
\par
resource changes, and measures of confidence in those models
\par
(Williams et al., 2007). In adaptive management, actions are taken
\par
sequentially over a timeframe, with the choice of an action at any
