\par
and future value. There is an explicit recognition of uncertainty
\par
about the biological processes influencing resources dynamics, and
\par
the uncertainty is tracked through time along with the status of
\par
the managed resource. The generic idea is to reduce uncertainty
\par
by monitoring and assessing resource responses to management
\par
actions, so that management can be improved over time based on
\par
the accumulation of learning (Walters, 1986; Williams et al., 2007).
\par
The objectives of this paper are to describe the value of reduc-
\par
ing or eliminating uncertainty through adaptive decision making.
\par
We describe several measures of the value of information in terms
\par
of management objectives that are appropriate for adaptive deci-
\par
sion making. The focus initially is on the expected value of perfect
\par
information, and later we include the expected value of partial and
\par
sample information. We highlight the mathematical properties of
\par
these values, develop the geometry of the expected value of perfect
\par
information, and provide a natural resource example to illustrate
\par
the value of information.
\par
2. Resource management under uncertainty
\par
A general statement of the natural resource problem under con-
\par
sideration here involves a dynamic resource system that changes
\par
