Recognition of uncertainty and the opportunity to reduce it is
\par
the basis of an adaptive approach to resource management, which
\par
emphasizes the influence of actions not only on resource status
\par
but also on learning through time (Walters, 1986). In this partic-
\par
ular case, learning is represented by changes in the model state
\par
that occur in response to management actions, as in Eq. (7). At
\par
each time the state of the system is identified through monitoring,
\par
and the model state is updated. The model state reveals the resid-
\par
ual uncertainty about resource structure, and systematic change
\par
in that uncertainty through time represents incremental learning
\par
based on the accumulation of monitoring data. Learning through
\par
management, with the use of what is learned to guide future man-
\par
agement actions, is definitive of adaptive management (Williams
\par
et al., 2007).
