\par
tation and the disruption of natural succession patterns. Scrub
\par
habitat composition and density were maintained historically by
\par
fire, but fragmentation and active fire suppression have resulted
\par
in unchecked scrub succession to the point where scrub-jays are
\par
unable to sustain viable populations (Breininger and Carter, 2003).
\par
Land managers therefore rely on prescribed fires and mechanical
\par
thinning of overgrown vegetation to produce conditions believed
\par
to be optimal for maintaining stable scrub-jay populations (Johnson
\par
et al., in press).
\par
Management actions are performed at the scale of individual fire
\par
management units (FMU) within a management reserve. Reserve
\par
managers have expressed a desire to base annual decisions on a
\par
combination of habitat and occupancy states of scrub-jay territories
\par
in an FMU. Florida scrub-jays are highly territorial, with a fam-
\par
ily (generally a breeding pair and one or more juvenile `helpers')
\par
defending a territory of approximately 10-ha when the landscape
\par
is at carrying capacity (Carter et al., 2006; Breininger et al., 1995).
\par
To incorporate the scales of both management and the biological
\par
system, we define habitat and occupancy states on the basis of a
\par
regular grid of 10-ha cells within an FMU.
\par
If an FMU contains N 10-ha scrub-jay territory patches, its state is
\par
described at the beginning of each management cycle by the num-
\par
ber of territories in each of 4 possible succession stages -- short
\par
(n1), optimal height with open sandy patches (n2), optimal height
\par
with no sandy patches (n3), and tall (n4) -- and the number of ter-
\par
ritories occupied by scrub-jays (c), which can take any value from
\par
zero to the total number of territories. At any given time step, each
\par
territory of the FMU can be in only one of the 4 habitat types and
\par
is either occupied or not. The number of possible states an FMU
\par
can take is the product of the combination of 4 habitat types dis-
\par
tributed across N territories and the number of territories that are
\par
occupied. Let N
