7. Discussion
\par
Thus, EVPXI expresses the minimum loss in value associated with
\par
qk, except in this instance the loss function for the value of infor-
\par
mation incorporates residual uncertainty about k{$\prime$}
\par
. In the example,
\par
EVPXI averages over the distribution of survival rates, using a loss
\par
function that includes uncertainty about recruitment. EVPXI(i,qk)
\par
has the same general computing form as EVPI(i,q) except it uses
\par
a different loss function, one that includes the conditional distri-
\par
bution qk{$\prime\vert$}k. Thus, EVPXI(i,qk) is ``conditional,'' in that the value of
\par
information it expresses will vary depending on the form of this
\par
distribution. Because the terms in Eq. (32) are piecewise linear, the
\par
expected value of partial information is as well.
\par
The expected value of partial information offers a way to address
\par
system complexity that might otherwise be problematic for adap-
\par
tive optimization. By investigating the components of uncertainty
\par
with EVPXI, one can compare the relative importance of differ-
\par
ent uncertainty sources and use the comparison to decide which
\par
uncertainty factor is most important to focus on with adaptive
\par
management.
\par
Expected value of sample information. It often is useful to consider
\par
the gain in value that could result from a sample of observations
\par
that produce less than perfect information. This ``expected value
\par
of sample information'' is sometimes written as EVSI (Yokota and
\par
Thompson, 2004)
\par
To simplify notation, consider a situation at time t in which the
\par
system state is i and model state is q. Optimization as in Eq. (13)
\par
produces Vt[i, q], along with a stochastic transition to a new system
\par
state j and an updated model state q{$\prime$} at time t + 1. Now imagine
\par
that somehow one could know the new system state j and updated
\par
model state q{$\prime$} at time t. With this new information, optimization
\par
could then produce Vt[j, q{$\prime$}]. The difference between Vt[i, q] and Vt[j,
\par
q{$\prime$}] represents the information value for the data that produced the
\par
updated system state j and derived model state q{$\prime$}
\par
. Of course, this
\par
difference is conditional on the state j that is produced. By averaging
\par
over the all system states using the average transition probabilities
\par
