\par
The value of information is a potentially useful but infrequently
\par
utilized construct in natural resources management. The few exam-
\par
ples documented in the literature typically are limited to decision
\par
making at a single point in time, and do not account formally
\par
for the future impacts of decisions (see Runge et al., 2011 for a
\par
recent example). Yet a great many important problems in natu-
\par
ral resources management are fundamentally sequential in nature,
\par
with the opportunity to improve resource understanding as deci-
\par
sions are made through time.
\par
The value of information as discussed here links naturally to
\par
economics through the framing of VOI in terms of potential looses,
\par
including opportunity costs, and potential benefits to be accrued
\par
through the reduction of uncertainty. It is straightforward to frame
\par
the decision making problem as aggressively seeking to eliminate
\par
uncertainty when the benefit for doing so exceeds the cost, but
\par
adopting less informative strategy when the cost in information is
\par
high relative to benefits.
\par
We have discussed 3 different measures for the value of infor-
\par
mation, namely the expected value of perfect, partial and sample
\par
information. In each case the notation makes clear that the mea-
\par
sure is a function of the model state, and is parameterized by the
\par
system state. The value of information will vary with the amount of
\par
uncertainty (i.e., the model state), and decline to zero as the most
\par
appropriate model is recognized. It also will vary with the state of
\par
the resource -- for a given model state the value of information may
\par
be high in one resource state and low in another (Fig. 5 and the
\par
above example).
\par
The value of information will exhibit general trends through
\par
time. Because adaptive management tends to identify the most
\par
appropriate model as decision making progresses, the model state
\par
can be expected to converge to a unit vector representing that
\par
model. In turn, the value of information is expected to decline as
\par
uncertainty is reduced. One implication is that the value of elimi-
\par
nating any residual uncertainty is expected to decrease over time.
\par
A few points about differences among the measures of informa-
\par
tion value are noteworthy. One is that the expected values of partial
\par
information do not sum to the expected value of perfect informa-
\par
tion. For example, if uncertainty is represented by the indices k and
\par
k{$\prime$}
\par
, then the measures EVPXI(i, qk) and EVPXI(i, qk{$\prime$} ), representing the
\par
expected values of partial information for each uncertainty factor,
\par
need not sum to the value of perfect information:
\par
Like EVPI, the expected value of sample information is a function
\par
of the system state i and model state q.
\par
In the context of adaptive management, the key difference
\par
between EVSI and EVPI is seen by comparing Eqs. (22) and (33).
\par
Thus, EVPI in Eq. (22) uses an average of model-specific optimal val-
\par
ues across model probabilities, and EVSI in Eq. (33) uses an average
\par
of weighted combinations of these values
