\par
The notation makes clear that EVSI and EVPI both are functions
\par
of model state q, and both are conditional on system state i. Like
\par
EVPI, the expected value of sample information is piecewise linear
\par
For that reason alone it seems reasonable that the 2 EVPXI values
\par
would not necessarily relate to EVPI in a straightforward way.
\par
A second point of comparison concerns the value of sample
\par
information. From Eqs. (22) and (34) it is clear that the only sub-
\par
stantive difference between EVPI and EVSI are the multiplicands for
\par
q(k) in the 2 expressions. Thus, EVPI uses model-specific value func-
\par
tions, whereas EVSI uses a linear combination of these functions.
\par
The net effect of this difference is that EVPI is effectively an upper3436 B.K. Williams et al. / Ecological Modelling 222 (2011) 3429-- 3436
\par
bound on EVSI for any particular system and model state (Yokota
\par
and Thompson, 2004).
\par
A possible application of the value of information for adap-
\par
tive management involves a decision about whether to conduct
\par
monitoring or not (McDonald-Madden et al., 2010). The amount of
\par
monitoring (including the possibility of foregoing monitoring alto-
\par
gether) can be incorporated in adaptive management as a decision
\par
to be made sequentially. For example, a decision about whether to
\par
monitor might depend on a combination of resource conditions
\par
and the amount of uncertainty, with monitoring deferred if the
\par
resource is abundant and well understood (Hauser et al., 2006). One
\par
of the metrics for the value of information could be used in such an
\par
assessment to decide if resource understanding is adequate to defer
\par
monitoring, through a comparison of the value gained by additional
\par
monitoring against the cost of acquiring the information. In the con-
\par
text of adaptive decision making, a decision to forego monitoring
\par
at a given time step could be handled naturally, simply by using the
\par
information about system state from the previous time for current
\par
decision making. In consequence, the model state from the previ-
\par
ous time would remain unchanged, i.e. the same model state would
\par
be used in successive time periods.
\par
Finally, a natural extension of the above treatment of VOI would
\par
focus on systems that include both partial observability and struc-
\par
tural uncertainty (Williams, 2009). Assuming partial observability
\par
but complete certainty as to model form, a VOI function that is
\par
analogous to Eq. (22) would be a function of belief state only:
\par
EVPI(b) =
\par
b(i)Vt[i] - Vt[b], (37)
\par
i
\par
where the ``belief state'' b represents a distribution of likelihoods
\par
over the possible system states, Vt[i] is the optimal value func-
\par
tion corresponding to state i, and Vt[b] is based on the average of
\par
state-specific value functions (Williams, 2009). A measure of the
\par
true value of information for processes that include both forms
\par
of uncertainty should account for both uncertainty components.
\par
Useful investigations might include comparative assessments of a
\par
``partial value of information'' from including one source of uncer-
\par
tainty but not the other, and a comparison of these partial values
\par
against the value of information resulting from the inclusion of
\par
both.
