using computer models for complex physical
\par
systems
\par
D Williamson" and M Goldstein
\par
Durham University, Durham, UK
\par
In this paper, we discuss combining expert knowledge and computer simulators in order to provide
\par
decision support for policy makers managing complex physical systems. We allow future states of the
\par
complex system to be viewed after initial policy is made, and for those states to influence revision of
\par
policy. The potential for future observations and intervention impacts heavily on optimal policy for
\par
today and this is handled within our approach. We show how deriving policy dependent system
\par
uncertainty using computer models leads to an intractable backwards induction problem for the
\par
resulting decision tree. We introduce an algorithm for emulating an upper bound on our expected loss
\par
surface for all possible policies and discuss how this might be used in policy support. To illustrate our
\par
methodology, we look at choosing an optimal C02 abatement strategy, combining an intermediate
\par
complexity climate model and an economic utility model with climate data.
\par
Journal of the Operational Research Society (2012) 63, 1021-1033. doi: 10.1057/jors.2011.110
\par
Published online 2 November 2011
