\par
1. Introduction
\par
Many areas of public policy require decisions to influence
\par
future states of complex physical systems. Climate change
\par
policy, for example, must seek to invest in CO2 emissions
\par
abatement and other programs in order to limit the
\par
negative effects of climate change (see, for example, Klein
\par
et al, 2005). In order to understand how complex systems
\par
evolve under different policies, scientists build computer
\par
models designed to mimic the behaviour of the system.
\par
Uncertainty regarding the behaviour of the system is then
\par
often derived using a combination of information gained
\par
by performing computer experiments and real world data
\par
(Linkov and Burmistrov, 2003).
\par
The public policy problems we are considering do
\par
not represent static, one-off, decisions. Policies must be
\par
dynamic and react and adapt to future states of the
\par
world appropriately (Lempert et al, 2000). The policies
\par
we consider can be thought of as decision trees over the
\par
policy relevant time horizon. Policy is made today and,
\par
at a finite number of points, will be revisited following
\par
continuous observation of the evolution of the complex
\par
system.
