\par
In practice, policy support often comes in the form of
\par
scenario analysis (see, eg, Budescu et al, 2009). This often
\par
means comparing the performance of a finite number of
\par
pre-chosen policy options. To avoid any bias in the
\par
generation and analysis of particular policy scenarios,
\par
we would like to treat each policy revision and system
\par
observation as part of a continuum. For example, if
\par
considering a policy on C02 abatement, we would like to
\par
consider any abatement from 0 to 100\%, instead of
\par
looking at a small number of arbitrarily chosen abatement
\par
strategies. By simultaneously considering all possibilities in
\par
this way, each of the nodes on our decision tree has
\par
infinitely many children, and the usual dynamic program
\par
ming solution, via backwards induction, becomes infea
\par
sible in all but the simplest problems. For the problems
\par
we consider, even putting probabilities on branches of the
\par
decision tree is very complicated due to the embedding
\par
of computer models in our uncertainty analysis. A full
\par
backwards induction is therefore infeasible in this context.
\par
In this paper, we introduce a method of gaining insight
\par
into such decision trees using emulators to mimic the
\par
backwards induction process. An emulator is a stochastic
\par
representation of a complex function that is often used in
\par
the computer experiment literature to represent beliefs
\par
about a computer simulator (Sacks et al, 1989). Our
\par
approach is to emulate each node on the tree as a function
\par
of the branch leading to that node. Having implemented
