\par
Highlights of GAO-11-654, a report to
\par
congressional committees
\par
Why GAO Did This Study
\par
Many Securities and Exchange
\par
Commission (SEC) employees leave
\par
the SEC each year, and some of
\par
these former employees go to work
\par
for firms regulated by SEC or the law
\par
or consulting firms that represent
\par
them. This practice raises questions
\par
about the potential impact on SEC's
\par
ability to effectively carry out its
\par
mission, including the potential for
\par
undue influence by former SEC
\par
employees on SEC matters or cases.
\par
The Dodd-Frank Wall Street Reform
\par
and Consumer Protection Act
\par
required GAO to examine the
\par
movement of former SEC employees
\par
to regulated firms and the associated
\par
concerns. Among other things, this
\par
report examines (1) the extent to
\par
which employees leave SEC to work
\par
for or represent regulated entities
\par
and the potential issues associated
\par
with such movements and (2)
\par
internal controls SEC has in place to
\par
manage potential conflicts of interest
\par
and how these controls compare
\par
across other agencies. To address
\par
these objectives, GAO analyzed data
\par
on former SEC employees, reviewed
\par
SEC's and other agencies' internal
\par
controls, and interviewed current and
\par
former SEC officials.
\par
What GAO Recommends
\par
GAO recommends that SEC
\par
establish standards for
\par
documentation of ethics advice on
\par
current and post-employment issues
\par
associated with the movement of
\par
employees between SEC and other
\par
employers. SEC generally agreed
\par
with GAO's recommendation and
\par
stated that it has begun drafting
\par
standards.
\par
View GAO-11-654 or key components.
\par
For more information, contact A. Nicole
\par
Clowers at (202) 512-8678 or
\par
clowersa@gao.gov.
\par
What GAO Found
\par
Because SEC historically has not collected future employer information from
\par
separating employees on an agencywide basis, complete information on
\par
where former SEC officials obtained employment is not currently available.
\par
Based on available SEC attrition data, about 37 percent of the more than
\par
2,000 employees who separated from SEC between October 2005 and
\par
September 2010 were in occupation categories that included examiners,
\par
accountants, economists, or attorneys---occupations particularly relevant to
\par
SEC examinations and investigations. GAO analyzed notice of appearance
\par
requests---which are required when former SEC employees wish to appear
\par
before SEC, within 2 years of their separation, for purposes of representing
\par
their firm or client---submitted between October 2005 and October 2010.
\par
Sixteen entities, consisting primarily of law and consulting firms, accounted
\par
for approximately 35 percent of the individuals filing these notices. GAO also
\par
selected a nongeneralizable sample of 150 former employees from
\par
occupation categories relevant to SEC's examination and investigative efforts
\par
and searched publicly available sources for information about their post-SEC
\par
employment. These individuals frequently obtained positions with financial,
\par
consulting, or law firms that represent firms regulated by SEC. According to
\par
SEC officials, representatives from law and financial firms, and academic
\par
researchers with whom GAO spoke, the potential benefits of employees
\par
moving between SEC and the private sector include bolstering SEC's ability
\par
to attract experts to help fulfill its mission and increasing understanding of
\par
SEC rules and regulations among industry participants. Academic
\par
researchers and citizen advocacy groups described potential challenges of
\par
such movements, such as the appearance of potential conflicts of interest
\par
when former SEC staff work for or represent regulated firms.
\par
SEC has a number of controls for managing post-employment and conflict-of-
\par
interest issues, and many of SEC's controls are similar to those of other
\par
agencies. For example, the SEC Ethics Office provides information to
\par
employees about ethics rules and regulations as well as agency-specific
\par
conflict-of-interest and post-employment restrictions. Also, some SEC
\par
divisions and offices take steps through staffing and work processes to
\par
manage potential conflicts of interest and have multiple levels of review and
\par
systems for documenting key decisions, such as closing SEC investigations.
\par
As previously recommended by GAO, SEC also recently began collecting
\par
future employer information from separating employees. This information can
\par
be used as part of SEC's mandatory exit interviews to advise departing staff
\par
about potential conflicts of interest they might encounter in their new
\par
positions related to their SEC experience. While SEC ethics officials routinely
\par
advise current and former employees on post-employment and conflict-of-
\par
interest issues, SEC has not consistently documented this advice. The
\par
agency's lack of documentation standards could limit SEC's and employees'
\par
ability to demonstrate that appropriate consultation occurred and could
\par
contribute to questions about the movement of employees between SEC and
\par
the private sector.
