\par
The Securities and Exchange Commission's (SEC) mission is to protect
\par
investors; maintain fair, orderly, and efficient securities markets; and
\par
facilitate capital formation. To meet its goals, SEC requires public
\par
companies to disclose meaningful financial and other information to the
\par
public, conducts examinations of firms it regulates, and conducts
\par
investigations of potential securities law violations. As of June 14, 2011, a
\par
total of 3,729 permanent employees were working at SEC. SEC employs
\par
many professionals, such as attorneys, securities examiners, and
\par
accountants to carry out its mission. A number of employees leave the
\par
SEC each year to pursue other opportunities, including working for
\par
financial services firms. However, members of Congress, the SEC
\par
Inspector General (SEC IG), and others have raised concerns about the
\par
potential impact on SEC's ability to effectively carry out its mission when
\par
employees leave the agency to work for firms regulated by SEC or for law
\par
firms that may represent firms regulated by SEC. These concerns include
\par
the potential for undue influence by former employees on SEC matters or
\par
cases, particularly those related to examinations and investigations of
\par
firms that may employ or be represented by former SEC employees.
\par
Congress has enacted specific, post-employment restrictions designed to
\par
protect the U.S. government from the improper use of government
\par
information or undue influence exerted by former government employees.
\par
These restrictions prohibit former federal employees from engaging in
\par
\textbf{Page 1 GAO-11-654}
\par
\textbf{Securities and Exchange Commission}certain activities, such as representing certain clients on particular
\par
matters before their former agency, for a specified period of time after
\par
leaving federal service.1 SEC also has established agency-specific
\par
policies to address post-employment and conflict-of-interest issues.2
\par
Nevertheless, questions remain about the potential for former SEC
\par
employees to work on matters in which they have personally and
\par
substantially participated and SEC's efforts to protect against outside
\par
influence on current SEC employees.3
\par
Section 968 of the Dodd--Frank Wall Street Reform and Consumer
\par
Protection Act required us to review concerns associated with former
\par
SEC employees obtaining employment at SEC-regulated entities---a
\par
phenomenon often referred to as the ``revolving door.''4 This report
\par
examines (1) the extent to which employees leave SEC to work for or
\par
represent SEC-regulated entities and the potential issues associated with
\par
