\textbf{Page 3 GAO-11-654 Securities and Exchange Commission}Background
\par
Various criminal statutes, agency-specific policies, and professional
\par
standards address potential post-employment and conflict-of-interest
\par
questions. Executive branch employees, including SEC employees, are
\par
subject to criminal conflict-of-interest statutes. For example, restrictions
\par
on the activity of former federal employees and restrictions on current
\par
federal employees negotiating for private employment are reflected in
\par
sections 207 and 208 of Title 18 of the United States Code (sections 207
\par
and 208) and associated regulations. Specifically, section 208 prohibits
\par
employees from personally and substantially participating in government
\par
decisions that affect their financial interests, including the interests of any
\par
organization with which he or she is in negotiations or with which he or
\par
she has a prospective employment relationship. After separating from an
\par
agency, section 207 provisions can restrict former employees from
\par
representing a firm to his or her former agency for defined cooling-off
\par
periods that vary according to the former employee's involvement and
\par
seniority.6 Examples of conduct prohibited by section 207 include the
\par
following:
\par
 Former personnel are permanently barred from communicating with or
\par
appearing before, with the intent to influence, their former agency on
\par
behalf of their new employer for particular matters on which they were
\par
personally and substantially involved, which involved a specific party
\par
or parties at the time of such participation.7
\par
 For 2 years after leaving federal service, former personnel may not
\par
communicate with or appear before, with the intent to influence, their
\par
former agency on behalf of their new employer on particular matters
\par
that were pending under their official responsibility in their last year of
\par
service, which involved a specific party or parties at the time it was
\par
pending, even if the employee was not directly involved with the
\par
matter.8
\par
618 U.S.C. \S{} 207. A former employee is not prohibited by this restriction from providing
\par
``behind-the-scenes'' assistance in connection with the representation of another person or
\par
firm. See 5 C.F.R. \S{} 2641.201(d)(2). Further, this restriction prohibits only those
\par
communications and appearances that are made ``with the intent to influence.''
\par
718 U.S.C. \S{} 207(a)(1). An employee can participate ``personally'' in a matter even though
\par
he or she only directs a subordinate's participation. He or she participates ``substantially'' if
\par
his or her involvement is of significance to the matter. Therefore, while a series of
\par
peripheral involvements may not be substantial, participation in a single critical step may
\par
be substantial.
