Collectively, SEC officials, academic researchers, citizen advocacy
\par
groups, and representatives from financial firms and law firms with whom
\par
we spoke said that there are a number of benefits and challenges of
\par
employee movement between SEC and the private sector. SEC officials,
\par
academic researchers, and representatives from financial firms and law
\par
firms cited the following benefits:
\par
 \emph{Better regulatory understanding and compliance}. SEC experience
\par
may bring about a better understanding of securities regulation and
\par
compliance in the private sector, which could benefit SEC and
\par
securities firms or firms that represent securities firms. Former SEC
\par
personnel who take positions in the regulated industry or their
\par
representatives, including law firms, may have enhanced credibility as
\par
a result of their SEC experience, and thus greatly aid in encouraging
\par
firms to adopt a culture of compliance.
\par
 \emph{Better communications}. When employees of regulated entities or law
\par
firms representing regulated entities are familiar with SEC regulations
\par
and the context of securities investigations and enforcement, SEC
\par
and the employees of regulated entities or law firms may
\par
communicate more efficiently and openly about the matter being
\par
discussed.
\par
 \emph{Recruitment of specialized expertise}. The perceived value of SEC
\par
experience may bolster the agency's ability to recruit individuals with
\par
current knowledge and expertise in securities products and other
\par
areas that SEC regulates. Attracting specialized market experts, as
\par
well as those with the expertise that SEC traditionally has sought
\par
(including lawyers, accountants, and compliance personnel) helps the
\par
agency fulfill its mission of investor protection.
\par
Academic researchers and citizen advocacy groups identified the
\par
following challenges of employee movement between SEC and the
\par
private sector:
\par
 \emph{Competing interests for current SEC employees}. SEC employees may
\par
be influenced by the prospect of future employment opportunities to
\par
\textbf{Page 11 GAO-11-654 Securities and Exchange Commission}be more lenient or favor prospective future employers while
\par
undertaking SEC actions. For example, according to one academic
\par
researcher, a current SEC employee could seek enforcement
\par
compromises through settlements rather than pursue prosecution
\par
actions. Such an outcome might result in more desirable terms for the
\par
securities firm, which might lead to a more favorable reputation of the
\par
SEC employee within the industry, while still successfully bringing to
\par
conclusion an enforcement action for SEC.
\par
 \emph{Appearance of undue influence}. Personal contact between current
\par
and former SEC employees may create the appearance of conflicts of
\par
interest. For example, even without direct evidence that undue
\par
influence has affected an enforcement action, the appearance of a
\par
conflict of interest could undermine confidence in the enforcement
\par
process and SEC.
