\par
Stakeholders with whom we spoke identified additional options for
\par
managing potential conflicts of interest, and existing controls at the other
\par
agencies provide examples of other options. These options include
\par
improving documentation of employee recusals, developing a database to
\par
identify potential conflicts, tracking employees' participation in SEC
\par
matters, and the extension or expansion of cooling-off periods. Each of
\par
these options has advantages and disadvantages.
\par
\textbf{Better documentation of recusals}. Establishing better documentation of
\par
employee recusals and related matters may provide SEC with additional
\par
information that it could use to screen for potential conflicts of interest.
\par
While SEC currently has an online system in which recusals can be
\par
documented, employee use of the system is optional. According to the
\par
SEC IG, better documentation of recusals and the related matters would
\par
help in creating a history of steps employees have taken to avoid
\par
potential conflicts of interest and would provide supervisors with
\par
information on matters for which certain employees have recused
\par
themselves and on which they cannot work. However, according to SEC
\par
officials, supervisors are generally aware of the matters from which their
\par
employees have recused themselves. Further, requiring documentation of
\par
recusals would require SEC supervisors or ethics officials to develop a
\par
method to enforce the requirement and ensure that recusals are in fact
\par
being documented. Some SEC employees stated that documenting
\par
recusals may have a limited impact on further managing potential
\par
conflicts of interest. Specifically, they stated that it is in an employee's
\par
interest to avoid potential conflicts of interest regardless of whether or not
\par
recusals are documented. Lastly, according to the SEC Ethics Counsel,
\par
SEC does not have the discretionary authority to require documentation
\par
of recusals.
\par
\textbf{Developing a database to help identify potential conflicts of interest}.
\par
Developing a database that contains information on each SEC
\par
employee's prior employment, financial interests, and work history while
\par
at SEC could help manage potential conflicts of interest involving current
\par
and former employees. SEC managers could use the information when
\par
considering staffing decisions, such as those related to SEC
\par
examinations or investigations. As previously mentioned, the Federal
\par
Reserve Banks use a system to track their employees' prior employment
\par
and banking relationships to identify potential conflicts of interest.
\par
According to Federal Reserve officials, the system is updated on an
\par
annual basis or as necessary based on such events as changes in an
\par
employee's investments. In SEC's case, such a database could also be
\par
used when employees separate from the agency, to document matters for
\par
\textbf{Page 21 GAO-11-654 Securities and Exchange Commission}which they were personally and substantially involved while an SEC
\par
employee. However, setting up such a database would require
\par
employees to identify and self report potential conflicts of interest to SEC.
\par
Thus, the system would not help identify any potential conflicts or issues
\par
that employees do not report. Establishing such a database also would go
\par
beyond the current online recusal system SEC has in place, and thus
\par
likely would require SEC to either enhance that system to include
\par
additional information or develop a new system entirely. Further, while the
\par
system would provide additional transparency about potential conflicts of
\par
interest, a few SEC employees and academics with whom we spoke
\par
suggested that such documentation would not deter an individual from
\par
violating conflict-of-interest restrictions if the individual has intentions to
\par
do so.
\par
\textbf{Tracking employees' participation in SEC matters}. Maintaining a list of
\par
matters in which employees participate, such as enforcement cases or
\par
examinations, may help to promote transparency by documenting what
\par
employees have worked on prior to separating from the agency. Such a
\par
list could help SEC identify potential conflicts of interest. For example, the
\par
list could be maintained by employees and then provided to the Ethics
\par
Office during their exit interview so that ethics officials could review the
\par
list of matters and discuss any potential issues that might be related to an
\par
employee's post-SEC employment. According to representatives from
\par
citizen advocacy groups with whom we spoke, documentation of matters
\par
SEC employees participate in may help to provide transparency on
\par
potential conflicts when they leave SEC to work for law firms or financial
\par
firms. Current systems in the Division of Enforcement, Division of
\par
Corporation Finance, and OCIE do not track employee participation in all
\par
matters, so SEC would need to evaluate methods to determine the best
\par
manner in which to document this information. For example, while
\par
providing a list of matters in which employees have participated might be
\par
sufficient for a review during their exit interview, documenting this
\par
information in a system may provide more benefits, such as the ability to
\par
readily search for the information in the future. Further, the level of
\par
participation in a matter also would need to be determined before
\par
establishing whether employee participation in a specific SEC matter was
\par
personal and substantial and therefore subject to specific post-
\par
employment restrictions. Lastly, according to SEC officials, such
\par
documentation may not be necessary because the Ethics Office can
\par
coordinate with SEC managers to determine in which matters an
\par
employee participated, and their level of participation.
\par
