August 20, 2012
\par
\textbf{Abstract}
\par
We analyze stock market reactions to announcements of political appointments from the
\par
private sector and corporate appointments of former government officials. Using unique data
\par
on corporate affiliations and announcements of all Senate-confirmed U.S. Defense
\par
Department appointees of six administrations, we find positive abnormal returns for political
\par
appointments. These estimates are not driven by important observations, volatile stocks or
\par
industry-wide developments. Placebo events yield no effects. Effects are larger for top
\par
government positions and less anticipated announcements. We also find positive abnormal
\par
returns for corporate appointments. Our results suggest that conflicts of interest matter also in
\par
a country with strong institutions.
\par
\emph{JEL}: G14, D73, G30, H57.
\par
\emph{Keywords}: Political appointees, revolving door, conflict of interest, event study, stock market
\par
 Address for correspondence: ETH Zurich, KOF Swiss Economic Institute, Weinbergstrasse 35, 8092 Zurich,
\par
Switzerland. E-mail: Christoph Moser (moser@kof.ethz.ch); Simon Luechinger (simon.luechinger@unilu.ch).
\par
We thank Philipp R\"uckheim, Heysook Shin and Omar Abdel Rahman for excellent research assistance. For
\par
comments and suggestions we thank Steffi Bansbach, Philipp Denter, Tim Friehe, James Kolari, Francis
\par
Kramarz, James Kwak, Sara Lovell, Nolan McCarty, Bernhard Obenhuber, Tobias Seidl and Joachim Voth as
\par
well as seminar participants at the KOF Swiss Economic Institute, the 5th BBQ Conference in Schiermonnikoog,
\par
University of Konstanz and Vienna University of Economics and Business and participants at the annual meeting
\par
of the Swiss Society of Economics and Statistics.\textbf{1. Introduction}
\par
When President Obama took office in January 2009, he faced a challenge common to
\par
incoming Presidents in the United States: Filling top-positions in the federal administration
\par
with political appointees. Among other sources, he tapped the reservoir of private sector
\par
experts, like all Presidents before him. For example, William J. Lynn III, a Pentagon official
\par
under Clinton and later Raytheon executive, was his nominee for Deputy Secretary of
\par
Defense. But the revolving door spins in both directions: Legions of leaving Bush appointees
\par
joined the industry.
\par
The academic literature on political appointments stresses the trade-off between a decrease in
\par
competence and an increase in political control (e.g., Lewis, 2008; Huber and McCarty,
\par
2004). Political appointees lack site-specific knowledge and institutional memory, but shared
\par
preferences, aligned incentives and lower informational asymmetries increase the control of
\par
elected officials over the bureaucracy. In contrast, the public debate often revolves around the
\par
value of industry expertise for regulation or procurement and concerns about potential
\par
conflicts of interest. Commenting on Lynn's nomination, a defense analyst said, ``On the one
\par
hand, if you don't have defense-industry experience, then you're lacking critical insight. On
\par
the other hand, if you do, it's inevitable that in some way it's going to impact on your
\par
judgment.''1 Lynn's nomination also met resistance over conflicts of interest in Senate
\par
hearings: ``[T]he revolving door is an important issue for us to talk about, between the
\par
Pentagon and the defense community. You went directly from the Pentagon to a defense
\par
contractor. You are coming back directly from a defense contractor [\dots ] into the Department
\par
of Defense. [\dots ] This is troubling to a lot of people [\dots ] and it's an incestuous business what's
\par
going on in terms of the defense contractors and the Pentagon [\dots ].''2
\par
Concerns over potential conflicts of interest are not unfounded. Political appointees have both
\par
opportunities and motives to act in favor of their former employers. Preferential treatments
\par
can come in different disguises: Directly in procurement, regulation and oversight, and
\par
supervision of mergers and acquisitions; indirectly in strategic planning and preferential
\par
access to decision makers and information. At the same time, political appointees also have
\par
incentives for preferential treatment towards their former employers either because they
\par
expect to rejoin the companies or because they want to return favors. Similarly, former
\par
political appointees bring valuable connections and information about processes, upcoming
\par
1 Loren Thompson cited in Defense Daily, ``Lynn confirmation vote expected today,'' February 11, 2009.
\par
2 Sen. Claire McCaskill cited in Federal News Service, ``Hearing of the Senate Armed Services Committee
\par
confirmation hearing on expected nominations by President-elect Barack Obama,'' January 15, 2009.
\par
2decisions and competitors to firms hiring them upon leaving government service. However,
\par
the generally favorable institutional environment of the United States with checks and
\par
balances and a free press as well as specific institutional safeguards embodied in ethic rules
\par
counter such concerns. Whether conflicts of interest exist and whether firms can benefit from
\par
them are ultimately empirical questions.
\par
Do firms profit from the political appointment of one of its members? Do firms profit from
\par
hiring a former political appointee? These two questions lie at the heart of this paper. We
\par
address these questions with an event-study. We estimate the effect of announcements of
\par
appointments and hirings on firm value. Given that stock prices reflect expected future profits
\par
and any news affecting these expectations is immediately priced in by financial markets, stock
\par
price reactions to the announcements measure the value of political connections associated
\par
with the revolving door. Thus, positive stock market reactions are consistent with the view
\par
that conflicts of interest indeed matter.
\par
Event studies have several advantages over alternative approaches. Compared to effects on
\par
realized firm performance or the volume of procurement contracts, effects on stock prices are
\par
immediate. Thus, it is easier to relate cause and effect and abstract from confounding factors.
\par
Further, unlike effects on the volume of procurement contracts, stock price reactions are a
\par
comprehensive measure for the value of political connections. Compared to respondents in
\par
surveys and expert interviews, financial market participants have a strong financial interest for
\par
well-informed and unbiased assessments.
\par
In our empirical analysis, we concentrate on political appointees in the United States
\par
Department of Defense. This department is by far the largest department in terms of budget,
\par
amounting to over USD 700 billions including supplementals in recent years, and in terms of
\par
procurement volume (Gansler, 2011). Furthermore, it has around 700,000 civilian employees
\par
and the largest number of executive political appointees, i.e., excluding attorneys, marshals
\par
and ambassadors (Lewis, 2008). Thus, focusing on the Department of Defense assures a large
\par
and homogenous sample. We cover appointees nominated by or serving under Presidents
\par
Bush Sr. to Obama. In other words, our analysis encompasses six administrations and over 20
\par
years. We consider the universe of Senate confirmed positions at the Department of Defense,
\par
totalling 527 person-position combinations or 383 individuals. For these observations we
\par
reconstruct the employment and affiliation history within two years of government service
\par
and gather the exact date of political and corporate appointments through a full-text search
\par
using LexisNexis and SEC-filings. We consider an appointee to be connected to a firm, if she
\par
3has served as a member of board of directors or advisory board, an executive officer or an
\par
employee of that firm. We then link the information on political and corporate announcements
\par
to daily stock market data of these connected firms. This yields a sample of 85 observations
\par
(72 firms and 58 persons) related to political appointments and 85 observations (75 firms and
\par
59 persons) for corporate hirings of former government officials. We use these data and a
\par
standard market model approach to estimate abnormal returns for each announcement.
\par
To anticipate our main findings: For political appointments (``ex ante''), we find positive
\par
average abnormal returns of 0.82\% and 0.84\% for the one- and two-day event windows,
\par
respectively. Abnormal returns are larger for (former) board members, top pay-grades in the
\par
Department of Defense, appointees nominated by a Democratic administration (for the one-
\par
day window) and for those events, where the announcement was at least partly unanticipated.
\par
But the revolving door works in both ways. For corporate appointments (``ex post''), the
\par
announcement results in average positive abnormal returns of 0.79\% and 1.05\% for one- and
\par
two-day event windows, although these results are statistically less strong. We find that stock
\par
market reactions tend to be higher for nominations to the board of directors and for corporate
\par
appointments of former political appointees of a Democratic President. The baseline results
\par
are robust to the exclusion of extreme abnormal returns, scaling of abnormal returns and the
\par
adjustment of returns by industry returns. Placebo events yield no effects.
\par
Our paper is related to five different strands of the literature: The literature on the value of
\par
political connections established by (i) political appointments and (ii) corporate appointments
\par
of former government officials in the United States, (iii) of political connections more
\par
generally, (iv) the literature on lobbying, and (v) the literature on the reaction of defense
\par
stocks to political decisions.
\par
Closest in spirit to our paper is Fisman, Fisman, Galef and Khurana (2006), who investigate
\par
the value of connections to Vice President Richard Cheney.3 Firms are defined as connected
\par
if either Cheney was on the board of directors or if one of their board members served on
\par
Halliburton's board during Cheney's watch as chairman and CEO. Fisman et al. (2006) do not
\par
find any significant stock market reactions to important news about Cheney and his health.
\par
This suggests that the checks and balances of U.S. institutions are strong enough to keep
\par
conflicts of interest in check. Another related paper is Acemoglu, Johnson, Kermani, Kwak
\par
and Mitton (2010), who document strong and positive abnormal stock returns for firms being
