\par
and results are fairly similar across these different variables. Thus, these two papers provide
\par
mixed evidence on the value of political connections in the United States. Our paper differs
\par
from these papers in several respects. Acemoglu et al. (2010) are not concerned with the
\par
revolving door or conflicts of interest stemming from political appointments of individuals
\par
from the private sector. Similarly, only a small subset of the political connections studied by
\par
Fisman et al. (2006) is related to the revolving door phenomenon. Both papers provide
\par
valuable insights into the value of political connections for one politician and appointee,
\par
respectively. In contrast, our paper is based on a large number of appointees over more than
\par
two decades. At the same time, appointees are from one department, assuring a homogenous
\par
sample. Importantly, we also include below cabinet level positions. As the tanker-leasing
\par
scandal has demonstrated, political appointees at lower levels have opportunities for
\par
preferential treatment of specific firms. Darleen A. Druyun, acting Assistant Secretary of the
\par
Air Force (Acquisition), gave Boeing Company an inflated price on the contract and shared
\par
confidential information about a competitor in return for a generous job deal at Boeing.4
\par
Moreover, sub-cabinet level appointees are more likely to fly under the radar of the media.
\par
Although we look at both sides of the revolving door, our main focus is on political
\par
appointments. In our view, conflicts of interest are more worrisome, when political appointees
\par
are in office and can exert direct influence within the administration in favor of their former
\par
employers. Nevertheless, we also look at corporate appointments of former government
\par
officials. In this context, the study of Goldman, Rocholl and So (2009) is closely related to
\par
ours.5 Goldman et al. (2009) analyze announcements of corporate board appointments of
\par
individuals who at any time of their past career held a high political position and who served
\par
on the boards of S\&P 500 from 1996 to 2000. Differences to our study exist with regard to
\par
corporate affiliations. We consider executive appointments in addition to board appointments
\par
and include all listed firms. Our results generally confirm the findings of Goldman et al.
\par
(2009). They find positive abnormal returns for corporate appointments. To highlight two
