\par
An important and growing literature assesses the value of political connections more
\par
generally, mainly in countries with weak institutions. In a seminal paper, Fisman (2001)
\par
demonstrates that political connections to the Suharto family were very valuable during
\par
Indonesian President Suharto's reign. For identification, he exploits negative news about the
\par
health of the elderly President and its effect on Indonesian stock returns. Similarly, Johnson
\par
and Mitton (2003) establish that connections to Malaysia's Prime Minister Mahathir paid out
\par
in the aftermath of the Asian financial crisis. Ferguson and Voth (2008) show that firms with
\par
close links to the National Socialist German Workers' Party enjoyed strong, positive
\par
abnormal returns during Hitler's accession to power in 1932-1933. For Thailand,
\par
Bunkanwanicha and Wiwattanakantang (2009) find not only that higher wealth and
\par
dependence on regulated industries make business leaders more likely to run for public office,
\par
but that their firms gain in market value during their tenure. Finally, in another seminal paper,
\par
Faccio (2006) documents that corporate political connections are widespread in a large cross-
\par
section of 47 countries. These connections can add to firm value, even though this holds true
\par
only for countries with weak institutions, when large shareholders or top executives get
\par
elected into a high political position.6
\par
There is empirical evidence for different channels through which political connections are
\par
valuable for firms in developing countries. Politically connected firms enjoy preferential
\par
access to finance, government bail-out or profit from regularity changes.7 For the United
\par
States, Goldman, Rocholl, and So (2010) are the first to provide evidence for a specific
\par
channel of political connections. Their study shows that firms with former politicians on the
\par
board of directors enjoy higher growth in procurement contracts after a change in the political
\par
landscape. Interestingly, these networks through former politicians are apparently more
\par
important for procurement than firms' campaign contributions.8, 9
\par
6 Analyzing the effect of geographical ties, i.e., firms headquartered in birth or home towns of politicians, Faccio
\par
and Parsley (2009) confirm that political connections matter more in countries with weaker institutions.
\par
However, Roberts (1990) found geographical ties to matter even in the United States.
\par
7 See Khwaja and Mian (2005), Faccio, Masulis and McConnell (2006), Leuz and Oberholzer-Gee (2006),
\par
Claessens, Feijen, and Laeven (2008) and Bunkanwanicha and Wiwattanakantang (2009) for this evidence.
\par
8 For the literature on political connections established through firms' campaign contributions to parties or
\par
specific politicians in the United States, see among others Roberts (1990), Jayachandran (2006), Cooper, Gulen
\par
and Ovtchinnikov (2010) and Huber and Kirchler (2012).
\par
9 Bertrand, Kramarz, Schoar and Thesmar (2007) provide evidence that political connections can also entail
\par
higher labor costs for connected firms in France.
\par
6Lobbying is often seen as another way through which firms seek to influence government
\par
decisions.10 Two innovative recent studies suggest that corporate lobbying in the United
\par
States indeed adds to firm value. Borisov, Goldman and Gupta (2011) exploit a top lobbyist's
\par
corruption scandal and consequent heightened public scrutiny of lobbying and show stronger
\par
negative market reactions to this news for firms with higher lobbying expenditures. Blanes i
\par
Vidal, Draca and Fons-Rosen (2012) analyze the lobbying effect of former government
\par
officials on lobbying firm's revenue and find that personal connections to U.S. Senators are of
\par
substantial value to these firms. In contrast to these studies, the focus of our paper is on
\par
personal ties through corporate affiliations and not on lobbying. Furthermore, lobbying firms
\par
cannot enter our event study, since they are not listed on the U.S. stock market.
\par
Finally, our paper is related to the literature on the reaction of defense stocks to Department of
\par
Defense decisions.11 Rogerson (1989) estimates the value of contract awards of major weapon
\par
systems. There is often competition in the development phase, but later one company
\par
typically becomes the sole supplier and earns economic profits (see also Lichtenberg, 1989;
\par
Gansler, 2011). Analyzing stock market reactions to the announcements of twelve aerospace
\par
contract award decisions, Rogerson (1989) indeed finds evidence for expected profits. In
\par
another event study, Karpoff, Lee and Vendrzyk (1999) demonstrate that firms responsible for
\par
defense procurement fraud are penalized by financial markets, but this penalty is smaller for
\par
established contractors. Finally, Bechtel and Schneider (2010) document that European
\par
defense stocks rise due to EU summit decisions that strengthen Europe's military capacity.
\par
Although these papers are loosely related to our paper, our study is not primarily or
\par
exclusively about the defense sector, but about the value of political connections for
\par
corporations more generally. The Department of Defense is also important for many firms
\par
beyond the defense sector.12
\par
The remainder of the paper is organized as follows. Section 2 discusses why and how
\par
(former) political appointees could preferentially treat their (former) employers. Section 3
\par
provides a description of the data and outline of the empirical strategy. Section 4 presents the
\par
empirical results and Section 5 offers concluding remarks.
