7\textbf{2. Background}
\par
The revolving door between industry and government entails potential conflicts of interest.
\par
Political appointees can both directly and indirectly act in favor of their former employers and
\par
they have various motives to do so. In a similar vein, former government officials have
\par
different means to bring value to firms hiring them. But there are numerous institutional
\par
safeguards at all levels preventing conflicts of interest. These arguments will be outlined in
\par
more detail in the remainder of this section.
\par
The potential of direct preferential treatment is probably most obvious in procurement.
\par
Political appointees are involved in decisions affecting their former employers at all stages of
\par
the procurement process. When Gerald A. Cann, General Dynamics Corp.'s former chief of
\par
undersea warfare, became Assistant Secretary of the Navy (Research, Development, and
\par
Acquisition), he had to select among three standoff anti-submarine weapons, one of which he
\par
developed at his former employer. ``It is a little wrong to be involved with a weapon system
\par
[in industry] and then be put in charge of deciding [at the Department of Defense] what
\par
system will replace the one that's performing the mission now,'' said Sen. Al Gore.13 When
\par
James G. Roche, a former Northrop Grumman Corp. executive, served as Secretary of the Air
\par
Force, he was the source selection authority in the F-35 competition and his former employer
\par
a main partner of one of the bidders. This team ultimately won and was expected ``[\dots ] to
\par
dominate warplane construction worldwide for the first half of this century.''14
\par
Direct preferential treatment is also possible in case of regulation and oversight and
\par
supervision of mergers and acquisitions. As Assistant Secretary of the Air Force (Installations
\par
and Environment) Terry A. Yonkers was in charge of drafting environmental regulations of
\par
the Air Force, affecting his former employer Arcadis NV.15 John M. Deutch, Under Secretary
\par
of Defense (Acquisition, Technology, and Logistics), followed an initiative of Martin
\par
Marietta's head and approved a new rule allowing the Department of Defense to partly cover
\par
merger costs. Later, as Deputy Secretary of Defense, he gave the nod to the merger between
\par
Lockheed Corp. and Martin Marietta Corp.16 This merger eventually cost the Department of
\par
