\par
advisory board of Martin Marietta Corp.?
\par
Political appointees can also influence the fate of their former employers in less direct ways.
\par
In particular, strategic planning can have important long-lasting implications on the Pentagon
\par
budget and prospective contractors. Thomas E. White pushed for the privatization of army
\par
base utilities, both as an Enron Corp. executive and later as the Secretary of the Army.18 At
\par
the time of the nomination of the Northrop Grumman executive Donald C. Winter as
\par
Secretary of the Navy, a debate over the future of Navy shipbuilding was ongoing with
\par
Northrop Grumman Corp. being one of the key players.19
\par
Firms also potentially profit from better access to decision makers and information, if one of
\par
their members joins the Pentagon. Sen. Claire McCaskill worried that ``[\dots ] there is maybe
\par
too much shortcutting of picking up the phone and dialing into the Pentagon from a defense
\par
contract agency because of former friends that are there and vice versa?''20 Indeed, Thomas E.
\par
White acknowledged such contacts and attempted contacts with former Enron colleagues on
\par
various occasions and Donald C. Winter met Northrop Grumman's CEO for a private
\par
luncheon (as he did with some other defense executives).21
\par
Political appointees have not only opportunities but also incentives to favor their former
\par
employers. They may expect to return to the company or at least want to keep this option
\par
alive. One prominent returnee is the Deputy Secretary of Defense and Secretary of Defense
\par
William J. Perry, who re-joined the board of directors of United Technologies Corp. after his
\par
stint at the Department of Defense. Further, political appointees may simply wish to return
\par
previous favors, such as generous help in the nomination process or lavish retirement bonuses.
\par
Enron supported Thomas E. White's nomination with a letter of recommendation by the
\par
