\par
There are various reasons why firms can benefit from hiring former government employees
\par
(see also Agrawal and Knoeber, 2001, Goldman et al., 2009, 2010). Former political
\par
appointees are valuable because of their connections, access and clout.23 According to
\par
Washington insiders, schmoozing government officials at all stages of the procurement
\par
process is essential for landing contracts.24 Probably, nobody is better qualified for this job
\par
than former political appointees. Furthermore, former political appointees have precious
\par
knowledge and information. First, they have considerable institutional insights and can
\par
instruct companies in the way the Pentagon works.25 Second, they have inside information
\par
about upcoming decisions. According to retired Admiral Robert Inman, former Secretary of
\par
Defense nominee and long-time SAIC board member, ``[t]hese people bring with them years
\par
of experience and can help the company identify what's ahead so they can better anticipate
\par
and prepare for the future.''26 Third, during their term, political appointees gain special
\par
insights into competitors of their future employers.27
\par
A favorable institutional environment -- like a vigilant free press, independent watch dogs and
\par
congressional committees -- and specific provisions are likely to prevent fraud and abuse and
\par
keep potential conflicts of interest in check in the United States. Numerous ethic regulations
\par
aim at preventing improper behavior. Prime among these are recusal requirements for political
\par
appointees in decisions affecting their former employers, demands to sever financial interests
\par
with companies doing business with the Pentagon by divesting from stock positions and by
\par
insuring pension claims, and prohibitions to lobby former colleagues during a cool-off period
\par
upon leaving the administration. Yet, despite numerous rules on the books, it is less clear to
\par
what extent they are actually enforced. While Senators usually take a tough stand during the
\par
confirmation hearings, political appointees are often successful in receiving waivers later on.
\par
For instance, in the confirmation process of the former corporate executives and service
\par
secretary nominees, Thomas E. White and Donald C. Winter, the members of the Senate
