\textbf{4. Empirical Results for Political Appointments}
\par
\emph{4.1. Baseline Results and Robustness}
\par
We start the discussion of our results by giving a picture of the revolving door of the
\par
Pentagon. Table 1 depicts the affiliations of all the appointees in our sample both before and
\par
after their current position at the Department of Defense. Two aspects are worth noting: First,
\par
the largest part of appointees comes from and remains in the Department of Defense. One
\par
explanation for this is that some appointees get nominated for other positions within the
\par
department, serve in an acting position or get promoted. Another reason is that career
