We now turn to stock market reactions to political appointments. Panel A of Table 2 presents
\par
the results for the main sample of 85 observations. Firm ties to these political appointees are
\par
well established, with an average tenure at firms of around seven and a half years.36 We find
\par
positive abnormal returns of 0.82\% for the announcement day and of 0.84\% for a two-day
\par
window including the announcement day and the next trading day. Both estimates are
\par
statistically significant at conventional levels. One might be concerned that these results are
\par
driven by either important or noisier observations or by industry-wide stock market
\par
movements. Another potential concern is that an omitted variable could induce the positive
\par
correlation between stock returns and political appointments. To address these concerns, we
\par
report four robustness tests.
\par
First, Panel B of Table 2 exhibits the average abnormal returns for a trimmed sample,
\par
excluding the top and bottom 5\% of the abnormal return distribution during the event
\par
windows. As can be seen, the point estimates are smaller, but statistically significant at least
\par
at the 95\% level.
\par
Second, Panel C of Table 2 presents average scaled abnormal returns, i.e., abnormal returns
\par
divided by the standard deviation of estimation period residuals. Thereby, abnormal returns of
\par
noisier observations receive less weight than more reliable ones. According to Kolari and
\par
Pynn\"onen (2010), scaled abnormal returns are a preferred signal detection device. At the same
\par
time, it is important to note that the point estimates are not informative about economic
\par
magnitude. The results remain statistically significant at conventional levels.
