\emph{4.2. Further Results for Subsamples}
\par
The overall effects in Table 2 may hide informative heterogeneity across subgroups.
\par
Therefore, Table 4 presents the results for four sample splits, whereby Panel A reproduces the
\par
baseline result from Table 2 for comparability. First, according to Panel B stock markets react
\par
more strongly to the political appointment of former board members than to other candidates.
\par
Second, we find larger effects for political appointments to top-position at the Department of
\par
Defense. To split samples, we use pay-grades of the executive schedule and code as a ``Top
\par
pay-grades'' the two highest pay-grades. This includes the Secretary of Defense, his deputy,
\par
the service secretaries, and the Under Secretary of Defense (Acquisition, Technology, and
\par
Logistics). ``Other high pay-grades'' includes the third and fourth highest level of pay-grades,
\par
i.e., all other Senate confirmed appointees. The results suggest that investors expect political
\par
connections to top officials at the Pentagon to be more valuable.
\par
Third, stock markets react more strongly to political appointments by Democratic than
\par
Republican administrations on the announcement day. This is consistent with the view
\par
expressed by Goldman et al. (2009) that there is a limited supply of political connections,
\par
which is especially limited in Democratic administrations. As can be seen from Table 4, there
\par
are fewer events related to Democrats.
\par
Fourth, not all announcements were equally surprising. For example, Secretary of Defense
\par
Richard Cheney was probed in a press conference in 1989 about rumors about his choice of
\par
17service secretaries more than a week before the official announcement. He virtually confirmed
\par
these rumors by saying that they were based on ``good sources''.44 We distinguish between
\par
two different levels of anticipation. We code as ``Higher anticipation'' all events for which the
\par
-- later appointed -- person but nobody else was rumored to be the candidate for this particular
\par
position. In contrast to that, ``Lower anticipation'' captures all other events. The latter
\par
announcements entail a higher surprise-content and, hence, are expected to lead to stronger
\par
stock market reactions. This is indeed what we find. Point estimates for largely unexpected
\par
political appointments are substantially larger and amount to 1.20\% and 1.40\% for the one-
\par
and two-day event window.
\par
Figure 2 compares the kernel densities for the one-day abnormal returns for political
\par
appointments of ``lower anticipation'' with the placebo appointments. The distribution for
\par
more surprising political appointments is peaking at a positive abnormal return of around 1
\par
percent and is slightly skewed to the right. The Kolmogorov-Smirnoff test rejects the equality
\par
of these two distributions at the 1 percent significance level.
\par
In Table 5, we look again at the subgroups, but this time in a multivariate setting. For both
\par
event-windows, top-pay grades and the extent to which announcements are anticipated play a
\par
role. There is also some evidence that being nominated by the Democratic party has some
\par
significant explanatory power on the announcement day, but having been a board member
