\par
\textbf{6. Conclusions}
\par
All our results point in the same direction. Investors expect firms to profit from their political
\par
connections to appointees. In particular, we find positive abnormal average returns of about
\par
between 0.82\% and 0.84\% for the one- and two-day event window in case of political
\par
appointments. These main results are insensitive to a number of robustness checks.
\par
Furthermore, these results for the overall sample are a lower bound of the true value of firm
\par
connection, because some of the appointments are largely anticipated. Restricting the sample
\par
to largely unexpected events, the average abnormal returns amount to between 1.20\% and
\par
1.40\%. Similarly, in case of corporate appointments there are also positive, but smaller and
\par
less robust, positive abnormal returns between 0.79\% and 1.05\% for the one- and two-day
\par
event window.
\par
48 For the sake of brevity and lack of robust results, we do not report the multivariate analysis for corporate
\par
appointments in the main text. There is some weak evidence that hiring a former political appointee is better
\par
news for smaller firms (see Online Appendix 4).
\par
20It is important to keep in mind that these stock market reactions reflect expectations of
\par
investors. Thus, this is obviously no proof for any wrong-doing of individual political
\par
appointees during their tenure for two reasons. First, expectations need not to realize in
\par
individual cases even if they are accurate on average. Second, our empirical strategy does not
\par
allow us to pin down one particular reason for why firms are expected to profit. However, it is
\par
hard to think of other reasons for higher expected returns than conflicts of interest. In the best
\par
case, conflicts of interest distort procurement contract award competitions, because some
\par
firms have informational advantages. In the worst case, decisions are tilted towards specific
\par
firms and against the interest of taxpayers and the armed forces.
\par
We find evidence consistent with conflicts of interest despite a generally strong institutional
\par
environment in the United States with checks and balances and a free media as well as
\par
specific institutional safeguards. However, our results do not necessarily suggest that the
\par
benefits of more regulation outweigh the costs. According to Gansler (2011), the tight net of
\par
regulation currently in place already entails enormous compliance costs for both the
\par
Department of Defense and the industry. Furthermore, our results do not allow for an overall
\par
assessment of the costs and benefits of political appointments from the private sector.
\par
Although our results highlight one important cost, they are silent about potential benefits.
\par
In our view, an important area for future research is to identify the channels through which
\par
firms profit from their connections to political appointees in office. Given the enormous
\par
volume of procurement contracts in the Department of Defense, one might expect preferential
\par
treatment in procurement decisions to be one important channel. A natural starting point
\par
would, therefore, be to look at the effect of political appointments on the volume or growth of
\par
procurement contracts (analogous to the study of Goldman et al., 2009, for corporate
\par
appointments). Yet, as we outlined above, other channels are likely to be important as well.
\par
Therefore, it might be instructive to investigate an agency with mainly regulatory
\par
competencies.
