\par
An employee may not take official action on a matter which can affect the financial interests of an organization with which she is
\par
negotiating or has an arrangement for future employment. The remedy is disqualification.
\par
18 U.S.C. \S{} 208
\par
In addition, an employee may have to disqualify herself from working on a matter when she is merely seeking employment, but
\par
before actually negotiating for a job. An employee would be considered to be seeking employment if she sends her resume to
\par
companies or if she is approached by someone about a position with a company and she responds that she is interested.
\par
5 C.F.R. \S{} 2635.601(see \textbf{Subpart F - Seeking Other Employment})
\par
\textbf{Three-Day Notice Requirement for Negotiations/Agreements}
\par
This requirement applies only to employees who file a Public Financial Disclosure report. Public Financial Disclosure filers must
\par
inform their Deputy Designated Agency Ethics Official ( DDAEO) of any negotiation or agreement for post-government employment
\par
or compensation \textbf{within three (3) business days of commencing negotiations or reaching agreement (whichever}
\par
\textbf{occurs first)}. An approved format for this notification and recusal may be found here. You may send a signed PDF copy to your
\par
Deputy DAEO, but the original must also be sent.
\par
The notification requirement is two-fold:
\par
1) First, the requirement to send the notice to your Component's Deputy DAEO is triggered when you enter into discussions or
\par
communications with another person, or such person's agent or intermediary, that is mutually conducted with the goal of reaching
\par
an agreement regarding possible employment or compensation. Simply submitting resumes to several law firms without prior
\par
invitation is not a negotiation. As soon as a communication takes place that is a meaningful step toward reaching an agreement
\par
(e.g., discussing the specific terms of a partnership offer), you must send the notice to your Deputy DAEO within 3 business days.
\par
2) Second, if the negotiation or agreement results in a conflict of interest, or appearance of a conflict, you must also sign the
\par
recusal statement at the bottom of the notice. However, filers should sign the recusal statement when submitting the negotiation
\par
notice even if a conflict does not currently exist, to serve as an acknowledgement to abide by the recusal obligation should it arise.
\par
\textbf{REMEMBER} \textbf{: The 3-day notice requirement does not change the longstanding obligation under the conflict of}
\par
\textbf{interest regulations (5 CFR 2635.601, et al) to recuse yourself from working on matters affecting a potential}
\par
\textbf{employer when you are seeking employment. The seeking employment recusal obligation will occur} \textbf{before} \textbf{your}
\par
\textbf{obligation to submit the negotiation/recusal statement arises, so you must be aware of both mandates at all times}
\par
\textbf{once you commence seeking employment with anyone.}
