There are statutory prohibitions on a former government employee that generally prevent her from ``switching sides'' after leaving
\par
the government. The following are the main restrictions:
\par
Lifetime Ban - An employee is prohibited from representing anyone else before the government on a particular matter involving
\par
specific parties in which she participated personally and substantially.US Justice: Print Friendly Version
\par
Page 2 of 2
\par
Two-Year Ban - An employee is prohibited for two years from representing another person on a particular matter involving specific
\par
parties which was pending under her responsibility during her last year of government service.
\par
One-Year Ban - A senior employee includes Executive Level officials and all other employees whose rate of basic pay is equal to or
\par
greater than 86.5\% of the rate for Level II of the Executive Schedule, which is \$155,441 as of January, 2010. Therefore, SES officials
\par
whose pay is at least \$155,441 are covered by this additional one year restriction. As of April, 2009, Senior Level (SL), Scientific or
\par
Professional (ST), or other employees whose pay is at least \$155,441 are senior employees, and are also covered by the additional
\par
one year restriction. Although some GS-15 employees may receive more than \$155,441 in overall pay, they are not covered by the
\par
additional one year restriction in that their rate of basic pay is below the threshold.
\par
Certain components of the Department of Justice are considered separate for purposes of the one-year ban. An employee who works
\par
in a designated separate component is barred only from appearing before her own component. An employee not from a separate
\par
component is barred only from parts of the Department not designated as separate. An employee paid according to the Executive
\par
Schedule does not benefit from the separation of components. She is barred from representing before the whole Department. 5 C.
