\textbf{APPENDIX C: Responsibilities of the Ethics Officer}
\par
-2-\textbf{CODE OF CONDUCT}
\par
\textbf{1. Basic Obligation}
\par
It is indispensable to the proper functioning of, and the maintenance of public confidence in, the
\par
Federal Reserve Bank of New York ("Bank") and the Federal Reserve System ("System") that every
\par
employee perform his or her duties with honesty, integrity and impartiality, and without improper
\par
preferential treatment of any person. Each employee has a responsibility to the Bank and to the System
\par
to avoid conduct which places private gain above his or her duties to the Bank, which gives rise to an
\par
actual or apparent conflict of interest, or which might result in a question being raised regarding the
\par
independence of the employee's judgment or the employee's ability to perform the duties of his or her
\par
position satisfactorily. Each employee should conduct his or her financial affairs with integrity and
\par
honesty. To ensure the foregoing, each employee, including all Bank officers, shall respect and comply
\par
with the principles and standards of conduct contained in this Code. An employee who needs
\par
assistance in interpreting the provisions of the Code or who desires additional information should
\par
contact the Bank's Ethics Officer.
\par
-3-\textbf{2. Employee Conduct}
\par
\textbf{2.1} \textbf{Gambling and Lotteries}
\par
An employee shall not participate in any gambling or illegal lottery activity, for money or anything of
\par
value, while on Bank premises.
\par
\textbf{2.2} \textbf{Alcoholic Beverages}
\par
Except at Bank approved functions, the Bank prohibits the sale or consumption of beer, wine or other
\par
alcoholic beverages by anyone on the Bank's premises. The Bank prohibits an employee from
\par
reporting to work or performing job duties if, as a result of consuming alcoholic beverages, the
\par
employee is under the influence of alcohol, i.e., the employee's physical or mental abilities are
\par
impaired.
\par
\textbf{2.3 Illegal Drugs}
\par
An employee shall not possess, use, sell, distribute, or be under the influence of any unauthorized
\par
substance on Bank premises or while conducting business on behalf of the Bank. An unauthorized
\par
substance is any illegal drug or illegal controlled substance, or any drug which has been legally
\par
obtained but is not being used in the prescribed dosage for prescribed purposes.
\par
\textbf{2.4 Firearms/Hazardous Materials}
\par
The possession or use of firearms or other lethal weapons, ammunition, explosives or hazardous
\par
materials by an employee on Bank premises is prohibited. This restriction does not apply to items
\par
that are owned by the Bank and used by a Bank employee in the conduct of Bank business.
\par
-4-\textbf{3.} \textbf{Bank Property and Information}
\par
\textbf{3.1 Bank Property}
\par
An employee has a duty to protect and conserve Bank property and ensure its use for proper
\par
purposes.
\par
\textbf{3.2 Bank Information}
\par
Bank information should be released or used only as authorized by Bank policy. Bank examination
\par
and other bank or bank holding company supervisory information is the property of the Board of
\par
Governors of the Federal Reserve System ("Board") and may be disclosed only in accordance with
\par
Board procedures.
\par
Information maintained as fiscal agent for any federal agency may be disclosed only in accordance
\par
with that agency's procedures.
\par
In the course of working at the Bank, an employee may have access to non-public information.
\par
Non-public information is information that the employee knows, or reasonably should know and:
\par
(a) Has not been made available to the general public.
\par
(b) Is designated as confidential, private or proprietary.
\par
(c) Is routinely treated by the Bank as confidential.
\par
This may include information related to the Bank, the System, the Federal Open Market Committee
\par
(``FOMC''), or another person or institution (such as a banking organization, a vendor, an employee
\par
or former employee of the Bank, or a federal agency). An employee must strictly preserve the
\par
confidentiality of such information. It can be disclosed only as required for Bank purposes and only
\par
as authorized.
\par
\textbf{3.3 Use of Non-public Information for Private Gain}
\par
An employee is prohibited from using non-public information for any purpose other than Bank
\par
business. In addition, an employee may not engage, directly or indirectly, in any financial
\par
transaction as a result of, or in reliance on, non-public information, whether such information relates
\par
to the Bank or any other person or institution. An employee may not allow the improper use of such
\par
non-public information to further the employee's own private interest or that of another person,
\par
whether through advice, recommendation, or a knowing, unauthorized disclosure.
\par
An employee with access to Class I FOMC information should avoid engaging in any financial
\par
transaction the timing of which could create the appearance of acting on inside information
\par
concerning Federal Reserve deliberations and actions. In order to avoid even the appearance of
\par
acting on confidential information, an employee authorized to have regular and ongoing access to
\par
Class I FOMC information should not knowingly:
\par
-5-a) Purchase or sell any security (including any interest in the Thrift Plan for Employees
\par
of the Federal Reserve System, but not including shares of a money market mutual
\par
fund) during the seven calendar day period prior to and the day (s) of a meeting of the
\par
FOMC.
\par
b) Hold any security for less than 30 days, other than shares of a money market mutual
\par
fund.
\par
This purchase or sale restriction does not apply if the investment decision is made before the seven
\par
day period (in the case of rollover, for example). An employee authorized to have regular and
\par
ongoing access to Class I FOMC information also should make every effort to ensure that the
\par
financial transactions of his or her spouse and dependent children comply with these restrictions. In
\par
unusual circumstances, after consultation with the Ethics Officer, these restrictions may be waived.
\par
-6-\textbf{4. Use of Position}
\par
\textbf{4.1} \textbf{For Private Gain}
\par
An employee may not, directly or indirectly, use the employee's position for his or her own private
\par
gain or that of any other person.
\par
\textbf{4.2 Endorsements}
\par
An employee shall not use or permit the use of his or her position or title, or any authority associated
\par
with his or her office, to endorse any product, service or enterprise except in connection with Bank
\par
products and services or as otherwise authorized by the Bank.
\par
-7-\textbf{5. Conflicts of Interest}
\par
\textbf{5.1 General Standard}
\par
An employee should avoid any situation that might give rise to an actual conflict of interest or even
\par
the appearance of a conflict of interest. An employee who routinely represents the Bank in dealing
\par
with the public must be particularly careful in this regard. Where the circumstances might cause a
\par
reasonable person to question the employee's impartiality or otherwise give rise to an appearance of
\par
a conflict of interest, the employee should not participate in a matter unless he or she has informed
\par
the Bank of the situation and received authorization from the Bank's Ethics Officer.
\par
\emph{Example}
\par
A conflict of interest, or the appearance of a conflict of interest, may arise where an
\par
employee is working on a matter involving a potential contract award and the
\par
employee's sibling or close friend works for one of the bidders. As a result, the
\par
employee should consult with the Bank's Ethics Officer before participating in the
\par
matter.
\par
\textbf{5.2 Statutory Prohibition on Conflicts}
\par
A. \emph{Background. }The rules in this section are derived from provisions of the federal criminal
\par
conflict of interest statute and related regulations. Key portions of the regulations, modified as
\par
appropriate for the Bank's use, are contained in Appendix A and are part of this Code. In light of
\par
the serious consequences of violating this criminal statute, each employee is strongly urged to read
\par
Appendix A in its entirety. An employee who has any questions about the prohibitions contained in
\par
this section should contact the Bank's Ethics Officer.
\par
B.\emph{ General Statutory Prohibition. }Notwithstanding the provisions of Section 5.3 (B), an employee
\par
may not participate personally and substantially in an official capacity in any particular matter in
\par
which, to the employee's knowledge, the employee or certain related parties listed in Section 5.2 (C)
\par
have a financial interest if the particular matter will have a direct and predictable effect on that
\par
interest. Participation in a particular matter includes making a decision or recommendation,
\par
providing advice, or taking part in an investigation.
\par
C.\emph{ Imputed Interests. }The financial interests of the following individuals and entities are imputed
\par
to the employee and will disqualify the employee from participating in a matter:
\par
(1) The employee's spouse.
\par
(2) The employee's minor children.
\par
(3) The employee's general partner(s).
\par
(4) An organization or entity for which the employee is an officer, director, trustee, general
\par
partner or employee (regardless of the nonprofit status of the organization or whether the
\par
employee is paid).
\par
-8-(5) A person or entity with whom the employee is negotiating for employment or has an
\par
arrangement concerning prospective employment. (See Section 6 for more detail.)
\par
\emph{Example}
\par
An employee whose job is to participate in the review of bank holding company
\par
applications also serves, without compensation, on the board of directors of a
\par
fraternal organization. At a meeting of its board of directors, the employee discovers
\par
that the organization, as part of its investment portfolio, owns stock in Bank A. After
\par
this meeting takes place, the employee is asked to review an application of Bank
\par
Holding Company B to acquire Bank A and to recommend whether the application
\par
should be approved. Even though the employee's personal financial interests will not
\par
be affected by the approval or denial of the application, the employee should not
\par
review the application. The approval or denial of the application will affect the value
\par
of Bank A stock, a financial interest of the organization. The organization's financial
\par
interests are attributed to the employee as a member of its board of directors.
\par
D. \emph{Particular Matter.} The term "particular matter" includes a supervisory matter involving a
\par
depository institution or its affiliate, rule making that is focused on a discrete class, an application,
\par
enforcement action, examination, request for ruling or other determination, an acquisition or sale
\par
(e.g., securities, foreign exchange or real estate), formation of contracts, and the provision of priced
\par
and non-priced services.
\par
E. \emph{Direct and Predictable Effect.} In order for a particular matter to have a direct effect on a
\par
financial interest, there must be a close causal link between any decision or action to be taken in the
\par
matter and any expected effect of the matter on the financial interest. An effect may be direct even
\par
though it does not occur immediately. A particular matter that has an effect on a financial interest
\par
only as a consequence of its effect on the general economy does not have a direct effect within the
\par
meaning of this rule.
\par
A matter will have a predictable effect on a financial interest if there is a real, as opposed to a
\par
speculative, possibility that the particular matter will affect the financial interest.
\par
\emph{Example 1}
\par
An employee in data processing is asked to serve on a technical evaluation panel to
\par
review proposals for a new computer system. ABC Computer Corporation ("ABC")
\par
has submitted a proposal. The employee owns ABC stock. The award of the
\par
systems contract to ABC or to any other company that bids will have a direct and
\par
predictable effect on the value of the ABC stock. The fact that the gain or loss
\par
cannot be measured and that the value of the gain or loss may be extremely small is
\par
irrelevant. The employee may not serve on the evaluation panel unless an exemption
\par
is applicable [see Section 5.2 (F)] or the employee receives a waiver [see Section 5.2
\par
(G)].
\par
-9-\emph{Example 2}
\par
An employee in the Check Processing Department is asked to buy a new software
\par
program. The employee has a choice between purchasing the software program
\par
designed by XYZ Computer Corp. or the software program designed by Mammoth
\par
Co. The employee's spouse works for Mammoth Co. and participates in Mammoth
\par
Co.'s stock option plan. Through that plan, the employee's spouse receives shares of
\par
Mammoth Co. The purchase or non-purchase of Mammoth Co.'s software program
\par
will have a direct and predictable effect on the value of Mammoth Co.'s stock and
\par
thus the spouse's financial interest. The spouse's interest is imputed to the employee
\par
and, therefore, the employee may not decide which software program to purchase
\par
unless an exemption is applicable [see Section 5.2 (F)] or the employee receives a
\par
written waiver [see Section 5.2 (G)]. The fact that the potential gain or loss is
\par
immeasurable is immaterial.
\par
\emph{Example 3}
\par
If, on the other hand, the employee's spouse was a salaried employee of Mammoth
\par
Co. and had no ownership interest in Mammoth Co., the employee could decide
\par
which software program to purchase, assuming that the purchase or non-purchase of
\par
Mammoth Co.'s software program would have no effect on the spouse's salary or
\par
employment. While the employee would not be prohibited by the criminal conflict
\par
of interest statute from participating in this decision, the employee's participation
\par
could still give rise to an appearance of a conflict of interest suggesting the
\par
employee's disqualification should be considered under Section 5.1. Therefore, an
\par
employee in this situation should not participate in the matter without first obtaining
\par
authorization from the Bank's Ethics Officer.
\par
F. \emph{Exempt Financial Interests}. Under regulations issued by the Office of Government Ethics
\par
(``OGE''), which are set forth in part in Appendix A, a number of financial interests are exempt, and
\par
therefore, an employee may participate in a particular matter that will affect those interests.
\par
1. The following interests are exempt:
\par
Investments held through a diversified non-sector mutual fund or unit investment trust.
\par
(b) An employee's interest in any System retirement and/or thrift plan.
\par
(c) Short-term federal government securities (maturity of one year or less) and US Savings
\par
Bonds.
\par
2. The OGE regulations also provide additional exemptions relating to financial interests:
\par
(a) In certain employee benefit plans.
\par
(b) In publicly traded securities, municipal securities, sector mutual funds, and long-term
\par
federal government securities where the aggregate fair market value of the securities
\par
(a) -10-owned by the employee and his or her spouse and any minor child is below a certain
\par
amount.
\par
(c) Of certain tax-exempt organizations whose interests are imputed to the employee
\par
because of his or her association with the organization.
\par
(d) Of an employee's general partner.
\par
The OGE regulations also provide for exemptions in addition to those summarized in this Section.
\par
The primary exemptions for the various financial interests, which are set forth in Part II of Appendix
\par
A, are complex. An employee who has such an interest or to whom such an interest is imputed from
\par
a family member or other related person should carefully read Part II of Appendix A to see whether
\par
his or her participation in a particular matter affecting the interest is allowable.
\par
G. \emph{Individual Waiver.} An employee who would otherwise be disqualified may participate in a
\par
particular matter if he or she receives a written waiver prior to participating in the matter. If you
\par
want to request a waiver, you should consult the Bank's Ethics Officer.
\par
\textbf{5.3 Prohibited Financial Interests}
\par
A. \emph{Prohibition of Certain Debt or Equity Interests.} Notwithstanding anything to the contrary in
\par
Section 5.2, an employee may not own or control, directly or indirectly, any debt or equity interest in
\par
a depository institution or an affiliate of a depository institution. A "depository institution" means a
\par
bank, a trust company, or any institution that accepts deposits, including a bank chartered under the
\par
laws of a foreign country. In addition, an employee with regular and ongoing access to Class I
\par
FOMC information and members of the Markets Group may not own or control, directly or
\par
indirectly, any debt or equity interest in a primary government securities dealer or an entity that
\par
directly or indirectly controls a primary dealer. The employee is regarded as controlling any debt or
\par
equity interest held by the employee's spouse or minor child.
\par
B. \emph{Exceptions. }The following are not prohibited debt or equity interests within the meaning of
\par
Section 5.3 (A):
\par
(1) An interest in a publicly traded money market fund or other mutual fund (including a fund
\par
that is an affiliate of a depository institution or primary dealer of government securities),
\par
provided that: (a) the fund does not have a policy of concentrating its investments in the
\par
financial services industry; and (b) neither the employee nor the employee's spouse has the
\par
ability to exercise control over the financial interests held in the fund.
\par
(2) An interest acquired by the employee's spouse or minor child---other than from the
\par
employee:
\par
(i) Prior to the marriage.
\par
(ii) Prior to the employee's being employed by the Bank.
\par
(iii) As compensation or a fringe benefit in connection with his or her employment, or as
\par
``qualifying shares'' as a condition of service as a director or employee.
\par
(3) A future interest created by someone other than the employee; his or her spouse or child.
\par
-11-(4) An interest of an employee, spouse or minor child as a beneficiary of an estate where the
\par
interest has not been distributed by the estate's legal representative.
\par
(5) An interest in a widely held, diversified pension or other retirement fund that is administered
\par
by a trustee independent from the employee and the employee's spouse.
\par
(6) An interest in a publicly-traded holding company that is not predominantly engaged in the
\par
banking or thrift business, is not supervised by the System, and does not control a state
\par
member bank, a foreign bank with U.S. operations or a federally insured U.S. office, or a
\par
``bank'' within the meaning of the Bank Holding Company Act.
\par
(7) For those employees prohibited from owning stock of a primary dealer or its affiliate, an
\par
interest in a publicly-traded holding company that owns a primary dealer provided that the
\par
holding company is not predominately engaged in the banking, thrift, or securities business.
\par
An employee who is required to file a disclosure statement pursuant to Section 9 must report an
\par
interest that the employee or the employee's spouse or minor child holds pursuant to any of the
\par
foregoing exceptions. Also, it is important to understand that the ability to retain an otherwise
\par
prohibited interest pursuant to one of the exceptions does not constitute a waiver for purposes of the
\par
general statutory prohibition on conflicts of interest that is described in Section 5.2.
\par
C. \emph{Definition of Debt or Equity Interest.} For purposes of this section, the term "debt or equity
\par
interest" includes secured and unsecured bonds, debentures, notes, securitized assets, commercial
\par
paper, preferred and common stock, short positions, instruments convertible into the above, as well
\par
as options, rights or warrants to acquire such instruments, but does not include a deposit, credit
\par
union shares, insurance policy or annuity. Although excluded from the definition of a ``debt or
\par
equity interest,'' insurance policies and annuities may be financial interests and may result in
\par
conflicts of interest under Section 5.2.
\par
D. \emph{Divestiture.} In addition to any appropriate disciplinary action, an employee who violates this
\par
section may be required to divest the prohibited interest. Divestiture also may be required if the
\par
ownership or control of an interest, though permissible under this section, would likely disqualify an
\par
employee from handling matters to an extent that substantially interferes with the employee's ability
\par
to perform his or her job.
\par
Whenever the Bank directs divestiture, the employee will be given a reasonable period of time to
\par
divest, considering the nature of the employee's particular duties and the nature and marketability of
\par
the interest. Except in cases of unusual hardship, as determined by the Bank, a reasonable period
\par
ordinarily will not exceed 90 days from the date divestiture is directed.
\par
E. \emph{Waiver.} The Bank's Ethics Officer, in consultation with the officer with responsibility for the
\par
department in which the employee works, may grant a written waiver permitting an employee to
\par
own or control a debt or equity interest prohibited by paragraph A of this section if extenuating
\par
circumstances exist and if any required disqualification from a particular matter(s) due to the
\par
financial interest would not unduly interfere with the full performance of the employee's duties.
\par
-12-(2) Examples of extenuating circumstances are:
\par
(1) Ownership or control of the interest (including a preemptive right or option) was acquired
\par
before Federal Reserve employment.
\par
Ownership or control of the interest was acquired through inheritance, gift, stock split, stock
\par
dividend, merger acquisition, or other change in corporate structure, or otherwise without
\par
specific intent on the part of the employee to acquire the interest.
\par
\textbf{5.4 Gifts, Meals, and Entertainment from Outsiders}
\par
A. \emph{Gifts, Meals, and Entertainment.}
\par
1. General Prohibition\emph{.} Except as permitted below, an employee may not solicit or accept,
\par
directly or indirectly, any gift, meal, favor, service, entertainment or other thing of monetary value
\par
("gift") from a person or institution that does, or seeks to do, business with the Bank or is supervised
\par
by the System, or has interests that are substantially affected by the employee's duties at the Bank
\par
("covered sources"), or from an organization, a majority of whose members are covered sources. A
\par
gift received by an employee's spouse or child, or given to a person or entity at the specific direction
\par
of the employee, is considered to be received by the employee.
\par
An employee who is offered a prohibited gift should decline to accept it. If an employee
\par
receives a prohibited gift, arrangements should be made to return or dispose of the gift, and the
\par
source should be advised of the Bank's policy.
\par
2. Exceptions\emph{.} (a) A gift that otherwise would be prohibited is permitted if the employee clearly
\par
can establish that the gift:
\par
(i) Has a de minimis market value (i.e., \$20 or less), provided the number of times the
\par
employee has received a de minimis gift from the same covered source is infrequent.
\par
(ii) Is a reduced or waived admission fee to attend, or a meal provided in connection with, a
\par
widely attended conference or gathering which is in furtherance of the employee's duties
\par
at the Bank.
\par
(iii) Is given or offered under circumstances that indicate it is motivated by a personal
\par
relationship that exists independently of his or her employment with the Bank.
\par
(iv) Results from his or her spouse's employment and has not been offered or enhanced
\par
because of the employee's position with the Bank.
\par
(v) Results from his or her outside employment or business activities and has not been
\par
offered or enhanced because of the employee's position with the Bank.
\par
(vi) Is a meal provided in connection with a charitable or civic function or organization in
\par
which the employee is a participant.
\par
-13-(vii) Is a meal authorized in writing in advance by the Bank's Ethics Officer based on a
\par
determination that:
\par
(\emph{a}) Special circumstances exist which make the acceptance of the meal appropriate in
\par
furtherance of Bank business.
\par
(\emph{b}) Payment by the employee is not feasible.
\par
(\emph{c}) Acceptance of the meal will not create a conflict of interest; or
\par
(viii) Is a discount or benefit available to the general public and is not offered to the
\par
employee or enhanced because of his or her employment with the Bank.
\par
Even when permissible under an exception listed above, an employee always may decline a
\par
gift offered by a covered source.
\par
(b) With the Bank's prior approval, an employee may accept a bona fide award given for
\par
achievement, provided that:
\par
(i) (ii) The award is made as part of an established program of recognition under which
\par
awards have been made on a regular basis or which is funded, wholly or in part, to
\par
ensure its continuation on a regular basis.
\par
The program selects recipients pursuant to established standards.
\par
B.\textbf{ }\emph{Examiners.} The exceptions described in Section 5.4 (A) (2) do not apply to a gift or a meal
\par
offered to an examiner by an institution that the examiner examines, has examined or has authority
\par
to examine. Furthermore, an examiner may never accept a meal or gift pursuant to the de minimis
\par
exception of Section 5.4 (A) (2) (a) from an institution for which the System is the primary federal
\par
regulator. An examiner should consult Appendix B, Part I, paragraph 4.
\par
C. \emph{Gift from Foreign Governmental Source.} An employee may not accept a gift from a foreign
\par
government, including a foreign central bank, unless the gift is valued at \$350 or less and is offered
\par
and received as a souvenir or mark of courtesy. An employee may accept a gift from a foreign
\par
government valued above \$350 under certain limited circumstances. An employee should consult
\par
with the Bank's Ethics Officer to determine whether the employee may accept such a gift.
\par
D. \emph{Treasury Borrowing Advisory Committee.} An employee may not accept any gift from the
\par
Treasury Borrowing Advisory Committee of the Securities Industry and Financial Markets
\par
Association (``SIFMA'') or any member of the advisory committee.
\par
-14-\textbf{6. Seeking Other Employment}
\par
An employee is prohibited from personally and substantially participating in an official capacity in
\par
any matter that will have a direct and predictable effect upon the financial interest of any entity with
\par
which the employee is seeking employment or has an arrangement for future employment. Under
\par
some circumstances such participation may violate the federal criminal conflict of interest statute.
\par
Refer to Part III of Appendix A for more information on this prohibition. The following examples
\par
illustrate when an employee is seeking employment for purposes of this prohibition.
\par
\emph{Example 1}
\par
An employee in the Credit and Discount Department receives a call from Bank B
\par
asking the employee whether the employee is interested in leaving the Reserve Bank
\par
and going to work for Bank B. The employee informs Bank B that she is involved in
\par
a project that will be completed by year-end and that, until the project is completed,
\par
she cannot consider leaving the Reserve Bank. After this conversation, Bank B
\par
approaches the Reserve Bank for an advance. The employee is typically asked to
\par
advise whether an advance should be made. The employee is considered to be
\par
seeking employment with Bank B because the employee did not clearly reject Bank
\par
B's offer but merely postponed discussion to the foreseeable future. Thus, the
\par
employee must refrain from participating in the decision to approve or deny the
\par
advance to Bank B.
\par
\emph{Example 2}
\par
An employee is considered to be seeking employment with a company as soon as she
\par
makes an inquiry about employment with the company, unless the inquiry is part of a
\par
mass-mailing to a large class of potential employers. If the employee's inquiry to the
\par
company is part of a mass mailing, the employee is not considered to have begun
\par
seeking employment unless she receives a response from the company that indicates
\par
an interest in employment discussions.
\par
-15-\textbf{7}. \textbf{Outside Activities}
\par
\textbf{7.1 General}
\par
An employee's outside activity, including outside employment, should not bring discredit to the
\par
reputation of the Bank or the System, interfere with the full and proper discharge of the employee's
\par
duties and responsibilities, or be incompatible with the employee's duties and responsibilities.
\par
Incompatible activities include, for example, those which are prohibited by statute, regulation or any
\par
provision of this Code, those which create the appearance of a conflict of interest, or those which
\par
would require the employee's disqualification from matters so central or critical to the performance
\par
of his or her duties and responsibilities that the employee's ability to give satisfactory service to the
\par
Bank would be materially impaired. Before engaging in compensated outside employment, an
\par
employee should obtain the prior approval of the Bank.
\par
The financial interests of any organization of which an employee serves as officer, director, trustee,
\par
general partner, or employee will be attributed to the employee for purposes of the statutory
\par
prohibitions of Section 5.2.
\par
\textbf{7.2 Teaching, Speaking and Writing}
\par
A. \emph{General Prohibition}. Except as provided in Sections 5.4 (A) (2) (a) (i) and (ii), 5.4 (A) (2) (b),
\par
and Section 5.4 (C), an employee may not accept compensation, a gift, or honorarium from any
\par
source other than the Bank for teaching, speaking or writing on a subject that relates to the
\par
employee's duties and responsibilities. This restriction precludes acceptance by an employee, but
\par
not the Bank, of reimbursement for travel or lodging expenses in connection with an appearance,
\par
speech or publication.
\par
B. \emph{Exceptions.} With the Bank's prior approval, an employee may accept compensation and
\par
reimbursement of travel/lodging expenses for:
\par
(1) Teaching a course related to the employee's duties if the course is offered as part of the
\par
regularly established curriculum of a school, college, or other educational entity (for
\par
example, the American Institute of Banking).
\par
(2) Preparing an article or other publication utilizing or incorporating information obtained by
\par
the employee in the course of, or as a result of, performing his or her job duties at the Bank,
\par
provided the publication is prepared on the employee's own time.
\par
C. \emph{Use of Bank Title in Connection with Teaching, Speaking, or Writing.} An employee may not use
\par
or permit the use of his or her title at the Bank in connection with teaching, speaking, or writing, or
\par
to promote any book, seminar, or course when such activities are not part of the employee's Bank
\par
duties except:
\par
(1) other.
\par
As one of several biographical notes when such note is given no more prominence than any
\par
-16-(2) As part of an article published in a scientific or professional journal, as long as a reasonably
\par
prominent disclaimer states that the views expressed in the article do not necessarily
\par
represent the views of the Bank.
\par
\textbf{7.3 Political Activity}
\par
A. \emph{General}. The Federal Reserve Banks have a unique need to protect their independence from the
\par
political process. Therefore, although an employee may participate or become involved in issues of
\par
general public concern or debate, the employee's association with the Bank must not be publicized
\par
in connection with any political activity. Further, an employee may not engage in political activity
\par
while on duty or on Bank premises, and must be extremely cautious to avoid any suggestion of Bank
\par
sponsorship or support of such activities.
\par
B. \emph{Partisan Politics.}
\par
1. Non-Officers\emph{.} An employee, other than an officer, may participate in a partisan political
\par
campaign, except that an employee may not run for any partisan political office (federal, state, or
\par
local) or solicit or accept political contributions (i.e., anything of value, including personal services)
\par
on behalf of any candidate, party or political organization. An employee may hold a position within
\par
a political party so long as the position does not otherwise interfere with the employee's
\par
performance of Bank duties.
\par
2. Officers\emph{.} An officer must be particularly vigilant in avoiding any appearance that his or her
\par
personal political views reflect the views of the Bank or the System. An officer may participate in
\par
partisan politics only as an individual voter, \emph{e.g.,} vote, express a private opinion, or make a
\par
contribution. An officer may not take an active role in partisan politics; for example, an officer may
\par
not run for any partisan political office; solicit or accept political contributions (i.e., anything of
\par
value, including personal services) on behalf of any candidate, party, or political organization; or
\par
hold a position (other than general membership) within a political party.
\par
C. \emph{Non-Partisan Public Service.} There are various types of public service activities that do not
\par
bring into question the independent and non-partisan character of the Bank, and that are viewed as a
\par
community service rather than as political activity, even if the position is one to which an employee
\par
is appointed by a partisan public official. Examples are serving as a member of a board of
\par
education, a roads and bridges commission, a parks and recreation commission, a planning
\par
commission, a board of health, or a university board, provided election or appointment under a party
\par
label is not involved. For instance, an employee, including an officer, may accept appointment by
\par
the governor of a state to that state's board of education. However, if there is a requirement that the
\par
board of education be comprised of a certain number of representatives from each major political
\par
party, then the employee may not accept the appointment. If an employee has any question about
\par
the propriety of a particular position or activity, the employee should consult the Bank's Ethics
\par
Officer.
\par
-17-\textbf{8. Post Employment Activities}
\par
\textbf{8.1 Post Employment Contacts}
\par
An employee who ceases to be employed by the Bank should not contact the Bank concerning a
\par
particular matter in which he or she participated while employed at the Bank. If a current employee
\par
is contacted by a former employee concerning such a matter, the current employee must not discuss
\par
the matter or provide any information to that individual that is not available to the general public,
\par
unless authorized to do so by Bank management. In performing his or her duties, a current
\par
employee may contact a former employee to obtain information concerning work performed by the
\par
former employee while employed at the Bank.
\par
\textbf{8.2 Non-public Information}
\par
An employee's duty to maintain the confidentiality of non-public information (as defined in Section
\par
3.2) continues after his or her employment ends. An employee must leave all Bank documents,
\par
files, computer diskettes, reports and records containing non-public information, and all copies of
\par
such information, with the Bank when his or her employment ends.
\par
-18-\textbf{9. Disclosure Statements}
\par
In order to obtain information about circumstances that might constitute an actual or potential
\par
conflict of interest or a violation of applicable Bank policy or law, the following employees are
\par
required to file a disclosure statement as prescribed by the Bank at least annually:
\par
(a) All officers.
\par
(b) Any employee:
\par
(i) Whose duties and responsibilities require that he or she participate personally and
\par
substantially in any supervisory matter, examination, application, investigation, etc.
\par
concerning a depository institution or any affiliate or subsidiary of a depository
\par
institution.
\par
(ii) Whose duties and responsibilities require that he or she participate personally and
\par
substantially through decision making or the exercise of significant judgment in taking
\par
action regarding contracting or procurement.
\par
(iii) Who has authority to make an exception to established operating or internal control
\par
procedures.
\par
(iv) Who has access to Class I FOMC information.
\par
(c) Any manager or supervisor in a valuables handling area.
\par
(d) Any other employee upon request of the Bank's Ethics Officer.
\par
-19-\textbf{10. Violations}
\par
\textbf{10.1 Reporting}
\par
Employees are encouraged to report violations of this Code to the Bank's Ethics Officer. A
\par
violation that involves corruption, fraud, or theft, should also be reported to the Bank's General
\par
Auditor.
\par
\textbf{10.2 Disciplinary Action}
\par
Any employee who violates any provision of this Code is subject to disciplinary action up to and
\par
including termination of employment.
\par
-20-\textbf{APPENDIX A}
\par
This Appendix contains a summary of significant portions of the federal conflicts of
\par
financial interest regulations (5 C. F. R. Parts 2635 and 2640), as modified for the Bank's use. It
\par
provides additional detail regarding: (i) the conflict of interest rule in Section 5.2 of the Code; and
\par
(ii) the rule regarding seeking other employment in Section 6 of the Code. A copy of the complete
\par
regulations may be obtained from the Legal Department or the Ethics Officer.
