\textbf{PART I. DISQUALIFYING FINANCIAL INTERESTS}
\par
\textbf{1. Statutory Prohibition. }An employee is prohibited by federal criminal law [18 U.S.C. \S{} 208 (a)]
\par
from participating personally and substantially in an official capacity in any particular matter in
\par
which, to the employee's knowledge, he or she, or any person whose interests are imputed to the
\par
employee under the statute, has a financial interest, if the particular matter will have a direct and
\par
predictable effect on that interest.
\par
\textbf{2. Definitions. }For purposes of this Part, the following definitions apply:
\par
if there is a close causal link between any decision or action to be taken in the matter and any
\par
expected effect of the matter on the financial interest. An effect may be direct even though it does
\par
not occur immediately. However, a particular matter will not have a direct effect on a financial
\par
A. \emph{Direct and predictable effect}\textbf{. }A particular matter1 will have a \emph{direct }effect on a financial interest
\par
interest if the chain of causation is attenuated or is contingent upon the occurrence of an event that is
\par
speculative or that is independent of, and unrelated to, the matter. A particular matter that has an
\par
effect on a financial interest only as a consequence of its effect on the general economy does not
\par
have a direct effect within the meaning of this paragraph.
\par
A particular matter will have a \emph{predictable }effect if there is a real, as opposed to a speculative
\par
possibility, that the matter will affect the financial interest. However, it is not necessary that the
\par
magnitude of the gain or loss be known, and the dollar amount of the gain or loss is immaterial.
\par
\emph{Example 1}
\par
An employee with procurement responsibilities has just been asked to serve on the evaluation panel
\par
to review proposals for a new Research Library computer search system. DEF Computer
\par
Corporation, a closely held company in which the employee and his wife own stock, has submitted a
\par
proposal. Because award of the systems contract to DEF or to any other offeror will have a direct
\par
and predictable effect on both his and his wife's financial interests, the employee may not participate
\par
on the evaluation panel unless he requests and receives a waiver or an exemption applies.
\par
1 If a particular matter involves a specific party, generally the matter will only have a direct and predictable
\par
effect on the financial interest of the employee in or with the party, for example where the employee owns
\par
stock in the party. However, there may be some situations in which a particular matter will have a direct and
\par
predictable effect on an employee's financial interests in or with a non-party. For example, if the party is a
\par
corporation, a particular matter also may have a direct and predictable effect on an employee's financial interest
\par
through ownership of stock in an affiliate of the party. Similarly, the award of a contract to a particular
\par
company also may have a direct and predictable effect on an employee's financial interest in another company,
\par
such as a subcontractor or a competitor.
\par
-21-\emph{Example 2}
\par
Upon assignment to the evaluation panel, the employee in the preceding example finds that DEF
\par
Computer Corporation has not submitted a proposal. However, LMN Corp., a competitor of DEF, is
\par
one of the offerors. The employee is not disqualified from serving on the evaluation panel. Any
\par
effect on the employee's financial interests as a result of the decision to award or not award the
\par
contract to LMN at most would be indirect and speculative.
\par
B. \emph{Financial interest }means the potential for gain or loss to an employee or other person specified in
\par
paragraph 2 (c) as a result of Bank action on a particular matter. The financial interest might arise
\par
from ownership of certain financial instruments or investments such as a stock, bond, mutual fund,
\par
or real estate. Additionally, a financial interest might derive from a salary, indebtedness, job offer,
\par
or any similar interest that may be affected by the matter.
\par
C. \emph{Imputed interests}\textbf{. }For purposes of the federal criminal law and this Part, the financial interests of
\par
any of the following persons will disqualify an employee to the same extent as if they were the
\par
employee's own interests:
\par
(i) The employee's spouse.
\par
(ii) The employee's minor child.
\par
(iii) The employee's general partner.
\par
(iv) An organization or entity in which the employee serves as officer, director,
\par
trustee, general partner or employee.
\par
(v) A person with whom the employee is negotiating for or has an arrangement
\par
concerning prospective employment. (An employee who is seeking other
\par
employment should comply with the standards in Part III of this Appendix regarding
\par
"Seeking Other Employment.")
\par
\emph{Example 1}
\par
An employee of the Department of Education serves without compensation on the board of directors
\par
of Kinder World, Inc., a nonprofit corporation that engages in good works. Even though her
\par
personal financial interests will not be affected, the employee must disqualify herself from
\par
participating in the review of a grant application submitted by Kinder World. Award or denial of the
\par
grant will affect the financial interests of Kinder World and its financial interests are imputed to her
\par
as a member of its board of directors.
\par
\emph{Example 2}
\par
The spouse of an employee of the Food and Drug Administration is employed by a biomedical
\par
research company that is seeking FDA approval for a new product. The employee ordinarily would
\par
be asked to participate in the FDA's review and approval process. The employee's spouse is a
\par
salaried employee and has no direct ownership interest in the company. Moreover, she does not
\par
have an indirect ownership interest such as an interest in a pension plan that holds stock in the
\par
company. Her position with the company is such that the granting or withholding of FDA approval
\par
-22-will not have a direct and predictable effect on her salary or on her continued employment with the
\par
company. Since the FDA approval process will not affect his spouse's financial interests, the
\par
employee may participate in that process.
\par
2
\par
D. \emph{Particular matter }means a matter that involves deliberation, decision or action that is focused
\par
upon the interests of a specific person, or a discrete and identifiable class of persons. Such a matter
\par
is covered by the federal conflict of interest regulations even if it does not involve formal parties,
\par
and may include governmental action such as legislation or policymaking that is narrowly focused
\par
on the interests of a discrete and identifiable class of persons. However, the term \emph{particular matter}
\par
does not extend to the consideration or adoption of a broad policy option that is directed to the
\par
interests of a large and diverse group of persons. The particular matters covered by the federal
\par
conflict of interest regulations include a judicial or other proceeding, application, request for a
\par
ruling or other determination, contract, claim, controversy, charge, accusation or arrest.
\par
\emph{Example 1}
\par
An employee's evaluation of proposals received in response to a Request for Proposal is a particular
\par
matter. An employee's review of an application filed by a bank holding company also is a particular
\par
matter.
\par
\emph{Example 2}
\par
Consideration by the Interstate Commerce Commission of regulations establishing safety standards
\par
for trucks on interstate highways involves a particular matter.
\par
E. \emph{Personal and substantial}. To participate \emph{personally }means to participate directly. It includes the
\par
direct and active supervision of the participation of a subordinate in the matter. To participate
\par
\emph{substantially }means that the employee's involvement is of significance to the matter. Participation
\par
may be substantial even though it is not determinative of the outcome of a particular matter.
\par
However, it requires more than official responsibility, knowledge, perfunctory involvement, or
\par
involvement on an administrative or peripheral issue. A finding of substantiality should be based not
\par
only on the effort devoted to a matter, but also on the importance of the effort. While a series of
\par
peripheral involvements may be insubstantial, the single act of approving or participating in a
\par
critical step may be substantial. Personal and substantial participation may occur when, for example,
\par
an employee participates through decision, approval, disapproval, recommendation, investigation or
\par
the rendering of advice in a particular matter.
