participating in a particular matter should notify the person responsible for his or her assignment or
\par
the Bank's Ethics Officer. An employee who is responsible for his or her own assignment should
\par
take whatever steps are necessary to ensure that he or she does not participate in the matter from
\par
which he or she is disqualified. Appropriate oral or written notification of the employee's
\par
disqualification may be made to coworkers by the employee or a supervisor to ensure that the
\par
employee is not involved in a matter from which he or she is disqualified.
\par
b)\textbf{ }\emph{Documentation}\textbf{. }An employee need not file a written disqualification statement unless asked to do
\par
so by the Bank's Ethics Officer or a supervisor. However, an employee may elect to create a record
\par
of his or her actions by providing written notice to a supervisor or other appropriate officer.
\par
\textbf{4. Waiver of disqualification. }An employee who would otherwise be disqualified may be permitted
\par
to participate in a particular matter where the otherwise disqualifying financial interest is exempt
\par
(see Part II of this Appendix), is the subject of an individual waiver described in this paragraph, or
\par
results from certain Indian birthrights as described in 18 U.S.C. \S{} 208 (b) (4). An individual waiver
\par
may be requested pursuant to 18 U.S.C. \S{} 208 (b) (1). In advance of the employee's participation,
\par
the employee must advise the officer responsible for the employee's appointment (or other officer to
\par
whom authority to issue such a waiver for the employee has been delegated) about the nature and
\par
circumstances of the particular matter and the employee's role in the matter; and make full
\par
disclosure to the officer of the nature and extent of the disqualifying financial interest. If the officer
\par
determines that the employee's financial interest in the particular matter is not so substantial as to be
\par
deemed likely to affect the integrity of the services which the Bank may expect from the employee,
\par
the officer may issue a written waiver to the employee.
\par
\textbf{5. Divestiture of a disqualifying financial interest}. Upon sale or other disposal of the interest that
\par
causes his or her disqualification from participation in a particular matter, the employee may
\par
participate in the matter.
\par
a)\textbf{ }\emph{Voluntary divestiture}\textbf{. }An employee who otherwise would be disqualified from participating in a
\par
particular matter may voluntarily sell or otherwise dispose of the interest that causes the
\par
disqualification.
\par
b)\textbf{ }\emph{Directed divestiture}\textbf{. }An employee may be required to sell or otherwise dispose of the
\par
disqualifying financial interest if the continued holding of that interest is prohibited by Section 5 of
\par
the Code or substantially interferes with the ability of the employee to perform his or her job.
\par
\textbf{6. Official duties that give rise to potential conflicts. }When an employee's duties create a
\par
substantial likelihood that the employee may be assigned to a particular matter from which he or she
\par
is disqualified, the employee should advise his or her supervisor or the Bank's Ethics Officer of that
\par
potential so that a conflicting assignment can be avoided.
\par
-24-\textbf{PART II. EXEMPTIONS PURSUANT TO 18 U.S.C. \S{} 208 (b) (2)}
\par
\textbf{1. Definitions. }For purposes of this Part, the following definitions apply.
\par
A. \emph{Diversified }means that a fund, trust or plan does not have a stated policy of concentrating its
\par
investments in any industry, business, single country other than the United States, or bonds of a
\par
single state within the United States. In the case of an employee benefit plan, \emph{diversified }means that
\par
the plan's trustee has a written policy of varying plan investments\textbf{.}
\par
B. \emph{Long-term federal government security }means a bond or note, except for a US Savings bond,
\par
with a maturity of more than one year, issued by the United States Treasury pursuant to 31 U.S.C.
\par
Chapter 31.
\par
C. \emph{Municipal security }means direct obligation of, or obligation guaranteed as to principal or interest
\par
by, a state (or any of its political subdivisions, or any municipal corporate instrumentality of one or
\par
more states), or the District of Columbia, Puerto Rico, the Virgin Islands, or any other possession of
\par
the United States.
\par
D. \emph{Mutual fund }means an entity which is registered as a management company under the Investment
\par
Company Act of 1940, as amended (15 U.S.C.\S\S{} 80a-1 et seq.). Mutual fund includes open-end and
\par
closed-end mutual funds and registered money market funds.
\par
E. \emph{Sector mutual fund }means a mutual fund that concentrates its investments in an industry,
\par
business, single country other than the United States, or bonds of a single state within the United
\par
States.
\par
F. \emph{Short-term federal government security }means a bill with a maturity of one year or less issued by
\par
the United States Treasury pursuant to 31 U.S.C. Chapter 31.
\par
\textbf{2. Exemption for Interest in Mutual Fund, Unit Investment Trust, and Employee Benefit}
\par
\textbf{Plan.}
\par
A. \emph{Diversified mutual fund and unit investment trust. }An employee may participate in a particular
\par
matter affecting holdings of a diversified mutual fund or a diversified unit investment trust
\par
where the disqualifying financial interest in the matter arises because of the ownership of an
\par
interest in the fund or trust.
\par
\emph{Example}
\par
An employee owns shares worth \$60,000 in several mutual funds whose portfolios contain stock
\par
in a nationally known computer company. Each fund prospectus describes the fund as a
\par
"management company," but does not characterize the fund as having a policy of concentrating
\par
its investments in any particular industry, business, single country (other than the U.S.) or bonds
\par
of a single state. The employee may participate in a matter affecting the computer company.
\par
-25-B\textbf{. }\emph{Sector mutual fund. }An employee may participate in a particular matter affecting holdings of a
\par
sector mutual fund where the disqualifying financial interest in the matter arises because of
\par
ownership of an interest in the fund and:
\par
(1) The affected holding is not invested in the sector in which the fund concentrates.
\par
(2) The aggregate market value of interests in any sector fund or funds does not exceed
\par
\$50,000.
\par
For purposes of calculating the \$50,000 de minimis amount, an employee must aggregate the market
\par
value of all sector mutual funds in which the employee has a disqualifying interest and that
\par
concentrate in the same sector and have one or more holdings that may be affected by the particular
\par
matter.
\par
\emph{Example 1}
\par
A Bank employee owns shares in a mutual fund that expressly concentrates its holdings in the stock
\par
of utility companies. In addition to holdings in utility companies, the fund contains stock in certain
\par
regional banks and bank holding companies whose financial interests would be affected by a matter
\par
in which the employee would participate. The employee is not disqualified from participating in the
\par
matter because the banks and bank holding companies that would be affected are not part of the
\par
sector in which the fund concentrates.
\par
\emph{Example 2}
\par
An analyst is assigned at the Federal Reserve Board's request to help draft standards for approving
\par
loan guarantee applications for the Air Transportation Stabilization Board. The analyst owns
\par
\$35,000 worth of shares in XYZ Global Transportation Fund, a sector mutual fund invested
\par
primarily in shipping firms and airlines. The analyst may participate in the recommendations. If the
\par
analyst's spouse also owns \$40,000 worth of shares in ABC Specialized Portfolios: Transport, a
\par
sector mutual fund that focuses on the same sector, and if both funds contain holdings that may be
\par
affected by the particular matter, the analyst may not rely on the \$50,000 \emph{de minimis }exemption.
\par
C\emph{. Employee benefit plan. }An employee may participate in:
\par
(1) A particular matter affecting holdings of an employee benefit plan where the disqualifying
\par
financial interest in the matter arises from membership in:
\par
(a) The Thrift Savings Plan for Federal Employees described in 5 U.S.C. \S 8437.
\par
(b) A pension plan established or maintained by a state government or any political
\par
subdivision of a state government for its employees.
\par
(c) A diversified employee benefit plan, provided:
\par
(i) The investments of the plan are administered by an independent trustee,
\par
and the employee, or other person specified in section 208(a), does not
\par
participate in the selection of the plan's investments or designate specific plan
\par
investments (except for directing that contributions be divided among several
\par
different categories of investments, such as stocks, bonds or mutual funds,
\par
which are available to plan participants).
\par
-26-(ii) The plan is not a profit-sharing or stock bonus plan.
\par
(2) A particular matter of general applicability, such as rulemaking, affecting the state or local
\par
government sponsor of a state or local government pension plan described in paragraph 2(C)(1)(b)
\par
where the disqualifying financial interest in the matter arises because of participation in the plan.
\par
\textbf{3. Exemption for Interest in Securities}.
\par
a)\textbf{ }\emph{De minimis exemption for a matter involving parties}. An employee may participate in a particular
\par
matter involving specific parties where the disqualifying financial interest arises from ownership by
\par
the employee, his or her spouse or minor children of securities issued by one or more entities
\par
affected by the matter, if:
\par
(1) The securities are publicly traded,3
\par
or are long-term federal government securities or
\par
municipal securities.
\par
(2) The aggregate market value of the holdings of the employee, his spouse and minor
\par
children in the securities of all entities does not exceed \$15,000.
\par
\emph{Example}
\par
A Bank employee owns \$10,000 worth of publicly traded stock in a major office equipment
\par
manufacturer, EQP Corp. The employee may evaluate the proposals submitted by EQP
\par
Corp. and others in response to an RFP. If the employee's spouse also owns \$6,000 worth of
\par
EQP Corp. stock, the employee may not evaluate the proposals unless the employee requests
\par
and is granted a waiver.
\par
particular matter involving specific parties in which the disqualifying interest arises from the
\par
b) \emph{De minimis exemption for matters affecting nonparties.}4 An employee may participate in a
\par
ownership by the employee, his spouse, or minor children of securities issued by one or more
\par
entities that are not parties to the matter but that are affected by the matter if:
\par
(1) The securities are publicly traded, or are long-term federal government or municipal
