\textbf{PART III. SEEKING OTHER EMPLOYMENT}
\par
\textbf{1. Overview}
\par
An employee who seeks other employment or has an arrangement for prospective employment must
\par
comply with the applicable disqualification requirements of this Part if his or her Bank duties would
\par
affect the financial interests of the prospective employer or the person with whom he or she has an
\par
arrangement concerning prospective employment.
\par
\textbf{2. Definitions}
\par
(a) \emph{Direct and predictable effect }and \emph{particular matter }have the respective meanings set forth in Part
\par
I of this Appendix regarding "Disqualifying Financial Interests."
\par
(b) \emph{Employment }means any form of non-Bank employment or business relationship involving the
\par
provision of personal services by the employee, whether to be undertaken at the same time as or
\par
subsequent to Bank employment, even if the employee is not compensated for the services. It
\par
includes but is not limited to personal services provided as an officer, director, employee, agent,
\par
attorney, consultant, contractor, general partner or trustee.
\par
(c) \emph{Prospective employer }means any person with whom the employee is seeking employment.
\par
Where a contact that constitutes seeking employment is made by or with an agent or other
\par
intermediary, the term prospective employer includes:
\par
-29-(1) The person using the agent or other intermediary for the purpose of trying to hire the
\par
employee, provided the agent identifies the prospective employer to the employee.
\par
(2) A person contacted by the employee's agent or other intermediary for the purpose of
\par
seeking a job offer for the employee if the agent identifies the prospective employer to
\par
the employee.
\par
\emph{Example}
\par
An examiner has retained an employment search firm to help her find another job. The search firm
\par
has just reported to her that it has given her resume to and had promising discussions with two bank
\par
holding companies in the District. Even though the employee has not personally had employment
\par
discussions with either, each bank holding company is her prospective employer. She began seeking
\par
employment with each upon learning its identity and that it has been given her resume.
\par
(d) \emph{Seeking employment }means that an employee has, directly or indirectly:
\par
(1) Engaged in negotiations for employment with any person. Negotiations mean discussion
\par
or communication with another person, or such person's agent or intermediary, mutually
\par
conducted with a view toward reaching an agreement regarding possible employment
\par
with that person. The term is not limited to discussions of specific terms and conditions
\par
of employment in a specific position.
\par
(2) Made an unsolicited communication to any person, or such person's agent or
\par
intermediary, regarding possible employment with that person. However, the employee
\par
has not begun seeking employment if that communication was:
\par
(a) For the sole purpose of requesting a job application.
\par
(b) For the purpose of submitting a resume or other employment proposal to a person
\par
affected by the performance or nonperformance of the employee's duties only as
\par
part of an industry or other discrete class. The employee is considered to have
\par
begun seeking employment upon receipt of any response indicating an interest in
\par
employment discussions.
\par
(3) Made a response other than rejection to an unsolicited communication from any person,
\par
or such person's agent or intermediary, regarding possible employment with that person.
\par
\emph{Example}
\par
An employee in the Check Collection Department has mailed his resume to 25 commercial banks.
\par
He has not begun seeking employment with any of the twenty-five. If he receives a response from
\par
one of the commercial banks indicating an interest in employment discussions, the employee will
\par
have begun seeking employment with that bank at that time.
\par
(e) \emph{Not seeking employment. }An employee is no longer seeking employment when:
\par
(1) The employee or the prospective employer rejects the possibility of employment and all
\par
discussions of possible employment have terminated.
\par
-30-(2) Two months have transpired after the employee sent an unsolicited resume or
\par
employment proposal, and the employee has received no indication of interest in
\par
employment discussions from the prospective employer\emph{. }A response that defers
\par
discussions until the foreseeable future does not constitute rejection of an unsolicited
\par
employment proposal, nor rejection of a prospective employment possibility.
\par
\emph{Example 1}
\par
An employee with procurement responsibilities is complimented by a vendor's representative who
\par
asks her to call if she is interested in leaving the Bank\emph{. }The employee explains to the vendor that she
\par
is very happy with her job at the Bank and is not interested in another job\emph{. }She thanks him for his
\par
compliment regarding her work and adds that she'll remember his interest if she ever decides to
\par
leave the Bank\emph{. }The employee has rejected the unsolicited employment overture and has not begun
\par
seeking employment.
\par
\emph{Example 2}
\par
The employee in the preceding example responds by stating that she cannot discuss future
\par
employment while she is working on a project affecting the vendor's relationship with the Bank but
\par
would like to discuss employment when the project is completed\emph{. }Because the employee has merely
\par
deferred employment discussions until the foreseeable future, she has begun seeking employment.
\par
\emph{Example 3}
\par
An economist responsible for reviewing certain bank holding company applications sends her
\par
resume to a bank holding company in the District\emph{. }The employee has begun seeking employment
\par
with that company and will be seeking employment for two months from the date the resume was
\par
mailed\emph{. }However, if she withdraws her application or is notified within the two-month period that
\par
her resume has been rejected, she will no longer be seeking employment with the company as of the
\par
date she makes such withdrawal or receives such notification.
\par
\textbf{3. Disqualification While Seeking Employment}
\par
a)\emph{ Obligation to disqualify. }Unless the employee's participation is authorized in accordance with
\par
paragraph 4 of this Part, the employee must not participate in a particular matter that, to the
\par
employee's knowledge, has a direct and predictable effect on the financial interests of a prospective
\par
employer with whom he or she is seeking employment\emph{. }Disqualification is accomplished by not
\par
participating in the particular matter.
\par
b)\emph{ Notification. }An employee who becomes aware of the need to disqualify himself or herself from
\par
participation in a particular matter should notify the person responsible for his or her assignment or
\par
the Bank's Ethics Officer\emph{. }An employee who is responsible for his or her own assignment should
\par
take whatever steps are necessary to ensure that he or she does not participate in the matter from
\par
which he or she is disqualified\emph{. }Appropriate oral or written notification of the employee's
\par
disqualification may be made to coworkers by the employee or a supervisor to ensure that the
\par
employee is not involved in a matter from which he or she is disqualified.
\par
-31-c)\emph{ Documentation. }An employee need not file a written disqualification statement unless he or she is
\par
specifically asked to do so by the Bank's Ethics Officer or a supervisor\emph{. }However, an employee may
\par
elect to create a record of his actions by providing written notice to a supervisor or other appropriate
\par
officer.
\par
d)\emph{ Bank determination of substantial conflict. }When the Bank determines that the employee's action
\par
in seeking employment with a particular person requires disqualification from a matter so central or
\par
critical to the performance of the employee's duties that the employee's ability to perform the duties
\par
of his or her position will be materially impaired, the Bank may take appropriate action, including
\par
termination of employment.
\par
\textbf{4. Waiver or Authorization Permitting Participation While Seeking Employment}
\par
a)\emph{ Waiver. }When an employee is engaged in discussions that constitute seeking employment, the
\par
employee may participate in a particular matter that has a direct and predictable effect on the
\par
financial interest of a prospective employer only after receiving a written waiver issued under the
\par
authority of 18 U.S.C.\S{} 208 (b) (1)\emph{. }The requirements for a waiver are described in paragraph 4 of
\par
Part I.
\par
b)\emph{ Authorization by Bank. }Where an employee is seeking employment and a reasonable person
\par
would be likely to question the employee's impartiality if the employee were to participate in a
\par
particular matter that has a direct and predictable effect on the financial interests of the prospective
\par
employer, the employee may participate in such matters only where the Bank has authorized his or
\par
her participation after consideration of all relevant factors, including:
\par
(1) The nature of the relationship involved.
\par
(2) The effect that resolution of the matter would have upon the financial interests of the
\par
person involved in the relationship.
\par
(3) The nature and importance of the employee's role in the matter, including the extent to
\par
which the employee is called upon to exercise discretion in the matter.
\par
(4) The sensitivity of the matter.
\par
(5) The difficulty of reassigning the matter to another employee.
\par
(6) Adjustments that may be made in the employee's duties that would reduce or eliminate
\par
the likelihood that a reasonable person would question the employee's impartiality.
\par
\textbf{5. Disqualification Based on an Arrangement Concerning Prospective Employment or}
\par
\textbf{Otherwise After Negotiations}
\par
A.\emph{ Employment or arrangement concerning employment. }An employee must not take any official
\par
action in a particular matter that will have a direct and predictable effect on the financial interests of
\par
the person by whom he or she is employed or with whom he or she has an arrangement concerning
\par
future employment, unless authorized to participate in the matter by a written waiver issued under
\par
the authority of 18 U.S.C. \S{} 208 (b) (1)\emph{. }The requirements for a waiver are described in paragraph 4
\par
of Part I of this Appendix.
\par
B. \emph{Offer rejected or not made. }The Bank may, in an appropriate case, determine that an employee
\par
who has sought but is no longer seeking employment nevertheless shall be subject to a period of
\par
-32-disqualification upon the conclusion of employment negotiations\emph{. }Any such determination shall be
\par
based on a consideration of all the relevant factors and a determination that the concern that a
\par
reasonable person may question the integrity of the Bank's decision making process outweighs the
\par
Bank's interest in the employee's participation in the particular matter\emph{. }Relevant factors are listed in
\par
paragraph 4 (B) of this Part.
\par
\emph{Example}
\par
An employee with purchasing responsibilities was relieved of responsibility for handing the
\par
evaluation of responses to an RFP while seeking employment with a bidder\emph{. }The firm did not offer
\par
her employment\emph{. }Even though she is no longer seeking employment with the firm, she may continue
\par
to be disqualified from evaluating the RFP responses based on a determination that the concern that
\par
a reasonable person might question whether, in view of the history of the employment negotiations,
\par
she could act impartially in the matter outweighs the Bank's interest in her participation.
\par
-33-\textbf{APPENDIX B}
\par
This Appendix contains a description of the rules adopted by the Board of Governors
\par
(the "Board"), that are applicable to examiners and also to other employees who participate in
\par
supervision or regulation matters other than examinations and inspections, such as an attorney or
\par
economist who reviews bank holding company applications or an employee who has a substantive
\par
role in discount/lending decisions, regardless of reporting relationships within the Bank.
\par
The rules described in this Appendix are a condensed version of the rules set forth in the document
\par
entitled "Banking Supervision and Regulation Administrative Policy Statements" [SR-05-02 dated
\par
February 3, 2005, which is set forth in Sections 5-035 and 5-041 of Part 5 of the Federal Reserve
\par
Administrative Manual (the "FRAM"); and SR-05-26, dated December 8, 2005, regarding post-
\par
employment restrictions for senior examiners, which is set forth in Section 5-043 of FRAM]. For
\par
more detailed information, you should review the SR-letters or FRAM.
\par
The Board's rules supplement the Bank's Code of Conduct (the "Code"). To the extent the rules set
\par
more stringent standards for employees covered by this Appendix than those contained in the Code,
\par
the Board's rules are to be followed.
\par
Part I contains a description of the rules for examiners; Part II contains a description of the rules for
\par
other employees with substantive responsibilities relating to supervision or regulation matters.
\par
When used in this Appendix:
\par
{$\bullet$} The word "examine" or "examination" refers both to examining a depository institution and
\par
to inspecting a bank holding company (including a financial holding company) or any non-
\par
bank subsidiary.
\par
{$\bullet$} "Supervisory matter" includes, but is not limited to, an application, audit, review (including
\par
report review), institution-specific analysis or surveillance, enforcement action,
\par
investigation, credit review, collateral analysis, or lending decision. However, supervisory
\par
matter does not include an examination.
\par
An employee who needs assistance in interpreting the Board's rules or who desires additional
\par
information, for example about a waiver, should contact the Bank's Ethics Officer.
\par
-34-\textbf{PART I. RULES FOR CREDENTIALED STAFF}
\par
The rules in this Part apply to all employees holding a Board issued standard, special, temporary or
\par
ad hoc credential. However, paragraph 1 (B), concerning prohibited borrowing relationships, applies
\par
only to employees holding a standard or special credential.
\par
\textbf{1. Prohibited Borrowing}
\par
a) \emph{Criminal Prohibition. }It is a crime under federal law (18 U.S.C. \S{} 213) for an examiner to accept
\par
a loan or gratuity from an institution the examiner examined. It is also a federal crime (18 U.S.C.
\par
\S{} 212) for an employee or director of a member bank or certain other institutions which an examiner
\par
