c) \emph{Prohibition Against Seeking Credit if Handling Supervisory Matter (Other Than}
\par
\emph{Examination/Inspection).}
\par
1)\emph{ General Prohibition. }An examiner may not, on his or her own behalf, or on behalf of
\par
anyone else, seek or accept a loan from, or renew or renegotiate a loan with, an institution
\par
-39-or any affiliate if the examiner is working on or knows he or she will be assigned a
\par
supervisory matter which involves the institution or any affiliate. Furthermore, an
\par
examiner must disqualify himself or herself from handling a supervisory matter involving
\par
an institution or any affiliate if the examiner learns that his or her spouse or dependent
\par
child or a related entity is seeking or has sought or accepted a loan from, or has renewed
\par
or renegotiated a loan with, the institution or any affiliate while the matter is pending
\par
before the Bank or the Board. The foregoing prohibitions continue for three months after
\par
the examiner's participation in the matter ends.
\par
2)\emph{ Exceptions. }These prohibitions do not apply to: i) obtaining or using a credit card; ii)
\par
borrowing against the cash value of a life insurance policy; or iii) an overdraft protection
\par
plan. The credit card, overdraft protection line, or insurance policy loan must not have
\par
been provided on terms more favorable than those available to the public.
\par
3)\emph{ Waiver. }A written waiver from the prohibitions of this paragraph 2 (C) may, in some
\par
limited circumstances, be obtained from the examiner's supervising officer, in
\par
consultation with the Bank's Ethics Officer.
\par
\textbf{3. Recusal Based Upon Past Employment, Family Relationships or Financial Interests}
\par
A. \emph{Past Employment. }An examiner may not examine, or participate in a supervisory matter
\par
involving, an institution or any affiliate if the examiner was employed by the institution within the
\par
preceding 12 months. The examiner's supervising officer, in consultation with the Bank's Ethics
\par
Officer, may determine that recusal should be required for a longer period.
\par
B. \emph{Continuing Participation in Pension/Retirement Plan. }If an examiner continues to participate in a
\par
pension or retirement plan obtained through prior employment at an institution or any affiliate, the
\par
examiner may not participate in an examination of that institution or any affiliate, nor may the
\par
examiner participate in a supervisory matter involving the institution or any affiliate unless the
\par
examiner receives a written opinion from the Bank's General Counsel authorizing such
\par
participation.
\par
C. \emph{Family Relationships. }An examiner may not examine an institution or any affiliate, or participate
\par
in a supervisory matter involving the institution or any affiliate if the examiner's spouse, child,
\par
parent, or sibling is employed by the institution or any affiliate. The examiner's supervising officer,
\par
in consultation with the Bank's Ethics Officer, may require recusal in other situations that might
\par
give rise to an appearance of a conflict of interest---for example, if the examiner's sister-in-law is
\par
employed by the institution.
\par
D. \emph{Financial Interests. }Under Section 5.2 of the Code and federal criminal law (18 U.S.C. \S{} 208), a
\par
Bank employee is prohibited from participating personally and substantially in an official capacity
\par
in any particular matter in which, to the employee's knowledge, the employee has a financial interest
\par
if the particular matter will have a direct and predictable effect on that interest. Participation in a
\par
particular matter may include making a decision or recommendation, providing advice, or taking
\par
part in an investigation. See Section 5.2 and Appendix A of the Code for more information.
\par
-40-\emph{Example}
\par
An examiner may not participate in the review of a credit file during an examination, inspection, or
\par
a shared national credit examination, if the examiner or the examiner's spouse, minor child, general
\par
partner, or any organization for which the examiner serves as an employee, director or trustee, has a
\par
financial interest in the borrower, or if the examiner is negotiating for or has an agreement
\par
concerning future employment with the borrower.
\par
An insurance policy may be a financial interest under the Federal conflicts of interest statute and an
\par
examiner holding an insurance policy should not participate in any particular matter affecting a
\par
company that issued the policy unless the examiner's participation is approved in advance and in
\par
writing by the Reserve Bank's legal department.
\par
\emph{Example}
\par
An examiner who has an insurance policy from an insurance subsidiary of a financial holding
\par
company is generally permitted to examine an affiliate. However, cases may arise that require an
\par
examiner to recuse himself from all matters involving the financial holding company or its affiliates.
\par
For example, recusal may be appropriate if an examiner is filing or appealing a claim under the
\par
policy or if the insurance company is experiencing financial difficulties. Such situations should be
\par
discussed with the Bank's Ethics Officer to determine if recusal is appropriate.
\par
E) \emph{Waiver. }In certain cases, the prohibitions described in paragraphs 3 (A) and 3 (C) may be waived
\par
by the examiner's supervising officer, in consultation with the Bank's Ethics Officer. The prohibition
\par
described in paragraph 3 (D) may be waived only in accordance with the federal statute, and the
\par
examiner should consult with the Bank's Ethics Officer if such a waiver is desired.
