\textbf{4. Acceptance of Meals and Gratuities}
\par
a) \emph{Gifts or Meals from Examined Entity. }Notwithstanding anything to the contrary in the Code,
\par
under federal criminal law an examiner may not accept a gift from an entity that the examiner
\par
examined. As a matter of policy, the Board has decided that an examiner may not accept a gift or
\par
meal from an entity that the examiner has examined, examines or is authorized to examine.
\par
However, an examiner may:
\par
1. Eat in the entity's cafeteria provided he or she pays for the meal at the rate charged the
\par
general public.
\par
2. Accept refreshments such as soft drinks, coffee and donuts offered other than as part of a
\par
meal.
\par
3. Accept items with little intrinsic value, such as a pen or calendar, provided such items are
\par
also offered to the general public.
\par
b) \emph{Gifts or Meals from other "Covered Sources". }All Bank employees are subject to the provisions
\par
in the Code concerning acceptance of gifts and meals from "covered sources." Under the Code, the
\par
term covered source includes other entities in addition to institutions subject to examination by the
\par
System (See Section 5.4 of the Code).
\par
-41-c) \emph{Exceptions. }The Code provides limited exceptions under which an employee may accept a gift or
\par
meal from a covered source. However, an examiner may never use the \$20 "de minimis" exception
\par
(see Section 5.4 (A) (2) (a) (i) of the Code) to accept a gift or a meal from an institution for which
\par
the System is the primary supervisor (see footnote 1 to this Appendix). An examiner may accept a
\par
gift or a meal from a covered source pursuant to one of the other exceptions, provided that the
\par
covered source is not an entity that the examiner is examining, has examined, or is authorized to
\par
examine.
\par
\emph{Example}
\par
An examiner's spouse is a loan officer at a state member bank. The examiner has never examined
\par
the bank, and will not be authorized to examine the bank as long as her spouse is employed by the
\par
bank. The examiner may accompany her spouse to the commercial bank's annual dinner dance for
\par
its employees and their guests pursuant to Section 5.4 (A) (2) (a) (iv) of the Code.
\par
\textbf{5. Special Post-Employment Restriction}
\par
A. \emph{Coverage.} An examiner who has served as the ``senior examiner'' for a state member bank, bank
\par
holding company, or foreign bank for two or more months during the examiner's final twelve
\par
months of employment with the Bank may not knowingly accept compensation as an employee,
\par
officer, director, or consultant from such state member bank, bank holding company, or foreign
\par
bank, or from certain related entities, for one year following the termination of the examiner's
\par
employment with the Bank.
\par
B. \emph{Definitions}. An ``examiner'' is considered to be a ``senior examiner'' for a particular state member
\par
bank, bank holding company, or foreign bank if the examiner meets all of the following criteria:
\par
1. The examiner has been authorized by the Board to conduct examinations or
\par
inspections on behalf of the Board.
\par
2. The examiner has been assigned continuing, broad, and lead responsibility for
\par
examining or inspecting that state member bank, bank holding company, or foreign
\par
bank.
\par
3. The examiner's responsibilities for examining, inspecting, and supervising the state
\par
member bank, bank holding company, or foreign bank:
\par
A. Represent a substantial portion of the examiner's assigned responsibilities.
\par
B. Require the examiner to interact routinely with officers or employees of the
\par
state member bank, bank holding company, or foreign bank or their
\par
respective affiliates.
\par
-42-By ``related entities,'' this restriction means:
\par
1. With respect to a state member bank, a subsidiary of the state member bank or a
\par
company that controls the state member bank.
\par
2. With respect to a bank holding company, any depository institution controlled by the
\par
bank holding company, including any subsidiary of the depository institution.
\par
3. With respect to a foreign bank, any United States branch or agency of the foreign
\par
bank or any United States depository institution controlled by the foreign bank
\par
(including any subsidiary of the depository institution).
\par
C. \emph{Limited Application of the Restriction.} This restriction on post-employment does not apply to
\par
an examiner who performs only periodic, short-term examinations of a depository institution or
\par
holding company and who does not have ongoing, continuing responsibility for the institution or
\par
holding company. Moreover, this restriction does not cover an examiner who spends a
\par
substantial portion of his or her time conducting or leading a targeted examination (such as a
\par
review of an institution's credit risk management, information systems, or internal audit
\par
functions) and who does not have broad and lead responsibility for the overall examination
\par
program for the institution or holding company.
\par
D. \emph{Penalty. }An examiner who violates this restriction shall be subject to (i) an order removing
\par
the examiner from the prohibited position, and (ii) an industry-wide employment prohibition for
\par
not more than five years, a civil penalty of not more than \$250,000, or both.
\par
E. \emph{Waiver}. In exceptional circumstances, the Chairman of the Board may waive this restriction
\par
for a senior examiner by certifying in writing that granting the examiner a waiver would not
\par
affect the integrity of the Federal Reserve System's supervisory program.
