\par
\textbf{Before Leaving Government}
\par
In general, an executive branch employee is free to seek post-Government employment, but the employee may need
\par
to be disqualified from working on some Government matters while doing so.
\par
\textbf{Disqualification While ``Seeking Employment''}
\par
A criminal conflict of interest statute, 18 U.S.C. \S{} 208, prohibits an employee from participating personally and
\par
substantially, in an official capacity, in any ``particular matter'' that would have a direct and predictable effect on the
\par
employee's financial interests or on the financial interests of a person or organization with whom the employee is
\par
negotiating or has an arrangement concerning prospective employment. A related executive branch-wide regulation,
\par
Subpart F of 5 C.F.R. part 2635, prohibits an employee from working on a particular matter if the employee is
\par
``seeking employment'' with a person or organization affected by that matter, even though the employee's job search
\par
has not progressed to actual negotiations. An employee who complies with the disqualification requirements in
\par
Subpart F will ensure compliance with the conflict of interest statute.
\par
An employee is ``seeking employment'' as defined in Subpart F, and the disqualification (``recusal'') requirement
\par
applies, if:
\par
 the employee is engaged in actual negotiations for employment;
\par
 a prospective employer has contacted the employee about possible employment and the employee makes a
\par
response other than rejection; or
\par
 the employee has contacted a prospective employer about possible employment, unless the sole purpose of
\par
the contact is to request a job application. (An employee is seeking employment with any person to whom
\par
he sends an unsolicited resume, regardless of how many resumes the employee sends to other employers
\par
at the same time.)
\par
If a search firm or other intermediary is involved, disqualification is not triggered unless the intermediary identifies the
\par
prospective employer to the employee.
\par
If the disqualification requirement applies, it extends to any particular matter (virtually any Government matter) that
\par
would have a direct and predictable effect on the financial interests of the prospective employer. However, if an
\par
employee has thus far acted only unilaterally, a narrow exception permits the employee to work on particular matters
\par
that affect the prospective employer only ``as part of an industry or other discrete class.'' Under the exception, work
\par
on this category of matters is permissible until the employee receives an expression of interest from the employer.
\par
An employee is no longer seeking employment if:
\par
 Two months have elapsed since the employee's dispatch of an unsolicited resume and the employee has
\par
received no expression of interest from the prospective employer; orU.S. Office of Government Ethics - Before Leaving Government
\par
Page 2 of 3
\par
 Either the employee or the prospective employer rejects the possibility of employment and all discussions of
\par
possible employment have ended. A response that merely defers discussion until the foreseeable future
\par
does not constitute rejection.
\par
Example: Ted has met with representatives of several companies while working on a rulemaking that will affect the
\par
financial interests of those companies. One of the representatives asks Ted if he would be interested in discussing a
\par
job opening. If Ted responds that he ``would like to discuss the opening, but not until the rulemaking is final,'' he has
\par
not rejected the possibility of employment.
\par
\textbf{Notification and Documentation of Disqualification}
\par
When an employee becomes aware of the need to be disqualified from a particular matter, the employee should
\par
notify the person responsible for his or her assignments, and may choose to document the disqualification in writing.
\par
Notification permits a supervisor to minimize any disruption of the agency's mission by arranging assignments
\par
accordingly. Moreover, as a practical matter, an employee may need to explain the lack of progress on an
\par
assignment.
\par
Any employee who is a public financial disclosure report filer \emph{must} file a signed notification statement with his or her
\par
agency ethics official within three business days after commencing negotiations or entering into an agreement with a
\par
non-Federal entity to accept post-Government employment or compensation. The statement must identify the entity
\par
and specify the date the negotiations or agreement commenced. A public filer must also document his or her
\par
disqualification from any particular matter that would have a direct and predictable effect on the financial interests of
\par
the entity and submit that signed disqualification document to his or her agency ethics official. The notification
\par
statement and written disqualification may be combined in a single submission.
\par
Some agencies may have established additional requirements concerning notification or the documentation of
\par
disqualifications (and see below concerning additional requirements for some procurement officials).
\par
\textbf{Interview Expenses}
\par
If disqualified as necessary in accordance with Subpart F, an employee may accept gifts of meals, lodging,
\par
transportation, and other benefits customarily provided by a prospective employer in connection with bona fide
\par
employment discussions. This gift provision is published at 5 C.F.R. \S{} 2635.204(e)(3).
\par
\textbf{Possible Disqualification After Employment Search}
\par
If an employee accepts an offer of post-Government employment, the period of disqualification must continue until
\par
the end of Government service. If an employee's search for other employment proves unsuccessful, the
\par
disqualification requirement ends, unless the agency imposes an additional period of disqualification.
\par
\textbf{Additional Requirements for Some Procurement Officials}
\par
If an employee is working ``personally and substantially'' on a procurement for a contract worth more than the
\par
simplified acquisition threshold, 41 U.S.C. \S{} 2103 (formerly 41 U.S.C. \S{} 423) requires that the employee provide
\par
written notice of a contact with an offeror about prospective employment, even if the employee or contractor
\par
immediately rejects the possibility of employment. This ``procurement integrity'' provision also requires the employee
\par
to file a written disqualification memorandum if the employee commences to seek employment. Guidance concerning
\par
the particular requirements of this provision is published in the Federal Acquisition Regulation (FAR), at 48 C.F.R.
\par
