\textbf{After Leaving Government}
\par
An executive branch employee may be affected by conflict of interest restrictions after leaving Government service
\par
(or after leaving certain high-level positions). As highlighted in the bullet points below, there are several legal
\par
authorities that address post-Government employment, and certain authorities contain more than one restriction. A
\par
particular former employee can be affected by more than one post-Government restriction.
\par
\emph{Caveat: The bullet points are not comprehensive. Ethics officials and others should consult the legal authorities,}
\par
\emph{regulatory guidance, and relevant legal opinions. In general, former executive branch employees should seek advice}
\par
\emph{from the Designated Agency Ethics Official or another ethics official at the agency in which the individual formerly}
\par
\emph{served.}
\par
\textbf{Restrictions on Contacts with the Government on Behalf of Others}
\par
 A former employee may be prohibited from having contact with an employee of any Federal agency or court,
\par
on behalf of another person or entity, concerning an official matter with which the former employee was
\par
involved as a Government employee. 18 U.S.C. \S{} 207.
\par
 A former high-level employee or former political appointee may be prohibited from having contact with an
\par
employee of his or her former Federal agency (and perhaps certain officials at other agencies), on behalf of
\par
another person or entity, concerning any official matter. 18 U.S.C. \S{} 207; Executive Order 13490 (the Ethics
\par
Pledge).
\par
 A former political appointee may be prohibited from lobbying a Government official on behalf of a client for
\par
whom he is registered as a lobbyist. Executive Order 13490 (the Ethics Pledge).
\par
\textbf{Restrictions on Providing Assistance to Others}
\par
 A former employee may be prohibited from providing certain assistance to another person or entity
\par
concerning an ongoing trade or treaty negotiation (even though the assistance does not involve contact with
\par
a Government employee). 18 U.S.C. \S{} 207.
\par
 A former high-level employee may be prohibited from providing certain assistance to a foreign government
\par
or foreign political party (even though the assistance does not involve contact with a Government
\par
employee). 18 U.S.C. \S{} 207.
\par
\textbf{Restrictions on Accepting Compensation or Employment}
\par
 A former employee may be prohibited from sharing in profits earned by others if the money was earned from
\par
having contact with the Government on behalf of third parties (e.g., clients) while the former employee was
\par
still in Government. 18 U.S.C. \S{} 203.
\par
 A former employee may be prohibited from accepting compensation from a contractor if the formerU.S. Office of Government Ethics - After Leaving Government
\par
Page 2 of 2
\par
employee served in a Government position or made a Government decision involving more than
\par
\$10,000,000 given to that contractor. 41 U.S.C. \S{} 2104 (formerly 41 U.S.C. \S{} 423).
\par
 A retired member of the uniformed services may not accept employment (or compensation for that
\par
employment) from a foreign government unless he or she first obtains approval from the Department of
\par
State. The Emoluments Clause of the U.S. Constitution.
\par
\textbf{Other Restrictions}
\par
A former executive branch employee may be subject to additional restrictions imposed by agency-specific laws. Also,
\par
every former employee must ensure that his or her post-Government activities are in compliance with other
\par
requirements that may apply without regard to the individual's employment by the Government. For example, if a
\par
former employee will serve as the agent of a foreign principal, the individual must comply with the Foreign Agents
\par
Registration Act.
