\par
\textbf{18 U.S.C. \S{} 207: Restrictions on former officers, employees,}
\par
\textbf{and elected officials of the executive and legislative branches}
\par
\textbf{Text of Statute}
\par
Since its enactment in 1962, 18 U.S.C. \S{} 207 has remained the primary source of restrictions that may limit the
\par
activities of individuals after they leave Government service (or after they leave certain high-level positions). None of
\par
the statute's seven restrictions bar any individual from accepting employment with any private or public employer.
\par
Instead, they prohibit individuals from engaging in certain activities on behalf of persons or entities.
\par
Two of the restrictions may affect any former ``employee,'' regardless of rank or position. The restrictions bar a former
\par
employee from representing another person or entity by making a communication to or appearance before a Federal
\par
department, agency, or court concerning the same ``particular matter involving specific parties'' (e.g., the same
\par
contract or grant) with which the former employee was involved while serving the Government. If the matter was
\par
pending under the employee's official responsibility during the employee's last year of Government service, the bar
\par
lasts for two years. If the employee participated in the matter ``personally and substantially,'' the bar is permanent.
\par
In addition, certain high-level officials are subject to a so-called ``cooling-off'' period. For a period of one year after
\par
leaving a ``senior'' position, a former senior employee may not represent another person or entity by making a
\par
communication to or appearing before the former employee's former agency to seek official action on any matter. A
\par
former ``very senior'' employee is subject to a similar prohibition, except that the bar lasts for two years and extends
\par
to contacts with specified high-level officials at any department or agency. Separately, both former senior and very
\par
senior employees are prohibited for one year from representing, aiding, or advising a foreign government or foreign
\par
political party with the intent to influence certain Government officials.
\par
The two other section 207 restrictions may affect only those individuals who performed specified duties for the
\par
Government. A former employee who participated personally and substantially in an ongoing trade or treaty
\par
negotiation covered by the statute, during his or her last year of Government service, is prohibited for one year from
\par
providing certain assistance concerning the negotiation. A former assignee under the Information Technology
\par
Exchange Program may not represent another person or entity, for one year, in connection with a contract with the
\par
agency to which the former assignee was assigned.
\par
OGE has published guidance, at 5 C.F.R. part 2641, concerning all of the prohibitions in section 207, as well as all of
\par
the exceptions in the statute. In addition, OGE issues a legal advisory every year that specifies a dollar threshold for
\par
purposes of the definition of ``senior employee'' at 5 C.F.R. \S{} 2641.104. (Agency ethics officials and others should
\par
consult 5 C.F.R. part 2637 in lieu of 5 C.F.R. part 2641 in relation to former employees who terminated service from
\par
July 1, 1979 through December 31, 1990.)
