\textbf{5 C.F.R. Part 2635: Standards of ethical conduct for}
\par
\textbf{employees of the executive branch}
\par
\textbf{Text of Regulation}
\par
In 1989, the President's Commission on Federal Ethics Law Reform recommended that individual agency standards
\par
of conduct be replaced with a single regulation applicable to all employees of the executive branch. Acting upon that
\par
recommendation, President Bush signed Executive Order 12674 on April 12, 1989. That Executive Order (as
\par
modified by Executive Order 12731) set out fourteen basic principles of ethical conduct for executive branch
\par
personnel and directed OGE to establish a single, comprehensive, and clear set of executive branch standards of
\par
ethical conduct. OGE published the Standards of Ethical Conduct for Employees of the Executive Branch on August
\par
7, 1992. The regulation became effective on February 3, 1993, and was codified in 5 C.F.R. part 2635. Part 2635
\par
has been amended several times. Review the rulemaking history of 5 C.F.R. part 2635.
\par
\emph{Note: 2635.}
\par
\emph{Some agencies have published agency supplemental regulations that modify or supplement 5 C.F.R. part}
\par
\textbf{Brief Summary of 5 C.F.R. Part 2635}
\par
\textbf{Subpart A -- General Provisions}
\par
Subpart A establishes the framework for the rest of the regulation. It includes definitions, provides authority for
\par
supplementation of the regulation when necessary by individual agencies, and encourages employees to seek
\par
advice from agency ethics officials. It also:
\par
 Restates the 14 principles of ethical conduct and instructs employees to apply them when considering
\par
situations not specifically addressed by the regulation; and
\par
 For situations that involve appearances of conflicts, provides that the circumstances be judged from the
\par
perspective of a reasonable person with knowledge of the relevant facts.
\par
\textbf{Subpart B -- Gifts from Outside Sources}
\par
Subpart B prohibits employees from soliciting or accepting gifts from prohibited sources or gifts given because of
\par
their official position. The term ``prohibited source'' includes anyone seeking business with or official action by an
\par
employee's agency and anyone substantially affected by the performance of the employee's duties. For example, a
\par
company bidding for an agency contract or a person seeking an agency grant would be a prohibited source of gifts to
\par
employees of that agency.
\par
The term ``gift'' is defined to include nearly anything of market value. However, it does not include items that clearly
\par
are not gifts, such as publicly available discounts and commercial loans and it does not include certainU.S. Office of Government Ethics - 5 C.F.R. Part 2635: Standards of ethical conduct for e...
\par
Page 2 of 4
\par
inconsequential items, such as coffee, donuts, greeting cards, and certificates.
\par
There are several exceptions to the prohibitions against gifts from outside sources. For example, with some
\par
limitations, employees may accept:
\par
 Unsolicited gifts with a market value of \$20 or less per occasion, aggregating no more than \$50 in a
\par
calendar year from any single source;
\par
 Gifts motivated by a family relationship or personal friendship;
\par
 Free attendance at certain widely-attended gatherings, such as conferences and receptions, when the cost
\par
of attendance is borne by the sponsor of the event; and
\par
 Food, refreshments, and entertainment at certain meetings or events while on duty in a foreign country.
\par
The subpart also contains guidance on returning or paying for gifts that cannot be accepted.
\par
\textbf{Subpart C -- Gifts Between Employees}
\par
Subpart C prohibits employees from:
\par
 Giving or soliciting for a gift to another employee who is an official superior; or
\par
 Accepting a gift from a lower-paid employee, unless the two employees are personal friends who are not in
\par
a superior-subordinate relationship.
\par
The following are among the exceptions to these prohibitions:
\par
 On an occasional basis, employees may give and accept items aggregating \$10 or less per occasion, food
\par
and refreshments shared in the office, or personal hospitality at a residence. This exception can be used for
\par
birthdays and those holidays when gifts are traditionally exchanged.
\par
 On infrequent occasions of personal significance, such as marriage, and on occasions that terminate the
\par
superior-subordinate relationship, such as retirement, employees may give and accept gifts appropriate to
\par
the occasion and they may make or solicit voluntary contributions of nominal value for group gifts.
\par
\textbf{Subpart D -- Conflicting Financial Interests}
\par
Subpart D contains two provisions designed to deal with financial interests that conflict with employees' official
\par
duties.
\par
The first provision, entitled ``Disqualifying financial interests,'' prohibits an employee from participating in an official
\par
government capacity in a matter in which he has a financial interest or in which his spouse, minor child, employer, or
\par
any one of several other specified persons has a financial interest. For example, an agency purchasing agent could
\par
not place an agency order for computer software with a company owned by his wife. The provision includes
\par
alternatives to nonparticipation, which may involve selling or giving up the conflicting interest or obtaining a statutory
\par
waiver that will permit the employee to continue to perform specific official duties.
\par
The second provision, entitled ``Prohibited financial interests,'' contains authority by which agencies may prohibit
\par
employee from acquiring or retaining certain financial interests.
\par
