\textbf{Subpart E -- Impartiality in Performing Official Duties}
\par
There may be circumstances other than those covered by Subpart D in which employees should not perform official
\par
duties in order to avoid an appearance of loss of impartiality. Subpart E contains two disqualification provisions
\par
addressing those appearance issues.
\par
The first provision, entitle ``Personal and business relationships,'' states that employees should obtain specific
\par
authorization before participating in certain Government matters where their impartiality is likely to be questioned.
\par
The matters specifically covered by this standard include those:
\par
 Involving specific parties, such as contracts, grants, or investigations, that are likely to affect the financial
\par
interests of members of employees' households; or
\par
 In which persons with whom employees have specific relationships are parties or represent parties. This
\par
would include, for example, matters involving recent employers, employers of spouses or minor children, or
\par
anyone with whom the employees have or seek a business or financial relationship.
\par
There are procedures by which employees may be authorized to participate in such matters when it serves the
\par
employing agency's interests. The process set out in Subpart E should be used to address any matter in which an
\par
employee's impartiality is likely to be questioned.
\par
The second provision, entitled ``Extraordinary payments from former employers,'' restricts employees' participation in
\par
certain matters involving former employers. If a former employer gave an employee an ``extraordinary payment'' in
\par
excess of \$10,000 prior to entering Federal service, it bars the employee from participating for two years in matters
\par
in which that former employer is a party or represents a party. A \$25,000 payment voted on an ad hoc basis by a
\par
board of directors would be an ``extraordinary payment.'' A routine severance payment made under an established
\par
employee benefit plan would not.
\par
\textbf{Subpart F -- Seeking Other Employment}
\par
Subpart F prohibits an employee from participating in their official capacities in particular matters that have a direct
\par
and predictable effect on the financial interests of person with whom they are ``seeking employment'' or with whom
\par
they have an arrangement concerning future employment.
\par
The term ``seeking employment'' encompasses actual employment negotiations as well as more preliminary efforts to
\par
obtain employment, such as sending an unsolicited resume. It does not include:
\par
 Sending an unsolicited resume, for example, to someone only affected by the employee's work on general
\par
rulemaking;
\par
 Requesting a job application or rejecting an unsolicited employment overture.
\par
An employee generally continues to be ``seeking employment'' until the employee or the prospective employer rejects
\par
the possibility of employment and all discussions end. However, an employee is no longer ``seeking employment''
\par
with the recipient of an unsolicited resume after two months have passed with no response.
\par
