\textbf{Abstract}
\par
Increased interest in the potential societal benefit of incorporating health economics as a part of
\par
clinical translational science, particularly nutrition interventions, led the Office of Dietary
\par
Supplements at the National Institutes of Health to sponsor a conference to address key questions
\par
about economic analysis of nutrition interventions to enhance communication among health
\par
economic methodologists, researchers, reimbursement policy makers, and regulators. Issues
\par
discussed included the state of the science, such as what health economic methods are currently
\par
used to judge the burden of illness, interventions, or health care policies, and what new research
\par
methodologies are available or needed to address knowledge and methodological gaps or barriers.
\par
Research applications included existing evidence-based health economic research activities in
\par
nutrition that are ongoing or planned at federal agencies. International and U.S. regulatory, policy
\par
and clinical practice perspectives included a discussion of how research results can help regulators
\par
and policy makers within government make nutrition policy decisions, and how economics affects
\par
clinical guideline development.
