\textbf{Methods in Economic Analysis of Nutrition Interventions}
\par
\textbf{Methodological Overview of Medical Cost-Effectiveness Analysis}
\par
Since 1960, annual health care spending has grown 2.5 \% more rapidly than the rest of the
\par
economy.1 Much of this growth is due to growth in quantity; in essence, Americans are
\par
spending more on health care because they are undergoing more procedures, receiving more
\par
care, or both. Concerns about the sustainability of this growth and opportunity costs have
\par
fostered growing demand for cost-effectiveness analysis (CEA). CEA may be able to show
\par
\emph{Nutr Rev}. Author manuscript; available in PMC 2012 September 1.NIH-PA Author Manuscript NIH-PA Author Manuscript NIH-PA Author Manuscript
\par
Wong et al. Page 3
\par
how best to decrease these costs and identify ways to increase the value obtained with
\par
current expenditures. Other countries, such as the United Kingdom and Canada, have used
\par
CEA in their national health systems for years, but in the United States, its use is more
\par
informal. For example, some private payers use CEA to encourage greater use of cost-
\par
effective treatments, and pharmaceutical companies regularly publish CEA studies.
\par
There are three types of CEA:2 1) \emph{Cost Minimization.} This approach identifies the least
\par
expensive method for accomplishing a fixed objective, such as preventing cancer. However,
\par
it assumes that the objective will be met and does not measure the health benefit of the
\par
intervention. Instead, the goal should be to maximize benefits with the available resources,
\par
which is the fundamental aim underlying all CEA approaches; 2) \emph{Cost Benefit.} In this
\par
approach, costs and benefits are measured in dollar terms, and all treatments for which the
\par
net benefit is greater than 0 are selected. This approach requires placing a dollar value on
\par
health outcomes; 3) \emph{Cost-Effectiveness.} This approach examines the ratio of changes in cost
\par
relative to changes in effectiveness and selects treatments with the lowest cost-effectiveness
\par
ratio (the lowest amount of incremental cost relative to the level of incremental effect) to
\par
determine the most efficient use of resources.
\par
The theory underlying CEA has a strong basis in ``constrained maximization,'' the standard
\par
approach to maximize utility or benefit, subject to the constraint of the total amount of
\par
resources available. The goal to maximize utility therefore depends on how much medical
\par
care is consumed subject to a budget constraint (income) minus the expenditures for medical
\par
care consumption. The cost and effectiveness comparisons in CEA result in 4 possible
\par
outcomes: 1) Cost increases and effectiveness decreases, indicating that the intervention
\par
should not be done; 2) Cost increases and effectiveness increases, indicating a need for
\par
CEA; 3) Cost decreases and effectiveness decreases, indicating a need for CEA; 4) Cost
\par
decreases and effectiveness increases, indicating that the intervention should always be
\par
done.
\par
CEA allows researchers to describe the cost-effectiveness of medical interventions in terms
\par
of their additional cost per additional unit of benefit (e.g., life-years). For example,
\par
screening neonates for phenylketonuria saves money and lives, so this intervention would
\par
always be advantageous no matter what value is placed on benefits. Conversely, a screening
\par
ultrasound every 5 years for abdominal aortic aneurism costs \$907,000 per life-year saved
\par
and is not cost effective based on studies suggest that people would give up about \$50,000--
\par
\$200,000 to gain a year of life, providing a baseline willingness to pay for determining
\par
which interventions are cost-effective. To aid policymakers, providers and patients, CEAs
\par
should be based on multiple perspectives, including private, public, and societal viewpoints,
\par
thereby explicitly accounting for the impact of these different perspectives on CEA results.
\par
Quality-adjusted life years (QALYs) are a composite measure reflecting changes in length
\par
and quality of life that accounts for morbidity. Based on decision theory, cost-utility
\par
analyses require eliciting health-related quality of life (HRQOL) utility weights, or
\par
preferences, for health states. These measures are scored on a scale of 0 (dead) to 1 (perfect
\par
health). Cost-utility analysis (CUA) use QALYs as the effectiveness measure in CEA and is
\par
clearly the dominant methodology. The primary approaches to measuring include linear
\par
analog scale, standard gamble, and time trade-off.
\par
\textbf{Methodological Overview of Quality of Life}
\par
A variety of methodologies can be used to measure the effectiveness of interventions to
\par
improve health. Concerns in population health include morbidity (illness, or how people feel
\par
and how health problems personally affect them) and longevity (length of life).3 To compare
\par
widely different interventions for different diseases, the measure of health must encompass
\par
\emph{Nutr Rev}. Author manuscript; available in PMC 2012 September 1.NIH-PA Author Manuscript NIH-PA Author Manuscript NIH-PA Author Manuscript
\par
Wong et al. Page 4
\par
differences not only in length but also in quality of life, symptoms, and ability to function.
\par
Such a measure would include mortality and life expectancy rates and morbidity-based
\par
measures that include health related quality of life (HRQOL) indexes.
\par
The purpose of quality-adjusted survival analysis is to summarize life expectancy with
\par
adjustments for quality of life. A QALY is a health outcome measure that assigns to each
\par
time period a weight ranging from 0 to 1 that corresponds to the quality of life during that
\par
period. The QALY combines morbidity and mortality into a single index, represents life
\par
expectancy with adjustments for QOL, and represents a year of life free of all disabilities
\par
and symptoms. Ideally, prospective studies measure HRQOL for a disease with and without
\par
treatment over time, thereby ultimately estimating the impact of treatment on QALYs.
\par
Similarly, cost with and without treatment can be evaluated. To facilitate data availability, in
\par
alternative ways of estimating QALYs using existing datasets have been developed. Cross-
\par
sectional methodologies allow researchers to estimate HRQOL at a point in time and
\par
combine this measurement with life tables and other methods to estimate the longitudinal
\par
impact. Currently, a small set of potential HRQOL indices are available. Each of these tools
\par
has an associated questionnaire ranging from 5 to nearly 60 questions. The developers of
\par
these indexes have collaborated in recent years to try to develop an aggregate index.
\par
Today, utility-based measures to estimate the impact of nutrition at the population level and
\par
nutritional interventions at the individual level are available. These generic methods allow
\par
comparisons of investments in nutrition with investments in other aspects of health care.
\par
Very few applications however currently exist, and we look forward to the development of
\par
these methods for comparative effectiveness studies.
\par
\textbf{Methodological Overview of Costs}
\par
Aggregate estimates of current incidence, survival, cost burden and future trends can assist
\par
policy and program planning. These estimates can be used to evaluate specific services,
\par
components of care or the care trajectory. For nutritional interventions, aggregate costs
\par
could be used in broad policy analyses. Longitudinal costs represent longitudinal per-person
\par
estimates that are useful for CEA and at the disease category level for tracking costs for
\par
individuals over long periods. These estimates typically reflect current patterns of care, not
\par
idealized care.
\par
In the ``phase of care'' approach, segmenting costs into phases that correspond to periods of
\par
differing average intensities of care avoids biases from analyzing a population at different
\par
disease stages and allows use of the maximal amount of data. Specifically, within the
\par
observation window, patients may be in one of three phases: 1) initial high-cost care, 2) late
\par
stage also high-cost care, and 3) intermediate stage lower cost care. Data on all available
\par
patients can then be used to estimate costs for these different phases of care. Combining
\par
these phase-specific estimates with the population-based incidence data on individuals in
\par
those stages enables the construction of a pseudo-longitudinal estimation of cost, and
\par
combining these phase-specific estimates with prevalence-specific estimates allows the
\par
calculation of prevalence cost. Without this type of approach, researchers risk developing
\par
misleading comparisons of cost.
\par
This method can be applied to other aspects of cost, such as patient time costs. Engaging in
\par
health care activities (such as physician visits) uses time that could be used for other
\par
activities, such as generating income. The vast majority of health economics studies,
\par
however, do not include time costs. Even when time-related costs are substantial, these
\par
studies considered only small convenience samples, focused on specific aspects of care
\par
without any comparison of the intervention to ``regular'' or ``routine'' care. A National
\par
Cancer Institute (NCI), AHRQ, and the Veterans Administration (VA) workshop on cost
\par
