\par
Wong et al. Page 5
\par
estimation (http://healthservices.cancer.gov/publications/workshops/hcc) discusses these
\par
issues in more detail.4
\par
\textbf{Methodological Overview of Sensitivity Analysis and Uncertainty}
\par
The types of uncertainty associated with economic evaluation5 include: 1) outcomes (e.g.,
\par
what will be the outcomes for the actual population of interest?); 2) parameters (e.g., what
\par
are the probabilities, utilities, and costs that govern the actual outcomes?); 3) methodology
\par
(e.g., what costs and consequences should be included and how should they be valued?); and
\par
4) model structures (e.g., what are the structural and causal relationships among the
\par
variables of interest?). Parameter uncertainty can include probabilities of disease prevalence
\par
and incidence, rates of disease progression, and treatment efficacy; health state utilities; and
\par
costs. Sources of uncertainty in parameter estimates include sampling variation and internal
\par
and external study validity.
\par
Probabilistic sensitivity analysis for CEA involves representing the uncertainty about the
\par
parameters in a given analysis as probability distributions. Adding this type of analysis to
\par
the model used to develop cost-effectiveness ratios produces a distribution of cost-
\par
effectiveness results. CEA results consist of an incremental effectiveness change (a QALY
\par
gain or loss) and an incremental cost or cost saving. Although doing so is statistically
\par
complex, a joint distribution of the incremental effectiveness and incremental costs of an
\par
intervention can be generated and compared with those of another intervention.
\par
The most common method for calculating distributions of inputs that lead to distributions of
\par
CEA outputs is Monte Carlo decision modeling, which requires the use of subjective
\par
distributions. Another approach commonly used with clinical trial data is non-parametric
\par
bootstrapping with replacement. Methods to generate distributions around outputs include
\par
joint probability distribution of incremental cost and incremental effectiveness, distribution
\par
of net monetary benefit, and cost-effectiveness acceptability curves. The joint distribution of
\par
incremental cost and incremental effectiveness can be used to determine the probability that
\par
the result is cost effective relative to some willingness to pay WTP threshold.
\par
Common superficial statements that often appear in the discussion sections of papers, such
\par
as ``more research is needed'' or ``we have insufficient evidence from which to draw definite
\par
conclusions,'' have little value. How do the authors know whether more research is needed?
\par
How consequential is this additional research? Mounting a large, expensive study might not
\par
be necessary to resolve such a question for which little is at stake. Furthermore, the Centers
\par
for Medicare and Medicaid Services (CMS) might not have time to wait for such a study to
\par
be conducted before making a decision on coverage. The most helpful type of uncertainty
\par
analysis for decision makers is the value-of-information analysis that shows, for example,
\par
the importance of finding out a parameter's true value or reducing a parameter's uncertainty.6
\par
\textbf{ECONOMIC ANALYSIS FOR HEALTH POLICY}
\par
\textbf{Institute of Medicine}
\par
The Institute of Medicine (IOM) Committee to Evaluate Measures of Health Benefits for
\par
Environmental, Health, and Safety Regulation published recommendations in 2006 for
\par
applying CEA in the economic assessment of federal regulations.7 The Office of
\par
Management and Budget (OMB) has required some form of economic analysis and
\par
centralized review of some federal regulations since the early 1970s. Historically, cost-
\par
benefit analysis (CBA) has been the predominant approach used to assess the economic
\par
impacts of major U.S. health and safety regulations. In 2003, OMB issued \emph{Circular A-4:}
\par
\emph{Regulatory Analysis}, requiring that agencies also conduct CEA whenever ``a valid
\par
effectiveness measure can be developed.'' The next year, OMB and several federal agencies
