\par
Wong et al. Page 6
\par
asked the IOM to convene a consensus committee to consider technical and ethical issues
\par
related to the selection of integrated effectiveness measures, such as quality-adjusted life
\par
years (QALYs, see below).
\par
The IOM committee included several members of the U.S. Panel on Cost-Effectiveness in
\par
Health and Medicine, convened by the U.S. Public Health Service in 1993 to assess the state
\par
of the science and to define best practices for conducting CEAs in health care. The
\par
recommendations for conducting and reporting on CEA in the panel's report, \emph{Cost-}
\par
\emph{Effectiveness in Health and Medicine}, have been adopted by many journals and practitioners
\par
as the standard approach.8
\par
The IOM committee deemed QALYs to be the most appropriate metric for regulatory
\par
analysis because of their simplicity, wide use, and extensive evaluation. The committee also
\par
followed the U.S. panel's endorsement of eliciting direct preferences for health states of
\par
interest in the regulatory framework. However, the committee acknowledged that direct
\par
elicitations of preferences for health states probably would not be practical in regulatory
\par
studies. The committee therefore recommended basing QALY estimates on generic indexes.
\par
Because preference-based EuroQol 5D (now known as EQ-5D, www.euroqol.org/) index
\par
values had been estimated for the general U.S. population recently, the EQ-5D index was
\par
deemed the ``leading candidate'' for adoption in the U.S. regulatory context. The dimensions
\par
of EQ-5D include mobility, self-care, usual activities, pain/discomfort, and anxiety/
\par
depression.
\par
Implementation of the IOM committee's recommendations has been inhibited by time and
\par
resource constraints and by competing priorities. OMB has not issued guidance on
\par
implementing the recommendations. For some agencies, most controversial or problematic
\par
is the recommendation not to use monetized QALYs in CBA, even though WTP estimates---
\par
the theoretically appropriate source of values for CBA---often are not available.
\par
The following four activities would encourage implementation of the IOM committee's
\par
recommendations: 1) assess the informational needs of decision makers; 2) develop criteria
\par
for matching particular measures to the circumstances in which those measures are most
\par
useful instead of routinely reporting numerous results; 3) promote cross-agency
\par
collaboration; 4) separate funding for improved data and methods from specific rule making.
\par
\textbf{An Historic Perspective}
\par
``American Exceptionalism,'' may explain why the United States appears to be more resistant
\par
than other countries to CEA of the incremental costs and health effects associated with
\par
different health interventions. American culture emphasizes liberty and freedom in a way
\par
that other cultures do not, so the U.S. political system rarely produces big government
\par
solutions. Discussions of comparative effectiveness research in the United States have
\par
highlighted the danger of government's intervening between patients and doctors.
\par
Medicare program officials have tried, at times, to incorporate CEA into their decision-
\par
making processes. In the late 1980s, the Medicare program's proposal to use CEA in making
\par
coverage decisions for new medical technology met fierce opposition from the
\par
pharmaceutical and device industries, the American Association of Retired Persons, and
\par
many medical professional societies. The proposal was eventually withdrawn.
\par
Medicare officials state that they do not use CEA when they make coverage decisions about
\par
new technologies, but some signs indicate that this is changing.9 Medicare covers several
\par
technologies that have incremental cost-effectiveness ratios of above \$100,000 per quality-
\par
\emph{Nutr Rev}. Author manuscript; available in PMC 2012 September 1.NIH-PA Author Manuscript NIH-PA Author Manuscript NIH-PA Author Manuscript
\par
Wong et al. Page 7
\par
adjusted life year (QALY) and other inefficient technologies, yet resistance to CEA remains
\par
strong.
\par
Private health plans do everything possible to manage utilization but tend not to use CEA in
\par
an open, explicit way. Some exceptions exist, however, and the industry appears to be
\par
changing slowly. Despite resistance to CEA, the landscape may be changing, in large part
\par
due to the unsustainable growth in medical costs. Peter Orszag, when Director of the OMB,
\par
has stated that ``better information about the costs and benefits of different treatment
\par
options\dots could eventually lower health care spending\dots '' At the highest levels of the federal
\par
government, CEA and the notion that it can help inform decision makers is gaining
\par
recognition.
\par
A database of cost-effectiveness studies is available at www.cearegistry.org. Analysis of the
\par
registry shows that the number of publications on cost-utility analysis (CUA)---CEA that
\par
measures health benefits in terms of QALYs--- is increasing. About half of these studies
\par
originate in the United States, with some funded by NIH. Further analysis also shows that
\par
the methodological underpinnings of CEA and CUA are improving. Increasingly, studies are
\par
characterizing uncertainty and trying to understand the robustness of conclusions under
\par
different assumptions. More economic analyses are being carried out alongside clinical
\par
trials, and the focus on good methodological practices is increasing.
\par
Medicare makes 10--15 national coverage decisions each year and program officials have
\par
begun citing CEA in a number of these decisions. Regression equations show that the value
\par
of technologies, measured as cost-effectiveness, is an independent predictor of what
\par
Medicare covers. The Medicare Improvement Patient and Provider Act of 2008 allowed
\par
Medicare to cover prevention for the first time without explicit Congressional authority, as
\par
long as evidence for the intervention has a high rating from the U.S. Preventive Services
\par
Task Force. The legislation also allows the DHHS Secretary to assess the relation between
\par
predicted outcomes and the expenditures for prevention services. Medicare now has the
\par
authority to use CEA for prevention services and used this authority recently when it
\par
decided to cover HIV screening.
\par
Going forward, the number of published CEAs in the United States will likely continue to
\par
increase; resistance to CEAs will likely continue. New comparative effectiveness research
\par
will likely not include CEAs by legislative mandate, but CEAs will play a more important
\par
role in clinical guidelines and in coverage and reimbursement decisions.
\par
\textbf{Limitations}
\par
The control of American health care costs must be costly to some group if it is to save
\par
money. For example, national medical expenditures must equal the income of health care
\par
industry entities. As a result, cost control must be controversial;10 the income of some
\par
component of the health care system will be less than it otherwise would have been and
\par
those whose income is threatened will try to protect their interests in the political process.
\par
Furthermore, if cost control must be costly to someone, it follows that no ``costless'' or
\par
benign form of cost control exists.
\par
Three benign approaches to reduce costs have been extensively promulgated: 1) increasing
\par
the prevention of illness, morbidity, and death; 2) using health information technology and
\par
electronic medical records to reduce errors and adverse drug interactions; 3) conducting
\par
comparative effectiveness research on the effects and costs of two or more interventions.
\par
Although each of these may be beneficial, the presumption that they would each reduce
\par
costs has yet to be demonstrated.
\par
