\par
Wong et al. Page 8
\par
Without a doubt, research on health politics, policy, and economics suggests that efforts to
\par
make a population healthier do not make medical care less expensive. This is widely
\par
accepted in other countries, such as Canada, but is not a central presumption of the health
\par
care reform discussion in the United States. In 1970, the United States and Canada spent
\par
approximately the same proportion, about 7\%, of their gross national product (GNP) on
\par
health care. By 2000, the United States was spending 14\% of its GNP on health care
\par
compared with 10\% in Canada. The best explanation for this difference is that Canadians
\par
spend less for the same medical care than Americans. For example, even though Canadians
\par
use more bed-days per 1,000 people and have more office visits per capita than Americans,
\par
overall medical costs are lower in Canada because the cost per bed-day and office visit is
\par
lower.
\par
Economic analyses of nutritional interventions must not ignore the political climate.
\par
Nutritional reform should not be oversold in the same way as prevention, health information
\par
technology and electronic medical records, and comparative effectiveness research have
\par
been in the past.
