\textbf{Food and Drug Administration Nutrition Interventions}
\par
Economic analysis brings social science and human behavior into decision making. It
\par
provides quantification of exposures, behavior changes, and health effects, and provides an
\par
estimate of the opportunities and consequences involved in applying interventions on the
\par
large scale. U.S. federal regulatory agencies have been required to carry out economic
\par
analyses of various regulations since the 1970s, under a series of laws and executive orders.
\par
Basic requirements for economic analyses of federal regulations include identifying the need
\par
for regulation, identifying regulatory options, and estimating the costs and benefits of
\par
options. The elements of effective regulatory analysis include: (1) addressing a public health
\par
problem, (2) explaining why regulation is the best way to address the problem, (3) providing
\par
regulatory options for addressing the problem, (4) identifying specific changes in the
\par
behavior of all affected, (5) determining cost changes in behavior, (6) identifying the
\par
effectiveness of changes in behavior, (7) determining the value of the reduction in the public
\par
health problem, and (8) identifying variability and uncertainty in estimates. Regulatory
\par
analysis has a very narrow purpose. It is informing (not deciding or advertising) regulatory
\par
(not clinical practice) policy (sufficient for law and decision making). A regulatory analysis
\par
needs to be an honest evaluation of a regulation to inform decision making. It is only one
\par
input in the decision making process. Examples of FDA economic analyses of major
\par
nutrition regulations include nutrition labeling (1993), folic acid fortification (1996), and
\par
\emph{trans} fat labeling (2003).
\par
The FDA estimates costs based on an engineering cost model for product changes, the cost
\par
of negative health consequences, and the cost for consumers and producers of behavioral
\par
changes. Benefit estimation at FDA is a product of the number of illnesses prevented,
\par
number of QALYs saved per illness prevented, and monetary value of a statistical life year.
\par
\emph{Nutr Rev}. Author manuscript; available in PMC 2012 September 1.NIH-PA Author Manuscript NIH-PA Author Manuscript NIH-PA Author Manuscript
\par
Wong et al. Page 16
\par
Several approaches are available for estimating willingness to pay to reduce risk and these
\par
different approaches result in a wide range of estimates.
\par
Many FDA projects involve estimates of the effects of food labeling. The consumer studies
\par
experts in FDA's Center for Food Safety and Applied Nutrition estimate how much labeling
\par
affects consumers' food choices. Labeling regulations can influence product formulation, as
\par
demonstrated by the experience with \emph{trans} fat labeling. When products are reformulated to
\par
improve a set of products' overall nutrition profile, the nutrition intake even of consumers
\par
who do not use nutrition labeling improves. FDA has conducted multiple economic analyses
\par
of nutrition interventions. For example, the standardized ``Nutrition Facts'' labeling on food
\par
packaging and established standards for and authorized nutrient content claims and health
\par
claims. FDA estimated that these standards prevented 39,000 cases of coronary heart disease
\par
and cancer and saved 13,000 lives as a result over a 20-year period, resulting in 81,000 life-
\par
years saved. Monetized benefits totaled \$3.6 billion. FDA required the reporting of \emph{trans} fat
\par
