\textbf{Centers for Medicare and Medicaid Services}
\par
Medicare is a national program that health insurance companies administer in 15 U.S.
\par
regions. The Medicare Modernization Act of 2003 stipulates that after the Medicare program
\par
issues a draft decision, it must publish its final version within 9--12 months. During this
\par
period, the Medicare program offers two 30-day public comment periods; one is required by
\par
law for any proposed decision and one occurs whenever Medicare opens a decision.
\par
Medicare must make its final decision public no later than 60 days following the close of the
\par
mandated public comment period. This is challenging because, in some cases, CMS receives
\par
more than 6,000 public comments and all public comments must be catalogued and
\par
responded to.
\par
Medicare rarely makes decisions regarding nutrition-related matters because Congress has
\par
not identified most nutritional interventions as insurance benefits under Medicare. Rather,
\par
Medicare beneficiaries typically self-administer nutritional interventions or obtain over-the
\par
counter nutrition supplements on their own. The challenges that are common in the
\par
comparative analysis of nutritional and other health interventions include using secondary
\par
health outcomes from clinical trials done primarily to address other questions, weighing
\par
public input and other important but methodologically weaker factors in decisions, the
\par
sensitivity of recommendations to changes in inputs, and addressing uncertainties about the
\par
consequences of adopting a particular strategy.
