\textbf{The Grades of Recommendation, Assessment, Development and Evaluation (GRADE)}
\par
\textbf{Approach for Incorporating Resource Use into Clinical Guidelines}
\par
Over the past two decades, guideline panels have begun to rate the quality of medical
\par
evidence and the strength of health-related recommendations, including nutritional
\par
recommendations, to provide informative summaries for consumers. Virtually every clinical
\par
organization in the United States has not only produced its own guidelines, but has also
\par
\emph{Nutr Rev}. Author manuscript; available in PMC 2012 September 1.NIH-PA Author Manuscript NIH-PA Author Manuscript NIH-PA Author Manuscript
\par
Wong et al. Page 17
\par
developed its own system for grading its recommendations, as have many national and
\par
international organizations. These myriad systems create much confusion.
\par
Ten years ago, an international group of methodologists and guideline developers began to
\par
create a common international system, Grades of Recommendation, Assessment,
\par
Development and Evaluation (GRADE), to grade evidence quality (Table 2) and strength of
\par
recommendation (Table 3). The GRADE rating system is described in detail in a 2008 issue
\par
of the \emph{British Medical Journal}.
\par
50
\par
In the past few years, more than 50 organizations have adopted the GRADE approach. The
\par
U.S. Preventive Services Task Force, an independent panel that systematically reviews
\par
effectiveness evidence and develops recommendations for clinical preventive services, uses
\par
many elements of the GRADE approach but continues to use its own system.
\par
The two topics evaluated by GRADE are: (1) the \emph{quality} of a body of evidence (i.e., the
\par
extent to which there is confidence that the estimates are adequate to support a decision),
\par
which is rated as high, moderate, low, or very low; and (2) the \emph{strength} of a
\par
recommendation, which is graded as strong or weak. Randomized trials start as high-quality
\par
evidence, but limitations, including risk of bias, inconsistency, indirectness, imprecision,
\par
and publication bias, may lower their quality ratings. Observational studies start as low-
\par
quality evidence, but factors such as very large effects or a dose-response relationship may
\par
increase their quality ratings. The output of the GRADE evaluation is an evidence profile,
\par
which permits different ratings of quality of evidence for different outcomes, and presents
\par
the best estimates of relative and absolute effects.
\par
Resource use is considered an outcome in GRADE. Because of the complexity of its
\par
assessment, some clinical guideline panels choose not to consider resource use. When a
\par
panel does decide to consider resource use, GRADE's approach is to identify the viewpoint
\par
(that is, costs to whom, since different payers bear the costs across and within societies),
\par
label the important resource use items, find relevant evidence, evaluate the quality of the
\par
evidence, and value resources in terms of cost. As with other outcomes, a systematic review
\par
is needed that includes the quality of the evidence and a summary of findings. Also, both
\par
resource use and costs are documented. Quality issues may arise, just as with other
\par
outcomes, and directness often is a major issue. Costs vary more than other outcomes, and
\par
even when resource use is the same, the implications and opportunity costs differ in different
\par
jurisdictions.
\par
GRADE defines the \emph{strength} of recommendations as the degree of confidence that the
\par
desirable effects of adhering to a recommendation outweigh the undesirable effects. In the
\par
case of ``strong'' recommendations, the benefits clearly outweigh the downsides, or vice
\par
versa for weak recommendations. The strength of recommendations may be downgraded if
\par
the \emph{quality} of evidence is low or the desirable and undesirable consequences are closely
\par
balanced. Values and preferences are important when making tradeoffs, especially with
\par
regard to costs. It is important that guideline panels make their values and preferences
\par
explicit.
\par
\textbf{International Perspectives (United Kingdom, Germany and Canada)}
\par
The United Kingdom's National Institute for Health and Clinical Excellence (NICE) is part
\par
of the U.K. National Health Service (NHS) and was created in 1999 to provide an evidence-
\par
based approach to evaluating the clinical effectiveness and cost-effectiveness of new
\par
medical technologies and procedures.51 NICE issues guidance to the NHS on the use of
\par
health care interventions, assesses new treatment methods and procedures, and evaluates
\par
clinical guidelines and public health interventions.
\par
\emph{Nutr Rev}. Author manuscript; available in PMC 2012 September 1.NIH-PA Author Manuscript NIH-PA Author Manuscript NIH-PA Author Manuscript
\par
Wong et al. Page 18
\par
NICE assesses interventions systematically by conducting scoping exercises, reviewing
\par
submissions from the technology's key sponsors, and independently reviewing the published
\par
evidence. Based on these activities, NICE issues guidance to the NHS and then monitors and
\par
reviews the implementation of this guidance.
\par
The clinical data and the economic modeling that NICE systematically reviews tend to be of
\par
higher quality than the actuarial analyses, which are only performed at the end of the review
\par
process and are not always done well. Many NICE evaluations do not consider indirect costs
\par
because of technical problems, although doing so would be desirable. These problems
\par
include uncertainty about how to measure productivity losses due to illness. Failure to
\par
consider indirect costs is rarely a major impediment to implementing NICE
\par
recommendations.
\par
When NICE first announces plans to study an innovation, it issues a call to a broad list of
\par
stakeholder groups, including professional organizations and patient advocacy groups. In
\par
addition, every NICE committee includes a patient representative. However, patient
\par
recommendations may be overruled by budgetary considerations in NICE decisions.
\par
NICE has evaluated many nutritional interventions over the past decade. Unfortunately, the
\par
evaluations have concluded that no evidence exists to support these interventions' use.
\par
However, a NICE review did result in a recommendation (but not a mandate) that health
\par
professionals should consider using omega-3 fatty acid ethyl esters in patients within 3
\par
months after a MI who are not consuming 7 g of omega-3 fatty acids per week by dietary
\par
means.
\par
NICE makes decisions for public health evaluations by examining the intervention types,
\par
how relevant they are to NHS costs, and how strong the evidence is. Recommendations
\par
about very broad interventions that the health care sector is not solely responsible for
\par
implementing, such as interventions involving exercise, rely primarily on goodwill for
\par
implementation because funding is rarely available to implement them. The NHS tends to
\par
follow NICE's recommendations regarding medical interventions, such as vaccinations,
\par
more rigidly. The NHS enforces guidance regarding nutritional interventions, such as
\par
nutrient supplementation for certain populations, if the evidence is very strong. However,
\par
the evidence for nutritional intervention is often weak; in these cases, NICE words its
\par
guidance less definitively, saying, for example, that ``clinicians may consider'' a specific
\par
course of action.
\par
The negative recommendations of NICE tend to be followed more rigidly in a cost-
\par
containment environment than the positive ones. Enforcement of coverage decisions is
\par
always problematic but it is easier in specialist than primary care. Primary care physicians
\par
who follow NICE guidance closely in their prescribing receive incentives.
\par
Some of the issues raised by NICE's experience that are relevant in the United States are the
\par
importance of clear authority, rigorous assessments of medical technologies and procedures,
\par
extensive stakeholder involvement in these reviews, and transparency in decision-making
\par
about these technologies and procedures.
\par
Similarly, Canada's mechanisms for reviewing health care technologies for funding
\par
decisions use economic analysis, and economists now play a prominent role on the
\par
committees that are making policy decisions. Economic analysis in decision making has
\par
begun to widen beyond its established role in pharmaceuticals, although this is still its major
\par
use. In Germany, the two formal health technology assessment agencies are the German
\par
Agency for Health Technology Assessment (DAHTA) at the German Institute of Medical
\par
Documentation and Information (DIMDI) and the Institute for Quality and Efficiency in
\par
\emph{Nutr Rev}. Author manuscript; available in PMC 2012 September 1.NIH-PA Author Manuscript NIH-PA Author Manuscript NIH-PA Author Manuscript
\par
Wong et al. Page 19
\par
Health Care (IQWiG).52 DIMDI's approach to technology assessment is similar to that
\par
already described for the NICE. IQWiG's mandate is to examine all types of technologies,
\par
although its' focus to date has been on expensive new drugs and special problems. German
\par
law requires comparison with existing technologies (without economic assessment). If a new
\par
intervention is superior to existing ones, IQWiG undertakes an economic evaluation. IQWiG
\par
must follow German Ministry of Health's Statement that: ``the approach of excluding drugs
\par
with costs above a fixed uniform threshold value from reimbursement is not compatible with
\par
legal regulations in Germany.'' In Germany and Canada, the focus is on drugs and
\par
technology with no assessments or analyses on dietary supplements to date.
\par
\textbf{Conclusions}
\par
With the maturation of methodologies, economic evaluations of nutrition interventions,
\par
including dietary supplements, in health promotion and chronic disease prevention have
\par
proceeded at both the research and policy levels in the United States. In contrast, economic
\par
evaluations of health technologies have been incorporated into the process for developing
\par
policy recommendations in the UK, Canada and Germany. Because these issues are difficult
\par
to understand, further study of economic issues related to nutrition interventions in chronic
\par
disease, however, would be valuable. Because ODS considers nutrition interventions at the
\par
population level, such evaluations require a great deal of data for modeling and must
\par
account for broad health effects that capture not only benefits but potential risks.
\par
Complicating such economic analyses is the variability in patient access, provider practice
\par
patterns and health reimbursement for disease management and prevention such as obesity
\par
and other chronic diseases. Potential future directions for ODS include 1) focusing on the
\par
unique methodological issues related to studying dietary supplements, such as considering
\par
the impact of third-party payments for dietary supplements instead of out-of-pocket funding;
\par
2) using value of information analysis to help guide the setting of research priorities; 3)
\par
determining quality of life associated with nutritional interventions at the population level,
\par
and 4) applying an epidemiological approach as an alternative to standard regression
\par
analyses by controlling for time-dependent confounding when the confounders are a cause
\par
of the exposure and outcome.
\par
ODS will apply the lessons from this workshop in its mission areas and work with other
\par
federal agency partners to consider the implications of these lessons in its planning. ODS
\par
will identify economic analyses of ongoing and planned studies, especially those that are
\par
directly related to dietary supplements and those that are more broadly relevant to nutrition
\par
interventions for chronic disease prevention. ODS also plans to form partnerships with
\par
federal agencies to advance research in this area, and it welcomes discussions on future
\par
research and policy directions to move the science forward.
