\par
For women with lymph node-positive hormone-receptor
\par
positive (HR(+)) breast cancer, National Comprehensive
\par
Cancer Network Guidelines (NCCN) recommend endocrine
\par
therapy and adjuvant chemotherapy [1]. Although adjuvant
\par
chemotherapy in this patient population has been shown to
\par
increase disease free-survival (DFS) [2], treatment is associ-
\par
ated with significant toxicities.
\par
A 21-gene expression profile (Oncotype DX\textregistered ) has been
\par
developed that provides a recurrence score (RS) on a
\par
continuous scale from 1 to 100 to reflect the risk of disease
\par
recurrence and probability of response to chemotherapy. The1118 W.B. Wong et al. / Contemporary Clinical Trials 33 (2012) 1117--1123
\par
21-gene profile has been shown to be prognostic (risk of
\par
recurrence regardless of treatment) and predictive (identifying
\par
patients who would benefit from adjuvant chemotherapy) in
\par
women with lymph node-negative breast cancer [3,4]. The
\par
benefit of the 21-gene profile in lymph node-positive HR(+)
\par
breast cancer is not well defined. There is limited evidence
\par
from a recent retrospective analysis of a subset of patients (367
\par
of 927) in a phase 3 chemotherapy trial (SWOG-8814)
\par
suggesting that the 21-gene profile may be useful in improving
\par
patient outcomes in this population [5]. Despite the promising
\par
preliminary evidence, the benefit of chemotherapy for patients
\par
with a low risk of recurrence is uncertain, and therefore the
\par
comparative effectiveness of management using OncotypeDX\textregistered{}
\par
vs. current practice (adjuvant chemotherapy for all patients) is
\par
an important question for this population.
\par
The RxPONDER (Rx for Positive Node, Endocrine Respon-
\par
sive Breast Cancer) randomized controlled trial (RCT) (SWOG
\par
S1007; clinical trials registry NCT01272037) was designed to
\par
identify an optimal cut-point for the recurrence score and to
\par
provide definitive evidence of the prognostic and predictive
\par
value of the 21-gene profile among women with lymph node
\par
(LN(+)), hormone receptor positive (HR(+)), and HER2-
\par
negative breast cancer. RxPONDER was designed in consul-
\par
tation with an external stakeholder group representing
\par
patient advocates, health insurers, expert clinicians, and
\par
manufacturers through the Center for Comparative Effective-
\par
ness Research in Cancer Genomics (CANCERGEN) study, and
\par
includes assessment of patient-reported outcomes and
\par
healthcare costs [6].
\par
Comparative effectiveness research (CER) has been de-
\par
fined by the Institute of Medicine (IOM) as``the generation
\par
and synthesis of evidence that compares the benefits and
\par
harms of alternative methods to prevent, diagnose, treat and
\par
monitor a clinical condition, or to improve the delivery of
\par
care. The purpose of CER is to assist consumers, clinicians,
\par
purchasers, and policy makers to make informed decisions
\par
that will improve health care at both the individual and
\par
population levels'' [7]. An important feature of CER is the
\par
development of a prioritized research portfolio through input
\par
from multiple stakeholders across different backgrounds
\par
representing the societal perspective. While CER studies
\par
provide potentially large benefits to society, CER may provide
\par
little individual return for specific groups of stakeholders,
\par
such as individual payers or providers, and thus the public
\par
role of CER (government agency funding such as, e.g. NIH) is
\par
of particular importance [8,9]. Public and private research
\par
funding agencies have limited resources and are faced with
\par
difficult investment decisions that will impact evidence
\par
generation from CER. Therefore, assessing the value of
\par
research in CER is crucial in allowing the prioritization and
\par
funding of the CER which will have the greatest value to
\par
society. We sought to clarify the value of investing in a large-
\par
scale comparative clinical trial, RxPONDER.
\par
2. Methods
\par
2.1. Approach
\par
We developed a mathematical model to project the long-
\par
term clinical and economic outcomes of use of the 21-gene
\par
profile in guiding chemotherapy decisions for women with
\par
lymph node-positive HR(+) breast cancer. Data from SWOG-
\par
8814 was used to estimate the patient outcomes (survival),
\par
while literature sources were used to identify cost and utility
\par
(quality of life) inputs. Our goal for the model was to
\par
estimate the clinical and economic value of the information
\par
that would be provided from the RxPONDER trial. We used a
\par
value of research approach (also known as`value of
\par
information', VOI) to estimate these values. The value of
\par
information analysis is based on the idea that research is
\par
valuable when its results lead to a change in patient
\par
management decisions that either improve patient outcomes
\par
or are more cost-effective; thus it accounts for both the
\par
magnitude of the additional benefit as well as the probability
\par
that a decision change will be made. When there is very little
\par
uncertainty surrounding the outcomes of a current decision,
\par
additional research has little chance of changing that decision
\par
and thus the value of research is low. On the other hand, if
\par
there is significant uncertainty surrounding the benefits and
\par
harms of competing approaches to manage a disease or
\par
condition, then additional research has the potential to
\par
influence that decision greatly, and the value of research is
\par
larger. In this case, the key decision is whether the use of the
\par
21-gene assay in this population results in better outcomes
\par
compared to current management of women with lymph
\par
node positive breast cancer. Specifically, we assessed the
\par
expected value of sample information (EVSI) which provides
\par
the (monetized) value of a trial given the design and sample
\par
size of the trial.
\par
2.2. Study design and model structure
\par
We constructed a decision-analytic model to consider two
\par
interventions: 1) women are screened with the 21-gene
\par
assay, and the recommendation of adjuvant chemotherapy
\par
depends on the RS, and 2) current standard of care (SOC), in
\par
which all women are recommended adjuvant chemotherapy
\par
based on NCCN guidelines [1]. We used a decision tree to
\par
stratify patient RS groups, then used partitioned survival
\par
methods to estimate the effectiveness of the interventions by
\par
calculating mean patient time spent in the disease-free,
\par
recurrence, and death health states (among each RS group)
\par
(Fig. 1). Time spent in the disease-free health state was
\par
determined by estimating mean area under the curve of DFS
\par
curves for below and above a cut-point in RS at which
\par
chemotherapy would be recommended (as to be determined
\par
by RxPONDER). Based on an exploratory re-analysis of DFS in
\par
the SWOG-8814 trial, we estimated that the cut-point above
\par
which chemotherapy would be recommended occurs at an
\par
RS of 19, which was used in our base-case scenario. We
\par
evaluated healthcare costs from the perspective of a U.S.
\par
payer. A patient lifetime horizon (40 years) was used in this
\par
analysis. Costs and outcomes were discounted at a rate of 3\%
\par
based on recommendations for conducting economic evalu-
\par
ations in healthcare in the U.S. [10].
\par
2.3. Patient population
\par
The patient population consisted of women with early
\par
stage breast cancer, HR(+), HER2(-), and 1--3 positive
\par
nodes. The patient population in the model (and in
\par
RxPONDER) differed somewhat from the previous SWOG-W.B. Wong et al. / Contemporary Clinical Trials 33 (2012) 1117--1123
