\par
2.4. Disease-free and overall survival inputs
\par
Table 1 shows the input parameters used in the analysis.
\par
We calculated Kaplan--Meier (KM) survival curves for
\par
disease-free survival based on a re-analysis of SWOG-8814
\par
data for women with a RS {$\leq$} 25 and 1--3 positive nodes. These
\par
survival curves were then further stratified by below and
\par
above a RS cut-point of 19. Hazard ratios (HR) calculated
\par
from the DFS curves were used to estimate treatment effect
\par
of adjuvant chemotherapy (HR in RS {$\leq$} 19: 1.13 (95\%CI 0.50--
\par
2.57), HR in RS {$>$} 19: 0.80 (95\%CI 0.25--2.52)), and the
\par
prognostic effect of a RS below the cut-point (RS {$\leq$} 19) vs.
\par
above the cut-point (RS {$>$} 19) (HR 1.84, 95\%CI 0.68--5.02). In
\par
the base-case scenario, we used data from the DFS KM curves
\par
for the first 10 years, and then extrapolated using a Weibull
\par
model for disease-free survival. Because limited information
\par
on overall survival (OS) was available, we made the
\par
assumption based on clinical studies that survival after
\par
recurrence was an average of 2 years [11,12]. Patients who
\par
recurred earlier in time (and who were presumably younger)
\par
had a slightly higher longer survival after recurrence while
\par
those recurring later in time had a slightly shorter survival
\par
after recurrence. We used U.S. life tables [13] and a long-term
\par
