\par
2.5. Quality-of-life
\par
Utility values based on multiple literature sources
\par
[12,15--17] were applied to the modeled life expectancies to
\par
obtain quality-adjusted life-years (QALYs).
\par
2.6. Cost inputs
\par
The cost of adjuvant chemotherapy was based on a
\par
retrospective claims analysis [18]. The attributable cost of a
\par
distant recurrence was estimated based on an average length
\par
of the recurrence of 2 years [11], and the cost of future
\par
recurrences were discounted to present value. All costs were
\par
updated to 2010 prices using the medical consumer price
\par
index [19].
\par
2.7. Patient preferences regarding use of chemotherapy
\par
Although chemotherapy is recommended for women
\par
with lymph node-positive breast cancer, in clinical practice
\par
not all patients will follow that recommendation [20--23]. In
\par
patient behavior studies in lymph node-negative women,
\par
patients tended to follow the RS recommendation, with only
\par
2--9\% [20,22,23] choosing to receive chemotherapy despite
\par
having a low RS. In node-positive women, however, the risk
\par
of recurrence of a low RS may be similar to that of a woman
\par
with node-negative disease but a high RS (\textasciitilde{} 10--25\%) [5,24].
\par
Given that there is a demonstrated benefit of adjuvant
\par
chemotherapy in LN(+) women and based on patient
\par
behavior studies in node-negative women [20--23], we
\par
assumed that a significant proportion of women (i.e., about
\par
45\%) would perceive the risk of forgoing adjuvant chemo-
\par
therapy as too large and, therefore, would want to receive
\par
chemotherapy despite having a low RS. However, due to the
\par
lack of information specifically in node-positive women, we
\par
used a wide range (10--80\%) in our analysis, described below.
\par
2.8. Study outcomes
\par
The main outcome of this study was the value of the
\par
RxPONDER trial measured in U.S. dollars. The estimated
\par
health outcomes (QALYs) gained in the model were con-
\par
verted into currency by using an incremental net benefit
\par
(INB) approach [25], where QALYs are monetized by
\par
multiplying by the willingness to pay threshold (WTP), to
\par
give a monetized health benefit value. The cost is then
\par
subtracted from the monetized health benefit to give the
\par
incremental net benefit.
\par
