\par
The INB estimates were then used in the value of research
\par
calculations described in the next section.
\par
2.9. Value of research calculations
\par
To estimate the expected value of sample information
\par
(EVSI), we followed the methodology outlined by Ades et al.
\par
[26]. This approach is based on a Bayesian framework andW.B. Wong et al. / Contemporary Clinical Trials 33 (2012) 1117--1123
\par
1121
\par
involves simulating the inputs in the model across their
\par
distributions. Survival parameters and hazard ratios were
\par
assigned log-normal distributions. Utilities and probabilities
\par
(with the exception of behavioral inputs (e.g., percent
\par
receiving chemotherapy, see below)) were assigned to beta
\par
distributions, while costs were varied under normal distri-
\par
bution assumptions. For patient behavioral inputs, we
\par
assigned a uniform distribution, as there currently is a lack
\par
of information (as discussed above) regarding their likely
\par
mean values in node-positive patients.
\par
The EVSI calculation takes into account gathering new
\par
information for all the parameters in the model and estimates
\par
the value of a trial of a specific sample size. We additionally
\par
assessed the expected value of sample parameter informa-
\par
tion (EVSPI), which estimates the value of a trial of a specific
\par
sample size which will collect information only on particular
\par
parameters in the model.
\par
The original protocol for RxPONDER aimed to collect
\par
information on survival parameters; however after feedback
\par
from external stakeholders, the protocol was modified to
\par
include collection of data on other parameters including
\par
utilities, costs and patient preferences. Therefore, because the
\par
stakeholder informed RxPONDER trial will collect informa-
\par
tion on all parameters in the model, the EVSI calculation
\par
represents the total value of the RxPONDER trial. On the
\par
other hand, the EVSPI calculations represent specific compo-
\par
nents of the RxPONDER trial, including the original protocol
\par
to collect data only on survival parameters, as well as
\par
parameters collected as a result of stakeholder feedback
\par
including utilities, costs and patient preferences.
\par
The value-of-research analysis results were projected
\par
based on an individual level estimate extrapolated to the
\par
U.S. population over a 10-year time frame using the
\par
Surveillance, Epidemiology and End Results (SEER) database
\par
[27] and then applying to U.S. census data [28]. We estimated
\par
results for three different potential societal willingness-to-
\par
pay (for a QALY) thresholds (\$100,000, \$150,000 and
\par
\$200,000). The thresholds represent how much society
\par
might be willing to pay for a gain in QALYs, with the
\par
\$100,000 threshold being the most commonly cited for most
\par
disease states. Some have argued that a higher threshold
\par
such as \$150,000 or \$200,000 may not be unreasonable in
\par
oncology [29,30]. At least 1 million simulations were run in
\par
the EVSI analyses to ensure convergence of results.
