4. Discussion
\par
We evaluated the potential value of research directed at
\par
understanding the benefits and harms of the 21-gene profile
\par
in women with 1--3 positive lymph nodes compared to
\par
current therapy, and the societal value of research of the
\par
RxPONDER trial. Our results indicate that research on the use
\par
of the 21-gene profile in this patient population is likely to
\par
provide high value to society. Specifically, the value of the
\par
RxPONDER trial-- including clinical, patient, and economic
\par
impacts-- ranged from \$450 million to \$1.05 billion. This
\par
represents a projected return on the investment (ROI) of 17
\par
to 39 times the NIH trial cost, suggesting the study is a good
\par
investment in research resources. These findings were driven
\par
by 1) the high level of uncertainty in outcomes based on
\par
current evidence, 2) the high incidence of breast cancer, and
\par
3) the severity of clinical and economic outcomes in lymph
\par
node-positive disease. Altogether, these results confirm the
\par
aims and objectives of the RxPONDER study which include
\par
collecting information on clinical, patient and economic
\par
outcomes associated with the 21-gene assay.
\par
The main objective of RxPONDER-- to collect information
\par
on clinical (survival) outcomes-- is supported by our analysis,
\par
which indicates collecting information on survival parame-
\par
ters has the largest value relative to other parameters in the
\par
model. Furthermore, our analysis indicates that modifications
\par
to the study design based on external stakeholder input
\par
added \$50 million to \$100 million to the value of the study.
\par
Interestingly, the added research objectives of the trial based
\par
on stakeholder input do not provide much value individually
\par
(relative to the overall trial), indicating that these parameters
\par
alone are not sufficient to change patient care decisions;
\par
however, when combined with information on survival
\par
outcomes they can provide additional information to influ-
\par
ence patient care decisions. Additionally, the value of
\par
research increases as the assumed societal willingness to
\par
pay for a QALY is increased. This is due to the driver of the
\par
value of research, survival, influencing patient outcomes
\par
(QALYs) greater than costs, and by definition patient out-
\par
comes are more relevant at higher willingness to pay
\par
thresholds. This finding implies that CER in areas of greater
\par
importance to stakeholders-- where there may be a higher
\par
willingness to pay for benefits-- may be more valuable.
\par
The role of VOI in shaping research prioritization is a
\par
developing area of research. In this case, we performed the
\par
VOI of RxPONDER after the design of RxPONDER was
\par
determined through the involvement of multiple stake-
\par
holders. This study was conducted to educate stakeholders
\par
on the concept of VOI and to evaluate the potential place of
\par
VOI in the research prioritization process. In the future, it
\par
would be ideal to use VOI to assist stakeholders throughout
\par
their decision making process, with the potential steps as
\par
follows: 1) seek initial feedback on study design from
\par
stakeholders 2) perform VOI analysis based on stakeholder
\par
feedback, and 3) present VOI analysis to stakeholders to
\par
determine final study design. Additionally, there is a
\par
potential role for VOI analyses at large public funding
\par
agencies to assess the return on investment for larger studies.
\par
Whether such analyses would be conducted by study
\par
investigators or independently is one of the important issues
\par
that need to be addressed, as discussed at a recent NHLBI
\par
workshop on the topic [http://www.nhlbi.nih.gov/meetings/
\par
workshops/info-modeling.htm].
\par
We did not conduct an analysis of the investment across
\par
multiple disease areas and technologies; given limited funds
\par
for federally supported clinical research, there could be other
\par
trials that would provide a greater ROI. Additionally, because
\par
VOI analyses rarely have been used to date in the United
\par
States to prioritize research, it is difficult to predict how these
\par
results would influence decision making (i.e., are the values
\par
large enough to prioritize this research?). However, in a
\par
stakeholder-driven prioritization exercise, we found differ-
\par
ences in EVSI/EVSPI estimates of 1 to 2 orders of magnitude
\par
across research areas influenced investment priorities in
\par
cancer genomics (Carlson J, unpublished results). Thus, we
\par
expect that VOI analyses may be useful for identifying studies
\par
that provide either particularly high or low value, but will be
\par
less useful for differentiating between studies with relatively
\par
similar VOI. In this analysis, the absolute value of the
\par
RxPONDER trial was large, thus from a societal perspective,
\par
there is a strong case that this is a good investment.
\par
Our findings on the relative importance of further
\par
research on clinical outcomes is in agreement with a recent
\par
study by Hall et al. [31]. In this study, they performed an
\par
expected value of perfect information (EVPI) analysis, and
\par
found that research on recurrence rates was at least 10 times
\par
more important compared with other types of evidence.
\par
However, Hall and colleagues calculated a hypothetical upper
\par
bound estimate (EVPI) [31], and did not account for the
\par
specific study design and sample size of RxPONDER.
\par
Our analysis has a number of limitations. First, our study was
\par
informed by limited information on overall survival and a lack
\par
of information on the decisions that women with lymph-node
\par
positive disease will make regarding adjuvant chemotherapy,
\par
even if they are in a low risk group. In regards to overall
\par
survival, the benefit of chemotherapy in the low RS group may
\par
be overestimated due to the small sample sizes. In regard to
\par
patient decisions, we attempted to address this issue by using a
\par
wide range of estimates based on the choices that have been
\par
observed for women with lymph node-negative breast cancer
\par
[20--23]. The specific chemotherapy regimen used in SWOG-8814 (cyclophosphamide/doxorubicin/fluorouracil (CAF)) is no
\par
longer a preferred treatment [2,5], and thus our analysis may
\par
underestimate the health benefits and costs of current
\par
chemotherapy regimens that will be used in the RxPONDER
\par
trial. Another potential limitation was that the difference in the
\par
overall trial population in SWOG-8814 (the data source)
\par
differed slightly from RxPONDER and our model. Despite this,
\par
we limited the influence this had on the results by analyzing a
\par
specific subgroup of SWOG-8814 trial data to populate our
\par
model. We also did not use VOI analyses to evaluate the return
\par
on investment of different sample sizes for the trial, as the
\par
sample size was driven by survival effect size estimates, and
\par
established before the VOI analyses were performed. Lastly,
\par
another limitation is that we did not include indirect costs such
\par
as the cost of lost productivity or patient time. By excluding
\par
such costs our estimates may be conservative and therefore
\par
including the indirect costs may increase the value of research if
\par
uncertainty in these estimates were resolved by additional
\par
research.
\par
In conclusion, our findings indicate that comparative
\par
effectiveness research on the use of the 21-gene profile in
\par
women with lymph node-positive breast cancer is of relatively
\par
large importance. Because of the significant uncertainty and
\par
subsequent value surrounding further research on clinical
\par
outcomes, the RxPONDER trial represents a valuable research
\par
investment. Comparative effectiveness research studies by
\par
necessity often will be relatively large and expensive; stake-
\par
holder engagement and assessment of the return on invest-
\par
ment should be considered to optimize and quantify their
\par
value.
