\par
Bendamustine Versus Chlorambucil for the First-Line Treatment of
\par
Chronic Lymphocytic Leukemia in England and Wales: A Cost-Utility
\par
Analysis
\par
Beth Woods, MSc1, Neil Hawkins, PhD1, William Dunlop, MEc2,*, Alison O'Toole, MB BS, FRCA, PhD, MFPM3
\par
,
\par
Steve Bramham-Jones, DipAM3
\par
1Oxford Outcomes, Oxford, UK; 2Mundipharma International Limited, Cambridge, UK; 3Napp Pharmaceuticals Limited, Cambridge, UK
\par
A B S T R A C T
\par
Objectives: To evaluate the cost-effectiveness of bendamustine com-
\par
pared with chlorambucil as first-line treatment for patients with
\par
chronic lymphocytic leukemia who would be considered unsuitable for
\par
treatment with fludarabine combination chemotherapy regimens.
\par
Methods: A semi-Markov approach was used to estimate time in each
\par
health state. The model was parameterized primarily by using data
\par
from a phase III randomized, open-label trial comparing bendamustine
\par
with chlorambucil. It captured the increased progression-free survival
\par
and improved response rates with bendamustine, and the cost and
\par
quality of life impacts of postprogression treatments. The analysis was
\par
conducted from the perspective of the National Health Service in Eng-
\par
land and Wales. A lifetime (35-year) time horizon was used. Determin-
\par
istic sensitivity analyses, probabilistic sensitivity analyses, and sub-
\par
group analyses in older patients and patients with poor performance
\par
status were carried out. Results: The estimated incremental cost-ef-
\par
fectiveness ratio was \pounds 11,960 per quality-adjusted life-year. None of the
\par
deterministic sensitivity analyses increased the incremental cost-ef-
\par
fectiveness ratio by more than \pounds 2000. Subgroup analyses showed that
\par
bendamustine remained cost-effective across different patient groups.
\par
Probabilistic sensitivity analysis showed that at the \pounds 20,000 threshold,
\par
bendamustine has a 90\% probability of being cost-effective.
\par
Conclusions: Bendamustine represents good value for first-line treat-
\par
ment of patients with chronic lymphocytic leukemia who are unsuit-
\par
able for treatment with fludarabine combination chemotherapy. The
\par
incremental cost-effectiveness ratio is below the thresholds commonly
\par
applied in England and Wales (\pounds 20,000 --\pounds 30,000 per quality-adjusted
\par
life-year).
\par
Keywords: bendamustine, chlorambucil, chronic lymphocytic leuk-
\par
emia, cost-effectiveness, cost-utility, QALY.
