The study by Knauf et al. [6] established progression status
\par
every 3 months; data for this end point are therefore interval
\par
censored, because we only know the time interval during which
\par
an event occurred, not the exact date of the event. In line with
\par
the clinical analysis, the cost-effectiveness modeling did not
\par
take account of the interval-censored nature of the data and
\par
instead used the date of observed progression as the event date.
\par
This approach biases the median PFS upward, although the dif-
\par
ference between treatment groups should not be systematically
\par
biased [34]. Again, it is not expected that this would have sub-
\par
stantively impacted the ICER. Further research to establish the
\par
impact of interval censoring on cost-effectiveness analysis gen-
\par
erally, however, is warranted.
