\par
Validation of a biases model in
\par
strategic security decision
\par
making
\par
Michael Workman
\par
Nathan M. Bisk College of Business, Florida Institute of Technology,
\par
Melbourne, Florida, USA and
\par
Security Policy Institute, Florida Institute of Technology,
\par
Melbourne, Florida, USA
\par
Abstract
\par
Purpose -- Funding agencies such as the Office of Naval Research, Department of Homeland
\par
Security, and others, have reduced funding for non-tactical operations. Simultaneously, organizations
\par
are squeezing their overhead budgets (where security initiatives fall) and are focusing more on revenue
\par
generation given current economic climates. Thus, in both governmental sectors and in commercial
\par
settings, there are reasons to believe that strategic security initiatives are being sacrificed, and those
\par
that survive must be compelling. To assist organizational leaders with these difficult choices, it is
\par
critical to understand biases that affect decisions about strategic security initiatives. The purpose of
\par
this paper is to validate and empirically test the predictability of a theoretical model, from which
\par
implications can be made for research and practice.
\par
Design/methodology/approach -- Using behavioral decision theory, a randomized longitudinal
\par
study was conducted over three years with a multinational corporation with headquarter-offices in the
\par
UK and the USA, and regional offices in India, Germany and France. From these data, a model was
\par
developed and tested for fit with a confirmatory factor analysis and its predictive ability was tested
\par
using structured equation modeling.
\par
Findings -- It was found that risk aversion, overconfidence, adjustment of cognitive anchors, and
\par
expected utility biases affected whether managers and other stakeholders continued or terminated
\par
strategic security initiatives.
\par
Originality/value -- Prematurely terminating or over commitment to a strategic initiative can be
\par
costly if not significantly damaging to an organization or government military or intelligence agency.
\par
Understanding how biases factor into these decisions can help strategic initiative decision makers
\par
improve their decisions and assist them in recognizing normative rules or optimal (straddle point)
\par
solutions.
\par
Keywords State security, Decision making, Decision support systems, Management strategy,
\par
Security and decision biases, Strategic security decisions, Security initiative theoretical model validation
\par
Paper type Research paper
\par
Information Management \& Computer
\par
Security
\par
Vol. 20 No. 2, 2012
\par
pp. 52-70
\par
q Emerald Group Publishing Limited
\par
0968-5227
\par
DOI 10.1108/09685221211235599
\par
1. Introduction
\par
By far, the vast majority of the security literature has concentrated on tactical and
\par
operational levels, for example, using criteria and standards associated with risk
\par
assessments and risk management (Monfelt et al., 2011). While some literature has
\par
touched on various strategic security aspects (Sommestad et al., 2011), they have tended
\par
to address situational factors and have not dealt with biases that may influences these
\par
factors. Other literature has touched on biases, for example, using Bayesian statistical
\par
decision theory (Shirtz and Elovici, 2011), but again, these have mainly focused on
\par
tactical and operational security elements. Our goal in this manuscript was to highlightthe roles that biases play in strategic security initiatives. In other words, the purpose of
\par
our study was to investigate how biases (across organizational positions) may affect
\par
strategic security decision making.
\par
This is important because the well-known research-practice curve shows that those
\par
issues researched in academic and corporate applied research and development (R\&D)
\par
institutes lead to what practitioners implement, therefore, research into security
\par
initiatives is important, particularly as the research applies to long-term horizons. For
\par
example, security research grant proposal seekers from agencies such as the US
\par
Department of Defence (DARPA) have been increasingly met with rejections stating that
\par
non-tactical research funding has been cut, and that any submissions for strategic
\par
initiatives must be extremely compelling (Dean, 2010). Concomitantly, budgets for
\par
strategic security initiatives have been cut in many commercial organizations from
\par
around 8 per cent (Bartels, 2006) down to about 2 or 3 per cent (Shim and McLendon,
\par
2011). Therefore, the strategic security initiatives in which organizations invest must be
\par
carefully considered and justified; and this most likely means to work from a validated
\par
theoretical model from which to derive a well justified security solution -- over time.
\par
Managers and other decision makers typically have at their disposals
\par
decision support systems that can generate probabilistic estimates and provide
\par
recommendations, yet sometimes people choose intuition over information and evidence
\par
provided by these technologies (Workman, 2005). The body of strategic decision
\par
research has put primacy on normative utility theories (von Neumann and Morgenstern,
\par
1947) and rational decision-making models (Baron, 1988) such as using Bayesian
\par
probability networks, and on economic factors such as estimations of returns on
\par
investments and payback periods (Wijnberg et al., 2002), but the importance to our
\par
manuscript in making these distinctions is to highlight the key roles that non-rational
\par
assessments play in decisions about strategic security projects (Larrick et al., 1990,
\par
1993). Consider for instance prospect theory (Thibaut and Kelley, 2005), which was
\par
illustrated in the depiction by one charged felon in a confession against another in a
\par
bargaining that places a competitive mindset on a premium of personal gain, stated as:
\par
``I'd rather we both hang than you get off easier than me.''
\par
The use of the term non-rational decision making is not derogatory nor does it imply
\par
irrational or mean necessarily incorrect; but rather the term refers to decisions that are
\par
not based on scientific or structured decision processes, and are sometimes referred to
\par
as na\i{}
\par
\textasciidieresis ve theories (Bjorklund, 1995; Wegener and Petty, 1997). An example of this was
\par
illustrated with a survey by Business Objects (2004) in which 43 per cent of the
\par
respondent managers said they made decisions based on ``gut feeling'' at least
\par
50 per cent of the time. Na\i{} \textasciidieresis ve theories are theories all people have about the workings
\par
of activities and events without operationalizing and testing these theories and they
\par
rely on their perceptions and experiences for judgments (Kahneman et al., 1982).
\par
Given the importance of strategic initiatives in general to organization survival over
\par
time (Jarzabkowski, 2008; Raisch and Birkinshaw, 2008), we were interested in the
\par
question: ``Does bias influence continuing or quitting a strategic security initiative?''
\par
While rational and utility models have been extensively studied in decision making --
\par
non-rational (heuristic) empirical research has been sparse in the literature relative to
\par
real-world business decisions such as those related continuing or terminating strategic
\par
projects. The few studies that have been conducted into real-world business problems
