\par
theoretical underpinning; from this, we develop our hypotheses. We then explain our
\par
research method, followed by the results from of our analyses. Our manuscript
\par
concludes with a discussion of our findings. Our purpose here is to create and validate
\par
a model that can be used in later research for consideration in strategic initiative
\par
proposals and project selection activities.
\par
2. Theory and model propositions
\par
2.1 Strategic projects and decisions -- broad generalized contexts
\par
The concept of strategy has little consensus in the literature about what it means
\par
(Jarzabkowski, 2008; Raisch and Birkinshaw, 2008). To some, it a process that can be
\par
engineered (Mintzberg, 1994) while to others it is the characteristic of future visioning
\par
(Denton and Wisdom, 1992), and for others it is exploiting core competencies (Hamel
\par
and Prahalad, 1993). Regardless, the body of literature on strategy divides along the
\par
lines in a continuum where at one polar end consists of processes that are scientific,
\par
planned or structured (Semler, 1997) and on the other end of the spectrum are forms of
\par
artistry, imagination, and intuition (Stacy, 1992). Thus, there are different perspectives
\par
about strategy, and by extension, about strategic projects.
\par
For our purposes, a strategic project is defined as a development or service that:
\par
. is used to gain competitive advantage (Porter, 1979, 1996); and
\par
. requires ``long-term'' commitment in time and resources to produce or provide
\par
(Simon and Houghton, 2003), and where the outcomes are computationally
\par
equivocal (Jarzabkowski, 2008).
\par
Strategic projects therefore differ from tactics and operations in that the former is a blend
\par
of various analyses, behavioural techniques, and the use of power and organizational
\par
politics to bring about broadly conceived outcomes (Quinn, 1992), whereas tactics are
\par
managerial actions that enact a strategy (Greer, 1995), and operations are the daily
\par
routines that result from management actions (Synnott, 1987). As such, realizing a
\par
strategy comes by way of a strategic product or service projects (Burgelman and
\par
Grove, 1996).
\par
This is important because especially during difficult economic times, strategic
\par
projects may be crucial to the long-term survival of a firm (Jarzabkowski, 2008), but
\par
because financial resources might be scarce during difficult economic times, there is a
\par
strong temptation to ``sacrifice'' them (Ardichvili et al., 1998; Baldwin et al., 2009).
\par
This is especially the case given that an escalation of a strategic move may
\par
escalate an opponent's competitive countermove giving rise to further costs
\par
(Derfus et al., 2008).
\par
2.2 Normative rules versus biases
\par
Normative rules might also be called normalized rules, because they are rules of thumb
\par
that are:. systematic;
\par
. and given two options, one is more optimized; and
\par
. they are based on economic and not emotional (biased) principles.
\par
Examples of normative versus biased decisions include where studies (Kahneman and
\par
Tversky, 1979) show that some people will spend more money to drive across town
\par
(spending more in gas money and auto wear) to save a little money on an item they wanted
\par
because it was ``on sale'' whereas the normative rule would be to drive less and save more
\par
on a ``non-sale'' item. In another study, the majority of people showed that when
\par
participants were asked if they preferred a job where they earned \$30,000 the first year,
\par
\$40,000 the second, and \$50,000 the third, or whether they preferred a job that paid \$60,000
\par
the first year, then \$50,000, then \$40,000, the majority of participants picked the first job
\par
option even though this yielded less money over three years (Loewenstein and Prelc, 1993).
\par
Information technologies such as decision support systems (especially in the
\par
security prediction arena) uses statistics and rational theorems such as min./max.,
\par
Nash equilibrium, or stochastic algorithms to suggest solutions that reduces to a
\par
normative rule, given a scenario and a set of constraints. Yet a study by Workman
\par
(2005), for example, showed that some people often ignore the recommendations of
\par
decision support systems even when the systems are shown to be effective in reducing
\par
the number of human-induced errors if their suggestions are followed. These domain
\par
general cognitive ``short-cuts'' apply across disciplines. Our objective in our study was
\par
to determine an explanatory model of how this applies to strategic security initiatives.
\par
2.3 Propositions
\par
Behavioural decision theory (Kahneman and Tversky, 1979; Poulton, 1994) is an
\par
omnibus theory that acknowledges heuristics in decision making (Saunders and
\par
Carpenter, 2003). Examples of heuristics show bias, such as the non-rational (subjective)
\par
weights that are given in decision matrices in social exchange theory, as was illustrated
\par
by Thibaut and Kelley (2005) in capturing the mindset that ``I'd rather we both hang than
\par
you get off easier than me'' as mentioned earlier. It also includes the false assumption
\par
that past success makes a good predictor of future success (Simon and Houghton, 2003).
\par
Heuristic bias also explains non-rational financial escalations and over commitments
\par
regardless of ``sunk costs'' commonly called pouring good money after bad, or believing
\par
in ``shooting streaks'' in the game of basketball (Gilovich et al., 1985).
\par
The key problem with heuristic bias is that people may draw wrong conclusions
\par
even in the face of disconfirming evidence (Workman, 2005) because heuristics rely on
\par
a rule of thumb as opposed to a formally specified, programmable rule. This form of
\par
educated guessing is prone to distortions, inaccuracies, and omissions that can lead
\par
people to choose a non-rational decision over a more rational one (Pollatsek et al., 1984).
\par
Risk acceptance (or tolerance) suggests that people adjust their behaviour according
\par
to the amount of risk they are willing to accept regarding some action they take versus
\par
the rewards they could receive from accomplishing the goals they seek (Tsohou et al.,
\par
2006; Pechmann et al., 2003). That is, the adjustments people make in their actions are
\par
based on the degree of gain or reward compared to costs or losses they perceive to be
\par
associated with a phenomenon or event of interest (Grothmann and Reusswig, 2006;
\par
Lauriola et al., 2007). Thus, people tend to adjust their behaviour in response to the
\par
extent of the risk versus reward they are willing to accept (Pyszczynski et al., 1997).
\par
