\par
Risk acceptance is contextually malleable and related to other heuristics people use
\par
in their decisions and actions. For instance, drawing upon traffic safety studies, there is
\par
evidence that the use of safety features such as car seat belts, antilock braking systems,
\par
and bicycle helmets can give some people a false sense of security causing them to take
\par
greater risks. One example is that people use seatbelts less when they are driving close
\par
to home (lower perceived danger) or at slower speeds (lower perceived damage) (Dorn
\par
and Brown, 2003).
\par
Nevertheless, there are individual differences in risk acceptance (Lauriola et al.,
\par
2007). For example, some people exhibit more risky decision making for a perceived
\par
valued benefit compared to others who prefer safer bets but with lower returns
\par
(Bandura, 1991). Consequently, people have a threshold level of risk they will accept,
\par
tolerate, prefer, desire, or choose; and this ``target level'' of risk is the level they will
\par
accept before calling it quits (Wilde, 2001). Since strategic security initiatives fit both
\par
within the broader context of strategic initiatives and as an overhead cost of doing
\par
business, decisions about continuing or terminating strategic security initiatives will,
\par
in theory, be affected by risk acceptance. Thus, our P1 is:
\par
P1. People who have higher risk acceptance (lower risk adversity) will be more
\par
likely to continue strategic security initiatives than those who have lower risk
\par
acceptance.
\par
Overconfidence represents the tendency to create an overoptimistic estimation of a
\par
future event (Kahneman and Tversky, 1972). Paradoxically, overconfidence can
\par
manifest when people receive little or ambiguous information or feedback (Simon and
\par
Houghton, 2003). This is because a contributor to overconfidence can be a hindsight bias
\par
(Lant and Hewlin, 2002), which is a contemporaneous reproduction of past events that
\par
are reframed in a more positive light than is objectively defensible (Poulton, 1994). Thus,
\par
based on reframed past experience, people might be inclined to enhance their
\par
assessments of the probability of future success for similar events (Lauriola et al., 2007).
\par
Note that this differs from representative and simulation heuristics in the sense that
\par
these latter heuristics are based on apprehensions and cognitions involving working
\par
(short-term) memory, whereas hindsight draws from recollections of past events (from
\par
long-term memory), and uses those reconstructed and distorted memories (Tversky and
\par
Tuchin, 1989) to project onto future similar events. Moreover, when an uncertain outcome
\par
about a future event is forecast, the referent comparison point from a reconstruction of a
\par
similar event can translate into an expectation of an outcome of that future event within
\par
a reduced range of alternatives; that is, the range of desirable alternatives from which to
\par
choose can be concomitantly narrowed according to the overconfidence heuristic in a
\par
contraction bias or regression effect (Poulton, 1994). This can lead to the perception of
\par
having fewer acceptable options available and thus one may take a more aggressive and
\par
intransigent position committing to an initiative where he or she may have underestimated
\par
the difficulty of achieving a successful outcome (Tversky and Shafir, 1992).
\par
As evidence, overconfidence has been associated with taking greater risk and
\par
protracted continuance commitment (Meyer et al., 1993) in terms of strategic product
\par
introductions (Simon and Houghton, 2003). In its simplest terms, the theoretical
\par
proposition is that people consider their future successes based on past successful
\par
experiences, which is a fallacious assumption (Lauriola et al., 2007). Given the nature of
\par
strategic security initiatives in terms of prediction about whether to continue to invest(an escalation of commitment) in a strategic security initiative in spite of evidence of
\par
failure (or disconfirming evidence about success) is higher for those who have been
\par
successful at past similar projects than those who have not been as successful.
\par
Therefore, our formal proposition regarding strategic initiatives is:
\par
P2. People who are overconfident will be more likely to continue strategic security
\par
initiatives than those who are not overconfident.
\par
In the estimation of a future event, people revise their estimates when new information is
\par
acquired, which increases or decreases their assessed probability of an event such as in
\par
the ``estimation of the probability that rival company is developing a new or improved
\par
product'' (Poulton, 1994, p. 192). Anchoring is the use of a subjective reference point for
\par
making judgments, and this anchor is typically influenced by an initial given estimate
\par
or magnitude, and it is affected by how information is framed (De Neys et al., 2008). If, for
\par
example, people are given a low number and then are subsequently asked for estimate on
\par
a task, they tend to choose a lower estimate compared to people who are given a higher
\par
initial number (Blankenship et al., 2008; Thorsteinson et al., 2008). An interesting
\par
example was illustrated by Strack and Mussweiler (1997) in which after considering
\par
anchors of 1215 or 1992 they asked subjects to estimate the year that Einstein first
\par
visited the USA. These implausible anchors produced anchoring effects just as large as
\par
more plausible anchors such as 1905 or 1939.
\par
Anchoring adjustments create conditions where people may overestimate or
\par
underestimate a risk or the probability of success (Mussweiler and Englich, 2005). As an
\par
example, when people make a medical prognosis it tends to be more positive when
\par
framed in terms of survival rates rather than mortality rates, even when the probabilities
\par
of the outcomes are identical (Reisberg, 1997). The initial anchor influences future
\par
assessments about the probability of failure or success (Tversky and Kahneman, 1983).
\par
Sometimes, this can cause problems in planning and developing strategic security
\par
initiatives because when the probability of success is presumed to be high for each
\par
component in the process, there is an assumption that the overall probability of success
\par
is therefore high. Conversely, in other cases, people may underestimate risks because
\par
they underestimate the probability of a failure in one or more critical components which
\par
can lead to unwarranted optimism about the probability of the success of a strategic
\par
initiative (Thorsteinson et al., 2008). Since research has shown that people's judgments
\par
tend to assimilate to irrelevant anchors (Oppenheimer et al., 2008) and because there is
\par
enthusiasm about strategic initiatives that are undertaken (Jarzabkowski, 2008), the
\par
anchor is set high, and since anchoring and adjustment bias can lead to unwarranted
\par
optimism about projects and an underestimation of risks (Poulton, 1994); we therefore
\par
propose that:
\par
P3. People who rely on high initial anchors for decisions about strategic security
\par
initiatives will be more likely to continue strategic initiatives than those who
\par
do not rely on anchors.
\par
In rational decision-making models such as found in Bayesian decision theory there is
\par
a concept of a utility of information. In a decision using utility models, the rational
\par
model will assert that if we double the stakes of a wager or a prediction, we also double
\par
the value of information concerning those stakes (Camerer, 2003). On the other hand,
\par
the expected utility in forecasting is an extrapolation, and extrapolation produces
