\par
fallacious reasoning (Kelley, 1998), for example, by being flawed with a confirmation
\par
bias where people seek confirming but not falsifying evidence (Buehler et al., 1994).
\par
Moreover, as described in prospect theory (Kahneman and Tversky, 1979), with this
\par
heuristic, people ignore the normative rule to look for objective evidence and make
\par
choices based on preferences for high presumed probabilities of gain but low
\par
probabilities of loss; however, with very low probabilities, the preferences are reversed
\par
(Poulton, 1994). As an illustration, ``Most people buy a lottery ticket that offers a very
\par
low probability gain [but] they buy insurance to avoid a very low probability loss''
\par
(Poulton, 1994, p. 194). Consequently, in a response contraction bias, large gains and
\par
losses are undervalued when compared with small gains or losses, and where losses
\par
matter more than gains (Kahneman and Tversky, 1979).
\par
Corporations do not have unlimited funds, and decision makers may not behave in
\par
ways that from a purely economic perspective might be regarded as rational (Reisberg,
\par
1997). The pressures to produce high returns with a focus on short-term performance can
\par
lead to avoiding delivering bad news, especially after a significant commitment of
\par
resources have already been made (Reisberg, 1997). Moreover, only a relatively small
\par
percentage of strategic initiatives achieve the desired end state (Buehler et al., 1994;
\par
Green et al., 2003; Mintzberg, 1994; Saunders and Carpenter, 2003; Simon and Houghton,
\par
2003; Tanriverdi and Lee, 2008). When it comes to strategic security initiatives then,
\par
some people may ignore the normative rule and seek confirming evidence of their
\par
decision to pursue a strategic initiative, whereas people who rely more on the normative
\par
utility rule will be less likely to continue a strategic initiative in the face of disconfirming
\par
evidence (Workman, 2005). Given the aversion toward large losses (typical in strategic
\par
initiatives that are developed over a period of time), we therefore propose that:
\par
P4. People who rely on expected utility for decisions about security strategic
\par
initiatives will be more likely to continue strategic security initiatives than
\par
those who do not.
