3. Method
\par
3.1 Participants
\par
We were under strict non-disclosure agreements regarding what we were allowed to
\par
divulge about our study participants and the organizational entity. In this section, we
\par
provide what was allowable by our agreement without violating our non-disclosure
\par
and other agreements. To ensure a consistent organizational context and also address
\par
the problem of changes over time as well as to be able to capture ``strategic decisions''
\par
we conducted a longitudinal study over three years with a multinational corporation
\par
participant, with head headquarter-offices in the UK and the USA, with regional offices
\par
in France, India, Ireland, Sweden, Belgium, and Germany, who were engaged in a wide
\par
variety of security technology and product initiatives.
\par
The importance of the selection of our participant was that they were involved in
\par
developing ``stochastic'' and ``semantic'' inference technologies and communications
\par
``futureware'' such as secure next generation wireless communications hardware and
\par
software, applications, and micro-sensors for assessing internal conditions of containers
\par
along with micro-transmitters and communications network software for distributing
\par
information to a control room -- all considered strategic security technologies. As a result,
\par
this company had many strategic initiatives in various planning stages or underway
\par
and ongoing at any given time, and because of the nature of their work, the conceptof a strategic initiative was well-defined. At this organization, members of a research
\par
and development committee determined the future of strategic initiatives.
\par
Because the company was a well-known international entity, to protect it from
\par
exposure we agreed to keep our descriptions of the organization and members generic;
\par
however, each member of the committee had at their disposal decision support systems,
\par
enterprise planning and scheduling systems, and financial forecasting technologies.
\par
These members included senior project managers, selected marketing personnel,
\par
selected engineers, and representative finance personnel who cast ``votes'' regarding the
\par
continuance or termination of strategic initiatives. All votes were equal. Where there
\par
were inconclusive or indecisive cases (or those decisions that were appealed by a
\par
due process), an executive team comprised of corporate officers would adjudicate the
\par
decision. A total of 283 participants began the study, but 12 left the company and were
\par
dropped, leaving 271 who participated.
\par
Since we were interested in knowing how cognitive biases affect strategic security
\par
decisions, and generalize this knowledge across organizations and populations, we
\par
aggregated the votes across strategic initiatives for use in parametric statistical
\par
analyses. Of 26 strategic initiatives that went past a proof of concept stage, 11 (41 per cent)
\par
were approved, while 15 (59 per cent) were terminated at some point in the lifecycle.
\par
The organization utilized an Agile process, hence voting members for a strategic
\par
initiative were a cross-sections of stakeholders. Each strategic initiative had at least one
\par
member from the marketing department responsible for the product line, one engineer
\par
who worked within the product line, and one representative from the finance department
\par
who allocated budgets for the projects. The majority of the voters were line managers
\par
and senior project managers. On average, 29 per cent were managers, 19 per cent were
\par
senior project managers, 13 per cent were marketing representatives, 13 per cent were
\par
developers, 13 per cent were QA engineers, 3 per cent were from the finance department,
\par
6 per cent were ``other administrative'', and 4 per cent were ``other technical''.
\par
Data collection, manipulation checks and validations. We developed scaled items for
\par
each heuristic factor drawing from Tversky and Kahneman (1983) and Tversky and
\par
Shafir (1992). We first administered our queries and our perceptual inventory prior to
\par
our post hoc data capture and dependent variable vote assessments in a three-year
\par
longitudinal study at which time we examined the percentages of continuation and
\par
termination votes for each participant. Therefore, we were able to compare predictive
\par
bias tendencies with actual observed behaviours. After collecting the independent
\par
measures, we monitored votes cast over the three-year period yielding substantial
\par
longitudinal comparative data (Table I).
\par
For scale validation, we screened the data by using a combination of scatter plots
\par
and the examination of Mahalanobis distances (Tabachnick and Fidell, 1996), and then
\par
we conducted a factor analysis rotated with Varimax. With eigenvalues set at 1.0, all
\par
communalities were greater than 0.70 and were retained in the analysis according to
\par
Kaiser's rule (Stevens, 1992; Tabachnick and Fidell, 1996). Cross-loadings were
\par
all under 0.35 (Stevens, 1992), and multicollinearity tolerances for the components all
\par
exceeded 0.85, indicating good validity among items and their constructs in the final
\par
scales. Also, composite construct alpha reliabilities all exceeded 0.89.
\par
Once we were satisfied with the validity and reliability of our scaled data, we ran a
\par
factor analysis to test the fit of our resulting model. We used the AMOS software
\par
package for conducting the model-fit confirmatory factor analysis. The components
