5. Discussion, limitations, and conclusions
\par
Research into more general business strategic projects has received some attention in
\par
the business and decision-making literature; however, not much beyond anecdotes has
\par
been published in the body of security literature. The security literature in general
\par
propagates the need for quantitative formula such as annualized loss expectancy
\par
(ALE) even though the compositions of these formulas are substantially subjective.
\par
While certainly helpful, these quantitative financial assessments overlook both the fact
\par
that they indeed incorporate guesswork (e.g. how much is a device going to be worth in
\par
three years), as well as other important qualitative components that should also be
\par
considered, such as damage to company reputation from a security breach.
\par
A key to determining risk is a blend of quantitative (often statistical) assessments and
\par
logical evaluations, and just plain good judgment -- and managers are paid as much for
\par
their judgments as any other aspect of their job. Therefore, since countermeasures are
\par
designed to prevent a security breach and their assessed value according to the total
\par
impact, both quantitative assessments (such as ALE) and the qualitative judgments of
\par
managers (such as following normative rules) are necessary for successfully managing
\par
security. Yet subjective weights given to these formulas have suffered from ambiguity
\par
in the literature and have failed to acknowledge the role that biases play in these
\par
decisions -- let alone that they have failed to articulate their effects. Research has been
\par
actively trying to adjust for these inaccuracies.
\par
A second issue has been that grant-funded projects have tended to be adopted in
\par
governmental practice more than those that are not funded. Moreover, well justified
\par
internal strategic proposals tend to be adopted over those that are not well justified. Over
\par
commitment to an initiative can be equally devastating to an organization. Cognitive
\par
biases and logical diffusion can therefore make or break a security technology adoption.
\par
The key point here is, though, even though logical and well justified projects do not
\par
necessarily lead to a good outcome, but those that are tend to be analyzed better and offer
