\par
Even though computational decision support and project planning systems exist for
\par
people to utilize in making determinations about the performance to objectives in a
\par
short-term project schedule. While in the short-term, people tend to rely more on these
\par
kinds of technologies, when initiatives are strategic in nature and are projected over a
\par
substantial timeframe, there are strong tendencies for people to switch to heuristics,
\par
gut feel, and naive theories for making commitment or termination decisions
\par
(Klaczynski and Narasimham, 1998). Many laboratory studies have been done to
\par
investigate the effects of using heuristics on various problems and decisions, and some
\par
field studies have examined how a given heuristic may affect various outcomes. We
\par
were interested in helping to fill a gap in the literature by looking specifically at how
\par
heuristics would impact the important security decision of whether to terminate or
\par
continue with strategic security initiatives.
\par
5.1 Limitations
\par
We encountered the problem of whether or not a strategic security initiative would
\par
succeed in its goals since there is no way to know this if a project is terminated. This
\par
remains an important unanswered question. Objective data can be obtained about
\par
successful or unsuccessful initiatives once they have been completed, but determining
\par
those outcomes is difficult if possible for terminated security initiatives. Just as
\par
important is the question of how decisions are made (i.e. the processes used) relative to
\par
whether or not to pursue or to terminate them?
\par
Next, we note that we utilized a field-study design to gain ecological validity; however,
\par
this creates a variety of limitations in terms of validating a theoretical model. First, our
\par
sample was fairly restrictive in terms of being comprised of individuals with an interest in
\par
software development issues. This is not broadly representative in relation to a
\par
cross-section of cultures, skills, and organizations. In addition, the self-report nature of our
\par
study raises the potential for common method variance. Objective measures and
\par
laboratory study would help to expand on our findings. Finally, clearly many other factors
\par
were not explained by our model since it left 66 per cent of the variance unaccounted for.
\par
We also envision that some of these factors may have interactive effects. All of these
\par
limitations present gaps and opportunities for future research to investigate.
\par
5.2 Broader conclusions
\par
Decisions during the security strategic initiative lifecycle to get to completion is often
\par
not objectively conducted, regardless of whether the decision to terminate or continue
\par
the strategic initiative was later determined to be a good one or not. Understanding
\par
how heuristics and biases function in that process are helpful to researchers and
\par
practitioners alike. While a vast body of research has studied user-centered technology
\par
designs and assessed factors such as perceptions of usefulness and ease-of-use,
\par
much more research is needed into the actual effectiveness of technology on solving
\par
business problems; for example, how much do they actually improve decisions?
\par
To illustrate, the use of stochastic modelling technology has been helpful in creating
\par
scenarios to stimulate imagination and help eliminate bias regarding terror attack
\par
episodes (Workman, 2005), but they also have the tendency to be misleading by
\par
exacerbating the conjunction fallacy, that is, by combining plausible events into
