a scenario that may be perceived as more probable than they are (Poulton, 1994).
\par
Critical then is that organizational developers and managers teach normative rules
\par
relative to heuristic (subjective) decisions, which previous have been based purely on
\par
objective economic models. While these are models (most based on the Nash
\par
equilibrium theorem) are helpful, they do not account for human idiosyncratic
\par
behaviours -- hence the vast number of government solicitations to infused human
\par
motives and behaviours into the modelling and reasoning facilities currently available.
\par
5.3 Implications for practice
\par
A critical point to note in regard to treatments used by security managers and
\par
organizational developers for facilitating strategic security project development is that
\par
biases may significantly influence whether decision makers continue or terminate
\par
strategic security initiatives. Biases may lead decision makers to continue to invest in
\par
projects that have a low potential for success, or prematurely abort projects that have a
\par
high likelihood of succeeding over time.
\par
Risk assessments and criteria used such as COBIT and ISO 27001 have been
\par
important in helping security managers contemplate and prepare for future incidents.
\par
We contribute to this dialog by suggesting how biases may affect these assessments and
\par
suggest adding criteria and training to help security managers become aware of biases
\par
and learn techniques for implementing normative rules. In other words, security
\par
managers and organizational developers need to design treatments and protocols to
\par
address these biases. Moreover, to reduce fallacious reasoning about strategic decisions,
\par
technologies must continue to advance in terms of their effectiveness in making
\par
predictions and helping human consumers of the information to make better decisions.
\par
Furthermore, while technologies exist that produce Bayesian belief networks (such
\par
as Netica) and other reasoning and prediction techniques, people still often rely on ``gut
\par
feeling'' and use heuristics for a substantial portion of their strategic decision making
\par
as evidenced by our study. For example, people who use decision support systems may
\par
instead choose to ignore the information and rely instead on their own na\i{} \textasciidieresis ve theories
\par
about causes and effects, and prefer their own solutions to those produced by the
\par
system (Workman, 2005). A na\i{} \textasciidieresis ve theory is a well-organized system of explanatory
\par
beliefs based on reasoning from everyday experience or common sense understanding
\par
(Wellman and Gelman, 1992). Nevertheless, because na\i{} \textasciidieresis ve theory is derived from one's
\par
belief system, it is subject to biases that may interfere with judgments and
\par
decision-making behaviour (Morris and Venkatesh, 2000).
\par
Such interference can result in biases consistent with beliefs. That is:
\par
Belief-consistent hypotheses are constructed with confirmation rather than falsification as the
\par
primary goal; consequently, belief-relevant evidence is interpreted, dismissed, or
\par
reinterpreted as a function of consistency of that evidence with the individual's original
\par
theory (Klaczynski and Narasimham, 1998, p. 175).
\par
As an example, studies (Klaczynski and Narasimham, 1998; Stanovich and West, 1999)
\par
have shown that hypothetical arguments are classified as correct or incorrect as a
\par
function of the consistency between the arguments' conclusions and the participants'
\par
beliefs. Because these biases may create fallibility in judgments, they may be less
\par
reliable in terms of solving complex problems that are targeted by a given technology.
\par
As the use of the technology and its information increases, the benefits to theorganization should increase, such as improving decisions and reducing the number of
\par
decision errors (Gregor and Benbasat, 1999).
