5.4 Implications for research
\par
Using heuristics in behavioural decision theory, we found support for influences from
\par
high risk tolerance in strategic initiative continuance commitment. Likewise, we found
\par
that overconfidence, and anchoring adjustment predicted continuance decisions, and
\par
we found that the expected utility factored into these decisions as well. Since we
\par
assume that people will continue to rely on heuristics to some extent in their decision
\par
making about strategic initiatives, some human decision factors issues should also be
\par
addressed. For instance, in dealing with overconfidence, receiving more concrete
\par
feedback can mitigate the tendency, even though some people still utilize heuristics
\par
regardless of their knowledge of reasoning, statistics, and logic (Poulton, 1994).
\par
Therefore, continued research is needed into developing better interventions for
\par
using heuristics in practice. Some suggestions have included gathering multiple
\par
perspectives and subjective points of view about strategic initiatives, and then writing
\par
justifications to expose logic flaws and fallacies, and bringing in objective outside
\par
consultants into this decision-making process may also be beneficial. Ultimately, even
\par
though bias has been well documented in decisions, managers continue to fall prey? We
\par
suggest that new training and development interventions should be developed based on
\par
the research so that important strategic security benefits are not lost among the chatter.
\par
In conclusion, our goal with this manuscript has been to provide a validated model
\par
to work from in future research, and to provide some guidance in terms of awareness
\par
and instruction on normative decision making versus biased judgments in practice.
\par
Our study contributes to the literature in two key areas:
\par
(1) in the creation and validation of a biases model; and
\par
(2) its relationship to strategic security initiatives.
\par
Its main function is to provide a model to help security managers and organizational
\par
developers in creating processes for risk assessments and risk management through
\par
incorporating how biases influence components such as ALE and other systematic
\par
formula, but perhaps its greater value is in showing how biases impact strategic security
\par
decisions to inform training and development programs.
