\par
applied sciences, such as clinical epidemiology and health eco-
\par
nomics. More recently, some networks have been established to
\par
facilitate collaboration and cooperation among HTA research-
\par
ers and agencies, both regionally (e.g., the European Network
\par
for Health Technology Assessment) [6] and internationally (e.g.,
\par
the International Network of Agencies for Health Technology
\par
Assessment [7]). Health Technology Assessment International pro-
\par
vides another important platform where dialog and interaction
\par
among all HTA stakeholders take place [8].
\par
Canada was one of the first pioneers in using HTA as a research
\par
tool in supporting evidence-based decision-making. Within a
\par
rather favorable and friendly environment, this community has
\par
grown rapidly, with more than 25 organizations currently actively
\par
conducting HTAs across the country (for a complete list, please
\par
refer to Battista et al. [9]). The objective of this article is primarily
\par
to describe the current application of HTA in Canada. It begins
\par
with a brief introduction to Canada's healthcare system, followed
\par
by an overview of HTA at different levels. This article will focus
\par
on some federal and Ontario initiatives on HTAs in which the
\par
authors have extensive knowledge and experience to illustrate
\par
how academic researchers conduct HTA in collaboration with
\par
decision makers.
\par
Canada's healthcare system
\par
It is well known that HTA is used as a tool to support evidence-
\par
based decision-making with the aim of improving healthcare
\par
efficiency. Therefore, the nature of the healthcare system in a
\par
jurisdiction might determine, at least in part, the nature and
\par
characteristics of HTA conducted in that jurisdiction. To gain
\par
an insight of HTA in Canada, it is necessary and important to
\par
first understand Canada's healthcare system.
\par
Canada has a publicly funded and decentralized healthcare
\par
system that provides all residents access to universal coverage
\par
for medically necessary hospital and physician services based on
\par
the need rather than the ability to pay [10]. The federal govern-
\par
ment contributes financially to provincial health insurance plans
\par
that meet the five basic criteria: comprehensiveness; universality;
\par
portability; accessibility; and public administration. The federal
\par
govern\- ment is also responsible for the delivery of healthcare serv-
\par
ices to selected groups (e.g., First Nations and Canadian Armed
\par
Forces), as well as for other functions, such as premarketing
\par
approval and pricing on patented drugs [10]. The ten provincial
\par
and three territorial governments are responsible for delivering
\par
most of the healthcare (i.e., hospital and physician services, and
\par
drug plans for certain populations), while other health services
\par
deemed not `medically necessary' according to the Canada Health
\par
Act (e.g., dental care) are supplemented through private insurance
\par
plans. Hospitals are financed and operated under global fund-
\par
ing by provincial governments, while most physicians are `gov-
\par
ernment contractors' for delivering care in the community [10].
\par
Decisions regarding which technologies are reimbursed by the
\par
publicly funded healthcare system largely rest with individual
\par
institutions or provincial governments. When managing and
\par
delivering healthcare, provincial and federal governments not
\par
only share common values of equity and solidarity but also face
\par
514
\par
similar pressures on how to efficiently allocate limited resources
\par
to healthcare services to meet the basic yet necessary medical
\par
standards of Canadians.
\par
The foundation of Canada's healthcare system has shaped not
\par
only the introduction of new technologies but also the establish-
\par
ment and evolution of HTA in this country. Almost all provinces
\par
have been considering, either implicitly or explicitly given their
\par
capacity and available research resources, some assessment to
\par
various extents before the uptake and diffusion of new technolo-
\par
gies. Since the first HTA agency was established in Quebec in
\par
1988, over 25 organizations have been formed to conduct HTA
\par
at either a national level (e.g., the Canadian Agency for Drugs
\par
and Technologies in Health [CADTH]), provincial level (e.g.,
\par
the Medical Advisory Secretariat [MAS] and the Ontario Health
\par
Technology Advisory Committee [OHTAC]), or institutional
\par
level (e.g., the Technology Assessment Unit at McGill University
\par
Health Center, Quebec, Canada) [11].
\par
HTA at the federal level
\par
As a national effort, the Canadian Coordinating Office for Health
\par
Technology Assessment (CCOHTA), with a mandate of review-
\par
ing clinical evidence of medical devices, was created in 1989 [12].
\par
CCOHTA was funded by and operated under the governance
\par
of federal, provincial and territorial ministries of health (exclud-
\par
ing Quebec, which has its own provincial HTA agency). Over
\par
the years, the mandate of CCOHTA was broadened, as a reflec-
\par
tion of increasing acceptance and importance of HTA, by imple-
\par
menting the Common Drug Review (CDR) in 2003, providing
\par
the Canadian Optimal Medication Prescribing and Utilization
\par
Service in 2004, adding the Health Technology Inquiry Service
\par
in 2005 (changed to Rapid Response Service in March 2011), as
\par
well as encompassing the general HTA program. CCOHTA was
\par
renamed CADTH in 2006. CADTH is currently the largest HTA
\par
producer in Canada [101].
\par
The CDR was established to ``reduce duplication, provide equal
\par
access to high-level evidence and expert advice, and contribute to
\par
the quality and sustainability of Canadian drug plans'' [101]. The
\par
CDR reviews the clinical effectiveness and cost--effectiveness of
\par
new drugs and provides recommendations to all publicly funded
\par
provincial drug plans in Canada (except Quebec). Individual
\par
provinces make final drug formulary listing and coverage deci-
\par
sions based on the CDR recommendation and their mandates,
\par
jurisdictional priorities and resources [101].
\par
Health technology assessment is a pivotal program aimed at
\par
providing impartial and high-quality evidence to inform those
\par
who make clinical and policy decisions. The collaboration
\par
between CADTH and academic institutions that can contribute
\par
expertise and experience needed for HTA is critical to achieve
\par
this objective. In 2007, the Partner in HTA was established
\par
between the Network of Excellence for the Assessment of Health
\par
Technologies (NEAHT) at McMaster University (Hamilton,
\par
Canada) and CADTH. This partnership is led by the Health
\par
Technology Assessment Program at the Programs for Assessment
\par
of Technology in Health (PATH) Research Institute (which the
\par
authors are mainly affiliated with) and supported by four other
\par
Expert Rev. Pharmacoeconomics Outcomes Res. 11(5), (2011)Health technology assessment in Canada
\par
Special Report
\par
international methodology leaders in this field, namely the Centre
\par
for Health Economics and Policy Analysis, the Evidence-Based
\par
Practice Centers, the Centre for Evaluation of Medicines and
\par
the Program in Policy Decision Making. Over the past 4 years,
\par
NEAHT has conducted and published HTAs in collaboration
\par
with CADTH. Other academic researchers across the country are
\par
also collaborating with CADTH outside of a formal agreement.
\par
The HTAs undertaken by NEAHT can be simplified into six
\par
steps (Figure 1). The first step is topic identification/refinement.
\par
Policy-relevant questions are typically identified by a CADTH
\par
advisory committee with representatives from stakeholders from
\par
individual provinces and territories. Once a topic is identified,
\par
a series of targeted searches are completed in order to identify
\par
the potential amount of literature that will have to be reviewed.
\par
This assists in determining appropriate timelines for the HTA
\par
and to clarify the research question. A protocol is then developed
\par
that will guide the answer to the proposed research question and
\par
determine how the entire project for the HTA will be conducted.
\par
Based on the information found during the topic refinement stage,
\par
an information specialist will create a search strategy to identify
\par
and systematically retrieve the required information [102]. The
\par
literature is screened and data are abstracted using standardized
\par
forms. The information gathered from the systematic literature
\par
review is assimilated into a narrative. The purpose of the narrative
\par
is to introduce and describe the main research question(s) and
\par
method\- ology prior to summarizing the three main components
\par
of the HTA report: clinical review, economic analysis and health
\par
services impact. The clinical review is a synthesis of the clinical lit-
\par
erature and may include a meta-analysis if viable data are provided
\par
through the systematic review. The economic analysis summarizes
\par
the economic literature and may also incorporate an economic
\par
model based on the information in the literature in order to pro-
\par
vide a cost--effectivness analysis on the topic. The health services
\par
impact explores issues such as population impact, budget impact
\par
analysis, planning, implementation and utilization. Any legal,
\par
regulatory and ethical implications are also summarized. The
\par
report then undergoes a series of reviews by academic research-
\par
ers and the manufacturer of the technology under evaluation.
\par
This process is quite rigorous and CADTH personnel as well as
\par
clinical experts are often involved in scrutinizing the document
\par
in order to provide comments that ensures that the evidence is
\par
clearly presented and accurate. Once the report has been final-
\par
ized and approved, it is returned to CADTH, which disseminates
\par
the information and makes it available to decision bodies across
\par
the nation. Since 2007, under the Partner in HTA agreement,
\par
NEAHT has completed HTAs on intravenous immunoglobulin
\par
for the treatment of: idiopathic thrombocytopenic purpura [13]
\par
and chronic inflammatory demyelinating polyneuropathy [14];
\par
triple therapy for moderate-to-severe chronic obstructive pulmo-
\par
nary disease [15]; anti-TNF-{$\alpha$} drugs for refractory inflammatory
\par
bowel disease [16]; and ablation procedures for rhythm control in
\par
patients with atrial fibrillation [17].
\par
More recently, the authors have acquired experience with
\par
CADTH's Rapid Response Service. On behalf of CADTH, the
\par
Health Technology Assessment Program creates reports that
\par
include brief summaries of clinical and/or economic literature.
\par
These summaries involve a less rigorous search strategy in com-
\par
parison to an HTA in order to inform a requestor of available evi-
\par
dence related to a topic of interest in a short timespan. Since 2007,
\par
reviews have been conducted by the Health Technology Inquiry
\par
Service, now known as the Rapid Response Service, regarding
\par
emergency physician-performed ultrasonography in small emer-
\par
gency departments, Bluetooth\textregistered -enabled hearing technologies,
\par
a1-proteinase inhibitor therapy for a1-antitrypsin deficiency, ven-
\par
tilation during patient transport, left atrial appendage occlusion,
\par
and optimizing the use of medical isotopes diagnostic imaging.
\par
Downloaded by [UQ Library] at 01:44 03 November 2015
\par
HTA at the provincial level
\par
Health technology assessment at provincial levels is largely shaped
\par
by the nature of the decentralized healthcare system in Canada.
\par
Quebec established the first provincial HTA organization: the
\par
Agence des Technologies et des Modes Intervention en Sant\'e
\par
(formally known as the Conseil d'Evaluation des Technologies
\par
de la Sant\'e) in 1988, followed by British Columbia, Alberta and
\par
Ontario [9]. In January 2011, the Institut d'Excellence en Sant\'e
\par
et en Services Sociaux was created, which includes the former
\par
Agence des Technologies et des Modes Intervention en Sant\'e
\par
and the Conseil du Medicament in Quebec. The assessment
\par
of pharma\- ceuticals is rather similar across the country, while
\par
non-drug HTA differs across provinces. A detailed description
\par
of HTAs in other provinces has been published elsewhere [9,11].
\par
This section focuses on both pharmaceuticals and non-drug HTA
\par
in Ontario.
