\par
Collection of contextualized real-world evidence
\par
To a certain extent, HTA could be performed based on currently
\par
available evidence. However, existing evidence might sometimes
\par
not be sufficient to support making decisions on the adoption and
\par
diffusion of new technologies. Such an insufficiency could come
\par
from quality, quantity or relevance. There is often concern about
\par
using efficacy data from randomized controlled trials as opposed
\par
to real-world effectiveness data, and of the general\- izability and
\par
relevance of the data collected outside the context to local policy-
\par
making. It may be worthwhile, at least in some cases, to collect
\par
new evidence to reduce decision uncertainty. Ideally, evidence col-
\par
lected in a contextualized real-world setting would be optimal to
\par
support decision-making within that context. As evident from the
\par
field evaluations completed by the authors, these contextualized
\par
real-world data were of high quality and relevance and have thus
\par
contributed significantly in informing health policy-making in
\par
Ontario [5]. However, a field evaluation typically takes between 2
\par
and 4 years to complete. This has been a barrier to the implemen-
\par
tation of field evaluations, in particular for those new technologies
\par
that have not been widely diffused in the healthcare system. It
\par
might not be feasible for healthcare institutions/professionals to
\par
collaborate on data collection. A potential approach to facilitating
\par
real-world data collection could be adoption of coverage with evi-
\par
dence development. Coverage with evidence development offers
\par
temporary funding for a health technology conditional upon the
\par
primary data collection to reduce uncertainty around the uptake
\par
of that technology. This scheme could be attractive for assessing
\par
those new and promising health technologies that may potentially
\par
improve healthcare outcomes. There have been a number of field
\par
evaluations conducted using coverage with evidence development
\par
in Ontario [20] (e.g., the drug-eluting stents study [23]).
\par
Contextualized real-world data collection is a time- and
\par
resource-consuming process that is implemented in some prov-
\par
inces in Canada. However, there is no evidence to suggest that
\par
`the context' must be specific at the provincial level. It might be
\par
worthwhile considering harmonization of individual provincial
\par
primary data collection activities. At the national level, it would
\par
utilize research resources more efficiently and create a platform
\par
for evidence sharing that would be meaningful, especially to those
\par
provinces without the capacity or resources to conduct data col-
\par
lection on their own. Obviously this is going to be another chal-
\par
lenging task, partly owing to the decentralized healthcare services,
\par
regional differences in healthcare administration and political
\par
will in Canada.
\par
Synthesis of evidence
\par
Meta-analysis is a statistical technique that has been widely used
\par
to synthesize clinical evidence from a systematic literature review.
\par
Recently, the development of indirect comparison allows the appli-
\par
cation of more advanced statistical techniques to address complex
\par
518
\par
research questions with large amounts of information retrieved
\par
from systematic reviews [31]. Given the broader scope of HTA
\par
compared with many other types of research (e.g., randomized
\par
controlled trials), these methods of synthesizing evidence would
\par
be more pertinent and useful for HTA (e.g., multiple head-to-head
\par
comparisons through network meta-analysis) [32]. More applica-
\par
tion of these complex meta-analysis methods will be seen in HTA
\par
in the future.
\par
A mathematical decision framework (i.e., economic model) is
\par
also often used to synthesize both clinical and economic evidence
\par
as a part of HTA. Model-based analysis has been criticized for vari-
\par
ous reasons since its first application in healthcare research [33,34].
\par
Fortunately, its quality has been substantially improved, largely
\par
due to methodological advances in health economic evaluation.
\par
Nevertheless, some issues remain inadequately addressed. First,
\par
the validity of model structure is the backbone of this type of
\par
economic evaluation. Although emphasized by the research com-
\par
munities on different occasions [35--38], model validation has not
\par
yet been adequately conducted and reported so far. Questions
\par
such as which level of validation to use, what `foreign' dataset is
\par
used and how a calibration is made might need to be kept in mind
\par
throughout the exercise.
\par
With the advances in computational technology, Bayesian meth-
\par
ods are more often being used in economic evaluations. A good
\par
example is the use of probabilistic analysis with randomly sampled
\par
values from prespecified distributions of uncertain parameters.
\par
However, as noted by Karl Claxton, ``the only valid reason to
\par
characterize the uncertainty surrounding outcomes of interest
\par
is to establish the value of additional information'' [39]. Value of
\par
information analysis can assist decision makers in determining
\par
the value of investing in additional research when the current
\par
available evidence is uncertain. It could be helpful in guiding the
\par
future research direction. Value of information could be added as a
\par
standard step in HTA where an economic evaluation is conducted.
\par
Multicriteria decision analysis
\par
Traditional HTA primarily concerns the costs and benefits of
\par
adopting a new technology in a healthcare system. However, such
\par
an adoption decision has never been simple. Other relevant factors
\par
are also incorporated into the decision-making process by HTA
\par
agencies such as the National Institute for Health and Clinical
\par
Excellence [40] and CADTH. Some of these factors (e.g., ethics)
\par
are considered explicitly, while others (e.g., health policy implica-
\par
tions) are not. Multicriteria decision analysis (MCDA), defined
\par
as a set of methods to aid decision-making based on more than
\par
one criterion, could make explicit the impact on the decision of all
\par
relevant criteria and the relative importance attached to them [41].
\par
MCDA could potentially improve the consistency and transpar-
\par
ency of the decision-making process. For example, OHTAC makes
\par
reimbursement recommendations for health technologies by con-
\par
sidering evidence on four criteria, namely overall clinical benefit,
\par
consistency with expected societal and ethical values, value for
\par
money and feasibility of adoption into the Ontario healthcare
\par
system [42]. Notably, OHTAC does not use a cost--effectiveness
\par
ratio threshold [42].
