\par
Health technology assessment (HTA) adopts a multidisciplinary approach to comprehensively
\par
assess safety, efficacy, effectiveness, economic and organizational impact, and any potential
\par
social and ethical implications associated with adoption and diffusion of a technology in a
\par
healthcare system. Canada was one of the first pioneers in using HTA as a research tool to
\par
support evidence-based decision-making. This article describes the current application of HTA
\par
in Canada, with a focus on some federal and Ontario initiatives in which the authors have
\par
extensive knowledge and experience to illustrate how academic researchers conduct HTA in
\par
collaboration with decision makers. Some issues and challenges are also highlighted that will
\par
hopefully stimulate a broader discussion among HTA stakeholders to move HTA forward.
\par
Keywords: coverage with evidence development {$\bullet$} health technology assessment {$\bullet$} multicriteria decision analysis
\par
Since the 1970s, healthcare systems in devel-
\par
oped countries have faced the challenge of
\par
improving healthcare quality while controlling
\par
healthcare spending. Increasing demand on
\par
healthcare services, largely associated with an
\par
aging population, coupled with much higher
\par
healthcare costs, is affecting many countries
\par
around the globe [1]. The concern of how to
\par
build and maintain sustainable healthcare sys-
\par
tems has become a prominent issue. Evidence-
\par
based medicine (EBM) was developed almost
\par
30 years ago and has since been widely applied
\par
in supporting decisions about managing patients
\par
by systematically synthesizing current evidence
\par
and circulating it among clinicians and decision
\par
makers [2]. Recently, comparative effectiveness
\par
research (CER) has become another popular
\par
term in the health services research commu-
\par
nity. CER focuses on generating evidence on
\par
the effectiveness of alternative treatments for a
\par
particular condition, primarily through head-to-
\par
head pragmatic trials [3]. Both EBM and CER
\par
arose, at different time points, out of an interna-
\par
tional environment in response to the need for
\par
clinical evidence for making decisions in the real
\par
world. More importantly, it has been increas-
\par
ingly recognized that decision-making is rather
\par
complex and dynamic as many factors (e.g., eco-
\par
nomic and ethical) need to be considered. Such
\par
complexity and dynamics inherent in medical
\par
decision-making processes fertilized the soil
\par
where health technology assessment (HTA) has
\par
blossomed in the past decade. However, EBM,
\par
CER and HTA do differ from each other, while
\par
sharing some commonalities [4].
\par
Health technology assessment is a multidisciplinary
\par
field adopting a broader perspective
\par
than EBM and CER. Although what a HTA
\par
covers might vary across jurisdictions and HTA
\par
organizations, a full HTA comprehensively
\par
assesses safety, efficacy, effectiveness, economic
\par
and organizational impact, and any potential
\par
social and ethical implications associated with
\par
the adoption and diffusion of a technology in
\par
a healthcare system. It could be used to build a
\par
bridge between academic research and real-world
\par
decision-making through generating evidence to
\par
support clinical and policy decisions [5]. HTA
\par
is an active and fast-growing field, which has
\par
been advanced by the evolution of methods in
