\par
Ministry of Health and Long-Term Care (MOHLTC) and the
\par
Ministry of Community and Social Services, the ODB program
\par
covers all of the costs of drugs listed in the Formulary (except
\par
for patient deductibles at either US\$6.11 or US\$2 per prescrip-
\par
tion). All requests for reimbursement under ODB undergo a
\par
formal review process. Specifically, manufacturers submit appli-
\par
cations to MOHLTC, which will be reviewed by both clinical
\par
and economic experts. The reports from clinical and economic
\par
experts will be reviewed by the Committee to Evaluate Drugs,
\par
who then make a recommendation to the Executive Officer of
\par
OPDP on whether the drug should be funded under the ODB
\par
program (Figure 2). If a drug is being considered under CADTH's
\par
CDR process, a final recommendation from CDR will be sent
\par
to the Committee to Evaluate Drugs, who will review it and
\par
make a recommendation. The CDR process does not replace
\par
the responsibility of the Committee to Evaluate Drugs (see
\par
the section on CDR). Notably, there is a similar but separate
\par
review process for the new cancer drugs administered by Cancer
\par
Care Ontario [104].
\par
Non-drug health technologies
\par
Health policy recommendations following an HTA for non-drug
\par
technologies in the province are made by OHTAC. OHTAC was
\par
formed in 2003 to provide a single point of entry for requests for
\par
funding health technologies from across the provincial health
\par
system (which is primarily publically funded), including requests
\par
from hospitals, community health services and from within
\par
MOHLTC [18]. The recommendations made by OHTAC gen-
\par
erally provide advice to the Minister of Health concerning the
\par
uptake and diffusion (as well as removal) of medical technologies
\par
within the Ontario health system.
\par
As shown in Figure 3, any stakeholder from Ontario's health-
\par
care system can submit a request for funding a non-drug health
\par
technology to MAS. The evidence related to the technology is
\par
gathered by MAS and presented to OHTAC in the form of health
