\par
team is recruited from clinical and academic centers
\par
from across the province to develop and implement a clinical
\par
study protocol. Upon study completion and analysis, the results
\par
of the field evaluation studies are presented back to OHTAC to
\par
help inform their policy decisions [19]. The authors have com-
\par
pleted field evaluations regarding drug\- -eluting stents [22,23],
\par
endo\- vascular aortic aneurysm repair [24,25] and multidetector
\par
computed tomographic coronary angiography [26]. The field
\par
evaluations completed by other academic research groups have
\par
been outlined by Levin et al. [20].
\par
Results from the conditionally funded field evaluations con-
\par
ducted by the authors at PATH Research Institute have been
\par
used to inform policy decisions regarding the utilization of
\par
drug-eluting stents, endovascular aortic aneurysm repair and
\par
multi\- detector computed tomographic coronary angiography
\par
in Ontario. Decisions have been made to provide funding for
\par
the use of these technologies in specific patient populations. A
\par
recent example of a funding decision is the list of computed
\par
tomographic coronary angiography in the
\par
Ontario Schedule of Benefit [27], based
\par
in part on the results of the study [26].
\par
These field evaluations have demon-
\par
