\par
circumstances, hospital-based HTA has emerged to provide
\par
information to support their decision-making [9]. In addition
\par
to the evidence typically included in a HTA conducted at the
\par
national or provincial level, hospital-based HTAs require close
\par
examination of organizational factors such as infrastructure,
\par
personnel, level of expertise, patient population and charac-
\par
teristics in a local context. McGill University Health Centre
\par
established the first hospital-based HTA program in the country,
\par
the Technology Assessment Unit, with a mandate of assisting
\par
hospital administration with the evaluation of technologies and
\par
the development of policy recommend\- ations [28]. Some other
\par
hospital-based HTA approaches have also been established in
\par
other provinces, such as Ontario and Alberta. Examples include
\par
the Technology Assessment at the Hospital for Sick Children
\par
in Toronto (Ontario), the High Impact Technology Evaluation
\par
Centre at London Health Sciences Centre (Ontario) and a local
\par
HTA decision-support program at the Department of Surgery
\par
and Surgical Services of the Calgary Health Region (Alberta).
\par
Most recently, PATH Research Institute, in collaboration
\par
with the administration at St Joseph's Healthcare Hamilton
\par
(Ontario), has established a pilot project, the St Joseph's
\par
Healthcare Hamilton HTA Appraisal and Review Program, in
\par
order to assist decision makers at the hospital level with funding
\par
requests for new health techno\- logies. The aim of this pilot project
\par
is to explore whether an evidence-based HTA approach could
\par
be applied more consistently across the hospital for informing
\par
decisions regarding new drugs, surgical interventions, labora-
\par
tory tests and capital acquisitions. Implementation of an evi-
\par
dence-based HTA process began with the identification of key
\par
stakeholders involved in decision-making
\par
processes within the hospital in order to
\par
review the existing processes in place for
\par
the introduction of new techno\- logies.
\par
PATH is working together with these key
\par
stakeholders to develop a common HTA
\par
framework that could be applied to all
\par
technology decisions across the hospital.
\par
The initial focus has been on appraisal and
\par
modifications to the current processes and
\par
tools (e.g., technology request forms) to
\par
improve consistency and transparency of
\par
the evidence considered by the hospital
\par
decision-making committees. A case study
\par
was conducted to assess the incremental
\par
benefit of using this structured HTA proc-
\par
ess for the evaluation of a new formulary
\par
drug. A focus group conducted with the
\par
committee responsible for making drug
\par
formulary decisions identified important
\par
benefits with an HTA approach, includ-
\par
ing a more comprehensive review of the
\par
evidence, a broader scope incorporating all
\par
relevant comparators, and the assessment
\par
of the implementation and organizational
\par
impact of new drugs.
\par
Future perspective
\par
Advancement of methodology is an essential and critical
\par
approach to improving the quality and credibility of HTA. In
\par
the past few decades, HTA has benefited significantly from the
\par
development and application of new methods in collecting and
\par
synthesizing evidence. Based on the experience and knowledge
\par
of the authors, some issues/challenges are provided in the fol-
\par
lowing paragraphs. The authors have to emphasize that the list
\par
was compiled from the perspective of academic HTA researchers
\par
and thus is by no means exhaustive, but we hope that it might
\par
help to stimulate a broader discussion among HTA stakeholders
\par
to move HTA forward.
\par
Prioritization of HTA topics
\par
Over the years, a significant increase in the volume of funding
\par
requests for pharmaceuticals and other health technologies has
\par
been seen across Canada. It is therefore more necessary than ever
\par
to prioritize technologies for assessment given the budget con-
\par
straints and limited research resources. A systematic literature
\par
review found that different methods are being used for HTA
\par
prioritization across HTA agencies and quantitative methods
\par
have not often been used [29]. Both CADTH and OHTAC state
\par
that all topics received were prioritized. However, little is known
\par
about what and how criteria are being used in prioritization.
\par
Recently, a practical approach that incorporates a multicriteria
\par
decision analytical method has been developed to help CADTH
\par
advisory committees to prioritize HTA topics [30]. Such a
\par
framework, with the prioritization criteria determined based
\par
on the inputs from different stakeholders (e.g., policy-makers,
