\par
A full health technology assessment (HTA) comprehensively assesses the safety, efficacy, effectiveness, economic and organizational
\par
impact, and any potential social and ethical implications associated with the adoption and diffusion of a technology in a
\par
healthcare system.
\par
A HTA could be used to build a bridge between academic research and real-world decision-making through generating evidence to
\par
support clinical and policy decisions.
\par
Canada was one of the first pioneers in using HTA as a research tool to support evidence-based decision making with the aim of
\par
improving healthcare efficiency.
\par
More than 25 organizations are currently actively conducting HTAs at different levels in Canada.
\par
The Canadian Agency for Drugs and Technologies in Health is the largest HTA producer at a national level in Canada.
\par
The Partner in HTA was established between the Network of Excellence for the Assessment of Health Technologies at McMaster
\par
University and the Canadian Agency for Drugs and Technologies in Health.
\par
The Programs for Assessment of Technology in Health Research Institute conducts HTAs involving primary local data collection to
\par
support reimbursement decision recommendations by the Ontario Health Technology Advisory Committee.
\par
The Programs for Assessment of Technology in Health Research Institute, in collaboration with St Joseph's Healthcare Hamilton,
\par
established a hospital-based HTA program, the St Joseph's Healthcare Hamilton HTA Appraisal and Review Program.
