\par
Keywords:
\par
Site remediation
\par
Fuzzy set theory
\par
Multi-criteria decision analysis
\par
Benzene
\par
TOPSIS
\par
A simulation-based fuzzy multi-criteria decision analysis (SFMCDA) method is developed for supporting
\par
the selection of remediation strategies for petroleum contaminated sites. SFMCDA integrates process
\par
modeling (using BIOPLUME III) and fuzzy ranking (based on fuzzy TOPSIS) into a general management
\par
framework, and can compare various remediation alternatives, in light of both cost-risk tradeoffs and
\par
uncertainty impacts. The proposed method is applied to a hypothetical contaminated site suffering from
\par
a benzene leakage problem. Six remediation alternatives are taken into consideration, including natu-
\par
ral attenuation (NA), pump-and-treat (PAT), enhanced natural attenuation (ENA), and a number of their
\par
combinations. Six fuzzy criteria, including both cost and risk information, are used to compare different
\par
alternatives through fuzzy TOPSIS. The results demonstrates that the proposed method can help system-
\par
atically analyze fuzzy inputs from contaminant transport modeling, cost implications and stakeholders'
\par
preferences, and provide useful ranking information covering a variety of decision-relevant remediation
\par
options for decision makers.
\par
Crown Copyright \copyright{} 2012 Published by Elsevier B.V. All rights reserved.
\par
1. Introduction
\par
Spill and leakage of petroleum hydrocarbons have resulted
\par
in significant environmental concerns in soil and groundwater
\par
systems. Removal of such contaminants is often expensive and
\par
time-consuming [1]. Over the past years, many remediation tech-
\par
niques (e.g. pump and treat, bioremediation) have been proposed
\par
to remove petroleum contaminants from the subsurface [2]. Due to
\par
the complexity of the remediation processes, decision-makers are
\par
often faced with difficulties in identifying the most desirable reme-
\par
diation strategy from the pool containing a wide range of options
\par
with varied remedial efficiencies and costs [3]. It is thus desired
\par
that effective tools for supporting site remediation decisions be
\par
advanced [4,5].
\par
In fact, identification of a suitable subsurface remediation tech-
\par
nology is a complex process, involving consideration of multiple
\par
factors such as environmental impact, social acceptance, and sys-
\par
tem cost [6,7]. Multi-criteria decision analysis (MCDA) is a potential
\par
tool for dealing with such discrete decision-making problems [8,9],
\par
where the potential management scenarios are compared and
\par
ranked based on a number of pre-defined criteria. Groundwater
\par
{$\ast$} Corresponding author. Tel.: +86 10 61772018.
\par
E-mail address: guohhuang@gmail.com (G.H. Huang).
\par
flow and contaminant transport modeling is a critical compo-
\par
nent in supporting remediation design [10--13]. MCDA is also
\par
advantageous in linking simulation to the decision-making frame-
\par
work for obtaining quantitative information related to cost and
\par
risk [9]. The conventional MCDA approach typically expresses all
\par
inputs as deterministic, however, in a real world groundwater
\par
remediation system, imprecise and vague information may exist
\par
[13--19]. It is thus desired that a systematic assessment approach
\par
for combining inputs from contaminant concentrations obtained
\par
by simulation model, health-risk guidelines, system costs analysis
\par
and stakeholder views be advanced to accomplish a sound analysis
\par
of available remediation options under uncertainty.
\par
Previously, many inexact MCDA techniques were developed
\par
for groundwater remediation. Bau and Mayer [20] developed a
\par
stochastic data-worth framework to select the optimal design
\par
of groundwater remediation operation that could minimize the
\par
cost and comply with cleanup goals under parameter uncer-
\par
tainty. In this method, a stochastic inverse flow and transport (FT)
\par
model was applied to integrate data towards the estimation of
\par
the geostatistical parameters. Singh and Minsker [21] proposed
\par
a robust multi-objective optimization method on a field-scale
\par
groundwater remediation design. Latin Hypercube sampling, a
\par
noisy multi-objective genetic algorithm were used within the opti-
\par
mization framework. Qin et al. [22] advanced a simulation-based
\par
stochastic MCDA method to choose remediation scenarios at a
\par
