\par
petroleum-contaminated site in western Canada, where Monte
\par
Carlo simulation and deterministic MCDA were combined to ana-
\par
lyze the final ranking outputs. However, the method would be
\par
restricted if the uncertainties could not be addressed by stochastic
\par
distributions. Many studies in various fields have focused on apply-
\par
ing fuzzy set theory to MCDA applications, in the sense that the
\par
imprecise and vague information regarding our knowledge of the
\par
state of a system or of human preferences in making trade-off deci-
\par
sions could better be described by fuzzy sets [23--25]. Guan and Aral
\par
[11,15] integrated fuzzy simulation into an optimization model for
\par
the optimal design of pump-and-treat systems under uncertainty.
\par
However, the authors considered only uncertainty in the simula-
\par
tion parameter. Chu and Chang [13] developed a multi-objective
\par
dynamic groundwater remediation by integrating fuzzy inference
\par
system (ANFIS) with constrained differential dynamic program-
\par
ming model to acquire time-varying pumping rates. The authors
\par
conducted the ANFIS procedure on the base of fuzzy if-then rules
\par
and fuzzification interface. Kerachian et al. [26] developed a fuzzy
\par
game theory approach combined with groundwater quantity and
\par
quality simulation models and the optimization model for ground-
\par
water resources management. The fuzzy set theory was utilized
\par
to define the utility functions of decision makers. In fact, differ-
\par
ent experts/stakeholders may have different preferences, which
\par
may lead to significant variations of the rules or values. Li et al. [3]
\par
advanced a fuzzy MCDA approach to help screen remedial alterna-
\par
tives for a petroleum-contaminated site in western Canada, where
\par
the approach was capable of dealing with uncertainty in the impact
\par
scores expressed as linguistic judgments (e.g. such as `high risk',
\par
`high cost') and experts' opinions.
\par
In the real-world applications, it has been widely recognized
\par
that the decision-making process for site remediation is associated
\par
with uncertainties from multiple sources, including (i) measure-
\par
ment and/or estimation errors related to hydrogeological and
\par
physicochemical parameters (e.g. dispersivity and porosity) [27];
\par
(ii) preferences of stakeholders with respect to the prioritization
\par
of criteria for a decision making process [8]; (iii) descriptions of
\par
available decision alternatives based on quantitative or qualitative
\par
data that are relying on expert opinions or experiential knowledge
\par
[28]. The fuzzy MCDA procedures must be able to address these
\par
uncertainties, along with other subjective uncertainties from either
\par
ranking criteria or qualitative description of information, for a more
\par
comprehensive consideration of uncertainties in site-remediation
\par
decisions.
\par
Thus, this study aims to develop a simulation-based fuzzy MCDA
\par
(SFMCDA) method for supporting selection of optimal remedial
\par
technologies for benzene-contaminated sites. The uncertainties
\par
associated with both subsurface conditions and human judgments
\par
are tackled as fuzzy sets. A hypothetical benzene-contaminated
\par
site is used as a case study to demonstrate the applicability of the
\par
proposed method. The paper is organized as follows: (Section 2)
\par
development of the SFMCDA method; (Section 3) case study, results
\par
analysis and discussions; (Section 4) conclusion.
\par
2. Methodology
\par
SFMCDA consists of two major components: (1) a fuzzy pro-
\par
cess simulation and a (2) fuzzy multi-criteria decision analysis. The
\par
general framework is shown in Fig. 1. The fuzzy process simulation
\par
is used to predict contaminant fate and transport under various
\par
temporal/spatial scales and remediation scenarios. The obtained
\par
contaminant concentrations and the related cost implications are
\par
presented as fuzzy sets, which will serve as uncertain inputs for
\par
MCDA evaluation. The fuzzy technique ordered preference by simi-
\par
larity to the ideal solution (i.e. fuzzy TOPSIS) method then is applied
\par
to rank various alternatives. The detailed procedures are described
\par
in the following sections.
\par
