\par
2.4. Simulation-based fuzzy-MCDA method
\par
Integration of the contaminant transport modeling, fuzzy sim-
\par
ulation and fuzzy TOPSIS into a general framework lead to the
\par
formulation of a simulation-based fuzzy-MCDA (SFMCDA) method.
\par
The detailed operation process of an SFMCDA can be summarized
\par
as follows:
\par
Step (1): Identify m alternatives (i.e. a1, a2,. . ., am), and l evaluation
\par
criteria (i.e. c1, c2,. . ., cl) for an MCDA problem, and obtain fuzzy
\par
weightings for the evaluation criteria.
\par
Step (2): Select an \k{}-cut level for the fuzzy parameters of the
\par
simulation model; use fuzzy simulation to calculate the interval
\par
values for contaminant concentration under various scenarios of
\par
alternatives.
\par
Step (3): Generate the performance matrix and weightings, deter-
\par
mine the ideal solution and the negative ideal solution by Eqs.
\par
(4a)--(4e).
\par
Step (4): Compute the fuzzy relative closeness of each alternative
\par
by solving the NLP models by Eqs. (5a)--(5d).
\par
Step (5): Repeat steps 2--4 for the other \k{}-cut levels, and the overall
\par
ratings of alternatives can be obtained by Eq. (6).
\par
Step (6): Rank alternatives in terms of their defuzzified relative
\par
closenesses.
\par
3. Case study
\par
3.1. Background and system configuration
\par
A hypothetical petroleum-contaminated site was adopted as
\par
a case study to demonstrate the capability of SFMCDA for deter-
\par
mining the most desirable remediation alternative. Fig. 2 shows a
\par
conceptual diagram of the study system. The contaminant plume
\par
was assumed to be produced by a leaking underground storage
\par
tank (UST) that was supposed to be removed after contamination
\par
has been detected. The concentration of the representative organic
\par
pollutant (i.e. benzene) was supposed to be highly exceeding SERM
\par
(Saskatchewan Environment and Resource Management) ground-
\par
water guidelines. The reason for choosing benzene was due to its
\par
well-known toxicity where direct exposure may cause acute non-
\par
lymphocytic (myelogenous) leukemia (ANLL) and a variety of other
\par
blood-related disorders in humans [39,40]. The receptors include:
\par
(i) a primary school, about 156 m to the downstream of the contam-
\par
ination source; (ii) a municipal drinking water supply well, about
\par
91 m to the upstream of the plume center; (iii) a residential area,
\par
about 158 m at the southwest of the plume center and (iv) the
\par
contamination source center (i.e. the starting location of the con-
\par
tamination). All of the receptors are considered at risk (the details of
