Thus, this site may pose significant threats to the surrounding com-
\par
munities and the environment; urgent remediation measures are
\par
needed for cleaning up the contaminated groundwater system. As
\par
there are many possible methods to deal with this, the decision
\par
makers need to know which remediation alternative is the most
\par
cost-effective in terms of risk and cost. The proposed methodol-
\par
ogy will be used for providing decision support for identifying the
\par
desired remediation option for the study site.
\par
The remediation technologies, well locations and its pumping
\par
rates are mostly referred to Prasad and Mathur [25] where a trial
\par
and correction procedure is used for identifying optimal operat-
\par
ing conditions. Six remediation scenarios were designed for the
\par
contaminated site (in Table 1). Fig. 3 shows the conceptual design
\par
for the remediation wells. Each remediation option operates for
\par
2 years. The pumping rates of the extraction wells and injection
\par
wells were kept constant at 0.566 and 0.396 L/s, respectively. The
\par
injection wells supply O2 dissolved in the injected water at a con-
\par
centration of 0.008 g/L.
\par
Six criteria were used for the evaluation, including both envi-
\par
ronmental and economical factors. The criteria weights were
\par
obtained through public survey, expert consultation and weights
\par
as described in Li et al. [3]. As shown in Table 2, they are expressed
\par
as triangular fuzzy sets. In defining membership functions, if the
\par
weight of installation cost is estimated to be within an interval of
\par
[0.08, 0.12], it may be reasonable to describe this criterion with
\par
a fuzzy set of ``about'' 0.1 using triangular membership functions.
\par
The other weights are defined similarly. Environmental perfor-
\par
mance was determined based on the dissolved concentrations of
\par
benzene in the groundwater beneath the locations of the primary
\par
school, residential area, supply well and the center of the con-
\par
tamination source. According to the SERM-based risk assessment
\par
method [41], the membership functions of the fuzzy risk levels can
