3.2. Result analysis
\par
Hydrological parameters, such as the porosity ( ) and aquifer
\par
dispersion coefficient (D), can vary significantly and exhibit high
\par
spatial variability even within the same site, leading to uncertain-
\par
ties in the subsurface modeling results [27]. In a real case, the
\par
parameters can be obtained by field measurements (i.e. use groups
\par
of pumping tests or slug tests in different regions), literature sur-
\par
vey, or expert consultation [28]. For example, based on the experts'
\par
knowledge of the concerned aquifer and also his experience on
\par
other aquifers (i.e. longitudinal dispersivity affecting contaminant
\par
transport at a certain scale in an aquifer), a triangular fuzzy mem-
\par
bership function of a hydrogeological parameter can be defined by
\par
specifying the most credible, the lowest and the highest possible
\par
values. In this study, the effective porosity of the sand media nor-
\par
mally varies from 0.2 to 0.4 [43]. So, the possibility distribution
\par
of this parameter is defined as a fuzzy set with a core of 0.3 and
\par
a support of (0.2, 0.4). According to Rifai et al. [29] and Shieh and
\par
Peralta [30], the longitudinal dispersivity is also assumed fuzzy and426 A.L. Yang et al. / Journal of Hazardous Materials 213-- 214 (2012) 421-- 433
