\par
3.2.4. Discussions
\par
The application of SFMCDA was demonstrated by a hypotheti-
\par
cal benzene-contamination case study, which was much simpler
\par
in magnitude than a possible real-world problem. This was to
\par
draw a clear picture of the problem-solving procedures and high-
\par
light the effectiveness of the proposed methodology. In real-world
\par
applications, a number of issues may be encountered in apply-
\par
ing the proposed methodology due to increased complexities: (i)
\par
the contaminant transport process is more complicated (i.e. due
\par
to increased heterogeneity and anisotropy of aquifer media) which
\par
could bring time-consuming computations in fuzzy simulation and
\par
extensive efforts in model calibration and verification; (ii) the
\par
characterization of the uncertainties in the groundwater remedia-
\par
tion system may need more careful site survey, literature review
\par
or expert consultation efforts; (iii) the remediation techniques,
\par
operation time, and cost analysis may be site specific; this brings
\par
difficulties in scenario design and identification of suitable evalua-
\par
tion criteria and weightings.
\par
At present, the available BIOPLUME III software cannot do an
\par
automatic run for a loop simulation. We actually did this manually,
\par
by inputting the data many times when running the fuzzy sim-
\par
ulation. Since the test example is not very large (one simulation
\par
requires about 30 s of CPU time), we managed to obtain the sim-
\par
ulation results in a reasonable time. For a complex site (i.e. more
\par
grid cells, complex boundary and initial conditions, etc.), it may not
\par
feasible to rely on manual input. It will be more preferable to use
\par
other software packages (such as UTCHEM or BIOF\&T) which are
\par
more programmable for automatic running. Since this paper aimed
\par
to focus on the development of a novel framework for evaluating
\par
the trade-offs between risk and cost, and finding the best remedi-
\par
ation alternatives while hedging against parameter uncertainties,
\par
we chose BIOPLUME III for easy configuration. The validity of such
\par
a model for simulating enhanced natural attenuation, pump-and-
\par
treat, and natural attenuation processes was well demonstrated by
\par
Prasad and Mathur [25]. The same physiology shall apply to other
\par
models and remediation processes.
\par
In real-world applications, identification of the fuzzy dis-
\par
tribution of a hydrogeological parameter needs careful site
\par
investigations. Strictly speaking, if a site is highly heterogeneous,
\par
every grid of the numerical domain should have an individual fuzzy
\par
membership function of the hydrogeological parameter. However,
\par
the data is difficult to obtain due to cost limitations. However, with
\par
some simplifications, a heterogeneous domain can be divided into
\par
a number of regions, where each region is assumed to be homo-
\par
geneous. This could be determined based on the geological surveyA.L. Yang et al. / Journal of Hazardous Materials 213-- 214 (2012) 421-- 433 431
