results. Then for each region, a pumping test campaign can be con-
\par
ducted to get a set of deterministic values for the hydrogeological
\par
parameter. The fuzzy membership function can then be generated
\par
based on the surveyed data. Eventually, we will have a number
\par
of fuzzy sets for the hydrogeological parameter. The greater the
\par
number of the regions to be divided, the more the pumping test
\par
campaigns is needed. If the entire site is considered homogeneous,
\par
a single pumping test campaign would be acceptable.
\par
In the remediation decision analysis process, the uncertainties
\par
can be related to parameter uncertain in natural porous media
\par
[45], risk assessment [46], alternative selection [3]. Fuzzy set
\par
theory provides an efficient mechanism for carrying out appropri-
\par
ate reasoning processes when available information is uncertain,
\par
incomplete, imprecise, or vague [15]. The involvement of experts
\par
has been identified as a critical aspect of the decision-making pro-
\par
cess [47]. In fact, the subjectivity may certainly exist in experts
\par
interviews. However, on one side, the weights value may not be
\par
given randomly, which needs experts and stakeholders to make
\par
trade-offs to finalize the weights. To a certain extent, given weight is
\par
a relative objective process. On the other side, different experts may
\par
have different preferences on each evaluation criteria; it is thus we
\par
made the weight of each criterion an uncertain value. In this paper,
\par
our study focused on the application of fuzzy simulation and fuzzy
\par
TOPSIS decision analysis to the evaluation and ranking of reme-
\par
diation alternatives instead of `decision by racking their brains'.
\par
Almost of the aforementioned uncertainties associated with the
\par
remediation decision analysis process were addressed. The pro-
\par
posed method can reduce the subjectivity as much as possible.
\par
Fig. 8 shows the remediation efficiency versus remediation cost
\par
at the water supply well for 1--5 years pumping for the six reme-
\par
diation alternatives. It is found that the tradeoff curve follows an
\par
exponent trend (i.e. the remediation cost increases exponentially
\par
with the increase of the pumping duration). Scenario ENA + PT is
\par
found to be the most efficient, as almost 100\% treatment efficiency
\par
could be obtained after only 1-year operation. Furthermore, at the
\par
same level of remediation cost (i.e. 223 \texttimes{} 103 \$), scenarios ENA + PT
\par
shows the highest efficiency (100\%), followed by PT2 (98\%) and
\par
PT1 (92\%). Fig. 8 also presents the results for treatment years vs.
\par
remediation costs under the most likely value. The installation costs
\par
(i.e. the remediation cost at the starting treatment year) for each
\par
remediation alternative are ca. 7.0, 38.9, 39.8, 22.4, 23.7, and
\par
45.8 \texttimes{} 103 \$, respectively. It can be observed that the costs for the
\par
different remediation alternatives increase with the number of
\par
operation years at stable increments of 9.8, 57.69, 61.57, 86.78,
\par
98.75, and 91.66 \texttimes{} 103 \$ per year; this is incurred by operation,
\par
analysis and labor costs. Scenario PT2 has the highest increment, as
\par
the pump-and-treat operation is costly. It can be obtained that the
\par
costs associated with PT2 may surpass that of ENA + PT after 4 years
\par
of operation. Thus, from a long-term management point of view, a
\par
time-series cost analysis could be added to the SFMCDA procedures
\par
for addressing such effects.
\par
The overall ratings of alternatives for this study case indicated
\par
that the designed remediation alternatives may not be efficient
\par
enough. Even the best choice ENA + PT, with a score value of 0.4883
\par
was far lower than 1. In real world applications, there are many
\par
remediation techniques and their operation conditions. A pre-
\par
screening or pre-analysis effort is necessary in order to reduce the
\par
number of alternatives [6]. For example, at a specified site, if a sin-
\par
gle remedial technology is to be considered, the most cost-efficient
\par
configuration of well numbers, well locations and pumping rates
\par
in applying such a technology could be identified first using an
\par
optimization model; then this technology with optimized operat-
\par
ing parameters could be considered as a potential alternative for
\par
further ranking. It is thus suggested to integrate both decision anal-
\par
ysis and optimization into a general framework for better decision
\par
making.
\par
In many practical applications, different experts/stakeholders
\par
may have different preferences on evaluation criteria, which may
\par
lead to significant variations of the weighting levels even for the
\par
same criteria. Thus, it should be appreciated that if the weights of
\par
the evaluation criteria change, the ranking results may be differ-
\par
ent. For example, if the experts consider ``less importance'' for the
\par
cost and risk at the primary school area, and ``high importance''
\par
for the risk at the locations of source zone, residential area and
\par
supply well, the weights of the six criteria may change to a new
\par
set of values (as shown in Table 2). The scores for alternatives NA,
\par
ENA1, ENA2, PT1, PT2 and ENA + PT would become 0.3589, 0.3156,
\par
0.3937, 0.1831, 0.3136 and 0.5212, respectively. Then, the ranking
\par
scheme would change to: ENA + PT {$>$} ENA2 {$>$} NA {$>$} ENA1 {$>$} PT2 {$>$} PT1.
\par
Scenario ENA + PT still ranks at the top due to its superior432 A.L. Yang et al. / Journal of Hazardous Materials 213-- 214 (2012) 421-- 433
\par
performances at the contamination source center and the supply
\par
well. The ranking for scenarios ENA2 and NA has changed due to
\par
the lowered weights of cost criteria. Scenarios with pump-and-
\par
treat operation rank behind due to their poor performance at the
\par
source zone. This indicates that the ranking results are sensitive
\par
to the weights of evaluation criteria. In real-world applications, it
\par
is highly suggested that the weights be carefully selected by the
\par
related experts and stakeholders.
\par
Appendix A. Supplementary data
\par
Supplementary data associated with this article can be found, in
\par
the online version, at doi:10.1016/j.jhazmat.2012.02.027.
\par
References
\par
4. Conclusions
\par
In this study, the focus is on the formulation and computa-
\par
tional aspects of the SFMCDA method. The following facts have been
\par
found:
\par
(1) Simulation models are important means to help analyze
\par
contaminant fate and transport under different remediation
\par
alternatives. Compared with the FMCDA method proposed by Li
\par
et al. [3], the proposed SFMCDA method could evaluate the envi-
\par
ronmental performance by transport modeling under a variety
\par
of remediating scenarios; the uncertainty associated with the
\par
simulation system could be tackled effectively by coupling the
\par
simulator and fuzzy vertex analysis that is less computationally
\par
intensive.
\par
(2) The weights for the decision-making process are important
\par
factors that would influence the ranking results. According to
\par
Bonano et al. [47], the remediation alternatives were relied
\par
on stakeholders' inputs which were described as deterministic
\par
values. Normally, different experts or stakeholders may have
\par
different preferences on each evaluation criterion, which may
\par
lead to significant variations of the weights. It is thus important
\par
to consider the weighting level of each criterion an uncertain
\par
variable, and enable the selection of remediation alternatives
\par
in an uncertain environment.
\par
(3) The ranking information obtained by SFMCDA takes uncer-
\par
tainty into consideration and is more reliable than those by
\par
conventional MCDA methods. Fuzzy TOPSIS decision analysis
\par
could produce a fuzzy estimate rather than a crisp-point esti-
\par
mate or an exaggerated interval estimate, where subjectivity
\par
has been reduced as much as possible.
\par
(4) Generally, the proposed SFMCDA method has the following
\par
advantages and innovations: (i) it systematically accounts for
\par
inputs from pollution impact, cost analysis and stakeholders'
\par
judgment; (ii) it is capable of addressing multiple-source uncer-
\par
tainties associated with simulation parameters, prioritization
\par
of criteria and description of alternatives; (iii) it could help mit-
\par
igate subjectivity of human judgment through the application
\par
of the fuzzy TOPSIS inference operation; (iv) it is also appli-
\par
cable for other environmental systems. SFMCDA also has much
\par
room for improvement. For example, the stochastic uncertainty
\par
and fuzzy uncertainty may occur in the same system, leading to
\par
difficulties of applying the proposed method; a coupled fuzzy
\par
stochastic simulation strategy may have to be used; it could also
\par
be combined with an optimization model to tackle continuous
\par
variable problems.
