\par
Closed-loop supply chain (CLSC), especially the reverse logistics system with the third-
\par
party reverse logistics providers (3PRLP) is very important for materials return, reman-
\par
ufacturing, repair, recovery, recycling, and reusing. In this paper, based on a one-leader
\par
and multi-follower Stackelberg game with the manufacturer being the leader, we model
\par
a multi-echelon CLSC with 3PRLP under the consideration of impacts of environmental
\par
legislation on scrap recycling. Furthermore, in order to encourage the 3PRLP to exert
\par
him to return activities we design a target rebate-punish contract between the manu-
\par
facturer and the 3PRLP under both stochastic price-dependent demands and stochastic
\par
effort-dependent returns. We analyze the closed-form analytic expressions for both united
\par
optimization strategies in centralized closed-loop system and the Stackelberg strategies
\par
in decentralized system, and conclude that the wholesale price contract may not coordi-
\par
nate the forward supply chain and the target rebate-punish may coordinate the reverse
\par
chain under certain conditions. Finally, according to the practice of Chinese steel enter-
\par
prises we carry out some simulation experiments to validate our theoretical analyses and
\par
gain some insights into the impacts of reverse logistics activities on the practitioners
\par
operations and decisions.
\par
Keywords: Closed-loop supply chain; third-party reverse logistics; Stackelberg games;
\par
contract coordination; scraps recycling.
\par
1. Introduction
\par
Environmental protection and sustainable development have become increas-
\par
ingly important in recent years. Many companies are challenged to integrate
