\par
Optimal Stackelberg Strategies for Closed-Loop Supply Chain
\par
reverse logistics activities on the practitioners operations and decisions. Our results
\par
may be helpful in analyzing CLSC operations and coordination for both theoretical
\par
researchers and practical practitioners, especially for those in steel manufacturing
\par
industry.
\par
The rest of the paper is organized as follows. In the next section, we offer
\par
a brief review of the literature on reverse logistics, CLSC, and the 3PRLP.
\par
Section 3 introduces our model framework, notation and assumptions. Section 4
\par
analyzes the CLSC models and optimal strategies, including integrated optimiza-
\par
tion strategies of the centralized system and the Stackelberg games (one leader
\par
and multiple followers) of the decentralized system. Section 5 compares the Stack-
\par
leberg strategies with the integrated optimizations and analyzes whether and on
\par
which conditions the contract can coordinate the chain. Section 6 uses numeri-
\par
cal experiments to illustrate our results, combining with practices of one Chinese
\par
large-scale steel-making company. Finally, a few concluding remarks are provided
\par
in Sec. 7.
\par
2. Literature Review
\par
This section provides a review of the existing literatures related to our research. In
\par
this section we provide an overview of recent work and examine the implications
\par
for our research.
\par
The CLSC focus on taking back products from customers and recovering added
\par
value by reusing the entire product, and/or some of its modules, components, and
\par
parts. Over the past 15 years, CLSC have gained considerable attention in indus-
\par
try and academia (Guide and Van Wassenhove, 2009). From different viewpoints
\par
they examined many issues emerged in CLSC, such as structure design, inventory
\par
control, capacity planning, and process improvement. For example, Savaskan et al.
\par
(2006) focus on the interaction between a manufacturer's reverse channel choice
\par
to collect postconsumer goods and the strategic product pricing decisions in the
\par
forward channel when retailing is competitive. They conclude that when a direct
\par
collection system is used, channel profits are driven by the impact of scale of returns
\par
on collection effort, whereas in the indirect reverse channel, supply chain profits are
\par
driven by the competitive interaction between the retailers. Different from theirs,
\par
we mainly consider the manufacturer incorporate three return channel together,
\par
namely in-house production return, buyback social return from retailers, and out-
\par
side purchase scraps, instead of considering each return choice separately. Prahinskia
\par
and Kocabasoglub (2006) review the literature in reverse supply chains (RSCs) and
\par
develop 10 research propositions to be studied using empirical research methods.
\par
Jung and Hwang (2007) study a remanufacturing in a reverse logistics chain with
\par
one OEM and one remanufacturer under take-back requirement. They show that
\par
the remanufacturers can be a potential competitor for the OEMs by cannibalizing
\par
the sales of the OEM's new product but the OEMs have the advantage of being
\par
free of take-back quota when remanufactures are active in collecting end-of-use
