\par
considers demand-related issues, such as the existence of green segments, original
\par
equipment manufacturer competition, and product life-cycle effects. They conclude
\par
that the profitability of a remanufacturing system strongly depends on these issues
\par
as well as on their interactions. Plambeck and Wang (2009) study the impact of
\par
e-waste legislation on new product introduction and find that ``fee-upon-sale'' type
\par
of legislation decreases the rate of new product introduction, and consequently, the
\par
quantity of e-waste, but does not provide firms the incentive to design products for
\par
recyclability. Pishvaee and Torab (2010) propose a bi-objective possibilistic mixed
\par
integer programming model to deal with uncertainties in CLSC and integrate the
\par
network design decisions in both forward and reverse supply chain networks. How-
\par
ever, to the best of our knowledge, almost all studies on the problem of reverse
\par
logistics and closed loop supply chain are based on the modes of retailer recycling
\par
or remanufacturer recycling. Most of the models in recent literatures research on
\par
the remanufacturing, recycling, recovery activities just from the view of separate
\par
reverse logistics and neglect the interaction between the forward flows and reverse
\par
flows.
\par
Furthermore, due to the revolution of green manufacturing aspect in the global-
\par
ization market, the reverse logistics concepts have become an important entity and
\par
play a pivotal role in a company's strategic decision. In real time situation, the col-
\par
lection of the returned products from the customer to manufacturer is complex and
\par
time consuming task. The separate logistics system has to be developed in order to
\par
inspect and maintain the collected returned products in the downstream manage-
\par
ment. This gives rise to the situation for outsourcing, which has recently given many
\par
third party providers with a unique opportunity to enter the reverse logistics market
\par
(Kannan et al., 2009; Jayarama and Tan, 2010). Saen (2009) propose a method for
\par
selecting the best 3PL providers in the presence of both cardinal and ordinal data
\par
based on Imprecise Data Envelopment Analysis. Xu et al. (2010) provide models of
\par
operation and profit model for 3PRLP and designed its information platform for
\par
accomplishing the former functions by applying the knowledge of expert system,
\par
network topology, and data base. Murugesan and Haq (2010) propose a structured
\par
model for evaluating and selecting the 3PRLP by considering factors such as 3PL
\par
services, organizational performance criteria, reverse logistics functions, and their
\par
respective sub-factors using the Analytic Hierarchy Process (AHP) and Fuzzy Ana-
\par
lytic Hierarchy Process (FAHP). However, most of researches mainly focus on how
\par
to select 3rd party logistics for reverse logistics activities and look the 3rd party
\par
logistics in CLSC operations only as one of the partner, not the decision-maker.
\par
So in this paper we try to analyze the new recycling mode with the 3PRLP and
\par
analyze its optimal strategy in Stackelberg games.
\par
Moreover, for simplification, some of recent researches often assume that the
\par
demand or/and supply rate for remanufactured products are determinate, which is
\par
not actual in practice. In fact, the uncertainties with respect to demands, returns,
\par
