\par
and recoveries reflect the complexity of the closed-loop system and challenge the
\par
theoretical modeling. In our study, we assume that demand is stochastic and price
\par
sensitive and returns is also stochastic and effort sensitive.
\par
Our research bridges and builds on the literatures by providing a method of how
\par
to integrate the reverse supply chains and traditional forward supply chain in which
\par
the stochastic demands are price-dependent and the stochastic returns are effort-
\par
dependent. In the next section, we will present our modeling framework, notation,
\par
and assumptions, and analyze models both in open-loop and CLSC.
\par
3. Modeling Framework, Notation, and Assumptions
\par
3.1. Framework description
\par
In this paper, we consider a multi-echelon supply chain including a manufacturer
\par
(remanufacturer), a retailer, and a 3PRLP. First, we present the basic modeling
\par
framework description of our research based on steel and iron scraps recycling (see
\par
Fig. 1). In Fig. 1, the real lines represent material flows and financial flows in forward
\par
supply chain and the dashed lines represent corresponding flows in reverse supply
\par
chain. Integrating the forward chain with reverse chain will constitute the closed-
\par
loop chain. In the forward chain, the manufacturer makes to order and retailer
\par
orders products from the manufacturer well in advance of the selling season with
\par
random and price-dependent demand. The contract between them is pure whole-
\par
sale price contract (or price-only contract), because it is very simple to administer
\par
and implement and commonly observed in practice. In the reverse chain, the man-
\par
ufacturer engages in remanufacturing with returned scraps from the 3PRLP who
\par
collects scraps with random and effort-dependent returns. Considering the impacts
\par
of environmental legislation and economic effects, the manufacturer will take mea-
\par
sures to encourage the 3PRLP to exert himself to return activities. So we design
\par
a target rebate-punish contract between them. Namely, the 3PRLP may take a
\par
rebate for the extra parts from the manufacturer if his total return quantity is
\par
beyond the target (minimum return) quantity, otherwise may incur punishments.
\par
Fig. 1. Framework of closed-loop supply chain based on 3PRLP.
\par
1250026-5N.-N. Yan \& B.-W. Sun
\par
In the whole modeling, we suppose that all the members in the supply chain system
\par
have symmetrical information and all of them are rational and risk-neutral.
\par
3.2. Notation
\par
In order to clearly describe and analyze our quantitative models, we first summarize
\par
