4. CLSC Models and Optimal Strategies
\par
In this section, we will present the CLSC models and analyze the optimal strategies
\par
of both centralized system and decentralized system.
\par
4.1. Centralized system: United optimization strategies
\par
As in the CLSC system (Fig. 1), we can find that the integration here have two-
\par
fold implications. The first means the integration of all system members to a cen-
\par
tralized system with a central planner/decision-maker, which usually considered
\par
in traditional supply chain. The second means the integration of two channels.
\par
The reverse and forward logistic channels could be integrated when they are both
\par
handled by the same party. The manufacturer could take advantage of their exist-
\par
ing efficient forward logistics infrastructure to collect their used products. So in
\par
our research, we consider the ``centralized'' system include these two integration
\par
together.
