5. Strategies Comparison and Coordination Analyses
\par
In this section, we mainly consider the coordination issues between multiple
\par
decision-makers in CLSC. The CLSC often consist of even more parties, so the scope
\par
of double marginalization or related effects would be increased further. However,
\par
it also introduces mechanisms for more flexible pricing without having to resort
\par
to two-part or more complex pricing schemes. Specially, in the recent emerging
\par
environment, i.e., CLSC environment, the research on contract coordination may
\par
be a new challenge. The researches on coordination of forward and reverse logis-
\par
tics decisions are still in its infancy stage. The current sporadic output of papers
\par
on the coordination of CLSC shows that there is still a need for much attention
\par
on this field. For instance, Savaskan et al. (2006) shows that the presence of a
\par
reverse channel can in fact eliminate the double marginalization problem in the
\par
forward channel. In a related study, Savaskan et al. (2004) also show that a man-
\par
ufacturer can use the payments in the reverse flows to price discriminate between
\par
retail outlets with different market sizes. Different from these researches, we focus
\par
on the contract coordination in decentralized CLSC, mainly in scraps recycling
\par
activities.
\par
As analyzed above, the incentive mechanism of our model is pure wholesale price
\par
contract between the manufacturer and the retailer in the forward supply chain
\par
and the target rebate-punish contract between the manufacturer and the 3PRLP
