6. Numerical Study: Take Example for Chinese Steel Enterprise
\par
We now proceed to demonstrate the explanatory power of the closed-loop scrap
\par
supply chain model presented in Secs. 4 and 5 using numerical examples which
\par
based on the steel and iron scrap recycling practice of Baoshan Iron and Steel Co.,
\par
Ltd. (Baosteel for short) in China.
\par
Baosteel is the largest and most modernized iron and steel complex in China.
\par
Baosteel has established its status as a world grade steel producer with compre-
\par
hensive advantages in reputation, talents, innovation, management, and technology.
\par
Recently, it specially directs its attention to the sustainable development and makes
\par
great efforts to lessen the environment burden in activities at different stages. Its
\par
specific environmental policy is to build Baosteel into an ecological iron and steel
\par
enterprise through pollution prevention, resources and energy conservation, and
\par
scrap recycling. From 2003, Baosteel started to find the third-party scraps bases,
\par
such as in the city of Taizhou, Jiashan and Ningbo, with responsibilities for collect-
\par
ing, sorting, machining, and other scrap recycling activities.
\par
Combining with its operation practice of scrap recycling and our model presented
\par
above, we carry out some simulation experiments. From a practical perspective, any
\par
simulation experiments will require certain assumptions and simplifications. Firstly,
\par
we look the Baosteel as the manufacturer (remanufacturer), and all of its 3PRLP
\par
(in Taizhou, Jiashan, and Ningbo) as a whole part, and all of its core retailers
\par
also as a whole, which may compose the CLSC. According to the data of Baosteel
\par
in 2004--2005 with our exiguous adjustment, we set the parameters needed in our
\par
models as follows, in which the dimension of quantity and price is ton and yuan (in
\par
Chinese currency) per ton: {$\alpha$} = 15 \%, {$\beta$} = 90 \%, a = 2, cn = 3, 000, co = 1, 200, v=
\par
3, 200, r = 800, {$\tau$}1 = 600, {$\tau$}2 = 500, D(p) =-1.185 \texttimes{} (p- 7500)(\texttimes 103), R(e) =
\par
0.85 \texttimes{} (e- 100)(\texttimes 103). In addition, we suppose the random factor {$\xi$} and {$\varepsilon$} of the
\par
\textasciimacron{}
\par
demands and returns follow the uniform distribution on the interval [0, 2], that is
\par
{$\xi$}
\par
and \textasciimacron{} {$\varepsilon$} are equal to 2.
\par
Based on our analyses in Secs. 4 and 5, we calculate the united optimiza-
\par
tion strategies of integrated closed-loop system and the one-leader-multiple-follower
\par
Stackelberg strategies of decentralized system. In the Stackelberg strategies, the
\par
retailer's optimal order quantity QS{$\ast$} is 10.19 \texttimes{} 106, the optimal retail price pS{$\ast$}
\par
is 5,850, and the optimal expected profit is 23.75 \texttimes{} 109; the 3PRLP's optimal
