\par
There are several contributions that arise from our analysis. The modeling
\par
framework as encapsulated in Fig. 1 can be used to design the CLSC structures
\par
for other products or other industries, especially for those face several uncertain-
\par
ties. Moreover, while our analysis is squarely focused on the strategies of central-
\par
ized/decentralized systems, the model also generalizes to determine the condition of
\par
contract mechanisms realizing coordination. Our results may be helpful in analyzing
\par
CLSC operations and coordination for both theoretical researchers and practical
\par
practitioners, especially for those in steel manufacturing industry.
\par
However, our analyses on coordination of CLSC decisions is only a groping and
\par
tentative research. There are several other important directions for future research.
\par
First, there is still an urgent need for some open problems about coordination and
\par
incentive mechanisms in closed-loop chains. Specifically, there are close interactions
\par
between forward chain and reverse chain, so the notion ``coordination'' of CLSC
\par
requires managing all material flows, financial flows, and information flows at once.
\par
It would greatly complicate the modeling framework and decision processes. Fur-
\par
thermore, how to explore the value of information in the context of uncertainties
\par
with respect to demands, returns, and recoveries is worthy of research. We have
\par
to design several strategies to reduce uncertainties in the timing and quantity of
\par
returns, balance return rates with demand rates, and make material recovery more
\par
predictable. They all may be possible future research areas.
