Abstract
\par
Purpose -- The purpose of this paper is to provide a framework to help the manufacturers to find the optimal decisions regarding the choice of channel
\par
member for information sharing.
\par
Design/methodology/approach -- A game-theoretical model plus Bayesian forecasting is developed to determine the optimal decisions for the
\par
manufacturer.
\par
Findings -- The results show that the optimal strategy for the manufacturer is to engage in information sharing with one small retailer exclusively, such
\par
that the manufacturer can gain the most benefit from information sharing arrangement in a marketing channel with a dominant retailer.
\par
Research limitations/implications -- The present study is analyzed by a theoretical model. Future research can explore the same study by collecting
\par
data to engage in an empirical test.
\par
Practical implications -- This paper provides a useful model framework and pricing strategy for upstream manufacturers who are engaging or
\par
planning to engage in information sharing with their retailers.
\par
Originality/value -- This paper provides practical and solid advice and examples demonstrating the optimal decisions regarding the choice of channel
\par
member for information sharing to best benefit of the manufacturer.
\par
Keywords Prices, Forecasting, Information sharing, Channel coordination, Game theory, Marketing strategy
\par
Paper type Research paper
\par
1. Introduction
\par
Information sharing strategy significantly impacts channel
\par
member behavior and performance in the retailing industry. It
\par
is well known that the business market is full of uncertainties.
\par
All channel members like to seek information about the
\par
market and about each other's decision (Li, 2002). Mastering
\par
more accurate demand forecast information contributes to
\par
better managerial decisions (Christopher, 1999). However, in
\par
many cases, channel members make decisions with limited
\par
information. Various efforts have been made to find better
\par
ways of producing accurate forecasts (e.g. Meredith, 2006;
\par
McDade et al., 2010; Yan, 2010a). The strategic application
\par
of information sharing between the channel members is
\par
becoming more and more important in the business market
\par
because different channel members possess different private
\par
The current issue and full text archive of this journal is available at
\par
www.emeraldinsight.com/1061-0421.htm
\par
information (Desrochers et al., 2003; Gal-Or et al., 2008). For
\par
instance, manufacturers often have only broad information
\par
about whole market and demographic patterns. However,
\par
retailers have point-of-sale (POS) data, the detailed
\par
knowledge of their location, regional consumer behaviors,
\par
and store measures and efforts. When the manufacturer and
\par
retailers share their information, their complementary
\par
information will be pooled and yield more accurate demand
\par
forecasts. In the business market, information sharing
\par
initiatives such as collaborative planning, forecasting, and
\par
replenishment (CRFR), radio frequency identification
\par
(RFID) technology, supply chain management integration,
\par
efficient consumer response (ECR), and electronic data
\par
interchange (EDI) have facilitated the sharing of demand
\par
information among the channel members. Information
\par
sharing effectively helps the channel members improve their
\par
forecasts and respond to changes in customers and
\par
competitive demands in a real time manner (Lancioni,
\par
2000). More accurate information can contribute to better
