\par
decision-making and higher firm performance (e.g. pricing
\par
products more accurately, reducing inventories, and lowering
\par
operational costs).
\par
Today, dominant retailers such as chain supermarkets, mass
\par
merchandizes, and wholesale clubs are becoming more and
\par
more powerful in the retailing industry. First, the dominant
\par
retailer is commanding a large market share in the retail
\par
market (Epstein, 1994) and often is the largest distributor for
\par
the manufacturer (e.g. Wal-Mart is the world's largest retail
\par
seller; the total market share of Home Depot and Lowe's is
\par
more than 50 percent in home improvement). Second, the
\par
dominant retailer often is the price leader in the retail market
\par
(Weinstein, 2000). Third, the dominant retailer has
\par
significantly reshaped marketing channel behavior. As a
\par
result, exploring the optimal decisions regarding the choice of
\par
channel member for information sharing to best benefit of the
\par
manufacturer has gained importance in recent times. Given
\par
that a dominant retailer generates larger volume of sales and
\par
has access to more information than other small retailers, it
\par
may be tempting to reason that sharing information with the
\par
dominant retailer always results in the greatest profit gain for
\par
the manufacturer. Our model, however, indicates that this
\par
reasoning may not always true. To illustrate, consider the
\par
following.
\par
Apparel manufacturer VF, snack producer Nabisco, Delta
\par
Faucet and some other suppliers would like to exclusively
\par
engage in information sharing with some smaller, specialty
\par
retailers such as ShopKo, Wegmans, and TrueVaue
\par
Hardware, respectively, but not with the dominant and
\par
power retailers like Wal-Mart, Home Depot, or Target (Hill,
\par
2000; VICS.org, 1999). This research investigates reasons
\par
and provides novel answers why some manufacturers may find
\par
that it is optimal to engage in information sharing with the
\par
small retailer instead of exchanging information sharing with
\par
the dominant retailer.
\par
In this paper, we consider a marketing channel consisting of
\par
one manufacturer, one dominant retailer, and two identical
\par
small retailers. We consider a maturity stage of product life
\par
cycle in which the manufacturer behaves as a monopolist in
\par
this market and supplies the same product to the dominant
\par
and small retailers. We thus assume that demand is a linear
\par
function of price. We first present a profit maximization
\par
model to study five different information scenarios:
\par
1 non-information sharing between the manufacturer and
\par
the retailers;
\par
2 information sharing between the manufacturer and the
\par
dominant retailer exclusively;
\par
3 information sharing between the manufacturer and one
\par
small retailer exclusively;
\par
4 information sharing between the manufacturer and both
\par
the dominant retailer and one small retailer
\par
simultaneously; and
\par
5 information sharing between the manufacturer and both
\par
the dominant retailer and all small retailers
\par
simultaneously.
\par
We then compare these scenarios and provide conditions
\par
indicating when and with which retailer the manufacturer
\par
gains the most benefit from information sharing. Our results
\par
show that engaging in information sharing with the dominant
\par
retailer exclusively or with both the dominant and one small
\par
retailer or all small retailers simultaneously is not an optimal
\par
strategy for the manufacturer. The optimal strategy for the
\par
Journal of Product \& Brand Management
\par
Volume 21 {$\cdot$} Number 6 {$\cdot$} 2012 {$\cdot$} 475 -- 485
\par
manufacturer is to engage in information sharing with one
\par
small retailer exclusively, such that the manufacturer can
\par
maximize its profit in a marketing channel with a dominant
\par
retailer. Furthermore, we examine the case where the
\par
manufacturer prices discriminate among the different
\par
retailers. Our results suggest that a manufacturer benefits
\par
the most from sharing information with one of the small
\par
retailers exclusively even if the manufacturer prices differently
\par
for the different retailers. Our study provides valuable
\par
managerial implications and insights to the manufacturers
\par
who supply personal computers, motor vehicles, digital
\par
products, and shopping products to a market channel with
\par
dominant and small retailers.
\par
The rest of our paper is organized as follows. Section 2
\par
contains a summary of the pertinent literature. Section 3
\par
discusses our model framework. Section 4 analyzes the cases
\par
of non-information sharing and different information sharing
\par
scenarios. Section 5 studies the value of information sharing
\par
and its impact on channel members. Section 6 analyzes the
\par
case in which the manufacturer prices differently to the
\par
different retailers. Conclusions are presented in Section 7. All
\par
relevant proofs are given in the Appendices for clarity of
\par
exposition.
\par
2. Literature review
\par
A number of studies have been done on a manufacturer's use
\par
of strategies to coordinate channel efforts. For example,
\par
Jeuland and Shugan (1983) showed that quantity discounts
\par
can be used to achieve channel coordination when price and
\par
non-price decision variables are involved. Ingene and Parry
\par
(1995) studied the case of a manufacturer that sells to
\par
independent retailers that directly compete between
\par
themselves for customers. They showed that coordination is
\par
not always in the manufacturer's interest when retailers
\par
compete. Iyer (1998) examined how manufacturers should
\par
coordinate channel distribution when two retailers compete
\par
under price and non-price attributes. He concluded that
\par
neither quantity discounts nor a menu of two-part tariffs are
\par
sufficient to coordinate such a channel. Tsay and Agrawal
\par
(2000) extended Ingene and Parry's (1995) research by
\par
generalizing the retail competition to both price and service
\par
effort dimensions. Raju and Zhang (2005) studied the
\par
channel coordination in the presence of a dominant retailer
\par
and showed the optimal conditions for the manufacturer to
\par
coordinate the channel through either quantity discounts or a
\par
menu of two-part tariffs. Yan and Pei (2009) showed that
\par
added retail services can effectively improve channel
\par
coordination between the manufacturer and the traditional
\par
retailer when the manufacturer opens a direct online channel.
\par
Yan (2010b) conducted a theoretical study to show that the
\par
manufacturer and the e-retailer can effectively improve
\par
channel coordination through cooperative advertising
\par
strategy and strategic alliance market structure. However, all
\par
of the above research focused solely on the situation of
\par
demand certainty and ignored the strategic value and
\par
importance of information sharing in channel coordination.
\par
Given the increasing importance of information sharing in
\par
marketing and economics literature, researchers have looked at
\par
the subject of information sharing from a variety of
\par
perspectives. For example, Bourland et al. (1996) showed
\par
that both the manufacturer and the retailer would profit from
\par
information sharing when their ordering cycles were
\par
476Price strategy, information sharing, and firm performance
\par
Ruiliang Yan, Chris Anthony Myers and John Wang
\par
significantly out of phase. Gavirneni et al. (1999) studied
\par
partial and complete shared information of inventory policies
\par
between a supplier and a retailer; they estimated the savings of
\par
the supplier due to information sharing and indicated when
\par
information sharing was more valuable. Cachon and Fisher
\par
(2000) investigated the value of information sharing between
\par
one supplier and multiple identical retailers. They found that
\par
information sharing led to savings due to lead-time and batch
\par
size reduction. Lee et al. (2000) looked at the value of
\par
information sharing in a two-level supply chain and found that
\par
information sharing can provide significant inventory reduction
\par
and cost savings. Corbett et al. (2004) found that it is better for
\par
the supplier to offer a two-part contract to the buyer once there
\par
is full information sharing. Li and Lee (2009) conducted a
\par
theoretical study to examine how information sharing (between
\par
the supplier and the retailer for order information) with order
\par
policy impacts the supply chain performance. Their results
\par
show that information sharing together with order
\par
postponement can effectively improve whole supply chain
\par
performance. However, these papers on information sharing
\par
focused solely on inventory, ordering batching, and logistics.
\par
Our focus is on the effects of information sharing on pricing
\par
decisions and the value of information sharing in a
\par
manufacturer -- multi-retailer channel.
\par
There are only a very few studies that examine information
\par
sharing in a manufacturer -- multi-retailer channel. Li and
\par
Zhang (2008) examined the effect of information sharing
\par
through varying degrees of confidentiality on supply chain
\par
performance and showed that with a full confidentiality, all
\par
retailers share their information truthfully with the
\par
manufacturer, and the supply chain profit will be
\par
maximized. Gal-Or et al. (2008) studied information sharing
\par
between a manufacturer and two competitive retailers with
\par
asymmetric market information and showed that a
\par
manufacturer prefers to share information with the less
\par
informed retailer when the cost of transmitting information is
\par
considered. However, our paper addresses a different issue.
\par
We employ a different channel model with new insights to
\par
study how a marketing channel with a dominant retailer can
\par
be coordinated through the choice of channel member for
\par
information sharing to best benefit of the manufacturer.
\par
Based on our results, we develop optimal policies and derive
\par
significant marketing strategies for business managers. Our
\par
study results in a fresh insight that sheds new light on what is
\par
the minimum cost for the manufacturer to obtain the most
\par
valuable information from competitive retailers.
\par
3. Model framework
\par
In this section, we consider a marketing channel consisting of a
\par
manufacturer, a dominant retailer, and two identical small
\par
