5. Choice of information sharing structure
\par
We are now in a position to compare all five cases (non-
\par
information sharing between the manufacturer and the
\par
retailers, information sharing between the manufacturer and
\par
the dominant retailer, information sharing between the
\par
manufacturer and one small retailer, information sharing
\par
between the manufacturer and both the dominant retailer and
\par
one small retailer, and information sharing between the
\par
manufacturer and both the dominant retailer and all small
\par
retailers) to develop some important managerial implications.
\par
5.1 Comparison between information sharing and non-
\par
information sharing
\par
Here we first assess the impact of information sharing
\par
structures on channel members' performances and then
\par
derive the optimal marketing strategy. Based on the
\par
comparisons of information sharing and non-information
\par
sharing, we have the P7 as follows. Proofs are given in
\par
Appendix 7:
\par
P7. If the forecasted demand of the manufacturer is larger
\par
than the forecasted demand of the retailer, it is
\par
beneficial for both the manufacturer and all retailers to
\par
engage in information sharing with each other;
\par
however, If the forecasted demand of the
\par
manufacturer is less than the forecasted demand of
\par
the retailer, the manufacturer benefits more from
\par
engaging in information sharing with one of its channel
\par
members (the dominant retailer or one small retailer)
\par
than from engaging in information sharing with both
\par
the dominant and one small retailers or all small
\par
retailers.
\par
P7 shows some important findings. When the forecasted
\par
demand from the manufacturer is larger than the forecasted
\par
demand from the retailer, the manufacturer will set up a
\par
higher wholesale price in the non-information sharing setting.
\par
However, when the dominant retailer or small retailer or both
\par
retailers shares their demand forecast with the manufacturer,
\par
the manufacturer realizes that the real market demand is not
\par
so high, thus he reduces the wholesale price to an optimal
\par
level, which leads to higher demand and profit for both the
\par
manufacturer and all retailers. Therefore, when the forecasted
\par
demand from the manufacturer is larger than the forecasted
\par
demand from the dominant retailer, both the manufacturer
\par
and all retailers would like to actively engage in information
\par
sharing.
\par
Alternatively when the forecasted demand from the
\par
manufacturer is less than the forecasted demand from the
\par
retailer, if the manufacturer engages in information sharing
\par
Journal of Product \& Brand Management
\par
Volume 21 {$\cdot$} Number 6 {$\cdot$} 2012 {$\cdot$} 475 -- 485
\par
with the dominant retailer or one small retailer, the
\par
manufacturer's increased profit will more than offset the
\par
dominant or small retailer's loss due to sharing information.
\par
Thus, the manufacturer can make a side payment to the
\par
dominant or small retailer to induce the dominant or small
\par
retailer to share its demand information so both the
\par
manufacturer and the dominant or small retailer can benefit
\par
from information sharing. However, when the forecasted
\par
demand from the manufacturer is less than the forecasted
\par
demand from the retailer, if the manufacturer engages in
\par
information sharing with both the dominant retailer and one
\par
small retailer or all small retailers simultaneously, its profit is
\par
less than the profit from sharing information with one of its
\par
channel members.
\par
Additionally, our results show that when the manufacturer
\par
and the dominant retailer (one small retailer) engage in
\par
information sharing, small retailers (dominant retailer and the
\par
other small retailer) will suffer. The rationale is that the
\par
manufacturer utilizes the shared information to extract extra
\par
profit from the small retailers (dominant retailer and the other
\par
small retailer). However, if the manufacturer can reach
\par
information sharing equilibrium with both the dominant and
\par
small retailers simultaneously, all channel members will
\par
benefit from their information sharing and no channel
\par
member will be hurt.
\par
Further, we compare the different information sharing
\par
structures and derive the optimal structure for the
\par
manufacturer. Thus we have P8 as follows. Proofs are given
\par
in Appendix 8:
\par
P8. Based on the comparisons, the information sharing
\par
structure selection preferences for the manufacturer
\par
from most to least favorable are given as follows. First
\par
choice: the manufacturer engages in information
\par
sharing with one small retailer exclusively; second
\par
choice: the manufacturer engages in information
\par
sharing with the dominant retailer exclusively; third
\par
choice: the manufacturer engages in information
\par
sharing with both the dominant and one small
\par
retailers simultaneously; final choice: the
\par
manufacturer engages in information sharing with
\par
both the dominant and all small retailers
\par
simultaneously.
\par
P8 shows an important finding. Intuitively, the manufacturer
\par
should engage in information sharing with the dominant
\par
retailer exclusively, because the dominant retailer has a large
\par
market demand and has access to large amounts of
\par
information, or the manufacturer should engage in
\par
information sharing with both the dominant and small
\par
retailers simultaneously, so that the manufacturer can have
\par
access to larger amount of market information. However,
\par
these intuitions are not true. The rationale is that if the
\par
manufacturer engages in information sharing with the
\par
dominant retailer, the manufacturer cannot benefit
\par
additionally from a higher wholesale price from the
\par
dominant retailer. On the other hand, if the manufacturer
\par
engages in information sharing with both the dominant
\par
retailer and one small retailer or all small retailers
\par
simultaneously, the overlap information delivers a
\par
diminishing return on the manufacturer's investment in
\par
sharing information with the additional retailer. The cost
\par
structure of the manufacturer (e.g. investment in
\par
communication technologies and software, personnel
