induce this small retailer to share its information. Then the
\par
manufacturer can extract greater surpluses from the dominant
\par
retailer and other small retailers by charging a higher
\par
wholesale price.
\par
Furthermore, we also investigate a question posed by the
\par
business market: how can the information sharing equilibrium
\par
be reached between the manufacturer and the small retailer?
\par
One such arrangement is as follows.
\par
First, all supply chain players (i.e. manufacturer, dominant
\par
retailer, and small retailers) operate in a non-information
\par
setting to optimize their own decision variables to maximize
\par
their individual profits. Then based on our derived findings,
\par
the manufacturer would like to share its information with one
\par
of the small retailers and asks this small retailer to share its
\par
information. Then the small retailer compares its forecast
\par
with the manufacturer's forecast to decide how to share its
\par
forecast information: if the small retailer' demand forecast is
\par
lower than the manufacturer' demand forecast, the small
\par
retailer will actively share its information with the
\par
manufacturer without pursuing any other compensation
\par
from the manufacturer; however, if the small retailer'
\par
demand forecast is higher than the manufacturer' demand
\par
forecast, because the small retailer has the manufacturer'
\par
forecast information and can use the profit function to infer
\par
the increased profits of the manufacturer, the small retailer
\par
will ask the manufacturer to sign a side-payment contract to
\par
offset its lost profits and to share the manufacturer's extra
\par
profits (due to sharing information).
