\par
manufacturer to engage in information sharing with
\par
is one of the small retailers; and the manufacturer
\par
benefits more as its' wholesale price discrimination
\par
increases.
\par
P9 indicates that even as the manufacturer employs a price
\par
discrimination strategy to charge different wholesale prices to
\par
different retailers, one of the small retailers still is the first
\par
choice for the manufacturer to share information with.
\par
Further, the manufacturer can benefit more when its' price
\par
discrimination increases. The rationale is that when the small
\par
retailer copies the dominant retailer's price as its market price,
\par
then shares this information with the manufacturer, the
\par
manufacturer will have a complete information about the
\par
dominant retailer's forecast (i.e. when the small retailer shares
\par
its information with the manufacturer, from the dominant
\par
retailer's profit function, the manufacturer can infer the
\par
dominant retailer's forecast perfectly). Thus the manufacturer
\par
only needs make a small price or profit concession to the small
\par
retailer, and then he can acquire the complete forecast
\par
information of the dominant retailer to improve its decision-
\par
makings and performances. Furthermore, as the price
\par
discrimination increases, the small retailer's inference effect
\par
about the manufacturer's true forecast reduces. Thus,
\par
through information sharing, the manufacturer can utilize
\par
its private information to appropriate more of the channel
\par
profit.
