\par
We then compare these scenarios and show when and with
\par
which retailer the manufacturer gains the most benefit from
\par
an information sharing arrangement. Through our research,
\par
we seek to characterize the most profitable way for a
\par
manufacturer to engage in information sharing with its
\par
channel members. Our results show that engaging in
\par
information sharing with the dominant retailer exclusively or
\par
with both the dominant retailer and one of the small retailers
\par
or all small retailers simultaneously is not an optimal strategy
\par
to the manufacturer. The optimal strategy for the
\par
manufacturer is to engage in information sharing with one
\par
of the small retailers exclusively, such that the manufacturer
\par
can achieve its largest profit in a marketing channel with a
\par
dominant retailer. Even if the manufacturer prices differently
\par
to different retailers, our results suggest that a manufacturer
\par
benefits the most from sharing information with one of the
\par
small retailers exclusively as well. We summarized all the
\par
different scenarios and the optimal decisions in Table I.
\par
Our findings are valuable and useful in the business market
\par
because they provide an information-based rationale to
\par
support some important strategic decisions made by channel
\par
members, particularly for the upstream manufacturers/
\par
suppliers. These businesses are able to use the managerial
\par
insights from our research to improve their marketing
\par
decisions to improve profit. In the business market, the
\par
manufacturers in personal computers, motor vehicles, digital
\par
products, and shopping products can fully utilize our findings
\par
to achieve a higher performance.
\par
Our research also can be extended in several directions.
\par
First, our model is a theoretical study. Thus future research
\par
can be done empirically to investigate if the derived analytical
\par
results from our paper can be generalized to empirical models.
\par
Future research can also fully incorporate all the relevant
\par
variables (e.g. price, service, location, brands, etc) into an
\par
empirical model to test the value of information sharing and
\par
its impact in a marketing channel with competitive retailers.
