  note = {\emph{Int. J. Decision Sciences, Risk and Management, Vol. 3, Nos. 3/4, 2011 }327
\par
\textbf{Logistics systems and optimisation strategies under}
\par
\textbf{uncertain operational environment}
\par
Turkay Yildiz*
\par
Izmir Institute of Technology (IYTE),
\par
Gulbahce kampusu, 35430 Urla, Izmir, Turkey
\par
E-mail: turkayyildiz@iyte.edu.tr
\par
*Corresponding author
\par
Funda Yercan
\par
Department of International Logistics Management,
\par
Yasar University,
\par
No:35-37, 35100, Bornova, Izmir, Turkey
\par
E-mail: funda.yercan@yasar.edu.tr
\par
\textbf{Abstract:} Transportation and logistics systems are characterised by their
\par
highly dynamic structures along with numerous interconnected processes. The
\par
natures of these systems involve various levels of resource allocation decisions
\par
where usually it is not always possible to execute these decisions in the field on
\par
time at the best possible way because of the unpredictable factors in plans. By
\par
considering the uncertain operational environment, this paper explores the
\par
uncertainty issue within operational systems and deals with the problem of
\par
allocating resources to maximise expected total profit and minimise
\par
inefficiencies under uncertainty. The aim is to design, develop, visualise, and
\par
effectively deal with a more realistic model to satisfy uncertain demand nodes
\par
by leaving minimal or no unsatisfied zones within an operational environment
\par
at seaports, transportation, logistics and supply chain systems. A representative
\par
optimisation model, which is developed to address the uncertainty issue, has
\par
been solved by using an optimisation algorithm. The results show that
\par
operational plans without the utilisation of uncertainty models could have
\par
negative impacts, including increased emissions, negative environmental
\par
effects, along with higher costs to organisations.
\par
\textbf{Keywords:} uncertainty; optimisation algorithm; resource allocation problem;
\par
transportation; logistics; environment.
\par
\textbf{Reference} to this paper should be made as follows: Yildiz, T. and Yercan, F.
\par
(2011) `Logistics systems and optimisation strategies under uncertain
\par
operational environment', \emph{Int. J. Decision Sciences, Risk and Management},
\par
Vol. 3, Nos. 3/4, pp.327--346.
\par
\textbf{Biographical notes:} Turkay Yildiz received his PhD from the Institute of
\par
Marine Sciences and Technology, Dokuz Eylul University, Izmir, Turkey. He
\par
received his Master in Logistics Management from Izmir University of
\par
Economics. He received his Science degree from Bogazici University, Istanbul,
\par
Turkey, and his BA degree from Anadolu University in Turkey. He has
\par
a number of publications and conference presentations at various countries
\par
around the world in the field of marine sciences, transportation and logistics.
\par
He also has various levels of expertise at the applications of information
\par
technology.
\par
Copyright \copyright{} 2011 Inderscience Enterprises Ltd.328 \emph{T. Yildiz and F. Yercan}
\par
Funda Yercan is a Professor of Maritime Transport and Logistics. She served
\par
as an Academic at Dokuz Eylul University between 1992 and 2010. She was a
\par
Visiting Professor at the Department of International Business and Logistics at
\par
Maine Maritime Academy in the USA during the academic years 2001--2002
\par
and 2006. She was an Adjunct Faculty at the Department of Logistics
\par
Management at Izmir University of Economics in Izmir, Turkey between 2003
\par
and 2009.
\par
This paper is a revised and expanded version of a paper entitled `Optimal
\par
allocation of logistical resources under uncertain operational environment'
\par
presented at the European Conference on Shipping, Intermodalism and Ports
\par
(ECONSHIP 2011), Chios, Greece, 22--24 June 2011.
\par
\textbf{1 Introduction}
\par
Logistics systems have been at the centre of attention of enterprises for over a decade
\par
with today's heightened expectation of customers and harsh competitive environment in
\par
markets. Enterprises are continuously seeking the best development and enterprise-level
\par
best solution strategies for their logistics systems to meet the present and possible future
\par
expectations and to stay beyond their counterparts. With the advancement of information,
\par
communication systems, and optimisation techniques, it became possible to model and to
\par
obtain solutions for large scale complex problems.
\par
Hall (2003) stated that transportation science covers research from many fields such
\par
as geography, economics, and location theory. Methodologies of transportation science
\par
come from physics, operations research, probability, and control theory; it is
\par
fundamentally a quantitative discipline, relying on mathematical models and optimisation
\par
algorithms to explain the phenomena of transportation (Hall, 2003). Frazelle (2002)
\par
explained the overall goal of transportation as it should be: to connect sourcing locations
\par
with customers at the lowest possible transportation cost within the constraints of the
\par
customer service policy. Thus, the transportation optimisation equation could be
\par
expressed as follows: minimising total transportation costs subject to customer service
\par
policy constraints (Frazelle, 2002). Frazelle (2002) also emphasised that transportation
\par
expenses are rising quickly versus other logistics costs, with smaller, more frequent
\par
orders, increasing international trade and global logistics, rising fuel charges, labour
\par
shortages, decreased carrier competition due to carrier mergers and acquisitions, and
\par
increased union penetration in the labour market.
\par
Optimisation is a well established field due to comprehensive research conducted
\par
over past decades. In decision science, optimisation is an essential tool. As stated by
\par
Nocedal in 1999, optimisation is an important tool in decision science and in the analysis
\par
of physical systems. In order to benefit from optimisation, it is necessary to identify some
\par
objectives and then some quantitative measures of the system.
\par
The objective of a system could be maximising profit, minimising total time for
\par
completing a task or a group of tasks, or maximising/minimising any quantity or
\par
composition of quantities that can be represented by mathematical models. The objective
\par
depends on certain characteristics of the system, called variables or unknowns. The goal
\par
of optimisation is to find correct values for the variables that optimise the objective. The
\par
variables are usually restricted, or constrained, in some way (Nocedal, 1999). Many types
\par
of optimisation problems have been addressed and various types of algorithms have been\emph{Logistics systems and optimisation strategies }329
\par
researched. Several methodologies for optimising objectives of systems have been used
\par
in various practical applications and the range of applications is continuously growing
\par
(Arora, 2007).
\par
Additionally, Simchi-Levi (2004) acknowledged that it is challenging to design and
\par
operate a supply chain so that total system wide costs are minimised and system wide
\par
service levels are maintained. Indeed, it is frequently difficult to operate a single facility
\par
so that costs are minimised and service level is maintained. The difficulty increases
\par
exponentially when an entire system is being considered. The process of finding the best
\par
system wide strategy is known as global optimisation (Simchi-Levi, 2004). The process
\par
of identifying objectives, variables, and constraints for a given problem is known as
\par
modelling.
\par
Construction of an appropriate model is the first step -- sometimes the most important
\par
step -- in the optimisation process. If the model is too simplistic, it will not give useful
\par
insights into the practical problem, but if it is too complex, it may become too difficult to
\par
solve (Nocedal, 1999). Before solving an optimisation model, it is important to consider
\par
the form and mathematical properties of the objective function, constraints, and decision
\par
variables.
\par
For example, the objective function might be linear or non-linear, differentiable or
\par
non-differentiable, concave or convex, etc. The decision variables might be continuous or
\par
discrete. The feasible region might be convex or non-convex. These differences each
\par
impact how the model can be solved, and thus optimisation models are classified
\par
according to these differences (Sarker, 2002).
\par
Global optimisation is made even more difficult because supply chains need to be
\par
designed for and operated in uncertain environments (Simchi-Levi, 2004). Operations
\par
research uses quantitative models to analyse and predict the behaviour of systems, and to
\par
provide information for decision makers. Two key concepts in operations research are
\par
optimisation and uncertainty. Uncertainty is emphasised in operations research that could
\par
be called `stochastic operations research' in which uncertainty is described by stochastic
\par
models. The typical models in stochastic operations research are queuing models,
\par
inventory models, financial engineering models, reliability models, and simulation
\par
models (Dohi, 2009).
\par
There are a large number of optimisation problems in organised systems, such as in
\par
industrial systems, business systems, transportation and logistics systems, where at
\par
strategic, tactical, and operational levels, planners, analysts, strategists, and engineers are
\par
confronted with uncertainty. Many optimisation problems arising from these systems
\par
have deterministic (certain)\emph{ }parts along with uncertain\emph{ }components, which planners could
\par
disambiguated based on the predictable and probabilistic information. Along with the
\par
other various factors, Pardalos (2004) pointed out the crucial issue of developing efficient
\par
methods of analysing this information in order to understand the internal structure of the
\par
market and make effective strategic decisions for the successful operation of a business.
\par
In addition, referring to the efficiency of the transportation infrastructure, Hall (2003)
\par
expressed that planners and engineers need to forecast the demand of transportation to
\par
make informed transportation infrastructure planning decisions. Arora (2007) stated for
\par
any engineering system that the uncertainties in system characteristics and demand
\par
prevent assurance from being given with absolute certainty. For supply chain systems,
\par
Simchi-Levi (2004) emphasised that it is necessary to design systems that eliminate as
\par
much uncertainty as possible and it is necessary to deal effectively with the uncertainty
\par
that remains.330 \emph{T. Yildiz and F. Yercan}
\par
Much research has been devoted to tackling optimisation problems under uncertainty.
\par
Researchers have investigated various systems and have dealt with the uncertainty issue.
\par
Table 1 highlights recent and leading studies on the applications of stochastic
\par
programming with uncertainty depending on the fields studied.
