\par
Moreover, while taking into consideration efficient systems design under uncertainty,
\par
critical awareness about the environment is important. The transport sector is one of the\emph{Logistics systems and optimisation strategies }331
\par
few sectors that have ever-increasing greenhouse gas (GHG) emissions. According to
\par
Intergovernmental Panel on Climate Change (IPCC) reports, transport accounts for
\par
13.1\% of GHG emissions, 23\% of global carbon dioxide (CO2) emissions, 26\% of total
\par
world energy use, and the transport sector is one of the constantly growing sectors. Thus,
\par
it is also critical to take seriously into consideration inefficient transportation and
\par
logistics system designs that can have negative effects on the environment.
\par
A report by Pronello and Andr\`e (2000) proposed that pollution caused by a set of
\par
vehicle routes can be unreliable. However, it is obvious that reducing the total distance
\par
and thus putting to practical use of optimal routes by algorithms will provide
\par
environmental benefits as a result of the reduction in fuel consumption and the
\par
consequent pollutants (Sbihi and Eglese, 2007). Palmer (2004) also analysed a study
\par
about the connection between efficient network design and thus reducing environmental
\par
pollutants.
\par
This paper is organised as follows. Section 2 provides general background
\par
information on mathematical programming with uncertainty components and recourse
\par
variables. Sub-section 2.2 presents a sample model. Then, the model initialised with data
\par
and the model is solved in Sub-section 2.3. Section 3 summarises the paper.
\par
\textbf{2 A background: mathematical programming}
\par
This section presents a brief general background about mathematical programming with
\par
\emph{second-stage} or \emph{recourse} variables. As stated by Sahinidis (2004), second-stage variables
\par
can be interpreted as corrective measures or recourse against any infeasibilities arising
\par
due to a particular realisation of uncertainty. The most common programming models are
\par
stochastic linear/non-linear/integer programming, probabilistic or chance-constrained
\par
programming, robust stochastic programming, stochastic dynamic programming, and
\par
fuzzy programming. The next section will present brief fundamentals of mathematical
\par
programming with uncertainty. Further details of mathematical programming concepts
\par
have been well documented in the textbooks of Bertsekas and Tsitsiklis (1996), Birge and
\par
Louveaux (1997), Kall and Wallace (1994), Pr\'ekopa (1995), and Zimmermann (1991).
\par
Early works involving uncertainty studies were included research by Beale (1955),
\par
Bellman (1957), Bellman and Zadeh (1970), Charnes and Cooper (1959), Dantzig (1955),
\par
and Tintner (1955).
\par
\emph{2.1 Stochastic linear programming with recourse}
\par
A stochastic programming can be stated as
\par
\emph{n}
