Waechter and Biegler (2006) stated that the interest in efficient optimisation methods led
\par
to the development of interior-point or barrier methods for large-scale non-linear
\par
programming. In addition, Waechter and Biegler (2006) further emphasised the fact that
\par
in particular, these methods provide an attractive alternative to active set strategies in
\par
handling problems with large numbers of inequality constraints. For the convergence
\par
properties of interior-point methods (Forsgren et al., 2002) over the past years there has
\par
been a better understanding and efficient algorithms with desirable global and local
\par
convergence properties have been developed (Waechter and Biegler, 2006). Waechter
\par
and Biegler (2006) also stated that to allow convergence from poor starting points,
\par
interior-point methods, in both trust region and line-search frameworks, have been
\par
developed that use exact penalty merit functions to enforce progress toward the solution
\par
(Byrd et al., 2000; Tits et al., 2003; Yamashita, 1998; Waechter and Biegler, 2006).
