\textbf{3 Conclusions}
\par
While there is no single effective solution that can be applied to various other types of
\par
real-world problems having some uncertainty components, the IpOpt optimisation
\par
method presented on this paper and theoretically applied based on a typical scenario. In
\par
brief, this paper has introduced optimisation issues under uncertainty along with brief
\par
fundamentals of mathematical programming concepts that address various possible
\par
uncertainty situations. It then presented a sample optimisation model with uncertainty
\par
variables. Finally, it showed optimal solutions found at the last iteration of the
\par
optimisation algorithm. The optimal solution found at the last iteration provides critical
\par
information, which addresses the best system configuration and conditions under
\par
changing operational environment.
\par
The sample optimisation model put the IpOpt optimisation algorithm to practical use;
\par
this model was developed to address the uncertainty issue. The results indicate that
\par
operational plans without taking into consideration of uncertainty models could have
\par
negative impacts that lead to non-optimal operational design and thus, higher costs to
\par
organisations along with possible harmful environmental effects (i.e., increased
\par
emissions, pollutants, and more). This study solved a sample model having more than a
\par
hundred thousand variables/constraints. The aim was to visualise an optimal solution
\par
under uncertainty for a sample logistics problem. Many other solvers and optimisation
\par
algorithms are capable of solving highly complex problems with millions of variables in
\par
a short period depending on the CPU power of the computer.
\par
Further research ought to be conducted to investigate optimal activity under the
\par
uncertainty of a logistic system, such as an automated container terminal that is utilising
\par
unmanned terminal vehicles and using automatic stacking (AS) and retrieval systems
\par
(RS) for container storage. However, there is a limitation of AGVs, for example, is that
\par
they are slower than the manned terminal vehicles and thus might have an adverse effect
\par
on terminal efficiency. On the other hand, future unmanned equipments will have
\par
superior properties and the trend appears to be going towards completely unmanned
\par
container terminal operations, which will eventually require running operations under
\par
uncertainty.\emph{Logistics systems and optimisation strategies }339
