@article{yuekselTutorialQuantizerDesign2011,
  title = {A Tutorial on Quantizer Design for Networked Control Systems: Stabilization and Optimization},
  shorttitle = {A Tutorial on Quantizer Design for Networked Control Systems},
  author = {Yueksel, Serdar},
  year = 2011,
  journal = {Applied and Computational Mathematics},
  volume = {11},
  pages = {365--403},
  publisher = {Citeseer},
  langid = {english},
  note = {A Tutorial on Quantizer Design for Networked Control Systems:
\par
Stabilization and Optimization
\par
Serdar Y\textasciidieresis uksel1
\par
Abstract
\par
In this article, a tutorial on optimal quantizer design in networked control systems is presented. The
\par
goal of this invited article is not to present a literature survey (although an effort is presented for a
\par
brief literature review); but to present a detailed discussion on the salient issues and some of the main
\par
results in the literature and by the author and collaborators, together with some previously unpublished
\par
contributions. The analysis is restricted to noiseless channels with finite capacity. The analysis and
\par
results presented consist of quantizer design for stabilization over noiseless channels, optimal quantizer
\par
design for minimizing a class of cost functions, quantizer design for stabilization of decentralized systems,
\par
and optimal quantizer design under a class of decentralized information structures.
\par
1 Networked Control Systems
\par
A Networked Control System is a control system in which the components are connected through real-time
\par
communication channels or a data network. Thus, there may be a data link between the sensors (which collect
\par
information), controllers (which make decisions), and actuators (which apply the controller commands).
\par
Due to such a networked structure, many modern control systems are decentralized. A system is said
\par
to be decentralized if there are multiple decision makers in the system (e.g., sensors, controllers, encoders)
\par
and these decision makers have access to different and imperfect information with regard to the system
\par
they operate in, and they need to either cooperate or compete with each other. Such systems are becoming
\par
ubiquitous, with applications ranging from automobile and inter-vehicle communications design, control of
\par
surveillance and rescue robot teams for access to hazardous environments, space exploration and aircraft
\par
design, among many other fields of applications (see [73], [26], [43] for a review of application areas).
\par
In such remote control applications, one major concern is the characterization of a sufficient amount
\par
of information transfer needed for a satisfactory performance. This information transfer can be between
\par
various components of a networked control system. One necessity for satisfactory control performance is the
\par
ability for the controllers to track the plant state under communication constraints. One other challenge
\par
is the determination of the data rate required for the transmission of control signals, and the construction
\par
of dynamic encoding, decoding, and control policies meeting some criteria. Another important problem is
\par
the coordination among multiple sensors or multiple controllers/decision makers with the lowest information
\par
exchange possible. Even in cases when communication resources are not scarce, a strong understanding of
\par
the fundamentals can be useful in the system architecture, and finally, such an insight can help reduce the
\par
computation requirements and complexity.
\par
As indicated earlier, there are usually three types of agents involved in a control system: sensors, con-
\par
trollers, and actuators (plants) (see Fig. 1). Various forms of this architecture have been introduced and
\par
studied in the literature in the networked control community. To be able to analyze different scenarios, it is
\par
important to identify the probabilistic description of the system and characterize the information structure
\par
in the system. Toward this goal; in the following we review information structures in decentralized control.
\par
S. Y\textasciidieresis uksel is with the Department of Mathematics and Statistics at Queen's University, Kingston, Ontario, Canada, K7L
\par
3N6. Email: yuksel@mast.queensu.ca.
\par
