\par
1.1 Information Structures in a Networked Control System
\par
In the following, we present a general discussion on information structures; which will also be useful in our
\par
analysis for quantized systems.
\par
We can present a general decentralized system based exclusively on the underlying probability space and
\par
the interaction dynamics of the decentralized decision makers. Such a model was considered by Witsenhausen,
\par
and is known as Witsenhausen's Intrinsic Model [153]. In this model, any action applied at a given time stage
\par
is regarded as applied by an individual station/decision maker/agent. The Intrinsic Model of Witsenhausen
\par
has three components:
\par
1. An information structure: I := \textbraceleft\textohm, F , (U k
\par
, U k ), 1 {$\leq$} k {$\leq$} N \textbraceright{} specifying the system's allowable decisions
\par
and events. N is the number of control actions taken, and each of these actions is taken by an individual
\par
station (hence, even a station with perfect recall can be regarded as a separate decision maker, at
\par
different time stages). (\textohm, F ) is the measurable space generating the probability space. (U k
\par
, U k )
\par
denotes the measurable space from which the action of decision maker k, uk is selected.
\par
2. A design constraint, which restricts the set of admissible N-tuples, control laws {$\gamma$}= \textbraceleft{$\gamma$}1, {$\gamma$}2, . . . , {$\gamma$}N \textbraceright,
\par
called designs to the set of all measurable Ik measurable control functions. Here, the sub-sigma field
\par
Ik denotes the information that can be used to select the kth control action.
\par
3. A probability measure P defined on (\textohm, F ).
\par
A decentralized problem is dynamic if the information available at one decision maker (DM) is affected by
\par
the actions of another decision maker. A decentralized problem is called static, if the information available
\par
at every decision maker is only affected by exogenous disturbances, or nature; that is no other decision maker
\par
can affect the information at any given decision maker.
\par
Witsenhausen defines information structures to be one of the three types: Classical, quasi-classical and
\par
non-classical. If the information available at the decision makers is nested in the sense that Ik {$\subset$} Ik+1
\par
,
\par
the information structure is classical. An information structure is quasi-classical, if whenever the control
\par
2actions of a DMi affects the observations of another decision maker DMj , the information available at DMi
\par
is known noiselessly by the affected decision maker. An information structure is non-classical, if it is not
\par
quasi-classical.
\par
In a large class of applications, there is a pre-defined, deterministic, ordering of control actions, and there
\par
is a state-space model; in which there are a number of decision makers with perfect recall (that is, these
\par
