\par
not have access to the information available to DMk , this information structure is said to be non-classical.
\par
Hence, an information pattern which is not partially nested is a non-classical information pattern. The
\par
one-step delayed control sharing pattern zi
\par
t = ut-1 is one such example [4], [129], [9].
\par
A systematic approach for generating optimal control policies by eliminating redundant information has
\par
been introduced in [88].
\par
[147], [6] and [123] have studied sufficiency conditions for tractability and convexity in optimal decentral-
\par
ized control problems.
\par
Even when the information structure is non-classical, one may obtain tractable solutions for optimal
\par
control problems. The stochastically nested information structure is an example of such problems [172].
\par
Shannon's point-to-point communication problem can be regarded as another example which is by definition
\par
non-classical, but tractable.
\par
When the information structures are non-nested, controllers might choose to communicate via their
\par
control actions, that is might wish to pursue signaling. Different types of signaling can occur: signaling to
\par
learn the dynamics of the system, signaling what the belief (that is, the conditional probability measure) on
\par
the state of the system is, signaling what the belief on the other agents controls are or signaling what the
\par
agent's own future control actions will be, depending on the effects on the cost performance. Especially, in
\par
decentralized control systems, the notion of information, and how it is generated, is often part of the control
\par
problem itself, and this has been captured in the so-called dual effect [8], so that control can serve both to
\par
improve the quality of estimation and also to achieve its primary objective. This leads to a triple effect, when
\par
there is also an incentive for signaling: the action that decision makers communicate each other through the
\par
plant. This arises prominently in networked control systems. We conclude this section by noting that, the
\par
information structures can be further identified, by also considering the probability measure and the cost
\par
functions; such an approach has been adopted in [91].
