\par
Several studies in the literature have focused on noiseless discrete channels with time-invariant encoders,
\par
where the main issue becomes one of design of an invariant quantizer; see [42] and [44]. The first of these,
\par
[42], adopts a Lyapunov-based approach to stabilize a system with limited information, and shows that the
\par
coarsest quantizer achieving stability is logarithmic and that the design is universal, i.e., it has the same base
\par
for construction regardless of the sampling interval. The second reference, [44], quantifies the relationship
\par
between the rate of convergence and communication rate for scalar systems, and provides conditions for
\par
set invariance as a measure of stability. An information theoretic approach to quantizer design leading to
\par
stabilizability was presented in [177]. [93] and [95] consider the disturbance rejection problem over noisy
\par
channels. In [73], a Lyapunov theoretic quantizer design leading to stability is provided, with further results
\par
reported in [72], and time-varying quantizer design presented also in [126]. Reference [29] studied control
\par
over channels with partial observations, and adopted an information theoretic approach, also with a robust
\par
formulation on the class of sources. For noisy binary forward channels with noiseless feedback, coding
\par
schemes were presented for the forward channel with noiseless feedback in [133], and linear time-invariant
\par
as well as time-varying control policies on continuous-alphabet channels modeled as Gaussian channels were
\par
considered in [24].
\par
For control over erasure channels there are also various studies: [71], [103], [164], [180], [167], [132], [60].
\par
For more general noisy channels, we refer the reader to [99], [93], [92], [41], [127], [181], among others.
\par
For coding and information transmission for unstable linear systems, there is an important difference
\par
between continuous alphabet and finite-alphabet (discrete) channels as discussed in [181]: When the space is
\par
6continuous alphabet, we do not necessarily need to consider adaptation in the encoders. On the other hand,
\par
when the channel is finite alphabet, and the system is driven by unbounded noise, a bounded range quantizer
\par
(a quantizer with bounded granular region) leads to almost sure instability. This was first recognized in view
\par
of the unboundedness of second moments in Proposition 5.1 in [108], transience of the process is established
\par
in Theorem 4.2 in [181]. [30] considers the conditions for stabilization when the control signals are bounded.
\par
As discussed above, zooming type adaptive quantizers, introduced by Brockett and Liberzon [25], for
\par
remote stabilization of open-loop unstable, noise-free systems with arbitrary initial conditions has been very
\par
useful. Reference [171] obtained a martingale characterization for stabilization for such zooming quantizers
\par
even when there is noise in the system, and the noise has unbounded support for its probability measure.
\par
This approach has been extended to control over erasure channels in [167] and [180].
\par
There is also a large body of literature on real-time quantizer design in the communications and infor-
\par
mation theory community, as conveniently presented in the survey paper [57]. There are many important
\par
contributions from this field on optimal quantization of general sources; in both in the context of block-codes
\par
(where infinite copies of a source are encoded simultaneously), as well as in the single-shot, or delay-limited
\par
settings. In this context, we wish to point out one observation: The distortion-rate problem [34] and the
\par
entropy-constrained quantization problem [61] exhibit the distinctness of the two problems: The distortion-
\par
rate (or the dual problem of rate-distortion) deals with efficient coding of an infinite copy of a source; whereas
\par
the entropy-constrained quantization problem deals with a real-time setup of encoding one realization of a
\par
random variable.
\par
One important reference is the work by Goodman and Gersho [51], where an adaptive quantizer was
\par
introduced and the adaptive quantizer's stationarity properties were investigated when the source fed to
\par
the quantizer is a second order and i.i.d. sequence. In fact, zooming type quantizers is a special class of
\par
Goodman and Gersho's adaptive quantization scheme. Kieffer and Dunham [76], have obtained conditions
\par
for the stochastic stability of a number of coding schemes when the source considered is also stable, where
\par
various forms of stability of the quantizer and the estimation error have been studied.
\par
There is also a large collection of work in the context of optimal real-time source coding, Related papers on
\par
real-time coding include the following: [111] established that the optimal optimal causal encoder minimizing
\par
the data rate subject to a distortion for an i.i.d sequence is memoryless. If the source is kth-order Markov,
\par
then the optimal causal fixed-rate coder minimizing any measurable distortion uses only the last k source
\par
symbols, together with the current state at the receiver's memory [155]. References [149] considered the
\par
optimal causal coding problem of finite-state Markov sources over noisy channels with feedback. [145]
\par
and [89] considered optimal causal coding of Markov sources over noisy channels without feedback. [87]
\par
considered the optimal causal coding over a noisy channel with noisy feedback. Reference [83] considered the
\par
causal coding stationary sources under high-rate assumption. Borkar, Mitter and Tatikonda [19] studies a
\par
related problem of coding of a partially observed Markov source, however, the construction for the encoders
\par
is restricted to take a particular form which uses the information at the decoder and the most recent
\par
observation at the encoder (not including the observation history). [110] considers decentralized coding of
\par
correlated sources when the encoders observe conditionally independent messages given a finitely valued
\par
random variable and obtain separation results for the optimal encoders. The paper also considers noisy
\par
channels. Reference [169] and [170] consider partially observed sources, and some results in these papers
\par
will be presented in this article. References [151] and [104] consider optimal causal variable-rate coding
\par
under side information and [178] considers optimal variable-rate causal coding under distortion constraints.
\par
A parallel line of consideration which has a rate-distortion theoretic nature is on sequential-rate distortion
\par
proposed in [143] and the feedforward setup, which has been investigated in [148] and [40]. A related work
\par
is Witsenhausen's indirect rate distortion problem [154] (see also [38]). Further related papers include [12],
\par
[69].
\par
This paper also presents a discussion for the decentralized setup. The corresponding brief literature
\par
review will be presented in Section 5.
\par
Here is a brief overview of the rest of the paper. In the next section, we revisit some preliminary
\par
definitions on quantization and information theoretic notions. In Section 3, we consider the problem of
\par
stabilization under quantization constraints, and in Section 4, we consider the problem of optimal single-
\par
7terminal quantization. In Section 5, stabilization of decentralized systems under quantization constraints
\par
is discussed. In Section 6, optimization for decentralized systems is considered. The paper ends with the
\par
concluding remarks of Section 7
