2 Quantization and Preliminaries
\par
Essential in communications problems is the embedding of information into a finite set, possibly with loss
\par
of information. This is done through quantization, which is a mapping from a larger alphabet to a smaller
\par
alphabet. We refer the reader to the survey paper [57] on a detailed review of quantization and introduction
\par
of different types of quantizers.
\par
Let us present a formal definition which we will adopt throughout the paper.
\par
Definition 2.1 Let M= \textbraceleft 1, 2, . . . , M \textbraceright{} with M= \textbar M\textbar. Let A be a topological space. A quantizer Q(A; M)
\par
is a Borel measurable map from A to M. {$\diamond$}
\par
We define the bins or cells in a quantizer as the sets:
\par
Bi = \textbraceleft a {$\in$} A : Q(a) = i\textbraceright, i {$\in$} M.
\par
We note that, traditionally, in source coding theory, a quantizer is also characterized by a reconstruction
\par
value in addition to a set of partitions; in our definition we only define the quantizer by the bins in the
\par
quantizer.
\par
We say the rate of such a quantizer is log2(\textbar M\textbar ) bits.
\par
When the spaces A and M are clear from context, we will drop the notation and denote the quantizer
\par
simply by Q.
\par
In practice, however, one may impose additional structure on the quantizers; for example one may impose
\par
the bins in the quantizers to be convex. Further structures are possible in this context. An important class of
\par
quantizers on Rn are those which are uniform, those which have their bins with identical Lebesgue measure.
\par
These will be discussed later in the paper.
\par
The information rate is measured by the number of bits. When the coding is variable-rate, information is
\par
measured by the average number of bits needed to be exchanged among the decision makers; whereas when
\par
the coding scheme is fixed-rate, information is measured by the actual number of bits that are exchanged
\par
for any given time stage t {$\geq$} 0.
\par
Let x be a finitely-valued random variable. Consider the following problem: What is the minimum
\par
amount of average bits needed to represent x with no loss? The answer to this leads us to the notion
\par
of entropy of a source [34]: Entropy of a finitely-valued random variable provides an almost tight lower
\par
bound on the average bit rate needed to compress the source losslessly. The entropy of such a source is
\par
defined as H(x) =- p(x) log2(p(x)). When the source is continuous, we use the term differential entropy:
\par
h(x) =- p(dx) log2(p(dx)), where p(dx) is the measure of the source.
\par
Mutual information between an input random variable, x, and a corresponding output, y, is
\par
I(x; y) = H(x)- H(x\textbar y),
\par
where H(x\textbar y) is the conditional entropy of x given y.
\par
We would like to point out one important distinction between information theoretic applications and such
\par
real-time problems considered in the paper. The relation between the rate-distortion function and the notion
\par
of distortion-constrained entropy minimization [61] [62] [177] is a striking example identifying the difference:
\par
In rate-distortion theoretic analysis, an infinite realization of a random variable is observed, and compression
\par
is performed based on the entire sequence. For a quantization problem; however, a single realization is
\par
observed, and the output it generated only for the single realization. Clearly, the rate-distortion function
\par
leads to a lower value, since one could shape the codebooks appropriately [83]. Only for quantization of
\par
8vector valued processes, in the limit of large dimensions, one observes an equivalence between the distortion-
\par
constrained entropy minimization and the rate-distortion function [57]. We note that, in the definition of
\par
the rate distortion function R(D) for a random variable x taking realizations in X with distortion metric
\par
{$\rho$} : X \texttimes{} X {$\rightarrow$} R:
\par
R(D) = inf P (\textasciicircum{} x\textbar x),{$\gamma$}:E[{$\rho$}(x,{$\gamma$}(\textasciicircum{} x))]{$\leq$}D
\par
I(x; \textasciicircum{} x),
\par
the minimization is over the space of stochastic kernels P (\textasciicircum{} x\textbar x), where I({$\cdot$}
\par
,{$\cdot$}) denotes the mutual information
\par
function. In the quantization framework, the optimization is over the space of quantizer-decoder pairs only.
\par
Note that, when \textasciicircum{} x admits a discrete probability measure and \textasciicircum{} x = {$\gamma$}(Q(x)), for some decoder function {$\gamma$},
\par
I(x; \textasciicircum{} x) reduces to H(\textasciicircum{} x). Thus, the essential difference is the space of optimization. Furthermore, even though
\par
the rate-distortion function is a convex function of distortion, the distortion-constrained entropy function is
\par
not a convex function of distortion (see [61], [62], where in the former study [61], an analysis for a uniform
\par
source has been considered). The space of quantizers is not a convex space.
\par
Although typical information-theoretic approaches require long delays; important special cases provide
\par
optimal performance even under delay-limited settings: One example is the problem of communicating a
\par
Gaussian source over a Gaussian channel. This topic is related to the matching principle; the readers
\par
are referred to [49] and [7]. On a parallel note, the multi-terminal source coding theorems [34], although
\par
insightful, are not always applicable for a real-time setting, as the asymptotic partitioning arguments in
\par
classical information theory [34] do not apply. In a control context, however, one method to achieve the
\par
information theoretic bounds is via binning; see [175], [176], [58] for discussions on binning in a decentralized
\par
control context and [120] for a discussion on binning in a general communications context.
