\par
be performed at both the encoder and the decoder. One particular class of adaptive quantizers is introduced
\par
by Goodman and Gersho [51], which we will consider in the following analysis.
\par
This system is connected over a noiseless channel with a finite capacity to an estimator (controller). The
\par
controller has access to the information it has received through the channel. The controller in our model
\par
estimates the state and then applies its control. As such, the problem reduces to a state estimation problem
\par
since such a scalar system is controllable. Hence, the stability of the estimation error is equivalent to the
\par
stability of the state itself.
\par
An example of Goodman-Gersho [51] type adaptive quantizers, which also has been shown to be very
\par
useful in control systems, are those that have their bin sizes as the quantizer states [25]. In the zooming
\par
