\par
7 Concluding Remarks and Some Open Problems
\par
This tutorial left out many problems and questions; in view of conciseness and space constraints.
\par
7.1 Quantizer Design for Optimal Control over Erasure and Noisy Channels
\par
The erasure channel is an important practical channel, and one could argue that it is the most relevant noisy
\par
channel for real-time control applications. For such channels, both stabilization and optimization have been
\par
considered in the literature. We refer the reader to [71], [103], [164], [180], [167], [132], [60], [166].
\par
Likewise, for discrete-alphabet as well as continuous-alphabet noisy channels, there has also been a
\par
significant activity. References [99], [143], [181], [145], [87] and [7] contain a rich literature review. There
\par
still remain many open problems, in particular on synchronization across noisy channels, and optimal encoder
\par
design with noisy feedback. The book [99] is an excellent reference for a comprehensive discussion of general
\par
channels and a literature review.
\par
7.2 Existence of Optimal Quantizers and Optimal Quantization of Probability
\par
Measures
\par
The problem of existence of optimal quantizer, and topological properties of information channels in net-
\par
worked control problems has been addressed in a number of papers, such as [19] and [179].
\par
In [179] existence of optimal quantizers is addressed for the case when the source admits a probability
\par
density. One further problem is on the existence and design of optimal quantizers. There are results available
\par
in the literature on optimal quantization of probability measures [53], however, more needs to be done in
\par
30the context of finite rate communications and for the cases when the source lives in an uncountable space.
\par
With a separation result paving the way for an MDP formulation, one could proceed with the analysis of [19]
\par
with the evaluation of the optimal quantization policies and existence results for infinite horizon problems.
\par
Nonetheless, quantization of probability measures remains an interesting problem to be understood in a
\par
real-time coding context; with important practical consequences. In [179], existence of optimal quantizers
\par
has been studied for finite dimensional Euclidean state spaces, when a source distribution admits a density.
\par
The separation results in the paper and in the literature will likely find many applications in sensor net-
\par
works and networked control problems where sensors have imperfect observation of a plant to be controlled.
\par
One direction is to find explicit results on the optimal policies using computational tools. One promising
\par
approach is the expert-based systems, which are very effective once one imposes a structure on the designs,
\par
see [62] for details.
\par
7.3 Optimal Information Exchange and Information Structures with an Infor-
\par
mation Theoretic Characterization
\par
One related problem is how to optimally encode the control actions; such an analysis will explicitly depend
\par
on the cost functions [146]. In this case, the exchanged information exhibits the properties of the triple effect
\par
of control; as such the analysis requires further research.
\par
In the paper, we discussed the case when the rate is high enough to allow belief sharing. The regime
\par
where this is not possible requires further research, since the exchange information affects both the current
\par
stage cost and the costs in future stages. Deterministic nestedness has been observed to be too restrictive
\par
in [172]. One observes that it is possible to transform an information structure into a desirable one leading
\par
to tractable decentralized optimization problems by exchange of minimal information. Such an analysis will
\par
have important practical consequences. The dynamic nature of such quantization problems, however, require
\par
first a precise notion of decentralized state of a system.
