\par
Objectives This study sought to examine the relationship between the aspirin dose prescribed at
\par
hospital discharge and long-term outcomes after ST-segment elevation myocardial infarction in pa-
\par
tients treated with primary percutaneous coronary intervention (PCI).
\par
Background Patients with ST-segment elevation myocardial infarction who undergo primary PCI are
\par
prescribed maintenance aspirin doses that vary between 75 and 325 mg daily. Whether the dose of
\par
aspirin affects long-term patient outcomes is unknown.
\par
Methods We compared 3-year outcomes in patients who were prescribed high-dose ( 200 mg
\par
daily) versus low-dose ( 200 mg daily) aspirin from the large-scale HORIZONS-AMI (Harmonizing
\par
Outcomes With Revascularization and Stents in Acute Myocardial Infarction) trial.
\par
Results Among 2,851 patients, 2,289 patients (80.3\%) were discharged on low-dose aspirin and 562
\par
patients (19.7\%) were discharged on high-dose aspirin. Patients discharged on high-dose rather than
\par
low-dose aspirin were more likely to have a history of hypertension, hyperlipidemia, family history of
\par
premature coronary disease, prior treatment with PCI or coronary artery bypass surgery, and to be
\par
enrolled in the United States. Patients discharged on high-dose aspirin had higher 3-year rates of
\par
major adverse cardiovascular events, reinfarction, ischemic target vessel revascularization, major
\par
bleeding, and stent thrombosis. After multivariable analysis, discharge on high-dose aspirin was an
\par
independent predictor of major bleeding (hazard ratio: 2.80; 95\% confidence interval: 1.31 to 5.99;
\par
p 0.008), but not of adverse ischemic events.
\par
Conclusions In patients with ST-segment elevation myocardial infarction undergoing primary PCI,
\par
discharge on high-dose rather than low-dose aspirin may increase the rate of major bleeding with-
\par
out providing additional ischemic benefit. (J Am Coll Cardiol Intv 2012;5:1231-- 8) \copyright{} 2012 by the
\par
