Clinical endpoints and definitions. The primary endpoints
\par
of HORIZONS-AMI have been reported (11,12). Major
\par
bleeding not related to coronary artery bypass graft (CABG)
\par
included: intracranial or intraocular hemorrhage; access site
\par
hematoma with diameter 5 cm, or one requiring inter-
\par
vention; overt bleeding with hemoglobin decrease 3 g/dl,
\par
or any hemoglobin decrease 4 g/dl; bleeding requiring
\par
reoperation; and blood transfusion. Net adverse clinical
\par
events were a composite of non-CABG--related major
\par
bleeding and major adverse cardiovascular events, including
\par
death, reinfarction, target vessel revascularization for is-
\par
chemia, and stroke. Stent thrombosis was defined as defi-
\par
nite or probable by Academic Research Consortium criteria
\par
(13). All events were adjudicated by an independent clinical
\par
events committee blinded to treatment assignment.
\par
Present analysis. For the current study, high-dose aspirin
\par
was defined as a discharge dose 200 mg daily. Low-dose
\par
aspirin was defined as a discharge dose 200 mg daily. Of
\par
the 3,602 patients enrolled in the HORIZONS-AMI trial,
\par
we excluded 81 patients due to in-hospital mortality, 95
\par
patients who were discharged on no aspirin, 177 patients
\par
discharged on no thienopyridine, and 398 patients for whom
\par
the aspirin discharge dose was uncertain. The baseline charac-
\par
teristics and out-of-hospital outcomes in the remaining 2,851
\par
patients were examined according to the prescription of high-
\par
dose versus low-dose aspirin at discharge (Fig. 1).
