\par
Statistical analyses. All analyses are by intention to treat.
\par
Categorical variables were compared using chi-square or
\par
Fisher exact test, and continuous variables were compared
\par
using the Wilcoxon rank-sum test. A propensity score was
\par
developed using a logistic regression model with high-
\par
versus low-aspirin discharge dose as a dependent variable
\par
and the following factors as independent variables: age; sex;
\par
race; enrollment in versus outside the United States; body
\par
mass index; hypertension; current smoking; diabetes; ane-
\par
mia; platelet count; white cell count; creatinine clearance;
\par
post-PCI TIMI (Thrombolysis In Myocardial Infarction)
\par
flow grade 3 versus 3 flow; pre-randomization heparin;
\par
assignment to bivalirudin versus unfractionated heparin
\par
glycoprotein IIb/IIIa inhibitor; radial versus femoral access;
\par
discharge prescription of beta blockers, angiotensin convert-
\par
ing enzyme-inhibitors or angiotensin receptor blockers, and
\par
statins; and thienopyridine discontinuation within the first 6
\par
months, 1, 2, or 3 years. A set of significant variables was
\par
selected using an entry and exit level of significance of 0.10,
\par
and a propensity score was determined. Cox proportional
\par
hazard models were used to predict time-to-event outcomes
\par
using high- versus low-aspirin discharge dose and the
\par
propensity score as covariates, and compared with the log-rank
\par
test. These models were used to determine the predicted
\par
survival rates at the actual event times in each group and to
\par
generate the adjusted time-to-event curves. All statistical anal-
\par
yses were performed with SAS (version 9.2, SAS Institute Inc.,
\par
Cary, North Carolina).
