\par
Results
\par
Patient characteristics and treatment. Among 2,851 pa-
\par
tients, 2,289 (80.3\%) were discharged on low-dose aspirin
\par
( 200 mg/day) and 562 patients (19.7\%) were discharged
\par
on high-dose aspirin ( 200 mg/day) (Fig. 1). Patients
\par
discharged on high-dose versus low-dose aspirin were more
\par
likely to have a history of hypertension, hyperlipidemia,
\par
prior PCI or CABG, family history of premature coronary
\par
disease, and to be enrolled in the United States (Table 1).
\par
Treatment assignments were similar between the 2 groups
\par
(Table 2). With respect to PCI variables, patients on
\par
high-dose compared with low-dose aspirin had more fem-
\par
oral (vs. radial) access, longer total stent length, longer
\par
fluoroscopy time, and more contrast volume, but also had
\par
shorter symptom-onset- and door-to-first-balloon inflation
\par
time. The proportion of patients who had final TIMI flow
\par
grade 3 following PCI was similar in the 2 groups. Patients
\par
maintained on high-dose aspirin were, however, less likely
\par
to have an angiotensin-converting enzyme inhibitor or
\par
angiotensin receptor blocker, or statin prescribed on dis-
\par
charge, and had a higher incidence of major bleeding before
\par
discharge. The use of Coumadin was balanced between the
\par
2 groups.
\par
Post-discharge antiplatelet therapy and clinical outcomes.
\par
From 1-year follow-up, patients on high-dose aspirin were
\par
more likely to continue with thienopyridine therapy than
\par
were patients on low-dose aspirin (Table 3). There was no
\par
significant difference in the rates of aspirin use between the
\par
2 groups during the 3-year follow-up. Patients discharged
\par
on high-dose aspirin had significantly higher rates of major
\par
adverse cardiovascular events, reinfarction, target vessel
\par
revascularization, non-CABG--related major bleeding, net
