\par
After propensity score--adjusted multivariable analysis,
\par
high-dose aspirin was an independent predictor of non-
\par
CABG--related major bleeding, but not of adverse ischemic
\par
outcomes (Table 5, Fig. 2A). The increase in major bleed-
\par
ing in patients treated with high-dose rather than low-dose
\par
aspirin emerged in the first 2 months after hospital dis-
\par
charge, although excess events continued to accrue in the
\par
high-dose aspirin group over the entire 3-year follow-up
\par
period (Fig. 2B). There was no significant interaction
\par
between enrollment within versus outside the United States
\par
and aspirin dose on adverse ischemic and bleeding end-
\par
points (Table 6). Formal interaction testing also showed no
\par
significant effect of the duration of thienopyridine use on
\par
the increased risk of major bleeding with high-dose versus
\par
low-dose aspirin (p 0.63).
\par
