Discussion
\par
In the present analysis from the HORIZONS-AMI trial,
\par
after adjustment for baseline and procedural differences,
\par
patients undergoing primary PCI for STEMI discharged on
\par
and maintained chronically on high-dose aspirin had similar
\par
long-term rates of adverse ischemic events but significantly
\par
more major bleeding than did patients on low-dose aspirin.
\par
Therefore, this analysis contributes to an increasing litera-
\par
ture that suggests that patients with ACS (both STEMI
\par
and non-STEMI) and those undergoing PCI should be
\par
maintained on low-dose rather than high-dose aspirin.
\par
Most, but not all prior studies support the results of the
\par
present analysis. Post-hoc analyses from the CURE (Clopi-
\par
dogrel in Unstable Angina To Prevent Recurrent Events)
\par
and the BRAVO (Blockade of the Glycoprotein IIb/IIIa
\par
Receptor to Avoid Vascular Occlusion) trials showed in-
\par
creased rates of bleeding rates in patients taking higher
\par
versus lower aspirin maintenance dose at long-term follow-
\par
up, without concordant reductions in their respective pri-
\par
mary study endpoint, composite ischemic events (14,15). A
\par
similar result was reported in a meta-analysis by Serebruany
\par
et al. (16). However, other meta-analyses showed no differ-
\par
ence in bleeding rates whether higher or lower aspirin
\par
maintenance doses were used (1,17). In the CURRENT-
\par
OASIS 7 (Clopidogrel and Aspirin Optimal Dose Usage to
\par
Reduce Recurrent Events-Seventh Organization to Assess
\par
Strategies in Ischemic Symptoms) trial, in which patients
\par
with ACS were randomized to high-dose (300 to 325 mg)
\par
versus low-dose (75 to 100 mg) aspirin, there were no
\par
