previously reported geographic trends in aspirin prescribing
\par
(19) and reflects discrepancies in local treatment guidelines
\par
(20--23). Notably, patients in the high-dose aspirin group
\par
also had a less favorable cardiovascular risk profile and (for
\par
unclear reasons) lower prescription rates of statins,
\par
angiotensin-converting enzyme inhibitors, and angiotensin
\par
receptor blockers. They were also more likely to continue
\par
with a thienopyridine beyond 1 year after STEMI. Again,
\par
regional patient characteristics and prescribing patterns may
\par
in part explain these differences.
\par
After adjusting for these differences, discharge aspirin
\par
dose did not affect long-term ischemic endpoints in this
\par
high-risk population, a finding that is consistent with most
\par
prior studies in which aspirin was combined with clopi-
\par
dogrel (1,14,24). However, in the PLATO (Platelet Inhi-
\par
bition and Patient Outcomes) trial (9), the lowest 1-year
\par
rates of cardiovascular death, MI, or stroke were noted in
\par
patients with ACS treated with ticagrelor and low-dose
\par
aspirin, whereas the highest rates occurred in those treated
\par
with ticagrelor and high-dose aspirin. Ischemic event rates
\par
were intermediate in patients discharged on clopidogrel and
\par
either low-dose or high-dose aspirin, with no significant
\par
differences between these groups. Thus, the PLATO results
\par
are consistent with the present analysis, in which almost all
\par
patients were discharged on clopidogrel (a few on ticlopi-
\par
dine, but none on prasugrel or ticagrelor). Whether there
\par
truly is an interaction between aspirin dose and ticagrelor
\par
deserves further study.
\par
In the current analysis, high-dose aspirin was an inde-
\par
pendent predictor of major bleeding. In contrast, in
\par
PLATO there was no significant difference in bleeding rates
\par
between patients enrolled within versus outside the United
\par
States (9), nor according to aspirin dose, with either
\par
concomitant clopidogrel or ticagrelor treatment (25). In
\par
part, this disparity may be explained by differences in patient
\par
selection criteria, bleeding definitions, or rates between the
\par
2 trials. Also, the high-dose aspirin group in PLATO was
\par
quite small, and in neither trial was aspirin dose random-
\par
ized, leading to the possibility for unmeasured confounders
\par
or chance findings. In CURRENT-OASIS 7, there was no
\par
significant difference in the secondary endpoint of 30-day
\par
major bleeding between the high- and low-dose aspirin
\par
arms in the overall cohort of 25,000 patients or in the
\par
pre-specified subgroup of patients treated with PCI (10,24).
\par
Rates of cardiovascular events were numerically lower
\par
among patients on high- versus low-dose aspirin, but this
\par
was not significant (4.2\% vs. 4.4\%, p 0.61) (10). How-
\par
ever, significant increases in major gastrointestinal bleeding
\par
and minor bleeding were observed in the higher dose aspirin
\par
group in this trial (10), adding credence to the hypothesis
\par
that high-dose aspirin indeed increases the risk of bleeding
\par
in patients with ACS and in those undergoing PCI, without
\par
providing additional protection from adverse ischemic
\par
events.
\par
Study limitations. As a post-hoc analysis, the findings from
\par
the present study are exploratory and should be considered
\par
hypothesis-generating. Data regarding changes in aspirin
\par
dose after discharge, use of enteric-coated formulations of
\par
aspirin and coprescription of nonsteroidal anti-inflammatory
\par
agents were not collected in the case report form. Even1238
