\par
\textbf{Abstract: }Energy is crucial in supporting people's daily lives and the continual quest for
\par
human development. Due to the associated complexities and uncertainties, decision makers
\par
and planners are facing increased pressure to respond more effectively to a number of
\par
energy-related issues and conflicts, as well as GHG emission mitigation within the multiple
\par
scales of energy management systems (EMSs). This quandary requires a focused effort to
\par
resolve a wide range of issues related to EMSs, as well as the associated economic and
\par
environmental implications. Effective systems analysis approaches under uncertainty to
\par
successfully address interactions, complexities, uncertainties, and changing conditions
\par
associated with EMSs is desired, which require a systematic investigation of the current
\par
studies on energy systems. Systems analysis and optimization modeling for low-carbon
\par
energy systems planning with the consideration of GHG emission reduction under
\par
uncertainty is thus comprehensively reviewed in this paper. A number of related
\par
methodologies and applications related to: (a) optimization modeling of GHG emission
\par
mitigation; (b) optimization modeling of energy systems planning under uncertainty; and
\par
(c) model-based decision support tools are examined. Perspectives of effective
\par
management schemes are investigated, demonstrating many demanding areas for enhanced
\par
research efforts, which include issues of data availability and reliability, concerns in\emph{Energies }\textbf{2011}, \emph{4}\textbf{ 1625}
\par
uncertainty, necessity of post-modeling analysis, and usefulness of development of
\par
simulation techniques.
\par
\textbf{Keywords: }optimization; GHG emission mitigation; energy systems; planning; uncertainty
\par
\textbf{1. Introduction}
\par
Energy is important in supporting people's daily lives and the continual quest for human
\par
development [1]. In the past decades, the demand for various energy resources, in both sufficient
\par
quantities and satisfactory structures, has been increasing worldwide, along with population expansion,
\par
economic development and living standard improvement. At the same time, the depletion of
\par
conventional fossil fuels, the limitations of new energy resources/technologies, as well as public
\par
concerns over energy-induced environmental issues (particularly GHG emission) have greatly
\par
weakened society's capabilities for addressing potential risks and impacts associated with our energy
\par
supply [2--5]. Although the ``energy crisis'' of the 1970s may not return soon, there is international
\par
consensus regarding the fact that energy resources can no longer be produced and consumed without
\par
addressing the issues of sustainability and a variety of associated problems. Thus, planners and decision
\par
makers are facing increased pressure to respond more effectively to a number of energy-related issues
\par
and conflicts, as well as GHG emission mitigation within multiple scales of energy management
\par
systems (EMSs). This quandary requires a focused effort to resolve a wide range of issues related to
\par
EMSs, as well as the associated economic and environmental implications. Consequently, effective
\par
planning of EMSs with the consideration of GHG emission mitigation has been a priority for
\par
energy-related and environmental professionals, as well as regulatory agencies [6,7].
\par
An EMS contains many processes such as energy exploration and exploitation, conversion and
\par
processing, production and consumption, importation and exportation, as well as the associated GHG
\par
emissions. These processes are undergoing many dramatic changes stemming from regulation
\par
implementation, regional/community development, and economic expansion, which would collectively
\par
result in significant effects on energy activities and the associated socio-economic and environmental
\par
implications [8]. In addition, the processes are generally complicated with a number of economic,
\par
technical, environmental, legislative and political factors. Such factors and their interactions are
\par
fraught with uncertainties that cannot be expressed as deterministic values or in a single format [9--11].
\par
The uncertainties have multiple dimensions and layered features, and are thus complex by nature. Such
\par
dynamics and uncertainties may lead to a variety of complexities in EMSs decision-making activities.
\par
Moreover, a number of regional and global environmental issues are closely connected with energy
\par
activities, calling for synthetic management of energy resources, activities and the resulting
\par
environmental issues. The development of effective systems analysis approaches under uncertainty to
\par
successfully address the above interactions, complexities, uncertainties, and changing conditions is
\par
desired, which require a systematic investigation of the current studies on energy systems. Therefore,
\par
in this paper, a large number of systems analysis and optimization modeling for GHG emission
\par
mitigation and energy systems planning under uncertainty will be discussed in Section 2, advanced
\par
mathematical programming methods will be presented in Section 3, and decision supporting tools\emph{Energies }\textbf{2011}, \emph{4}\textbf{ 1626}
\par
based on optimization will be discussed in Section 4, followed by a summary discussion and
\par
conclusions in Section 5.
\par
\textbf{2. Deterministic Optimization Modeling}
\par
Optimization is considered as an effective tool for identifying optimal strategies within complex
\par
management systems. Conventionally, a large number of deterministic models were used for both
\par
energy systems planning and the associated GHG emission mitigation. These methods posed solid
\par
basis for the development of inexact optimization. Particularly, due to the complexities co-existing
\par
within energy activities and GHG emission problems, many optimization models were developed in
\par
these two areas.
\par
\emph{2.1. Optimization of Energy Systems Planning}
\par
Over the recent decades, a number of optimization models were developed for aiding in the
\par
planning of EMSs under multiple scales [12--28]. The models were widely used for supporting an
\par
optimum allocation of energy resources, technologies and relevant services under one or several
\par
administrative objectives. For example, Sharma \emph{et al}. proposed a method for the optimal design of a
\par
compressed air storage and power generation system [29]. Kavrako\u glu developed a dynamic linear
\par
programming model for the planning of energy systems at a national scale [30]. Smith proposed a
\par
linear optimization model for the planning of New Zealand's energy supply and distribution
\par
system [31]. In view of the close relationships between economic development and energy
\par
consumption in Pakistan, Riaz proposed an optimization approach resulting from the joint
\par
consideration of a set of production models for five typical energy industries in this country [32]. In
\par
order to facilitate the management of energy activities within a free-market economy in a given region,
\par
a linear optimization model was proposed by Schulz and Stehfest [33]. Samouilidis \emph{et al}. made a
\par
thorough evaluation of the modelling approaches for electricity and energy systems planning [34]. It
\par
was based on two linear optimization models, including a global energy system model and an
\par
electricity generation subsystem model. Wene and Ryd\"en discussed the adoption of a linear
\par
programming model for the planning of community-scale energy systems [35]. Groscurth and K\"ummel
\par
developed a linear optimization model for evaluating industrial energy-saving potentials in several
\par
developed countries such as Germany, USA, The Netherlands and Japan [36]. Kahane made a
\par
thorough review on optimization modeling for the management of various energy systems [14]. Kaya
\par
and Keyes proposed a multi-level controlling and optimization approach to support the efficient
\par
operations of heat and power cogeneration systems [15]. Tiris \emph{et al}. developed a linear optimization
\par
model and a multi-attribute value model to coordinate long-term interactions among energy, the
\par
economy, and the environment in Turkey [37]. Arivalagan \emph{et al}. presented a mixed integer linear
\par
programming (MILP) model to identify economically optimum energy mixes in a processing
\par
industry [38]. Schoenau \emph{et al}. developed a model for identifying optimum strategies in small-scale
\par
energy systems [18]. Lehtil\"a and Piril\"a proposed a bottom-up energy system optimization model for
\par
supporting the formulation of policies related to sustainable energy utilization [39]. For optimally
\par
identifying heat and power generating strategies in an industrial factory, a MILP model was proposed
\par
by Boji\ae{} and Stojanovi\ae{} [40]. As stated by Henning, MODEST was developed based on linear\emph{Energies }\textbf{2011}, \emph{4}\textbf{ 1627}
\par
programming to minimize the capital and operating costs of energy supply and demand [41]. Heyen
\par
and Kalitventzeff proposed an optimization methodology to improve energy-utilization efficiencies in
\par
processing industries [42]. Farag \emph{et al}. attempted to develop integrated, reliable, and cost-effective
\par
approaches for identifying optimal plans and meeting future energy demands in several utilities [43].
\par
In order to identify the optimum technology and resource mixes for the design of energy-conscious
\par
buildings, a numerical multivariate optimization procedure was adopted by Peippo \emph{et al}. [19].
\par
El-Sayed proposed a linear optimization model for the management of energy systems at multiple
\par
scales [44]. In order to identify optimization solutions for energy and production in many industries,
\par
a global, multi-objective optimization methodology was proposed by Santos and Dourado [45].
\par
A genetic-algorithm based solution method was also proposed. In order to determine an economically
\par
optimal energy supply structure based on biomass, a MILP model was proposed by Nagel [46].
\par
Yokoyama and Ito proposed an optimization model for supporting the structural design of energy
\par
systems as well as their associated long-term operations [47]. Drozdz developed a linear optimization
\par
model for the management of geothermal utilization and conversion [21]. Due to the multiplicity of
\par
criteria for judging decision alternatives related to energy management, Koroneos \emph{et al}. advanced a
\par
multi-objective optimization model. It was then applied to a real-world case in Lesvos Island,
\par
Greece [48]. Ostadi \emph{et al}. developed a nonlinear programming approach for identifying optimal energy
\par
consuming patterns/schedules within a typical manufacturing factory [49]. Considering that energy
\par
consumption is a very important quality index in most of the manufacturing industries in China, an
\par
energy optimization model was developed for minimizing energy consumption based on an energy
\par
prediction model and genetic algorithm [27]. Beck \emph{et al}. proposed a modeling approach for supporting
\par
the optimal planning of energy networks through combining global optimization and agent-based
\par
modeling tools [50]. The approach was demonstrated through a case study of regional electricity
\par
generation management in South Africa. Bujak proposed a mathematical model to determine optimal
\par
energy consumption quotas for a set of boilers [51]. The model was then applied to multiple-scale steam
\par
systems that had a group of liquid- or gas-fired shell boilers.
\par
A number of large-scale modeling systems were also developed and then applied to many locations
\par
across the World. For instance, the Brookhaven Energy System Optimization Model (BESOM) was
\par
used to identify the optimal mixing patterns of energy resources, technologies and investments in
\par
accordance with the minimum system cost [35,52--54]. The Time-stepped Energy System Optimization
\par
Model (TESOM) was proposed as a consecutive BESOM-type optimization modeling system for
\par
supporting energy management [55]. The Market Allocation Model (MARKAL) was developed as a
\par
large-scale, technology-oriented energy-activity analysis model [5,56--65]. Multiple Energy System of
\par
Australia (MENSA) was developed to identify the optimal combinations of demand- and supply-side
\par
technologies with the objective of the lowest economic cost [66--68]. The Energy Flow Optimization
\par
Model (EFOM) was established as an engineering-oriented bottom-up model for energy management
\par
systems planning and was widely used in European countries [54,69--76]. There were also a number of
\par
software packages, such as Long-range Energy Alternatives Planning System (LEAP), New Earth 21
\par
Model (NE21), National Energy Modeling System (NEMS) and Energy 2020, which were developed
\par
to evaluate environmental and economic effects of energy activities [77--84]. Papagiannis \emph{et al}. applied
\par
LEAP2006 to a number of European countries [85]. Based on MARKAL, Jiang \emph{et al}. estimated future
\par
consumptions of natural gas in three Chinese cities: Beijing, Guangzhou and Shanghai [86]. Nguyen\emph{Energies }\textbf{2011}, \emph{4}\textbf{ 1628}
\par
examined the economic and environmental impacts of power production and capacity expansions in
\par
Vietnam [87,88]. The production of synthetic natural gas from wood in a Swiss methanation plant was
\par
effectively assessed through the adoption of MARKAL by Schulz \emph{et al}. [89]. In Japan, Endo employed
\par
MARKAL to analyze the market penetration of fuel cell vehicles [90]. The MARKAL model was also
\par
successfully applied to a number of countries and regions for identifying sustainable development
\par
strategies and policies in South Africa, India, US, and European countries [60,91--119]. Silversides
\par
investigated biomass consumption patterns in a number of provinces of Canada, and evaluated their
\par
effects on forest management practices [120]. Huang \emph{et al}. comprehensively investigated public policy
\par
discourse, energy systems planning methods, as well as relevant measures towards sustainable energy
\par
development in Canada [121]. In order to maximize bio-energy production from Lake Winnipeg,
\par
Cicek \emph{et al}. evaluated various policies and technologies related to biomass harvesting in
\par
Manitoba [122]. Liebig \emph{et al}. estimated GHG contributions and the mitigation potentials of various
\par
agricultural practices in several province of western Canada [123]. For examining the effects of
\par
fertilizer management on external energy inputs and GHG emissions, a grazing experiment was
\par
conducted in Brandon, Manitoba [124].
\par
\emph{2.2. Optimization of GHG Emission Mitigation}
\par
At the same time, a number of optimization modeling methods were proposed for supporting
\par
the management of GHG emission mitigation particularly through the adoption of renewable
\par
energies [54,125--143]. In the middle of the 1970s, Duff (1975) presented an optimization model to
\par
design solar thermal energy systems in accordance with the minimum system cost [144]. Leledakis \emph{et al}.
\par
evaluated the potential distribution of renewable energy resources for reducing GHG emissions in the
\par
region of the Cyclades, a Greek island group in the Aegean Sea [145]. Alidi (1988) argued that the
\par
utilization of wood residues as a renewable energy source would probably be limited by two major
\par
problems (\emph{i.e}., (a) the high cost of transportation due to their high volume with respect to their energy
\par
content, and (b) the stochastic generation of these materials) and would not definitely lead to GHG
\par
emission reduction [146]. In order to further support the management of wood residues as an energy
\par
source, he proposed a dynamic optimization model. As an effective method for investigating the role of
\par
renewable energy resources for mitigating GHG emissions within many EMSs in Australia, the
\par
so-called Australian Energy Policy System Optimization Model (AEPSOM) was used by Islam [125].
\par
Bose and Anandalingam presented a goal programming model to reflect multiple objectives of
\par
sustainable energy development, which was then applied to the city of Delhi, India [147]. Lehtil\"a and
\par
Piril\"a used an optimization model to support energy-policy formulation for GHG emission mitigation
\par
in Finland [39]. Martins \emph{et al}. developed a multi-objective, linear programming model for power
\par
generation planning and demand management to reduce GHG emissions [148]. Watson and Ter-Gazarian
\par
presented an optimization system to study operational and economic impacts for incorporating
\par
renewable power source utilization and GHG emission reduction within a large-scale electricity
\par
grid [149]. Badin and Tagore proposed an analysis framework to evaluate costs and impacts that might
\par
result from the incremental production, storage, transport, and use of different fuels or energy carriers
\par
that could reduce GHG emissions [150]. \"Ozelkan \emph{et al}. proposed a linear quadratic programming
\par
model for addressing aggregated multi-criteria decision making problems in order to maximize\emph{Energies }\textbf{2011}, \emph{4}\textbf{ 1629}
\par
hydroelectric power generation and to minimize GHG emissions [151]. Gopalakrishnan \emph{et al}. proposed
\par
an optimization model for the management of stand-alone energy systems that consisted of windmills,
\par
diesel generators and power storage equipment [152]. In order to dramatically reduce the costs of
\par
energy storage within hybrid energy-storage systems, a time-dependent model of a stand-alone, solar
\par
powered, battery-hydrogen hybrid energy storage system was developed by Vosen and Keller [153].
\par
Based on a bottom-up procedure, an energy-module network, and a dynamic programming method,
\par
Boji\ae{} proposed an optimization modeling system for supporting the management of renewable
\par
energies and reduce the usage of high-carbon fuels [154]. Suganthi and Williams developed an
\par
optimization model to determine optimum allocation of renewable energies among a number of
\par
end-users in India [155]. Iniyan and Sumathy developed an Optimal Renewable Energy Mathematical
\par
(OREM) model for renewable energy source utilization and GHG emission mitigation in India [156].
\par
Kong \emph{et al}. adopted a linear programming model to deal with planning problems of the combined
\par
cooling, heating and power production systems for minimizing the associated cost and GHG
\par
emissions [157]. Chinese \emph{et al}. proposed a nonlinear optimization approach for assessing technical and
\par
economic feasibility of various renewable-energy-based GHG reduction schemes in northeastern
\par
Italy [24]. K\'elouwani \emph{et al}. developed a dynamic simulation-optimization model for supporting the
\par
management of stand-alone renewable energy systems with hydrogen storage equipments
\par
(RESHS) [158]. In order to investigate efficient ways for green electricity and heat supply in rural
\par
Japan, Nakata \emph{et al}. developed an optimization modeling system [159]. In order to identify the desired
\par
renewable energy options for electricity generation, Dudhani \emph{et al}. proposed an optimization allocation
\par
model. The model was formulated based on linear programming [160]. Dufo-L\'opez \emph{et al}. presented a
\par
genetic-algorithm-based optimization model for supporting the management of a hybrid renewable
