\par
\textbf{3. Inexact Optimization Modeling}
\par
Optimization modeling was often based on a number of mathematical equations to represent a
\par
series of interactions between relevant system components, processes, and factors. In most of the
\par
conventional methods, the modeling parameters/coefficients were usually specified as deterministic.
\par
However, in realistic energy management systems, many parameters/coefficients as well as the
\par
irrelations may have uncertain natures with multiple dimensions and layers [9,10,179--183]. Over the
\par
past decades, the most common approaches for dealing with uncertainties in optimization modeling
\par
included interval, stochastic, and fuzzy-set-based methods as well as their hybrids [9--11,184--188].
\par
\emph{3.1. Fuzzy Mathematical Programming}
\par
Fuzzy mathematical programming (FMP) was derived through the incorporation of fuzzy set theory
\par
within conventional mathematical programming frameworks [189]. Normally, the FMP methods could
\par
be classified into two major categories: fuzzy flexible programming (FFP) and fuzzy parameter
\par
programming (FPP) [190]. In FFP, the flexibility in the constraints and fuzziness in the system
\par
objective, which were presented by fuzzy sets and denoted as ``fuzzy constraints and goals'', were
\par
introduced into the conventional optimization models [191,192]; in FPP, fuzzy parameters were
\par
introduced into mathematical programming frameworks that could then be used to formulate various
\par
intermediate models based on the detailed specifications and analyses of the specific problem. The
\par
uncertain parameters were represented as fuzzy regions where they possibly lie and were regarded as
\par
possibility distributions [193]. A general linear programming model can be presented as follows:
