According to Bellman and Zadeh, Negoita \emph{et al}., Dubois and Prade, and Inuiguchi \emph{et al}., FMP could
\par
deal with the following three types of uncertainties: vagueness, ambiguity, as well as their combination
\par
(\emph{i.e}., FMP with both vagueness and ambiguity) [194--197]. Moreover, there were many extensions of
\par
fuzzy set theory to other mathematical programming problems, leading to a number of hybrids, such as
\par
fuzzy linear programming, fuzzy robust programming, fuzzy dynamic programming, fuzzy multi-objective
\par
programming, fuzzy nonlinear programming, and fuzzy (stochastic) linear programming. Fuzzy linear
\par
programming (FLP) is a typical FMP method. Based on these method, there were a large number of
\par
applications to environmental and energy management and planning [198--206]. For example, Chen
\par
and Sheen introduced a generalized fuzzy mathematical model for energy-load management [207].
\par
Kagan and Adams addressed the problems of electrical power distribution systems by planning under
\par
uncertainty through the formulation of a fuzzy mathematical model [208]. In order to examine the
\par
potentials energy savings and emission reduction for municipal energy systems, a set of three
\par
optimization models with high spatial and temporal disaggregation were adopted by Groscurth and
\par
Kress [209]. Borges and Antunes presented a fuzzy multi-objective linear programming approach for
\par
tackling the uncertainty and imprecision associated with the coefficients of an input-output
\par
energy-economy planning model [203]. Mavrotas \emph{et al}. presented a fuzzy linear programming model,
\par
including both continuous and integer variables (which represent energy flows and discrete energy
\par
technologies, respectively) for supporting the planning of energy resources in buildings [204].
\par
Mavrotas \emph{et al}. proposed an optimization model to address uncertain parameters in the objective\emph{Energies }\textbf{2011}, \emph{4}\textbf{ 1633}
\par
function by introducing Triangular Fuzzy Numbers (TFN) into a general MILP modeling
\par
framework [210]. Sadeghiand Hosseini attempted to use FLP for the optimal management of the
\par
energy supply systems in Iran [76]. Mazur proposed a fuzzy nonlinear programming model in which a
\par
number of fuzzy constraints could be considered [211]. Nguene and Finger proposed an optimization
\par
modeling methodology based on fuzzy set theory and strategic choices for enabling decision makers to
\par
evaluate and identify optimal policies for energy allocations over different time horizons [212].
\par
Martinsen and Krey introduced a series of fuzzy constraints to identify optimal strategies and
\par
compromising policies related to energy management by considering a number of contradictory targets
\par
such as minimized economic cost, minimized environmental impacts, and maximized energy-supply
\par
safety [213]. Bitar \emph{et al}. used a fuzzy multi-objective mathematical programming model to analyze the
\par
tradoffs between the variations of system costs, the emissions of CO2, and the number of potential
\par
jobs, as well as the expansion options of thermoelectric power in a specific region [214].
\par
\emph{3.2. Stochastic Mathematical Programming}
\par
Stochastic mathematical programming (SMP) was developed by Beale [215]. Normally,
\par
SMP can be viewed as an extension of mathematical programming to decision problems whose
\par
coefficients/parameters are not known but can be represented as chances or probabilities. According to
\par
Li, the inherent uncertainties in a decision can manifest themselves throughout the model as stochastic
\par
elements in the constraint matrix, the RHS stipulations, and/or the objective coefficients [189]. The main
\par
advantage of SMP methods is that they do not simply reduce the complexity of programming
\par
problems; instead, they allow decision makers to have a complete view of the effects of uncertainties
\par
and the relationships between uncertain inputs and the resulting solutions [216--218]. Typically, SMP
\par
models can be replaced by suitable deterministic versions (named deterministic equivalents) and then
\par
solutions of the deterministic models can be extended to represent the stochastic solutions [219].
\par
Among them, chance-constrained programming (CCP) was often used for reflecting the reliability of
\par
satisfying (or risks of violating) system constraints under uncertainty. The CCP methods do not require
\par
that all of the constraints be totally satisfied. Instead, they can be satisfied in a proportion of cases
\par
under given probabilities [220]. Also, the CCP methods are effective in dealing with uncertainties in
\par
elements of RHSs when their probability distributions are available [221]. CCP programming was first
\par
formulated by Charnes \emph{et al}., and, thereafter, issues of CCP were widely investigated [222--236]. Also,
\par
two-stage stochastic programming and chance-constrained programming are the major two methods.
\par
Two-stage stochastic programming (TSP) is effective for optimization problems in which an analysis
\par
of policy scenarios is desired and the related data are mostly uncertain. Generally, when uncertainties
\par
of some elements in the right-hand sides (B) can be expressed as probabilistic distributions,
\par
chance-constrained programming (CCP) can be used to deal with them. In terms of uncertainties in B,
\par
consider a general stochastic linear programming (SLP) problem as follows:
