In the planning of EMSs under multiple scales, a number of methods based on SMP (particularly
\par
the TSP and CCP methods) were developed [237--240]. Craig \emph{et al}. analyzed optimal investment
\par
strategies for energy conservation with the consideration of probability distributions for various energy
\par
prices [241]. Pena-Taveras and Cambel developed a stochastic nonlinear model for supporting optimal
\par
energy system design through the adoption of the Schumpeter Clock Model (SCM) and the Ising
\par
Model (IM) [242]. Bakirtzis and Gavanidou employed a stochastic dynamic programming method for
\par
facilitating the optimal management of power generation and battery storage in a small autonomous
\par
system with both conventional and unconventional energy resources [243]. Goumas \emph{et al}. adopted
\par
several computational methods to provide a rigorous analysis for improving the decision-making
\par
process related to the optimum exploitation of geothermal energy resources [244]. Gamou \emph{et al}.
\par
proposed an optimal unit-sizing method for energy cogeneration systems planning through employing
\par
continuous random variables for describing energy demands [245]. Falc\~ao presented an optimization
\par
model for allocating mechanical and electrical equipment within an oscillating-water-column (OWC)
\par
wave power plant [246]. Beraldi \emph{et al}. proposed a two-stage stochastic integer programming model for\emph{Energies }\textbf{2011}, \emph{4}\textbf{ 1635}
\par
the integrated optimization of power generation and trading [247]. Krey \emph{et al}. used a stochastic
\par
programming technique to explicitly consider uncertainties associated with energy prices [248].
\par
A two-stage stochastic programming model for short- or mid-term cost-optimal electric power
\par
production planning was developed by N\"urnberg and R\"omisch [249]. Krukanont and Tezuka proposed
\par
a TSP optimization model for supporting energy allocation in Japan [240]. Beraldi \emph{et al}. proposed a
\par
two-stage stochastic integer programming model for integrated optimal management of power
\par
production and trading in order to reduce power shortage risks [247]. Kim \emph{et al}. developed a TSP
\par
model for optimal designing hydrogen supply chains, which consisted of various activities such as
\par
production, storage and transportation [250]. Yang \emph{et al}. proposed a new method for optimal
\par
transmission system expansion planning based on CCP with the consideration of several uncertain
\par
factors such as the locations and capacities of new power plants as well as demand growth [251].
\par
Arun \emph{et al}. adopted a CCP approach for incorporating uncertain energy resources into a facility sizing
\par
and design system [252]. Based on CCP, Held \emph{et al}. explored many effective policies on the utilization
\par
of fossil fuel and renewable energy resources in terms of economic investments [253].
\par
\emph{3.3. Interval Mathematical Programming}
\par
As an alternative approach to deal with uncertainty, interval analysis was advanced by Moore [254].
\par
In interval analysis, the only information that is available for an inexact parameter is the lower and
\par
upper bounds that are insufficient for creating distribution or membership functions. Interval analysis
\par
was then extended into interval mathematical programming (IMP) (Huang \emph{et al}., [255]). According to
\par
Huang \emph{et al}. [256,257], the IMP improved upon the existing optimization methods with the following
\par
features: (a) it allowed uncertainties to be directly communicated into the optimization and solution
\par
processes;(b) it did not lead to more complicated intermediate models, and thus had relatively low
\par
computational requirements; and (c) it did not require distributional or membership information for
\par
model parameters since interval numbers were acceptable as uncertain inputs, which was particularly
\par
meaningful for practical applications because it was typically much more difficult for
\par
planners/engineers to specify a distribution than to define a fluctuation interval. According to
\par
Huang \emph{et al}. [216--218], a general IMP model can be written as follows:
