Over the past decades, a number of methods were developed based on IMP. For instance, Jansson
\par
developed a self-validating method for solving linear programming (LP) problems with interval input
\par
data [258]. Urli and Nadean proposed interactive approaches for solving multi-objective linear
\par
programming problems with interval coefficients [259]. Matloka investigated the generalization of
\par
inexact LP methods and provided a relevant solution algorithm [260]. Xia and Zhang introduced
\par
several methods for solving interval nonlinear programming problems and applied them to water
\par
pollution control planning [261]. Huang and Baetz proposed an inexact quadratic programming (IQP)
\par
method through the introduction of interval numbers into a quadratic programming framework [256,257].
\par
Yeh proposed several inexact linear and quadratic programming models for planning water resource
\par
management systems [262]. Sugimoto \emph{et al}. advanced a parallel relaxation method for handling
\par
quadratic programming models with interval constraints [263]. Huang and his colleagues proposed
\par
several interval-parameter mathematical programming methods as a special group of IMP, and applied
\par
them to a number of environmental and energy decision analysis problems in Canada, the US, Japan,\emph{Energies }\textbf{2011}, \emph{4}\textbf{ 1637}
\par
Taiwan, and China [255,264--267]. Bass \emph{et al}. presented an inexact multi-objective programming
\par
method for the planning of climate change adaptation within a water resources management
\par
system [268]. Chi proposed an interval-parameter mixed integer linear programming (IMILP) model
\par
for the planning of waste diversion in the city of Regina [269]. Liu developed an inexact
\par
chance-constrained mixed-integer linear programming (ICCMILP) approach for the planning and
\par
management of a regional energy-environmental system [270]. Huang \emph{et al. }developed an
\par
interval-parameter fuzzy stochastic programming for environmental management [256,257]. Chen and
\par
Wu presented an interval optimization method for the dynamic response of structures with interval
\par
parameters [23]. Noureldin and Hasan examined new opportunities for energy saving inside a
\par
Bisphenol-A (BPA) plant through optimal selection of process operating temperatures [271].
\par
\textbf{4. Model-Based Decision Support Tools}
\par
There are many potential decision alternatives for the planning of multi-scale EMSs under a variety
\par
of changing conditions. Decision makers must systematically evaluate economic and environmental
\par
performance of energy technologies, resources and services and choose a desired plan for each
\par
sub-sector of EMSs. To facilitate this decision-making process, expert systems and decision support
\par
tools can be employed based on scientific modeling tools. In the past decades, a number of
\par
research efforts have been directed toward developing such systems for energy management systems
\par
planning [203,272--280]. For assisting in decision making of an advisory council in identifying areas of
\par
interest for energy research and development, Lootsma \emph{et al}. employed a number of multi-criteria
\par
decision analysis methods to develop an expert system [281]. Harhammer and Infanger developed a
\par
DSS for operation planning (DSS-OP) to assist decision makers in the planning of multiple scales of
\par
energy systems [282]. In order to provide interference analysis for the development of geothermal
\par
energy in Mexico, Arellano \emph{et al}. developed a computerized expert system (ANAPPRES V1.0) [283].
\par
Liu \emph{et al}. described a computer-based DSS for evaluating quality of life improvement, as well as
\par
technological and environmental impacts of energy planning and consumption [16]. Robin \emph{et al}.
\par
proposed a computer-aided DSS for supporting thermal design of buildings [284]. Clarke and Grant
\par
adopted a computer-based DSS (EnTrack) for supporting the development of renewable energy
\par
resources at a regional level [285]. Georgopoulou \emph{et al}. presented a DSS for assisting decision makers
\par
in the promotion of renewable energy resource utilization [286]. In order to support the analysis of
\par
energy consumption in various buildings, Kim and Degelman developed a computer-aided interface
\par
system [287]. As an effective decision-support tool for energy systems planning, a set of computerized
\par
decision-support software was developed by Rylatt \emph{et al}. [288]. A specialized group DSS was designed
\par
by van Groenendaal for providing long-term support related to energy policy formulation on the island
\par
of Java, Indonesia [289]. Freppaz \emph{et al}. proposed a DSS for supporting forest biomass exploitation and
\par
renewable energy production [290]. When planning bioenergy production from biomass, in order to
\par
take into account opinions from multiple groups of stakeholders (e.g., biomass resources suppliers, and
\par
transportation, conversion and electricity suppliers), a two-level general bioenergy decision system
\par
(gBEDS) was developed by Ayoub \emph{et al}. [291]. In order to identify the most appropriate set of energy
\par
options for providing sufficient power to fulfill local demands and improve rural livelihoods,
\par
Cherni \emph{et al}. developed a new multi-criteria DSS [292]. Yue and Yang established a DSS for\emph{Energies }\textbf{2011}, \emph{4}\textbf{ 1638}
\par
strengthening the utilization of renewable energy resources and meeting new international
\par
environmental requirements and providing self-sufficient domestic energy supplies in Taiwan [293].
\par
Blanco \emph{et al}. developed a DSS for the planning of micro-hydro power plants in the Amazon region
\par
under a sustainable development perspective [294]. Panichelli and Gnansounou presented a GIS-based
\par
DSS for supporting the selection of the least-cost bioenergy utilization patterns when there was
\par
significant variability in biomass farmgate price and when more than one bioenergy plant with a fixed
\par
capacity needs to be placed in a region [295]. In order to enhance the utilization of renewable energy
\par
sources, an information decision system was developed by Patlitzianas \emph{et al}. based on an expert
\par
subsystem and a multi-criteria decision making subsystem [296]. Aydin \emph{et al}. developed a GIS-based
\par
DSS for site selection of wind turbines in Turkey [297]. Frombo \emph{et al}. developed a GIS-based
\par
environmental decision support system (EDSS) for optimal planning of forest biomass
\par
utilization [298]. Vainio \emph{et al}. proposed a GIS-based DSS for wood procurement management in order
\par
to identify wood harvesting alternatives [299]. Several studies were also conducted on both
\par
environmental and energy management [300--318].
\par
\textbf{5. Discussions and Conclusions}
\par
As extensions of deterministic optimization approaches, a number of inexact ones have been
\par
proposed for dealing with uncertainties associated with energy systems planning and GHG emission
\par
mitigation. These methods can be broadly categorized into fuzzy mathematical programming (FMP),
\par
stochastic mathematical programming (SMP), and interval mathematical programming (IMP). Each of
\par
these methods could deal with an individual type of uncertainties that can be expressed as fuzzy sets,
\par
probability density function, and intervals. For FMP-based methods, they are effective in address
\par
uncertain information that can be expressed as fuzzy sets in both objective functions and system
\par
constraints. However, there are quite a number of improvements that could be made upon FMP. Firstly,
\par
many methods could be used for determining fuzzy membership functions that might be used in the
\par
optimization system (mainly for FPP). These methods are mainly subject to a certain degree of
\par
subjectivity. Secondly, for both FFP and FPP, the solution procedures could lead to complicated
\par
inter-medium processes. Thirdly, for most of the FPP methods, the increased computational efforts
\par
may also lead to system infeasibility. Fourthly, most of the FMP methods could only deal with a
\par
specific type of function membership (e.g., triangle forms). At the same time, in terms of SMP-based
\par
methods, there are effective in handling uncertainties that can be expressed as probability density
\par
functions. However, they are also subject to several disadvantages. Firstly, in practical problems it is
\par
difficult in establishing probabilistic distributions of relevant parameters. Secondly, most of these
\par
methods are mainly proposed based on assumptions that distributions of relevant parameters are fixed
\par
and can be identified. This might lead to system infeasibility when the parameters are dynamic and are
\par
associated with intensive variations. Thirdly, the computation requirements of stochastic mathematic
\par
programming are relatively high especially for large-scale practical problems. Moreover, regarding
\par
IMP-based methods, they could easily deal with uncertainties that can be expressed as intervals.
\par
However, they are associated with the following three shortcomings. Firstly, since most of the
\par
parameters in IMP are expressed as intervals without any distribution information, it might lead to
\par
simplification of real-world problem when some of the parameters could be expressed as probabilistic\emph{Energies }\textbf{2011}, \emph{4}\textbf{ 1639}
\par
and/or possibilistic distributions. Secondly, due to the adoption of inter-medium submodels, solution
\par
feasibility could sometimes be an inherent problem for large-scale real-world cases. Thirdly, when
\par
ranges of the intervals are broad, the optimization model could be infeasible. Accordingly, there are
\par
still a number of areas that need further studies, including: (a) studies that could incorporate multiple
\par
technologies, multiple resources, multiple sectors, as well as their complex interactions within a
\par
general modeling framework, particularly those could be used for supporting the planning of
\par
renewable energy management systems; (b) efforts in systematically studying multiple formats of
\par
uncertainties (e.g., intervals, fuzzy membership functions, probability distributions, as well as their
\par
combinations) in a flexible manner; (c) studies that could effectively deal with issues of diverse data
\par
availability and reliability. When sufficient information is available to represent the uncertainties as
\par
probability distributions but an interval method is used, valuable probabilistic information will be lost.
\par
On the contrary, when a stochastic method is used without sufficient information support, detailed
\par
probabilistic distributions might be generated based on unrealistic assumptions, resulting in errors in
\par
modeling inputs. In general, such manipulation in uncertain modeling inputs would result in
\par
considerable effects on modeling results, reducing the system robustness, and (d) studies that could
\par
incorporate post-modeling analysis, and energy simulation/prediction models to improve applicability
\par
and robustness of the obtained solutions.
