Abstract
\par
Purpose -- The purpose of this paper is to examine the effect of initial trust on mobile banking user
\par
adoption.
\par
Design/methodology/approach -- Based on the valid responses collected from a survey
\par
questionnaire, structural equation modeling (SEM) technology was employed to examine the
\par
research model.
\par
Findings -- The results indicate that structural assurance and information quality are the main
\par
factors affecting initial trust, whereas information quality and system quality significantly affect
\par
perceived usefulness. Initial trust affects perceived usefulness, and both factors predict the usage
\par
intention of mobile banking.
\par
Research limitations/implications -- The sample was mainly composed of users having rich
\par
mobile Internet experience, which may affect their trust in mobile banking. Future research needs to
\par
generalize these results to other samples, such as those users without much mobile Internet experience.
\par
Originality/value -- Extant research has mainly adopted information technology adoption theories
\par
such as TAM to explain mobile user behavior, and has seldom examined the effect of initial trust on
\par
mobile banking user adoption. However, the high perceived risk and low switching cost highlight the
\par
necessity to build initial trust to facilitate user behavior. This research fills the gap.
\par
Keywords Initial trust, Mobile banking, Information quality, System quality, Trust, Banking
\par
Paper type Research paper
