Introduction
\par
The application of third generation (3G) mobile communication technologies has
\par
triggered the rapid development of mobile commerce. A report issued by China Internet
\par
Network Information Center (CNNIC) indicates that the number of mobile Internet users
\par
has exceeded 303 million, accounting for 66 percent of the Internet population (457
\par
million) (CNNIC, 2011). In the United States, about 59 percent of the adult people go
\par
online wirelessly (PewInternet, 2010). A variety of mobile services such as mobile instant
\par
messaging, mobile search and mobile music have been very popular among users. These
\par
services are mainly related to communication, information and entertainment
\par
application. However, mobile transaction services such as mobile banking have been
\par
adopted by a minority of users. For example, only 7.1 percent of mobile users have ever
\par
used mobile banking (CNNIC, 2011). Many traditional banks such as the Industrialized
\par
and Commercial Bank of China (ICBC), which is the largest commercial bank in China,
\par
have released their mobile banking services. In order to achieve success, a prerequisite is
\par
to facilitate user adoption and usage of mobile banking.
\par
This work was partially supported by a grant from the National Natural Science Foundation of
\par
China (71001030), and a grant from the Zhejiang Provincial Natural Science Foundation
\par
(Y7100057).
\par
