\par
Mobile banking means that users adopt mobile terminals to conduct payment such
\par
as balance enquiry, transference and bill payment at anytime from anywhere
\par
(Dahlberg et al., 2008). Mobile banking frees users from spatial and temporal
\par
limitations, and enables them to conduct ubiquitous payment. This provides great
\par
convenience to users. However, due to the virtuality and lack of control, mobile
\par
banking involves great uncertainty and risk. Thus users need to build trust in order to
\par
adopt and use mobile banking. Initial trust develops when users interact with mobile
\par
banking for the first time (McKnight et al., 2002a). Establishing users' initial trust is
\par
critical for mobile service providers. On one hand, due to the lack of previous
\par
experience, users will perceive great uncertainty and risk when they adopt mobile
\par
banking for the first time. They need to build initial trust to overcome perceived risk.
\par
On the other hand, the switching cost is low. Users may switch back to online banking
\par
if they cannot build initial trust in mobile banking. Thus mobile service providers need
\par
to engender users' initial trust to acquire and retain them.
\par
Extant research has used information technology adoption theories such as technology
\par
acceptance model (TAM) (Kim et al., 2010; Schierz et al., 2010), innovation diffusion theory
\par
(IDT) (Mallat, 2007), and the unified theory of acceptance and use of technology (UTAUT)
\par
(Luo et al., 2010) to examine mobile banking user behavior. Perceived usefulness, relative
\par
advantage and performance expectancy are found to affect user adoption of mobile
\par
banking. However, the effect of initial trust on mobile banking user behavior has seldom
\par
been examined. As noted earlier, the high perceived risk and low switching cost highlight
\par
the necessity to build users' initial trust in order to facilitate their adoption and usage of
\par
mobile banking. The purpose of this research is to identify the factors affecting initial
\par
trust in mobile banking. We draw on two factors including information quality and
\par
system quality from information system success model. In addition, structural assurance
\par
and trust propensity are also included as the determinants of initial trust.
\par
The rest of the paper is organized as follows. We review related literature in the next
\par
section. Section three presents the research model and hypotheses. Section four reports
\par
instrument development and data collection. We present data analysis and results in
\par
section five, followed by a discussion of these results in section six. Section seven
\par
presents the theoretical and managerial implications. We summarize the limitations
\par
and conclude the paper in section eight.
\par
Literature review
\par
Initial trust
\par
Due to the great uncertainty and risk involved in online transactions, trust has received
\par
considerable attention in the electronic commerce context. Trust has been found to
\par
affect user adoption of various services, such as online news services (Chen and
\par
Corkindale, 2008), Internet banking (Flavian et al., 2005), health web sites (Fisher et al.,
\par
2008), and mobile shopping (Lu and Su, 2009). Trust includes initial trust and
\par
continuance trust. As the first stage of trust development, initial trust is significant for
\par
user behavior and various factors have been identified to affect initial trust. The first
\par
category of factors is associated with website. Due to the lack of previous experience,
\par
users will rely on their perception of website quality to build initial trust (Lowry et al.,
\par
2008). In addition, information quality has been found to affect initial trust in health
\par
infomediaries (Zahedi and Song, 2008). Other factors such as website appeal and
\par
usability also affect online consumers' initial trust (Hampton-Sosa and Koufaris, 2005).Downloaded by Universitas International Batam, User UIB Batam At 03:13 10 December 2015 (PT)
\par
The second category of factors is associated with the consumer. Trust propensity has a
\par
significant effect on initial trust (Li et al., 2008), although this effect may gradually
\par
diminish with increased experience (Zahedi and Song, 2008). The third category of
\par
factors is associated with company. Reputation, company size and corporate image act
\par
as trust signals and will affect consumers' initial trust (Flavian et al., 2005; Chen and
\par
Barnes, 2007; Fuller et al., 2007). The fourth category of factors is associated with third
\par
parties. Consumers may transfer their trust in third parties to online vendors. Web
\par
assurance seals such as VeriSign and TRUSTe are found to affect initial trust (Hu et al.,
\par
2010). Brand association and portal affiliation also affect online consumers' initial trust
\par
(Delgado-Ballester and Hernandez-Espallardo, 2008; Sia et al., 2009).
\par
Similar to online transactions, mobile commerce also involves great uncertainty and
\par
risk. Thus trust is critical to facilitating mobile user behavior. However, compared to
\par
the abundant research on online trust, there exists less research on mobile trust. Siau
\par
and Shen (2003) divided mobile trust into initial trust and continuous trust, both of
\par
which are affected by the factors related to mobile vendor and technology. Li and Yeh
\par
(2010) noted that design aesthetics affect mobile trust through perceived usefulness,
\par
perceived ease of use and customization. Lee (2005) examined the effect of mobile
\par
interactivity on user trust. Mobile interactivity includes user control, responsiveness,
\par
personalization, connectedness, ubiquitous connectivity and contextual offer. Chandra
\par
et al. (2010) reported the effect of trust on user adoption of mobile payment systems.
\par
Mobile banking adoption
\par
As an emerging service, mobile banking has not been widely adopted by users. Thus
\par
researchers have paid attention to identify the factors affecting user adoption.
\par
Information technology adoption theories such as TAM, IDT and UTAUT are often used
\par
as the theoretical bases. Gu et al. (2009) found that structural assurance and perceived
\par
ease of use affect trust in mobile banking. Lin (2011) drew on IDT and trust theory to
\par
examine the effects of innovation attributes and knowledge-based trust on mobile
\par
banking adoption. Innovation attributes include relative advantage, compatibility and
\par
perceived ease of use. Knowledge-based trust includes perceived competence,
\par
benevolence and integrity. Kim et al. (2009) reported that structural assurance, relative
\par
benefits and personal propensity to trust affect initial trust in mobile banking. Zhou et al.
\par
(2010) integrated UTAUT and task technology fit theory to examine user adoption of
\par
mobile banking. Luo et al. (2010) found that performance expectancy and perceived risk
\par
have significant effects on the intention to use mobile banking services.
\par
Research model and hypotheses
\par
Information quality
\par
As noted earlier, due to the lack of direct experience, users need to rely on their own
\par
perceptions such as information quality and system quality to form their initial trust in
\par
mobile banking. Both information quality and system quality are the factors affecting
\par
information system success (DeLone and McLean, 2003). Information quality reflects
\par
information relevancy, accuracy and timeliness (Kim et al., 2004). Users expect to access
\par
mobile banking to acquire their payment information at anytime from anywhere. If the
\par
information provided to them is irrelevant, inaccurate and out-of-date, users may doubt
\par
whether mobile service providers have enough ability and benevolence to provide
\par
quality services. Thus information quality will affect initial trust. For example, if mobile
