\par
decrease users' trust in mobile banking. In addition, information quality may also affect
\par
perceived utility of mobile banking. Users rely on quality information to conduct
\par
ubiquitous payment, which improves their living and working performance and
\par
effectiveness. In contrast, low quality information will decrease users' perceived utility of
\par
mobile banking. Prior studies have noted the effect of information quality on initial trust
\par
in health infomediaries (Zahedi and Song, 2008). Information quality also affects
\par
perceived usefulness of data warehousing software (Wixom and Todd, 2005). Thus:
\par
H1. Information quality positively affects initial trust.
\par
H2. Information quality positively affects perceived usefulness.
\par
System quality
\par
System quality reflects the access speed, ease-of-use, navigation and appearance of
\par
mobile banking (Kim et al., 2004). Due to the constraints of mobile terminals such as
\par
small screens and inconvenient input, users may find it difficult to search for information
\par
with mobile banking. Thus an interface with powerful navigation, clear layout and
\par
prompt responses may be critical to using mobile banking. Poor system quality may lead
\par
users to feel that service providers have not spent enough effort and investment on
\par
mobile banking. This will affect their evaluation on the credibility and benevolence of
\par
service providers. Vance et al. (2008) reported that system quality including navigational
\par
structure and visual appeal affects users' trust in mobile commerce technologies. In
\par
addition, system quality may also affect perceived usefulness. Poor system quality will
\par
decrease user expectation of acquiring positive outcomes in future. For example, if users
\par
often encounter service interruption or unavailability, they will not be able to conduct
\par
ubiquitous payment. This may lower their perception of mobile banking utility:
\par
H3. System quality positively affects initial trust.
\par
H4. System quality positively affects perceived usefulness.
\par
Structural assurance
\par
Structural assurance means that there exist legal and technological structures to
\par
ensure payment security. Compared to online banking, mobile banking is built on
\par
mobile networks and may be more vulnerable to hacker attack and information
\par
interception. Viruses and Trojan horses may also exist in mobile terminals. These
\par
problems will incur users' concern on their account and payment security. Structural
\par
assurance as an institution-based trust mechanism has been found to affect users'
\par
initial trust (McKnight et al., 2002b). Especially, due to the lack of direct experience,
\par
users may rely much on these structural assurances to build their trust in mobile
\par
banking. According to trust transference mechanism, users will transfer their trust in
\par
third parties to mobile banking (Pavlou and Gefen, 2004). Thus:
\par
H5. Structural assurance positively affects initial trust.
\par
Trust propensity
\par
Trust propensity reflects a user's natural tendency to trust other people (McKnight
\par
et al., 2002a). Those users with high trust propensity tend to have positive attitudesDownloaded by Universitas International Batam, User UIB Batam At 03:13 10 December 2015 (PT)
\par
towards new technologies. Thus they will more readily build trust in mobile banking.
\par
In contrast, those users with low trust propensity may doubt the credibility of mobile
\par
banking, which represents an emerging service. Thus, we propose:
\par
H6. Trust propensity positively affects initial trust.
\par
Initial trust and perceived usefulness
\par
Trust reflects a willingness to be in vulnerability based on the positive expectations
\par
towards another party's future behavior (Mayer et al., 1995). Trust often includes three
\par
dimensions: ability, integrity and benevolence (Benamati et al., 2010). Ability means
\par
that mobile service providers have enough knowledge and skills to fulfill their tasks.
\par
Integrity means that mobile service providers keep their promises and do not deceive
\par
users. Benevolence means that mobile service providers will concern users' interests,
\par
not just their own interests. Perceived usefulness reflects the utility derived from using
\par
mobile banking. Initial trust will affect perceived usefulness. Trust provides a
\par
guarantee that users will acquire future positive outcomes (Gefen et al., 2003). In other
\par
words, trust enables users to believe that mobile service providers have enough ability
\par
and benevolence to provide useful services to them. Thus:
\par
H7. Initial trust positively affects perceived usefulness.
\par
Usage intention
\par
According to the theory of reasoned action (TRA), initial trust and perceived
\par
usefulness as beliefs will affect behavioral intention (Fishbein and Ajzen, 1975). In
\par
addition, initial trust can help mitigate perceived uncertainty and risk, and promote
\par
usage intention. Perceived usefulness has been found to be a significant factor affecting
\par
initial usage and continuance usage (Venkatesh and Davis, 2000). Much research has
\par
reported the effects of initial trust and perceived usefulness on behavioral intention
\par
(Chen and Barnes, 2007; Lu et al., 2010; Shin et al., 2010):
\par
H8. Initial trust positively affects usage intention.
\par
H9. Perceived usefulness positively affects usage intention.
\par
Figure 1 presents the research model. Information quality, system quality, structural
\par
assurance and trust propensity are proposed to affect initial trust, which in turn affects
\par
perceived usefulness and usage intention.
\par
