Discussion
\par
As listed in Table III, all hypotheses are supported. Structural assurance, information
\par
quality, trust propensity and system quality affect initial trust, whereas information
\par
quality and system quality affect perceived usefulness. In addition, initial trust affects
\par
perceived usefulness and both factors determine usage intention.
\par
Among the factors affecting initial trust, structural assurance and information
\par
quality have relatively larger effects. These results are consistent with prior findings
\par
(Zahedi and Song, 2008; Gu et al., 2009; Kim et al., 2009). Mobile banking based on
\par
wireless networks involves great uncertainty and risk. Thus users need to rely on
\par
structural assurances to ensure their payment security and build their trust in mobile
\par
banking. Mobile service providers can use advanced encryption technologies such as
\par
secured socket layer (SSL) and third-party certification to engender users' initial trust.
\par
Information quality reflects information accuracy, relevancy and timeliness. Quality
\par
information will signal service providers' trustworthiness. On the other hand, if
\par
information quality is poor, users may feel that mobile service providers lack the
\par
ability and benevolence to provide quality services to them. This will decrease their
\par
trust. Mobile service providers can provide personalized information and services to
\par
users based on their account balance and payment records. This may help build users'
\par
trust. Trust propensity also has a significant effect on initial trust. Thus users with
\par
high trust propensity will more readily build initial trust in mobile banking. System
\par
quality has a relatively low effect on initial trust. Compared to information quality,
\par
system quality may be more easily improved with advanced technologies. Thus users
\par
mainly rely on information quality to build their trust in mobile banking.
\par
Both information quality and system quality have significant effects on perceived
\par
usefulness. Users expect to conduct payment via mobile banking at anytime from
\par
anywhere. Thus the low quality information and system will decrease their evaluation
\par
on the utility of mobile banking. For example, if mobile banking has a slow access
\par
speed, users may need to wait a long time for the response. They may also encounter
\par
service unavailability or interruption because of the unreliable system. These problems
\par
will lower users' perception of mobile banking utility. Thus mobile service providers
\par
need to present quality information and system to users. Especially, due to the
\par
constraints of mobile terminals such as small screens and inconvenient input, mobile
\par
service providers need to present users with a well-designed interface, including clear
\par
layout, powerful navigation and prompt response (Lee and Benbasat, 2004). Otherwise,
\par
users may feel difficult to use mobile banking. This will significantly decrease their
\par
perceived usefulness. In addition, users often need to download, install and configure
\par
the relevant software according to their mobile phone type before they can use mobile
\par
banking for the first time. This process may be complex for initial users. Mobile service
\par
providers can provide online tutorial and help to users. This may improve their
\par
perceived ease of use of mobile banking.
\par
Initial trust affects perceived usefulness, and both factors predict usage intention.
\par
Initial trust provides a guarantee that users can adopt mobile banking to meet their
\par
expectations, such as the improvement of living and working performance. In addition,
\par
both initial trust and perceived usefulness as enablers will facilitate user adoption and
\par
usage of mobile banking. These results provide further support to extant findings (Kim
\par
et al., 2009; Shin et al., 2010).
