Theoretical and managerial implications
\par
From a theoretical perspective, this research examined the effect of initial trust on
\par
mobile banking user adoption. As noted in the literature review, although initial trust
\par
has received considerable attention in the electronic commerce context, it has seldom
\par
been examined in the mobile commerce context, especially with mobile banking that
\par
involves great risk. Thus we need to extend extant findings to mobile banking context.
\par
On the other hand, extant research has mainly adopted information technology
\par
adoption theories such as TAM, IDT and UTAUT to explain mobile user behavior
\par
(Mallat, 2007; Kim et al., 2010; Schierz et al., 2010), and has seldom examined the effect
\par
of initial trust on mobile banking user adoption. However, the high perceived risk and
\par
low switching cost highlight the necessity to build initial trust to facilitate user
\par
behavior. Thus it is necessary to identify the factors affecting initial trust in mobile
\par
banking. We find that structural assurance and information quality are the main
\par
factors affecting initial trust. This advances our understanding of mobile banking user
\par
behavior. The results indicate that initial trust affects perceived usefulness, and both
\par
factors have mediation effects on usage intention. Thus initial trust and perceived
\par
usefulness act as enablers of user behavior. Future research can examine the effect of
\par
inhibitors such as switching cost on user behavior.
\par
From a managerial perspective, our results imply that mobile service providers need
\par
to engender users' initial trust in order to facilitate their adoption and usage of mobile
\par
banking. They cannot just focus on technological perceptions such as perceived
\par
usefulness when promoting user behavior. We found that structural assurance has a
\par
strong effect on initial trust. Thus mobile service providers need to adopt legal and
\par
technological structures such as third-party certifications to ensure payment security.
\par
The results also indicate that compared to system quality, information quality has a
\par
larger effect on initial trust. System quality may be rapidly improved with advanced
\par
technologies, whereas information quality improvement will require mobile service
\par
providers' continuous effort and resources investment. Thus information quality acts
\par
as a stronger trust signal. This suggests that mobile service providers need to attach
\par
great importance to delivering quality information to users.
\par
Conclusion
\par
Mobile banking as an emerging service has not been widely adopted by users. Thus it
\par
is necessary to identify the factors affecting user adoption. Due to the high risk and low
\par
switching cost, building users' initial trust is critical for mobile banking service
\par
providers. The purpose of this research is to examine the effect of initial trust on mobile
\par
banking user adoption. The results indicate that structural assurance and information
\par
quality are the main factors affecting initial trust, whereas both information quality
\par
and system quality affect perceived usefulness. Initial trust affects perceived
\par
usefulness and both factors predict usage intention. Thus mobile service providers
\par
need to highlight improving initial trust in order to facilitate users' adoption and usage
\par
of mobile banking.
\par
This research has the following limitations. First, we collected data in China, where
\par
mobile commerce is developing rapidly but still in its early stage. Thus our results need
\par
to be generalized to other countries with developed mobile commerce. Second, we did not
\par
consider the effect of bank reputation on initial trust. Due to the lack of direct usage
\par
experience, users may rely on trust signals such as reputation to build their initial trustDownloaded by Universitas International Batam, User UIB Batam At 03:13 10 December 2015 (PT)
\par
in mobile banking. Future research can examine this possible effect. Third, users often
\par
need to bear some usage costs, such as communication fees and transactions fees when
\par
using mobile banking. These costs may also affect user trust in service providers' ability
\par
and benevolence. Future research can explore the effect of perceived cost on initial trust.
\par
Fourth, our sample was mainly composed of users having rich mobile Internet
\par
experience, which may affect their trust in mobile banking. Future research needs to
