secondary use are the main factors affecting perceived risk, whereas errors are the main factor
\par
affecting trust. Trust affects perceived risk, and both factors determine usage intention.
\par
Practical implications -- Mobile service providers need to alleviate users' privacy concern and
\par
perceived risk in order to encourage their adoption and usage of LBS.
\par
Originality/value -- Extant research has mainly examined LBS user behavior from the perspectives
\par
of technology acceptance model, task technology fit and unified theory of acceptance and usage of
\par
technology. The results provided in this paper indicate that privacy concern also has a significant
\par
effect on user adoption of LBS. This advances our understanding of LBS user behavior.
\par
Keywords Privacy, Trust, Consumer risk, Consumer behaviour, Mobile communication systems
\par
Paper type Research paper
\par
1. Introduction
\par
The application of third generation (3G) mobile communication technologies has
\par
triggered the rapid development of mobile commerce. According to a report issued by
\par
China Internet Network Information Center (CNNIC), the number of mobile internet
\par
users has reached 277 million, accounting for 66 percent of the internet population
\par
(CNNIC, 2010). A variety of mobile services such as short message services, mobile
\par
instant messaging, mobile search and mobile games have become popular among users.
\par
Compared to electronic commerce, the main advantages of mobile commerce include
\par
ubiquity and immediacy, both of which free users from temporal and spatial limitations
\par
(Junglas and Watson, 2006). They can access mobile internet to acquire information and
\par
services at anytime from anywhere.
\par
Another advantage of mobile commerce is locatability. With the help of location-based
\par
services (LBS), mobile service providers can provide the optimal information and
\par
services to users based on their locations and preferences. This advantage is also called
