\par
advertisements, emergency evacuation and check-in services of mobile social network.
\par
LBS will create value for users and improve their loyalty (Pura, 2005). Owing to their
\par
unique advantage, LBS are deemed the killer application of mobile commerce (Junglas
\par
and Watson, 2008). However, LBS need to collect and utilize users' location information.
\par
This leads users to feel that they are tracked and their privacy is violated. They will be
\par
unwilling to adopt and use LBS because of the perceived risk.
\par
Extant research has been drawn on information technology adoption theories such as
\par
the technology acceptance model (TAM) (Junglas and Watson, 2008), task technology fit
\par
(TTF) (Junglas et al., 2008a) and the unified theory of acceptance and usage of
\par
technology (UTAUT) (Xu and Gupta, 2009) to examine LBS user behavior. Perceived
\par
usefulness, TTF and performance expectancy are found to affect user adoption of LBS.
\par
However, LBS user behavior is not only affected by enablers such as perceived
\par
usefulness, but also affected by inhibitors such as privacy risk associated with personal
\par
information disclosure. In this research, we are mainly concerned with the effect of
\par
privacy concern on LBS user behavior. Privacy concern reflects a user's attitude towards
\par
personal information. Those users with high privacy concern will feel that disclosing
\par
their location information incurs great privacy risk to them. They are worried whether
\par
their information is properly collected, stored and used. If they cannot engender enough
\par
trust to alleviate this perceived risk, they will be reluctant to adopt and use LBS. Thus,
\par
we propose that privacy concern affects users' perceived risk and trust, both of which
\par
further determine their adoption and usage of LBS. Privacy concern includes four
\par
dimensions: collection, improper access, errors and secondary use.
\par
The rest of this paper is organized as follows. We review relevant literature on LBS
\par
adoption and privacy concern in the next section. Section 3 proposes the research model
\par
and hypotheses. We report instrument development and data collection in Section 4.
\par
Section 5 presents data analysis and results, followed by a discussion in Section 6.
\par
Section 7 presents the theoretical and managerial implications. Section 8 summarizes the
\par
limitations and concludes the paper.
\par
2. Literature review
\par
2.1 LBS adoption
\par
As an emerging mobile service, LBS have not received wide adoption among users
\par
(Junglas and Watson, 2008). Thus, researchers try to identify the factors affecting user
\par
adoption of LBS. They have mainly drawn on information technology adoption theories
\par
such as TAM, TTF and UTAUT as the theoretical bases. Junglas and Watson (2008)
\par
argued that LBS include two categories: location-tracking services and location-aware
\par
services. They found that compared to location-aware services, location-tracking
\par
services are perceived to be more useful, easier to use and generate higher efficiency.
\par
Junglas et al. (2008a) noted that only when location sensitiveness as task characteristics
\par
fits locatability and mobility as technology characteristics will individual performance
\par
of using LBS be improved. Xu and Gupta (2009) employed UTAUT as the theoretical
\par
base and found that performance expectancy, effort expectancy, privacy concern and
\par
personal innovativeness predict the usage intention of LBS.
\par
In addition to information technology adoption theories, other theories such as
\par
perceived value, perceived justice and personality traits are also used to examine LBS
\par
user behavior. Pura (2005) examined the effect of perceived value on the continuance
