\par
intention of LBS. Perceived value includes social value, emotional value, conditional
\par
value, monetary value, convenience value and epistemic value. Xu et al. (2009b) drew
\par
on justice theory to examine the effects of three privacy-related interventions on
\par
LBS users' intention to disclose personal information. These interventions include
\par
compensation, which represents the distributive justice, and industry self-regulation
\par
and government regulation, which represent the procedural justice. Junglas et al. (2008b)
\par
noted that personality traits including agreeableness, extraversion, emotional stability,
\par
openness to experience and consciousness have significant effects on LBS user
\par
behavior. Xu et al. (2009a) examined the effectiveness of text and multimedia
\par
location-based advertisements. Their results indicate that compared to text
\par
location-based advertisements, multimedia location-based advertisements lead to
\par
more favorable attitude and greater usage intention.
\par
2.2 Privacy concern
\par
Privacy concern reflects the user's concern about his/her information privacy (Dinev and
\par
Hart, 2006a). As a broad concept, privacy concern has been viewed inconsistently in
\par
extant research. Smith et al. (1996) proposed that enterprise users' concern for
\par
information privacy (CFIP) includes four dimensions: collection, errors, secondary use
\par
and improper access. Stewart and Segars (2002) later found that these four dimensions
\par
can be integrated into a second-order factor. Malhotra et al. (2004) argued that internet
\par
users' information privacy concern as a second-order factor includes three dimensions:
\par
collection, control and awareness. Liu et al. (2005) noted that privacy includes notice,
\par
access, choice and security.
\par
Privacy concern has been found to directly affect users' behavioral intention in a
\par
variety of contexts, including ubiquitous commerce (Sheng et al., 2008), electronic health
\par
records (Angst and Agarwal, 2009), radio frequency identification (RFID)-based
\par
u-commerce (Lee et al., 2007), e-recruitment technology (Tong, 2009) and social
\par
networking communities (Fogel and Nehmad, 2009). Dinev and Hart (2006b) proposed
\par
that users' internet literacy and social awareness affect privacy concern, further
\par
determining their intention to transact. Dinev et al. (2008) suggested that perceived need
\par
for government surveillance affects users' privacy concern, which further affects their
\par
willingness to provide personal information to transact on the internet. Son and Kim
\par
(2008) found that information privacy concern affects users' privacy protection
\par
responses, which include information provision, private behavior and public behavior.
\par
Information provision includes refusal and misrepresentation. Private behavior
\par
includes removal and negative word-of-mouth, whereas public behavior includes
\par
complaining directly to online companies and complaining indirectly to third-party
\par
organizations.
\par
In addition to its direct effect on user behavior, privacy concern also indirectly affects
\par
user behavior through trust, perceived risk and perceived usefulness. Liu et al. (2005)
\par
found that consumers' privacy concern affects their trust, further determining their
\par
behavioral intention, which includes repurchase, revisit, recommendation and positive
\par
comments. Bansal et al. (2010) noted that privacy concern affects users' trust in health
\par
web sites, further affecting their intention to disclose health information. Slyke et al.
\par
(2006) found that privacy concern affects online consumers' purchase intention through
\par
perceived risk and trust. Kumar et al. (2008) noted that privacy concern affects the
\par
intention to use firewall through perceived usefulness.3. Research model and hypotheses
\par
As noted earlier, privacy concern is a multi-dimensional concept. In this research, we will
\par
adopt CFIP to measure privacy concern, which includes four dimensions: collection,
\par
improper access, errors and secondary use.
\par
Collection reflects that users are concerned whether their personal information
\par
is properly collected. When collecting users' personal information, mobile service
\par
providers may require much unnecessary information, such as identification card
\par
number and credit card account. This may raise users' concern about information
\par
over-collection. Improper access reflects information storage security. Mobile service
\par
providers should ensure that only those authorized users can access the databases
\par
containing personal information. However, they may be unable to prevent hacker attack
\par
on and intrusion into the databases. This leads to users' worry about improper access.
\par
Errors reflect the accuracy of personal information storing in databases. Mobile service
\par
providers may not adopt measures such as verification to ensure information accuracy.
\par
This has a negative effect on the service quality of LBS and increases users' concern
\par
about information errors. Secondary use reflects that mobile service providers use
\par
personal information for other purposes without users' permission. They may share this
\par
information with third parties, or even sell it for profit. This hurts users' benefits and
\par
brings great privacy risk to them.
\par
Users' concern on information collection, errors, improper access and secondary
\par
use will increase perceived risk. They may worry whether their personal information is
\par
properly collected, securely and accurately stored and appropriately used. This raises
\par
their concern about future potential loss associated with disclosing personal
\par
information. The effect of privacy concern on perceived risk has been supported in
\par
previous research (Malhotra et al., 2004; Junglas et al., 2008b). Thus:
\par
H1.1-H1.4. User concern on collection, improper access, errors and secondary use
\par
positively affects perceived risk.
\par
Trust reflects a positive expectation towards another party's future behavior (Kim et al.,
\par
2008). Trust includes three beliefs: ability, integrity and benevolence (Gefen et al., 2003;
\par
Benamati et al., 2010). Ability reflects that mobile service providers have the necessary
\par
skills and knowledge to protect users' privacy. Integrity reflects that mobile service
\par
providers keep their promises and will not deceive users. Benevolence reflects that
\par
mobile service providers concern users' interests, not just their own benefits (Palvia,
\par
2009). Those users with high privacy concern will doubt the trustworthiness of mobile
\par
service providers. For example, they may doubt whether mobile service providers have
\par
enough ability to prevent the unauthorized access to their personal information. They
\par
may also suspect whether mobile service providers commit their promises to only
\par
collect necessary information. They probably worry whether mobile service providers
\par
are concerned with their interests and do not share their information with other parties:
\par
H2.1-H2.4. User concern on collection, improper access, errors and secondary use
\par
negatively affects trust.
\par
Trust will affect perceived risk and behavioral intention. Trust enables users to believe
\par
that mobile service providers will bring favorable outcomes to them in the future. This
\par
will decrease their perceived risk and promote their usage behavior (Luo et al., 2010).
\par
In addition, according to the theory of reasoned action, users' beliefs affect their
