intention (Fishbein and Ajzen, 1975). Thus, trust as users' belief will affect their
\par
behavioral intention. Prior research has also pointed out the significant effect of trust
\par
on user behavior (Beldad et al., 2010; Varnali and Toker, 2010). Thus, we propose:
\par
H3. Trust negatively affects perceived risk.
\par
H4. Trust positively affects usage intention.
\par
Compared to the positive effect of trust on behavioral intention, perceived risk will
\par
have a negative effect on behavioral intention. Perceived risk includes economic risk,
\par
personal risk, performance risk and privacy risk (Pavlou, 2003). In this research, we are
\par
mainly concerned with privacy risk, such as potential loss and uncertainty derived
\par
from disclosing personal information. Users with high perceived risk will feel lack of
\par
control and be unwilling to adopt LBS:
\par
H5. Perceived risk negatively affects usage intention.
