4.2 Data collection
\par
Data were collected at a university and two service halls of China Mobile and China
\par
Unicom, which are the two main telecommunication service providers in China. The
\par
university and service halls are located in an eastern China city, where mobile commerce is
\par
better developed than other regions. There are plenty of mobile users at these places and
\par
this will expedite our data collection. Researchers randomly contacted users and explained
\par
the meaning of LBS to them. Then we asked those with actual usage experience to fill the
\par
questionnaires. We scrutinized all questionnaires and dropped those with too many
\par
missing values. As a result, we obtained 210 valid responses. Among them, 61.9 percent
\par
were male and 38.1 percent were female. In terms of age, 38.6 percent of them were aged
\par
between 20 and 29 years old. Most of them (69.5 percent) used LBS over once each day. The
\par
frequently used LBS include mobile navigation and location-based advertisements.
\par
We conducted two tests to examine the common method variance (CMV). First, we
\par
performed a Harman's single-factor test (Podsakoff et al., 2003). The largest variance
\par
explained by individual factor is 13.852 percent. This indicates that none of the factor
\par
can explain the majority of the variance. Second, we modeled all items as the indicators
\par
of a factor representing the common method effect (Malhotra et al., 2006). The results
\par
show a poor fit. For example, the goodness of fit index (GFI) is 0.580 (, 0.90) and the root
\par
mean square error of approximation (RMSEA) is 0.172 (. 0.08). With both tests, we feel
\par
that CMV is not a significant problem in our research.
\par
5. Results
\par
According to the two-step approach recommended by Anderson and Gerbing (1988), our
\par
data analysis includes two steps. First, we examined the measurement model to test the
\par
reliability and validity. Then we examined the structural model to test research
\par
hypotheses and model fit.
\par
First, we conducted a confirmatory factor analysis to examine the reliability and
\par
validity. Validity includes convergent validity and discriminant validity. Convergent
\par
validity measures whether items can effectively reflect their corresponding factor,
\par
whereas discriminant validity measures whether two factors are statistically different.
\par
Table I lists the standardized item loadings, the average variance extracted (AVE), the
\par
composite reliability (CR) and Cronbach alpha values. Most loadings are larger than
\par
0.7 and t-values show that all loadings are significant at 0.001. All AVEs, CRs and alpha
\par
values exceed 0.5, 0.7 and 0.7, respectively. Thus, the scale has a good reliability and
\par
convergent validity (Nunnally, 1978; Bagozzi and Yi, 1988; Garver and Mentzer, 1999).
\par
To examine the discriminant validity, we compared the square root of the AVE and
\par
factor correlation coefficients. As shown in Table II, for each factor, the square root of
\par
AVE is significantly larger than its correlation coefficients with other factors, showing a
\par
good discriminant validity (Fornell and Larcker, 1981; Gefen et al., 2000). We also list the
\par
cross-loading matrix in Appendix 2. Each item has a higher loading on its corresponding
\par
factor than the cross-loadings on other factors, showing a clear loading matrix.
