\par
Among four dimensions of privacy concern, collection and secondary use have
\par
relatively larger effects on perceived risk. This indicates that users are concerned
\par
whether mobile service providers properly collect and use their information. Thus,
\par
mobile service providers should only collect necessary information and they cannot
\par
over-collect users' personal information. In addition, they need to acquire users'
\par
permission before collecting users' personal information and presenting LBS to them.
\par
Otherwise, if users receive location-based advertisements without their knowledge, they
\par
may feel that their privacy is violated and this will lead to their negative attitudes
\par
towards mobile service providers (Tsang et al., 2004). Mobile service providers should
\par
also commit to their promises on information usage. They cannot use personal
\par
information for other purposes, or share this information with third parties without
\par
users' knowledge, or even sell it for profit. Otherwise, users may feel that their benefits
\par
are hurt. This will significantly increase their perceived risk. To mitigate users'
\par
perceived risk, mobile service providers can post privacy statements and display
\par
privacy seals to demonstrate that their privacy practices are certificated and
\par
trustworthy (Vail et al., 2008; Tsai et al., 2010). They can also use privacy-related
\par
interventions such as compensation, industry self-regulation and government
\par
regulation to alleviate perceived risk (Xu et al., 2009b).
\par
On the other hand, among the dimensions of privacy concern, errors have the
\par
strongest loading on trust. Errors reflect information accuracy, which affects user trust
\par
in mobile service providers' technological ability. Thus, service providers need to verifyIMDS
\par
111,2
\par
220
\par
the personal information and ensure its accuracy. Then they can provide the optimal
\par
services to users based on this information. Otherwise, they may provide the
\par
information or services that do not cater to users' individual demands. This will reduce
\par
users' trust. Improper access has a moderate loading on trust. This shows that
\par
mobile users are concerned with information security. Compared to wired network,
\par
mobile network is more vulnerable to hacker attack and information interception. This
\par
raises users' concern about their information security. Mobile service providers can use
\par
encryption and certification to prevent the unauthorized access to the databases
\par
containing user information. We did not find the direct effect of improper access on
\par
perceived risk. However, it indirectly affects perceived risk through trust. This shows
\par
that trust mediates the effect of improper access on perceived risk.
\par
Trust has a significant effect on perceived risk, and both factors further determine user
\par
behavior. Trust provides a guarantee that users will acquire positive future outcomes
\par
(Gefen et al., 2003). This will decrease their perceived uncertainty and risk, and facilitate
\par
their behavioral intention. Mobile service providers can use reputation, brand and
\par
linkages with other well-known service providers to build users' trust. For those service
\par
providers who have established successful traditional or online channels, they will have
\par
more advantages in building user trust. According to trust transference mechanism
\par
(Kim, 2008), users will transfer their trust in traditional or online channel to the mobile
\par
channel. In addition, the strong effect of perceived risk on behavioral intention deserves
\par
attention. Mobile service providers can use legal structures and third-party certification
\par
to alleviate users' perceived risk associated with disclosing personal information.
\par
7. Theoretical and managerial implications
\par
From a theoretical perspective, this research identified the significant effect of privacy
\par
concern on user adoption of LBS. As an emerging service, LBS can present the optimal
\par
information and services to users based on their locations and preferences. This will
\par
improve their experience and promote their usage. However, due to the collection and
\par
utilization of personal information, LBS arouse users' privacy concern, which may
\par
negatively affect their usage intention. Thus, it is necessary to examine the effect of
\par
privacy concern on LBS user behavior. As noted earlier, prior research has mainly
\par
employed information technology adoption theories such as TAM (Junglas and Watson,
\par
2008), TTF (Junglas et al., 2008a) and UTAUT (Xu and Gupta, 2009) to examine LBS
\par
user behavior. Perceived usefulness, TTF and performance expectancy are found to be
\par
the enablers of user behavior. However, the effect of inhibitors such as privacy risk on
\par
user adoption has seldom been examined. Our results indicate that privacy concern has
\par
significant effects on perceived risk and trust, both of which predict usage intention.
\par
This not only enriches extant research on LBS user behavior, but also advances our
\par
understanding of user adoption of LBS. We also found that among four dimensions of
\par
privacy concern, collection and secondary use have strong loadings on perceived risk,
\par
whereas errors have the largest loading on trust. This demonstrates the different effects
\par
of the components of privacy concern on user behavior. Future research can generalize
\par
our results to other contexts, such as mobile purchase. The results suggest that both
\par
trust and perceived risk mediate the effect of privacy concern on behavioral intention.
\par
Thus, future research needs to pay more attention to the effects of trust and perceived
\par
risk rather than technological perceptions such as perceived usefulness on mobile user
\par
behavior.From a managerial perspective, our results imply that mobile service providers need
\par
to mitigate users' privacy concern in order to facilitate their adoption and usage of LBS.
\par
They should regulate their privacy practices on information collection, storage and
\par
usage. The results indicate that collection and secondary use are the main factors
\par
affecting perceived risk, whereas errors are the main factor affecting trust. Mobile
\par
service providers can post privacy statements to inform users about their privacy
\par
practices on information collection and usage. They can also display privacy seals to
\par
demonstrate that their privacy practices are certificated by the trustworthy third parties
\par
or authoritative organizations. Both privacy statements and seals can mitigate users'
\par
perceived risk. To build user trust, mobile service providers can use advanced
\par
encryption and verification to ensure information security and accuracy. In addition,
\par
users may conduct a privacy calculus to evaluate perceived risk. That is, when the
\par
benefits exceed the costs associated with disclosing personal information, users may
\par
perceive low risk and be willing to provide their information to service providers. Thus,
\par
service providers can offer privacy benefits such as locatability and personalization to
\par
increase users' behavioral intention.
\par
8. Conclusion
\par
As an emerging mobile service, LBS have not received wide adoption and usage among
\par
users (Junglas and Watson, 2008). A possible reason may be the privacy risk associated
\par
with using LBS. This research identified the effect of privacy concern on LBS user
\par
adoption. The results indicated that privacy concern has significant effects on trust and
\par
perceived risk, further determining users' behavioral intention. Privacy concern consists
\par
of four dimensions: collection, improper access, errors and secondary use. Thus, mobile
\par
service providers need to mitigate users' privacy concern in order to facilitate their
\par
adoption and usage of LBS.
\par
This research has the following limitations. First, we conducted this research in
\par
China, where mobile commerce is developing rapidly but still in its infancy. Thus, our
\par
results need to be generalized to other countries with developed mobile commerce.
\par
Second, the explained variance of both trust and perceived risk is about 50 percent. Thus,
\par
besides privacy concern, there exist other factors affecting trust and perceived risk, such
\par
as information quality and structural assurance. Future research can consider their
\par
effects. Third, we mainly conducted a cross-sectional study. However, user behavior is
\par
dynamic. A longitudinal research may provide more insights on user behavior
\par
development.
