\par
Service fairness and customer
\par
satisfaction in internet banking
\par
Exploring the mediating effects of trust
\par
and customer value
\par
Yu-Qian Zhu and Houn-Gee Chen
\par
Graduate School of Business Administration, National Taiwan University,
\par
Taipei, Taiwan
\par
Abstract
\par
Purpose -- The purpose of this paper is to explore and empirically test fairness in predicting
\par
online customer satisfaction in the internet banking context. The paper also aims to further explore
\par
the mechanism through which fairness influences customer satisfaction online, i.e. identifying the
\par
mediators.
\par
Design/methodology/approach -- A survey is used to validate a research model based on equity
\par
theory and relationship marketing theory that incorporates fairness, trust, perceived value and
\par
customer satisfaction. A total of 331 surveys were analyzed using Partial Least Square.
\par
Findings -- In internet banking, fairness that includes distributive fairness, procedural fairness and
\par
informational fairness is positively related to customer satisfaction. Trust is identified as the key
\par
mediator of fairness to customer satisfaction.
\par
Research limitations/implications -- In an online context, where human interactions are replaced
\par
by graphic user interfaces, fairness still plays an important role in customer satisfaction. Further
\par
comparison of online and offline context could be conducted.
\par
Practical implications -- For online service managers, it is crucial to incorporate the fairness
\par
perspective into operations, which involves topics like segmented marketing efforts, consistency in
\par
procedures, and providing ample information to customers.
\par
Originality/value -- This research is one of the first attempts to examine fairness in internet banking,
\par
as well as to delineate the mediating process of fairness to satisfaction online.
\par
Keywords Internet banking, Service fairness, Perceived value, Trust, Customer satisfaction
\par
Paper type Research paper
\par
Introduction
\par
Internet banking is one of the most popular B2C ecommerce activities (Eastin, 2002). In
\par
January 2008, 10.9 million customers logged in to at least one internet banking account,
\par
which represented 33 percent of the total UK online population (Comscore, 2008).
\par
Similarly, Forrester Research (2008) has reported that 67.9 million households, or 80
\par
percent of the estimated 85.1 million US online households, use internet banking
\par
services. The wide-spread adoption of internet banking is not unique to developed
\par
countries only. Developing countries, such as India, have also reported increasing
\par
diffusion of internet banking in the past few years (Malhotra and Singh, 2010).
\par
Despite the prevalent usage of internet banking, our understanding of the drivers of
\par
customer satisfaction with internet banking is limited, as studies of the determinants
\par
of customer satisfaction with online banking are relatively lacking (Yoon, 2010).
\par
Current available literature concerning customer satisfaction shows that service
