H-002-203.quality, web site design quality, security, trust, customer support, and consumer
\par
experience are the main drivers of customer satisfaction for online banking customers
\par
(Hwang, 2007; Santouridis et al., 2009; Yoon, 2010).
\par
Fairness, or justice, has been found to be an important factor leading to customer
\par
satisfaction in the offline world in banking services (Han et al., 2008), as well as various
\par
other settings, e.g. airline, hotel, IT service, hospitals, phone service (Carr, 2007;
\par
Han et al., 2008). However, it has received scant attention in internet banking, or
\par
ecommerce as a whole. Ecommerce users are different from traditional channel
\par
customers. They have a greater need for trust (Urban et al., 2000) and have different
\par
trust and satisfaction mechanisms (Flavia \textasciiacute{}
\par
n et al., 2005; Papadopoulou et al., 2001;
\par
Shankar et al., 2003). They are also likely to be more price sensitive (e.g. Degeratu
\par
et al., 2000; Lynch and Ariely, 2000). Two marked differences between the online and
\par
offline world make the study of fairness in internet banking an interesting question.
\par
First, in the offline world, much of the fairness perception is formed via interpersonal
\par
interactions. On the internet, however, service provider and customer interactions
\par
are delivered by computer graphic user interfaces (GUI). Second, when evaluating
\par
fairness, a comparison with others is a quick way to reach a conclusion. Over the
\par
internet, a comparison with similar others (i.e. customers) is both easier and more
\par
difficult to do. It is easier in the sense that with the help of search engines
\par
and discussion boards information is readily accessible, but it is more difficult because
\par
one cannot physically see other customers or their interactions with the service
\par
providers. Furthermore, extra effort must be exerted to search to make these
\par
comparisons.
\par
Because of the differences between the online and offline setting that we discussed
\par
above, the research findings obtained from the offline setting may not be readily
\par
applicable to the online setting. Thus, further research is warranted to understand the
\par
role fairness plays in customer satisfaction in internet banking. In addition, although
\par
prior work has confirmed the importance of fairness to customer satisfaction, linking
\par
fairness directly to customer satisfaction, little is known about how and through
\par
what process fairness affects satisfaction. The purposes of this study, thus, are twofold:
\par
first, to explore and empirically test fairness in predicting internet banking customer's
\par
satisfaction. Second, to further explore the mechanism through which fairness
\par
influence customer satisfaction online, i.e. identifying the mediators. Based on the
\par
relationship marketing literature, we identify two constructs: perceived customer value
\par
and trust, as mediators in the fairness to satisfaction relationship.
\par
Theoretical background
\par
This research draws on two theoretical streams: the fairness theory and the
\par
relationship marketing theory. The fairness theory links fairness to various customer
\par
attitudinal outcomes, such as satisfaction; the relationship marketing literature
\par
explains how the process works: fairness nurtures two key elements in establishing,
\par
nurturing, and sustaining long-term customer relationships: trust and perceived value,
\par
which in turn, lead to higher customer satisfaction. Below, we will first define the
\par
fairness construct and provide a brief review of related literature, and then define and
\par
discuss the construct of trust and value.
\par
Service fairness
\par
Scholars have reasoned that fairness is especially exposed in service industries
\par
because the intangibility of service heightens customers' sensitivity to fairness issues
