\par
(Berry et al., 1994). Drawing on equity theory, Carr (2007) proposes that an important
\par
set of service evaluations results from a comparison of services received. Equity theory
\par
states that individuals are motivated by a comparison of the ratio of their inputs to
\par
their outcomes relative to the same ratio of others that are comparable to them,
\par
i.e. comparison others (Adams, 1963). Jacoby (1976) refines the concept of ``comparison
\par
others'' to `` reference others,'' where a ``reference others'' may be ``another person,
\par
a class of people, an organization, or the individual himself relative to his experiences
\par
from an earlier point in time.'' He further suggests that comparisons can also be
\par
made against a pertinent standard or norm (Jacoby, 1976, p. 1053). Service equity is
\par
reported to be the main driver of satisfaction and customer loyalty in service
\par
encounters (Olsen and Johnson, 2003).
\par
Traditionally, there are four dimensions of service fairness (Lind and Tyler, 1988;
\par
Cohen-Charash and Spector, 2001; Greenberg, 1993): distributive fairness, procedural
\par
fairness, interpersonal fairness and informational fairness. One of the critiques to the
\par
existing dimensions of fairness is that it neglects the fact that people might perceive
\par
fairness not only in terms of the specific inputs and outcomes of a relationship, but also
\par
in terms of the overarching system that determines those inputs and outputs.
\par
Extending the fairness heuristic theory, Beugre and Baron (2001) suggest that
\par
evaluations of perceived distributive, procedural, and interactional justice serve as a
\par
basis for forming general impressions of systemic fairness. Systemic fairness is
\par
defined as an evaluation of overall fairness derived from the evaluation of original
\par
four dimensions of fairness and thought to be a mediating construct through which
\par
the components of fairness (e.g. distributive, procedural, interpersonal, and
\par
informational fairness) have effects on outcome variables (Table I).
\par
Berry and Parasuraman (1991) propose that ``relationship marketing concerns
\par
attracting, developing, and retaining customer relationships.'' Relationship marketing
\par
is recommended as a strategy to overcome service intangibility and may be
