\par
relationships than ever before.'' Among important relationship marketing constructs,
\par
value and trust are two central constructs that are of particular relevance to internet
\par
banking.
\par
Trust
\par
Trust is defined as the willingness of a party to be vulnerable to the actions of another
\par
party based on the expectation that the trustee will perform a particular action
\par
important to the trustor, irrespective of the ability to monitor or control that other
\par
party (Mayer et al., 1995). Morgan and Hunt (1994) argue that trust is central to
\par
understanding relationship marketing. Trust, together with commitment,
\par
distinguishes productive, effective, relational exchanges from those that are
\par
unproductive and ineffective. The importance of trust on the internet has been
\par
consistently argued (Fang et al., 2011; Kim et al., 2009; Palvia, 2009). Trust is crucial
\par
in online transaction processes, given the impersonal nature of the online environment
\par
(uncertainty), and the inability to judge product quality prior to purchase (information
\par
asymmetry) (Ba, 2001).
\par
The survival of internet banking depends on the bank's ability to convince
\par
customers to bank online, therefore building customer trust is vital to internet banking.
\par
Customers' trust in internet banking transactions has some unique dimensions:
\par
the impersonal nature of the online environment, the extensive use of technology, and
\par
the inherent uncertainty of using an open infrastructure for transactions (Yousafzai
\par
et al., 2009). Extant research has identified a number of factors leading to customer
\par
trust in internet banking, for example, perceived trustworthiness, perceived security,
\par
and perceived privacy (Yousafzai et al., 2009).
\par
Customer-perceived value
\par
Perceived value is a consumer's overall assessment of the utility of a product (or
\par
service) based on perceptions of what is received and what is given (Zeithaml,
\par
1988).Value is considered to be an important constituent of relationship marketing
\par
and the ability of a company to provide superior value to its customers is regarded as
\par
one of the most successful competitive strategies (Treacy and Wiersema, 1993). By
\par
adding more value to their products or services, companies try to improve customer
\par
satisfaction so that the bonds are strengthened and customer loyalty is thereby
\par
achieved.
\par
For ecommerce customers, perceived value is one of the main reasons to engage in
\par
online transactions. Researchers have observed that a significant number of
\par
ecommerce customers are motivated by low prices, which affects the ``give'' or ``cost''
\par
portion of perceived value (Sabiote et al., 2012; Tanaka, 1999). Anderson and
\par
Srinivasan (2003) argue that perceived value in ecommerce is of particular importance
\par
as it is easy to compare product features as well as prices online. Although price
