\par
To summarize, fairness is important to customer satisfaction, and trust and
\par
perceived value are two key elements in building long-term customer relationships
\par
and customer satisfaction. In the next section, we discuss in detail how trust and
\par
perceived value mediate the fairness-satisfaction relationship.
\par
Research model
\par
Based on the relationship marketing literature, we propose fairness as a main driver to
\par
customer satisfaction mediated through trust and perceived value. Previous research
\par
has shown that service fairness adds a significant new set of predictors of service
\par
satisfaction via a direct effect with service quality controlled for (Carr, 2007). This
\par
research further explores the mechanism of how fairness affects satisfaction by
\par
examining two mediators: trust and value in the fairness-satisfaction relationship. The
\par
proposed research model is presented in Figure 1.
\par
Fairness and satisfaction
\par
In his framework for customer relationship management through the internet, Winer
\par
(2001) accords central attention to customer satisfaction as the overall goal of a firm's
\par
relationship programs. Satisfaction is primarily an affective variable (Oliver, 1997). Thus,
\par
cognitive evaluations such as value and fairness will be antecedents of satisfaction
\par
following the cognition-affect causal ordering (Bagozzi, 1992; Lazarus, 1991). When
\par
customers think they have been treated unfairly their reactions tend to be immediate,
\par
emotional, and enduring (Seiders and Berry, 1998). Previous research has substantiated
\par
that fairness has a significant impact on customer satisfaction (Carr, 2007; Han et al.,
\par
2008). We would like to further verify this with internet banking customers:
\par
H1. Systemic/overall fairness directly and positively affects satisfaction.
\par
Trust as a mediator
\par
Fairness is deemed as a necessary condition for trust (Seiders and Berry, 1998). Folger
\par
and Konovsky (1989) proposed that fair treatment will lead to the development of trust.
\par
Scholars have regarded trust as the central construct in customer loyalty and
\par
repurchase intentions (Sirdeshmukh et al., 2002; Verhoef et al., 2002).
