Conceptual framework
\par
Distributive
\par
fairnessBased on the cognitive-emotive causal order (Oliver, 1997) and the social exchange
\par
theory, a number of commentators have theorized that trust evaluations will exert
\par
a direct influence on perceptions of satisfaction. Andaleeb (1996) posits that when the
\par
focal party trusts the source, it will feel secure by way of an implicit belief that the
\par
actions of the source will result in positive outcomes, which should also lead to high
\par
satisfaction. Singh and Sirdeshmukh (2000) reasoned that consumers prefer to transact
\par
with service providers they can trust and with whom they share an understanding
\par
about implied and unspecified obligations that govern their relationship. Thus, trust
\par
positively influences customer satisfaction. Trust has also been studied in various
\par
research linking customer satisfaction in buyer-seller relationships (Selnes, 1998;
\par
Garbarino and Johnson, 1999; Hennig-Thurau and Klee, 1997).
\par
Thus we propose:
\par
H2. Systemic/overall fairness indirectly and positively affects customer satisfaction
\par
mediated through trust.
\par
Value as a mediator
\par
Xia et al. (2004) have explored how price fairness affects perceived value and suggest
\par
that perceived price unfairness increases perceptions of monetary sacrifice, thereby
\par
lowering perceived value. We argue that fairness may affect perceived customer value
\par
in three ways. First, price fairness will have a direct impact on customer's perceived
\par
value with increased sacrifice perceptions (Xia et al., 2004). Second, the emotional
\par
distress caused by unfair services may also reduce customer-perceived value. Fairness
\par
involves calculating the input/outcome ratio of self and others. According to Adams
\par
(1963), when the input/outcome ratio, compared to the ratio of the comparison
\par
other, is unequal, the individual is distressed and seeks to reduce this distress. The
\par
extra effort in relieving this distress may be considered as part of the price/sacrifice
\par
paid concerning the service, thus it may reduce perceived customer value. Lastly,
\par
with respect to informational fairness, the reduced risks as a result of complete
\par
and comprehensive information will lead to an increase in perceived benefits, thus
\par
increasing perceived customer value. On the other hand, the extra efforts spent in
\par
searching as a result of lack of information by the service provider could be considered
\par
as extra sacrifice, thus negatively influencing perceived value.
\par
Scholars have found that perceived value plays a key role in customer satisfaction
\par
(McDougall and Levesque, 2000; Patterson and Spreng, 1997). Value has been
\par
positioned as the central mediating construct to customer satisfaction in research
\par
(Anderson and Fornell, 1994; Fornell et al., 1996; McDougall and Levesque, 2000).
\par
Empirical research by Chu and Lu (2007) shows that perceived value of online music
\par
is a significant factor in predicting the purchaser's intention of buying online music in
\par
Taiwan. In summary, fair service is positively related to perceived customer value,
\par
which in turn affects customer satisfaction. Thus, we propose:
\par
H3. Systemic/overall fairness indirectly and positively affects satisfaction mediated
\par
through perceived customer value.
\par
Value and trust
\par
Some conceptual support of the trust-value relationship has been developed
\par
(Ratnasingham, 1998; Grabner-Kraeuter, 2002), however, limited and mixed empirical
\par
evidence has been reported (Harris and Goode, 2004). Sirdeshmukh et al. (2002)
