Research methodology
\par
Data for this research was drawn from surveys taken by customers of four major
\par
commercial banks in Taipei, Taiwan. We collected data from all branch offices that
\par
were willing to participate in the study in Taipei city. Questionnaires were handed out
\par
to bank customers that identified themselves as internet banking users and completed
\par
on a voluntary basis. A small gift, valued at approximately USD \$1, was given as a
\par
token of appreciation to users who completed the survey. A total of 362 surveys were
\par
collected; 331 out of 363 are valid. A detailed sample profile is presented in Table II.
\par
All questions adopted the five-point Likert scale from extremely disagree (1) to
\par
extremely agree (5). All items for the questionnaire were drawn from previous research.
\par
Some wording was adapted to fit the internet banking context. Since interpersonal
\par
fairness items ask about staff-to-customer personal interactions (e.g. staff treat users
\par
with dignity), which do not apply to the GUI-driven interaction pattern of ecommerce,
\par
this section of the questionnaire was removed.
\par
