The findings support H1, H2, and H3. Specifically, service fairness has a direct
\par
effect on customer satisfaction (coefficient 0.293). The direct effect, however, was much
\par
stronger in a separate model without the two mediators (coefficient 0.629). Together
\par
more than 50 percent of the effect of fairness to satisfaction is mediated through
\par
the two mediating constructs. In addition, the trust-value link is confirmed with a
\par
positive coefficient of 0.722. H3, however, which states that perceived customer value
\par
is a mediator to the service fairness and satisfaction association, is not supported. The
\par
fairness to perceived customer value association is not significant with a coefficient
\par
of 0.024. Further examination into the model reveals interesting findings of why
\par
perceived value failed to be a statistically significant moderator. In a separate
\par
model where the trust to perceived value link is removed, perceived value is found to
\par
be a statistically significant moderator of the fairness to satisfaction association
\par
(see Figure 3). Thus, it seems that because we added trust to perceived value
\par
association besides the moderating effects, trust effectively becomes a moderator to
\par
the fairness-value association. And because the moderating effect of trust is so
\par
strong, the fairness to perceived value relationship is fully moderated by trust,
\par
resulting in the no-significant result of the fairness to perceived value relationship.
\par
