Conclusion and discussion
\par
This study aims to explore the role of fairness in internet banking customer
\par
satisfaction, and to examine possible mediators, namely trust and perceived customer
\par
value, between service fairness and consumers' satisfaction. The empirical analyses
\par
provide unique contributions to our knowledge in the domain of ecommerce service
\par
evaluation.
\par
First, our study provides one of the first studies of fairness with internet-based
\par
services. From our results, we can see that despite the lack of service provider and
\par
customer personal interaction in internet banking, perceived fairness still has positive
\par
effects on customer satisfaction. Second, our research provides insights into the
\par
mechanisms through which fairness influences customer satisfaction, which was
\par
previously assumed to be only a direct effect (Carr, 2007; Han et al., 2008). Trust
\par
emerges as an important mediator not only for the fairness to satisfaction association,
\par
but also for the fairness to value association, signifying the pivotal role of trust in the
\par
ecommerce context, and this finding echoes with Harris and Goode's (2004) conclusion
\par
that trust is central to online service dynamics. Compared with prevalent traditional
\par
service evaluation models identified by Brady et al. (2005), the service evaluation
\par
models for ecommerce may differ substantially in the importance of the role trust
\par
plays.
\par
Managerial implications
\par
These results have important implications for service managers. The results signify
\par
the importance of service fairness in internet banking customer's service evaluation
\par
process. Consistent with what Jacoby (1976) suggests, the concept of fairness, or
\par
justice, is not only a relative term depending on comparison with other people, but also
\par
an absolute term where norms and expectations are checked with service experience.
\par
Thus, even in an environment where most transactions are completed with the help
\par
of technology, such as internet banking, the importance of fairness is still not to be
\par
overlooked.
\par
Fairness consists of four dimensions: distributive, procedural, informational,
\par
and interpersonal. First, distributive fairness implies that service providers cannot
\par
favor any one or groups of their customers, at least openly, because consumers will
\par
care about whether they receive service equally (Carr, 2007). Distributive fairness is
\par
based on input/output ratio calculations. However, inputs (customers consider their
\par
inputs to be not only money but also emotion and affect) are subject to strong
\par
self-enhancing perceptual biases that cause people to give themselves more credit
\par
than is deserved and others less (Cook and Yamagishi, 1983). As a result, people
\par
usually consider themselves more worthy customers than they actually are and
\par
what service providers deem as fair treatment of customers according to their revenue
\par
contribution may not work well with the customers' own understanding. Thus, it is
\par
probably more appropriate not to advertise details of differential services favoring
\par
certain groups (e.g. VIP customers) to the whole customer base in order to avoid
\par
negative feelings of being unfairly treated. Instead, it is probably wiser to only
\par
advertise to eligible customers that are already qualified as VIP or close to receiving
\par
VIP status. Some empirical evidence has been reported supporting this view point. For
\par
example, Mcguire and Kimes (2006) found that open VIP treatment (VIP seatingpriority in restaurants) has a negative impact on other consumer's perception of
\par
fairness.
\par
Second, procedural fairness requires the service procedures to be of consistency,
\par
non-biased to any group, accuracy (free of error), correctability (allowing appeals
\par
and reversals), representativeness of all groups and ethicality (Seiders and Berry, 1998).
\par
The end no longer justifies the means. Service providers should work on providing
\par
a consistent, reasonable, and bias-free procedure in order to create fair perceptions. It is
\par
important to be both consistent and correctable, thus, in designing the procedures,
\par
the attitude should be do it right the first time in order to avoid major changes in the
\par
future (consistency); however, in further revisions, once appeals are confirmed to be
\par
reasonable, the response should be quick and flexible (Colquitt et al., 2001). In
\par
ecommerce, since customers usually interact with a GUI, it is important to provide
\par
ways of appeals, for example, links to send a comment or talk to an agent online. For
\par
business process design, every webpage or step in shopping/account management
\par
should have a ``back'' button so that decisions can be undone or modified before being
\par
finalized. Also, for procedural fairness, it is important to provide a ``confirm'' screen
\par
before a final decision is processed. These suggestions complement, and a considerable
\par
portion of them overlap with what have been proposed by information systems
\par
researchers as good ecommerce web site design from different theoretical perspectives.
\par
For example, in the two-factor model (hygiene and motivator) proposed by Zhang and
\par
von Dran (2000), navigation control and aids are listed as important motivating factors
\par
for user satisfaction. Cyr's (2008) model of information design, navigation design and
\par
visual design from the usability literature also stresses the importance of a
\par
navigational scheme that can help users easily access a web site.
\par
Third, informational fairness requires that service providers supply information or
\par
knowledge about procedures that demonstrate regard for people's concerns
\par
(Greenberg, 1993). For online services, building a sterling web site is only part of the
\par
story and the rest lies in the content of the web site. We offer four tips below on how to
\par
ensure informational fairness on web sites. First, product or service information
\par
should be complete, e.g. not only benefits, but also risks involved in financial products
\par
should be clearly and adequately described. Second, information should be unbiased,
\par
e.g. when selling financial services or products, it is against informational fairness to
\par
only promote and provide information about certain products or services that are the
\par
most profitable for the bank while providing no reference to other less lucrative
\par
services or products that the customers may actually need. Third, information should
\par
be available and easily accessible to users, e.g. intentionally shrinking the font size of
\par
disclaimers on web sites so that it is difficult for people to read does not fit into good
\par
informational fairness. Finally, information of various forms, e.g. text files, video, or
\par
audio, should be available for different people's needs as some may have reading
\par
difficulties or disabilities. These suggestions are consistent with Zhang and von Dran
\par
(2000)'s two-factor model (hygiene and motivator) for web site design and evaluation,
\par
where complete information, unbiased information, and updated information are
\par
important hygiene factors for user satisfaction. Cyr's (2008) model also listed
\par
information accuracy as one of the most important aspects of information design.
\par
Limitation and future research
\par
Several limitations of the study need to be noted. First, our data were collected from the
\par
customers of four major commercial banks in a single city. Customers from a more
\par
diverse background, i.e. nationality, culture, and ethnicity, on a random sampling basis,
\par
Satisfaction in
\par
internet banking
\par
493INTR
\par
22,4
\par
494
\par
would be preferred for better generalizability. Second, we concentrated on the effects
\par
of fairness in customer satisfaction and have neglected several other well-known
\par
constructs that has been identified to be related to customer satisfaction, for example,
\par
service quality. This may undermine our model's estimation accuracy. Third, in order
\par
to focus our research on fairness and satisfaction, other important dependent variables,
\par
such as loyalty or behavior intentions are not included in the model. This could be an
\par
avenue for future research.
\par
With that said, future research can proceed in several other directions. We believe
\par
that the fairness model has general applicability to other forms of ecommerce,
\par
e.g. online shopping. Second, recent research has addressed the role of cultural
\par
differences on consumers' behavior in ecommerce and called for more research from
\par
a cultural perspective (Gefen and Heart, 2006; Lim et al., 2004; Sia etal., 2009). How
\par
cultural differences affect customer's perception of fairness, trust, value, and
\par
satisfaction, thus, is worthy of further examination.
