The solution procedure for full-information strategy consists of two phases. The fi rst
\par
phase (coded in Matlab 2009b) is enumerating for each demand point all possible J*K
\par
supplier/carrier pairs that can serve it (i.e., all J*K*I combinations of supplier, carrier and
\par
demand point). The second uses output from the fi rst phase to formulate and solve the
\par
problem of determining the supplier-carrier pair that should be assigned to each demand
\par
point to minimise the network's total expected logistics cost. The steps in the fi rst phase are:
\par
{$\bullet$} From among the total of J*K*I possible combinations (assignments) select an arbitrary
\par
one that is yet to be enumerated; i.e., demand point \emph{i }is assigned to supplier and carrier
\par
pair (\emph{j,k})\emph{.}
\par
{$\bullet$} For the assignment in step one, the Matlab algorithm computes fi xed ordering cost
\par
and determines order quantity and reorder point (using formula 7 and 8) to minimise
\par
the objective function for demand point \emph{i}. Then \emph{TECijk},\emph{ }which is the corresponding
\par
minimum expected logistics cost for demand point \emph{i }when its replenishments from
\par
supplier \emph{j }are delivered by carrier \emph{k, }is stored for use as a parameter in the second phase.
\par
{$\bullet$} Check if all J*K*I possible assignments have been enumerated. If so, the algorithm
\par
goes to the second phase; otherwise, it returns to step 1.
\par
With the \emph{TECijk} values from the fi rst phase as inputs, the second phase optimisation problem
