\par
A key concern for critical terrorism studies is the extent to which counterterrorism con-
\par
tributes to the promotion and perpetuation of terrorism. When dealing with either the
\par
events leading to 9/11 or the current anti-Muslim movements in Europe, we owe serious
\par
attention to the self-generating process by which terrorism and counterterrorism operate
\par
as an edge that simultaneously and constitutively links and separates both aspects of the
\par
phenomenon. The 9/11 Commission Report established that the events could probably
\par
have been prevented; there were after all 50--60 officers who knew two of the future
\par
attackers were living in the United States. What requires analysis are the blind spots
\par
in counterterrorist thinking that lead to such failures and ultimately to the self-fulfilling
\par
nature of the war on terror. This article will examine the conceptual similarities between
\par
witchcraft societies and the counterterrorist thought and policies put in practice by var-
\par
ious US administrations -- having to do with the perversions of temporality, the logic of
\par
taboo, non-hypothetical knowledge, secret information, the passion for `expert' igno-
\par
rance, mystical causation and dual sovereignty. The need for an epistemic shift that will
\par
take into account the constitutive nature of discourse and the political subjectivities of
\par
the actors will be advocated.
\par
Keywords: counterterrorism; fantasy; self-fulfilling; epistemology; discourse;
\par
subjectivity
\par
At the end of the Cold War, with the armaments race and fears of nuclear holocaust over,
\par
the United States emerged as the only superpower with no hint of any military enemy on
\par
the horizon. And yet the US Defence budget has almost doubled during the last decade
\par
after 9/11; it is now larger than that of all other countries combined. Such costs are jus-
\par
tified because, after the Cold War, a new enemy has surfaced: the terrorist. The vast new
\par
security bureaucracy created after 9/11 encompasses some 1200 government organisations
\par
and 1900 companies. Estimates of the total bill for the war on terror range from \$3 trillion
\par
to \$4 trillion.
\par
But the economy is only one aspect of the political climate forced by terrorism. What
\par
is most extraordinary is that the state of exception was imposed on American politics as a
\par
result of the war on terror. The evidence is there for anyone to see: Guant\'anamo, indefinite
\par
detention, rendition, torture and extra-judicial killings by drones. These are the daily staple
\par
news of current politics, officially sanctioned and normalised and broadly accepted by the
\par
general public. And yet, these are borderline realities at the intersection of law and politics,
\par
*Email: zulaika@unr.edu
\par
ISSN 1753-9153 print/ISSN 1753-9161 online
\par
\copyright{} 2012 Taylor \& Francis
\par
http://dx.doi.org/10.1080/17539153.2012.659909
\par
http://www.tandfonline.com52 J. Zulaika
\par
in the past clearly illegal and anathema, now official policy. The state of exception means
\par
that lawbreaking can be approved by the highest officials and go unpunished. As Agamben
\par
put it, a situation obtains in which `it is impossible to distinguish transgression of the
\par
law from the execution of the law, such that what violates a rule and what conforms to it
\par
coincide without any reminder' (1998, p. 7).
\par
What concerns me here is a crisis of knowledge in terrorism studies. Right from its
\par
inception, a sign of this crisis has been the difficulties of not only defining the term itself,
\par
but the very interpretive frameworks of the events covered by the concept. A critical anal-
\par
ysis must inquire into the genealogy of this discourse and world view, beginning with the
\par
very naming of the phenomenon; it must examine its conceptual premises and policies
\par
and question its politics and ethics. In my latest work, I have been engaged in showing
\par
the extent to which counterterrorism, caught in its own spiral of a self-fulfilling prophecy,
\par
becomes terrorism's best ally. The events leading to the attack on the twin towers provide
\par
evidence for such an argument. It is a fact recognised by the 9/11 Commission Report
\par
that the plotters could have been found and the attacks prevented. It is well known that
\par
the blind sheik, bin Laden, and his followers had been in the past close associates of US
\par
counterintelligence services. After the removal of Saddam Hussein, there was information,
\par
based on the CIA's interviews with him, that he had been bluffing as to his possession or
\par
attempts to possess weapons of mass destruction (WMD). It is also established that reac-
\par
tions to 9/11, such as the war in Iraq and policies implemented by Paul Bremer after the
\par
invasion, added to the problem of terrorism. On the basis of this set of facts, the criti-
\par
cal question should be, ex ante, did counterterrorism actually contribute, by omission or
\par
by action, to making 9/11 happen? Ex post facto, what are the premises and blind spots
\par
in counterterrorism that not only allowed 9/11, but contributed with reactions to making
\par
the problem much worse? It is in response to such questions that I presented the thesis
\par
of counterterrorism as a self-fulfilling prophecy (see Zulaika 2009). In the section below,
\par
I will add how the latest chapter in counterterrorism -- the drone attacks -- conforms to the
\par
same pattern.
\par
My theoretical ground was borrowed initially from discourse analysis and from the
\par
anthropological study of taboo, to which I added in my latest book current psychoanalyt-
\par
ical understandings of subjectivity. I did not argue that the intelligence communities were
\par
unconcerned about terrorist threats, much less voluntarily negligent. The officers in charge
\par
of preventing terrorism are likely to be the most concerned ones and the first in wanting to
\par
do a more efficient job. And I am not surprised that over 700 security studies scholars wrote
\par
in their `Open Letter to the American People' after admitting that the war in Iraq was `the
\par
most misguided one since the Vietnam period', the following: `One result has been a great
\par
distortion in terms of public debate on foreign and national security policy -- an emphasis
\par
on speculation instead of facts' (cited in Horgan and Boyle 2008, p. 54). My contention
\par
was that the generally admitted massive failure of counterterrorism in preventing 9/11 is
\par
paradigmatic of its blind spots and that we can learn the most from them. My argument
\par
was that these are problems that have to do directly with a faulty epistemology -- beginning
\par
with the placement of the entire phenomenon in a context of taboo and the wilful ignorance
\par
of the political subjectivities of the terrorists.
\par
The investigation of the crisis of knowledge in the field of terrorism takes me to exam-
\par
ine in this article the force of fantasy in counterterrorism. One has to begin such a study by
\par
examining what counts as the standard of evidence and as valuable information in such a
\par
context of taboo, what type of experience should be respected and what sort of associative
\par
logic links together various kinds of events. My argument is that such faulty epistemol-
\par
ogy derives critically from the lack of attention paid to the role played by fantasy in theCritical Studies on Terrorism 53
\par
entire terrorism phenomenon. Thus, I am not primarily concerned with the usual criticisms
\par
received by the critical current of terrorism studies, namely, whether the liberal state should
\par
be included in the analysis, what is understood by `critical', its `emancipation' agenda and
\par
so on. My contention is rather that, in order to identify the blind spots in our knowledge of
\par
terrorism, the role of fantasy is a central issue. The cultural and political consequences of
\par
such work could not be more urgent.
\par
Flying drones over Pakistan from Las Vegas
\par
Pilotless drones to identify and destroy terrorist targets are the latest in counterterrorism --
\par
`the only game in town', as it is widely referred to, echoing the former CIA director and
\par
current defence secretary Leon Panetta. Counterterrorism's basic approach has always con-
\par
sisted in tabooing outlawed terrorists as untouchables with whom no communication of
\par
any sort was permitted. The latest in counterterrorism, pilotless drones flying in the sky at
\par
10,000 feet, the only contact between the hunter and the hunted, a Hellfire missile launched
\par
from a drone that might be operated by CIA agents 7500 miles away at Nellis Air Force
\par
Base near Las Vegas in the Nevada desert, perfects the strategy of contact at a distance. The
\par
combat air patrols of the drones at any one time has gone from 21 in 2007 to 38 in 2009 to
\par
54 in 2011 (Sluka 2011, p. 70). The flying robots, we are told, soon will be `in the position
\par
to take the initiative against the enemy on a battlefield' and that `the pressure to let robots
\par
take the shot will be very hard to resist' (Caryl 2011, p. 58). Not only in the air, `there are
\par
already more robots operating on the ground (15,000) than in the air (7000)' (Caryl 2011,
\par
p. 55). There are 7000 drones ready to fly in the air and 15,000 on the ground. This is the
\par
new technological scenario, a Nintendo war in which subjectless machines will identify
\par
and eliminate terrorists. Terrorism's metaphoric hierarchy used to be that a superhuman
\par
entity (a country, an army, an organisation) killed a subhuman being (a `dog', a `pig' or
\par
some other beast); in the new weaponry, `drones' mimic animals such as hummingbirds
\par
and seagulls.
\par
The strength of the emerging robotic weaponry, we are told, consists in `their ability
\par
to see and think' (Caryl 2011, p. 55). The drones can programme a destination and fly by
\par
themselves; they can follow a target for days from the invisible altitude, while the faraway
\par
operator is never in danger of being killed from below. One thing that is obvious about
\par
unmanned machines is that they do not have desires of their own, nor intentions, nor are
\par
subjectively responsible for their actions -- and they cannot commit war crimes, as the
\par
required intent to commit them is missing from machines. The killing itself might perhaps
\par
now be done in a subjectless manner by machines finding out who the terrorists are and
\par
acting on their own.
\par
The disturbing thing for counterterrorism is that both groups, terrorists and
\par
counterterrorists, have desires and subjectivities. Studies have shown (Lindlaw 2008) that
\par
the far-removed operators of the drone suffer the same combat stress as the combatants in a
\par
war zone. They cannot avoid the fact that innocent civilians are being killed by the Hellfire
\par
missiles they fire -- including an estimated 168 children. It is hard to know how many peo-
\par
ple have been killed by drones (between 2000 and 3000), let alone how many of them are
\par
civilians. Estimates vary widely. Based on media reports, some Western estimates put the
\par
number of militants among the dead at 85\%. The Pakistani daily Dawn calculated that of
\par
the 708 people killed in 2009, only five were known militants; the other major English-
\par
speaking daily in Pakistan, The News, estimated that of the 701 people killed by drones
\par
between January 2006 and April 2009, only 14 were known militants (Ahmad 2011). The
\par
area is sealed by Pakistan, the available sources being the CIA and Pakistani secret services.54 J. Zulaika
\par
Of the people killed, the CIA knows the names of 125 people and considers 35 of them as
\par
`high value targets' (Ahmad 2011).
\par
Who are these hundreds of killed people of whom we do not even know their names?
\par
Counterterrorists' `passion for ignorance' is well documented on the grounds of lack of
\par
basic knowledge regarding the languages or cultures of the peoples they are engaged with,
\par
let alone disinterest in their political goals or subjective motivation. Turning into fatal tar-
\par
gets, anonymous people about whom not even their names are known, marks a new bar.
\par
But such issues, let alone the objection of why the 35 high-value targets should be subject
\par
to extrajudicial killing when the United States is not at war with Pakistan, are superfluous
\par
in the counterterrorist mindset. You only need apply the logic of taboo typical of terrorist
\par
contamination: when anti-Americanism is at an all-time high of 90\% in countries such as
\par
Pakistan, due in good part to the drone attacks, can anyone on the ground be completely
\par
`innocent' from actual or potential terrorism? Indeed, `the agency can target all suspect
\par
militants based on ``pattern of life'' analysis collected from surveillance cameras' (Ahmad
\par
2011). Since most adult males traditionally carry guns, such `pattern of life' makes them
\par
all potential targets. But guns might not be necessary, for, as a CIA officer told Jane Mayer
\par
of the New Yorker, `no tall man with a beard is safe anywhere in Southwest Asia' (Ahmad
\par
2011). Why should you want to know their names before you kill them?
\par
It is not surprising therefore that reliable information is extremely hard to get and that
\par
American and Pakistani official sources issue wildly divergent claims. The impossibility
\par
of even counting whether the dead bodies are militants or civilians, when both countries
\par
are allegedly close allies in the war on terror, is one more implication of the secrecy sur-
\par
rounding counterterrorism; so secret are the attacks that the CIA denies that the drone
\par
programme even exists, let alone the criteria by which it chooses its targets. There is not,
\par
however, anything secret about such wilfully upheld secrecy turned into a tool of informa-
\par
tion control, so that even a body count becomes an act of `magical realism' (Ahmad 2011),
\par
as there are recurrent cases of people `killed' who rose from the dead several times and
\par
were killed in later attacks. The Pakistani government publicly disapproves of the attacks
\par
but privately they provide intelligence and support. Pakistani sovereignty is another area
\par
shrouded in mystery, as it depends on the secret agreements it has with the Americans.
\par
Are these drone attacks legal? `Outside the context of armed conflict, the use of drones
\par
for targeted killing is almost never likely to be legal,' wrote Philip Alston (cited in Caryl
\par
2011, p. 56). But for the global war on terror, is there anything anywhere that is not
\par
`armed conflict'? Thus, under the guise of combating terrorism, US counterterrorism con-
\par
siders itself legitimised to overrule national sovereignties and flaunt international law; in
\par
short, to establish a state of exception by which actions that ordinarily are illegal and
\par
immoral are suddenly tolerable because the fight against the terrorist demands it. When
\par
in the 1980s, various European countries enacted policies of `shoot-to-kill' against sus-
\par
pected terrorists, they were met with international outrage (Zulaika and Douglass 1996,
\par
pp. 158--160). Extrajudicial killings of hundreds of suspect militants, of whom we even
\par
ignore their names, are now perpetrated in the name of the war on terror; they no longer
\par
elicit international scandal but are praised as most effective.
\par
Ignoring that we know: the impasses in knowledge
\par
What are the practical results of the drone campaign? The number of terrorist attacks in
\par
Pakistan has gone up sharply in a wave of anti-Americanism, for Pakistanis `overwhelm-
\par
ingly believe that most of those who die in the attacks are civilians' (Caryl 2011, p. 56).
\par
Given the fact that drone strikes `have had a particular affinity for hitting weddings andCritical Studies on Terrorism 55
\par
funerals, and appear to be seriously fuelling the insurgency' (Sluka 2011, p. 72), it is no
\par
surprise that Taliban recruits have increased. Air strikes are prominent in motivating sui-
\par
cide attacks, a UN report concluded; surveys show direct links between family members
\par
killed and joining or supporting the insurgency (Sluka 2011, p. 72). One concrete instance
\par
of such links was provided by Faisal Shahzad, the Pakistani-American known for the failed
\par
bomb in Times Square in May 2010, who declared in his trial that `I'm avenging the attack'
\par
for the `drones [that] kill women, children. . . everybody. . . I am part of the answer' (cited
\par
in Hari 2010). Sluka's conclusion is shared by many experts: `By losing hearts and minds,
\par
the UAV [drones] war in Afghanistan and Pakistan is losing the fight against and increasing
\par
the threat of terrorism, and making further terror attacks on American more likely, not less'
\par
(Sluka 2011, p. 76).
\par
Do counterterrorists ignore these too obvious links? Of course not. David Kilcullen
\par
is, among others, an example of a `counterterrorist guru' aware that drone attacks have a
\par
negative effect. He told Congress in April 2009:
\par
Since 2006, we've killed 14 senior Al-Qaeda leaders using drone strikes; in the same period,
\par
we've killed 700 Pakistani civilians in the same area. The drone strikes are highly unpopular.
\par
They are deeply aggravating to the population. And they've given rise to a feeling of anger
\par
that coalesces the population around the extremists and leads to spikes of extremism. . . The
\par
current path that we are on is leading us to loss of Pakistani government control over its own
\par
population. (Cited in Sluka 2011, p. 73)
\par
The ratio of civilians killed for each militant is 50:1, Kilcullen observed; that is, 98\% of
\par
drone casualties are civilians -- so much for technological surgical `precision'. That this is
\par
self-fulfilling is a given for Kilcullen: `Every one of these dead non-combatants [creates]
\par
an alienated family, a new desire for revenge, and more recruits for a militant movement
\par
that has grown exponentially even as drone strikes have increased' (cited in Sluka 2011,
\par
p. 73). Kilcullen concluded that the use of such drones is `immoral'.
\par
Counterterrorists know all of this. Yet why is it that these very drones, which help
\par
increase terrorist insurgency `exponentially', are still `the only game in town'? In short,
\par
counterterrorism knows that its tactics operate clearly along the path of a self-fulfilling
\par
feedback, and yet there is nothing else better to do. Such an impasse -- if we do nothing,
\par
terrorism will flourish; if we do something it will flourish even more -- shows dramatically
\par
the current crisis in counterterrorist knowledge.
\par
Kilcullen's (2009) recent book, The Accidental Guerrilla, dramatically states the
\par
changes needed in US counterterrorist thinking while also showing the core deadlocks in
\par
which it is trapped. A close associate of General David Petraeus in the successful `surge'
\par
to end the war in Iraq, Kilcullen is a vocal advocate of applying a strategy of protecting
\par
the population and, contrary to the usual counterterrorism strategy that saw this as a `soft
\par
approach' (2009, p. 145), negotiating with and of co-opting local leaders. Kilcullen is one
\par
of the most vocal exponents of the implications of terrorism's `asymmetric warfare' -- the
\par
stark facts that `the United States is spending in excess of \$400 million per day in Iraq'
\par
and that `the United States has so far spent \$1.4 million per dollar of AQ [Al Qaeda]
\par
investment in the attacks on the response' (2009, pp. 25, 274). He is also much aware
\par
that, in relation to the other countries, `the U.S. defense budget accounted for 54.5 percent
\par
of total global defense spending in 2007', and internally, `the U.S. Defense Department
\par
is about 210 times larger than the U.S. Agency for International Development and the
\par
State Department combined' (2009, pp. 22, 298). Faced with these staggering numbers,
\par
Kilcullen does not hesitate to state that with bin Laden `we have turned a mouse into an56 J. Zulaika
\par
elephant' and that the war on terror has `largely played into the hands of this AQ exhaustion
\par
strategy'. He makes a strong case for `a radical rethinking of some key Western policies,
\par
strategies, and attitudes' (2009, pp. 38, 263, 264).
\par
Kilcullen, however, does not see in his own proposals anything radical or controver-
\par
sial conceptually, for `[m]uch of the best strategic work by the State Department, defense
\par
departments of contributing powers, and NATO planners conforms exactly to these pre-
\par
scriptions' (2009, p. 114). He saw the decision to invade Iraq as `an extremely serious
\par
strategic error' and warned against it, but then he adds, again, so did `almost every other
\par
counterinsurgency professional' (2009, p. 268). So how is it that the military establish-
\par
ment knew that the consequences of Iraq would be disastrous, officers being aware that the
\par
hubris behind the assumption of the efficacy of American military power was breathtaking
\par
and yet we went to war? Much like how is it that, previous to 9/11, 50--60 officers knew
\par
for many months that two of the future hijackers were in the country and nothing was done
\par
about it. Much like how it is that currently counterterrorism knows that drones will produce
\par
terrorism `exponentially' and yet this is the much hyped `only game in town'? These are
\par
known facts, yet they are made to be unknown when concrete policies are to be taken. This
\par
`passion for ignorance' points to the core of the crisis of knowledge in counterterrorism.
\par
Droning, writing, divining: the perversion of temporality
\par
`The self-fulfilling prophecy is, in the beginning,' writes sociologist Robert Merton,
\par
a false definition of the situation evoking a new behavior which makes the original false con-
\par
ception come true. This specious validity of the self-fulfilling prophecy perpetuates a reign of
\par
error. For the prophet will cite the actual course of events as proof that he was right from the
\par
very beginning. . . .Such are the perversities of social logic. (1968, p. 477)
\par
It was false that there was al-Qaeda in Iraq before the invasion but then it became true
\par
after the invasion. Anti-American radical Islamists could never afford to have antiaircraft
\par
missiles, until the CIA provided Stinger missiles to Afghan rebels battling the Soviets in
\par
the mid-1980s. Similarly, over 40 countries are currently developing drone technology to
\par
be used as military robots, with the likelihood that in a not faraway future they might fall
\par
into the hands of terrorists. Such self-fulfilling prophecy of counterterrorist drones being
\par
used by terrorists, we are told, `is not far away' (Caryl 2011, p. 58).
\par
Kilcullen's main concern is with the formation of what he labels `the accidental
\par
guerrilla' -- ordinary people caught in the fight between terrorists and counterterrorists,
\par
who end up supporting the local fight against the outsiders and who would be impossi-
\par
ble to distinguish from the terrorists, `except by accident'. His thesis is that most of the
\par
adversaries encountered by Western powers after 9/11 are the products of such accident--
\par
`people who fight us not because they hate the West and seek our overthrow but because
\par
we have invaded their space to deal with a small extremist element' (Caryl 2011, pp. 34,
\par
263). But even if `accidental' and a `guerrilla' (and not a `terrorist'), still Kilcullen remains
\par
within the counterterrorist mindset when he immediately proposes that: `I theorise that the
\par
accidental guerrilla emerges from a cyclical process that takes place in four stages: infec-
\par
tion, contagion, intervention, and rejection' (2009, p. 35). The `cyclical process' is thus
\par
not ruled by cultural or political circumstances or by the logic of historical repetition and
\par
action/reaction linkages, but by the medical analogy of `a virus or bacterium' -- the pri-
\par
mary associations of `infection' and `contagion' which point to a thinking that is typical ofCritical Studies on Terrorism 57
\par
how the logic of witchcraft and taboo operate in ethnographic communities (Zulaika and
\par
Douglass 1996).
\par
The failed approach to insurgency in Iraq is characterised by Kilcullen as `enemy-
\par
centric' (2009, p. 129), with the focus on killing the terrorists. But despite his criticisms
\par
of the very notion of the war on terror, still he cannot avoid framing counterterrorism as
\par
a war, his main reference for comparison/contrast being the Cold War. Kilcullen finds it
\par
a `limited analogy', `[y]et in at least one dimension, that of time, the enduring trends that
\par
drive the current confrontation may mean that the conflict will indeed resemble the Cold
\par
War' (2009, p. 301). I agree that time is central to our analysis but not in the sense that `we
\par
are at the beginning of a very long road', rather, in the perversion of temporality implied
\par
in such `waiting for terror'. In such temporality of waiting, one has to start with the non-
\par
hypothetical premise that no matter what, there will be terrorism. Imagine all the terrorists
\par
disappeared tomorrow; still, for Kilcullen, `All these [terrorism] trends would endure even
\par
if AQ and its takfiri allies disappeared tomorrow' (2009, p. 294). What is indisputable is
\par
that we should look at `AQ as a harbinger of a new era of conflict' (2009, p. 294). It is
\par
not if, but when. The bedrock premise is that there will always be war and now there will
\par
always be terrorism.
\par
And once terrorism is defined as inevitable, its very logic will tend to make it self-
\par
fulfilling by producing a behaviour that will make the original false definition come true.
\par
The reality itself is that `terrorism constantly morphs into new mutations,' Kilcullen tells
\par
us, and therefore `counterterrorism methods. . . are almost by definition already obso-
\par
lete' (2009, p. 294). Yet, the one fact that can never be put into question is that the thing
\par
itself is terrorism. The assassination of President Kennedy was not a terrorist act at the
\par
time; nowadays, by one of those `mutations' into which it is morphed, it would be the ter-
\par
rorist act by excellence. Your own discursive definitions make things be what you want
\par
them to be. Kilcullen is very aware that there are other threats as well, such as prolif-
\par
eration of nuclear weapons, climate change, resource conflict, rogue states, actual wars
\par
between states and so on; but terrorism will always be a distinct one, non-reducible to
\par
those other threats.
\par
`A new lexicon' is Kilcullen's first proposal among his `tentative conclusions' (2009,
\par
p. 300). Again, counterterrorism knows that its vocabulary and discourse produce in part
\par
the thing itself of terrorism. But what I find most surprising is that, in the study of terror-
\par
ism, he advocates not international relations (the grand narrative we read about in daily
\par
news about the internationalisation of terrorism), but anthropology -- with its method-
\par
ology to `[g]et as close as possible (in time and space) to the actual events, ideally by
\par
being present when they unfold but, at the very least, by seeking firsthand descriptions
\par
from eyewitnesses' (2009, p. 304). As an ethnographer of political violence myself, I find
\par
this recommendation extraordinary in its incongruence with counterterrorism's ordinary
\par
modus operandi.
\par
In fact, as I completed my ethnography of Basque violence (1988), I was faced with
\par
the existence of an international committee of terrorism experts to tackle the very problem
\par
I had just studied and which issued a report to much media fanfare that left me wonder-
\par
ing what claims to `knowledge' did anthropology, as opposed to counterterrorism, have
\par
(Zulaika 1991). The basic recommendation by the terrorism experts was blunt: never see a
\par
terrorist (not even on television), never listen to a terrorist (not even on a radio) and never
\par
talk to a terrorist. Anthropology was called upon as a discipline that could help bring valu-
\par
able information to carry out the grand counterterrorist agenda of cleansing the Basque
\par
Country from terrorism. The Irish were given a similar advice, lest someone might identify
\par
with their cause.58 J. Zulaika
\par
Kilcullen's proposal that counterterrorism should turn into anthropology, I assume on
\par
the basis of the experience gained in Iraq's surge, is a radical departure from the former
\par
strategy of complete tabooing and distancing from the untouchable terrorists. But this is
\par
unrealistic and misses the key issue: counterterrorism has established a political and moral
\par
cordon sanitaire regarding terrorism, a strict taboo backed with the most stringent laws that
\par
de facto prohibits conducting an ethnography of terrorism. Even interviewing a terrorist in
\par
prison, let alone while active, is out of the question, as proven recently by the Bureau of
\par
Prisons' rejection to grant access to a group of scholars proposing to investigate how they
\par
got into terrorism (Shane 2011, p. 26). Truman Capote could write a compelling narrative
\par
of multiple murder, identifying with the killers; an equivalent identification with terrorists
\par
would turn into the anathema of an apology of terrorism (Zulaika 2009, pp. 37--59). Indeed,
\par
one could argue that, as the result of writing, the same `murder' becomes a different real-
\par
ity in the hands of a historian, a sociologist, a journalist, a novelist or a counterterrorist.
\par
Similarly, you could argue that the Basque political violence of my ethnography at a village
\par
level was not the same `thing' as the Basque terrorism of the international experts (Zulaika
\par
and Douglass 1996, pp. 31--63). In short, there is again this impasse in Kilcullen's proposal,
\par
by which an anthropological approach might in fact dissolve the violence into cultural pat-
\par
terns and political causes, and yet while terrorism remains the undisputable, permanent
\par
category and counterterrorism `the only game in town'.
\par
A central dimension of terrorism, and one that is crucial to show its self-fulfilling qual-
\par
ity, has to do with threats and their perception and the reactions they provoke. A threat plays
\par
with the sign as representing a future event, while we never know whether the issuer actu-
\par
ally means it or not or whether he might change his opinion in the future. The Unabomber
\par
brought the traffic in California airports to a halt by simply sending a letter to a newspaper
\par
with the threat of bringing down an airliner, while he sent another letter to another newspa-
\par
per admitting that the threat was a `prank'. The actual reality of the threat might be nothing
\par
but play: a zero that can yet have deadly serious consequences. Counterterrorism is a prime
\par
example of what Merton labelled `the Thomas theorem', namely: `If men define situations
\par
as real, they are real in their consequences' (1968, p. 475). Once the situation is defined
\par
as one of inevitable terrorism and endless waiting, what could happen weighs as much as
\par
what is actually the case; once a threat, whose intention or possibility is unknown to us,
\par
is taken seriously, its reality requires that we must act on it. Terrorism is the catalyst for
\par
confusing various semantic levels of linguistic, ritual and military actions.
\par
Anthropologists have examined phenomena such as divination, which manipulates the
\par
axis of time in a cultural context of magic and witchcraft. They have compared premodern
\par
mystical notions of causation and temporality to our own modern standards of rationality.
\par
The discussion leads directly to issues of ontology and epistemology: what is the thing
\par
itself of magic or taboo or divination? What is the nature of `reality' and what type of
\par
`knowledge' is involved in such `beliefs' by the natives of those cultures as well as by the
\par
`interpretations' produced by their analysts?
\par
As we ask what the rationale and the nature of the information supporting
\par
counterterrorism's most fateful decisions were, such as those taken by the Bush
\par
Administration before going to war with Iraq or by Bremer after the invasion, an anthropo-
\par
logically informed observer is reminded of secret oracle consultations among the Azande,
\par
as famously described by Edward Evans-Pritchard, and which were summed up thus by
\par
Peter Winch:
\par
Oracular revelations are not treated as hypotheses and, since their sense derives from the way
\par
they are treated in their context, they therefore are not hypotheses. They are not a matter ofCritical Studies on Terrorism 59
\par
intellectual interest but the main way in which Azande decide how they should act. If the oracle
\par
reveals that a proposed course of action is fraught with mystical dangers from witchcraft or
\par
sorcery, that course of action will not be carried out; and then the question of refutation or
\par
confirmation just does not arise. We might say that the revelation has the logical status of an
\par
unfulfilled hypothetical. (1977, p. 88)
\par
The need to consult oracles concerning witchcraft responds to the urge to know the causa-
\par
tion of unfortunate events. How they happen is perceived by the senses; why they happen
\par
is what magical thinking is all about. This is where revelations by oracles become essential
\par
to Azande thought. The spirit of consulting an oracle is obviously very unlike a scientist's
\par
experiments.
\par
The central premise of counterterrorism thinking is the oft-repeated formula that `it
\par
is not if, but when'. Hypotheticals are premised with the conditional `if': `if A, then B'.
\par
What characterises basic counterterrorist knowledge about the next impending attack is
\par
that it will happen. In a mindset that parallels Azande witchcraft, the counterterrorist axiom
\par
of `not if' rules out mere hypotheses. The revelations are thus `unfulfilled hypotheticals'
\par
that will become real with time. Counterterrorist projections are the equivalent of oracular
\par
certainties: the horror will happen no matter what. This leads in pragmatic terms to the
\par
fatalistic attitude of disregarding actual knowledge and not taking responsibility for actual
\par
decisions: what does it really matter what we decide since it is going to happen anyway and
\par
whatever happens is out of our hands? What matters, therefore, is that we sort of divine
\par
what the course of action will be.
\par
The practical aspect of this temporality of waiting, in which the certainty of the impend-
\par
ing evil is beyond any hypothetical (`not if'), is that we need to act pre-emptively now
\par
against events that are to happen in the future. The rationale behind nuclear deterrence was
\par
that developing armaments now, ready to strike at the push of a button, guaranteed that they
\par
would not be used in the future. Many commentators saw in such logic the quintessence
\par
of technological madness. But that was not enough. Since future nuclear attacks by terror-
\par
ists are only a matter of time, we must wage a war now pre-emptively, even in a nuclear
\par
context, thus breaking the historic assumption that nuclear arsenals were for deterrence,
\par
not for actual usage. What justifies the use of a `just war' in the nuclear era is the desire
\par
of terrorists for having the WMD we possess. There is nothing evil or irrational about
\par
having or using them, as it is an established fact that terrorists desire them and one day
\par
will have them. Thus, the formula of `not if, but when' becomes a self-fulfilling prophecy.
\par
The counterterrorist thinking makes it an imperative that the war must start now -- against
\par
Saddam Hussein, against al-Qaeda, against Iran and against all potential terrorists. This is
\par
how the American public, including the liberal media, accepted the rationale to go to war
\par
against Iraq.
\par
It is the play with the axis of time that is most revealing of the manipulations of associa-
\par
tive magic, as well as of counterterrorist thinking. The oracle, based on secret knowledge,
\par
reveals whether witchcraft has transpired and whether its danger looms ahead. The terrorist
\par
threat also creates the temporality of waiting. Actual historical temporality becomes sub-
\par
servient to the feared future. If there are no terrorism attacks, the counterterrorist can claim
\par
success in preventing them; but if the attack does occur, then the counterterrorist can say
\par
`I told you so' and argue that he was right in his predictions. At this point, terrorism fore-
\par
told becomes prophecy fulfilled. Such imperviousness to error in actual historical events
\par
points to a time warp that goes to the heart of counterterrorist mythology. Such waiting
\par
implies in fact that historical time has surrendered itself to a fateful future.60 J. Zulaika
\par
The result of this passive temporality regarding events we can do nothing to prevent is
\par
a fateful mindset in which the terror events are closer to nature than society and politics,
\par
and there is hardly any point in looking into the intellectual premises or subjective moti-
\par
vations that guide terrorist actions. The great political victory of the suicide bombers is
\par
that they imposed on US politics their own suicidal temporality of waiting and a culture
\par
grounded on the oracular knowledge of secret intelligence, which then justified the war on
\par
terror.
\par
Secret knowledge and the state of exception
\par
Kilcullen's trenchant, internal criticism of counterterrorism goes to the core of its culture
\par
when in his concluding `new paradigms' he dares question a belief-based intelligence:
\par
Why, for example, did Western intelligence miss the imminent fall of the Soviet Union in
\par
1992? In part, at least, because we were reading the Soviet leaders' mail -- and they themselves
\par
failed to understand the depth of grassroots disillusionment with Communism. Why did most
\par
countries (including those who opposed the Iraq war) believe in 2002 that Saddam Hussein had
\par
WMDs? Because they were intercepting the regime's communications, and many senior Iraqi
\par
regime members believed Iraq them. . . [while] their resources were. . . following analytical
\par
dead ends. (Kilcullen 2009, p. 293)
\par
The crisis of counterterrorist knowledge begins with the nature and context of the `intelli-
\par
gence' gathered. We need to inquire as to what is valid information in the counterterrorist
\par
situation, what critical information is missing from it and what controls are exerted over
\par
such information. Forty percent of the US military budget is secret (Johnson 2007, p. 209),
\par
as are the budgets of the intelligence agencies.
\par
The basic political context of counterterrorist intelligence in the Bush-declared war on
\par
terror parallels what anthropologists have categorised as `dual sovereignty'. In many soci-
\par
eties, there is an opposition and complementarity between the jural/administrative power
\par
of the chiefs and the secretive, mystical authority of priests and sorcerers. A classic case is
\par
the Shilluk's divine kingship studied by Edward Evans-Pritchard (1948). The relationship
\par
between the open political process and the secret covert action required by the war on terror
\par
evokes in our own society a similar dual sovereignty by which legal authority is comple-
\par
mented with a different type of power that finds its legitimacy in the elusive principle of
\par
national security. If overt politics is based on the rule of law, covert politics is grounded on
\par
secret information that cannot be shared with the public at large.
\par
The secretive power needed to confront `unseen forces' in societies ruled by dual
\par
sovereignty can be related to the concept of the `state of exception', when, in Agamben's
\par
formulation, the sovereign is both inside and outside the law, and execution and trans-
\par
gression of law coincide. Guant\'anamo can be seen as both civilisation's ultimate bastion
\par
against terrorism and the ultimate denial of international law. Who can decide whether the
\par
thousands of people killed by drone attacks are the lawful killings required by the war on
\par
terror or are illegal murders? The sovereign is paradoxically both inside and outside the
\par
law.
\par
What is the meaning of `information' in such terrorist scenarios of states of exception
\par
and in the presence of a community of believers whose basic structure separates those
\par
who `know' the secret information and the rest of us who are to be kept in the dark?
\par
Secrecy means that no critical judgment can be exercised, much like in mystical societies
\par
where knowledge belongs only to the sacred specialist. Questioning the effectiveness of the
\par
information amounts to lack of faith and goes directly against the community of believers.Critical Studies on Terrorism 61
\par
In the days previous to invading Iraq, the frequent visits by Cheney to the intelligence
\par
community are well documented; essentially, the community did not know what it was
\par
asked to know regarding Iraq and the war on terror. Thus, knowledge had to be created
\par
by tying together the bits and pieces of scattered information while adding inferences and
\par
guesswork in order to come up with a plausible scenario. A new intelligence unit was
\par
formed at the Pentagon. Only a handful of senators read the classified intelligence about
\par
Iraq prior to authorising war.
\par
Intelligence becomes ancillary information when belief drives knowledge. Once the
\par
decision has been made that the enemy is Hitler, the ordinary standards of factual evidence
\par
are supplemented with untested premises grounded on moral and political principles. The
\par
main role of information is no longer procuring factual evidence, but helping uncover the
\par
secret intentions of the evildoer. And, given the final goal of defeating evil, if information
\par
that contradicts the belief system is provided, then it is better to reject it. Thus, the likes of
\par
Wolfowitz become `this know-it-all who won't believe the intelligence community' (Ricks
\par
2006, p. 30). The `know-it-all' is essentially in possession of a secret type of knowledge,
\par
having to do with the realisation that the world is confronting evil -- a secrecy that allows
\par
the knower to disregard ordinary information. In factual terms, there might be a problem,
\par
namely, that `[h]e's deeply misguided, he's impervious to evidence' (Ricks 2006, p. 31) --
\par
including the evidence of lack of evidence -- but we are by now deeply immersed in a type
\par
of knowledge consisting in a world view engaged in transforming the evil reality at hand.
\par
The concerns of military men about the lack of preparation to go to war, as well as of
\par
the advisers of the previous administration who suspected that evidence was being fabri-
\par
cated, were dismissed by the Bush administration. Once you are a crusader who `knows'
\par
that Saddam Hussein stands for the apocalyptic combination of terrorism and WMD, the
\par
ordinary standards of evidence, information and knowledge no longer apply -- not even
\par
those of your own military and intelligence communities. By then the only moral imper-
\par
ative is creating and manipulating information to defeat the evil enemy. This is no longer
\par
ordinary politics, but counterterrorism is by then `a religiously sanctioned moral duty'
\par
(Leach 1977, p. 36).
\par
But, if the reports of Hussein's WMD bluff are true, such a crusading attitude was
\par
unable to detect it. The inability to sort out real threat from a feigned one is in a nut-
\par
shell the problem with counterterrorism. The problem is fundamentally epistemological,
\par
namely, what type of knowledge is missing if the actuality or the fakery of a threat cannot
\par
be assessed? It is the kind of problem an ethnographer faces in an alien culture, a psychoan-
\par
alyst in the symptoms of a patient, a writer such as Capote when he wants to produce a true
\par
account of a multiple murderer or when a detective has to solve a crime. Why should we
\par
not demand from a counterterrorist the basic knowledge and skills of current knowledge
\par
as we do from these other types of writers? This is also the pragmatic knowledge expected
\par
from a good poker player always facing the probability of the opponent's bluff.
\par
The issue does not affect only the counterterrorist official and expert, but the entire
\par
political establishment and intellectual culture and media discourse in general. The prob-
\par
lem is that not even a Colin Powell, with all his experience and access to the most privileged
\par
information, can penetrate what is behind the `secret knowledge' of counterterrorism -- the
\par
secret being essentially ignorance regarding the terrorists. And that even the New York
\par
Times can publish on its front page fabricated reports because it assumes the legitimacy of
\par
a `secret knowledge' forced on the community of believers. The role of critical terrorism
\par
studies has to be to remain fiercely sceptical about this new mythology. It is reminiscent
\par
of the myth of the wild man and points to a discourse that, at its core, holds on to the
\par
dichotomy between their `barbarism' and our `civilisation'. The unrecognised premise62 J. Zulaika
\par
is one of tabooing their voices and desires, by categorising their madness, suicides and
\par
killings as qualitatively distinct from ours, that is, by denying them a complex subjectivity
\par
like ours. The final outcome of such discourse is to justify a war of choice as a `just war',
\par
for the phantom of terrorism has become the only justification in the nuclear era. The latest
\par
fantasy scenario is one in which the West's superior drone technology can detect terrorists
\par
from 10,000 feet above in the sky and kill them by remote control as if in a video game.
\par
The British historian Hugh Trevor-Roper, having analysed the `grotesque mental con-
\par
struction' of European witchcraft, concluded that during the Renaissance, there was in fact
\par
a regression in superstitious modes of thought from the Middle Ages. He had the following
\par
uncanny observation: `Indeed, the more learned a man was in the traditional scholarship
\par
of the time, the more likely he was to support the witch-doctors' (1969, p. 154). Is current
\par
counterterrorism knowledge also in the ironic predicament that the more it `knows' the
\par
more fooled it is? As a sample, here is a similar commentary on experts predicting bio-
\par
logical terrorism by Milton Leitenberg: `The less the commentator seems to know about
\par
biological warfare the easier he seems to think the task is' (cited in Mueller 2006, p. 22).
\par
Following on from the earlier discussion on the value of `information' in the
\par
counterterrorist episteme, the question is, does having more of it help? Or is not having
\par
more of the same thing even more obfuscating? What historians of the CIA have concluded
\par
is that its game of deception included fooling itself and thereby becoming incapable of sort-
\par
ing out true information from self-deception. Already in the mid-1950s, an internal report
\par
regarding widespread frustration in the agency came to the conclusion that its young offi-
\par
cers saw that the intelligence service `was lying to itself' (Weiner 2007, p. 78). Lying, in
\par
fact, becomes an imperative sworn under oath. There was nothing more destructive to the
\par
agency's morale than Carter's post-Nixon moral allergy to deceit and its pledge never to
\par
lie to the American public, when deceit is the quintessence of a secret intelligence service.
\par
Carter's na\"ive aversion to deception and secret action amounted to a radical dismantling of
\par
the `dual sovereignty' of American politics throughout the Cold War.
\par
The other issue with information being by definition `secret' is what you do with infor-
\par
mation that is in the open. Imagine that the name of your next airplane hijacker is in a
\par
city's phone book, as was the case, and that 50--60 officers know that the hijacker is in
\par
the country: should you take it seriously? When such information is by nature ultra-secret,
\par
would paying attention to a phone book not betray in itself ignorance of what you are deal-
\par
ing with? In short, the information was in the system, yet why did the system refuse to read
\par
and act on it? Does such oversight not suggest that there is, at some level, unconscious
\par
but systemic complicity between terrorists and counterterrorists? Perhaps the complicity
\par
springs from sharing a culture of ultra-secrecy in which open information is dismissed as
\par
irrelevant and in which the temporality of waiting tends to grant more urgency to what
\par
could happen than to what is actually the case.
\par
From the one per cent doctrine to poker thinking
\par
It is well known that Vice President Cheney wanted to rule out any chance from the sys-
\par
tem when he maintained that if Saddam Hussein had a 1\% chance of obtaining WMD,
\par
the United States had to act to prevent it (Suskind 2006). In order to see the limits and
\par
catastrophic consequences of such an inflexible doctrine, one only has to look at the role
\par
of change in human action through the lens of the most prevalent American card game --
\par
poker. The art of the game of poker consists in minimising the element of luck, yet is based
\par
on the knowledge that not only can it never be completely eliminated from the game, but
\par
luck becomes the key strategic component to use to one's advantage and prevail.Critical Studies on Terrorism 63
\par
If detecting bluffs and false threats is central to counterterrorism, then poker might
\par
indeed be its best model for systemic knowledge -- not chess, where the moves can be
\par
predicted in advance, but poker, where the cards are unpredictable and bluffing is an
\par
essential tactic to the game. The crucial tactic for poker thinking is: `cheating and thwart-
\par
ing cheaters, leveraging uncertainty, bluffing and sussing out bluffers, managing risk and
\par
reward' (McManus 2009, p. 18). The admission that there are elements of `luck' or
\par
`chance' or `bluff' in poker is not a denial of its systemic nature, but only a realisation
\par
of its more complex statistics (see Neuman and Morgestern 1944).
\par
Playing with chance is pivotal to the terrorist modus operandi. Non-feedback action
\par
based on chance, innocence of the victim, ritual sacrifice and suicidal madness are all
\par
aspects of a logical space dominated ultimately, even if within a previously delimited field,
\par
by random election. The arbitrary election of the victim by chance carries the action to a
\par
different realm of possibility and meaning. The terrorist typically engages in a course of
\par
behaviour that strategically rules out feedback: once the airplane is hijacked for a suicide
\par
mission, there is no way back. It is the study of purpose that should be of key concern
\par
for terrorism studies: if feedback is required for teleological behaviour, the terrorist's non-
\par
feedback action is not only `purposeful' in its objectives but also deliberately `purposeless'
\par
in its means. The use of purposeless tactics in pursuit of political goals is what makes
\par
terrorism strategically so unpredictable. This is a system closer to poker than chess.
\par
There is one ancient concept in military theory that is central to terrorism: stratagem.
\par
It includes deception, surprise, ambush, feigned moves, disinformation, betrayal and other
\par
ploys. These trickeries have been in use from primitive warfare to nuclear deterrence. They
\par
are key to the ritualised action of warfare in traditional societies. The military strategy
\par
of the Cold War has been frequently compared with poker, beginning with D-day when
\par
Eisenhower employed various bluffing manoeuvres to outfox the Nazis. The second part of
\par
the twentieth century has been characterised `as an age of stratagem, in which theories of
\par
deterrence exploit the idea of bluff, intelligence agencies have become masters of deceit,
\par
and guerrilla warfare and terrorism consistently employ surprise, psychological tactics, and
\par
avoidance of battle' (Wheeler 1991, pp. 24--25). Diplomacy during the nuclear age was well
\par
described by the mathematician theorist of poker, Morgenstern, as a `real-life version of
\par
poker' (cited in McManus 2009, p. vii). The most dramatic example of such poker playing
\par
was when, on the brink of triggering a nuclear war, John Kennedy called Khrushchev's
\par
bluff during the Cuban missile crisis.
\par
Terrorism is the culmination of these tactics of deception at all military and informa-
\par
tion levels; hence, the need for poker thinking. There is a Basque card game, mus, which
\par
I used as a model of activism in my work on political violence. I compared the ekintza
\par
`action' of the militant with the all-or-nothing hordago in the mus game. The hordago
\par
point eliminates the point-by-point continuous process and, if accepted, condenses all alter-
\par
natives in a single bet. Hordago is the final self-corrective point, usually a bluff, when one
\par
is losing. The hordago interruption of the gradual process introduces a premise of ritual
\par
condensation -- an on/off, either/or digitalisation. The hordago is the last recourse of the
\par
loser -- an almost obligatory bluff -- and ritual premises of condensation and discontinuity
\par
are intrinsic to the entire phenomenon. Even if counterintuitive, professional gamblers also
\par
know that one should use the worst hands when bluffing, since, unlike for checking, one
\par
expects to lose if called for bluffing, it does not matter what hands are used. What is critical
\par
for counterterrorist thinking is not to be fooled by randomness and bluffing.
\par
The national trauma of 9/11 confronted the United States with the alternative of either
\par
not responding to the attack and thus implying defencelessness or replying massively and
\par
thus possibly making things worse. In terms of counterterrorist thinking, the alternative64 J. Zulaika
\par
was between a regression to a premodern mindset or a jump into a more creative thinking
\par
that would avoid its own self-generating repetition. The reaction by the Bush administration
\par
happened in terms of mechanical action/reaction responses with a medieval type of think-
\par
ing, including the use of torture, a crucial component of the entire witchcraft demonology.
\par
In political terms, the regression consisted in returning to a `state of exception' that led
\par
to Guant\'anamo and was visualised in the Abu Ghraib photographs of torture and sexual
\par
humiliation committed by the US forces.
\par
A different approach requires a radical departure from this counterterrorist mindset.
\par
It must borrow clues from a detective as to how to read the actor's desire; it must have a
\par
novelist's capacity for projection into the murderer's subjectivity; it must be able to inter-
\par
pret the politics of the unconscious of the suicide killer's `death drive'; it must strategise the
\par
way a poker play does. The political commentator Bill Schneider called President Bush's
\par
decision to go to war `Texas political poker, the ultimate high-stakes gamble' (McManus
\par
2009, p. 332) -- but it was poker premised on Cheney's blind inflexibility of the one per
\par
cent doctrine. It has been remarked that, in the nuclear era, the most important skill for a
\par
president may be the ability to sort out a genuine threat from a bluff. This requires genuine
\par
poker thinking, dominated by probability and making use of various kinds of inference and
\par
chance. This is a kind of thinking in which
\par
players gauge pot odds, randomise bluffs, fold when their hand is a statistical underdog, raise
\par
when it's the favourite. . . decoys and stealing are crucial, but patience is just as important. . .
\par
players spend most of their time picking up signs, moving into position, and working the count,
\par
but once every nine plays or so, on both offence and defense, their skills really have to pay off.
\par
Yet more than in most competitions, luck becomes pivotal. (McManus 2009, p. 339)
\par
Such complexity of various types of information, including those of the detective and the
\par
novelist and the art of the poker face, is what is missing from counterterrorism.
\par
Buddhist thinkers have argued that technical knowledge is not enough to master an art
\par
and that it must become an `artless art' that grows out of the unconscious. Not surpris-
\par
ingly, good poker players are believed to rely on intuition in order to read the states of
\par
mind of their opponents. This is also called `strategic flexibility', the ability to playfully
\par
randomise by a sense of `feel' and create algorithms that, in the words of the mathematician
\par
David Berlinski, `belong to the world of memory and meaning, desire and design' (cited
\par
in McManus 2009, p. 389). As Bill Gates put it:
\par
In poker, a player collects different pieces of information -- who's betting boldly, what cards
\par
are showing, what this guy's pattern of betting and bluffing is -- and then crunches all that
\par
data together to devise a plan for his own hand. I got pretty good at this kind of information
\par
processing. (Cited in McManus 2009, p. 397)
\par
Being skilful at poker requires first of all that one learns to view the world from the other's
\par
point of view. The poet Katy Lederer even talks of poker's `dirty' intimacy when you are
\par
able to `read' peoples' thoughts and feelings (cited in McManus 2009, p. 417).
\par
Getting into the other's subjective intimacy nowhere appears as `dirty' as in terrorism,
\par
but if one wants to have the skills of a good poker player, it becomes necessary to project
\par
oneself into the other's mind and subjectivity. In the end, this requires that the analyst,
\par
as Capote observed of the writer, and as it is standard in ethnography, should look at the
\par
terrorist subject through his/her own subjectivity and conclude that they belong to the same
\par
field. But this would require as a prerequisite dissolving the essential myth of the terrorist
\par
as a human being entirely unlike the rest of us.Critical Studies on Terrorism 65
\par
Conclusion: the force of fantasy and the real
\par
We began with the statement that terrorism testifies to a crisis in knowledge and as such
\par
is a salient condition for thought in the current world. Those of us critical of the uses
\par
of terrorism discourse to further military approaches to world problems such as nuclear
\par
armaments or world poverty tend to take to heart Giambattista Vico's advice that the first
\par
science to be learned should be mythology or the interpretation of fables. A current way
\par
of updating Vico's teaching is to say that one must take into account the force of fantasy
\par
behind current politics.
\par
A critical perspective on terrorism discourse confronts us at the outset with the onto-
\par
logical ambivalence of what is the real of the thing itself. The figure of the terrorist gives
\par
ground to a reality, the menace of which requires the almost doubling of the defence bud-
\par
get since the Cold War. The task of a critical approach is to problematise that real as
\par
necessarily imbued in fantasy. This requires we deploy a valid theory by which fantasy is
\par
not equal to the not-real, but rather `it constitutes a dimension of the real' (Butler 1990,
\par
p. 108). This is a theory of fantasy removed from the representational realism of the media
\par
whose reports on terrorism tend to be oblivious of the `state of the exception' in which they
\par
are gathered and produced (censorship, one-sided sources, information obtained under tor-
\par
ture, etc.). In such realism, `representation becomes a moment of the reproduction and
\par
consolidation of the real' (Butler 1990, p. 106). In our view, fantasy interrogates the real,
\par
considers it a political postulate and does not have a mimetic relationship to it. A pos-
\par
itivist view of the real stabilises itself by the phantasmatic exclusion of all absence as
\par
unreal. Terrorism is that disavowed phantasmatic exclusion, included in the system as
\par
exception that solidifies and gives ground to the politically real. As this real is shaped
\par
by the phantom of terrorism constrained by the state of exception, the exceptional phan-
\par
tasmatic draws the boundaries of the real and `assumes the status of the real, that is, when
\par
the two become compellingly conflated' (Butler 1990, p. 107). Thus, fantasy merges with
\par
the mask of the real.
\par
Not surprisingly, the current drone war has been described as `sheer fantasy, if not
\par
literally science fiction' (Sluka 2011, p. 72). It has become part of the huge military--
\par
media--entertainment industry. Politicians easily become fascinated with technological
\par
novelties and special operations. The fantasy plays into the seductive illusion of virtual war
\par
`as a surgical scalpel and not a bloodstained sword,' wrote Michael Ignatieff, adding: `We
\par
need to stay away from such fables of self-righteous invulnerability' (2000, pp. 214--215).
\par
But these `fables' are now the dominant culture, believed not only by the general public but
\par
also by the political class and the media elite. You will not read or hear in the mainstream
\par
media reports indignant of the drone killings on grounds that, in Kilcullen's numbers, 98\%
\par
of the victims are innocent. John Quincy Adams wrote in 1821 that `America. . . goes not
\par
abroad in search of monsters to destroy'; in that case, he added, `She might become the dic-
\par
tatress of the world: she would be no longer the ruler of her own spirit' (cited in Kilcullen
\par
2009, p. 1). Currently, America is taken by the powerful fantasy of the monster/terrorist.
\par
A candidate such as John Kerry, a war hero, may lose the presidency for being per-
\par
ceived as `soft' on terrorism. For the same reason, President Obama is unable to close
\par
Guant\'anamo.
\par
The phantom of the terrorist is responsible for historically charged events, such as
\par
sending the blind sheik, Omar Abdul Rahman, to life in prison after a trial that for the
\par
New York Times was a sham. He is among Islamists, the maximum spiritual and legal
\par
leader, kind of what the Pope is for Catholics, and a blind man who was brought to the
\par
United States repeatedly with visas issued by the CIA in order to help recruit mujahidin
\par
in the US-led anti-Soviet Afghan war. How much more `passion for ignorance' can a66 J. Zulaika
\par
counterterrorist culture display -- including the judicial system, the public opinion and
\par
academic silence -- than sending such a man to life in prison on the basis of an infil-
\par
trated informant working for money, disregarding the effect it was likely to have on his
\par
followers, including his closest associates, Osama bin Laden and Ayman al-Zawahiri? The
\par
informant, according to the New York Times, `began his testimony by admitting that he
\par
had lied to just about everybody he ever met' and whose testimony sounded `like sheer
\par
fantasy' (MacFarquhar 1995, p. A9). For the blind sheik to be condemned, it `only required
\par
[the government] to prove the intention to wage a terror campaign' (Editorial, New York
\par
Times 1995, p. A14). This is also the informer who turned the alleged fatwa issued by the
\par
blind sheik -- a charge he could never prove in court -- into a self-fulfilling prophecy: once
\par
he was imprisoned for life, the blind sheik did issue a fatwa, a supreme command that made
\par
9/11 morally imperative for his followers.
\par
But fantasy, in its semantic excess, also has the potential to interrogate and contest the
\par
claims of the real. In an individual's wish-fulfilment, fantasy is both the `I' who fantasises
\par
and the `I' who is in the fantasised narrative. Fantasy provides the frame and the setting for
\par
the individual who is caught in the narrative of images: `As a result, the subject, although
\par
always present in the fantasy, may be so in a desubjectivised form' (Laplanche and Pontalis
\par
1986, p. 27). The terrorist provides that fantasy setting in which the counterterrorist can
\par
participate in a desubjectivised manner. This also implies that the very identity of the sub-
\par
ject of fantasy is put into question by the multiple identifications available in a fantasy
\par
setting; the mastery the subject claims over the fantasy is already undone by the fantasy's
\par
own power of fragmentation.
\par
In the final analysis, what is most needed for counterterrorism might be more fan-
\par
tasy and fiction, not less. For anyone who has dealt with real `terrorists' on the ground,
\par
counterterrorism's `latest game in town' (terrorism resolved by drones shooting from the
\par
sky at alleged terrorists, as if in a Nintendo game) will appear as fatal fantasy. Eminent
\par
jurists and theologians believed in the seventeenth century that witches flew to the Sabbath,
\par
a belief that resulted in many thousands of them being burnt on the bonfire. It is no sur-
\par
prise that in our technology-driven era, counterterrorism officials will expect no less from
\par
drones and other flying objects. There is no point in trying to convince people that their
\par
fantasy images are unreal. What matters is to realise what kind of existence they do have
\par
as constitutive of the real. Here is where more fiction, an intensification of the crisis, might
\par
be a way out for the ontological problematic of perception and identity provoked by the
\par
terrorist phantom.
\par
One way of articulating this solution is the one proposed by Hayden White regarding
\par
the myth of the wild man, when he advocated a
\par
dissolution [of the grounds of the concept]. . . that permits us to distinguish between wild-
\par
ness as a myth and as a fiction, as an ontological state and as a historical stage of human
\par
development, as a moral condition and as an analytical category of cultural anthropology, and,
\par
finally, to recognise in the notion of the Wild Man an instrument of cultural projection that is
\par
as anomalous in conception as it is vicious in application. (1978, p. 157)
\par
We need fiction to dissolve the myth of the culture of terror and realise the role of fantasy
\par
in constituting the real; Montaigne's irony used the concept of savagery to question the
\par
myths of civilisation of his own society. Further, fictionalising the myths of terrorism is
\par
one strategy to destabilise its realist representations. Eventually, even the learned theolo-
\par
gians, prompted by the detective-like work of inquisitor Salazar (Henningsen 1980), were
\par
convinced that witches did not fly and that fantasy must have played a key role in the entireCritical Studies on Terrorism 67
\par
phenomenon. Eventually, even counterterrorists will come to grips with the hard fact that,
\par
as sadly proven by drones fired from Las Vegas into the Pakistani mountains being `the
\par
only game in town', it is the force of fantasy that is most real in the current culture of
\par
terror.
\par
Journal Editor's note
\par
This article was first presented as the Third Annual Critical Studies on Terrorism Lecture,
\par
an event inaugurated by the journal to celebrate scholars who have made a significant
\par
contribution to critical terrorism studies. Professor Zulaika's lecture was presented at `A
\par
Decade of Terrorism and Counter-terrorism since 9/11: Taking Stock and New Directions
\par
in Research and Policy', the BISA Critical Studies on Terrorism Working Group (CSTWG)
\par
Annual Conference, 8--11 September 2011, University of Strathclyde, Glasgow, UK. The
\par
editors are grateful to Professor Zulaika for accepting our invitation and to the conference
\par
organisers for including it in the programme.
\par
