1. Introduction
\par
Point-of-care testing (POC) is a growing area of diagnostic medicine that provides
\par
clinicians rapidly with laboratory data to improve the efficiency and speed of bed-
\par
side medical decision-making. Over the past 20 years, in vitro diagnostic (IVD)
\par
companies have made significant technological advances that allow accurate, rapid
\par
testing of basic laboratory panels at the bedside, or within close proximity in a sat-
\par
ellite laboratory. The instruments commonly require minimal technical skills to
\par
operate, a smaller sample volume, and less regulatory oversight. POC systems pro-
\par
vide a significant potential advantage for emergency departments where the goal of
\par
patient care is the rapid diagnosis and treatment of life-threatening conditions.
\par
Despite the presumed time value of information for physicians, hospital organiza-
\par
tions have been hesitant to implement emergency department (ED) POC systems.
\par
The current market trends can be explained by understanding how organizations
\par
view the potential value of a new technology and its effect on the decision-
\par
making process. A framework is provided to understand the challenges organiza-
\par
tions face when considering the adoption of a new technology, explore the market
\par
potential for ED POC systems, and discuss trends that will affect POC's future
