2. A marketplace decision-making
\par
perspective: determining the value
\par
of a new technology
\par
Tversky and Kahneman's Nobel Prize theories on how indi-
\par
viduals value alternative choices in the marketplace can pro-
\par
vide a framework for understanding the decision-making
\par
process associated with the adoption of a new technology,
\par
in this case POC devices [1]. New technologies involve
\par
trade-offs for the organization. As they evaluate a new inno-
\par
vation, it will be viewed through the lens of pre-existing
\par
processes. It will be compared relative to the old system
\par
and any improvements will be considered gains, whereas
\par
shortcomings will be considered losses. Research has shown
\par
that losses have a far greater influence on the decision-
\par
making process than similarly sized gains [2]. The ability
\par
of the organization to define clearly gains and losses is an
\par
important factor in its decision to adopt a new technology.
\par
In addition, most organizations are subject to a `status quo
\par
bias', which prevents change unless there is overwhelming
\par
evidence of a significant improvement [3]. Understanding
\par
the inherent bias against behavioral change is an important
\par
consideration when implementing the system required for
\par
the use of a new technology. Behavioral changes are consid-
\par
ered one of the largest managerial obstacles to implementa-
\par
tion. Applying this knowledge to ED-based POC systems
\par
reveals that the slow adoption rate is a function of not
\par
only the debate over the realizable benefits of POC when
\par
compared with the `status quo', but also the significant
\par
managerial obstacles required by an organization to adopt
\par
this new technology. This fundamental business psychology
\par
theory of decision-making will be used as a basis for the
\par
authors' discussion on the future of point-of-care testing
\par
in emergency departments.
\par
3. Point-of-care testing and emergency
\par
medicine
\par
At present there are high-quality point-of-care products
\par
in the market that could be used to provide most laboratory
\par
testing required by emergency departments [6,7]. In 2007,
\par
there were 116.8 million ED visits, of which 66.4\% received
\par
some form of diagnostic testing. The most common labora-
\par
tory tests included complete blood counts (35.4\%), urinaly-
\par
sis (22.5\%), BUN/creatinine (22.1\%), glucose (19.6\%),
\par
electrolytes (19.5\%) and cardiac biomarkers (12.1\%) [8].
\par
These utilization statistics suggest that there is a large poten-
\par
tial for market demand in the ED segment. Market research
\par
suggests that there is a growing demand for cardiac bio-
\par
markers, coagulation testing and infectious disease tests
\par
such as Streptococcus A, Helicobacter pylori, infectious
\par
mononucleosis, HIV and influenza A/B [9]. For years emer-
\par
gency departments have been using POC glucometers,
\par
occult blood testing, urine pregnancy testing and urine dip
\par
analysis [7]. The use of whole blood analyzers has been
\par
more common in the intensive care unit setting, especially
\par
with blood gas machines, but there is a growing demand
\par
for whole blood analyzers in the emergency department for
\par
critically ill patients [4,10,11]. There is a significant number
\par
of existing POC products that could provide potential value
\par
to emergency physicians.
\par
4. Outcomes of ED POC systems
\par
Market research data in the field of POC testing (POCT) is
\par
vast, but access is restricted by the high cost of obtaining
\par
full industry reports. Winters published, in 2010, data from
\par
the Enterprise Analysis Corp. (EAC), a healthcare market
\par
research firm that surveyed 317 hospitals in 2007 and
\par
2008 projecting the POC market to grow 7-- 8\% a year for
\par
the next few years [4]. This was a significant decline from ear-
\par
lier market projections of a growth rate of 10-- 12\% [5].
\par
Unique to EAC surveys is the ability to compare the use of
\par
POC products such as blood glucose, coagulation and blood
\par
gas testing within hospital systems. Although these devices
\par
have been widely adopted within most hospital systems,
\par
many of these products are not being used in their respective
\par
emergency departments.
\par
Despite improvements in laboratory processing, central lab
\par
turnaround time (TAT) has generally remained \ddag{} 1 h since
\par
at least 1965 [12,13]. There is a lack of published up-to-date
\par
evidence that assesses current central laboratory TAT for
\par
most emergency departments, as this is an institution-
\par
specific metric. In general, faster laboratory TAT is seen
\par
by emergency physicians as a desirable goal. For cardiac bio-
\par
markers, polled emergency physicians have a documented
\par
expectation of median TAT of {$<$} 37.5 min, in comparison
\par
with laboratorians' expectations of {$<$} 60 min [14]. In this
\par
study, the median reporting time for 90\% of tests was
\par
93 min. In a survey of emergency physicians, 42.9\%
\par
reported that the lab TAT delayed treatment and 61.4\%
\par
reported that lab TAT increased emergency department
\par
length of stay (ED-LOS) in over half of their patient
\par
encounters [15]. Many studies have shown an improved
\par
TAT with implementation of POCT when compared with
\par
central laboratory testing; however, there is less evidence of
\par
direct benefit in clinical outcomes. When evaluating an
\par
organization's decision-making process for an ED POC sys-
\par
tem, it is important to discuss the current and future direc-
\par
tions of research in clinical, operational and financial
\par
outcomes that may affect an institution's decision to adopt
\par
specific POC devices.
\par
4.1 Clinical and ED operational outcomes
\par
Research in POCT has focused on the impact on ED effi-
\par
ciency metrics and, to a lesser extent, clinical outcomes.
\par
POC systems using a range of various products have shown
