\par
consistent improvements in ED laboratory TAT compared
\par
with the central laboratory [11,16,17]. Although POC testing
\par
is generally accepted as a faster methodology, there are still
\par
mixed data on whether this improved TAT has clinical sig-
\par
nificance for patient outcomes. Several studies have shown
\par
that the reduction in TAT improves ED throughput using
\par
the metric of ED-LOS [10,17,18]. There is also research to sug-
\par
gest that studies such as POC electrolytes, blood gases and
\par
hematocrit have failed to show improvement in overall
\par
ED-LOS, despite the improved TAT [19,20]. The inconclu-
\par
sive outcomes of research are probably due to several factors,
\par
including institution-specific variables, physician work-
\par
flow and the difficulty of correlating laboratory TAT to an
\par
improvement in a multivariable complex process such as
\par
ED-LOS and patient outcomes. Research in the field of
\par
ED crowding has shown that ED-LOS is a multifactorial
\par
metric influenced by factors such as diagnostic imaging,
\par
serial physical examinations, specialty consultations and
\par
ED crowding [21-24]. This research suggests that ED-LOS
\par
is a poor proxy for measuring the clinical impact of
\par
improved laboratory TAT and clinical decision-making for
\par
all POC tests.
\par
POC cardiac biomarkers provide a potentially powerful
\par
case for POC testing, with standardized clinical outcomes
\par
metrics that can be directly measured to provide evidence of
\par
a quantifiable effect. There is a significant body of research
\par
that has focused on the potential for POC cardiac biomarkers
\par
to improve time to treatment for acute coronary syndrome
\par
(ACS) [25-28]. Despite the significant amount of research in
\par
the field of POC biomarkers, there is still controversy regard-
\par
ing the sensitivity of the assays and their appropriate use in the
\par
ED evaluation of potential ACS patients [29-33]. In the future,
\par
more accurate metrics need to be developed to assess the phy-
\par
sician decision-making process by reflecting the initiation of
\par
a therapeutic intervention, the change in disposition, or a
\par
change in perceived severity of illness.
\par
4.2 Financial outcomes
\par
The current perception is that POCT is significantly more
\par
expensive than central laboratory testing. Most studies use a
\par
simple cost analysis that creates a division between expenses
\par
that are related to volume (variable costs), and expenses related
\par
to owning and maintaining the equipment (fixed costs). In gen-
\par
eral, POC systems have higher variable costs and lower fixed
\par
costs [16,34-36]. The relative variable cost increase is a function
\par
of the single sample POC product design. It requires more
\par
expensive consumables and direct labor time. For example, if
\par
there are 10 patient samples, each must be run separately on
\par
a POC machine by a dedicated person, and may require the
\par
use of single packaged reagents. In the central laboratory ali-
\par
quots of cheaper bulk reagents are available and tests are run
\par
at the same time on a single machine. Automated analyzers
\par
do not require a dedicated full time employee (FTE) per
\par
machine and labor costs can be spread over multiple central
\par
laboratory processes. A simple financial analysis based on the
\par
fixed and variable costs is an inadequate method when making
\par
the decision to adopt a new technology.
\par
There are more factors that should be considered when
\par
developing the financial business case for evaluating a POC
\par
system or device. Using a 5- or 10-year project model will
\par
allow decision-makers to factor in the costs of continuing
\par
maintenance, machine replacement costs, continuing training
\par
costs for operators, costs to maintain accreditation for non-
\par
waivered devices, volume price bargaining, and other
\par
institution-specific costs that are not accounted for in the ini-
\par
tial implementation costs of a device or system. The returns
\par
can be estimated by comparing the cost-savings created by
\par
using the POC device relative to the alternative central labora-
\par
tory method. These could include estimated operational effi-
\par
ciencies, labor cost reductions, opportunity costs obtained
\par
from receiving test results faster, and savings from the cost
\par
of defects from central laboratory testing. Financial account-
\par
ing and project valuation can be a difficult task for clinicians
\par
unfamiliar with robust accounting systems. There are few
\par
published case examples that evaluate the broad financial
\par
implications of adopting a POC system or device [11,37,38].
\par
Future research and case reports will need to explore the
\par
financial implications of adopting a POC device beyond the
\par
simple cost analysis model to provide decision-makers with
\par
meaningful benchmarking data.
\par
5. Managerial obstacles to adoption
\par
The authors identified five key managerial obstacles to imple-
\par
mentation of an ED POC system. Each of these has a direct
\par
impact on the organization's current behaviors and can there-
\par
fore affect a manager's decision to support or oppose new
\par
technology adoption. It is important to keep in mind that
\par
the status quo comparisons are at the institutional level and
\par
generalizations have been used in order to provide a broader
\par
discussion of these issues.
\par
5.1 Management structure
\par
A robust POC system requires a management structure vastly
\par
different from a centralized laboratory system [11,39,40]. Adminis-
\par
trators are accountable for providing high-quality services that
\par
meet strict regulatory guidelines laid out by CLIA'88 and the
\par
accreditation organizations, the Joint Commission and the Col-
\par
lege of American Pathologists. There are several POC tests that
\par
are CLIA waived and require fewer managerial resources; how-
\par
ever, most of the whole blood tests used in the ED require signif-
\par
icant documentation to maintain accreditation. The test menu
\par
provided by an institution will ultimately dictate the necessary
\par
structure. As the test menu broadens and the number of non-
\par
waived products expands, there will be a greater need to have a
\par
strong management system. The evidence-based guidelines for
\par
the management of POC systems published by the National
\par
Academy of Clinical Biochemistry (NACB) recommend
\par
the use of a multidisciplinary management committee, the
\par
establishment of continuing training programs, and the use of
\par
