\par
The future of point-of-care testing in emergency departments
\par
continuous quality improvement techniques [6]. The implemen-
\par
tation of an extensive interdepartmental management system is a
\par
significant obstacle for the adoption of POC systems. Tradition-
\par
ally, hospitals are large complex organizations that have long-
\par
standing cultures of departmental silos. This phenomenon is
\par
described well in the healthcare business literature, and the
\par
implementation of a functional cross-departmental management
\par
structure is notoriously difficult.
\par
5.2 Cost of the system
\par
The cost of a new POC system is a significant barrier for
\par
hospital and departmental administrators. They must con-
\par
sider the cost of equipment, work space development, infor-
\par
mation technology interface, direct and indirect labor, and
\par
the recurring costs associated with establishing a quality sys-
\par
tem. In institutions where there is limited use of computer
\par
systems to manage clinical data, there is a significant cost
\par
associated with developing connectivity between POC sys-
\par
tems and the central laboratory. Administrators may have a
\par
difficult time justifying the allocation of funds to a new sys-
\par
tem that provides an equivalent data set to the existing sys-
\par
tem. The authors predict that organizations will attempt to
\par
curtail the full implementation costs of a POC system by
\par
adopting select products that require minimal resources
\par
and not implementing a dedicated management structure.
\par
5.3 Workflow impact
\par
POCT requires operators to be trained and competent on
\par
instrument use and maintenance. It is necessary to determine
\par
which workforce will be used in the POC system. One option
\par
is to use already existing personnel such as nurses, respiratory
\par
therapists or other licensed clinical professionals to operate the
\par
instruments, thereby saving on direct labor costs. This can
\par
create a potential to overburden task-saturated professionals
\par
and create resistance to adoption by the managers represent-
\par
ing these professionals. Another option is for an organiza-
\par
tion to hire laboratory-trained professionals to operate the
\par
machines in a satellite location within or near the emergency
\par
department. In some circumstances this may be a preferable
\par
workflow alternative for the clinical staff; however, it may cre-
\par
ate extra direct labor costs if there is not excess labor in the
\par
central laboratory. In addition, laboratory personnel may
\par
not adapt to the pace and demands of the ED work environ-
\par
ment. In either case, disruptive complex workflow changes
\par
create barriers to adoption.
\par
5.4 Clinical necessity
\par
Administrators are faced with the challenge of resource
\par
allocation and identifying projects that are clinically neces-
\par
sary. At present, there are clear data that cardiac bio-
\par
markers, glucose and coagulation markers in certain
\par
circumstances are medically necessary. In addition, POC
\par
vital parameters, including sodium, potassium, calcium,
\par
glucose, creatinine, blood gases, hemoglobin or hematocrit,
\par
ammonia and lactate, are generally preferred over central
\par
laboratory if turnaround time is {$>$} 1 h [6,41,42]. Furthermore,
\par
in addition to growing pressures to improve ED through-
\par
put, patient safety and clinical outcomes, there are now
\par
requirements for cardiac biomarker TAT of {$<$} 60 min for
\par
accredited chest pain centers. With regulatory bodies and
\par
insurance companies more focused on improving clinical
\par
outcomes, there is a potential for extra requirements on
\par
TAT for other diagnostic tests. Institutions must continu-
\par
ously evaluate their current central laboratory processes to
\par
determine whether their emergency department stands to
\par
benefit from the greatly reduced TAT associated with a
\par
POC system.
\par
5.5 Diagnostic accuracy
\par
Clinicians must be able to rely on the results produced by
\par
POC products and understand the differences in the diag-
\par
nostic test characteristics compared with central laboratory
\par
platforms. This requires close communication between ED
\par
clinicians and laboratorians. Some POC products fall short
\par
of core laboratory sensitivity and standard of error [33,43].
\par
It is important that ED physicians be confident in the test
\par
accuracy to promote the widespread use of POCT after
\par
implementation, thereby realizing the full financial bene-
\par
fits [17]. Quality initiatives and technology updates must be
\par
designed to optimize result accuracy and sensitivity [31]. In
\par
the future, clinicians and administrators will need to become
\par
familiar with the specific products in the market that pro-
\par
vide the highest quality results, and the limitations when
\par
compared with central laboratory standards.
\par
The business decision to adopt POCT depends on the
\par
administrators' and the clinicians' ability to evaluate criti-
\par
cally the clinical and financial benefits of this new technol-
\par
ogy and to understand the obstacles to the implementation
\par
and maintenance of a successful program in their unique
\par
institution. The current limited adoption of ED-based
\par
POC products is probably a result of the lack of overwhelm-
\par
ing evidence for the benefits of general POC testing systems
\par
and the perceived managerial obstacles. Based on the
\par
authors' analysis, institutions will be less inclined initially
\par
to start a fully robust ED POC system. They will probably
\par
adopt individual products when they are unable to meet
\par
clinical standards for decreased TAT for a specific test using
\par
the status quo. Products that are CLIA-waived and require
\par
minimal oversight are also more likely to be adopted in
\par
the ED.
\par
For emergency physicians, the natural desire to imple-
\par
ment a POC system must be viewed in light of these
\par
continuing managerial challenges and the relative lack of
\par
current evidence to support the claim that POC testing
\par
will improve their operational efficiency. It is important
\par
for emergency physicians who want to implement a
\par
POC test to tailor their requests based on the above
\par
discussion of the underlying business psychology that is
\par
driving key stakeholders' decision-making process within
\par
their institutions.
\par
