\par
6. Expert opinion
\par
Expert Opin. Med. Diagn. Downloaded from informahealthcare.com by Technische Universiteit Eindhoven on 01/11/15
\par
For personal use only.
\par
The current healthcare system places increasing pressure on EDs
\par
to provide efficient care using more advanced diagnostic tests to
\par
rule out conditions with high morbidity and mortality [44,45].
\par
The future of POCT in emergency medicine will be a function
\par
of two forces: the perceived clinicians' need for rapid laboratory
\par
turnaround and the ability of organizations to justify the mana-
\par
gerial obstacles to implementation. At present, these two forces
\par
are in opposition. In this section, the authors provide their opin-
\par
ion on future directions for outcomes research in the field of
\par
POC testing, and the potential industry advancements that
\par
would provide for future growth in the ED POC testing market.
\par
6.1 Future research focus
\par
Outcomes research should begin to focus on determining the
\par
impact of POC testing on the ED physicians' clinical deci-
\par
sion-making. There are few studies that have attempted to
\par
quantify the impact of receiving a laboratory result on an
\par
ED physician's decision-making process [10,19]. The authors
\par
believe that emergency physicians and researchers need to
\par
develop a scoring system or proxy metric that will allow for
\par
the measurement of the time value of information. Deriving
\par
this value requires an understanding of the unique cognitive
\par
strategy used in emergency medicine. Clinical decisions are
\par
made in an environment of diagnostic uncertainty, inade-
\par
quate information, and significant time pressure in a setting
\par
fraught with competing demands and distractions. Research
\par
in the field of clinical decision-making suggests that emer-
\par
gency physicians use a quick series of cognitive techniques
\par
to eliminate or discriminate among competing diagnoses [46].
\par
Emergency physicians continually triage the acuity of
\par
patients; this process relies heavily on their ability to obtain
\par
clinical data points as quickly as possible. Diagnostic and
\par
screening laboratory data allow physicians either to increase
\par
or to decrease the perceived level of acuity for an individual
\par
patient and redirect their focus as clinically necessary. By
\par
directly measuring the door to clinical decision-making
\par
time, one can compare the impact of POC testing relative
\par
to central laboratory testing and assign a time value
\par
for information.
\par
6.2 Market advancements
\par
The POC market is poised for growth over the next
\par
10 years and the IVD industry will have continued
\par
competitive market pressures that will bring about signifi-
\par
cant technological advances [4,5,9,47,48]. As more institu-
\par
tions become familiar with POC systems, there will
\par
probably be a significant growth in the literature published
\par
regarding management structures, financial returns and
\par
business cases on how to overcome perceived mana-
\par
gerial obstacles to adoption. The major hurdles for the
\par
industry include the development of POC assays that
\par
have sensitivities and specificities similar to their cen-
\par
tral laboratory counterparts, the ability to offer non-
\par
reusable components at a lower cost, and the ability to
\par
prove a meaningful clinical impact from adopting this
\par
new technology.
\par
6.3 Opinion summary
\par
The authors believe that further research on the impact
\par
of the time value of information for emergency physicians
\par
and the advancements in the POC market will produce sig-
\par
nificant growth in ED POC systems over the next 10 years.
\par
If the diagnostics industry continues to grow at predicted
\par
levels market demand and competition will drive the tech-
\par
nological and financial advances necessary to promote the
\par
adoption of these products by institutions and emergency
\par
departments. In the immediate future the growth of POC
\par
systems in emergency medicine will depend on researchers'
\par
abilities to provide organizations with data to evaluate crit-
\par
ically POCT benefits and provide best practices to help
\par
clinicians and administrators critically evaluate the decision
\par
to adopt this technology.
